    <node id="Honey Bee">
      <data key="d0">Honey Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Honey Bee is a specific type of social insect, with only six species worldwide and one in the Western Hemisphere, and is often confused with other insects like wasps. It is the central subject of beekeeping, with its biology, hive occupants, development, behavior, life cycle, and communication all being areas of study.

A Honey Bee's life is divided into distinct stages with different roles, such as a house bee and a field bee, and its lifespan and labor are influenced by seasonal activity and the needs of the colony. The species *Apis mellifera* is a common example, and its biology includes developmental stages like the larva and anatomical parts like the abdomen. Key behaviors include reproduction through swarming and communication, as well as absconding. As a social insect, it produces valuable products like honey and propolis.

The Honey Bee population follows a natural cycle, with a peak in the summer and a decline in the winter. It serves as a host for tracheal mites, which can shorten the lives of infested bees and weaken or destroy colonies, especially during the winter months. However, a strong colony can sometimes overcome the mite infestation by out-reproducing them in the spring and summer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a5bf9269446157b7f585b574d80342a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Africanized Bee">
      <data key="d0">Africanized Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Africanized bee is a hybrid honey bee that resulted from the crossbreeding of different honey bee races, including the African bee. This crossbreeding is considered an example that did not work out well, and its introduction was not deliberate. Known for being highly defensive, the Africanized bee is considered a potentially serious threat to humans and is categorized as a pest or predator. Its spread has prompted increased regulation and public fear over stinging incidents, which can lead to complete bans on beekeeping in certain areas. The introduction of the Africanized bee has also resulted in legal restrictions; for instance, in some regions, capturing wild swarms of unknown origin is a potential risk, and authorities may mandate that such swarms be handled and destroyed only by authorized personnel to prevent their spread. Consequently, controlling this bee is a major concern for apiary inspection programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a5bf9269446157b7f585b574d80342a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
