    <node id="Swarming">
      <data key="d0">Swarming</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Swarming is the natural reproductive process for a honey bee colony, ensuring the perpetuation of the species. This event involves the colony dividing, with the old queen and a large portion of the bees, often about half, leaving the parent hive to establish a new one. The process is driven by a "swarm impulse" and is triggered by factors such as a strong nectar flow, a large colony population, and the age of the queen.

From a beekeeper's perspective, swarming is generally considered an undesirable event because it severely weakens the original colony, which can reduce honey production and negatively affect its ability to survive the winter. Consequently, beekeepers often try to manage, control, or prevent swarming. One method to reduce the likelihood of swarming is to introduce a new, young queen in the fall. Despite being undesirable in modern beekeeping, swarming is also a method for beekeepers to acquire new bees. Historically, it was even encouraged, as collecting swarms was the primary way to increase the number of colonies. Due to its significant impact, swarming is a key topic for beekeepers, with its definition, process, and control methods being widely covered.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a5bf9269446157b7f585b574d80342a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diseases, Pests, And Predators">
      <data key="d0">Diseases, Pests, And Predators</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A major section of the book dedicated to the various health challenges and threats that honey bee colonies face, requiring management by the beekeeper.&lt;SEP&gt;This is a general classification of threats to honey bees, which can be caused by bacteria, viruses, protozoa, and fungi. These threats can affect either adult bees or brood and range in severity from minor to colony-destroying.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a5bf9269446157b7f585b574d80342a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parasitic Mites">
      <data key="d0">Parasitic Mites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Parasitic Mites are a classification of non-native pests that pose a significant and severe threat to honeybee colonies in the Western Hemisphere. This group, which includes species like the tracheal mite and the Varroa mite, arrived and began spreading in North America in the mid-1980s. Since their arrival, Parasitic Mites have caused the loss of thousands of bee colonies and can destroy a beekeeper's entire stock if not managed, making them a major concern for both beekeepers and regulators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a5bf9269446157b7f585b574d80342a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pesticides">
      <data key="d0">Pesticides</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A suggested potential contributor to Colony Collapse Disorder (CCD), representing one of the environmental stressors affecting honeybee health.&lt;SEP&gt;Pesticides are a broad category of chemical substances, including insecticides, herbicides, fungicides, and miticides, that pose a significant chemical threat to the health and survival of honey bees. While rarely sprayed on bees deliberately, the primary risk comes from accidental exposure in locations such as orchards, farms, and gardens. The effects of these pesticides on honey bees can vary widely, ranging from benign to lethal, representing a significant threat to entire bee colonies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a5bf9269446157b7f585b574d80342a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27bb80a716045bb643a01fd3aae1d84b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey">
      <data key="d0">Honey</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific category of value-added farm product listed under 'Other ideas' for farm businesses."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Honey is the sweet, viscous food substance produced by honey bees from the nectar of flowers. Worker and house bees process and convert the nectar, storing the resulting honey in combs within the hive as the colony's primary carbohydrate and energy source. This stored food is crucial for the colony's survival, serving as a reserve to sustain them through the winter or other periods when foraging is not possible, and is also a component of the food fed to bee larvae. Because it is such a valuable resource, considered the colony's "wealth," bees will defensively sting to protect their stores from predators.

For beekeepers, honey is the primary crop and a main benefit of their work, alongside pollination. Beekeepers harvest the surplus honey, but must ensure enough is left for the colony's winter survival, with a guideline being at least three to four frames equivalent. The potential for a surplus in a colony's first year is a key consideration, with nucleus hives generally being more productive than package bees. While selling surplus honey can help a beekeeper recover some costs, its value has not consistently kept pace with the rising price of equipment.

Honey has several distinct physical properties that affect its handling. It naturally contains pollen and small amounts of vitamins. It is hygroscopic, meaning it absorbs moisture from the air, and must be protected from high humidity and other bees after harvesting to prevent spoilage and robbing. Its viscosity means it flows best when warm, which is a key factor during extraction. Honey is also prone to a natural process called granulation, or crystallization, and its quality can be negatively impacted by excessive or prolonged heat. The entire process of extraction, storage, and bottling revolves around managing these properties.&lt;SEP&gt;The subject of a February 2010 statistical report from the USDA, National Agricultural Statistics Service.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a5bf9269446157b7f585b574d80342a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comb Honey">
      <data key="d0">Comb Honey</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Comb honey is a type of honey that is eaten directly from its natural wax comb. The text provides detailed instructions on how to cut, drain, and package it in square plastic boxes. It can be eaten by the spoonful, spread on toast, or given as a gift. The wax is edible."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A specific hive product where honey is kept and sold within its original beeswax comb.&lt;SEP&gt;Comb honey is a specialized type of honey crop that is sold and eaten within its original wax comb. Its production is described as a difficult operation requiring significant beekeeping knowledge and a strong bee colony, and is generally not recommended for beginners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a5bf9269446157b7f585b574d80342a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
