    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeper">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeper</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Beekeeper is an individual who practices beekeeping by managing and caring for honey bee colonies. Their success is highly dependent on their ability to be committed, observant, and continuously learn about bee biology and behavior, as their attitude and actions directly influence the bees' reactions. This distinguishes a "committed beekeeper" from a passive "bee haver" who does not actively manage the hives.

The Beekeeper's responsibilities are extensive and vary seasonally. Their tasks include conducting regular hive inspections, using essential tools like a smoker and hive tool, and wearing protective gear, although some experienced beekeepers may work without a smoker. They are responsible for a wide range of management activities such as installing new bee packages, managing the queen's introduction and acceptance, evaluating and replacing the queen (requeening), and making decisions like reversing hive bodies. Seasonal chores include spring maintenance and preparing the colony for winter by assessing food stores, installing mouse guards, and setting up windbreaks.

A critical aspect of the Beekeeper's role is ensuring colony health. This involves identifying, preventing, and treating pests and diseases, such as Varroa mites and American Foulbrood (AFB). The responsibility for mite control now rests solely on the individual beekeeper, whose practices, like transporting colonies, can also contribute to the spread of pests. If necessary, a beekeeper must destroy infected equipment to prevent disease transmission and should seek expert help when needed. They also protect their colonies from external threats like pesticides by moving or confining the hives.

Furthermore, the Beekeeper manages the colony's nutrition by providing supplementary food like sugar, candy, or pollen substitutes, as they have limited control over the bees' natural pollen diet. They also handle the harvesting and processing of hive products like comb honey and beeswax, a process which can be messy and time-consuming. Advice for novice beekeepers often emphasizes sourcing bees from a mentor, selecting a good hive location, and starting with standard equipment. Some beekeepers engage in the short-sighted practice of destroying drone brood, viewing them as non-contributory to the hive. Ultimately, the beekeeper's year is focused on these management interventions, which differs from the bee's natural, year-round cycle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
