    <node id="Universities">
      <data key="d0">Universities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of higher education institutions, which the text links directly to 'farm to college movements'.&lt;SEP&gt;Based on the provided information, universities are multifaceted institutions with a significant, though complex, role in agriculture and related fields. They are key centers for research, continually generating new knowledge in areas like bees and beekeeping, thereby serving as a vital resource for practitioners. However, universities are also portrayed as a source of information, hearsay, or hype about new farming projects, and farmers are advised to test such information on their own farms before committing fully. Furthermore, it is noted that the traditional farming methods and new crop varieties promoted by universities, such as new corn varieties, can disproportionately benefit large-scale farmers over small ones. Finally, professors from these institutions are involved in the debate over farm definitions, an interest that is partly financial, as the number of farmers in a state can determine the size of their federally funded programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e64fb1fe152a62cd06403742bd6beac5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeper Organizations">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeper Organizations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"Beekeeper Organizations, such as local, state, or national clubs and associations, are described as crucial for beekeepers to network, share information, learn about new solutions, and avoid being overwhelmed."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="North America">
      <data key="d0">North America</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">North America has a diverse agricultural history, being the site of the first wheat harvest in 1602 and the native habitat for a unique wild rice (manoomin), which grows in the northern lakes and is a central food for the Anishinabeg people. The continent is also where the Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) model originated in 1986 with the founding of Indian Line Farm and Temple-Wilton Community Farm.

Regarding its fauna, North America is home to between 2,000 and 3,000 native bee species, but the Western honey bee (*Apis mellifera*) is not one of them. Honey bees were introduced to the continent's East Coast by settlers around 1628, spreading to the West Coast by 1850. Today, all honey bees found there belong to this species, with beekeepers commonly raising European races. These colonies face a difficult existence due to challenges like harsh winters. While the list of major predators is relatively small—consisting mainly of bears, skunks, mice, and wax moths—the most significant threats are widespread diseases and invasive pests. Since the mid-1980s, invasive parasitic mites, such as tracheal and Varroa mites, have created significant problems, likely introduced through the illegal importation of bees. These challenges are compounded by diseases like Nosema and Chalkbrood, as well as the northward migration of the Africanized bee, which reached the United States in 1991.

In the context of public health, the form of avian influenza that is contagious to humans has not been found in North America, a fact that mitigates a key argument against the practice of raising backyard chickens.&lt;SEP&gt;The continent where the Amish population has grown from 5,000 in 1900 to over 100,000 today.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104466acc0eb6f55c05f02aa258ea31a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollination">
      <data key="d0">Pollination</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Pollination is a key benefit of beekeeping and a primary motivation for some people to acquire bees. It is essential for ensuring adequate crops in both small home gardens and commercial operations."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The ecological process facilitated by bees. The text states that there was no understanding of this process or the bees' vital role in it until the 1700s.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of transferring pollen between flowers of the same species, which is a valuable outcome of a honey bee's targeted foraging behavior. The text highlights this as a reason for the honey bee's value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feral Colonies">
      <data key="d0">Feral Colonies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Feral Colonies are wild, unmanaged colonies of honey bees. The text notes that they often die out due to natural pressures, especially in North America, and are replenished by swarms from other colonies."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeper's Commitment">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeper's Commitment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Beekeeper's Commitment is the high level of dedication, learning, and regular work required to be a successful beekeeper. The text emphasizes that a lack of this commitment is a primary reason for failure in beekeeping."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Novice Beekeepers">
      <data key="d0">Novice Beekeepers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Novice Beekeepers are beginners in the field who often underestimate the commitment required for success. The text states that for every successful beekeeper, two or three novices do not succeed."&gt;</data>
