    <node id="Bees">
      <data key="d0">Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, here is a comprehensive summary about Bees:

Bees are social insects that live in colonies, typically housed in hives, with a structured society composed of a queen, workers, and drones. The general population of bees is responsible for essential tasks such as foraging for nectar and pollen, building wax comb which they strengthen each year, and caring for the brood. The collective activities of the bees, such as drawing out foundation and their level of activity at the hive entrance, serve as key indicators of the colony's health, progress, and strength, which is heavily dependent on the queen's productivity. Their lives are governed by odors, and their behavior changes significantly with the seasons. In the spring, they expand the colony and reproduce naturally through swarming, which perpetuates the species. During fall and winter, their activity shifts to survival; they process sugar syrup into stores, propolize cracks in the hive to seal out drafts, and form a tight cluster to generate warmth, consuming their honey stores as they move upwards through the hive. The presence of young bees is particularly crucial for the colony's winter survival.

Central to the practice of beekeeping, bees require careful and respectful handling. Their temperament can vary, and while they can be calmed by smoke, they are sensitive to various stimuli and may become upset by dark colors, woolly fabrics, shiny objects, substrate vibrations, and strong scents like perfume or gasoline. Bees are reactive creatures that will sting to defend themselves, their colony, or their honey stores, although they are often less defensive when being handled in a package for installation into a new hive. Beekeepers manage them with specialized equipment, the design of which is influenced by the bees' natural behaviors, such as sealing any gap that violates "bee space." Management also involves providing supplementary feeding with sugar syrup, especially during winter, spring, or when establishing a new colony, and care must be taken as burned sugar is lethal to them. When honey is harvested, it must be protected, as the bees will attempt to rob or steal it back. In nature, bees face challenges from disease and predators and prefer elevated homes with a southerly exposure and a balance of sun and shade, often inhabiting hollow trees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Challenges">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Challenges</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Beekeeping Challenges refers to the numerous problems that can cause a bee colony to weaken and die. The text lists specific examples such as disease, drought, harsh winters, and predators."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swarms">
      <data key="d0">Swarms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Swarms are a natural part of bee life and reproduction. The text mentions that feral colonies that die off are often replaced by swarms from other wild colonies or from a beekeeper's holdings."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A natural group of bees that have left a hive to form a new colony. They are a potential source of bees for beekeepers, but are only available seasonally (spring to early summer) and may carry diseases, mites, or be of the Africanized variety, which can pose legal and safety issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Groups of bees seeking a new home. The text references studies of swarms to understand the natural preferences of bees for their hive locations, such as elevation and exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Beekeeping Knowledge is the essential understanding of bees and management techniques required for success. The text stresses that this knowledge is dynamic and must be acquired through continuous learning from various sources."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Program">
      <data key="d0">Training Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Training Program represents formal and informal learning opportunities for beekeepers, such as classrooms, bee schools, and workshops. Attending these is presented as a crucial first step for acquiring basic knowledge."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive">
      <data key="d0">Hive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Hive is the man-made structure, dwelling, or nest that houses a honey bee colony, serving as the central location for all bee activities. It is composed of multiple components, including a bottom board, hive bodies or supers, frames that hold foundation or drawn comb, an inner cover, and an outer cover for protection. Inside the hive, bees store pollen, nectar, and honey; raise their brood; and communicate through dance language and pheromones. It is the operational base for house bees, the residence of the queen, and the site of social interactions that can lead to wing wear.

Beekeepers interact with the hive for various tasks such as inspections, maintenance, and honey harvesting, with visits lasting from a few minutes to half an hour. These tasks involve opening the hive, cleaning out dead bees and debris, scraping excess propolis, rearranging its parts, and adding or removing frames when installing a new colony. A hive's weight is often assessed to estimate the colony's food stores. Because bees are highly defensive of their home, a perceived threat can trigger stinging.

The hive's management includes treating for issues like tracheal mite infestations and preparing it for seasonal changes. For winter, this involves ensuring proper insulation and ventilation, using mouse guards to protect the entrance, clearing away snow, and shielding it with windbreaks. Proper placement is critical; a hive should be on a stable stand on level or gently sloped ground with at least 3 to 4 feet of clear space, away from depressions where moist air can collect. As a hive can become tall and unstable, and a full one can weigh up to 80 pounds, care must be taken during handling. It is also noted that hives can be targets of theft and vandalism and are sensitive to vibrations, which can agitate the colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Bees">
      <data key="d0">Honey Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Honey Bees are social insects that live year-round in colonies, typically within hives. They are a non-native species to the Western Hemisphere, having been introduced to North America in the 1600s. The colony is comprised of individual bees with specific roles—the queen, workers, and drones—and a complex life cycle. While individual bees have short lifespans, resulting in a high daily population turnover, the colony as a whole persists through continuous brood-rearing.

The behavior of Honey Bees is governed by their physiological development, genetic composition, and external stimuli like odors and sounds, rather than human-like emotions. However, they exhibit patterned behavior and are capable of learning from experience. Their collective activities include foraging for resources, performing household chores within the hive, clustering together for warmth, and creating comb and propolis. To communicate, they use sophisticated methods, including a "dance language" and chemical signals known as pheromones. For sustenance, Honey Bees collect nectar, pollen, and water, requiring a balanced diet for their health and survival. They store surplus honey, which allows the colony to survive through the winter.

Central to beekeeping, Honey Bees are critically important to the human way of life due to their role as pollinators. Their typical foraging range is 1 to 2 miles, though it can extend up to 3 miles, and they will always work the closest available source of forage first. This extensive range, however, exposes them to environmental threats. The health and survival of Honey Bees are threatened by a variety of factors, including pests like ants, wax moths, mice, and skunks; parasites such as *Varroa jacobsonii*; and diseases like American Foulbrood, which targets the brood and can lead to colony collapse. They are also highly vulnerable to pesticides and insecticides. A healthy, vigorous colony is the best defense against many of these threats, and their well-being can be negatively impacted by an over-saturation of hives in one area. To protect their colony, home, and stored honey from predators, Honey Bees exhibit defensive behaviors, including stinging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Equipment">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Equipment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beekeeping Equipment encompasses the various man-made tools, gear, and supplies necessary for the practice of beekeeping, or apiculture. This equipment includes essential items for housing and managing bees, such as a complete hive, hive bodies, honey supers, frames, a foundation, and a feeder. For performing tasks like installation and general management, a beekeeper requires a set of tools including a smoker, a hive tool, pliers, and a jackknife, as well as protective gear like a veil.

For new beekeepers, acquiring this equipment represents a significant initial financial expenditure, estimated to be between $210 and $275. Novices are strongly advised to purchase standard, "tried and true" equipment and to understand the specific use and value of each piece before buying. It is also important to be aware that stored equipment, especially components like supers and hive bodies, is vulnerable to infestation and damage by wax moths. This equipment, which can also include nucleus hives and package bees, is available from various organizations that act as suppliers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beeyard">
      <data key="d0">Beeyard</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A beeyard is the location where beekeeping is practiced. It can be a place of discomfort, characterized by heat and buzzing bees, but it is also described as a place of calmness and serenity where one can observe the bees' activities.&lt;SEP&gt;The location where beehives are kept. The text notes that electricity is usually not available in the beeyard, which can make certain tools like shop vacuums impractical for clearing bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crop Pollination">
      <data key="d0">Crop Pollination</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A commercial service offered by beekeepers, particularly sideline and commercial operators. It is recognized as a key agricultural component and a source of income.&lt;SEP&gt;Crop pollination is mentioned as a potential sideline business for experienced beekeepers who have a sufficient number of hives. It represents a way to transition the hobby into a more commercial endeavor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeepers">
      <data key="d0">Beekeepers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beekeepers are individuals who practice the craft of keeping and managing honey bee colonies. Their work is multifaceted, involving a deep understanding of the colony's life cycle, from managing swarm control to harvesting honey. A significant aspect of their responsibility is ensuring the colony has enough food stores to survive the winter, which involves various management techniques such as hive insulation, ventilation, and monitoring food levels, with practices often adapted to the local climate and advice from fellow beekeepers. The knowledge, methods, and equipment used by beekeepers have evolved significantly over time, from rudimentary practices to modern, science-based hive management.

Beekeepers face numerous challenges. They must protect their hives from predators like skunks and bears and manage significant threats from diseases and pests, such as mites. Their responsibility in managing bee health has increased, especially in light of cuts to government support programs. They are also concerned with public perception issues and must follow state-level regulations. A major concern is the arrival of the Africanized bee; beekeepers can inadvertently spread these bees through pollination activities but are also crucial for their control and for educating the public and officials on the matter.

The personal experience of a beekeeper involves frequent interaction with bees. While they often work alone, facing challenges like heat and discomfort, many find great enthusiasm and serenity in the practice. Bee stings are a regular occurrence, and beekeepers typically build up both a physiological and psychological tolerance, experiencing minimal reactions over time, though this tolerance may need to be re-established after a period without stings. However, alongside developing tolerance, they also face a risk of developing allergies to stings.

There are different types of beekeepers, and their needs and practices can vary. Beginners, for example, are advised to ensure they have easy and regular access to their hives for monitoring and adequate space to work. Small-scale operators receive specific advice on honey processing, such as heating it to no more than 120°F (49°C) to manage granulation and deciding between producing comb or extracted honey. There is also a distinction between general and commercial beekeepers, who may have different preferences for equipment, such as hive covers optimized for transportation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colony">
      <data key="d0">Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A honey bee colony is the complete social and biological unit of honey bees, which lives and works together in a hive year-round. Functioning as a single, complex organism with its own annual cycle, a colony is typically composed of a single queen, thousands of female worker bees, and, at certain times, male drones. The survival and success of the colony depend on the coordinated efforts and communication of all its members, as the collective needs of the unit dictate the activities of individual bees.

The colony's primary survival strategy is to store honey, which it will defend vigorously. Its population, honey production, and overall health are affected by factors like nutrition and events such as swarming, which is its natural method of reproduction that occurs every two to three years. After a swarm, the parent colony remains and raises a new queen to continue its life cycle. The colony's ability to survive, particularly through the winter, is dependent on its population dynamics, having a productive queen, and maintaining adequate food stores. While internal life can be disrupted by hive visits, management is often necessary, involving controlling growth, preparing for winter, and sometimes providing supplemental feeding. For beginners, it is recommended to start with two colonies for comparison and the ability to support a weaker one.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Beekeeper">
      <data key="d0">New Beekeeper</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A new beekeeper is someone in the early stages of the hobby, typically the first one to two years. They are advised to treat beekeeping strictly as a hobby and to expect a significant initial financial outlay with little to no chance of making money.&lt;SEP&gt;The intended audience of the text, likely in their first year of beekeeping. They are guided on hive management practices like supering and preparing for winter, and are encouraged to learn from experienced beekeepers and keep methods simple initially.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experienced Beekeeper">
      <data key="d0">Experienced Beekeeper</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An experienced beekeeper is a knowledgeable individual who has practiced the hobby for several years and can serve as a valuable resource, particularly for novices. They can be consulted to observe a beekeeper's practices and techniques to help identify the causes of persistent problems, such as issues with a bee colony's temperament. An experienced beekeeper can also act as a potential mentor, though it is cautioned that years of experience do not always guarantee competence or teaching ability, so a mentor should be chosen with care. Furthermore, unlike new beekeepers, they may have the opportunity to turn the hobby into a sideline business, such as selling honey or providing crop pollination services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Predators">
      <data key="d0">Predators</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Predators pose a significant threat to farm animals and honey bee colonies. They encompass a variety of animals, including coyotes, owls, raccoons, opossums, cats, skunks, and mice, as well as human vandals. For farm animals, especially poultry and other livestock, protective shelters are needed. In the case of honey bee colonies, predators raid beehives to steal stored honey. This harassment agitates the bees, causing them to become hyper-reactive and defensive for days, triggering their defensive stinging behavior. Chicken coops need to be properly secured to protect backyard chickens from both ground and aerial predators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Year">
      <data key="d0">First Year</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'first year' or 'first season' of beekeeping is highlighted as a critical period involving the largest financial investment (estimated at $210-$275 for a hive and gear) and a steep learning curve, with unlikely financial returns from honey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Down Side">
      <data key="d0">Down Side</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'down side' refers to the negative, challenging aspects of beekeeping. This includes the physical discomfort of heat, stickiness, and heavy equipment, working alone, and the disconcerting experience of being stung, especially inside protective clothing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Up Side">
      <data key="d0">Up Side</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'up side' refers to the positive and rewarding aspects of beekeeping that are said to far outweigh the negatives. It includes the enthusiasm shared among beekeepers and the calmness and serenity experienced when observing the bees in the beeyard.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sideline Business">
      <data key="d0">Sideline Business</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sideline business is a potential long-term outcome for an experienced beekeeper. It involves generating income from beekeeping activities, such as selling surplus honey or offering crop pollination services, but is not a realistic goal for a beginner.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Visits">
      <data key="d0">Hive Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hive visits are the periodic inspections a beekeeper makes. They can be brief or last up to 30 minutes, are described as a 'substantial disruption to colony life,' and are considered essential chores that cannot be ignored without leading to problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stinging">
      <data key="d0">Stinging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Stinging is a defensive behavior or action performed by bees when they perceive a threat to themselves or their hive. It can be triggered by various stimuli, such as proximity to a hive, swatting, or even certain fragrances worn by people."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Sting">
      <data key="d0">Bee Sting</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A bee sting is described as an interesting event, which is the physical result of a bee's defensive stinging action. For most people, a single sting is momentarily painful but has no lasting aftereffects, though reactions can vary significantly."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A painful and potentially dangerous event where a bee injects venom into a victim. The stinger, which is barbed, remains in the skin along with the venom sac, continuing to pump venom. The act is fatal to the bee and releases an alarm odor that can attract other bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Reaction">
      <data key="d0">Local Reaction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A local reaction is the most common type of response to a bee sting, characterized by pain, a welt, itching, and sometimes severe swelling confined to the sting site. Although it can be unpleasant, it is not considered a life-threatening or true allergic reaction."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anaphylactic Shock">
      <data key="d0">Anaphylactic Shock</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Anaphylactic shock is a severe, systemic, and life-threatening allergic reaction to a bee sting. Symptoms include nausea, body rash, difficulty breathing, and dizziness, and it requires immediate medical attention."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physicians">
      <data key="d0">Physicians</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Physicians are medical professionals who diagnose and treat reactions to bee stings. They may diagnose a person as allergic, often erring on the side of caution, and can prescribe treatments like sting kits or desensitization shots for those with severe reactions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tolerance to Stings">
      <data key="d0">Tolerance to Stings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Tolerance to stings is a physiological adaptation where the body's reaction to bee venom lessens with repeated exposure. Beekeepers often develop this tolerance, which requires periodic stings (e.g., every 10 days) to be maintained."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allergic People">
      <data key="d0">Allergic People</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Allergic People are individuals who experience systemic, life-threatening reactions to insect stings, such as anaphylactic shock. They are often advised by physicians to consider any sting life-threatening and take special precautions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeper Reaction">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeper Reaction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Beekeeper Reaction is described as the first and mildest level of response to a bee sting. It involves minimal, momentary pain, a small welt, and is quickly forgotten, which is typical for individuals with high tolerance."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Severe Swelling Reaction">
      <data key="d0">Severe Swelling Reaction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Severe Swelling Reaction is the second level of response to a bee sting, characterized by significant, startling, and persistent swelling at the sting site. Although alarming, it is considered a local, non-life-threatening reaction."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sting Kit">
      <data key="d0">Sting Kit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Sting Kit is a medical kit prescribed by a physician for individuals with severe allergies. It is meant to be carried at all times to treat the onset of anaphylactic shock immediately following a sting."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Desensitization Shots">
      <data key="d0">Desensitization Shots</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Desensitization Shots are a medical treatment involving a series of injections designed to build up the body's tolerance to bee venom, thereby reducing the severity of future allergic reactions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General Public">
      <data key="d0">General Public</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The General Public is a group whose perception of bee stings is often based on fear and a lack of understanding. The text contrasts their potential for severe reactions with the tolerance developed by beekeepers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allergic Reaction">
      <data key="d0">Allergic Reaction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A severe and increasing intolerance to bee stings that can develop over time in both beekeepers and non-beekeepers. It is considered a true allergy requiring medical assistance and can be caused by stings or even airborne particles from a beekeeper's clothing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apiary Inspection Program">
      <data key="d0">Apiary Inspection Program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">State-level regulatory programs created to control the spread of honey bee diseases, parasitic mites, and the Africanized bee. Many of these programs are facing budget cuts, shifting more responsibility onto beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Bee Brood Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Honey Bee Brood Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Highly contagious diseases that can cause the demise of bee colonies. The detection and control of these diseases have historically been a primary focus of beekeeping regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Western Hemisphere">
      <data key="d0">Western Hemisphere</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Western Hemisphere is a geographical region comprising North and South America. It is the only region where the Western honey bee species (_Apis mellifera_) is found, although a significant event was the introduction of the _Apis mellifera scutellata_ subspecies, which was a different type of bee than any previously kept there. Following this introduction, Africanized bees have been spreading throughout the hemisphere for over 30 years. Additionally, the Western Hemisphere is a region to which the two species of parasitic mites that threaten honeybees are not native.&lt;SEP&gt;The geographical region mentioned in the foreword as the location of native agriculture (maize, squash) prior to Columbus's arrival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2b2fd01aa45dadaa82a243d140ec5f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tolerance">
      <data key="d0">Tolerance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A physiological state of reduced reaction to bee venom that can be developed through repeated stings. The text notes that beekeepers often build this tolerance, but it can wear off after a period without stings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nonbeekeepers">
      <data key="d0">Nonbeekeepers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of people who do not work with bees. The text clarifies that they, like beekeepers, can develop severe allergic reactions to bee stings over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeepers' Families">
      <data key="d0">Beekeepers' Families</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Individuals who reside with beekeepers. They are identified as being at risk of developing bee sting allergies, even without direct contact with bees, by inhaling dried venom particles from the beekeeper's clothing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee">
      <data key="d0">Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The insect responsible for stinging. The act of stinging is fatal to an individual bee because its barbed stinger and attached venom sac are torn from its body and left in the victim.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stinger">
      <data key="d0">Stinger</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The barbed, sharp organ of a bee used to inject venom. It has two moving lancets that work it progressively deeper into the victim's skin and is left behind after the bee pulls away.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Venom Sac">
      <data key="d0">Venom Sac</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An anatomical part of the bee attached to the stinger that is left behind in the victim after a sting. It continues to pump venom into the wound even after the bee is gone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alarm Odor">
      <data key="d0">Alarm Odor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Alarm Odor is a specific type of pheromone associated with bee venom. It is released upon stinging an intruder to signal its location to other bees, directing further defensive attacks to that spot."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical pheromone released by a bee when it stings. This scent serves as a stimulus, alerting and provoking other nearby bees to attack.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Children">
      <data key="d0">Children</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Children are a central demographic group discussed in the provided texts, particularly in the contexts of family labor on farms, health concerns, and community engagement. They are identified as a specific group within "Family Labor," and the texts explore how to integrate them into farm chores while teaching them safety, responsibility, and business skills to ensure a positive experience. With appropriate patience and supervision, children can contribute to family labor by performing age-appropriate tasks such as caring for small animals, weeding, or picking fruit.

The texts also highlight children's vulnerability to health issues. They are identified as the demographic most affected by the obesity epidemic and are the main beneficiaries of educational and nutritional initiatives promoted by food movements. Furthermore, children are noted to experience more pain from bee stings, potentially due to psychological factors or their smaller physical size.

Finally, the texts note a shift in interest among the younger generation of the Navajo community, with children increasingly moving away from "mainstream America" and volunteering and participating in farming activities at the community farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea6dd73e41a475c9933ac4783be5f57a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Municipalities">
      <data key="d0">Municipalities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Local government entities that are increasingly imposing their own regulations, including bans or restrictions, on beekeeping activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Federal Laws">
      <data key="d0">Federal Laws</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">National-level regulations that govern beekeeping. The text mentions a specific federal law that bans the importation of live bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Humans">
      <data key="d0">Humans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group identified as being at risk, as the text states the Africanized bee is a 'potentially serious threat to humans'.&lt;SEP&gt;A group mentioned in contrast to bees, particularly regarding hive placement. The text notes that humans often cannot accommodate the bees' preference for elevated hives because humans need to be able to access them.&lt;SEP&gt;Humans are discussed as consumers of bee products like pollen, propolis, and royal jelly, often for health benefits that are supported by anecdotal rather than scientific evidence. They are also the subjects of medical treatments using bee venom for allergies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Regulations">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A set of laws governing the practice of beekeeping. These regulations are primarily at the state level in the U.S., as there are no federal laws except one banning the importation of live bees. They are influenced by issues like parasitic mites and the spread of Africanized bees."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State's Department Of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">State's Department Of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A state-level government agency that provides beekeepers with information on local laws and regulations concerning beekeeping."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cooperative Extension System">
      <data key="d0">Cooperative Extension System</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An organization that serves as a resource for beekeepers seeking information about state and municipal laws and regulations."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Beekeeping Organization">
      <data key="d0">Local Beekeeping Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A local group that assists beekeepers in understanding and navigating the laws and regulations in their specific area."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honeybees">
      <data key="d0">Honeybees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Honeybees are a species of insect central to the practice of beekeeping. They are highly valued for honey production and are also essential for pollination, a process that underpins both the human diet and the environment. Honeybees are the subject of practical guides that cover how to acquire, manage, start, and maintain them, with publications like the 'American Bee Journal' providing information on the species. From an economic standpoint, an apiary operation focused on honey can be modeled on a production of 60 pounds of honey per hive, which generates an income of $180.00. This model yields a net profit of $90.00 after accounting for $90.00 in variable costs and requires 11 hours of labor. Despite their importance, honeybees are threatened by factors such as mites and the negative public perception of related bee types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Era Of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d0">First Era Of Beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A historical period of beekeeping from ancient times until around 1500, characterized by exploiting bees rather than managing them, using primitive hives like skeps, and a philosophy that led to breeding for weakness."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skep">
      <data key="d0">Skep</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A domed hive made of twisted straw, used during the First Era of Beekeeping as a precursor to modern wooden hives."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Public">
      <data key="d0">The Public</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The general population, which is described as often not understanding the scope and importance of honeybees. Their perception and fear, particularly concerning Africanized bees, can lead to total bans on beekeeping in certain areas."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The general population, who need to be educated about Africanized bees to avoid panic. Their fear can lead to pressure on public officials for restrictive regulations. They must also learn to live with and deal with the bees in affected areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Importation Ban Of Live Bees">
      <data key="d0">Importation Ban Of Live Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The only federal law in the United States that regulates beekeeping. The text suggests that the illegal violation of this ban is the likely reason parasitic mites were introduced to North America."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Certificate Of Inspection">
      <data key="d0">Certificate Of Inspection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A document required by some state regulations for the importation of live bees. It serves as proof that the bees were recently inspected and found to be free of diseases and mites."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mentor">
      <data key="d0">Mentor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An experienced beekeeper who can provide guidance and information to new beekeepers, especially concerning local laws and regulations."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An experienced gardener, often a graduate of the program, who volunteers to support a new home gardener for a full season, providing guidance and making home visits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Equipment Supplier">
      <data key="d0">Local Equipment Supplier</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A business that provides beekeeping supplies and also serves as a source of information for beekeepers regarding local and state regulations."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stone-Age People">
      <data key="d0">Stone-Age People</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Identified as the first beekeepers from the earliest period of human history. Evidence from ancient cave paintings shows they robbed bees of their honey."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Era Of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d0">Second Era Of Beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A distinct historical period in beekeeping, lasting from approximately 1500 until 1850, following the first era."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Third Era Of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d0">Third Era Of Beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A distinct historical period in beekeeping, lasting from 1850 until the present day, following the second era."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fourth Era Of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d0">Fourth Era Of Beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A proposed current era in beekeeping, characterized by extensive research, the increasing role of bees as commercial pollinators, pressures from human population growth, and modern problems like mites and Africanized bees."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Romans">
      <data key="d0">Romans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An ancient society active during the first era of beekeeping. They developed some knowledge, such as feeding bees, but had a limited understanding of the colony's internal dynamics."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An ancient civilization that practiced a rudimentary form of beekeeping. They had limited and often incorrect knowledge about bee biology, such as believing the queen was a male 'king' and that wax was collected from plants, largely because they could not observe the inner workings of a hive without removable comb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hollow Log Hives">
      <data key="d0">Hollow Log Hives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A primitive type of beehive made from a hollowed-out log section. It was one of the earliest forms of hives used by beekeepers and could still be found in use in the 20th century."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Middle Ages">
      <data key="d0">Middle Ages</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical period noted in the text for the development of protective clothing for beekeepers, which was an improvement over the minimal protection previously used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Second Era of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d0">The Second Era of Beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A period in beekeeping history from approximately 1500 to 1850. It was characterized by expanding scientific knowledge of bee biology, including the identification of the queen as female, drones as male, the source of beeswax, and the bee's role in pollination. Honey bees were also first brought to North America during this era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Third Era of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d0">The Third Era of Beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The modern era of beekeeping that began around 1850. It was initiated by Lorenzo L. Langstroth's discovery of 'bee space,' which led to revolutionary equipment like the Langstroth hive with movable frames, allowing for larger honey crops without destroying colonies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lorenzo L. Langstroth">
      <data key="d0">Lorenzo L. Langstroth</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A reverend known as the 'father of modern beekeeping.' In 1850, he discovered the principle of 'bee space,' which led to his invention of the Langstroth hive and revolutionized beekeeping practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Lorenzo L. Langstroth is an individual mentioned in the text, associated with the Langstroth hive, a fundamental piece of modern beekeeping equipment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Space">
      <data key="d0">Bee Space</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A foundational concept in modern beekeeping discovered by Langstroth. It is the specific gap (approximately % inch) that bees naturally leave open for passage and will not fill with comb or propolis. This discovery enabled the creation of hives with removable frames.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific gap measurement (roughly 1/4 to 3/8 inch) that bees respect and do not fill with comb or propolis. Its discovery in the second era of beekeeping history was crucial for the development of modern hives with removable frames.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific, precise gap that bees maintain between combs. The text notes that violating this space, such as with the queen cage, prompts bees to build undesirable wild comb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Langstroth Hive">
      <data key="d0">Langstroth Hive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A modern beehive design created by Lorenzo L. Langstroth. Its dimensions are based on the principle of bee space, allowing frames to be moved and inspected without being glued to the hive walls by the bees.&lt;SEP&gt;The Langstroth hive is a type of modern beehive with removable frames, implicitly linked to Lorenzo L. Langstroth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Movable Frames">
      <data key="d0">Movable Frames</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key piece of beekeeping equipment whose effective use was made possible by the discovery of bee space. These frames allow beekeepers to inspect hives and extract honey without killing the bee colony, a major advancement over previous methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Era of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d0">Early Era of Beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The earliest period of beekeeping discussed in the text, preceding 1500. It was characterized by a lack of scientific knowledge about bee biology, leading to misunderstandings about the sexes of bees, the origin of wax, and the process of pollination. Hives lacked removable combs, making observation difficult.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Queen">
      <data key="d0">The Queen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The sole egg-laying female in a honeybee colony. Her health and productivity are a primary concern, assessed by observing the 'brood pattern' she creates on the frames."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The Queen is the central figure in a bee colony responsible for laying eggs. Her performance, age, and health are critical factors for the colony's population and overall success. Beekeepers often evaluate and replace her, a process known as requeening, to ensure the hive's vitality, especially before winter.&lt;SEP&gt;The sole egg-laying female in a honey bee colony. Early beekeepers incorrectly identified the queen as a male 'king.' It was not until the 1500s that the queen was correctly identified as a female. The modern queen excluder is designed to control her movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Drone">
      <data key="d0">The Drone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The male bees within a hive. Their sex was correctly identified during the 1600s, though their precise role in mating remained unclear for some time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Worker">
      <data key="d0">The Worker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The non-reproductive female bees in a hive. They were correctly identified as female during the 1700s as part of the growing scientific understanding of bee biology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wax">
      <data key="d0">Wax</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wax is a substance produced and secreted by honey bees, which they use to construct the comb within their hive. Its production is stimulated by feeding, specifically depending on the consumption of sugar rather than pollen. Historically, wax was misunderstood as a substance that bees collected from plants, and its true origin as a bee secretion was not discovered until the 1700s. During honey extraction, the crushing and straining method can result in honey full of wax particles if it is not filtered properly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollen">
      <data key="d0">Pollen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, here is a comprehensive summary about Pollen:

Pollen is the dust-like male reproductive element of flowers, which honey bees collect and consolidate into pellets. It serves as a vital and essential food source for the bee colony, providing the primary source of protein, fats, vitamins, and minerals. These nutrients are crucial for the development of muscles and glands and are a key ingredient in the brood food fed to larvae. Pollen is particularly important for newly emerged young bees and for raising brood, with large quantities being collected in the early season for this purpose.

Field and forager bees gather pollen from plants, a process that is an instinctual, patterned behavior. They carry it back to the hive on their hind legs, and beekeepers can often see them arriving with these colorful pellets. Information regarding the availability, quantity, and quality of pollen sources is communicated within the colony using the bees' "Dance Language."

Inside the hive, pollen is stored in the comb's cells, and its presence is a sign of a well-provisioned colony. It can be identified by its varied colors, including yellow, orange, gray, red, green, blue, and white. Beekeepers are advised to check for adequate stores, with recommendations suggesting at least one to two frames, and ideally three to four frames, saved for winter.

Pollen is also a hive product that can be collected by beekeepers. Historically, early beekeepers sometimes mistook these pollen pellets for wax. Today, it is sold to humans as a health food supplement, purported to contain all necessary vitamins and minerals. However, both its efficacy and its safety are debated, as pollen can be a carrier for insecticides and pesticides brought back to the hive, and its source is often unknown. Beekeepers can also collect pollen and feed it back to their own bees when needed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pilgrims">
      <data key="d0">Pilgrims</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of early European settlers in North America. The text mentions them to provide a historical timeframe, noting that honey bees were introduced to the continent 'soon after the Pilgrims' arrived.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="East Coast">
      <data key="d0">East Coast</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific region in North America where honey bees were first successfully introduced around the year 1628.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="West Coast">
      <data key="d0">West Coast</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The region of North America that honey bees had spread to by 1850, having been transported by ships from the East Coast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Propolis">
      <data key="d0">Propolis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Propolis, also known as bee glue, is a resinous substance or mixture that worker honey bees produce by collecting materials from plants, specifically the buds and bark of trees. The availability of propolis sources is communicated between bees using the Dance Language.

Within the hive, propolis has several critical functions. It is primarily used as a sealant to fill cracks, crevices, and unwanted open spaces, which is particularly important in preparing the hive for winter. Bees also use it to reinforce and strengthen the comb structure. However, bees will not deposit propolis in areas known as "bee space." In a unique defensive use, bees can also use propolis to embalm intruders, such as a mouse, that they have killed but are too large to remove.

For beekeepers, propolis can present challenges. Its adhesive quality can glue hive components, like the outer cover, so firmly that a sharp whack is needed to free them. Beekeepers often scrape off excess propolis during spring cleaning to ease hive management. It can also be an undesirable substance in late-season comb honey production and may get mixed in with hive debris during honey extraction, requiring the honey to be strained.

As one of the five "other hive products," propolis is also collected by humans. It is used as a natural health aid in products like toothpaste and tinctures, though scientific evidence for its health benefits is limited.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beeswax Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Beeswax Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A product used within beehives that is made from recycled beeswax. A large quantity of beeswax collected by beekeepers is used for this purpose."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A piece of beekeeping equipment developed in the Third Era. It consists of a sheet of wax used in a movable frame to guide bees in building straight, new comb.&lt;SEP&gt;A starter base that beekeepers place in hive frames. New colonies must secrete wax and build comb on this foundation, a process that requires a steady supply of syrup or nectar.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Extractor">
      <data key="d0">Honey Extractor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant piece of equipment developed in the Third Era. This machine allows beekeepers to remove honey from the comb of movable frames without destroying the comb itself.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen Excluder">
      <data key="d0">Queen Excluder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Queen Excluder, also known as a 'honey excluder', is a specialized and optional piece of beekeeping equipment, described as a perforated sheet or screen. Developed in the Third Era, this device is placed in a beehive between the brood box and the honey supers. Its design features openings that are large enough to allow smaller worker bees to pass through but are too small for the larger queen and drones. The primary purpose of the Queen Excluder is to restrict the queen's movement, preventing her from laying eggs in the honey supers and ensuring those areas are used exclusively for honey storage. It is noted that this piece of equipment is rarely needed by first-year beekeepers, as bees can be reluctant to work above it, especially during a weak nectar flow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Langstroth">
      <data key="d0">Langstroth</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical figure whose discovery of movable, renewable frames in the 1800s revolutionized beekeeping, allowing for the study of hives without destroying colonies and enabling the rise of commercial honey operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrial Revolution">
      <data key="d0">Industrial Revolution</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical period that brought significant societal and lifestyle changes, which hastened and enhanced the evolution of the modern classification of beekeepers.&lt;SEP&gt;The Industrial Revolution is a historical period that, along with the Green Revolution, constructed a food system that became highly dependent on nonrenewable energy resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hobbyist Beekeeper">
      <data key="d0">Hobbyist Beekeeper</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of beekeeper who keeps a small number of hives (from one to around twenty) for reasons other than primary income. This group constitutes 90-95% of beekeepers in the country.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sideline Operator">
      <data key="d0">Sideline Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A beekeeper who maintains a small business with 12 to 200 hives for supplementary income, often alongside other full-time employment. They represent at least 5% of all beekeepers and may focus on honey production or crop pollination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commercial Operator">
      <data key="d0">Commercial Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A professional beekeeper who earns their entire income from bees, managing from a few hundred to several thousand hives. Despite comprising only about 1% of beekeepers, they own the majority of hives in the country.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Italian Bees">
      <data key="d0">Italian Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific race of bees that is the standard in the country, known for being common, readily available, gentle, and easily handled. They are recommended for beginners.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific type of bee, recommended as a 3-pound package with a queen for starting a new hive. They are the inhabitants of the hive and interact with its components.&lt;SEP&gt;Italian bees are a specific race or subspecies of the Western honey bee (Apis mellifera), popular among beekeepers for their productivity and gentle nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Industry">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Industry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The overall sector related to beekeeping, which includes honey producers, pollinators, queen producers, equipment manufacturers, research facilities, and publishers of beekeeping literature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Federal Research Facilities">
      <data key="d0">Federal Research Facilities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Governmental institutions that were developed to conduct scientific research on beekeeping as it gained recognition as a significant agricultural component.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colleges and Universities">
      <data key="d0">Colleges and Universities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Higher education institutions that offer coursework, degrees, and research opportunities in apiculture (the science of beekeeping), contributing to the increased knowledge in the field.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apiculture">
      <data key="d0">Apiculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The science of beekeeping. The text notes that knowledge and understanding of apiculture has increased immensely due to academic and government interest, and that colleges and universities offer coursework and degrees in it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Movable, Renewable Frames">
      <data key="d0">Movable, Renewable Frames</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A revolutionary beekeeping technology discovered by Langstroth. These frames allow for hive manipulation and study without destroying the colony, which enabled larger honey crops and the rise of commercial beekeeping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Production">
      <data key="d0">Honey Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Honey Production is a key activity of a bee colony and a primary goal for beekeepers. The process of swarming severely reduces or eliminates a colony's honey production for the season due to population loss and a temporary halt in egg-laying."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A key task within the colony, mentioned as a primary activity alongside brood rearing and foraging. It involves the receiving and processing of nectar by worker bees.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary commercial activity for sideline and commercial beekeepers. The text notes this can be done on a large scale, with honey shipped by the carload, and is a way for operators to make an income.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen or Package Production">
      <data key="d0">Queen or Package Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized business within commercial beekeeping that involves breeding and selling queen bees or packages of bees to other beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Package of Bees">
      <data key="d0">Package of Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method for starting a new bee colony, recommended for beginners. It involves installing a purchased package of bees into new equipment, allowing the novice to grow in confidence with the colony.&lt;SEP&gt;A method of acquiring bees, consisting of a screened cage with a measured quantity of bees (e.g., 2, 3, or 5 pounds) and a queen. They typically originate in the southern United States and are recommended for beginners.&lt;SEP&gt;The entire transport unit being installed, which includes a population of bees, a feeder can, and a caged queen. The text describes how to open and handle this package.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nucleus Hive">
      <data key="d0">Nucleus Hive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Nucleus Hive, often called a "nuc," is a small, complete, and pre-established starter bee colony used to start a new hive. It is one of the primary options recommended for a beginning beekeeper to acquire bees and is presented as an alternative to a package of bees. A Nucleus Hive contains all the components of a functioning colony, including frames, comb, brood, adult bees, stores of honey and pollen, and an accepted, laying queen. Because it is a small but complete colony, it is ready for immediate growth and may even produce surplus honey in its first year. Its small size makes it less defensive, which is an advantage for beginners. However, its primary disadvantage is the potential to transmit diseases through its pre-existing comb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Established Colony">
      <data key="d0">Established Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A mature bee colony that has existed for at least one season and contains a large number of bees. It is generally not recommended for beginners as its size and population can be intimidating. Colonies for sale may be old or neglected, potentially having issues like disease or poor equipment that a novice might not recognize."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A fully-formed, mature bee colony. The text advises that beginners should not start with an established colony, but rather with a package or a nuc.&lt;SEP&gt;An option for acquiring bees that is not recommended for beginners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Manufacturers and Distributors of Beekeeping Equipment">
      <data key="d0">Manufacturers and Distributors of Beekeeping Equipment</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A component of the beekeeping industry that is responsible for creating and selling the various supplies and equipment needed by beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Publishers of Beekeeping Literature">
      <data key="d0">Publishers of Beekeeping Literature</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A component of the beekeeping industry focused on creating and distributing informational materials such as magazines, journals, and books about beekeeping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beginner Beekeeper">
      <data key="d0">Beginner Beekeeper</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A novice individual starting in beekeeping. The text advises them to start with two colonies, find a mentor, join local organizations, read books, and understand that they may not get a honey surplus in their first year.&lt;SEP&gt;The target audience for the text's advice. The text provides specific recommendations on equipment and bee acquisition methods for those new to beekeeping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Beekeepers' Organization">
      <data key="d0">Local Beekeepers' Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A group that beginners are encouraged to join, even before owning bees. These organizations are a source of information about books, classes, workshops, and bee schools, and they welcome and support new members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="County Extension Office">
      <data key="d0">County Extension Office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local government office that can provide information on how to find the local beekeepers' association.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wasp">
      <data key="d0">Wasp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An insect, such as a hornet or yellow jacket, that is related to but distinct from bees. Wasps are often mistaken for honey bees and are frequently the culprits in stinging incidents blamed on bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southeast Asia">
      <data key="d0">Southeast Asia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Southeast Asia is a geographical region where goat meat is a prominent feature of the local cuisine, which has influenced its growing acceptance in the U.S. diet. The region is also the native home to five of the six species of honey bees in the world, serving as the primary area where species such as Apis cerana (the Eastern honey bee), Apis dorsata, and Apis florea are found. Additionally, Southeast Asia is the geographic region where the Varroa mite originated as a parasite of the Eastern honey bee.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Schools">
      <data key="d0">Bee Schools</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Educational events, often held annually by beekeeping associations, specifically designed for beginners to learn about beekeeping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Package Bees">
      <data key="d0">Package Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Package bees are a common commercial product used by beekeepers, especially novices, to start a new honey bee colony. Described as a "bunch of bees in a box," a package is a starter unit that lacks a true colony structure. It consists of a screened container holding a specific quantity of adult worker bees, typically around 3 pounds or 10,000 bees, along with a separate, caged queen and a food source like a can of syrup.

These bees are sourced from large parent colonies, often in southern beeyards, where they are separated from their original queen. They are then shipped, frequently in bulk, with a new, young queen, a process described as traumatic for the bees. Package bees are widely advertised in beekeeping publications, sold by equipment dealers, and are readily available in the spring, particularly in April and May.

For beekeepers, package bees offer several advantages. They are affordable, less defensive, and generally easy for beginners to handle and install. Because they are shipped without comb, there is a minimized risk of transmitting comb-related diseases, and it is unlikely that a colony started from a package will swarm in its first season. However, there are also disadvantages. The new colony gets a slow start and is initially dependent on the beekeeper for resources. There is a risk that the worker bees will not accept the new, non-original queen. Furthermore, while comb-based diseases are less of a concern, mites can still be a problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nucleus Hive (Nuc)">
      <data key="d0">Nucleus Hive (Nuc)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Nucleus Hive (nuc) is a small, complete starter colony of bees, typically containing three to five frames of brood, bees, food, and a queen.&lt;SEP&gt;A small, established bee colony (a nuc) that is one of the recommended ways for a beginner to start beekeeping.&lt;SEP&gt;A small, self-contained starter bee colony that includes a laying queen, bees, and several frames of comb. It is sold as an alternative to a package of bees and is transferred into a full-sized permanent hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Equipment Dealer">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Equipment Dealer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A business that sells beekeeping supplies and can be a source of information for finding local beekeeping associations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Magazines">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Magazines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of publication recommended for beginners to subscribe to. They provide valuable instructional information, lead to sources, and keep readers updated on local and national developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Appendix A">
      <data key="d0">Appendix A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A section of the book from which the text is excerpted, intended to help beginners select good books about bees and beekeeping from the many available.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Western Honey Bee">
      <data key="d0">Western Honey Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A species of honey bee, the subject of the beekeeping index topics.&lt;SEP&gt;The Western honey bee (Apis mellifera) is a species of honey bee that became a host for the Varroa mite in 1960. It is considered a good host for this parasite due to its relatively long pupal development time, which allows the mites to mature and mate within the brood cell.&lt;SEP&gt;The only species of honey bee found in the Western Hemisphere.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066653911102a71446dc7a3246a0c193</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow Jacket">
      <data key="d0">Yellow Jacket</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The yellow jacket is a type of wasp that is often mistaken for a honey bee due to some resemblance. However, it is only distantly related to the honey bee at the suborder level and is known for its repeated stings."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A type of wasp that somewhat resembles a honey bee. It is known for its repeated stings and is often the actual culprit in stinging incidents blamed on honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bumblebee">
      <data key="d0">Bumblebee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The bumblebee is a close relative of the honey bee, belonging to the same superfamily (Apoidea). It is often found in the same locations as honey bees and its similar appearance can cause confusion."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A type of bee that is a closer relative to the honey bee than a wasp, but is not a honey bee itself. It contributes to the public's confusion about bee identification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Animal Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">Animal Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The broad biological classification mentioned in a chart (not shown) to provide context for where honey bees fit.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apis Mellifera">
      <data key="d0">Apis Mellifera</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Known as the Western or European honey bee, Apis mellifera is the primary species of honey bee discussed. Its origins are traced to the Near East, and it has spread globally, partly through natural migration and partly through human introduction. It is the only species of honey bee found in the Western Hemisphere, and it has numerous subspecies (races) and man-made hybrids."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The common honey bee, which is described as a good host for the Varroa mite due to its pupal development time of about 12 days, allowing at least one mite to mature per infested cell.&lt;SEP&gt;The scientific name for the honey bee species, which includes various races like African and European bees. The text notes that colonies of any race can be gentle when small.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brother Adam">
      <data key="d0">Brother Adam</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Brother Adam was a monk at the Buckfast Abbey in Great Britain who is credited with developing the Buckfast hybrid bee through many years of selective crossbreeding."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A monk at Buckfast Abbey in Great Britain who, through many years of effort, developed the Buckfast hybrid bee.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Near East">
      <data key="d0">Near East</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Near East is identified as the geographical region where the honey bee, Apis mellifera, is believed to have originated before spreading to other parts of the world."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Britain">
      <data key="d0">Great Britain</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"Great Britain is the location of the Buckfast Abbey and the region for which the Buckfast hybrid bee was specifically developed to suit its climatic conditions."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The country where Buckfast Abbey is located and the origin of the native British black bees (Apis mellifera mellifera).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Italian Peninsula">
      <data key="d0">Italian Peninsula</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Italian peninsula is the region of geographic isolation where the Italian race of honey bee (Apis mellifera ligustica) evolved."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buckfast Abbey">
      <data key="d0">Buckfast Abbey</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"Buckfast Abbey is a monastery in Great Britain where Brother Adam conducted his work on breeding the Buckfast hybrid bee."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An abbey located in Great Britain where Brother Adam conducted his work to develop the Buckfast bee line.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Italian Bee">
      <data key="d0">Italian Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Italian bee (Apis mellifera ligustica) is a well-known race of the Western honey bee that originated on the Italian peninsula. It is a parent stock for hybrids like the Starline and Buckfast."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A race of honey bee (A.m. ligustica) that is the most common in the country. It was used in the cross to create the Buckfast hybrid and makes up the majority of drone genetics for mating.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carniolan Bee">
      <data key="d0">Carniolan Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Carniolan bee (A. m. carnica) is a race of the Western honey bee. It is used as parent stock for the Midnite hybrid."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caucasian Bee">
      <data key="d0">Caucasian Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Caucasian bee (A. m. caucasica) is a race of the Western honey bee. It is used as parent stock, along with the Carniolan bee, to create the Midnite hybrid."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buckfast Hybrid">
      <data key="d0">Buckfast Hybrid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Buckfast hybrid is a bee developed by Brother Adam at Buckfast Abbey. It was created primarily from a cross of British black bees and Italian bees and is noted for high honey production and gentleness."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Starline Hybrid">
      <data key="d0">Starline Hybrid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Starline hybrid is a bee developed from Italian stock. Its desirable traits include good honey production, rapid spring buildup, and gentleness. It is a parent line for the Double Hybrid."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Midnite Hybrid">
      <data key="d0">Midnite Hybrid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Midnite hybrid is derived from a cross of Caucasian and Carniolan bee stocks. These bees are dark, gentle, and are said to work at lower temperatures and have excellent honey production. It is a parent line for the Double Hybrid."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Double Hybrid">
      <data key="d0">Double Hybrid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Double Hybrid is a bee created by crossing the Starline and Midnite hybrid lines, presumably to combine the best characteristics of both."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hymenoptera">
      <data key="d0">Hymenoptera</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Hymenoptera is the taxonomic order to which bees, wasps, and ants belong. It is a high-level scientific classification mentioned in the honey bee's family tree."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Africa">
      <data key="d0">Africa</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The continent of origin for the _Apis mellifera scutellata_ subspecies of honey bee. These bees evolved in southern Africa, developing aggressive and defensive traits suited to that environment."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Africa is a continent where the honey bee (Apis mellifera) spread naturally from its origin in the Near East. It is also the origin of the Apis mellifera scutellata subspecies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Europe">
      <data key="d0">Europe</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Europe is a continent where the honey bee (Apis mellifera) spread naturally from its origins. It is now home to this species and several of its races, like the Italian, Carniolan, and Caucasian.&lt;SEP&gt;Identified as one of the source continents, along with Mexico, for the tracheal mites that were introduced to North America.&lt;SEP&gt;Europe is the continent where Allison Sosna studied and developed her love for food, which later influenced her career path.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asia">
      <data key="d0">Asia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Asia is a continent where the honey bee (Apis mellifera) spread naturally from its origins in the Near East.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="South America">
      <data key="d0">South America</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A continent whose flower industry produced cut-rate flowers that outcompeted and ultimately led to the closure of the Schenkel's rose business."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"The continent where the Africanized bee first established itself and spread widely after escaping from a research program in Brazil."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;South America is a continent where all honey bees normally kept or found are of the species Apis mellifera, introduced by humans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Australia">
      <data key="d0">Australia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Australia is a continent and country notable for several reasons. It is the only continent mentioned where the Varroa mite has not been found. All honey bees present in Australia are of the *Apis mellifera* species, introduced by humans. The permaculture system was developed there in the late 1970s. In some areas, the introduction of earthworms into soils where they were not naturally present led to a significant increase in pasture production (30-50%). The barramundi fish is native to Australia and is well-known for its culinary qualities. Australia is also used as an example of a distant, unverifiable source for meat, contrasting with local sourcing models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16c3fe8c089550fd16987a1c4f301a2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonization">
      <data key="d0">Colonization</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonization is the historical process by which settlers carried the honey bee (Apis mellifera) to new parts of the world, such as North America, leading to its global distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apis Cerana">
      <data key="d0">Apis Cerana</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Known as the Eastern honey bee, Apis cerana is a species of honey bee found primarily in Southeast Asia. It is a distinct species from the Western honey bee (Apis mellifera).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
