    <node id="African Bee">
      <data key="d0">African Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The African bee (Apis mellifera scutellata) is a race of the Western honey bee known for being a parent stock of the Africanized bee.&lt;SEP&gt;The original race of bee from which Africanized bees inherit their dominant defensive characteristics. They evolved in a warm climate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="British Black Bees">
      <data key="d0">British Black Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A native race of honey bee from Great Britain (Apis mellifera mellifera) that was crossed with Italian bees to create the Buckfast hybrid.&lt;SEP&gt;The native British black bee (Apis mellifera mellifera) is a race of the Western honey bee. It was crossbred with Italian bees to create the Buckfast hybrid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apoidea">
      <data key="d0">Apoidea</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Apoidea is a taxonomic superfamily that includes honey bees and their close relatives, the bumblebees and orchid bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apis (Genus)">
      <data key="d0">Apis (Genus)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Apis is the taxonomic genus for honey bees, containing six species worldwide, including Apis mellifera and Apis cerana.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apidae">
      <data key="d0">Apidae</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Apidae is the taxonomic family for bees, which includes the genus Apis (honey bees) and contains about 3,500 North American species.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Bee Hybrids">
      <data key="d0">Honey Bee Hybrids</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A general category of bees created by crossing specific, highly selected, and inbred lines to fix desired characteristics. The text mentions that maintaining these lines is complex and expensive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buckfast Bee">
      <data key="d0">Buckfast Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific hybrid bee line developed by Brother Adam from a cross of British black bees and Italian bees. It is reported to have superior honey production, gentleness, and other favorable traits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Starline Bee">
      <data key="d0">Starline Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific, readily available line of hybrid bees. It is one of the parent lines, along with Midnite, of another hybrid mentioned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Midnite Bee">
      <data key="d0">Midnite Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific, readily available line of hybrid bees. It is one of the parent lines, along with Starline, of another hybrid mentioned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Double Hybrid Bee">
      <data key="d0">Double Hybrid Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific line of hybrid bee mentioned as one of four readily available in the country.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen Bee">
      <data key="d0">Queen Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Queen Bee is the central figure, sole reproductive female, and mother of an entire honey bee colony. Her development is unique, as she is raised from a larva fed an exclusive diet of royal jelly, which shortens her larval stage and results in her being the largest bee in the colony. After emerging, she undertakes orientation and mating flights, mating with multiple drones and storing the sperm for her lifetime.

The Queen Bee's essential roles are to lay eggs and maintain colony morale and cohesion. She accomplishes the latter by releasing a specific pheromone (odor) that signals her presence. Her primary function is reproduction; at her peak, she can lay up to 2,000 eggs per day, laying both fertilized eggs (which become female workers or new queens) and unfertilized eggs (which become male drones). Her laying rate is adjusted seasonally; it is highest in spring and summer, decreases or ceases in late fall and early winter, and then resumes even during cold weather. This productivity is a critical factor in the colony's health, as a high egg-laying rate can help the population grow faster than a mite infestation.

Once her egg-laying is routine, the Queen Bee is not the focus of daily colony life unless a problem occurs. Her status is also a critical factor when starting a new colony. In a package of bees, she is a young, introduced stranger kept in a small cage to allow the bees to acclimate to her scent, and her successful acceptance is vital for the new colony's survival. In a nucleus hive, however, she is already an established and accepted member, which provides a significant advantage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Worker Bee">
      <data key="d0">Worker Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Worker Bee is a caste of sterile, female honey bees that are the smallest and most numerous members of a colony, constituting the majority of its population and serving as its primary life force. Developing from a fertilized egg over a 21-day period, a Worker Bee is fed royal jelly for only the first three days of its larval stage. Its adult life in the summer lasts for approximately 4-5 weeks and is divided into two main phases: for the first 2-3 weeks, it is an indoor "house bee," and for the remaining 2 weeks, it becomes an outdoor "field bee."

Worker Bees perform all non-reproductive tasks essential for the colony's survival, with their specific duties determined by their age, physical capabilities, and the colony's needs. These responsibilities include cleaning the hive, nursing the brood, building honeycomb, guarding the colony, and foraging for resources. The amount of work a Worker Bee performs directly influences its lifespan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drone Bee">
      <data key="d0">Drone Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Drone Bee is the male bee caste within a honey bee colony. Its sole and critical biological function is to mate with a queen to ensure the colony's reproduction. The Drone Bee is born from an unfertilized egg, a process known as parthenogenesis, and has the longest development period among the bees. Physically, it is bulkier than a worker bee but shorter than the queen. Upon maturing, the drone takes orientation flights and then daily mating flights in search of a queen. The Drone Bee does not contribute to any other hive tasks and, unlike worker bees, is not a daily consideration in the hive's functioning. After successfully mating with a queen, the Drone Bee dies. If a drone fails to mate by the end of the active season, it is expelled from the hive before winter to conserve resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Royal Jelly">
      <data key="d0">Royal Jelly</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Royal Jelly, also known as "bee milk," is a protein-rich, glandular secretion produced from the hypopharyngeal glands in the heads of nurse worker bees. It serves as a crucial food substance for bee larvae (brood). While all larvae are fed royal jelly for the first few days of their lives, a larva that is fed an exclusive diet of royal jelly for its entire larval life will develop into a queen bee instead of a worker. The queen bee continues to be fed royal jelly throughout her life.

Alongside other beehive products like beeswax, pollen, propolis, and venom, Royal Jelly is collected and sold to humans as a health supplement. This use is based on the belief that the unique benefits it provides to the queen bee will transfer to people; however, this claim lacks significant scientific evidence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Starline-Midnite Hybrid">
      <data key="d0">Starline-Midnite Hybrid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific hybrid bee line resulting from the cross of the Starline and Midnite lines, intended to combine the better characteristics of both.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Country">
      <data key="d0">The Country</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">An unspecified country where the Italian bee is the most common race and where four specific hybrid bee lines (Midnite, Starline, Double Hybrid, and Buckfast) are readily available.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Artificial Insemination">
      <data key="d0">Artificial Insemination</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An expensive breeding procedure involving time and money, used by breeders to maintain the genetic stock of hybrid bee lines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hybrid Queen">
      <data key="d0">Hybrid Queen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A queen bee belonging to a hybrid line. The text recommends that colonies headed by a hybrid queen should be requeened every 2-3 years to prevent the loss of the line through crossbreeding with common drones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertile Eggs">
      <data key="d0">Fertile Eggs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Eggs laid by a queen bee that are capable of developing into female bees, either workers or new queens, which form the life force of the colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Larva">
      <data key="d0">Bee Larva</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The developmental stage of a bee after hatching from an egg. The diet of a larva, specifically whether it is fed royal jelly exclusively, determines if it will become a queen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Pupa">
      <data key="d0">Bee Pupa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The developmental stage of a bee following the larval stage, where it is encased in a cocoon and transforms into an adult.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Bee Colony">
      <data key="d0">Honey Bee Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Honey Bee Colony is a highly organized social structure consisting of three bee castes: a single queen, numerous drones during the active season, and thousands of workers. The colony's functions include growth, foraging, and reproduction, with cohesion and morale maintained by the queen's presence.&lt;SEP&gt;A Honey Bee Colony is a social unit of bees that functions as a single organism. It overwinters, rears brood, collects nectar and pollen, and reproduces through swarming. The colony has the potential for immortality, though individual colonies can perish.&lt;SEP&gt;A complex social unit of bees, described as having a collective life force. Its activities, such as brood rearing, foraging, and honey production, are carried out by its members, primarily the worker bees, based on stimuli and overall needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parthenogenesis">
      <data key="d0">Parthenogenesis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Parthenogenesis, or 'virgin birth', is the biological process by which unfertilized eggs develop. In honey bees, this is how drones are produced, meaning they have a grandfather but no father.&lt;SEP&gt;Parthenogenesis, or virgin birth, is a reproductive event in which male honey bees develop from unfertilized eggs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egg">
      <data key="d0">Egg</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Egg is the first stage of life for all honey bees. It is laid by the queen in a cell and hatches after 3 days. The queen determines whether the egg is fertilized (to become a female) or unfertilized (to become a drone).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Larva">
      <data key="d0">Larva</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Larva is the second stage of honey bee development, hatching from an egg. It is a grub-like stage focused on growth, where it is fed continuously by nurse bees. The type of food it receives (royal jelly vs. brood food) determines its future caste.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pupa">
      <data key="d0">Pupa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pupa is the third, non-feeding stage of honey bee development, occurring after the larva spins a cocoon. During this stage, the bee undergoes metamorphosis, maturing into its adult form. The duration of the pupal stage varies by caste.&lt;SEP&gt;The third stage in the developmental cycle of a bee brood. Following the pupal stage, the bee emerges as a fully formed adult.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mating Flights">
      <data key="d0">Mating Flights</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An essential event where a new queen flies out of the hive to mate with multiple drones. This event is the sole purpose of a drone's life and results in his death upon successful mating.&lt;SEP&gt;Mating Flights are a crucial event in a young queen's life, where she leaves the hive for 1-2 days to mate with a dozen or so drones high in the air. This event provides her with a lifetime supply of sperm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Active Season">
      <data key="d0">Active Season</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Active Season is the period of the year when a honey bee colony is actively growing, plants are blooming, and bees are foraging and mating. Drones are typically only present during this season.&lt;SEP&gt;The period of the year when the bee colony is actively growing, reproducing, and foraging. Drones are typically only tolerated during this time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spermatheca">
      <data key="d0">Spermatheca</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An organ found within a queen bee that serves to store millions of sperm collected from drones during her mating flights.&lt;SEP&gt;The Spermatheca is a specialized organ in a queen bee's body. It is used to store the sperm she collects from multiple drones during her mating flights, allowing her to fertilize eggs throughout her life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brood Food">
      <data key="d0">Brood Food</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brood Food is a mixture of glandular secretions, honey, and pollen fed to worker and drone larvae after their first three days of life. This diet differs from the pure royal jelly diet given to queen larvae.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="House Bee">
      <data key="d0">House Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A House Bee is a young, adult worker honey bee that represents the first phase of its life, a period which typically lasts for two to three weeks. During this time, the House Bee performs a variety of essential duties within the hive. Its tasks often progress from the center of the brood nest outwards and include cleaning cells, acting as a "nurse bee" by feeding larvae and rearing brood, attending to the queen, secreting wax, and building comb. Additionally, the House Bee is responsible for processing nectar brought into the hive by foragers and converting it into honey. It communicates the quality of the incoming nectar to foragers by the speed at which it accepts the delivery. Towards the end of this stage, a House Bee may also guard the hive's entrance. The bee can mature more quickly if needed to fill workforce gaps within the colony, and it eventually graduates to become a field bee.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Field Bee">
      <data key="d0">Field Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Field Bee is an older worker honey bee in the second and final phase of its adult life, a stage that follows its time as a house bee. This period typically lasts for about two weeks, during which the bee's duties are performed outside the hive. The primary role of a Field Bee is to forage for resources vital to the colony, such as nectar, pollen, water, and propolis. Due to its dedication to a single flower species per trip, the Field Bee is also an effective pollinator. This strenuous foraging labor wears out the bee's body, leading to a shorter lifespan, especially in the summer. The life of a Field Bee ends when its wings become too worn and tattered to fly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cocoon">
      <data key="d0">Cocoon</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Cocoon is a protective casing spun by a honey bee larva at the end of its larval stage. The developing bee then enters the pupal stage within this cocoon to mature into an adult.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Orientation Flights">
      <data key="d0">Orientation Flights</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Periodic flights taken by young house bees from the hive entrance. These flights have a dual purpose: to help the bee memorize the hive's location for future foraging and to exercise and strengthen its wing muscles.&lt;SEP&gt;Preparatory flights taken by young queens and drones to learn the geography around their hive. These flights are necessary for the success of subsequent mating flights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brood">
      <data key="d0">Brood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brood is a collective term for the developing stages of honey bees within a hive, encompassing the eggs, larvae, and pupae, which represent the colony's next generation. These immature bees develop within the wax cells of the hive, with development time and conditions varying for the three castes: queen, worker, and drone. It typically takes three weeks for an egg to develop into an adult bee, which is why a new colony's population may initially decline.

The presence and health of the brood are critical indicators of a colony's overall condition. A healthy brood pattern signifies that the queen is active and laying, and the colony is functioning and growing. Consequently, finding brood is a primary goal during hive inspections. The amount of brood dictates the need for nurse bees and influences the development of younger bees. The brood communicates its needs, such as feeding or requiring cell capping, to worker bees by emitting specific pheromones. The diet for the larval stage consists of royal jelly, honey, and pollen, and a lack of pollen can cause brood rearing to cease.

Brood plays a significant role in colony management and survival. During winter, the brood must be covered by the winter cluster to prevent it from chilling and dying. A late-season brood boost, resulting from fall requeening, provides young bees essential for the colony's winter survival. Additionally, a frame of capped brood can be used to strengthen a weaker colony. The presence of brood is also why nurse bees will not abandon a super, which can cause bee escape devices to fail. However, the brood is vulnerable and is the exclusive target of diseases like American Foulbrood (AFB), European Foulbrood (EFB), Sacbrood, and Chalkbrood, which can infect and kill the developing bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nurse Bees">
      <data key="d0">Nurse Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A role taken by young worker bees who are responsible for producing royal jelly from glands in their heads and feeding all larvae in the colony.&lt;SEP&gt;A role typically held by young bees that feed the brood. Their population can be decimated by insecticide-laced pollen, and older bees can regenerate the ability to become nurse bees in response.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific type of bee whose role is to care for the brood. They will not abandon the brood, which can prevent the effective use of a Porter bee escape if brood is present in the honey super.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ordinary Brood Food">
      <data key="d0">Ordinary Brood Food</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A food mixture of glandular secretions, honey, and pollen fed to worker larvae after their first three days of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nectar">
      <data key="d0">Nectar</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nectar is a sugar-rich, sweet liquid produced and secreted by plants and flowers. It is collected by foraging honey bees, also known as field bees, and serves as the primary raw material that is processed into honey. For the bee colony, Nectar is a key resource and the main source of carbohydrate energy, with its quality, based on sugar content and volume, determining its economic value. It also contains small amounts of vitamins and minerals. After collection, Nectar is brought back to the hive, where house bees convert it into honey by reducing its moisture content and inverting its sugars; this process ceases during the winter. The resulting honey serves as the colony's food supply. Bees that are alighting heavily at the hive are likely carrying a full load of Nectar.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Larval Period">
      <data key="d0">Larval Period</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The second stage of a bee's development, following the egg. During this period, the larva is fed continuously, and its diet determines its future caste (worker or queen).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pupal Stage">
      <data key="d0">Pupal Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The third stage of a bee's development, following the larval period. The bee spins a cocoon and quietly matures for 12 days before emerging as an adult.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Foraging">
      <data key="d0">Foraging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The practice of searching for and gathering wild food resources. The text describes it as a largely forgotten art that ForageSF is working to revive as both a commercial enterprise and a backyard activity through education and community support programs."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Foraging is the activity of gathering wild food. The text describes it as a way to connect with a 'forgotten food system,' reduce food miles, and build a local food economy.&lt;SEP&gt;Foraging is the essential activity where older worker bees, known as field bees, leave the hive to collect resources from the environment. This is a highly coordinated, efficient, and non-random process, guided by communication within the hive to target the most rewarding sources. The resources gathered include natural foods like nectar and pollen, as well as water and propolis, all of which are crucial for the colony's survival. To aid in effective pollination, bees specialize in collecting one type of material per trip. Foraging is a seasonal activity; it generally ceases during the winter, though some activity may be seen on warm, late-winter days. However, it does not become a sustainable food source for the colony until the first major nectar flow begins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6db795582f52e35a0bb9aea86e66f7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dbce12e45fe13ef4f6973f92e57e779</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emergence">
      <data key="d0">Emergence</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Emergence is a biological event defined as the birth of an adult bee, when it completes its pupal development and leaves its cell.&lt;SEP&gt;The event where a fully developed adult bee hatches from its pupal cell, marking the transition from the brood stage to adult life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brood Nest">
      <data key="d0">Brood Nest</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Brood Nest is the central and specific area within a hive where the queen bee lays her eggs and the young bees, known as brood (comprising eggs, larvae, and pupae), are raised in waxen cells. It is also the area where a newly emerged queen will wander. The condition, size, and pattern of the Brood Nest are primary indicators of the colony's health and the queen's performance, with a minimum of six to eight frames and a tight pattern being good signs. An expanding Brood Nest signifies a healthy and growing colony population, and its growth is monitored during inspections.

To ensure proper development, the bees must maintain the Brood Nest at a constant temperature of approximately 94°F (34°C). Beekeepers are advised to place supplemental food near the Brood Nest and to avoid making massive disruptions or splitting it, particularly during cool weather. Unseasonably warm weather can also pose a risk by causing the bees to expand the Brood Nest prematurely, leaving it vulnerable when cold weather returns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Behavior">
      <data key="d0">Bee Behavior</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The collection of activities and reactions of honey bees. The text explains it as being driven by internal physiological development and external stimuli, resulting in instinctual, patterned actions rather than conscious thought or emotion."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physiological Development">
      <data key="d0">Physiological Development</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The internal maturation of a bee's organs and systems, such as venom glands and muscles. This process, occurring over the first half of a bee's life, is a primary factor governing its abilities and behaviors, like stinging and flight."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Factors">
      <data key="d0">External Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Environmental stimuli that play a significant role in influencing bee behavior. The text lists these as odor, light, heat, cold, and vibration (sound), the presence or absence of which can control bee activities."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anthropomorphism">
      <data key="d0">Anthropomorphism</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The attribution of human traits, characteristics, or emotions to nonhumans. The text discusses it as a convenient but potentially inaccurate and misleading method for interpreting and discussing honey bee behavior."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Learning">
      <data key="d0">Bee Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The ability of bees to modify their behavior based on experience, which is distinct from intelligence. Examples include associating odors with food, recognizing feeding times, and memorizing terrain around the hive."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Individual Bee">
      <data key="d0">Individual Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Represents a single honey bee. Its behavior and reactions are dictated by its specific age, physiological development (e.g., maturation of venom glands), and immediate external stimuli, rather than conscious thought."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An external stimulus, specifically a tool used by beekeepers. Its application induces a predictable, patterned response in bees, causing them to engorge on honey and become less defensive."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A substance used by beekeepers to calm bees during an inspection. The text warns that even in winter, bees from the warm cluster can fly and sting, making the use of smoke advisable.&lt;SEP&gt;A tool used in beekeeping to calm bees, typically generated by a bee smoker. It is mentioned as being used when opening a nucleus hive for transfer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Composition">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Composition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An internal factor that influences the inherent tendencies of a bee colony, such as its level of aggression or resistance to diseases. It is a key part of the bees' biological makeup."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Venom Glands">
      <data key="d0">Venom Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The specific organs in a bee responsible for producing venom. Their functionality is directly tied to a bee's age and physiological maturation, becoming fully functional about two weeks into its life."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pheromones">
      <data key="d0">Pheromones</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pheromones are chemical substances, signals, and odors released by adult honey bees and their brood, which serve as a primary governing force and form of communication within the colony. Functioning as both odors and tastes, these chemical cues act as stimuli that trigger specific behavioral responses and regulate the physiological development of individual bees without any conscious thought process. Pheromones govern many aspects of colony life by communicating its status and needs, including information about queen recognition, brood care, homing, forage locations, and danger through alarm odors. Additionally, they influence bee interactions and can affect how bees react to other animals, including people.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intelligence">
      <data key="d0">Intelligence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Defined in the text as the ability to reason and think abstractly. The document explicitly states that bees do not possess intelligence in this sense, though they are capable of learning."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skunk">
      <data key="d0">Skunk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An example of an external source of harassment or threat to a bee colony. Constant disruption by a skunk can temporarily lower the colony's defensive threshold, making it appear 'mean'."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Brood Nest">
      <data key="d0">The Brood Nest</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The central, dark, and warm area within the hive where worker bees are born. Young bees perform their first duties in this location before moving on to other tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Hive">
      <data key="d0">The Hive</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The central structure housing a bee colony. Its weight is an indicator of its contents and health. It is composed of hive bodies and frames, and its inspection is a core beekeeping activity to monitor growth and production."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The physical structure where the bee colony lives. It is the location for all house bee activities and is the home base from which field bees conduct their foraging trips.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mating">
      <data key="d0">Mating</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical reproductive event in a honey bee colony involving the queen and drone bees. After mating is complete, the queen's egg laying becomes a routine activity for the colony's continuation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egg Laying">
      <data key="d0">Egg Laying</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A fundamental, routine activity performed by the queen bee after she has mated. This process is essential for the growth and sustenance of the bee colony's population.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary function of the queen bee, who lays all the eggs for the colony. The rate and pattern of egg laying are key indicators of colony health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brood Rearing">
      <data key="d0">Brood Rearing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brood Rearing is the primary and crucial process within a honey bee colony of raising new bees from eggs through the larval and pupal stages to adulthood. The process is initiated by the queen bee, who lays the eggs, while worker bees, particularly house bees, are responsible for the care and feeding of the young, known as the brood.

This activity is essential for the colony's population growth but is highly dependent on resources. It consumes a significant amount of pollen and nectar, and a lack of a continuous food supply will cause the bees to reduce the activity. Additionally, Brood Rearing requires the brood nest to be maintained at a constant temperature of approximately 94°F (34°C).

Brood Rearing is a seasonal task. While it is significantly reduced during the winter, it resumes in late winter, sometimes referred to as the "depths of winter," even during cold weather. This early start prepares the colony for a significant increase in population and activity during the spring, which in turn leads to a higher rate of food consumption.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guarding">
      <data key="d0">Guarding</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A defensive task performed by worker bees as they mature and move towards the hive entrance. It is one of the final duties of a house bee before it transitions to becoming a field bee.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feeding Brood">
      <data key="d0">Feeding Brood</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific task carried out by young worker bees, whose hypopharyngeal and mandibular glands secrete brood food for a limited period of 1 to 2 weeks early in their lives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secreting Wax">
      <data key="d0">Secreting Wax</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A construction-related task performed by house bees. The ability to secrete wax is dependent on the maturity of the bee's glands and is necessary for building and capping cells in the hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Receiving and Processing Nectar">
      <data key="d0">Receiving and Processing Nectar</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A task performed by house bees as part of the honey production process. This activity is seasonal and involves taking nectar from returning foragers and preparing it for storage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Hive Entrance">
      <data key="d0">The Hive Entrance</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific location and transition point in the hive between the internal and external environment. House bees gradually move toward it and use it as a launching point for orientation flights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Colony">
      <data key="d0">The Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A social unit of honey bees whose collective needs dictate the roles, development, and activities of individual bees. The colony's status is signaled by pheromones, and it experiences an annual population cycle. Its needs can cause bees to mature at different rates to maintain balance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annual Population Cycle">
      <data key="d0">Annual Population Cycle</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Annual Population Cycle describes the yearly fluctuation in a honey bee colony's population. It typically reaches its lowest point in mid-March and its peak around the summer solstice, driven by seasonal changes, forage availability, and brood rearing rates.&lt;SEP&gt;The yearly fluctuation in a honey bee colony's population, which is low in the winter and high during active seasons. This cycle is influenced by season, climate, and forage availability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insecticide">
      <data key="d0">Insecticide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A chemical substance described as a cause of massive colony disruption. It can kill the foraging force directly or be carried in pollen, killing young nurse bees inside the hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Winter Months">
      <data key="d0">Winter Months</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A seasonal period of relative inactivity for the colony in temperate climates. During this time, foraging, nectar processing, comb building, and brood rearing are drastically reduced or stopped.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Needs of the Colony">
      <data key="d0">Needs of the Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The collective requirements of the hive, such as the need for food or brood care. This is a central driving force that dictates the roles, developmental speed, and labor of individual bees to ensure colony balance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colony Disruption">
      <data key="d0">Colony Disruption</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant negative event, such as an insecticide wipeout, that upsets the balance of the colony. It triggers rapid maturation and role changes in surviving bees to compensate for the losses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comb Building">
      <data key="d0">Comb Building</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A seasonal task performed within the hive, which involves creating wax structures. This activity is mentioned as not being done during the winter months.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee's Year">
      <data key="d0">Bee's Year</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of the honey bee colony's natural, continuous, year-round activity cycle, which is governed by seasons and climate rather than human intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeper's Year">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeper's Year</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of a beekeeper's schedule of managing a hive, which is based on human convenience and observation, and is distinct from the bee's own natural annual cycle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temperate Climate">
      <data key="d0">Temperate Climate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A climate type that is presented as a governing factor for the seasonal activities and the annual population cycle of a honey bee colony, with North America used as an example.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summer Solstice">
      <data key="d0">Summer Solstice</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Summer Solstice is a key astronomical event that signals a behavioral shift in a honey bee colony. After the solstice, the shortening days prompt the bees to transition from population expansion to preparing for winter by building up honey stores.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Cluster">
      <data key="d0">Bee Cluster</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tight formation of bees, primarily occurring during winter and cool periods, to generate and conserve heat. This behavior allows normal colony life to continue inside the cluster and is essential for keeping the brood nest warm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parent Colony">
      <data key="d0">Parent Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Parent Colony is the original bee colony from which a swarm departs. During swarming, it loses about half its population and its laying queen, weakening it significantly. The remaining bees raise a new queen to ensure the colony's survival."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An established bee colony, typically in a southern beeyard, from which bees are taken to create a package. The bees in a package are separated from their parent colony and original queen.&lt;SEP&gt;The original, established colony that remains after a swarm has departed. It contains the remaining bees and new brood and is where a new queen will emerge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mid-March Low Point">
      <data key="d0">Mid-March Low Point</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The time of year identified as the arbitrary start of a new cycle for an established honey bee colony. It marks the population's lowest point before the rate of new bees emerging outpaces the winter die-off.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seasons">
      <data key="d0">Seasons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The distinct periods of the year (Winter, Spring, Summer, Autumn) that provide critical environmental signals, such as changing day length and temperature, which dictate the colony's activities, including foraging, brood rearing, and clustering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swarm Control">
      <data key="d0">Swarm Control</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Swarm Control refers to the specific hive management techniques, methods, and manipulations used by beekeepers to prevent or manage the natural impulse of a honeybee colony to swarm. This practice is a key and important aspect of beekeeping management, although it is often misunderstood and can sometimes be unsuccessful. While swarming is a natural reproductive process, it results in the loss of a large portion of the bees, which beekeepers seek to manage. Effective swarm control requires a thorough understanding of bee behavior and the swarming process, and successful techniques must account for the underlying swarm impulse and the colony's overall needs, rather than just suppressing symptoms. It is also highlighted as a new challenge during "The New Season".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen">
      <data key="d0">Queen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Queen is the single, central, and sole reproductive female in a honey bee colony, making her the most important bee and absolutely essential for the colony's growth, survival, population, and overall function. Distinguished by her longer abdomen, the Queen is responsible for laying all the eggs in the hive. She contains a spermatheca, an organ used to store sperm from her mating flights.

The Queen's performance and the colony's health are directly indicated by the quality and pattern of her brood (eggs and larvae). Beekeepers often assess her work during inspections rather than trying to locate her directly. If a queen is underperforming, which can be seen in a poor brood pattern or unfavorable colony disposition, beekeepers may replace her in a process called "requeening." This is a key management task, often done in the fall, to boost the population for winter and prevent future swarming.

The Queen is a central figure in swarming. The development of a new queen is a precursor to a swarm, and the age of the existing queen can be a factor. Before leaving, the old queen stops laying eggs and worker bees reduce her food intake to make her lose weight for flight. She then leaves with the swarm to establish a new colony, while the parent colony rears a new queen to replace her. While a beekeeper can use a queen excluder to control her movement, confining the queen is considered an inappropriate swarm control method.

When a new colony is started from a purchased "package of bees," the Queen is included but housed separately in a small queen cage, often with a candy plug to facilitate a slow release. Worker bees attend to her and cluster around this cage. Her survival, successful introduction, and acceptance by the other bees are critical for the new colony's success, and beekeepers must verify that she has been released from her cage and has begun laying eggs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Swarm Season">
      <data key="d0">Primary Swarm Season</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Primary Swarm Season is the main period when swarming occurs, typically in late spring and early summer, lasting about 6 weeks. It accounts for approximately 80% of all swarms in a year and its timing varies by geographical location."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Swarm Season">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Swarm Season</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Secondary Swarm Season is a less common period for swarming that occurs for about a month starting in August. It accounts for the remaining 20% of swarms, which have a much lower chance of long-term survival."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The South">
      <data key="d0">The South</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical region of the United States where Sally Eason's father established the 'first commercial trout farm' in 1948.&lt;SEP&gt;The South is a region of the United States characterized by a warm, humid climate, which fosters a flourishing environment for worm parasites that affect sheep and goats. The Primary Swarm Season begins earlier in the South compared to other areas. Specific ant species pose a serious threat to honey bee colonies in the South, even causing their demise. Cowpeas are a better cover crop choice in the South, illustrating the site-specific nature of farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The North">
      <data key="d0">The North</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The North is a geographical region where the Primary Swarm Season begins later compared to other areas."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Workers">
      <data key="d0">Workers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Workers are a caste of bees within a colony responsible for key activities related to swarming. They regulate the queen's egg-laying by reducing her feeding, harass her to reduce her weight for flight, and build queen cells to raise a new queen for the parent colony."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The main population of bees in a colony responsible for its functioning. A three-pound package contains about 10,000 workers.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary caste of sterile female bees in a honey bee colony responsible for all labor, including foraging, nursing, and hive construction. Their development and roles are detailed in the index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066653911102a71446dc7a3246a0c193&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Colony">
      <data key="d0">New Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A New Colony is formed when a swarm, consisting of about half the bees from a parent colony and the old queen, leaves to find and establish a new home. This is the primary method of species propagation for bees."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A new bee colony established from a package of bees. Its first month is a critical period for survival, characterized by an initial population decline due to the natural death of the original bees before new ones hatch. The colony's success heavily depends on the beekeeper's management, particularly ensuring the queen is accepted and begins laying eggs. It starts with no comb and must build it using a steady supply of nectar or syrup.&lt;SEP&gt;A new colony refers to a recently established bee colony, typically in its first year. These colonies are characterized by having a smaller population of bees, which makes them generally unsuitable for producing a large surplus honey crop, especially comb honey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nectar Flow">
      <data key="d0">Nectar Flow</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Nectar Flow is a crucial seasonal period when abundant nectar is available from flowering plants, prompting bees to actively forage, produce wax, and store honey. During a strong nectar flow, bees are often more docile and preoccupied with foraging, making it an ideal time for beekeepers to work with the hive. The timing and duration of this event vary significantly by geographic location and are critical for beekeeping management.

The Nectar Flow dictates key activities, such as when to add honey supers for collection and how to manage feeding, with some beekeepers using syrup to simulate its effects. This environmental condition, along with colony population and queen age, can also serve as a signal to trigger swarming. The end of the nectar flow is an equally important marker; it naturally causes the queen to reduce her egg-laying rate and signals to beekeepers that it is time to perform fall management tasks like requeening and evaluating food stores for winter. Conversely, the absence of a nectar flow can lead to empty supers, which then become vulnerable to wax moth infestations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swarming Impulse">
      <data key="d0">Swarming Impulse</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Swarming Impulse is described as a compelling, instinctual force that takes over a bee colony several days before the actual swarm. This impulse must be satisfied by the colony dividing for it to be resolved."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen Cells">
      <data key="d0">Queen Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Queen Cells are special structures built by worker bees to raise new queens. The departure of the swarm from the parent colony is timed to coincide with the capping of the first of these cells."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Beekeepers">
      <data key="d0">Local Beekeepers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Local Beekeepers are an experienced group of people who can provide valuable, location-specific information about the timing of the primary swarm season in a particular area."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swarm Impulse">
      <data key="d0">Swarm Impulse</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The swarm impulse is described as a compelling, instinctual force that takes over a bee colony, leading it to swarm. Effective swarm control measures must address and satisfy this impulse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Novice Beekeeper">
      <data key="d0">Novice Beekeeper</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A novice beekeeper is someone new to beekeeping. The text advises them not to be overly concerned with swarming in their first season but to prioritize reading and learning about bee biology and swarm control for the future.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key biological factor in livestock management. The text emphasizes providing a high level of nutrition from sources like legumes and quality grass to support pregnancy, lactation, and ovulation in animals like sheep and hogs."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition is a critical aspect of bee health, encompassing the food sources bees need to survive and thrive. It dictates colony activities, brood rearing, and the ability to build up reserves for winter or periods of scarcity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Winter">
      <data key="d0">Winter</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">For agricultural operations, Winter is a key and challenging season that dictates the entire operational schedule. It requires specific strategies for managing livestock, equipment, and honey bee colonies. A common farming strategy is to avoid major labor and feeding expenses during these cold months, while still generating some income through value-added sales. For livestock, Winter presents challenges as natural forage declines, necessitating strategies such as providing shelter from wind, ensuring access to unfrozen water, and supplementing feed. It is also a period of storage or low activity for farm equipment, which must be either thoroughly winterized or run periodically to keep internal components lubricated.

Winter is an especially critical, challenging, and often dangerous season for a honey bee colony, posing a significant survival challenge. Preparations begin in preceding seasons, as the colony must build enough comb and store sufficient honey. Just before Winter, bees seal any cracks in the hive with propolis to protect it from the elements, and hive placement should consider factors like wind and snow depth. During Winter, the bees are confined to the hive and do not forage. They form a tight cluster for warmth, which starts at the bottom of the hive and moves upward as they consume their stored honey. The queen's egg-laying pauses during this time but resumes in the later stages of Winter.

The bee colony's survival is highly dependent on its food stores, and beekeepers may need to provide supplementary food like candy or sugar to prevent starvation. The season is particularly perilous because the bee population is at its lowest, making a weakened colony unlikely to survive. Furthermore, Winter is the most dangerous period for colonies infested with tracheal mites, as the mite population peaks when the bee population is at its minimum, leading to severe losses. The consistency of the weather during this time also significantly impacts the colony's resource consumption and overall chance of survival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swarm Season">
      <data key="d0">Swarm Season</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The period when swarming is most likely to occur. The text advises that the best swarm control measures begin well in advance of this season.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific time of year, from spring to early summer, when honey bee colonies are most likely to reproduce by swarming, making it the primary window of opportunity for a beekeeper to capture a swarm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nectar Flows">
      <data key="d0">Nectar Flows</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An environmental condition where there is a strong and abundant availability of nectar from flowers. Strong nectar flows are a factor that encourages swarming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Biology">
      <data key="d0">Bee Biology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A field of scientific study. The text notes that great increases in the knowledge of bee biology have led to more effective bee management techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;The scientific study of bees, their life cycle, and behaviors. The text recommends that novice beekeepers study this to correctly understand swarm control and prevention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Worker Bees">
      <data key="d0">Worker Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A caste of bees within the colony responsible for various tasks, including secreting royal jelly (bee milk) from glands in their heads to feed the brood.&lt;SEP&gt;The attendants to the queen found inside the queen cage. They groom and feed the queen. The text also refers to the larger population of bees in the package that will form the new colony.&lt;SEP&gt;The general population of bees within the package. They have an average lifespan of 4-5 weeks. In a new colony, these original bees die off from natural causes before new bees hatch, leading to a temporary population decline. They are responsible for building comb, foraging, and caring for the brood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flowers">
      <data key="d0">Flowers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Flowers are the primary source of nectar for bees. Their value to the colony is determined by factors like sugar content, volume of nectar, and concentration in a specific area."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The source of both nectar and pollen for honey bees. Not all flowers are suitable, as some do not produce nectar or have insufficient sugar concentration.&lt;SEP&gt;A product category Greensgrow strategically began to grow. This decision was a breakthrough that helped the farm gain the attention, respect, and patronage of serious Philadelphia gardeners and influential horticulture clubs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e143d750d8e9b45fec3f4d1a9ec2a04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adult Bees">
      <data key="d0">Adult Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The mature stage of honey bees. Removing adult bees is mentioned as a swarm control technique. Their diet changes as they age, with pollen consumption diminishing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamins">
      <data key="d0">Vitamins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamins are essential nutrients required for the growth and development of organisms like bees. The text highlights that pollen has an exceptionally high vitamin content.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minerals">
      <data key="d0">Minerals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Minerals are elements collected by bees through nectar, pollen, and water. They are essential for various biological functions, but deficiencies or excesses can be harmful to bees.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrients present in the soil that are essential for plant growth. They can only be absorbed by plants when they are in a solution, making water a critical component for nutrient uptake.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fats">
      <data key="d0">Fats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fats are a nutrient category available to bees from pollen. Bees also have fat bodies that store food reserves, which are developed primarily from sugar.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sugar">
      <data key="d0">Sugar</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A raw material used by beekeepers to create supplementary feed for bees. It is the primary ingredient in both the sugar syrup and candy recipes described in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Sugar, specifically pure granulated sugar (sucrose) and high-fructose corn syrup, is a substance fed to bees by beekeepers as a food source. The text warns that impure forms of sugar can be harmful or fatal to bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollen Substitute">
      <data key="d0">Pollen Substitute</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Pollen Substitute is a nutritional supplement for bees, used when natural pollen is scarce. It can be mixed with sugar syrup to form patties and placed inside the hive near the brood nest, or fed dry outside the hive in the spring."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Pollen substitute is a manufactured material containing components like soy flour and brewers yeast. It is fed to bees to provide protein when natural pollen is scarce.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollen Supplement">
      <data key="d0">Pollen Supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pollen supplement is a mixture created by combining pure, bee-collected pollen with a pollen substitute. It is used as a feed to enhance the diet of honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Country">
      <data key="d0">Country</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to a geographical region where pollen shortages or deficiencies can occur, affecting the natural diet of local bee populations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dance Language">
      <data key="d0">Dance Language</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dance language is a complex form of communication used by honey bees to convey specific information to other members of the colony, such as the location of nectar, pollen, or water sources.&lt;SEP&gt;The sophisticated communication system of bees, consisting of various dances, used to convey complex information about food sources, dangers, and other colony needs. It is vital for the colony's efficiency and survival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flower">
      <data key="d0">Flower</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The reproductive structure of a plant from which honey bees collect pollen (the male reproductive part) and nectar. Bees may collect both or just one from a given flower species.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollen Baskets">
      <data key="d0">Pollen Baskets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Specialized structures on the hind legs of honey bees used to transport consolidated pollen pellets back to the hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Water">
      <data key="d0">Water</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Water is a vital and essential resource for all farm life, including plants, livestock, and insects like honey bees. It is crucial for plant growth, enabling key functions such as photosynthesis, nutrient absorption, and development. Water fills the pore spaces in soil, where it is absorbed by plant roots, and the soil's capacity to hold water is a key aspect of its health and structure. The availability of water determines the types of plants that can be cultivated, as different crops have vastly different requirements, and the timing of its delivery, such as through rainfall, is critical.

For animals, water is essential for the survival of livestock. Honey bees also collect water, along with nectar and pollen, as a source of minerals and for various other needs within the hive; they can communicate the location of water sources to other bees using their dance language.

As a fundamental resource for farming, water must be evaluated, managed, and conserved, with important considerations for its quality, quantity, and associated rights. Its sources include rivers, ponds, and wells, in addition to being freely provided by rain and snow. Beyond its life-sustaining properties, water can also serve as a free energy source on a farm, with running water having the potential to be used to generate power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Salt">
      <data key="d0">Salt</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific mineral that is mentioned as being detrimental to honey bees as it can reduce their longevity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fat Bodies">
      <data key="d0">Fat Bodies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Structures within the body cavity of a honey bee that serve as production and storage sites for food reserves. Their contents are primarily developed from sugar and vary with the bee's age and the season.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Granulated Sugar (Sucrose)">
      <data key="d0">Granulated Sugar (Sucrose)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pure form of sugar, the same as table-grade sugar, that is acceptable and safe for feeding to honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Fructose Corn Syrup">
      <data key="d0">High-Fructose Corn Syrup</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of sugar that is acceptable for feeding bees, provided it is of food quality and has no additives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Impure Sugars">
      <data key="d0">Impure Sugars</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Various forms of sugar, such as molasses, candymaking by-products, and surplus soft drink syrup, that are harmful and can potentially kill honey bees if fed to them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollen Traps">
      <data key="d0">Pollen Traps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A device used by beekeepers at the hive entrance to collect pure pollen from bees, which can then be stored and fed back to them later.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollen Substitute Components">
      <data key="d0">Pollen Substitute Components</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of ingredients, such as soy flour, brewers yeast, dry skim milk, and powdered casein, that are used to manufacture pollen substitute for bee feed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Supply Sources">
      <data key="d0">Bee Supply Sources</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Commercial vendors or stores where beekeepers can purchase supplies, including pre-packaged pollen supplements and dry pollen substitutes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swarm">
      <data key="d0">Swarm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A group of bees captured by a beekeeper to start a new colony. While a viable option, their availability is unpredictable and seasonal. A major disadvantage is their unknown origin, which means they may carry diseases or mites."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A group of honey bees that has left the hive and is seeking a new home. Bees use communication, like the dance language, to decide on a location.&lt;SEP&gt;A method of acquiring bees that is not recommended for beginners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Colony">
      <data key="d0">Bee Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Bee Colony is a highly organized social structure of bees living together in a hive. It functions as a single social unit with specialized roles for its members, including a queen, workers, scouts, and guards. The colony's success in activities like defense, reproduction, resource gathering, and foraging is heavily dependent on its complex communication systems, which include chemical signals through pheromones and coordinated behaviors like the dance language.

The health and strength of the Bee Colony are crucial for its survival. A vigorous, healthy colony can effectively police its hive and defend against pests, such as wax moths, while a weak colony is vulnerable to these threats as well as robbery by stronger colonies. The colony's natural behaviors, which are key factors in a beekeeper's management decisions, include expanding upward, producing wax to build comb, and raising brood. A new colony's primary focus is on establishment, population growth, and storing enough resources to survive the upcoming winter.

Survival during winter is a central challenge for the Bee Colony. Its members cluster together for warmth, consume their food stores, and their activity level becomes highly dependent on weather conditions. On warmer days, they may perform cleansing flights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scout Bee">
      <data key="d0">Scout Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Scout Bee is a specialized forager bee whose role is to search the surrounding environment for new, high-quality sources of resources like nectar. Upon finding a valuable source, it returns to the hive and communicates its discovery using the dance language."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guard Bee">
      <data key="d0">Guard Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Guard Bee is a bee positioned at the hive entrance responsible for the colony's defense. It identifies friendly bees from intruders by their specific odor (or pheromones) and repels any threats."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Dance Language">
      <data key="d0">The Dance Language</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Dance Language is a sophisticated form of communication used by bees, consisting of specific movements like the round dance and wag-tail dance. It conveys detailed information about the location, quality, and quantity of resources such as nectar or potential homesites."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Forager Bee">
      <data key="d0">Forager Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Forager Bee, also referred to as a field bee, is a worker bee whose primary role is to travel outside the hive to collect resources such as nectar and pollen from known sources. They communicate with House Bees upon return and can be redirected to better sources via the Dance Language."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homing Odor">
      <data key="d0">Homing Odor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Homing Odor is a specific type of pheromone released during times of confusion, such as when a swarm is moving. It serves as a navigational beacon to help lost bees find their way back to the main colony or to a new homesite."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Processing">
      <data key="d0">Honey Processing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Honey Processing is the activity carried out by House Bees within the hive to convert raw nectar, brought in by foragers, into honey. This process involves chemical changes and dehydration."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Round Dance">
      <data key="d0">Round Dance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Round Dance is one of the two primary communication dances used by bees. It is performed on the comb to signal the availability of a new resource source that is located near the hive."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A type of bee dance used to communicate the location of food sources that are within 100 meters of the hive. It informs other bees about the type of forage but does not provide specific directional information, prompting them to search randomly within that radius.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wag-Tail Dance">
      <data key="d0">Wag-Tail Dance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Wag-Tail Dance is one of the two primary communication dances used by bees. It is a more complex dance used to signal the location, distance, and quality of a resource source that is farther away from the hive."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Potential Homesites">
      <data key="d0">Potential Homesites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Potential Homesites are new locations scouted by bees as possible new hives for a swarm. The location and suitability of these sites are communicated to the rest of the swarm through the Dance Language."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wag-tail Dance">
      <data key="d0">Wag-tail Dance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A complex, figure-eight patterned bee dance used for food sources located more than 100 meters from the hive. It communicates precise information about the distance and direction of the forage relative to the sun's position.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alarm Dance">
      <data key="d0">Alarm Dance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A dance performed in irregular zigzags or spirals to signal that contaminated or poisonous food has been brought into the hive. This dance causes all flight activity to stop as a protective measure.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific communicative behavior or dance performed by honey bees to signal a threat or disturbance to the colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sickle or Crescent Dance">
      <data key="d0">Sickle or Crescent Dance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A recognized bee dance that serves as a transitional form between the round dance and the wag-tail dance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cleaning Dance">
      <data key="d0">Cleaning Dance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bee dance that is a form of solicitation, where the dancing bee requests to be cleaned and groomed by other nearby bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Researchers">
      <data key="d0">Researchers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The scientists involved in the experimental breeding program in Brazil. They recognized the potential dangers of the _Apis mellifera scutellata_ subspecies and attempted to keep them under careful control before an accidental escape occurred."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A group of scientists who studied a bee colony in a deciduous forest for a summer. They observed and interpreted the bees' dances to analyze their foraging behavior over a 36-square mile area.&lt;SEP&gt;Scientists and experts who are studying Africanized bees, discovering and publishing new information about their behavior, history, and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hazards">
      <data key="d0">Hazards</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to dangers that threaten the bee colony, such as pesticides, toxic nectar or pollen, and predators. The dance language system helps protect the colony from these threats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dancer">
      <data key="d0">Dancer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The individual bee that performs a dance to communicate information. The dancer's movements, sounds, and the odors she carries convey specific details about food sources or dangers to her hivemates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Forager">
      <data key="d0">Forager</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A bee whose role is to search for and collect nectar and pollen. Upon finding a resource, a forager returns to the hive and becomes a 'dancer' to communicate its location and quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hivemates">
      <data key="d0">Hivemates</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The other bees within a hive who are the audience for the dancer. They follow the dancer's movements and touch her with their antennae to interpret the information being communicated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="House and Field Bees">
      <data key="d0">House and Field Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Designations for bees within the colony. The text specifies that both types of bees will perform the alarm dance when contaminated food is detected, indicating a colony-wide response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sun">
      <data key="d0">Sun</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A celestial body used as the primary navigational reference for bees. The wag-tail dance's orientation is set at a specific angle relative to the sun's position to indicate the direction of a food source.&lt;SEP&gt;An environmental element affecting a hive's exposure. The text states that bees prefer a lighted entrance but that too much or too little sun is undesirable, suggesting a balance of sun and shade like that found in a hollow tree.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary free energy source for a farm. Its benefits include enabling photosynthesis for crops, generating heat, facilitating water collection, sanitizing soil, and providing vitamin D.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comb">
      <data key="d0">Comb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A comb is a structure made of hexagonal wax cells built by bees within a hive, often on frames or a foundation. Its primary functions are to store food resources, such as honey, nectar, and pollen, and to serve as the brood nest where the queen lays her eggs and the colony raises its young. The comb is also the physical surface where bees perform communicative dances; for instance, a vertical line on the comb is used during the wag-tail dance to symbolically represent the direction of the sun.

The comb is essential for a colony's survival, and beekeepers must ensure enough is drawn out before winter. Its presence and condition are key factors in evaluating starter colonies, as it can transmit diseases and is susceptible to significant damage from pests like wax moths and mice. While a "package of bees" does not come with established comb, the bees may begin building it inside the package. The wax structure is notably tender and fragile, especially when new, requiring careful handling during honey extraction. Some extraction methods, like the crushing method, destroy the comb completely, making its preservation an important consideration for beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deciduous Forest Area">
      <data key="d0">Deciduous Forest Area</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The location where researchers conducted a summer-long study of a bee colony's foraging behavior. The study revealed the bees searched a 36-square mile area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Observation Hive">
      <data key="d0">Observation Hive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized hive setup used by researchers as a tool to watch, interpret, and analyze the dancing and foraging activities of a bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stinging Behavior">
      <data key="d0">Stinging Behavior</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A defensive activity of a bee colony. The text explicitly states that this behavior is likely as important as the dance language for the long-term success and survival of the colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollination Process">
      <data key="d0">Pollination Process</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A biological process enhanced by the presence of bees. The text briefly mentions this as a benefit of beekeeping at the beginning of the chapter.&lt;SEP&gt;The ecological process of transferring pollen. The text links the success of the bee colony, enhanced by the dance language, to the enhancement of this process, which benefits the wider ecosystem and human lifestyles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food">
      <data key="d0">Food</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A central theme of the latter half of the article, which connects the utility of goats for land clearing to their growing economic viability as a food source, specifically goat meat.&lt;SEP&gt;A general term for the resources bees consume, such as nectar and pollen. The quality of food is a key piece of information in bee dances, with contaminated or poisonous food triggering the specific alarm dance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Location">
      <data key="d0">Hive Location</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific site chosen for a beehive. The text details numerous considerations for selection, such as visibility, accessibility for the beekeeper, exposure to elements (wind, sun), and proximity to traffic and animals. A good location is crucial for the hive's success and the beekeeper's convenience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vandalism">
      <data key="d0">Vandalism</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A potential risk to beehives, particularly in remote or unsecured locations. It encompasses acts of destruction like pelting with rocks or gunshot, tipping hives over, or theft of the hive, frames, or the queen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exposure">
      <data key="d0">Exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of environmental conditions affecting a hive, primarily wind, sun, and traffic (vehicular, pedestrian, animal). The text advises managing overexposure through protective measures like windbreaks and careful site selection to avoid air currents and flight path conflicts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Animals">
      <data key="d0">Animals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group that can interact with beehives. The text specifically mentions that horses and bees have difficulty coexisting due to odor, livestock can knock hives over, and skunks and bears pose a special threat.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to natural harassers of bees, such as skunks or bears. Past negative encounters with these animals can make bees more reactive and defensive towards beekeepers wearing attire (e.g., woolly fabrics) that resembles them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neighbors">
      <data key="d0">Neighbors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the provided descriptions, neighbors are defined as the people or owners of adjoining farms and properties who live in proximity to a farmer or beekeeper. Neighbors are presented as a valuable and potential resource, particularly for small farmers, fostering community-based solutions. One can exchange, barter with, rent machinery from, or hire neighbors for custom work, which serves as a practical and cost-effective alternative to purchasing expensive equipment. Maintaining a good relationship with neighbors is crucial. This involves practical considerations such as ensuring good fencing is in place to prevent livestock from crossing property lines. Additionally, a beekeeper must consider the potential apprehensions and comfort of neighbors when choosing a location for a hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d20bcb040d48598530ec2b7c42edba7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b34a5b7dc86e95b65f36512f72980b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Beekeepers">
      <data key="d0">New Beekeepers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beginners in the practice of beekeeping. The text advises them to place hives nearby to facilitate frequent visits and learning, as they need to get together with their new colonies regularly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vandals">
      <data key="d0">Vandals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Individuals who might intentionally damage property. Hives are described as prime targets for vandals, who may pelt them with rocks, tip them over, or otherwise trash them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thieves">
      <data key="d0">Thieves</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Individuals who might steal property. Beehives are noted as being at risk of theft, with thieves potentially taking whole hives or valuable components like frames of brood and the queen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wind">
      <data key="d0">Wind</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural element listed as a potential free energy source that can be harnessed to generate power.&lt;SEP&gt;An environmental element that can be excessive and harmful to a hive. The text advises constructing a windbreak if a hive must be placed in a windy location, with special consideration for winter winds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Snow">
      <data key="d0">Snow</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of winter precipitation. The text notes that snow piled around a hive provides some protection, but a location where the hive could become completely covered should be avoided so bees can exit on warmer days.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traffic">
      <data key="d0">Traffic</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of movement, including vehicular, pedestrian, and animal traffic. Placing a hive near busy traffic can cause the loss of bees due to turbulent air and create potential stinging incidents if the flight path is obstructed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Horses">
      <data key="d0">Horses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Horses are a type of livestock for which specific fencing, such as wood or vinyl, is often used on horse farms."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A specific type of animal that has difficulty coexisting with bees. The text states that the odor of horses seems to offend bees and can trigger stinging incidents, and horses react violently to stings.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of livestock that is mentioned as requiring a source of freestanding water to drink during the winter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Livestock">
      <data key="d0">Livestock</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A general category of farm animals, with the text specifically focusing on the challenges and benefits of raising them, like goats, in urban or city environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Livestock is an agricultural category discussed in the text, with specific references to beef and The Goat Justice League.&lt;SEP&gt;Livestock refers to a diverse category of domesticated farm animals raised in an agricultural setting for profit and integrated farm benefits. This category includes, but is not limited to, cattle (Santa Gertrudis, Irish Dexter), sheep, goats, hogs, turkeys, poultry, rabbits, alpacas, bison, quail, deer, game birds, and chicken pullets.

Livestock are essential for a balanced rotation, contributing to soil fertility by converting roughage into salable products and spreading manure, distributing labor throughout the year, and providing economic stability. Integrating livestock with crops, such as using hogs in cornfields or grazing hay strips in a strip-cropping system, enhances farm diversity and productivity. The animals' nutritional requirements, particularly during pregnancy and lactation, necessitate careful dietary management with high-quality forage to improve reproduction.

Raising livestock involves significant considerations, including costs of production (feed, veterinarian care, fencing), substantial water needs, and land requirements. Location is crucial for accessing breeding stock and markets. Managing, protecting, and moving livestock requires fencing and specialized handling equipment. The animals' health, productivity, and survival are critically dependent on climate, access to water, and proper forage.

Effective management strategies include creating reserves by saving female animals, purchasing new rams to prevent inbreeding, and selecting for quality to improve the herd. Providing appropriate shelter, especially during winter, is essential for conserving heat and moisture, potentially saving up to 50% in feed costs. Protecting livestock from wind using windbreaks is also important. Some livestock, like cattle and sheep, thrive when birthing on open grass pastures but require shelter and a clean water source. Adapting livestock to winter conditions and selecting breeds that can care for themselves reduces labor and healthcare costs. Children can be safely involved in livestock care.

Different types of livestock require specific regulatory permits and registration, especially for purebred stock. Their prices significantly impact traditional farms, and various raising methods, such as feedlots or pastures, affect their integration with crops. Livestock can also serve as a "free energy source" for plowing, and their body heat can be utilized in structures like greenhouses. Reports indicate that livestock may use beehives as scratching posts, potentially knocking them over.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfed52d46f5bf9d5489c4d6b6dddd2cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-766988b011c2a15c7312ce3e9bb55cf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db06dd0bc574d59f7ffa8848b9ae23fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8448fec2abb01962b8a2c019dd0efd50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skunks">
      <data key="d0">Skunks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skunks are identified as significant animal predators of honey bees in North America, considered one of the few pests of real consequence to a hive. As nocturnal predators, they approach the hive at night to prey on adult bees. A skunk attacks by scratching at the hive's entrance to lure individual guard bees out, which it then kills and eats. This constant harassment can severely weaken a colony over time and also cause the bees to become excessively irritable. Evidence of a skunk attack includes holes dug in the yard, bare ground in front of the hive, muddy landing boards, and the presence of regurgitated bee remains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bears">
      <data key="d0">Bears</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bears are large mammals and predators that pose a significant threat to honey bee hives, being listed as one of the few pests of real consequence in North America, particularly in 'bear country'. Attracted to both the honey and the brood within a hive, bears are major and highly destructive predators that will often destroy the woodenware of the hive completely to steal its contents. Due to their strength and persistence, these animals are difficult to deter, often requiring electric fences as a primary defense.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comfort and Workability">
      <data key="d0">Comfort and Workability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A practical consideration for the beekeeper when choosing a hive location. The site should have enough level space for the beekeeper to move, disassemble the hive, and use equipment like a wheelbarrow or lawn mower.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equipment">
      <data key="d0">Equipment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A broad category encompassing machinery, high-quality hand tools, buildings, and fencing necessary for farm operations. It is described as expensive to buy and maintain.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical resource for farming, including machinery. The text suggests renting from neighbors or dealers as a capital-saving strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tools and items used in beekeeping, such as a smoker or hive tool. The text notes the annoyance of being at a remote hive site without an essential piece of equipment, reinforcing the benefit of a nearby hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Continent">
      <data key="d0">The Continent</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Continent is mentioned as a large geographical area where one or two bee hives can be successfully kept almost anywhere, including in large cities and suburbs, alleviating concerns about finding a suitable location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Active Beekeeping Season">
      <data key="d0">Active Beekeeping Season</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Active Beekeeping Season is the period when bees are most active. During this time, honey supers are added to the hive to collect surplus honey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Two-Story Hive">
      <data key="d0">Complete Two-Story Hive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Complete Two-Story Hive is described as a popular, standard hive configuration in much of the country. It consists of two full-depth, ten-frame hive bodies and is recommended for beginners. The text lists its specific components.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Supers">
      <data key="d0">Honey Supers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, Honey Supers are a specific component of a beehive, often referred to as a superstructure, consisting of boxes that are added to the hive for bees to store surplus honey. This surplus constitutes the honey crop for the beekeeper. Honey Supers come in various sizes, with two standard options being shallow, which can hold 30 lbs of honey, and mid-depth, which can hold 35 lbs. It is recommended that beekeepers stick to a single size to avoid compatibility issues with frames.

These boxes are typically added to the hive during the active season, usually after the first year, in a process known as 'Supering'. The presence of Honey Supers on a hive is a critical factor in the timing of hive treatments. To prevent the contamination of the honey, they must be removed before any medications are administered. This prohibition applies to various treatments, including mite treatments like Menthol and Miticur, and medications for American Foulbrood (AFB), such as Terramycin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066653911102a71446dc7a3246a0c193&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large Cities">
      <data key="d0">Large Cities</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Large Cities are mentioned as unlikely but successful locales for keeping honey bees, demonstrating the adaptability of bee colonies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Suburbs">
      <data key="d0">Suburbs</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Suburbs are mentioned alongside large cities as viable and successful locations for beekeeping, expanding the potential areas for new beekeepers.&lt;SEP&gt;The geographic areas from which 40% of the teens participating in The Food Project's summer program are recruited. This is done intentionally to create a diverse mix of participants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flowering Plants">
      <data key="d0">Flowering Plants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Flowering Plants are the source of forage for honey bees. A substantial amount of these plants within a 1-to-2-mile radius of the hive is crucial for the colony's success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Equipment">
      <data key="d0">Standard Equipment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard Equipment refers to the 'tried and true' beekeeping supplies that have evolved over time and are recommended for novices. This includes common configurations like the ten-frame hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Catalog">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Catalog</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Beekeeping Catalog is a publication that lists a myriad of equipment and supplies. The text warns that the variety can be overwhelming for new beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eight-frame Units">
      <data key="d0">Eight-frame Units</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Eight-frame Units are a less common size of hive body and super, mentioned as an alternative to the standard ten-frame configuration that beginners should avoid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eleven-frame Units">
      <data key="d0">Eleven-frame Units</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Eleven-frame Units are another less common size for hive bodies, which the text advises beginners to stay away from in favor of standard equipment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bottom Board">
      <data key="d0">Bottom Board</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bottom Board is the floor or base of a beehive and is considered a necessary component of a complete hive. It is listed as basic equipment for a two-story hive and often includes an entrance reducer. There is a "reversible" type of Bottom Board that has two different depths, though the practice of reversing it for the winter is no longer common. Because the Bottom Board is susceptible to dampness and rot, it is essential to keep it elevated off the ground using a hive stand. A key spring maintenance chore for beekeepers is to clean the Bottom Board of any dead bees and debris that has accumulated over the winter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Bodies">
      <data key="d0">Hive Bodies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hive Bodies are the main box-like sections or chambers that constitute the structure of a beehive. This is where the bee colony lives, raises its brood, and stores food for itself. Hive Bodies contain frames, which can be manipulated by beekeepers, and have covers that rest on top of them. It is recommended to paint their exterior surfaces to protect them from the weather. Different configurations are possible; a standard setup may use two full-depth or deep (9½-inch) hive bodies, while an alternative is to use three mid-sized (6⅝-inch) bodies. An essential spring chore for beekeepers involves reversing the hive bodies. It is also noted that bees may sometimes neglect undrawn frames in the hive bodies, preferring to move up into the honey supers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Frames">
      <data key="d0">Frames</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Frames are removable, rectangular structures that hang inside hive bodies and supers, serving as a key component of modern hives like the Langstroth. They hold the foundation, which can be secured with pins or wire, and provide the structure on which bees build their comb. This comb is essential for the colony, used for raising brood and storing honey and pollen.

The removability of frames is crucial for beekeeping management. During an inspection, a beekeeper pulls and examines individual frames to assess colony health, using metrics such as the number of "frames of brood". This process also involves cleaning propolis from the frame end bars. Frames are also manipulated during other tasks; for example, three or four are typically removed to create space when installing a bee package. They can be used to deliver emergency syrup feed and, during honey harvesting, are placed in an extractor to have the honey spun out, requiring their weight to be balanced to prevent vibration.

Frames are available in various types, including wood and plastic, and different styles such as wedge-top and grooved-top, making their compatibility with the chosen foundation critical. A standard hive body or super typically holds ten frames.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Foundation is a sheet, typically made of wax or plastic and often imprinted with a hexagonal cell pattern, that is placed inside a hive frame. Its purpose is to serve as a base or guide for bees to build straight, orderly honeycomb. Bees build upon this base in a process known as "drawing out" the comb, which requires an active nectar flow and is a common starting point for new beekeepers. Different types of foundation exist, such as hook-wired beeswax and plastic-based versions, and different sizes are required for hive bodies versus supers. It is crucial to use compatible frame and foundation pairings to prevent buckling and to keep the foundation's edges straight until the comb is fully drawn. If existing comb is destroyed, for example through the crushing and straining method of honey extraction, bees must start over by building on new foundation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inner Cover">
      <data key="d0">Inner Cover</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Inner Cover is a standard, typically ventilated component of a beehive that is located on top of the uppermost hive body or super, directly beneath the main outer cover. It features a central hole that serves several crucial functions. This hole allows for the installation of a Porter Bee Escape or the placement of an inverted feeder, enabling bees to access the food source from below. Additionally, the hole acts as an observation point; if the bee cluster is visible through it, this is a strong indicator that the hive's food stores are low. The Inner Cover is also used for emergency feeding, as dry granulated sugar or candy can be placed on it for the bees. In some wintering methods, the Inner Cover is reversed with its flat side up and placed beneath an insulation board to help prevent moisture from condensing directly above the bee cluster.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Outer Cover">
      <data key="d0">Outer Cover</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Outer Cover is the final top piece of the hive, designed to protect the colony from the elements. The text mentions a telescoping style as part of the basic equipment list.&lt;SEP&gt;The final top lid of the beehive, which provides protection from weather and pests. It is placed on top after the feeder and any necessary spacers (like an empty hive body) are in place.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supers">
      <data key="d0">Supers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supers are beekeeping equipment consisting of boxes that are placed on top of a hive's main brood chambers, or hive bodies, to serve as a superstructure for honey storage. Their specific purpose is for the bees to store surplus honey, which the beekeeper can then harvest.

These boxes come in various depths, such as shallow or mid-sized, and it is recommended to have two or three available for each hive. Supers can be configured with a mixture of frames to produce both extracted honey and comb honey. The timing for adding supers is critical; they should be placed on the hive during a nectar flow. If they are added when not needed, they can become an unoccupied space, creating a risk of infestation by wax moths. In addition to their primary use for honey, an empty super can also be utilized to hold a feeder jar as part of an alternative feeding arrangement.

During harvesting, the supers full of honey are removed from the hive. This process can be complicated by the presence of burr and bridge comb, which makes clearing the bees from the boxes difficult. After being removed, the supers will often drip honey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Tool">
      <data key="d0">Hive Tool</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Hive Tool is an essential, standard, and specialized piece of equipment for beekeepers. Its primary purpose is to pry apart and manipulate various hive components, such as bodies, supers, and frames, which are often sealed together by the bees. It is also used for other tasks, like prying the lid from a bee package. A 10-inch standard version is recommended to avoid damaging the hive, unlike substitutes such as screwdrivers. However, the Hive Tool is still powerful enough that it can break plastic frames if not used with care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoker">
      <data key="d0">Smoker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Smoker is an essential and critical piece of equipment for beekeepers. This tool is used to produce and puff cool smoke into a hive, which calms and soothes the bees. This calming effect makes working with the hive and conducting inspections both safer and easier for the beekeeper. It is advised to have the Smoker lit and ready during hive work, including during the installation of bees, although its effect may be minimal in that specific situation. For durability, stainless steel models are recommended, and a larger 4-by-10-inch size is suggested as a better option for beekeepers who manage multiple hives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feeder">
      <data key="d0">Feeder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Feeder is a necessary piece of equipment or device used to provide supplementary food, typically sugar syrup, to a bee colony. It is especially important for stimulating the growth of a new colony and for providing sustenance during cool weather. The Feeder is placed inside the hive, with some advocating for feeding in large quantities, such as by the gallon, rather than using small, external feeders. A common type of feeder is an inverted container, which can be a repurposed 1-gallon glass jar or a specially designed plastic pail, filled with syrup. This container is placed over a hole in the hive's inner cover, and small holes in the lid allow the bees to access the food without it leaking into the hive. The beekeeper is responsible for refilling the Feeder as needed, which can be checked during hive inspections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Veil and Helmet">
      <data key="d0">Veil and Helmet</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An essential piece of a beekeeper's protective attire designed to protect the head and face from stings. Standard veils, available in round or square styles, are worn with a helmet or a dedicated broad-brimmed hat.&lt;SEP&gt;Necessary protective gear worn by beekeepers to protect the head and face from stings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Stand">
      <data key="d0">Hive Stand</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A necessary piece of equipment that serves as the base or platform on which the entire hive rests."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A piece of equipment essential for keeping the hive's wooden bottom board off the damp ground to prevent rot. The text critiques standard commercial stands and recommends a wider, more stable, homemade stand made of treated wood.&lt;SEP&gt;A piece of equipment used to support one or two beehives. The text recommends a homemade, wide-based stand made of treated lumber for greater stability compared to narrower, commercially-available stands. The preferred dimensions are 18 inches deep and 4 feet wide.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gloves">
      <data key="d0">Gloves</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Optional protective equipment worn on the hands by beekeepers to prevent stings while working with the hive."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A piece of protective equipment for a beekeeper's hands. The text suggests it is a good idea for a beekeeper to have a pair.&lt;SEP&gt;Protective equipment for beekeepers. The text suggests having a pair for emergencies but recommends working without them for regular tasks to be more attuned to the bees and less likely to crush them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coveralls">
      <data key="d0">Coveralls</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Optional protective clothing worn by beekeepers to cover their body and prevent stings."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A type of protective clothing for beekeepers, available from beekeeping catalogs. They are described as being identical to bee suits, except they do not have a veil attached.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Pins">
      <data key="d0">Support Pins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Small fasteners used as a modern alternative to wiring for securing foundation within a frame to prevent it from buckling."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Frame Wire">
      <data key="d0">Frame Wire</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The traditional material used to cross-wire frames, providing strength and stability to the foundation and the resulting comb."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plastic">
      <data key="d0">Plastic</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An alternative material used for making hive components like frames and foundation. The author expresses a preference against it, citing that it breaks more easily and bees are less willing to work with it."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wood">
      <data key="d0">Wood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The traditional and implied material for making frames, which the author prefers over plastic."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beeswax">
      <data key="d0">Beeswax</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A product from the beehive with a variety of uses, including beeswax foundation for hives, candles, cosmetics, creams, salves, and polishes. It is sourced from cappings, scrapings, and old comb, with lighter-colored wax being of higher quality."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"The natural, implied material for foundation, which the author prefers. It is noted that bees can readily rebuild damaged or stripped beeswax foundation."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A natural wax produced by honey bees, used to build the comb structure of their hive. It is also a valuable product with many human uses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Covers">
      <data key="d0">Covers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of hive equipment used to protect the top of the hive. The text describes several types, including weathertight telescoping outer covers, ventilated inner covers, and migratory covers used by commercial beekeepers. It also discusses material choices, recommending solid wood over plastic or masonite to prevent sagging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wiring">
      <data key="d0">Wiring</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method for securing and supporting foundation within a hive frame using wire. It is described as adding strength and stability but can be a tedious process for beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pins">
      <data key="d0">Pins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of fastener used as an alternative to wiring for securing foundation into a frame. The text suggests that for most beekeepers, two pins on each end bar are sufficient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Construction Materials">
      <data key="d0">Hive Construction Materials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the materials used to build hive components. Pine and cypress are recommended as satisfactory woods, while hardwoods are deemed too heavy and costly. Plastic and masonite are discouraged for covers due to sagging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paint">
      <data key="d0">Paint</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A protective finish applied to the exposed wooden parts of a beehive. The text discusses its application, the need for it to be dry and odorless before use, and how color choice (e.g., white vs. light green) can affect hive temperature and camouflage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Frame Lifter">
      <data key="d0">Frame Lifter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific, highly recommended style of hive tool that excels at removing frames from hives without causing damage to the frames or the hive body. The author suggests carrying both a standard hive tool and a frame lifter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boardman Feeder">
      <data key="d0">Boardman Feeder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific style of feeder often included in beginner beekeeping kits. The text criticizes it as being too small and ill-suited for feeding new colonies in the spring because it is used outside the hive where bees in their cluster won't reach it in cool weather.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of external feeder used to provide sugar syrup to the bee colony. While useful, it has drawbacks, such as being less accessible in cool weather and potentially attracting robber bees due to its external location and risk of spillage. The text advises using it with an entrance reducer to mitigate this risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alexander Veil">
      <data key="d0">Alexander Veil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An alternate style of a round veil that features a built-in soft top and is used without a helmet. It is considered less satisfactory for regular use as it may touch the face, but it is useful as a spare or guest veil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commercially-available Stands">
      <data key="d0">Commercially-available Stands</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hive stands that can be purchased from suppliers. The text contrasts them with a homemade version, describing the commercial stands as narrower and less stable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treated Lumber">
      <data key="d0">Treated Lumber</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A building material recommended for constructing a homemade hive stand. It is noted for its durability and longevity, even when in direct contact with the ground.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Screwdriver">
      <data key="d0">Screwdriver</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A common tool mentioned as an example of an improper substitute for a hive tool. Its use is discouraged as it can severely damage hive parts, leading to deterioration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beginner Outfits">
      <data key="d0">Beginner Outfits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kits of equipment for new beekeepers, typically sold through catalogs. The text points out that they usually include a Boardman feeder, which is considered inadequate for new colonies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Equipment Catalogs">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Equipment Catalogs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A source for purchasing beekeeping supplies, such as beginner outfits. Mentioned as a common channel for new beekeepers to acquire equipment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gallon Glass Jars">
      <data key="d0">Gallon Glass Jars</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Large containers, often repurposed from restaurants or cafeterias, that are recommended as an effective way to feed bees in large quantities. They are placed on the hive's inner cover.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Restaurants Or School Cafeterias">
      <data key="d0">Restaurants Or School Cafeterias</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Establishments mentioned as a source for obtaining empty gallon glass jars, which can be repurposed as effective and large-capacity bee feeders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plastic Pails">
      <data key="d0">Plastic Pails</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Containers specifically designed for use as bee feeders. They are noted as an alternative to repurposed glass jars and are available from beekeeping equipment suppliers.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic pails, typically 5-gallon, are essential containers for a beekeeper to collect and store honey. The text stresses the importance of using only food-quality containers, which can often be obtained from bakeries or restaurants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Veil">
      <data key="d0">Veil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Veil is an essential piece of protective headwear for a beekeeper, designed to cover the head and face. It is considered a minimum safety measure when working with bees, as the face is a vulnerable area and a primary target for upset bees. There are two main styles of veils, round and square. A Veil can be purchased separately to be used with a hat or helmet, and most standard versions require a helmet for support. Alternatively, it can come attached to a bee suit via a zipper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Helmet">
      <data key="d0">Helmet</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Protective headwear used in beekeeping, primarily to support a veil and keep it away from the beekeeper's face. The text suggests that any broad-brimmed hat can also serve this purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Square Veil">
      <data key="d0">Square Veil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A style of beekeeper's veil recommended by the author. Its main advantage is that it can be folded flat for storage, which prevents creasing and wear.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Round Veil">
      <data key="d0">Round Veil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A style of beekeeper's veil that must be rolled for storage. The text describes it as prone to wrinkling and creasing, which requires reshaping and causes wear and tear over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Broad-brimmed Hat">
      <data key="d0">Broad-brimmed Hat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An alternative to a formal beekeeper's helmet. The text states that any hat with a wide brim can be used to support a veil, but it should be dedicated to beekeeping use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Suits">
      <data key="d0">Bee Suits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Protective clothing for beekeepers that includes a veil attached with a zipper. The text notes that buying this requires wearing the full suit to use the veil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wild Colony">
      <data key="d0">Wild Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An option for acquiring bees that is not recommended for beginners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mites">
      <data key="d0">Mites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mites are a type of pest, specifically a category of ectoparasites like Tracheal and Varroa mites, that pose a significant threat to honey bee colonies. They can infest a colony through various means, as they have been historically carried by package bees and are also a potential problem with swarms. This risk requires beekeepers to take precautions, as managing mites is a crucial aspect of beekeeping. It is particularly emphasized that mites must be managed in a colony's second year, referred to as 'The New Season'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Florida">
      <data key="d0">Florida</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A southern U.S. state mentioned as a primary origin for package bees due to its climate and long season.&lt;SEP&gt;Florida is a state in the United States mentioned as being part of climate Zone 9, where temperatures do not typically fall below 30 degrees Fahrenheit.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Georgia">
      <data key="d0">Georgia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A southern U.S. state mentioned as a primary origin for package bees.&lt;SEP&gt;A state in the Southern Appalachian region that is included within the 100-mile 'local' definition established by ASAP.&lt;SEP&gt;The state where the Serenbe community is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1544f96bf3906c3ffd91d3f551a278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alabama">
      <data key="d0">Alabama</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Alabama is a southern U.S. state with a population of 4.3 million. Birmingham is a city located within Alabama. The state is notable for several reasons: it was once considered "pumpkin-deficient" due to its reliance on pumpkin imports, highlighting a local market opportunity. Alabama is also a primary origin for package bees. Public health is a concern in Alabama, as it struggles with high obesity rates, which have been the subject of news reports and a "fat fee" policy for state workers. Furthermore, Alabama is where Jamie Ager purchased South Polls cattle, a breed selected for its ability to thrive on a grass diet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c575c019780e689390cd685c690159f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c8f3e81fd6708a9852f46f0d4ac0a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9297eb734555e7b850045590e4fc5cb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mississippi">
      <data key="d0">Mississippi</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A southern U.S. state mentioned as a primary origin for package bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Louisiana">
      <data key="d0">Louisiana</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A southern U.S. state mentioned as a primary origin for package bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Texas">
      <data key="d0">Texas</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Texas is a southern U.S. state with diverse agricultural and ecological characteristics. It is noted for its significant cattle production and for having a large number of small farms, where ponds are uniquely referred to as 'tanks'. The state's agricultural history includes a period where many farmers engaged in the ratite business, which they later abandoned after the market collapsed, resulting in stock being sold cheaply or birds being released. Texas agriculture is also susceptible to long-distance threats, such as wheat rust spores that can be transported by wind from as far away as Canada.

In the realm of apiculture, Texas serves as a primary origin for package bees. Concurrently, it is the state where Africanized bees have officially entered the United States and are continuing to spread. Climatically, the southern part of Texas is classified as being within Zone 9.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e2a281158355949bfa9c35a51c5622a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="California">
      <data key="d0">California</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">California is a U.S. state with a significant focus on agriculture and sustainability. It is a primary origin for package bees and the primary domestic market for organic corn used in natural food production. The state cultivates the majority of domesticated wild rice, harvested by machines, and its almond crop relies heavily on honeybee pollination, utilizing approximately half of the honeybees in the United States. Sustainable wheat farming and distribution are also present, as exemplified by Huasna Valley Farm.

California is home to the Village Homes neighborhood in Davis, an early example of a green development. The state is the location of the California School Garden Network, a network of the Genetic Engineering Policy Alliance, and the site of the Chez Panisse Foundation's model program. California State University at Chico passed a resolution to source a portion of its food from within the state. The Life Lab Science Program focuses its school assistance efforts primarily in California.

The state is located in climate Zone 9. The small community farm described in Michael Ableman's book 'On Good Land' is located in California. Forage Oakland, a grassroots organization, was founded and operates in the state. The Monterey Bay Aquarium, which runs the Seafood Watch program, is also located in California. Additionally, Jennie Grant's sister resides in California, whose backyard chicken-raising sparked Grant to pursue raising goats. Agricultural projects looking for relevant advice should consult others in similar temperate zones such as California.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e64fb1fe152a62cd06403742bd6beac5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27bb80a716045bb643a01fd3aae1d84b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f1544f96bf3906c3ffd91d3f551a278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104466acc0eb6f55c05f02aa258ea31a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Helmets">
      <data key="d0">Helmets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of protective headgear for beekeepers, used to support a veil. The text notes that some may be heavier and more cumbersome than others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Broad-brimmed hat">
      <data key="d0">Broad-brimmed hat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of hat that can be dedicated to beekeeping use to support a veil, serving as an alternative to a helmet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nectar flow">
      <data key="d0">Nectar flow</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The seasonal availability of nectar from flowers for bees to collect. A 'weak nectar flow' is a condition that makes bees reluctant to draw foundation, especially above a queen excluder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supers with foundation">
      <data key="d0">Supers with foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hive components consisting of boxes (supers) filled with frames of foundation (a base for bees to build comb). Beginners often use these, and bees are reluctant to work in them when placed above a queen excluder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping catalogs">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping catalogs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Publications or listings that show available beekeeping equipment. The text cautions beginners against the temptation to buy too much equipment from them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Pathogens that can affect a bee colony. The risk of transmission is a key consideration when starting a hive. Package bees have a lower risk as they lack comb, while nucleus hives and swarms have a higher risk due to included comb or unknown origins."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A specialized category of beekeeping literature focusing on honey bee health, pathology, pests, and predators. Books in this section include 'Honey Bee Pathology' by Leslie Bailey and B. Ball.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f1d34c0baad55bf46a357beac030d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feral Colony">
      <data key="d0">Feral Colony</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A wild colony of bees living outside the control of a beekeeper, often in inaccessible locations like hollow trees or inside walls. Capturing a feral colony is described as a difficult option for beginners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Police and Fire Departments">
      <data key="d0">Police and Fire Departments</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Local government agencies that are often contacted by householders about bee problems. They serve as a point of contact for beekeepers looking to be notified about available swarms or feral colonies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeper Associations">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeper Associations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Groups for beekeepers that act as a source of information. Members can learn about available existing bee colonies through word-of-mouth within these associations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nucleus Hives">
      <data key="d0">Nucleus Hives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Also known as nucs, these are small, established bee colonies sold to start a new hive. They are typically produced by local commercial or sideline beekeepers and can be sourced through equipment suppliers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State or Provincial Authorities">
      <data key="d0">State or Provincial Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Governmental bodies that may legally restrict the collection of wild bee swarms to prevent the spread of Africanized bees, requiring authorized personnel for their removal and destruction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Authorized Personnel">
      <data key="d0">Authorized Personnel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Individuals officially permitted by state or provincial authorities to handle and potentially destroy bee swarms to control the spread of Africanized bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Exterminator">
      <data key="d0">Local Exterminator</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A professional who is often called to eliminate stinging insects and can be a source of leads for beekeepers to acquire unwanted honey bee colonies that they might otherwise be paid to destroy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Householders">
      <data key="d0">Householders</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Residents who discover unwanted bee colonies on their property and typically report them to agencies like police, fire departments, or local exterminators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Equipment Supplier">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Equipment Supplier</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A commercial vendor that sells beekeeping equipment and often serves as a primary source or dealer for purchasing package bees and nucleus hives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experienced Beekeeper Mentor">
      <data key="d0">Experienced Beekeeper Mentor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A seasoned, knowledgeable, and patient beekeeper who can introduce a novice to hive inspection and help them overcome any fear of handling bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Foragers">
      <data key="d0">Foragers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Older bees in a colony responsible for collecting nectar and pollen. Their venom glands are fully functional, and their behavior is highly influenced by weather and the availability of a nectar flow.&lt;SEP&gt;Worker bees that travel outside the hive to collect resources. The beekeeper is advised to watch for foragers returning with nectar and pollen as a sign of a healthy, active colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Protective Beekeeping Gear">
      <data key="d0">Protective Beekeeping Gear</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Specialized clothing and equipment such as bee suits, helmets, veils, and gloves that beekeepers wear for protection against stings, though the text notes that its use varies among individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeper's Attire">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeper's Attire</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the clothing and protective gear worn when interacting with bees. The text discusses a range from full protection (bee suit, helmet, veil, gloves) to minimal protection. It recommends light-colored, smooth fabrics, tucked-in pant cuffs, and avoiding shiny items like rings or watches to prevent agitating bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Odors">
      <data key="d0">Odors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of scents that can influence bee behavior. The text specifies that attractive fragrances like perfume or hair spray can cause bees to investigate and get tangled, while offensive odors like gasoline can provoke them. The smell of bananas is also discussed as a potential, though unproven in the author's test, alarm signal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeper's Behavior">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeper's Behavior</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The recommended conduct for a person working with bees. This involves being calm, moving slowly and deliberately, and minimizing vibrations transmitted to the hive. The text advises that slow movements make a person less noticeable to bees, while sudden or frantic actions can attract negative attention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vibrations">
      <data key="d0">Vibrations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of sensory input that bees feel through the hive structure (substrate). The text emphasizes that the beekeeper's movements transmit vibrations that can have a cumulative upsetting effect on the bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Movement">
      <data key="d0">Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The physical actions of a beekeeper around the hive. The text explains that bees' vision is highly sensitive to motion, so calm, slow movements are recommended to avoid attracting attention, while fast or thrashing motions can provoke a defensive response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shiny Objects">
      <data key="d0">Shiny Objects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A class of items, such as rings or wristwatches, that glitter in the sun. The text warns that these can attract the attention of aroused bees, causing them to attack the moving, flashing object.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Installing Bees">
      <data key="d0">Installing Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary beekeeping activity involving the transfer of a new bee colony, such as from a package, into a permanent hive. The process requires specific preparations, tools, and a calm approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Package Of Bees">
      <data key="d0">Package Of Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A commercially available, screened box used to transport bees for starting a new hive. A typical package contains about 10,000 bees, including workers, a few drones, a caged queen, and a can of syrup for food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sugar Syrup">
      <data key="d0">Sugar Syrup</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Sugar Syrup is a liquid bee feed made from sugar. It is used as an ingredient to make pollen patties and can also be fed directly to bees in a top feeder, especially in warmer weather during spring."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A feed for bees, made from a 1-to-1 mixture of table sugar and water. It is essential for nourishing a new colony during its first month and for feeding bees in a package if installation is delayed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuc">
      <data key="d0">Nuc</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A nucleus colony, which is an alternative to a package of bees for starting a new bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drones">
      <data key="d0">Drones</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Drones are the male honey bees in a colony, with their primary role being to mate with a queen. The text mentions their life span.&lt;SEP&gt;Male bees within a colony, a few of which are included in a commercial package of bees.&lt;SEP&gt;Male honey bees that develop from unfertilized eggs. Their primary role is to mate with the queen, after which they have a limited life span.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Protective Gear">
      <data key="d0">Protective Gear</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Equipment worn by beekeepers to prevent stings. It includes a veil as a minimum requirement.&lt;SEP&gt;Safety equipment, specifically a veil, that the beekeeper is instructed to wear to prevent stings during the installation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weather">
      <data key="d0">Weather</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An external factor that can influence bee temperament. Oppressive weather is mentioned as a potential cause for bees being difficult to work with.&lt;SEP&gt;Described as the first and biggest variable and problem for farms. A good farm plan must include strategies to mitigate its effects, such as using cover crops and irrigation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Installation of the Package">
      <data key="d0">Installation of the Package</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary activity described in the text, involving the step-by-step process of transferring a package of bees from their shipping container into a new hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen Cage">
      <data key="d0">Queen Cage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Queen Cage is a small container or cage used for both transporting and introducing a queen bee into a hive or colony. The cage is designed to hold the queen and her attendants. It features a screen that allows the existing bees of the colony to communicate with and acclimate to the new queen. For introduction into the hive, the Queen Cage has a candy plug at one end, which the bees eat through. This process creates a delayed or timed release, which is crucial for facilitating the colony's safe acceptance of the new queen. When used for introduction, the cage is temporarily placed between the frames of the hive. For transportation, it might be suspended by a metal disk within a larger package.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feeder Can">
      <data key="d0">Feeder Can</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A can of food for the bees that is located inside the bee package. It must be removed during the installation process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supplier">
      <data key="d0">Supplier</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The provider of the package of bees. The text advises contacting the supplier immediately if the queen is found to be dead, as a reputable supplier will provide a replacement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Candy Plug">
      <data key="d0">Candy Plug</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A semi-solid white candy at one end of the queen cage. It serves as a food source and a slow-release mechanism, preventing the queen from being released immediately into the confusion of the new hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Frames">
      <data key="d0">Hive Frames</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Removable structures within the hive. The text instructs the beekeeper to remove three or four frames from the center to make space for the bees during installation.&lt;SEP&gt;The removable structures within a hive body where bees build their wax comb. The text emphasizes the importance of the bees drawing out comb on all twenty frames across two hive bodies during the first season for winter storage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Covers">
      <data key="d0">Hive Covers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The protective top layers of the hive. The text instructs the beekeeper to remove both covers and set them aside at the beginning of the installation process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Package Lid">
      <data key="d0">Package Lid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The cover of the bee package, held in place by fasteners. It must be pried off to access the queen cage and feeder can.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metal Disk">
      <data key="d0">Metal Disk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A metal tab or disk that suspends the queen cage from the top of the package. It must be bent back to access the candy plug.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tools">
      <data key="d0">Tools</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Implements and equipment used on a farm for repair and maintenance. They must be stored in a dry shed to prevent rust and weather wear, which shortens their lifespan.&lt;SEP&gt;Implements used by the beekeeper during installation, such as pliers to pull out the feeder can and a thin nail or jackknife to poke a hole in the candy plug.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fasteners">
      <data key="d0">Fasteners</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nails or staples mentioned as securing the package lid. They must be overcome to open the package.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marshmallow">
      <data key="d0">Marshmallow</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A substance recommended as an emergency substitute for the candy plug in the queen cage if the original candy has been consumed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Installing A Package Of Bees">
      <data key="d0">Installing A Package Of Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a detailed process for introducing a new set of bees into a hive. The procedure involves preparing the hive by removing frames, installing the queen in her cage, shaking the bulk of the bees from their transport container into the hive, and then setting up a feeder to provide them with nourishment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Installing A Nucleus Hive">
      <data key="d0">Installing A Nucleus Hive</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the process of transferring a pre-established small colony (a nuc) into a permanent hive. It is often simpler than installing a package, as it involves moving frames that already contain a queen, bees, and comb from a temporary container to the new hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Candy">
      <data key="d0">Candy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sugar-based plug in the queen cage that worker bees eat through over several days. This process facilitates the gradual acceptance and release of the new queen.&lt;SEP&gt;A sugar-based plug used in a queen cage. It is designed to be eaten by the bees over several days, which allows for a slow, gradual release of the queen, thereby increasing the colony's acceptance of her. The text suggests poking a small hole in it to help the bees start.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drawn Comb">
      <data key="d0">Drawn Comb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fully constructed beeswax cells built by bees on foundation or from scratch. It is the structure used for storing honey and raising brood. New beekeepers often have foundation instead of drawn comb.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to frames on which bees have already built out the wax honeycomb. The text notes that if a hive has drawn comb, a small amount may need to be cut away to properly place the queen cage to facilitate communication between the queen and the colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Package Container">
      <data key="d0">Package Container</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The temporary, usually screened, box used to transport a package of bees. The procedure involves shaking most of the bees out of this container into the hive and then propping the container at the hive entrance for stragglers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Syrup">
      <data key="d0">Syrup</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sugar and water mixture prepared as food for the bees. It is placed in a feeder, and the text cautions against leaks, which can cause wet bees and attract pests.&lt;SEP&gt;A sugar-water solution provided by the beekeeper to feed the colony. The text discusses monitoring the consumption rate of syrup to decide whether to continue feeding.&lt;SEP&gt;A sugar-water solution provided in a feeder to supplement the bees' natural diet. The text explains it mimics a nectar flow and stimulates productive activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southern Beeyard">
      <data key="d0">Southern Beeyard</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical location in the south where package bees are sourced. Bees from one or two established colonies are shaken into a package cage to be sold and shipped, often north.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The First Inspection">
      <data key="d0">The First Inspection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A crucial management event that should occur around the third day after installing a package of bees. The primary purpose is to quickly and with minimal disturbance check if the queen bee has been released from her cage. It is one of the first and most important checks on a new colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Package Installation">
      <data key="d0">Package Installation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The initial event of introducing a new package of bees and a queen into a hive. The inspections described in the text are direct follow-ups to this event.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of transferring package bees into a new hive. It is described as a traumatic and confusing event for the bees, involving thumping, banging, and smoke, which necessitates a delayed release of the queen to ensure her safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nectar/Syrup">
      <data key="d0">Nectar/Syrup</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary food source for bees, mentioned as either natural nectar from forage or artificial syrup provided by the beekeeper. A steady supply is crucial for stimulating wax glands to build comb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Delayed Queen Release">
      <data key="d0">Delayed Queen Release</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific management technique used during package installation. The queen is kept in her cage for a day or two to allow the stressed colony to settle down and acclimate to her before she is released, preventing them from potentially killing her.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colony Population Decline">
      <data key="d0">Colony Population Decline</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural phenomenon that occurs during the first month of a new colony. The population slowly declines because the original adult bees from the package die off from natural causes before the first batch of new bees (brood) has had time to develop and emerge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nucleus Colony (Nuc)">
      <data key="d0">Nucleus Colony (Nuc)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A small, established bee colony mentioned as an alternative to a package. In a nuc, the queen is typically already free and laying eggs on the frames at the time of transfer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Inspection">
      <data key="d0">First Inspection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An inspection conducted on the third day after installing a new bee package. Its primary purpose is to confirm the queen's release from her cage and to remove any initial wild comb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Inspection">
      <data key="d0">Second Inspection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A follow-up inspection to determine if the queen has started laying eggs. It also involves checking the progress of comb building and resource collection by the bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wild Comb">
      <data key="d0">Wild Comb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Comb built by bees in undesirable locations, such as the space left by the queen cage. It must be removed during inspections to prevent structural problems like bridge comb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Entrance">
      <data key="d0">Hive Entrance</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The opening of the hive where bees enter and exit. It serves as a key observation point for the beekeeper to assess foraging activity and the general demeanor of the colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bridge Comb">
      <data key="d0">Bridge Comb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unwanted comb built between and connecting two adjacent frames. It is a significant problem that results from not removing initial wild comb and can make inspections destructive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brood Area">
      <data key="d0">Brood Area</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The central part of the hive, concentrated across two to four frames, where the queen lays eggs and the brood is raised. The expansion of this area is a key sign of a healthy colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollen Pellets">
      <data key="d0">Pollen Pellets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Visible clumps of pollen carried on the hind legs of foraging bees. Their presence is a positive sign of active foraging and resource collection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Veil and Protective Gear">
      <data key="d0">Veil and Protective Gear</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Safety equipment, including a face-covering veil, that the beekeeper is instructed to wear to prevent bee stings during hive inspections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Inspection">
      <data key="d0">Hive Inspection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The act of assessing the condition of a beehive. This can be done externally by lifting the hive to gauge its weight, or internally by opening it to observe bee activity, check frame usage, and examine the brood, pollen, and honey stores."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A recurring procedure of opening and examining the hive to monitor the health and progress of the colony. The main goals are to check for brood, assess growth, and manage the hive structure.&lt;SEP&gt;An activity performed by the beekeeper to assess the health and condition of the colony. This includes external checks for blockages and internal checks to locate the cluster and determine the amount of remaining food stores.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eggs">
      <data key="d0">Eggs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The first stage of the bee life cycle, laid by the queen in the cells of the comb. Identifying eggs is a part of a detailed frame inspection."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The first stage of a bee's life cycle, laid by the queen in the cells of the comb. Finding eggs during an inspection is a clear sign that the queen is present and working.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Larvae">
      <data key="d0">Larvae</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The second stage of the bee life cycle, which hatches from an egg. The text notes that larvae are delicate and must be protected from direct sun and wind during inspections."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The second stage of a bee's life cycle, which hatches from an egg. Along with eggs, the presence of young larvae is a key indicator of a healthy, growing colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Frame">
      <data key="d0">Frame</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A rectangular structure held within the hive body on which bees build their comb. The text describes removing, inspecting, and managing frames during a hive inspection.&lt;SEP&gt;A removable structure contained within a Super, on which bees build honeycomb for honey storage. Frames are individually handled during honey harvest to remove bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Supplier">
      <data key="d0">Local Supplier</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A source for beekeeping supplies, including new queens, mentioned as a place to acquire a replacement queen if needed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brood Pattern">
      <data key="d0">Brood Pattern</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The arrangement of capped brood on a frame, which indicates the queen's health and egg-laying effectiveness. A solid, consistent pattern is desirable, while a spotty or irregular pattern suggests potential problems."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The arrangement of eggs, larvae, and pupae across the comb. A good, solid pattern is an indicator of a healthy and productive queen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Body">
      <data key="d0">Hive Body</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Hive Body is the main structural component and primary, box-like section of a beehive, typically located in the lower part of the structure. It contains frames and serves as the core of the colony's living space, housing the main brood nest where the queen bee lays her eggs and the brood is raised. The Hive Body also holds the colony's primary food stores, which are essential for winter survival. In managing a colony, a beekeeper might start with one hive body and then add a second one on top to provide more space as the colony expands, often when the bees are actively using seven to eight frames in the first box. During honey harvesting, bees are encouraged to move down from the upper honey supers and into the Hive Body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The First Summer">
      <data key="d0">The First Summer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical period for a new bee colony, during which it must become established, increase its population, and draw out comb. This phase requires weekly visits and careful monitoring by the beekeeper to ensure the colony is on a proper growth curve for winter survival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robber Bees">
      <data key="d0">Robber Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bees from stronger, nearby colonies that may attempt to steal honey or syrup from a weaker colony. They are a significant threat, and their activity can be catalyzed by external feeders like the Boardman feeder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Season Population Growth">
      <data key="d0">First Season Population Growth</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A crucial process for a new colony, described as a growth curve. It starts with a decline due to the die-off of original bees, followed by an upturn in the second month as new bees emerge. This progress must be monitored closely to ensure the colony will be strong enough for winter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="You (The Beekeeper)">
      <data key="d0">You (The Beekeeper)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The individual addressed in the text responsible for managing the new bee colony. Their duties include monitoring the colony's growth, adding hive bodies, managing feeders, shifting frames to encourage comb development, and performing weekly inspections to ensure the colony is healthy and preparing for winter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Entrance Reducer">
      <data key="d0">Entrance Reducer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A piece of equipment used to shrink the size of the hive entrance. It helps a weaker colony defend itself from robber bees by creating a smaller, more defensible opening, especially when using a Boardman feeder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weekly Visits">
      <data key="d0">Weekly Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The recommended frequency for a beekeeper to inspect their colony during the first summer. These visits include checking for forager activity, assessing hive weight, and monitoring population growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summer Management">
      <data key="d0">Summer Management</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The phase of beekeeping that follows the initial establishment of the colony. It is a critical period of ongoing management focused on ensuring population growth and preparation for winter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feeder Jar">
      <data key="d0">Feeder Jar</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A container used for feeding syrup to bees, which can be placed inside an empty hive body or supers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Weight">
      <data key="d0">Hive Weight</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A relative measure obtained by lifting the rear of the hive, serving as a quick, external indicator of the colony's food stores (honey). Changes in weight can signal a need to feed the colony or add a honey super."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A key indicator of a colony's progress, which the beekeeper assesses by lifting the back of the hive. An increasing weight over time suggests the bees are successfully gathering nectar and storing it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Super">
      <data key="d0">Honey Super</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An additional box placed on top of the main hive bodies, intended for the bees to store surplus honey. The decision to add one depends on colony strength, frame development, and the timing of the nectar flow."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colony Population">
      <data key="d0">Colony Population</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The number of bees within the hive. A primary goal of early-season beekeeping is to ensure the population grows, which is inferred by the expansion of the brood nest and the number of frames the bees occupy."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Colony Population refers to the total number of bees in a hive. This number naturally declines in winter but must be maintained above a minimum threshold for the colony to generate sufficient heat and survive. It is a key indicator of colony health and a primary factor for successful wintering and spring growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Beekeeper's Area">
      <data key="d0">The Beekeeper's Area</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The specific geographic location of the hive. The nature and timing of local nectar flows in this area are crucial considerations for hive management decisions, such as when to add a honey super."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pupae">
      <data key="d0">Pupae</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The third, non-feeding stage of bee development, which occurs inside a sealed cell. The presence of pupae is indicated by 'capped brood'."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capped Brood">
      <data key="d0">Capped Brood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Cells on a frame that have been sealed with a wax cap, containing developing pupae. A solid, consistent pattern of capped brood is a sign of a healthy, productive queen."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capped Honey">
      <data key="d0">Capped Honey</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Cells on a frame filled with honey that have been sealed with a wax cap by the bees for storage. Beekeepers must learn to distinguish its appearance from capped brood."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feeding">
      <data key="d0">Feeding</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The practice of providing a bee colony with supplemental food, such as sugar syrup. This is typically done for new or weak colonies and should be stopped when honey supers are on the hive or a strong nectar flow begins."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experienced Beekeepers">
      <data key="d0">Experienced Beekeepers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of knowledgeable individuals who can provide localized information, specifically regarding the timing and patterns of nectar flows in a particular area. They serve as a resource for new beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supering">
      <data key="d0">Supering</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core beekeeping practice involving the placement of supers (boxes) onto a hive to provide dedicated space for bees to store surplus honey. The text discusses the timing, methods, and considerations for this task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fall Management">
      <data key="d0">Fall Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of beekeeping tasks aimed at preparing a colony for winter survival. This process is described as starting as early as August and involves ensuring the hive is well-prepared for the cold months.&lt;SEP&gt;Fall Management refers to a specific period of beekeeping activities and preparations conducted in the autumn to ensure a colony's survival through winter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geographic Area">
      <data key="d0">Geographic Area</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The location of the beehive, which is a critical factor determining the local pattern of nectar flows. The text emphasizes that beekeeping practices must be adapted to the specific conditions of one's area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wax Production">
      <data key="d0">Wax Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The biological process by which bees secrete wax to build comb. The text states this process is stimulated by incoming nectar, following the principle of 'no nectar, no wax'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="August">
      <data key="d0">August</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific month mentioned as a time when fall management tasks may begin, even though it is considered midsummer. This highlights that beekeeping schedules are based on colony cycles rather than the calendar.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Winter Cluster">
      <data key="d0">Winter Cluster</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tight formation of bees within the hive during winter to generate and conserve heat for survival. The text describes how the cluster tightens as temperatures drop and maintains a high temperature at its center.&lt;SEP&gt;The Winter Cluster is a tight formation of bees created during cold weather to generate and conserve heat. The cluster's internal temperature can be as high as 94°F (34°C). The size and integrity of the cluster are dependent on the colony's population and are crucial for protecting the bees and brood from freezing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Stores">
      <data key="d0">Food Stores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Food Stores for a bee colony are the reserves of honey and pollen that the bees collect and store for sustenance, particularly to survive the winter. Primarily consisting of honey, which is made from collected nectar, these stores are the essential fuel source for the colony. Bees consume the honey to shiver their flight muscles, a process that generates the necessary heat to keep the winter cluster warm. The amount of Food Stores a colony needs varies by climate, but it is often around 60-70 pounds of honey. It is critical for the colony's survival that the cluster remains in constant contact with its Food Stores, as insufficient reserves will lead to starvation and poor population growth. Beekeepers are responsible for assessing the hive's Food Stores after the nectar flow ends, which they do by checking the hive's weight and performing internal inspections. If the stores are found to be insufficient, the beekeeper must provide supplementary feeding to ensure the colony's survival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Foul-brood">
      <data key="d0">American Foul-brood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">American foul-brood is a disease that can affect a bee colony and is a specific concern for beekeepers when preparing a hive for winter. Medication may be necessary to treat or prevent it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nosema Disease">
      <data key="d0">Nosema Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nosema disease is a widespread, serious, and infectious condition that affects the digestive system of adult honey bees, including workers, drones, and the queen. Caused by a protozoa that infects the gut, the disease leads to a less productive life for the bees. The effects on the colony can be severe, resulting in reduced egg-laying by the queen, premature death of workers, and a weakened, dwindling overall population. Nosema disease is a key health concern to address before winter, as its most devastating effects occur in late winter, and it may require medication for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tracheal Mites">
      <data key="d0">Tracheal Mites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tracheal Mites are microscopic, internal parasites that infest the respiratory system, specifically the breathing tubes (tracheae), of honey bees. Their entire life cycle, which includes eggs, larvae, and adults, occurs within the host bee. As a specific health threat considered by beekeepers, these mites are the direct cause of infestations that shorten the lives of bees and can lead to significant colony losses. The mite population is cyclical, reaching its peak in the winter, which is opposite to the honey bee population cycle. Consequently, beekeepers may need to medicate colonies before the winter season to prevent losses. One effective treatment mentioned is Miticur, which, when used to treat for Varroa mites, also provides protection against Tracheal Mites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066653911102a71446dc7a3246a0c193&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Varroa Mites">
      <data key="d0">Varroa Mites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A parasitic mite that is a significant pest for honey bees, mentioned in the beekeeping index. The species 'Varroa jacobsonii' is specifically named.&lt;SEP&gt;Varroa mites are a specific type of external parasitic mite that attacks and feeds on honey bees, posing a major threat to colony health.&lt;SEP&gt;Varroa mites are parasites that pose a significant threat to the health of a bee colony. They are a primary concern for beekeepers preparing for winter, and preventative medication is often considered.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066653911102a71446dc7a3246a0c193</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fall Requeening">
      <data key="d0">Fall Requeening</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The practice of replacing a bee colony's queen in the fall. This is done to introduce a young, productive queen who will lay eggs to boost the population of young bees for the winter, correct problems like a poor brood pattern or aggressive bee disposition, and reduce the likelihood of swarming the following spring."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Fall Requeening is the practice of replacing the colony's queen in late summer. This is done to introduce a new, more vigorous queen who will boost the population with young bees before winter, thereby increasing the colony's chances of survival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colony Health">
      <data key="d0">Colony Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colony Health is the overall state of a bee colony's well-being. It is a primary concern for beekeepers, especially before winter, and can be threatened by various diseases and mites, such as American foul-brood, nosema disease, tracheal mites, and Varroa mites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Protection From The Elements">
      <data key="d0">Protection From The Elements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Protection From The Elements is one of the five key factors a beekeeper must consider for a colony's winter survival. It refers to the physical shelter of the hive that protects the bees from harsh weather.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spring Growth">
      <data key="d0">Spring Growth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Spring Growth is the period in early spring when the colony's population begins to expand again after winter. This process is dependent on the number of bees that successfully survived the winter and involves the winter cluster loosening to allow for more brood rearing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medication">
      <data key="d0">Medication</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medication refers to the treatments a beekeeper may use to prevent or cure diseases and mite infestations within the colony. The text suggests it may be necessary to medicate against threats like American foul-brood and Varroa mites before winter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Protection">
      <data key="d0">Hive Protection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Measures taken to safeguard a beehive from winter hazards. This includes installing windbreaks to block excessive wind, placing mouse guards at the entrance to prevent rodent infestation, and ensuring proper ventilation and insulation."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouse Guards">
      <data key="d0">Mouse Guards</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A metal device placed across the hive entrance to prevent mice from entering and building nests during the winter. Mice can damage comb, create a mess, and cause a colony to abscond, making this protection essential."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Mouse guards are a piece of protective equipment installed on a hive entrance, typically in the fall, to prevent mice from entering and nesting inside the hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Windbreaks">
      <data key="d0">Windbreaks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Windbreaks are structures or natural features, such as lines of trees, shrubs, grasses, fences, or anchored pieces of plywood, that serve as barriers to slow or shield an area from wind. Used in both agricultural and environmental contexts, they offer numerous benefits, including protecting crops and soil from wind erosion, increasing crop yields, conserving moisture, preventing water and heat loss, and moderating temperatures to create protective microclimates. Windbreaks are also specifically used in beekeeping to shield beehives from harsh winter winds, which prevents the wind from sapping energy from the colony. The effectiveness of a windbreak depends on factors like its height, length, and density.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fall Feeding">
      <data key="d0">Fall Feeding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The practice of providing a bee colony with a sugar and water solution when their natural food stores are insufficient for winter. It should be done after the nectar flow ends but before cold weather prevents the bees from processing the syrup."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fall Hive Inspections">
      <data key="d0">Fall Hive Inspections</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"An activity conducted by beekeepers in the fall to assess the colony's readiness for winter. This involves checking for a minimum amount of stored pollen and ensuring no mice have already nested in the hive before guards are installed."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ventilation and Insulation">
      <data key="d0">Ventilation and Insulation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A critical aspect of hive protection for winter. The text describes using an insulation board over a reversed inner cover to provide an upper entrance, which helps manage moisture and keep the hive warm and dry."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"A crucial set of beekeeping practices for managing the hive environment during winter. It involves ensuring the removal of excess moisture and carbon dioxide, products of bee metabolism, while helping the bees stay warm. This is often achieved using an insulation board and an upper entrance to the hive."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wrapping Hives">
      <data key="d0">Wrapping Hives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A method of hive insulation, described as a traditional but less common practice today, except in areas with extreme winters. It involves wrapping the hive in materials like tar paper and is considered more labor-intensive than other insulation methods."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spring">
      <data key="d0">Spring</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Spring is a season of increasing bee activity, brood rearing, and food consumption. It marks a transition period from supplementary feeding to natural foraging as the weather warms and flowers begin to bloom."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"The season following winter, which is a period of significant colony growth. The success of the colony in spring is dependent on the preparations and conditions experienced during the preceding winter, such as adequate food stores and successful brood rearing."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The season when the bee colony begins to expand. It is a critical time for the beekeeper to perform hive cleanup, assess colony strength, and provide space for growth, for instance by 'reversing hive bodies'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insulation Board">
      <data key="d0">Insulation Board</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific material (also called building board or wallboard) used for hive wintering, placed over the inner cover. It provides insulation, serves as a wick to absorb moisture, and helps create an upper entrance for ventilation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Entrance">
      <data key="d0">Upper Entrance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A ventilation port at the top of the hive, crucial for removing moisture and carbon dioxide. It also provides an exit for bees on warm winter days, especially if the lower entrance is blocked by snow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cluster">
      <data key="d0">Cluster</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The tight formation of bees inside the hive during cold weather. This behavior conserves heat and energy, but its state is affected by temperature variations, which in turn affects food consumption.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autumn">
      <data key="d0">Autumn</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The season preceding winter, identified as the time when a beekeeper should prepare the colony for the cold months ahead, such as by installing mouse guards and setting up insulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cleansing Trips">
      <data key="d0">Cleansing Trips</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Short flights bees take on warmer winter days to defecate outside the hive. The text mentions that an upper entrance allows for these trips, which are important for hive hygiene and bee health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metabolism">
      <data key="d0">Metabolism</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The biological process in bees that consumes oxygen and food stores to produce energy, heat, moisture, and carbon dioxide. Managing the byproducts of metabolism is a key aspect of wintering a hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouse Guard">
      <data key="d0">Mouse Guard</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Mouse Guard is a piece of protective equipment for the hive, likely used during winter. Its removal is specified as one of the first chores of the spring season."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A protective barrier, preferably made of metal, placed over the hive entrance to prevent mice from entering and nesting inside the hive during the fall and winter.&lt;SEP&gt;A protective device installed on a hive entrance in autumn to prevent mice from moving in for the winter, where they can damage the hive and harm the colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tar Paper">
      <data key="d0">Tar Paper</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A material mentioned as commonly used in the past for wrapping hives as a form of insulation against extreme winter conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weather Patterns">
      <data key="d0">Weather Patterns</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The atmospheric conditions that significantly affect the bee colony's behavior and survival. The text explains that consistent, cooler weather is ideal, while excessively cold weather immobilizes the cluster and unseasonably warm weather can cause the bees to consume too many food reserves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cleansing Flights">
      <data key="d0">Cleansing Flights</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An important activity where bees fly out of the hive on warm winter days to void waste and remove the bodies of dead bees. The occurrence of these flights is a key indicator of colony health for the beekeeper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emergency Feeding">
      <data key="d0">Emergency Feeding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The practice of providing supplementary food to a bee colony when its natural stores are insufficient. The text details this as a critical intervention by the beekeeper, with two primary methods discussed: feeding syrup and feeding candy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feeding Syrup">
      <data key="d0">Feeding Syrup</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific method of emergency feeding using a heated two-to-one mixture of sugar and water. The text advises squirting the syrup into empty frames and placing them near the brood nest for best results in cold weather.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feeding Candy">
      <data key="d0">Feeding Candy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An alternative method of emergency feeding that involves creating a hard candy from boiled sugar and water. This candy is poured into thin sheets and placed in the hive. The text warns that using burnt sugar for this process can be lethal to the bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Cluster">
      <data key="d0">The Cluster</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tight ball of bees formed inside the hive during winter to maintain warmth. The cluster's position and movement are critical; it starts at the bottom of the hive and moves upward, consuming honey. Its proximity to the top indicates low food reserves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dead Bees">
      <data key="d0">Dead Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The bodies of bees that have died inside the hive. They can block entrances and are typically removed by other bees during cleansing flights on warm days.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Winter Thaw">
      <data key="d0">Winter Thaw</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A short period of warm weather during February or March. These thaws are important because they allow the bees to take cleansing flights to void waste and clean the hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hard Ball Candy">
      <data key="d0">Hard Ball Candy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Hard Ball Candy is a type of solid bee food made by heating a sugar mixture to 260°-270°F. It is fed to bees during winter by placing it directly on the frames to provide a long-lasting food source."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dry Granulated Sugar">
      <data key="d0">Dry Granulated Sugar</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Dry Granulated Sugar is an emergency food for bees, used when food reserves are very low. It is spread on the hive's inner cover and requires bees to have access to moisture to consume it. It is used for subsistence and not stored."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Meaningful Nectar Flow">
      <data key="d0">First Meaningful Nectar Flow</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The First Meaningful Nectar Flow is a key event in spring when enough natural nectar becomes available for bees to collect and store. It signals the end of the need for supplementary feeding and the start of honey production season."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spring Chores">
      <data key="d0">Spring Chores</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Spring Chores are a series of essential tasks performed by the beekeeper once the first nectar flow begins. These include removing winter insulation, cleaning the hive's bottom board, and checking the condition of the bee colony."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dandelions">
      <data key="d0">Dandelions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Dandelions are a type of flowering plant that serves as a common natural signal in many areas for the start of the first meaningful nectar flow of the season."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Area / Geography">
      <data key="d0">Local Area / Geography</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"Local Area / Geography refers to the specific geographic location and climate of the beekeeper. This determines the timing of seasons, the start of the nectar flow, and the types of local bee forage available, such as dandelions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollen Patties">
      <data key="d0">Pollen Patties</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Pollen Patties are a man-made food source for bees, created by mixing pollen substitute with sugar syrup to form a dough. They are placed inside the hive to provide nutrition, but they can dry out and become rock hard, requiring monitoring by the beekeeper."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Surpluses">
      <data key="d0">Honey Surpluses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Honey Surpluses refer to the excess honey produced by the bees beyond their own needs. The beekeeper can start to anticipate these surpluses once the spring nectar flow begins and supplemental feeding is no longer necessary."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reversing Hive Bodies">
      <data key="d0">Reversing Hive Bodies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific spring management technique where the beekeeper swaps the positions of the hive bodies. This is done to provide the upwardly-moving bee cluster with empty space to expand into, but it must be done with caution to avoid splitting the brood nest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The New Season">
      <data key="d0">The New Season</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the bee colony's second year, which presents new and more complex challenges for the beekeeper. During this period, issues like swarm control, diseases, and mites become more prominent, requiring the beekeeper to increase their knowledge and skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Removing The Crop">
      <data key="d0">Removing The Crop</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of harvesting honey from the hive. The text describes this as a key beekeeping operation that involves two phases: clearing the bees from the honey supers at the hive, and later extracting the honey from the comb indoors. Several methods for clearing bees are mentioned, such as using a bee brush.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Disease And Mite Control">
      <data key="d0">Disease And Mite Control</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical component of beekeeping focused on preventing and managing pests and diseases within the colony. The text notes that this becomes an emphasized aspect of beekeeping that the keeper must address in 'The New Season'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burr Comb">
      <data key="d0">Burr Comb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extra honeycomb built by bees in non-standard locations within the hive to increase honey storage capacity. The beekeeper is instructed to remove it to prevent difficulties when opening the hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen Excluders">
      <data key="d0">Queen Excluders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific piece of beekeeping equipment mentioned as something a beekeeper must learn to use during 'The New Season', implying its role in more advanced hive management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Brush">
      <data key="d0">Bee Brush</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A basic beekeeping tool used to gently brush bees off frames. Its primary use described in the text is during honey harvest.&lt;SEP&gt;A basic beekeeping tool used to manually brush live bees off frames, primarily during honey harvest. Its use can be tedious for large operations and messy if it contacts uncapped honey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fall">
      <data key="d0">Fall</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The season when bees prepare for winter by organizing the hive, positioning the cluster at the bottom and storing honey above it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Stores">
      <data key="d0">Honey Stores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The supply of honey that bees accumulate and store in the hive. This is their food source for winter and is what the beekeeper harvests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Porter Bee Escape">
      <data key="d0">Porter Bee Escape</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of one-way exit device installed in an inner cover that allows bees to leave a honey super but prevents their return. It requires at least 24 hours to be effective and its function can be impeded by improper spring adjustment or the presence of brood in the super.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Escape">
      <data key="d0">Bee Escape</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A general category of beekeeping devices that create a one-way exit for bees to move from a honey super down into the main hive body. The Porter bee escape is a well-known type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Escape Board">
      <data key="d0">Escape Board</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A board that allows bees a one-way passage out of a super. It works on the same principle as a Porter bee escape, with some designs using a simple maze structure without moving parts to prevent bees from re-entering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fume Board">
      <data key="d0">Fume Board</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A beekeeping tool, also known as an acid board, that is used with a foul-smelling liquid to drive bees out of a honey super. It is most effective on warm days and with single shallow supers.&lt;SEP&gt;A piece of equipment used with a foul-smelling liquid to clear bees from honey supers. The text warns to store it and the associated liquid outside the house due to the strong, persistent odor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blower">
      <data key="d0">Blower</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tool, such as a household leaf blower, that is described as a surprisingly effective way to remove bees from honey supers. Its use involves directing airflow between the frames to push the bees out, though its effectiveness is reduced by burr and bridge comb.&lt;SEP&gt;A tool, such as a household leaf blower, that is used to forcefully and effectively blow bees off frames and out of honey supers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Harvest">
      <data key="d0">Honey Harvest</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of collecting honey from beehives, which necessitates clearing bees from the honey supers. The text describes various tools and methods, such as the bee brush and escape boards, that are used for this purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Super">
      <data key="d0">Super</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A box-like component of a beehive, placed above the main hive body, where bees store surplus honey. Supers contain frames and are the primary focus of honey harvesting activities, requiring bees to be cleared from them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Foul-Smelling Liquid">
      <data key="d0">Foul-Smelling Liquid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A chemical repellent, available from bee equipment suppliers, that is applied to a Fume Board. Its strong odor drives bees out of the super.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carbolic Acid">
      <data key="d0">Carbolic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An older type of foul-smelling liquid historically used with Fume Boards (then called acid boards) to drive bees from supers. Modern preparations are now more common.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Equipment Suppliers">
      <data key="d0">Bee Equipment Suppliers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of commercial vendor that provides specialized materials for beekeeping. The text mentions them as the source for square plastic boxes for packaging comb honey and for pollen-trapping apparatus."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Bee equipment suppliers are businesses that provide specialized materials for beekeeping, such as the square plastic boxes used for packing cut comb honey.&lt;SEP&gt;Commercial entities that provide beekeeping equipment. They are mentioned as the source for modern foul-smelling liquids used with fume boards, which are less likely to taint honey than carbolic acid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cart">
      <data key="d0">Cart</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A piece of equipment, such as a wheelbarrow or wagon, used during honey harvest to hold spare supers and bee-free frames.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Harvesting">
      <data key="d0">Honey Harvesting</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The overall process of removing honey-filled supers from a beehive. It involves clearing bees from the supers and then protecting the harvested honey from bees and environmental factors like temperature and humidity before extraction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Extraction">
      <data key="d0">Honey Extraction</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Honey extraction is the central process described in the text, involving the removal of honey from the honeycomb. It is characterized as a messy and sticky operation that requires significant time, a dedicated workspace like a kitchen, and specialized equipment.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of removing honey from the combs within the supers, described as the 'last big chore of the season.' The primary method is using an extractor, with an alternative being the crushing and straining of the comb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extractor">
      <data key="d0">Extractor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A machine used to remove honey from frames by spinning them. They come in various sizes (measured by frame capacity) and two main styles: tangential and radial. They typically have a reservoir and a honey gate for draining.&lt;SEP&gt;A piece of equipment that uses centrifugal force to whirl frames and remove honey from the comb. It is described as the best and most common method for honey extraction.&lt;SEP&gt;An extractor is a key piece of equipment for honey extraction. It is described as expensive (starting over $200), and the text suggests alternatives to individual purchase, such as cooperative buying, building one from plans, or renting one from suppliers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burr and Bridge Comb">
      <data key="d0">Burr and Bridge Comb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wax structures built by bees between frames or other hive parts. Their presence makes it difficult to clear bees from supers as they provide hiding places. Breaking them during super removal causes honey to drip.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brush">
      <data key="d0">Brush</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tool recommended to always have on hand during honey harvesting, as it is used to remove the last few bees from the supers when other methods are not completely effective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Robbing">
      <data key="d0">Honey Robbing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A behavior where agitated bees, from the harvested hive and other colonies, are attracted by the smell of honey and attempt to steal it back. The text warns this can lead to the loss of the entire crop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crushing and Straining">
      <data key="d0">Crushing and Straining</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An alternative method for honey extraction that does not require an extractor. It involves crushing the honey-laden comb in a mesh bag or strainer. It is described as a messy process that destroys the comb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dehumidifier">
      <data key="d0">Dehumidifier</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An appliance recommended for use in the honey storage area if the supers must be stored for a long time before extraction. It helps maintain or lower the moisture content of the honey to prevent fermentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Fermentation">
      <data key="d0">Honey Fermentation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A negative process where honey spoils due to high moisture content. The text explains that honey is hygroscopic and can absorb atmospheric moisture once removed from the hive, potentially leading to fermentation if not stored properly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kitchen">
      <data key="d0">Kitchen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A kitchen is mentioned as a facility that can be established for processing. The cost of setting up a kitchen is a factor that will initially reduce profits from a processing operation."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The kitchen is mentioned as a common but potentially problematic location for a beekeeper to perform honey extraction, especially for beginners. It is noted that using the kitchen for this purpose requires extensive cleanup.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crushing Method">
      <data key="d0">Crushing Method</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The crushing method is an alternative to using an extractor for honey extraction. It is described as a major disadvantage due to its messiness and the fact that it destroys the honeycomb, forcing bees to rebuild.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uncapping Knife">
      <data key="d0">Uncapping Knife</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An uncapping knife is a tool used to remove the wax cappings from the honeycomb before extraction. The text mentions that a simple serrated bread or carving knife can suffice for small jobs, while more sophisticated options include electric knives or planes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capping Scratcher">
      <data key="d0">Capping Scratcher</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A beekeeping tool with points used to uncap brood cells and lift out pupae for the purpose of inspecting them for Varroa mites.&lt;SEP&gt;A capping scratcher is a handy tool used to break open wax cappings in low or uneven spots on the honeycomb that an uncapping knife might miss, ensuring all honey can be extracted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cappings Container">
      <data key="d0">Cappings Container</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A cappings container is a tub or pail used to catch the wax cappings as they are removed. It should be wide and ideally have a strainer at the bottom to allow the significant amount of honey adhering to the cappings to drain and be collected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Strainer">
      <data key="d0">Strainer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A strainer is a device used to filter honey after it comes from the extractor. It removes particles of wax, propolis, and other hive debris. The text describes using a coarse mesh initially, with the option of a second, finer filtering (e.g., 70-mesh nylon) for a cleaner product.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Gates">
      <data key="d0">Honey Gates</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A honey gate is a specialized valve or faucet designed for thick substances like honey. It provides a quick and clean shut-off to minimize dripping and is a key component for bottling tanks. They are available in plastic for small operations and brass for heavy-duty use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bottling Tank">
      <data key="d0">Bottling Tank</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bottling tank is a container used to hold strained honey for bottling. The text explains that a beekeeper can easily make one by fitting a plastic honey gate onto a 5- or 6-gallon plastic pail.&lt;SEP&gt;A container, which can be purchased or self-made from a 5- or 6-gallon plastic pail and a honey gate, used for holding strained and settled honey before bottling. It is considered essential to prevent spillage due to honey's viscosity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Equipment Suppliers">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Equipment Suppliers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Beekeeping equipment suppliers are businesses where beekeepers can purchase or rent necessary equipment, such as honey extractors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bakeries and Restaurants">
      <data key="d0">Bakeries and Restaurants</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Bakeries and restaurants are mentioned as potential sources for beekeepers to obtain used food-quality pails for free or at a small cost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wax Particles">
      <data key="d0">Wax Particles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wax particles are small pieces of wax that become mixed with honey during the extraction process. They are considered a form of debris that needs to be removed by straining to ensure the purity of the final honey product.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Debris">
      <data key="d0">Hive Debris</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hive debris is a general term for the various impurities found in honey after it is extracted from the comb. This includes particles of wax, bits of propolis, and occasionally parts of bees. This debris must be removed through straining or skimming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Foundation">
      <data key="d0">New Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">New foundation is the man-made base structure that beekeepers provide to bees to encourage them to build a new comb. It becomes necessary to provide this in the following season if the original comb is destroyed during honey extraction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uncapping">
      <data key="d0">Uncapping</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Uncapping is a crucial preparatory step in the honey extraction process. It involves using a tool, like a special knife, to remove the wax seals (cappings) from the honeycomb cells to allow the honey to flow out.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wax Cappings">
      <data key="d0">Wax Cappings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wax cappings are the thin wax seals that bees build over cells of ripe honey. These must be sliced off during the uncapping process. A large amount of honey adheres to these cappings, which can be collected separately.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Plane">
      <data key="d0">Electric Plane</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An electric plane is a powered tool used for uncapping honeycomb, functioning as an alternative to an electric knife. The text notes that some beekeepers find it too heavy and awkward to use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skimming">
      <data key="d0">Skimming</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Skimming is a method for purifying honey that serves as an alternative to immediate straining. It involves letting the honey sit in a warm room for 24 hours, which causes impurities like wax and debris to rise to the surface where they can be skimmed off.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Filter Cloth">
      <data key="d0">Filter Cloth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A filter cloth, such as 70-mesh nylon, is a material used for fine straining of honey. It is used after an initial coarse straining or skimming to remove any remaining very fine particles for a cleaner final product.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food-Quality Containers">
      <data key="d0">Food-Quality Containers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Food-quality containers are pails and tanks specifically manufactured to safely hold food products. The text emphasizes that beekeepers must exclusively use these types of containers for honey to avoid contamination and should verify their past usage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Gate">
      <data key="d0">Honey Gate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A valve designed to quickly and cleanly shut off the flow of honey with minimal dripping. It is an essential component for bottling tanks and extractors, and is available in plastic for small operations and brass for heavy-duty applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Extracting Process">
      <data key="d0">The Extracting Process</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The multi-step process of removing honey from comb frames. It involves using an extractor, managing vibration, draining the honey, and minimizing air bubbles. The efficiency of the process can be improved by warming the honey beforehand.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tangential Extractor">
      <data key="d0">Tangential Extractor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A common style of extractor for small operations where frames are positioned at an angle. It extracts honey from only one side of a frame at a time, requiring the frames to be manually reversed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radial Extractor">
      <data key="d0">Radial Extractor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A style of extractor, typically used in larger operations, where frames are positioned along a radius. It is more efficient as it spins honey from both sides of the frames at once.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Granulation">
      <data key="d0">Honey Granulation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural process, also known as crystallization, that occurs in most honeys over time due to the combination of sugars. It does not damage the honey and can be reversed by applying heat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Storing Your Honey">
      <data key="d0">Storing Your Honey</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of storing honey after extraction, particularly in large quantities. A key consideration during storage is managing honey granulation, which is a natural occurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plastic Pail">
      <data key="d0">Plastic Pail</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 5- or 6-gallon plastic container that can be used to construct a DIY honey bottling tank by fitting it with a honey gate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beginners">
      <data key="d0">Beginners</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of beekeepers, typically new to the practice, who most often use smaller two-, three-, or four-frame tangential extractors.&lt;SEP&gt;Novice beekeepers who are the target audience for introductory beekeeping books and are advised to choose recent publications from their own country.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heat">
      <data key="d0">Heat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An element used to manage honey. Applying heat makes honey less viscous for easier extraction and can reverse the process of granulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Heat is presented as a method to control honey granulation by dissolving seed crystals. However, it is also described as a potential source of damage to honey's quality. The text specifies different temperature guidelines for large commercial producers (160°F) and small operators (120°F).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bubbles And Foaming">
      <data key="d0">Bubbles And Foaming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An undesirable result of splashing honey during processing. While not harmful, these air bubbles are considered unsightly in the final bottled product and can be removed by letting the honey settle and skimming the top.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Granulation">
      <data key="d0">Granulation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Granulation, or crystallization, is a natural process that occurs in most honey due to its sugar composition. It is influenced by time and temperature, with 57°F (14°C) being the optimal temperature for it to occur. The process can be delayed or reversed by applying controlled heat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large Commercial Honey Producers And Packers">
      <data key="d0">Large Commercial Honey Producers And Packers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Large commercial honey producers and packers are organizations that process and package honey on a large scale. They use controlled, rapid heating and cooling processes, with temperatures reaching 160°F (71°C), to prevent granulation in their products almost indefinitely.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extracted Honey">
      <data key="d0">Extracted Honey</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extracted honey is honey that has been removed from the wax comb. The text presents it as the more practical and recommended type of honey crop for beginner beekeepers to focus on in their first year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Operators">
      <data key="d0">Small Operators</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small operators are individual or small-scale beekeepers. The text contrasts their honey processing capabilities with those of large commercial producers, advising them to never heat honey above 120°F (49°C) to avoid damaging it, as they lack the equipment for rapid cooling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surplus Honey Crop">
      <data key="d0">Surplus Honey Crop</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The surplus honey crop is the amount of honey produced by a colony beyond its own needs, which can be harvested by the beekeeper. The text notes that for a new colony in its first year, this crop is expected to be small or nonexistent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wired Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Wired Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wired foundation is a type of frame base used in beekeeping for producing extracted honey. The embedded wires provide structural support, making the comb strong enough to withstand the extraction process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comb Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Comb Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Comb foundation is a specific type of thin, white wax foundation without embedded wires, manufactured for the purpose of producing comb honey. Since it is intended to be eaten with the honey, it is handled differently from wired foundation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microscopic Seed Crystals">
      <data key="d0">Microscopic Seed Crystals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microscopic seed crystals are naturally present in honey and are the starting points for the granulation process. The application of heat is used to dissolve and destroy these crystals to delay or prevent granulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Products">
      <data key="d0">Hive Products</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A general category for materials produced or collected by bees. The text explicitly names beeswax, pollen, propolis, royal jelly, and venom as 'other hive products' besides honey. These products have various uses, primarily as natural food or health supplements."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Venom">
      <data key="d0">Venom</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A product from the beehive, specifically from bees, that is frequently discussed as one of the five 'other hive products' alongside beeswax, pollen, propolis, and royal jelly."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apitherapy">
      <data key="d0">Apitherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The collective term for health treatments that involve products from the beehive, such as honey, beeswax, and pollen. It is described as a growing field of interest, though its efficacy is not universally accepted by the medical community."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A practice associated with bees, likely therapeutic, as it is linked to the American Apitherapy Society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Apitherapy Society">
      <data key="d0">American Apitherapy Society</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An organization in the United States dedicated to advancing the study and application of Apitherapy. Its mailing address is provided in the text."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An organization associated with the practice of apitherapy, which involves using bee products for therapeutic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="North Hartland, VT">
      <data key="d0">North Hartland, VT</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The geographic location (town and state) of the American Apitherapy Society, as specified by its P.O. Box address."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Community">
      <data key="d0">Medical Community</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A professional group representing mainstream medicine. The text notes that this community is largely in disagreement with the health claims associated with Apitherapy."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The medical community is the professional body of doctors and researchers. According to the text, this community does not widely accept the health benefits of bee venom, aside from its established use in treating sting allergies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Square Plastic Boxes">
      <data key="d0">Square Plastic Boxes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific type of packaging available from bee equipment suppliers, recommended for packing cut comb honey for aesthetic reasons and perfect fitting."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wired Extracting Comb">
      <data key="d0">Wired Extracting Comb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of durable, reinforced honeycomb frame used in beekeeping. It is mentioned as a standard of strength that new comb can achieve over several years as bees fortify it with wax and propolis."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cappings">
      <data key="d0">Cappings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Wax seals from the honey extracting process. They are identified as a primary source for yielding high-quality, light-colored beeswax when melted down and cleaned."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burr And Bridge Comb">
      <data key="d0">Burr And Bridge Comb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Excess comb that bees build between frames or other hive parts. Scrapings of this comb are noted as a source of high-quality beeswax."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brood Comb">
      <data key="d0">Brood Comb</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of comb from the hive used for raising bee larvae. When it is old and melted down, it produces dark, impure wax, which is considered of lower quality than wax from cappings."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pollen-Trapping Apparatus">
      <data key="d0">Pollen-Trapping Apparatus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A special piece of equipment, available from bee supply sources, that is used to collect pollen from bees at the hive entrance."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Food Supplement">
      <data key="d0">Health Food Supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A market category for products sold for their nutritional benefits. The text states that hive products, especially pollen, are sold as health food supplements, while also questioning their efficacy and safety."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Venom">
      <data key="d0">Bee Venom</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bee venom is a substance collected from bees that has a medically accepted value for desensitizing individuals with allergic reactions to stings. It is also anecdotally used for arthritis, but this and other health benefits are not widely accepted by the medical community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drug Companies">
      <data key="d0">Drug Companies</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Drug companies are organizations that seek and use bee venom for medical purposes. They have established market channels to acquire the small volumes of venom needed to produce desensitization treatments for bee sting allergies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Apiary Inspection Programs">
      <data key="d0">State Apiary Inspection Programs</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">State apiary inspection programs are governmental bodies that were created to combat serious bee diseases like American Foulbrood. They serve as a resource for beekeepers to diagnose and manage diseases in their hives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Foulbrood (AFB)">
      <data key="d0">American Foulbrood (AFB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the provided descriptions, American Foulbrood (AFB) is the most serious brood disease affecting honey bees. It is a highly infectious bacterial disease caused by highly persistent, spore-forming bacteria that specifically infects the brood (larvae) stage. The spores are spread to the larvae through their food, causing them to die, putrefy, and emit a sour odor. The dead larvae then dry into a hard, black scale that is difficult to remove and contaminates the hive. If left untreated, American Foulbrood is deadly and will ultimately lead to the death of the colony, which has prompted the establishment of inspection programs to manage it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Apiary Inspector">
      <data key="d0">State Apiary Inspector</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A state apiary inspector is a government official and expert who can be contacted by beekeepers for help in diagnosing puzzling or persistent hive conditions. Their role is part of a state-level program to manage bee diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brood Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Brood Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brood diseases are a classification of illnesses that specifically affect the brood (larvae) of honey bees. The text identifies them as generally being the most devastating type of disease for a colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adult Bee Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Adult Bee Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adult bee diseases are a classification of illnesses that affect mature honey bees. While they can have serious effects, the text suggests they are generally less devastating to a colony than brood diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terramycin">
      <data key="d0">Terramycin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Terramycin is an antibiotic, specifically oxytetracycline hydrochloride, used in beekeeping to treat bacterial bee diseases. It is indicated for treating both American Foulbrood (AFB) and European Foulbrood, but it is noted as being ineffective against the viral disease Sacbrood. In the United States, Terramycin is the only antibiotic specifically labeled and approved for use in beehives to combat American Foulbrood. It works by preventing infection in the brood rather than by killing the AFB spores, which necessitates continuous, preventative treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066653911102a71446dc7a3246a0c193</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Supply House">
      <data key="d0">Bee Supply House</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A recommended source for beekeepers to purchase beekeeping supplies, specifically mentioned as the best place to obtain the proper formulation of Terramycin for treating honey bees."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colony Infection">
      <data key="d0">Colony Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The event of a bee colony becoming infected with American Foulbrood. The text notes that if discovered in its early stages, the infection may be treatable, but advanced infection often requires the entire colony to be destroyed."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Destruction of Diseased Equipment">
      <data key="d0">Destruction of Diseased Equipment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A biosecurity measure for handling a colony that has died from or been destroyed due to American Foulbrood. The process involves killing any remaining bees and burning the frames, comb, and dead bees in a pit to eliminate the source of infection."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spores">
      <data key="d0">Spores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The infectious agent of American Foulbrood. They are carried in brood food, germinate in the larval gut, and are highly resilient, contaminating the comb and equipment even after the colony dies."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inspection Programs">
      <data key="d0">Inspection Programs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Formal programs designed for the detection of bee diseases like American Foulbrood. The text mentions them as an important, but not solely sufficient, method for discovering infections, urging beekeepers to also conduct personal inspections."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Regulations">
      <data key="d0">State Regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Rules and laws at the state level within the United States that govern the management of American Foulbrood. Some states prohibit any form of treatment and mandate the destruction of infected colonies."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oxytetracycline Hydrochloride">
      <data key="d0">Oxytetracycline Hydrochloride</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The chemical name for the antibiotic Terramycin. It is the active ingredient used in the prevention and treatment of American Foulbrood in honey bees."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Foulbrood">
      <data key="d0">European Foulbrood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bacterial disease affecting bee larvae, considered less serious than American Foulbrood. Symptoms include discolored, decomposing larvae that die before cell capping. Treatment options include adding capped brood, requeening, using Terramycin, or, as a last resort, burning the hive.&lt;SEP&gt;European Foulbrood (EFB) is a specific bacterial disease that afflicts the larvae of honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Foulbrood">
      <data key="d0">American Foulbrood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A serious bacterial disease affecting bee larvae, mentioned for comparison with European Foulbrood. It is characterized by a longer 'ropy thread' from decomposing larvae and requires burning of hive components for sanitation.&lt;SEP&gt;American Foulbrood is a severe and contagious bacterial disease that affects honey bee larvae.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sacbrood">
      <data key="d0">Sacbrood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sacbrood is a viral stress disease that specifically affects the brood of honey bees. This infection impacts the larvae after their cells are capped, causing them to die before pupation. A characteristic sign of the disease is that the infected larvae form a fluid-filled sac. There is no chemical treatment available for Sacbrood; instead, management focuses on keeping colonies strong. This can be accomplished by requeening the colony or by introducing young bees and capped brood from a healthy hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chalkbrood">
      <data key="d0">Chalkbrood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chalkbrood is a fungal disease that affects honey bee brood. When larvae are infected, they die and mummify into hard, chalk-like pellets or lumps. This disease is widespread, particularly in North America. Management and treatment techniques for Chalkbrood include requeening the colony, adding young bees from a healthy colony, and, in cases of heavy infection, removing and burning the affected frames.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burning">
      <data key="d0">Burning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sanitation and treatment method for bee diseases. It is used as a last resort for hives severely infected with European Foulbrood and American Foulbrood, and for frames heavily infected with Chalkbrood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Requeening">
      <data key="d0">Requeening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A hive management technique used to treat European Foulbrood, Sacbrood, and Chalkbrood. It involves replacing the queen to create a break in brood rearing, allowing worker bees to clean the hive.&lt;SEP&gt;Requeening is the management process of replacing an old, failing, or undesirable queen bee in a colony with a new one.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scorching With Propane Torch">
      <data key="d0">Scorching With Propane Torch</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method for sterilizing reusable hive equipment, such as bodies and supers, by scorching their inner surfaces after they have been scraped clean.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adding Capped Brood">
      <data key="d0">Adding Capped Brood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment method for European Foulbrood and Sacbrood. It involves adding frames of capped brood from a healthy colony to boost the population of young worker bees, which helps in cleaning the hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bacterium">
      <data key="d0">Bacterium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of microorganism identified as the cause of bee diseases such as European Foulbrood and American Foulbrood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virus">
      <data key="d0">Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An infectious agent that causes Sacbrood in bees. The text notes that there are no known chemical treatments for viral infections in bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fungus">
      <data key="d0">Fungus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of organism that forms a symbiotic association with plant roots, known as mycorrhiza, to help plants absorb essential nutrients and water from the soil."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A type of organism whose spores, when ingested, cause the fungal disease Chalkbrood in bee larvae.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Protozoa">
      <data key="d0">Protozoa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A single-celled organism that is the cause of Nosema disease, which is an infection of the gut of adult bees.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of microscopic organism that is identified as the direct cause of Nosema disease, an infection of the gut in adult honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boiling In Lye Water">
      <data key="d0">Boiling In Lye Water</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An alternative method for sterilizing hive equipment. It is presented as having a significant drawback, as lye is a dangerous substance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Chamber Sterilization">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Chamber Sterilization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An alternative method for sterilizing hive equipment by placing it in a radiation chamber. Its primary drawback is that this method is not always available to beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad055bc816d7df3212732523b38332d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fumidil B">
      <data key="d0">Fumidil B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The trade name for the medication fumagillin, which is used to treat Nosema disease. It is administered twice a year in sugar syrup to bring the infection under control, allowing the colony to thrive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Honey Bee Tracheal Mite">
      <data key="d0">The Honey Bee Tracheal Mite</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An internal parasite (_Acarapis woodii_) of the honey bee that lives and breeds in the bee's tracheal tubes. First identified in England in 1921, it has spread throughout the United States, causing severe colony losses. Its life cycle from egg to egg is 14 days.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="England">
      <data key="d0">England</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A country in the United Kingdom, mentioned as the geographical focus of the 'Country Smallholding' magazine.&lt;SEP&gt;The country for which Nicolas Lampkin's rotation designs, which are discussed in the text, were originally created.&lt;SEP&gt;The country where the honey bee tracheal mite was first identified in 1921.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mexico">
      <data key="d0">Mexico</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Identified as one of the source continents, along with Europe, for the tracheal mites that were introduced to North America.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EFB">
      <data key="d0">EFB</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A honey bee disease, likely an abbreviation for European Foulbrood, that can be managed with similar techniques as chalkbrood and sacbrood, such as requeening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fumagillin">
      <data key="d0">Fumagillin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The active medicinal ingredient used for treating Nosema disease in honey bees. It is commercially available under the trade name Fumidil B.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acarapis Woodii">
      <data key="d0">Acarapis Woodii</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acarapis woodii is the scientific name for the tracheal mite, a parasite that infests honey bees.&lt;SEP&gt;The scientific name for the honey bee tracheal mite, an internal parasite that inhabits and reproduces within the breathing tubes of honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Varroa Jacobsonii">
      <data key="d0">Varroa Jacobsonii</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The scientific name for the Varroa mite, an external parasite that attacks both adult honey bees and their brood by feeding on their hemolymph (blood).&lt;SEP&gt;The scientific name for the Varroa mite, an external parasite that infests and feeds on honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulatory Agencies">
      <data key="d0">Regulatory Agencies</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Governmental or official bodies that, according to the text, have reduced their focus on large-scale control of tracheal mites, shifting the responsibility for management to individual beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tracheal Mite Infestation">
      <data key="d0">Tracheal Mite Infestation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Tracheal Mite Infestation is the condition of a honey bee colony being parasitized by tracheal mites. Positive diagnosis requires microscopic inspection, but symptoms can include bees crawling at the hive entrance, poor winter clustering, and K-wing. This condition leads to higher bee mortality and can cause the entire colony to die, particularly in late winter."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menthol">
      <data key="d0">Menthol</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Menthol is a medication in crystalline form used to treat honey bee colonies for tracheal mites. It works by vaporizing in the hive and kills adult mites, but not eggs or larvae, requiring a 14-day continuous treatment period. Its application is temperature-dependent (60-80°F) and is disruptive to the colony, reducing foraging and brood production. It cannot be used when honey supers are on the hive."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Menthol is a medication for treating mites in beehives that requires 14 days of continuously warm weather to be effective. Its use is prohibited when honey supers are on the hive to prevent honey contamination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Miticur">
      <data key="d0">Miticur</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Miticur, with the active ingredient amitraz, is a contact miticide used to treat tracheal mites. It is delivered via impregnated plastic strips hung in the hive for at least 6 weeks. Bees come into direct contact with the strips, transferring the pesticide to the mites. It is applied after honey supers are removed and requires careful handling with protective gloves."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An approved chemical treatment material used against both tracheal and Varroa mites. Like Apistan, it is a contact miticide available as plastic strips. The text notes that using it for one type of mite provides protection against the other and it should be reserved for confirmed infestations.&lt;SEP&gt;Miticur is a medication used to control mite populations in beehives. It is notable for not requiring warm temperatures to be effective and is typically applied after honey supers are removed from the hive at the end of the season.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K-wing">
      <data key="d0">K-wing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"K-wing is a specific physical symptom that suggests a tracheal mite infestation in a honey bee. It is characterized by the bee's wings being angled out to the sides at rest, rather than folded along the abdomen, forming a shape similar to the letter 'K'."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northerly Regions">
      <data key="d0">Northerly Regions</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"Northerly Regions are geographic areas mentioned as places where honey bees have increased difficulty surviving the winter. The combination of normal winter stress and the life-shortening effects of tracheal mites makes this period especially deadly for colonies in these colder climates."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summer">
      <data key="d0">Summer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Summer is the season when the honey bee population normally peaks. It is also identified as the most logical time to treat with menthol due to the consistently warm temperatures required for the treatment to be effective, though this timing conflicts with the period when honey supers are on the hive."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amitraz">
      <data key="d0">Amitraz</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Amitraz is the active chemical ingredient in Miticur, a contact miticide formulated into plastic strips for the treatment of tracheal mites in honey bee colonies."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Re-infestation">
      <data key="d0">Re-infestation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Re-infestation is the event where a previously treated honey bee colony becomes infested with tracheal mites again. This is a significant problem because treatments are not 100% effective and nearby untreated hives serve as a constant source of mites, necessitating annual treatment."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dissection and Microscopic Inspection">
      <data key="d0">Dissection and Microscopic Inspection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Dissection and Microscopic Inspection is the definitive diagnostic procedure required for a positive identification of a tracheal mite infestation. This laboratory method is necessary because the mites are microscopic and live entirely within the bee's respiratory system."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spring Requeening">
      <data key="d0">Spring Requeening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Spring Requeening is a beekeeping management technique mentioned as a way to mitigate mite problems. It involves replacing a colony's queen in the spring to boost egg-laying and population growth, allowing the bee population to outpace the mite population."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menthol Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Menthol Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Menthol Treatment is the specific 14-day process of using 50 grams of vaporizing menthol crystals to kill adult tracheal mites. Its success is highly dependent on a continuous warm temperature range and it is known to be disruptive to the colony's normal activities."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honey Crop">
      <data key="d0">Honey Crop</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Honey Crop is the surplus honey produced by a bee colony that is intended for human consumption. The text highlights the critical importance of protecting it from contamination by ensuring no medications are used while honey supers are on the hive."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Protective Gloves">
      <data key="d0">Protective Gloves</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Protective Gloves are a piece of personal protective equipment. The text recommends their use when handling Miticur, as it is a pesticide and care should be taken to avoid direct contact."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tracheal Mite">
      <data key="d0">Tracheal Mite</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tracheal Mite is a parasite affecting honey bees, first discovered on the American continent ('this side of the Atlantic'). Its spread is now considered so widespread that control measures like restricting bee movement have been abandoned, leaving control to individual beekeepers. Its response to treatment can vary, possibly due to different strains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Honey Bee">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Honey Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A species of honey bee, _Apis cerana_, mentioned in the index.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern honey bee (Apis cerana) is the original host of the Varroa mite and is native to Southeast Asia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brood Cell">
      <data key="d0">Brood Cell</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A cell within a beehive where bee larvae develop. It is a critical part of the Varroa mite's life cycle, as the female mite enters the cell to lay eggs, and the new mites mature and mate inside before the host bee emerges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drone Brood">
      <data key="d0">Drone Brood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the male bee larvae and pupae within a hive. The Varroa mite shows a distinct preference for infesting drone brood cells over worker bee cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The male pupae of honey bees. Mites are easier to detect on drone brood, making the uncapping and inspection of drone cells a primary method for identifying infestations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Atlantic">
      <data key="d0">Atlantic</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Atlantic Ocean is used as a geographical reference point to indicate the arrival and discovery of the Tracheal mite in the Americas ('this side of the Atlantic').</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apis Mellifera Scutellata">
      <data key="d0">Apis Mellifera Scutellata</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A race of honey bee, also known as the African bee. It is a less suitable host for the Varroa mite because its shorter development time hinders the mite's ability to reach maturity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apis Mellifera Capensis">
      <data key="d0">Apis Mellifera Capensis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A race of honey bee, also known as the Cape bee. Similar to the African bee, it has a shorter development time, which makes it a less effective host for the Varroa mite.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ether Roll">
      <data key="d0">Ether Roll</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method for detecting Varroa mites on adult bees. It involves placing about 500 bees in a jar, using ether to stun them, and rolling the jar to make mites adhere to the moist sides for easy viewing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shaking Method">
      <data key="d0">Shaking Method</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A mite detection technique similar to the ether roll. It involves shaking a sample of bees vigorously in a jar with a liquid (such as alcohol or detergent) to dislodge mites, which are then found by straining the liquid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colony Damage and Mortality">
      <data key="d0">Colony Damage and Mortality</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The collective negative effects of a Varroa mite infestation on a honey bee colony, which can lead to the colony's death within 3 to 5 years if no control measures are taken.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Loss of Hemolymph">
      <data key="d0">Loss of Hemolymph</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of harm caused to individual bees by Varroa mites feeding on their 'blood' (hemolymph). This is described as a serious issue that contributes to bee weakness and weight loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Puncture Wound">
      <data key="d0">Puncture Wound</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical damage caused by a Varroa mite's bite, which serves as an entry point for secondary infections and diseases in the bee.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Deformities">
      <data key="d0">Physical Deformities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Developmental abnormalities in bees caused by mite infestation during the brood stage. Examples mentioned include asymmetrical wings, misshapen legs, and shortened abdomens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inspecting Brood">
      <data key="d0">Inspecting Brood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key method for detecting Varroa mites by uncapping and visually examining bee brood, particularly drone pupae, where mites are more obvious.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inspecting The Hive">
      <data key="d0">Inspecting The Hive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A mite detection method that involves examining the hive environment rather than bees directly, using tools like bottom board inserts to collect mites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drone Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Drone Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A beekeeping supply item, specifically a frame placed in the brood area to encourage bees to build drone comb, thereby concentrating drone brood in one place for easier mite inspection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bottom Board Inserts">
      <data key="d0">Bottom Board Inserts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A two-part tool used for inspecting mite infestations in a beehive. It consists of a sticky paper to catch mites and a screen to keep bees and larger debris off the paper. It can be used with chemical treatments like Apistan and Miticur or with non-chemical methods like tobacco smoke to monitor mite fall.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-part tool, typically a sheet of sticky paper or cardboard, placed at the bottom of a hive to collect and detect mites that fall off the bees, especially after a treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apistan">
      <data key="d0">Apistan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An approved chemical treatment material for mites, specifically the Varroa mite. It comes in the form of impregnated plastic strips placed in the hive and acts as a contact miticide that does not harm bees when used correctly. The text advises reserving its use for actual infestations to prevent mites from developing tolerance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-chemical treatment method used in conjunction with bottom board inserts to detect mites. Smoke from 2-3 grams of tobacco is blown into the hive, causing stunned mites to fall onto a sticky board for inspection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ants">
      <data key="d0">Ants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ants are described as a serious pest for honey bee colonies in certain areas, particularly the South. They can invade hives and lead to the colony's demise. Defense methods include raising hives on legs placed in oil or coated with sticky substances.&lt;SEP&gt;Insects that are mentioned as a potential problem or pest for honey bee colonies.&lt;SEP&gt;Insects that can be either a nonproblem or a serious pest for honey bees. While some species simply live on the inner cover without causing harm, other species, particularly in the South, can invade and destroy colonies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow Jackets">
      <data key="d0">Yellow Jackets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of predatory wasp mentioned in the beekeeping index as a significant threat to honey bees.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of wasp that is sometimes found near hives, primarily interested in stealing honey. Their impact is considered negligible in most cases, but certain species in other parts of the world are a serious problem for bee colonies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066653911102a71446dc7a3246a0c193</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mice">
      <data key="d0">Mice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A small rodent listed as one of the few pests or predators of real consequence to honey bees in North America.&lt;SEP&gt;Mice are mentioned as a type of predator or pest that can inhabit and damage beehives, especially during winter.&lt;SEP&gt;Mice are pests that seek shelter in beehives during the winter. They enter in the fall, chew through the comb to build nests, and can cause enough disruption to make the bee colony abscond. Metal entrance guards are the recommended defense.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wax Moths">
      <data key="d0">Wax Moths</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pest that can damage beehives, listed as a topic in the beekeeping index.&lt;SEP&gt;An insect listed as one of the few pests of real consequence to honey bees in North America.&lt;SEP&gt;Wax Moths are a serious pest that primarily affects weak bee colonies or stored beekeeping equipment. Their larvae burrow through comb, consuming impurities and leaving behind webbing and frass, which can destroy the comb and kill developing bees. They are vulnerable to freezing temperatures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066653911102a71446dc7a3246a0c193</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Earwigs">
      <data key="d0">Earwigs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A nonproblem creature that is often found living on the inner cover of a beehive. They are described as being interested in a dry, secure space and doing little harm to bees or honey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spiders">
      <data key="d0">Spiders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A nonproblem creature sometimes found under the rim of the outer cover of a beehive. They are seeking shelter and are not considered harmful to the bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Problem Creatures For Honey Bees">
      <data key="d0">Problem Creatures For Honey Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification for insects and animals that are harmful to honey bee colonies. The text identifies ants (in some regions), bears, skunks, mice, and wax moths as being in this category.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nonproblem Creatures For Honey Bees">
      <data key="d0">Nonproblem Creatures For Honey Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification for creatures that live in or around a beehive without causing significant harm. The text lists earwigs, some species of ants, spiders, and opossums in this category.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pests And Predators Of Honey Bees">
      <data key="d0">Pests And Predators Of Honey Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A general classification used in the text to describe creatures that are detrimental to honey bees. The text notes that the number of truly consequential pests and predators in North America is small, listing bears, skunks, mice, and wax moths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Opossums">
      <data key="d0">Opossums</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An animal that is sometimes found near beehives. While they have been reported as pests, the text clarifies that they apparently do no real damage and are considered a 'nonproblem creature'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Birds">
      <data key="d0">Birds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of animal that can prey on flying honey bees. The text mentions that this is generally not a significant problem for colonies, except possibly for queen breeders. The kingbird is mentioned as an example.&lt;SEP&gt;Animals mentioned as causing potential problems for beekeepers, likely by preying on bees.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of wildlife mentioned as beneficiaries of windbreaks, which provide them with nesting places. They are noted for feeding on insects in the fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kingbird">
      <data key="d0">Kingbird</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of bird mentioned as an example of a predator that preys on flying bees near a beeyard.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paradichlorobenzine (PDB)">
      <data key="d0">Paradichlorobenzine (PDB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Paradichlorobenzine (PDB) is a chemical, available as moth crystals, used to protect stored beekeeping equipment from wax moths. It is considered safe for bees as long as the equipment is aired out for about 24 hours before use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Napthalene">
      <data key="d0">Napthalene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Napthalene is a chemical moth repellent that is explicitly warned against for use in beekeeping. Its residue in the comb can be harmful to honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Brood">
      <data key="d0">Bee Brood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the immature stages of bees (eggs, larvae, pupae). The text mentions that maturing brood can become trapped in their cells and die due to the activities of wax moth larvae.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hive Management">
      <data key="d0">Hive Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A book by Richard E. Bonney, described as a seasonal guide to beekeeping tasks, published by Storey Publishing.&lt;SEP&gt;The set of practices employed by a beekeeper to maintain the health and vigor of a bee colony. The text emphasizes that good hive management is a primary defense against wax moths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066653911102a71446dc7a3246a0c193</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Africanized Bee">
      <data key="d0">The Africanized Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A hybrid bee resulting from the interbreeding of the African honey bee subspecies, _Apis mellifera scutellata_, with European honey bee races in the Americas. They are known for highly undesirable traits, including excessive stinging and swarming. Originating from an accidental release in Brazil in 1957, they have since spread across the Americas."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="_Apis mellifera scutellata_">
      <data key="d0">_Apis mellifera scutellata_</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A subspecies of honey bee from southern Africa, commonly known as the African bee. It is characterized by a low tolerance for disturbances, excessive stinging, and a high tendency to swarm. This subspecies is the African progenitor of the Africanized bee."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Honey Bees">
      <data key="d0">European Honey Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A general term for the various European races of _Apis mellifera_, such as Italians, Caucasians, and Carniolans, which are commonly kept by beekeepers in the Americas. These bees have interbred with _Apis mellifera scutellata_ to produce the Africanized bee."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experimental Breeding Program">
      <data key="d0">Experimental Breeding Program</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A research initiative that took place in Brazil in 1957. The program's goal was to introduce desirable traits from African bees (_Apis mellifera scutellata_) into the honey bee stock of Brazil. An accidental release of queens from this program led to the creation of the Africanized bee."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brazil">
      <data key="d0">Brazil</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">In 1957, the South American country of Brazil was the location where the African honey bee (Apis mellifera scutellata) was introduced for a research and experimental breeding program. These bees subsequently escaped and interbred with local European bee populations, leading to the creation of the Africanized bee. In addition to its role in entomological history, Brazil is also a significant player in global agriculture. The country is a source of meat imported into the U.S. and is noted for its move into ultra-large-scale industrial agriculture, with some farms reaching sizes of 70,000 to 80,000 acres.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e970626c7a63b367949cb5600957bcab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central America">
      <data key="d0">Central America</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The region through which the Africanized bee population migrated northward from South America."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beehive">
      <data key="d0">Beehive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A structure housing a bee colony, which is the target of predators. Skunks attack the entrance, while bears can completely destroy the hive and its woodenware to access the honey and brood inside."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="_Apis mellifera_">
      <data key="d0">_Apis mellifera_</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The scientific name for the common honey bee species, which includes various European races (like Italians, Caucasians) commonly kept by beekeepers in the Americas, as well as the African subspecies _Apis mellifera scutellata_."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skunk Defenses">
      <data key="d0">Skunk Defenses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Methods used by beekeepers to deter skunks from attacking hives. These include placing materials like rolled poultry netting or shingles studded with tacks in front of the hive to create uncomfortable footing for the predator."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Fence">
      <data key="d0">Electric Fence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The recommended defense mechanism against bear attacks on beehives. It is most effective when erected before a bear has already tasted the contents of a hive, as a determined bear may break through it otherwise."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Africanized Bees">
      <data key="d0">Africanized Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A hybrid of African and European honey bees, known for their highly defensive behavior. They react to disturbances more quickly, sting more persistently, and swarm more often than European bees. Their spread and management are the central topic of the text.&lt;SEP&gt;A hybrid of the European honey bee and the African subspecies, _Apis mellifera scutellata_. They are known for their highly defensive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Bees">
      <data key="d0">European Bees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A race of honey bees commonly used in beekeeping, which are generally less defensive than Africanized bees. They are interbreeding with the advancing Africanized bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Public Officials">
      <data key="d0">Public Officials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of authorities who may react to public panic by imposing regulations and restrictions on the beekeeping community, potentially without full understanding of the situation.&lt;SEP&gt;Government officials at various levels who are responsible for creating plans and regulations to deal with Africanized bees. The text warns they may react with panic if not properly informed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Canada">
      <data key="d0">Canada</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Canada is a country known for several distinct characteristics. It is the origin point for rust spores that wind can carry as far south as Texas, impacting wheat crops. While it is mentioned in connection with the northward spread of Africanized bees, their ability to survive Canada's cold winters remains uncertain. Canada is also home to a farmer who raises chickens specifically bred for fly-tying feathers, demonstrating a niche poultry market. The country is the location of "Off the Hook," a community-supported fishery focused on low-impact fishing methods. Furthermore, Vancouver, a city within Canada, boasts the largest green roof in the country at the Vancouver Convention Centre.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e9fc423425c82be2483c04c11e6577f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740808</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minnesota">
      <data key="d0">Minnesota</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Minnesota is a U.S. state located in the northern region. It is known for its native, lake-harvested wild rice, which was designated as its official state grain in 1977. The state is a key location for wild rice preservation and study, with efforts supported by its Department of Natural Resources and advocates like Winona LaDuke. Minnesota is home to the White Earth Land Recovery Project and the Anishinabeg people, with the White Earth Reservation also located within the state. Legal and legislative efforts by the Ojibwe to protect their traditional food sources are centered in Minnesota. The state also contains part of the Clinton-Boone-Lindley soil region and is the location of the Sustainable Farming Association of Minnesota, which provides information on sustainable farming. While mentioned as a hypothetical northern limit for the survival of Africanized bees, this is debated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Media">
      <data key="d0">Media</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Media is identified as a factor that can influence public perception through 'media hype', potentially hastening the perceived presence of Africanized bees and contributing to panic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Governments">
      <data key="d0">State Governments</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Individual state-level authorities that are responsible for creating plans to control and manage the arrival and spread of Africanized bees. The text notes that coordination between states is needed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southern States">
      <data key="d0">Southern States</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Southern States are a region where Growing Minds is working to foster the introduction of more local, fresh food in schools, engaging with six states in this effort."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A tier of states in the southern United States where it is strongly believed Africanized bees will be confined, as they are not expected to survive colder northern winters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c16ea96bc625614680cc6d8276e6ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Educators">
      <data key="d0">Educators</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of professionals, mentioned alongside researchers and public officials, who are actively working on the Africanized bee problem by helping to inform beekeepers, the public, and officials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beekeeping Community">
      <data key="d0">Beekeeping Community</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A collective group of individuals who keep and manage honey bees. This community faces challenges from public perception, potential regulations, and the need to protect their bees from environmental threats like pesticides.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insecticides">
      <data key="d0">Insecticides</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of pesticide identified as being of 'most concern' to beekeepers. They can be lethal to bees on contact in the field or can contaminate hive resources like nectar and pollen, leading to widespread death within the colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Orchards">
      <data key="d0">Orchards</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of location that is tempting for establishing a beeyard due to floral resources, but is considered a high-risk area because of the regular and varied application of pesticide sprays, which are harmful to honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Corn">
      <data key="d0">Corn</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Corn is a major grain and commodity crop with significant cultural, agricultural, and economic importance. It holds particular cultural and agricultural significance for indigenous groups like the Hopi and Navajo (Diné) people, serving as a sacred food source integral to their ceremonies and a metaphor for the human life cycle. For the Hopi, it is the central element of existence, symbolizing life itself, and is a fundamental part of daily prayers, ceremonial offerings, and spiritual sustenance. The failure of corn crops is considered a key sign of prophecy fulfillment.

Agriculturally, corn is mass-produced, sometimes at artificially low prices due to federal incentives, and used as a primary input for processed foods. It is also cultivated as feed for pigs, sometimes grazed directly, and as a source of winter income when sold as ear corn for seed, with its management integrated with livestock to save labor. Corn is used to illustrate the economic benefits of value-added processing, such as transforming it into cornmeal.

Corn cultivation requires specific conditions, including at least 20 inches of rain during a 150-day growing season with hot weather, and is best suited for Mollisol soils. Water requirement estimates vary, with one study listing 368 lbs of water per pound of dry matter and another at 271 lbs. As a nitrogen-using crop with a low biomass root system, it benefits from rotation with nitrogen-fixing crops like soybeans. Farmers often add N-P-K fertilizers when rotating corn. Planting corn after green-manure crops have been turned under is also beneficial. Windbreaks can increase yields.

Corn is a mesophyte with high water usage, transpiring up to 2 quarts of water per plant per day, especially during the silking phase. Research indicates that windbreaks can increase corn yields by 20%, and corn rows can be used as windbreaks for other crops like soybeans. It is a high-value row crop used in strip-cropping, alternated with crops like soybeans or hay. The "Corn Belt" is named for it. However, corn requires more fertile soils and is not ideal for the Clinton-Boone-Lindley soil areas in large quantities. A 1930s USDA study found that a cornfield absorbed only 69.4% of rainfall, less than grass crops.

Corn pollen is attractive to honey bees, posing a contamination risk due to insecticide spraying during pollen production. Corn is also grown at places like Greensgrow primarily for educational and entertainment purposes, to show city dwellers how food grows and as a fun height comparison for children, rather than for production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82a428e9ecd2ac447a8293c4680becb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Public Library">
      <data key="d0">Public Library</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An institution mentioned as an obvious and accessible source for beekeepers, especially beginners, to find books and other publications about beekeeping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
