    <node id="Bee Diseases and Pests">
      <data key="d0">Bee Diseases and Pests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bee Diseases and Pests are defined in the glossary as a significant concern for honey bees. Examples include American foulbrood (AFB), European foulbrood (EFB), Chalkbrood, Nosema, and the tracheal mite (Acarapis woodii).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="African Honey Bee">
      <data key="d0">African Honey Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The African honey bee (Apis mellifera scutellata) is a subspecies of honey bee noted for being brought to Brazil for research in 1957.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Honey Bee">
      <data key="d0">European Honey Bee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The European honey bee (Apis mellifera) is the scientific name for the species of bee most commonly kept by beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Foulbrood (EFB)">
      <data key="d0">European Foulbrood (EFB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of brood disease that affects honey bees, as listed in the index.&lt;SEP&gt;European Foulbrood (EFB) is a specific bacterial disease that can infect the brood stage of honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nosema">
      <data key="d0">Nosema</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nosema is a protozoan infection affecting the mid-gut of adult honey bees, as described in the glossary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vatroa Jacobsonii">
      <data key="d0">Vatroa Jacobsonii</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vatroa jacobsonii is a type of mite mentioned as a pest that affects honey bees. Note: This is likely a typo in the source for Varroa jacobsoni.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee Bread">
      <data key="d0">Bee Bread</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bee bread is a food source for bees, created by mixing collected pollen with honey and storing it in the hive's comb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Absconding">
      <data key="d0">Absconding</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Absconding is the event where an entire bee colony completely abandons its hive, usually due to unfavorable conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virgin Birth">
      <data key="d0">Virgin Birth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A biological phenomenon where certain male insects, such as honey bees, develop from eggs that have not been fertilized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pheromone">
      <data key="d0">Pheromone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A chemical substance secreted by bees and other animals. When sensed by others of the same species, it triggers specific actions or responses. Pheromones are spread within a bee colony through trophallaxis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trophallaxis">
      <data key="d0">Trophallaxis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The exchange of food that occurs constantly among the bees of a colony. This behavior is also a key mechanism for distributing pheromones and other information throughout the hive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="_Acarapis Woodii_">
      <data key="d0">_Acarapis Woodii_</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A species mentioned in the index, likely a pest or parasite affecting honey bees, as it is listed near other diseases and pests. It is also known as the tracheal mite.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dancing">
      <data key="d0">Dancing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary method of communication for honey bees. Different dances, such as the alarm dance, round dance, and wag-tail dance, convey specific information to other bees in the colony.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spiracle">
      <data key="d0">Spiracle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">External respiratory openings on the sides of a bee's thorax and abdomen through which it breathes. They connect to the tracheal tubes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thorax">
      <data key="d0">Thorax</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The second, or middle, division of an insect's body, located between the head and the abdomen. The bee's legs and wings are attached to the thorax.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tracheal Tubes">
      <data key="d0">Tracheal Tubes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A network of breathing tubes that begin at the spiracles and branch out throughout the bee's body, forming its respiratory system. They can be infested by tracheal mites (_Acarapis woodii_).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acid Board">
      <data key="d0">Acid Board</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A piece of beekeeping equipment, likely used for applying treatments or clearing bees from honey supers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="_Apis Mellifera Scutellata_">
      <data key="d0">_Apis Mellifera Scutellata_</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A subspecies of honey bee from Africa, known for its role in the lineage of Africanized bees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
