    <node id="Small Farm">
      <data key="d0">Small Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A small farm is a category of agricultural operation with several contested definitions based on acreage and income. One prominent definition suggests a small farm has 179 acres or fewer, or a gross income of $50,000 or less per year. Similarly, another source defines it as a farm grossing less than $50,000 a year. In contrast, the National Small Farm Commission offers a broader definition, classifying a small farm as any farm grossing $250,000 or less annually. In a more specific context, the scale of 2 to 80 acres is presented as the ideal size where draft animals can be an economically viable and practical alternative to tractors.

Small farms are a primary focus for many resources due to their specific needs. It is noted that most farms in America fall into this category, and their numbers are increasing in the United States. While often family farms, this is not always the case, and they may or may not practice sustainable agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2b2fd01aa45dadaa82a243d140ec5f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Management">
      <data key="d0">Farm Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, Farm Management is a comprehensive approach and critical tool for integrating all farming principles to ensure an operation runs smoothly, profitably, and successfully. It represents the practical application and synthesis of various strategies, including planning, marketing, and production. This process involves carefully considering a wide range of interconnected factors, such as crop diversity, rotations, reserves, production costs, machinery, land use, labor management, and soil maintenance. A central aspect of Farm Management is balancing the dual goals of taking from the soil for profit while simultaneously returning nutrients to maintain fertility, thereby ensuring the long-term sustainability of the farm. The Library of Congress also identifies Farm Management as a primary subject category, underscoring its importance in the agricultural field.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2b2fd01aa45dadaa82a243d140ec5f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
