    </node>
    <node id="Sustainability">
      <data key="d0">Sustainability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sustainability is a core concept and primary theme that appears in multiple contexts, including discussions by Councilman Conlin regarding the goat ordinance, within texts concerning agriculture and community development, and as a key element for successful farm management. It refers to ecologically sound and resource-conscious practices, as well as the long-term viability of systems, especially food systems, operating without depleting nonrenewable resources or degrading the environment. The concept emphasizes the importance of both large-scale and small-scale personal acts in contributing to a more sustainable urban environment. For farms, sustainability requires that enterprises be profitable, environmentally sound, and socially acceptable to ensure their long-term operations. It also encompasses the use of renewable energy, eco-friendly farming methods, recycling, composting, and supporting the local economy, as exemplified by Seeds of Solidarity farm and the North Quabbin Garlic and Arts Festival. Discussions around sustainability include sub-topics like 'gaining ground' and 'strategies for'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2b2fd01aa45dadaa82a243d140ec5f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cfeabc3f21e3329484ec062ac1c4b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrialization of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Industrialization of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical process where farming shifted from small, self-sufficient units to large, mechanized, and specialized operations using chemicals, hybrid seeds, and a 'Bigger is better' philosophy.&lt;SEP&gt;A major economic and social trend discussed in the book as the backdrop and challenge for small-scale farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2b2fd01aa45dadaa82a243d140ec5f5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.s. Department Of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">U.s. Department Of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A U.S. government agency that published 'A Citizen's Guide to Food Recovery' in 1999. This guide provides information and ideas for food recovery projects.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) is a U.S. government agency that defines who qualifies as a farmer based on annual sales of agricultural products. It has changed the definition of a farm nine times but lacks an official definition for different farm sizes.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Department of Agriculture is a U.S. federal government department. Howard W. 'Bud' Kerr, Jr. formerly worked there, and the text suggests its programs have neglected small farms and no longer provide the advice they once did.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740880</data>
    </node>
