    <node id="Missouri Farm">
      <data key="d0">Missouri Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">'Missouri Farm' was a magazine launched by Ron Macher in the early 1980s, aimed at farm families in the Midwest. It grew in popularity and was later renamed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Farm Today">
      <data key="d0">Small Farm Today</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Originally called "Missouri Farm," "Small Farm Today" is a magazine for the small farm community that was founded by Ron Macher in 1984. Macher, who also served as the publication's publisher, coined the term 'agripreneur' to describe the magazine's readership. Considered an "industry bellwether" that has been in circulation for over 15 years, "Small Farm Today" is a bimonthly publication providing how-to information on alternative and traditional crops, livestock, and direct marketing.

The magazine plays a significant role in guiding farmers, often receiving calls from its community seeking marketing advice. A readership study highlighted a trend of newcomers to the field, finding that 47 percent of its readers had been farming for less than five years. It was the readership's expressed interest in making small farms profitable that prompted the creation of the book "Making Your Small Farm Profitable." Key figures associated with the magazine include its editor, Paul Berg, and frequent contributor Steve Salt, a vegetable farmer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="America">
      <data key="d0">America</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">America is a nation grappling with complex issues related to its food system. It is described as having an "obesogenic" society, driven by an industrial agricultural system that promotes unhealthy food consumption and a "National Cheap Food Policy" that puts a cost/price squeeze on farmers, leading to significant public health and environmental problems. Despite being a "land of plenty," America faces the paradox of widespread food waste alongside 40 million hungry people who could be fed by redirecting this waste.

However, America is also the site of a vibrant and growing local food movement, with the goal of ensuring all citizens have easy access to healthy food. Over one-third of American households participate in food gardening, reflecting a grassroots effort towards agricultural and cultural revitalization. The agricultural and cultural revitalization efforts of Native Americans are presented as a powerful lesson for the rest of the nation. The text's focus on farming is set in America, which has nearly two million farms and a significant small farm community. New farmers are seen as vital to continuing to grow America's own food and strengthening local economies.

America's society is comprised of the four cornerstone groups (farmers, lenders, consumers, institutions) necessary for a sustainable community. It is the subject of historical analysis in books like "The Development of American Agriculture" and "The Unsettling of America." The 2009 survey "The Impact of Home and Community Gardening in America" by the National Gardening Association focuses on America. It is the country to which Allison Sosna returned after studying in Europe, with the goal of combining her passion for food with socially responsible work. It is also the country where Joel Salatin observes and rejects a cultural axiom that business growth requires the sacrifice of family and personal well-being. Ultimately, America is presented as a nation where positive community-level change is happening through its grassroots food movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fee18f76430d121ab613984069f209e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07373ff8a8e2c42149614780aa736a26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tragic Loss Of Many Family Farms">
      <data key="d0">Tragic Loss Of Many Family Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An event in the early 1980s characterized by the failure of numerous family farms. This event motivated Ron Macher to create his magazine to provide support and information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Making Your Small Farm Profitable">
      <data key="d0">Making Your Small Farm Profitable</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">'Making Your Small Farm Profitable' is the title of a book written by Ron Macher. It serves as a guide for novice and veteran farmers on how to apply business skills to make their farms profitable.&lt;SEP&gt;'Making Your Small Farm Profitable' is the title of the book being introduced in the text. It serves as a blueprint and a 'how-to' guide for farmers, offering a whole-farm planning approach based on the author's extensive experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Farms">
      <data key="d0">Small Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of farms, defined as 178 acres or fewer, whose numbers are increasing. They often focus on direct marketing to nearby cities and represent a growing segment of American agriculture.&lt;SEP&gt;Small Farms are a specific category of agricultural operation, defined as farms grossing less than $50,000 annually. They represent the majority of farms in America but are described as often being neglected by national programs.&lt;SEP&gt;Small farms are the primary subject of the text, which provides advice on appropriate machinery, tools, and practices for operations of this scale. The text discusses tractor sizes (30-50 horsepower) and tool categories (Category I) suitable for them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrialized Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Industrialized Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Industrialized Agriculture is a term for the modern, complex, and large-scale farming system. It represents the challenging environment that small farm operators face.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Departments Of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">State Departments Of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">State-level government bodies that are described as offering plentiful marketing advice but being of little help to newcomers regarding credit, cropping recommendations, and cultural information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Federal And State Government">
      <data key="d0">Federal And State Government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A collective term for the government bodies that, according to the text, have failed to provide the necessary information that small farm operators require, a failure which motivated Ron Macher's work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Farm Programs">
      <data key="d0">National Farm Programs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">National-level agricultural support programs which, according to the text, have often neglected the operators of small farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agricultural County Agents">
      <data key="d0">Agricultural County Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical source of advice for farmers that has diminished, with agents now admitting they only have time to service the declining number of full-time farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Described as a critical on-farm resource provided by the operator. It is gained through activities like reading and attending workshops and leads to improved farming procedures and income.&lt;SEP&gt;Described as the 'most important piece of farm equipment' in the modern era, essential for understanding complex agribusiness problems, making plans, and ensuring profitability.&lt;SEP&gt;Knowledge is described as the most important and best on-farm resource for a farmer. It guides management decisions and is acquired through reading, attending workshops, and learning from others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agribusiness Production">
      <data key="d0">Agribusiness Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A term for the production side of commercial farming. Understanding the complexities of agribusiness production is crucial for staying in business.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Journalism">
      <data key="d0">Journalism</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A field of expertise possessed by Ron Macher, which he combined with his farming experience to create and publish his magazine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sustainable Communities">
      <data key="d0">Sustainable Communities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ultimate goal that the author hopes will be fostered by the adoption of sustainable agriculture practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Business Theory">
      <data key="d0">Business Theory</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A field of academic study that many modern, specialist farmers are now trained in, highlighting the increasing complexity of the farming business.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sustainable Community">
      <data key="d0">Sustainable Community</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Sustainable Community, from an agricultural viewpoint, is a society where farmers, lenders, consumers, and institutions cooperate. The text argues that such a community requires sustainable agriculture to thrive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Author">
      <data key="d0">The Author</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, The Author is a multifaceted figure who is a farmer, publisher, and the narrator of the text. With 34 years of farming experience and 15 years as a publisher, he founded "Small Farm Today" magazine in 1984 and wrote the book 'Making Your Small Farm Profitable', published by Storey Publishing, to share his keys to success and help other farmers avoid costly mistakes.

The Author owns and operates an 80-acre farm in Missouri, which he also uses for research. He manages this farm despite significant health challenges, including diabetes and congestive heart failure. He identifies as an "agripreneur" and is a strong advocate for small, sustainable, and low-input farming. His philosophy emphasizes a knowledge-based, practical approach, diversification, direct sales, and viewing potential problems as resources, such as turning fallen treetops into profitable firewood. He believes practical solutions in agriculture are more important than official government definitions of "small farm" or "farmer."

On his farm, The Author integrates livestock and crops, implementing rotation practices that include corn, winter rye, hairy vetch, and tomatoes. He raises Katahdin Hair sheep, hogs for sausage, feeder pigs, and poultry. He also grows open-pollinated corn and has experience raising and selling catfish, trout, and specialty heirloom tomatoes to a deli. Furthermore, The Author encourages farmers to be involved in agricultural legislation and to become spokespeople for the agriculture industry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e410bfae74fb6896e8e0079e060cf83d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Storey Books">
      <data key="d0">Storey Books</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Storey Books is a book publisher recommended by the author as a source of useful materials and books on topics relevant to farmers, such as agriculture and gardening.&lt;SEP&gt;Storey Books is the publisher of 'Making Your Small Farm Profitable'. The author thanks its staff for their professionalism and ideas.&lt;SEP&gt;The publisher of the book, recognized for its professionalism and for employing Deborah Burns and Marie Salter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Rural Crisis In 1984">
      <data key="d0">First Rural Crisis In 1984</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The First Rural Crisis in 1984 was a significant event that highlighted the lack of information for small farmers, motivating the author to create 'Small Farm Today' magazine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paul Berg">
      <data key="d0">Paul Berg</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Paul Berg is the editor at 'Small Farm Today' magazine. He is credited with providing help, inspiration, and encouragement to the author, effectively prodding him to create the book.&lt;SEP&gt;The editor at Small Farm Today magazine. He is described as a 'prodder' who provided crucial help, editing skills, and suggestions to the author, playing a significant role in the book's completion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lenders">
      <data key="d0">Lenders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lenders are a cornerstone group of society who supply start-up capital to agripreneurs and processing facilities, enabling farmers to sell products directly.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the four key groups in agriculture, alongside farmers, consumers, and institutions. Their support is crucial for farming operations, and the text argues they must be educated and convinced of the benefits of sustainable practices for widespread change to occur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consumers">
      <data key="d0">Consumers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Consumers are a crucial stakeholder group within the food system and agricultural ecosystem. They are the end-users whose purchasing choices are significantly influenced by factors like the cost of food, often favoring subsidized, unhealthy options. However, they also represent a driving force behind the demand for safe, high-quality food, and are increasingly interested in understanding the origins and production methods of their food, thus shaping agricultural trends towards sustainability and transparency. Furthermore, consumers are essential to the economic viability of farms, as their purchases from farmers and institutions provide the necessary income for farm operations. Organizations like ASAP recognize consumers as the cornerstone of the local food movement, focusing on educating them and building their trust in local products to create sustainable demand. Educating consumers about food policies is seen as vital to inspire them to take action and facilitate positive policy changes within the food system. They are also the target of direct marketing, allowing farmers to sell high-quality products at retail prices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6824e03de6d996c4161ce46d959096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Institutions">
      <data key="d0">Institutions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Institutions, including universities, government agencies, and businesses, are a cornerstone group of society that create consumer goods, add value to products, and perform research.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the four major groups of importance to agriculture. For agriculture to evolve towards sustainability, institutions must be convinced, along with farmers, lenders, and consumers, that the changes are beneficial.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrial Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Industrial Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the provided descriptions, Industrial Agriculture is the dominant farming system that emerged from the Green Revolution. It is characterized by a narrow focus on achieving high productivity through technology and is heavily critiqued by figures like Michael Pollan and organizations such as the Union of Concerned Scientists for its significant hidden costs.

This system relies on taxpayer subsidies to produce food that is artificially cheap but often nutrient-poor. These practices lead to major externalized costs, including public health crises like obesity and severe environmental degradation, such as soil erosion, loss of biodiversity, and the pollution of waterways with nutrients. Economically, Industrial Agriculture is the prevailing model that creates immense pressure on small and new farmers, making it difficult for them to survive and forcing many out of business. The texts from which these descriptions are drawn present sustainable farming methods as a viable alternative to this system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Readership of Small Farm Today">
      <data key="d0">Readership of Small Farm Today</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The readership of 'Small Farm Today' magazine is the target audience for the publication and the group that specifically requested the author to write a book on making small farms profitable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Missouri">
      <data key="d0">University of Missouri</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A university that hosts the Missouri Alternative Center, providing information packets on agricultural alternatives.&lt;SEP&gt;An academic institution where Jim Gerrish, an intensive-grazing expert, is based.&lt;SEP&gt;An institution whose Rural Sociology Department conducted a study in the 1950s to understand why people, both large and small farmers, choose to farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
