      <data key="d0">1997 Census of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific government census cited as the source for the statistic that 79 percent of all farms in the United States are classified as small farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rural Sociology Department At The University Of Missouri">
      <data key="d0">Rural Sociology Department At The University Of Missouri</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific academic department within the University of Missouri. It is credited with conducting a significant study in the 1950s that questioned farmers about their reasons for choosing to farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University Of Missouri Study">
      <data key="d0">University Of Missouri Study</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A research study conducted in the 1950s by the Rural Sociology Department at the University of Missouri. The study surveyed both large and small farmers to determine their motivations, concluding that lifestyle factors were the predominant reasons.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doanes Agricultural Service Study">
      <data key="d0">Doanes Agricultural Service Study</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A research study from 1996, commissioned by the DuPont Company and conducted by Doanes Agricultural Service. It revisited the topic of farmer motivations and was used to compare findings with the earlier University of Missouri study, showing income had grown in importance but lifestyle remained key.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Farm">
      <data key="d0">Family Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'family farm' is defined as any farm, regardless of size, where family members provide the majority of the labor. The text clarifies that a family farm is a distinct concept from a small farm or sustainable agriculture, though there can be overlap.&lt;SEP&gt;A farm that is owned and operated by a family across multiple generations. Hickory Nut Gap Farm is a prime example, being a fifth-generation family farm undergoing a modern transformation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e930a3eb466e9bb1dfa38b0df50bfd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farmer">
      <data key="d0">Farmer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'farmer' is a role with multiple, conflicting definitions. The USDA defines a farmer based on a $1,000 annual sales threshold, the IRS defines a farmer for tax purposes based on income sources and profitability, and Webster's Dictionary provides a qualitative definition based on earning a living from farming. The author proposes a self-identification definition.&lt;SEP&gt;A person engaged in the growing process of a farm with substantial risk of loss. The text provides a detailed legal definition, distinguishing farmers from those in forestry or who rent land for a fixed price without material participation. The role is contrasted with that of an 'agripreneur'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Small Farm Commission">
      <data key="d0">National Small Farm Commission</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Small Farm Commission is a body that provides a definition for a small farm as any farm that grosses $250,000 or less per year. According to the text, this definition includes 94 percent of all farms in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.s. Internal Revenue Service">
      <data key="d0">U.s. Internal Revenue Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. Internal Revenue Service (IRS) is a U.S. government tax agency that has its own strict definition of a farmer to distinguish them from hobbyists for tax purposes. The definition is based on farming providing at least two-thirds of gross income and demonstrating profitability over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.s. Bureau Of The Census">
      <data key="d0">U.s. Bureau Of The Census</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. Bureau of the Census is a U.S. government agency that proposed changing the monetary threshold for the definition of a farm in 1996, though the proposal was ultimately rejected after an outcry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Webster's New World Dictionary">
      <data key="d0">Webster's New World Dictionary</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Webster's New World Dictionary is cited for providing a qualitative, non-numerical definition of a farmer as someone who earns their living by operating a farm. This is contrasted with the numerical definitions used by government agencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hobbyist">
      <data key="d0">Hobbyist</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'hobbyist' is a classification used by the IRS to distinguish from a professional 'farmer'. A farming operation is considered a hobby if it is not profitable in three of five consecutive years, and hobbyists do not qualify for the same tax reductions as farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Government Bureaucrats And University Professors">
      <data key="d0">Government Bureaucrats And University Professors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">These two groups are cited as being engaged in prolonged, and in the author's view unproductive, arguments about the definitions of large and small farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The 'Biggest Little Farm In America'">
      <data key="d0">The 'Biggest Little Farm In America'</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific farm used as an example of a highly profitable operation on a very small piece of land (grossing $238,000 from ½ acre). It serves to illustrate that acreage is not the only measure of a farm's scale or success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Author's Farm">
      <data key="d0">Author's Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The author's 80-acre farm where research plots are used to test the profitability of combining vegetables and livestock. The results from this farm are used to project potential income on a per-acre basis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farmer's Tax Guide 225">
      <data key="d0">Farmer's Tax Guide 225</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication from the U.S. Internal Revenue Service that provides a short explanation of the IRS's definition of a farmer for tax purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Publication N-1000, 'Farmers For Tax Purposes'">
      <data key="d0">Publication N-1000, 'Farmers For Tax Purposes'</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific IRS publication that offers a more detailed explanation of who qualifies as a farmer for tax purposes, outlining the two-fold test of significant participation and substantial risk of loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agripreneur">
      <data key="d0">Agripreneur</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An agripreneur is an individual who combines agricultural production with an entrepreneurial and business-oriented mindset, running an agricultural business like farming at their own financial risk. Reportedly coined in 1987, the term defines a "thinking" farmer who strategically manages their operations, understanding both the practical "how" and the strategic "why" behind their work. Rather than simply increasing labor hours, an agripreneur focuses on business planning, innovation, and profitability. This involves taking full responsibility for their own success, often acting as their own middleman, and practicing a form of agriculture that is both sustainable and profitable while aligning with their family's goals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Full-Time Farming">
      <data key="d0">Full-Time Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An approach to farming where it is the individual's sole profession. It is described as offering autonomy but being a difficult path for inexperienced individuals due to the high stakes of tying one's business, home, and lifestyle together.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Part-Time Farming">
      <data key="d0">Part-Time Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An approach that combines farming with an off-farm job, providing more financial security and reducing risk. It is presented as a safer transition for new farmers, allowing them to learn without jeopardizing their entire income.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Business of Farming">
      <data key="d0">Business of Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept central to the text, representing the entrepreneurial aspect of agriculture. It involves managing financial risks, planning for income, and making strategic decisions, which is the core of being an 'agripreneur'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Webster's Dictionary">
      <data key="d0">Webster's Dictionary</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An authoritative source cited in the text for its definition of an 'entrepreneur', which the author uses as a foundation to define 'agripreneur'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Entrepreneur">
      <data key="d0">Entrepreneur</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A person who operates a business at their own financial risk. This definition, cited from Webster's dictionary, serves as the basis for the author's term 'agripreneur'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oyster Farming">
      <data key="d0">Oyster Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of aquaculture mentioned in the text as an example of an activity that qualifies as the business of farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Furbearing Animal Raising">
      <data key="d0">Furbearing Animal Raising</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The business of breeding and raising animals such as chinchillas, mink, and foxes, which is classified as a form of farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedlot Operators">
      <data key="d0">Feedlot Operators</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of agricultural business professional who is considered a farmer, both for stock they own and for stock handled for customers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Forestry">
      <data key="d0">Forestry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The business of raising timber, which the text explicitly states is not considered to be engaged in farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Christmas Tree Selling">
      <data key="d0">Christmas Tree Selling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The activity of selling Christmas trees that are grown without planting or cultivation, which the text specifies is not considered farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Self-Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Self-Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical process for an aspiring agripreneur that involves assessing personal goals, financial needs, skills, and resources to determine the right farming path.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gaining Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">Gaining Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of acquiring practical farming skills and understanding the principles behind farming operations, which is essential for becoming a successful agripreneur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3360008b23c172924071e48d0800392e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farming Life">
      <data key="d0">Farming Life</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Farming Life is a lifestyle characterized by living and working in a rural environment. It involves a close connection with nature's cycles, daily chores, and a strong interdependence among family members. It presents challenges such as isolation, lack of urban amenities, and financial unpredictability, but also offers benefits like developing independence, a strong work ethic, and close family bonds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d0">Varroa Mite</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A parasitic mite that infects honeybees, suggested as a potential contributor to Colony Collapse Disorder (CCD).&lt;SEP&gt;The Varroa Mite, specifically *Varroa jacobsonii*, is a parasitic mite that infests honey bee colonies and hives. Originating in Southeast Asia on the Eastern honey bee, it crossed over to the Western honey bee in 1960 and has since spread to every continent except Australia. As an external parasite, it feeds on the hemolymph of both adult bees and brood, completing its development in about 10.5 days. This weakens the bee, shortens its life, and can lead to colony death within a few years if left untreated. The Varroa Mite is considered a major problem for beekeepers and a very serious problem for honey bees, particularly in the United States. Methods for inspecting and treating Varroa mite infestations include the use of bottom board inserts, tobacco smoke, and chemical treatments like Apistan and Miticur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-940d85de3b7092a6c76b3ef360a50ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27bb80a716045bb643a01fd3aae1d84b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ef2a61b557f4940ffb63973d9ebeaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marketing">
      <data key="d0">Marketing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Marketing, as it relates to agricultural enterprises, is a crucial business function and skill. It encompasses the comprehensive process of selling farm products, requiring farmers to learn and embrace it to become successful agripreneurs. Key aspects include identifying markets, niche marketing, pricing strategies, choosing sales channels such as wholesale, retail, direct sales, restaurants, trade shows, U-pick farms, and roadside stands, advertising, and establishing a market presence. For programs like Nuestras Raíces, marketing presents a major challenge, as many farmers dislike the sales aspect of their work. To address this, organizations may provide support by offering to sell produce for a fee. More generally, marketing refers to the activities a company undertakes to promote the buying or selling of a product or service and is closely linked to branding. It is a vital part of the 'business side of farming' applicable to all major types of farming, including field-crop, vegetable-crop, vine-crop, cattle, hogs, and sheep farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ee782c1d8a631cff4fafc639994eb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2b2fd01aa45dadaa82a243d140ec5f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Midwest">
      <data key="d0">Midwest</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Midwest is a geographically significant agricultural region in the United States, encompassing nine states served by the Rainbow Farmer's Cooperative. Historically, it exemplifies farming trends in relation to population centers, as documented in "Farming in the Midwest 1840–1900." The region is known for its massive feedlots, contrasting with smaller-scale, sustainable models like Country Natural Beef. Grain production and processing have consolidated in the Midwest over the past century, contributing to the decline of local grain economies elsewhere. The Midwest is a distant source of meat for restaurants in other regions. It is also a location where farming communities have replicated the gleaning program model pioneered by Marin Organic. The annual wheat harvest typically occurs in hot and dry conditions. April and May are considered ideal months for livestock to give birth on pasture due to grass growth and temperatures in the 60s. Studies in the Midwest have demonstrated that delaying the plowing under of sweet clover from April to May increases nitrogen content in the soil. The Midwest is located in climate Zone 6, where winter temperatures generally do not fall below –10 degrees Fahrenheit.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6db795582f52e35a0bb9aea86e66f7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104466acc0eb6f55c05f02aa258ea31a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d4150698732f393c235b8b3e1cb2d88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sustainable Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Sustainable Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sustainable Agriculture is a multifaceted farming philosophy and set of practices prioritizing economic viability, environmental soundness, and social acceptability. It represents an agricultural ethic and practice focused on whole-system health, ecological stewardship, and natural efficiencies. This approach, contrasting with monoculture farming, is not a rigid system but rather a site-specific, whole-farm approach emphasizing continuous learning and the interconnectedness of farm elements. Sustainable Agriculture aims to reduce costs, increase efficiency, conserve resources, promote diversity, and provide food at a reasonable cost while ensuring a fair income for farmers and supporting a sustainable community. Diversification is key as a management tool. It necessitates educating all stakeholders about its benefits.

While being organic is not a guarantee, Sustainable Agriculture aligns with ecological health and long-term viability, tracing its origins back to ideas from the 1940s and 1950s. Organizations such as SARE and ATTRA support Sustainable Agriculture through research, grants, and information services. Multiple models for success exist, as illustrated by examples such as the use of draft animals by the Amish. The Food Project emphasizes Sustainable Agriculture, working to make it a viable career option and teaching its principles to participating youth. Hickory Nut Gap Farm practices Sustainable Agriculture as its core philosophy, exemplified by the Ager family. Polyface is also dedicated to Sustainable Agriculture, and its internship program is designed for young people interested in learning about its principles and practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab7679e841cce082d6f5f864896c9afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebf6866ed6ad144c3c4b844913be03e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c07830327394a77dc9e8ac1a53acd63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f712d3550c8612228f9fb4dc999ba9b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740840</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Farmers are a central group within the food system, essential for both industrial and sustainable agriculture. They are the primary producers of food and fiber, local food, and other agricultural products. They also play a crucial role in maintaining natural habitats and instilling values, acting as cornerstones of society despite representing a small percentage of the population.

Farmers face numerous challenges, including economic difficulties like a shrinking share of the food dollar, new pests, changing environmental conditions, and the need to adapt to market demands and the payment terms of the food-service industry. Many farmers are also managing chronic health conditions, finding therapeutic benefits in the physical work and healthy food production.

To thrive, farmers must manage production, marketing, and organizational aspects of their operations, adapt to customer and retailer needs, and actively engage with legislative processes. They are encouraged to adopt an "agripreneur" mindset, utilize direct marketing, engage in careful farm planning, and implement sustainable practices to achieve profitability, particularly on a small scale. Support systems like the Farm to School movement, Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP), and other organizations are designed to aid farmers by creating market access, providing mentoring, developing marketing plans, connecting with retailers, and helping them transition to more sustainable practices. These programs, like "Appalachian Grown" certification, aim to augment income and help farmers stay on their land by granting access to new markets. Cooperatives and other partnerships play a vital role in supporting farmers by providing viable markets and fair purchasing agreements. Sharing farmer stories is also a key marketing strategy to personalize their products and connect with consumers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea6dd73e41a475c9933ac4783be5f57a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fef6f9e3cdaa27cfe32361bcab20e464&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db06dd0bc574d59f7ffa8848b9ae23fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efae0d6c5dabc751b9ceb56518857dff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d67ba0430fa075f3a4e6609250b9a01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497b3487d00eabefe426a10fa32f8010</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farming">
      <data key="d0">Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The overarching category of the resources listed, encompassing various agricultural practices, organizations, and educational materials."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Farming, as presented in the provided information, is a core activity encompassing the management of crops and livestock. It's portrayed as both a lifestyle and a business, requiring careful planning, adherence to principles, and a long-term goal of profitability and sustainability. This broad-scope endeavor necessitates significant research and lifelong learning. Various approaches to farming are discussed, including pasture-based, diversified, and organic methods. Farming is defined as a two-part process: extracting resources from the soil for profit and replenishing those resources to maintain and increase soil fertility, with the balance between these being crucial for sustainability. It is a complex endeavor that can be rewarding, frustrating, and tough, with motivations often rooted in lifestyle choices. Resources such as the North Dakota State University Cooperative Extension provide information on the general practice of agriculture. The text details farming's history, future, and types of production, emphasizing the need for a comprehensive farm plan.&lt;SEP&gt;The core activity discussed in the text, encompassing the physical labor of agriculture and the business challenges of marketing, packaging, and regulatory compliance. The text contrasts small-scale community farming with farming for the industrialized system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16e699b4bb77d0bdc27d883c3cafa88c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5944a88ca415780f3d4838a47d068512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sustainable Farming">
      <data key="d0">Sustainable Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sustainable Farming is an agricultural philosophy and practice centered around creating a self-sufficient and financially viable operation. It represents a systems-oriented approach that aims for long-term ecological health, economic viability, and social acceptability, positioning it as a desirable and more evolved approach to modern agriculture. This involves healing the land and producing healthy food. This system focuses on maintaining soil fertility, utilizing crop rotations, diversifying products, and managing livestock efficiently. Key principles include environmental responsibility by preventing soil erosion, strategic marketing (often direct to consumers), and being profitable to ensure the farm's long-term survival. It operates in a continuous cycle that avoids depleting the land, the farmer, or the livestock. While encompassing elements like diversity and low-input methods, it also seeks to avoid social opposition. Sustainable Farming is exemplified by farms such as Polyface Farm and Australis Aquaculture. The approach is promoted by JVUF through its educational programs, including 'Weekend Workshops', and demonstration farm. Youth programs can teach Sustainable Farming by hiring staff with relevant experience and passion, and it is an educational topic taught at youth-focused community farms like The Food Project and Growing Power, encompassing learning about healthy food, nutrition, and environmentally sound agricultural practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aaeba6a87de6589129068300a4f26db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8965f3e3965322fdf5c53aebddde1e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfed52d46f5bf9d5489c4d6b6dddd2cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd285deff310269197e8718b28f6076b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rural Environment">
      <data key="d0">Rural Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Rural Environment is the geographical and social setting for farm life, contrasted with an urban setting. It is characterized by natural landscapes, dirt roads, a lack of city services like streetlights and snow removal, and greater distances from emergency services. It is described as a place that fosters independence and strong family ties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conventional Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Conventional Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A term used to describe the dominant, industrial approach to farming. It is noted for its political clout and the economic difficulty for large operations to scale down, making it resistant to change."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"A traditional farming model where a farmer raises a crop and sells it to a middleman, who then distributes it to retailers and consumers. It is presented as a contrast to the direct-to-consumer model of agripreneurship."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Conventional Agriculture is a traditional farming model where a farmer focuses solely on raising a crop or livestock. After production, the goods are sold to a middleman, who then passes them on through the supply chain. This model separates the farmer from the final sale and consumer interaction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family">
      <data key="d0">Family</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The family is portrayed as an integral part of the farming operation and the core social and economic unit of farm life. Farming goals should align with family goals, and all members, including husband, wife, and children, need to be prepared for the farming lifestyle and contribute to the overall operation. The family is a significant source of labor, characterized by a distinct lifestyle. Clients are often motivated by the desire to grow food for home-cooked meals for their family. Raising chickens is considered a valuable experience for children within the family, helping them learn responsibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nature">
      <data key="d0">Nature</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nature is described as a fundamental force in farm life, characterized as being cyclical, slow, and unpredictable. It directly impacts farming operations through weather events (drought, freeze) and the biological timelines of livestock and crops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Work Ethic">
      <data key="d0">Work Ethic</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Work Ethic is a key value described as a product of growing up on a farm. It is developed through the necessity of performing daily chores, teaching children responsibility and reliability that carries into their adult lives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Profitability">
      <data key="d0">Profitability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Profitability is identified as the most important and critical principle of sustainable farming and agripreneurship, as well as a primary business goal. Defined as taking in more money than is spent, it is essential for a farm's long-term survival, improvement, and its ability to sustain financial operations. The provided text emphasizes that a farm "must be profitable" to be sustainable. Achieving profitability allows a farm to operate without relying on external subsidies or off-farm income, and methods to attain it include careful planning, keeping costs low, and avoiding debt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Middleman">
      <data key="d0">Middleman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Middleman is an intermediary role within the conventional agriculture model. They buy products directly from the farmer and pass them along the supply chain to retailers and consumers, separating the farmer from the final market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-farm Income">
      <data key="d0">Off-farm Income</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Off-farm Income is an external financial source used to subsidize a farm that is not generating enough profit on its own. The text argues that relying on this is not a sustainable long-term strategy for a farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emergency Services">
      <data key="d0">Emergency Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Emergency Services refers to critical public services, including medical help, fire departments, and the police. The text notes that a significant characteristic of the rural environment is the long distance to these services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b11383420e2171e9a69799dbddd3b13</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Specialty Crop Farming">
      <data key="d0">Specialty Crop Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of farming focusing on specific, often high-value crops. Examples provided include herbs, flowers, industrial crops like guayule and kenaf, fruits, and vegetables, which can be sold fresh or as value-added products."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exotic Animal Farming">
      <data key="d0">Exotic Animal Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of farming that involves raising non-traditional livestock. Examples from the text include elk, bison, deer, mountain sheep, donkeys, and ratites like ostriches and emus."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southern Missouri">
      <data key="d0">Southern Missouri</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A geographic region in the United States mentioned as the location of a successful shiitake mushroom farmer who started with a very small operation."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Plan">
      <data key="d0">Farm Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Farm Plan is a crucial, foundational document and process in agripreneurship, essential for both starting a new farm and improving an existing one. Functioning like a business plan, it is a comprehensive written document that outlines a farm's goals, which are often family-oriented, along with its strategies and financial projections. The process involves assessing personal and property resources to guide the farm's operations, covering key aspects such as management, specific enterprises, and the overall farm type. It also includes analyzing the needs for crops, livestock, and machinery. Ultimately, a Farm Plan is considered the first step toward achieving a desired farm and lifestyle, and it is vital for ensuring success, profitability, and securing loans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Crop Farm">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Crop Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of farm that produces single, large-scale field crops such as corn, soybeans, wheat, and milo, often associated with the conventional agriculture model."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crop And Livestock Farm">
      <data key="d0">Crop And Livestock Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A diversified farm type that combines the raising of traditional field crops with the pasturing of livestock, often utilizing different types of land for each purpose."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unusual Crop Farm">
      <data key="d0">Unusual Crop Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A niche category of farming focused on non-traditional products. The text provides examples such as raising live butterflies for events and renting insects for pollination services."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Butterfly Farming">
      <data key="d0">Butterfly Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific type of unusual crop farming that involves raising live butterflies for sale to be used at events like weddings and parties."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740489</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bee And Insect Farming">
      <data key="d0">Bee And Insect Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific type of unusual crop farming where bees and other insects are raised and rented out for the purpose of pollinating crops."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fee Fishing">
      <data key="d0">Fee Fishing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A business model within aquaculture where a farm charges consumers a fee (daily or per-pound) to catch fish on the farm's property."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A type of agritourism business model where consumers pay a fee to catch fish on a farm, often charged per day or per pound of fish caught.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Fish Farming">
      <data key="d0">Food Fish Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of aquaculture focused on raising fish such as catfish and trout for direct sale to stores, restaurants, and consumers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shiitake Mushroom Farming">
      <data key="d0">Shiitake Mushroom Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific type of specialty crop farming. The text uses a farmer from Southern Missouri as an example of a successful business that started with just eight mushroom logs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Fish">
      <data key="d0">Food Fish</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of fish, such as catfish and trout, that are raised for commercial sale to stores, restaurants, and directly to consumers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exotic Animals">
      <data key="d0">Exotic Animals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of non-traditional livestock, including elk, bison, deer, and ratites (ostriches, emus), raised for meat, hides, and specialty by-products like emu oil or velvet from antlers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value-added Crops">
      <data key="d0">Value-added Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Crops that have been processed from their raw commodity state to increase their market convenience and shelf life, such as cornmeal, jams, jellies, and tomato juice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aquaculture">
      <data key="d0">Aquaculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aquaculture is a form of farming centered around the cultivation of aquatic organisms, including fish, crustaceans, mollusks, aquatic plants, algae, and other aquatic life. This practice encompasses a broad spectrum of activities, from raising worms for bait and goldfish for the aquarium trade to fee fishing operations and raising food fish like catfish, trout, and tilapia. Various species like crawfish, shrimp, and clams can be involved, and aquaculture is practiced for meat production, breeding, or recreational fishing, holding potential for both commercial sales and home consumption. It can be implemented in on-farm ponds to generate additional income. The practice of fish farming is also presented as a growing trend to produce safe, local food and supplement wild fish catches, with entities like Growing Power engaging in this by raising yellow perch and tilapia. While aquaculture faces environmental concerns such as pollution and disease, and economic challenges including investment costs and a lack of subsidies, it is seen as having potential as a sustainable food source in the future, especially with innovative closed-containment systems. Resources and information on aquaculture are available from sources like Langston University and periodicals such as "Fish Farming News".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9d28e4a28ca972c9e289441c78c0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16c3fe8c089550fd16987a1c4f301a2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-157caea7b572636fefb27a23c9261c73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2920f4362eb05a4692fa915ca812b425</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Planning">
      <data key="d0">Farm Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Farm Planning is an essential, strategic process for achieving success in both new and existing farms, often described as a "road map" that provides clear direction. This critical process involves establishing comprehensive short-term, intermediate, and long-term goals that cover business, financial, family, personal, and lifestyle aspects. A key component of Farm Planning is the evaluation of resources, which includes the strategic consideration of land (analyzing its slope, moisture, and soil), in order to select enterprises that match the available resources. The process also entails analyzing budgets and profit potential, as well as determining appropriate planting methods and management strategies, with the ultimate objective of ensuring a steady cash flow and achieving overall success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Resources">
      <data key="d0">Farm Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The collection of assets that must be managed and balanced on a farm. This includes soil, water (ponds, organic matter retention), buildings, machinery, and available labor.&lt;SEP&gt;The collective assets available to a farm, which need to be evaluated during the planning phase. This includes capital, skills, labor, land, soils, water, location, climate, equipment, and marketing needs.&lt;SEP&gt;The fundamental assets a farmer must manage, explicitly identified in the text as land, labor, capital, and management. Taking stock of these resources is a foundational step in farm planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capital">
      <data key="d0">Capital</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A financial resource that must be evaluated as part of the initial planning phase for an agricultural enterprise, as listed under 'Resource evaluation'.&lt;SEP&gt;The financial resources required for a farming operation. This includes savings, borrowing capacity, and non-monetary options like renting equipment or land, and bartering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skills">
      <data key="d0">Skills</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The abilities and knowledge necessary for farming. The index emphasizes the need to evaluate one's skills as a key resource and identifies necessary skills for agripreneurship.&lt;SEP&gt;The practical abilities and knowledge necessary to run a farm. The text emphasizes that these are best acquired through hands-on experience, mentorship, or workshops, and they must be evaluated as part of farm planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barter">
      <data key="d0">Barter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method of acquiring capital or resources by trading goods, skills, or labor instead of using money. Examples include farming land on shares or trading crops for services like hay baling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marketing Channels">
      <data key="d0">Marketing Channels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Various methods for selling farm products, such as roadside stands, farmers' markets, and sale barns. The choice of marketing channel is a key part of initial farm planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Worms For Fish Bait">
      <data key="d0">Worms For Fish Bait</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A product category referring to worms raised and sold as bait for fishing, listed as a type of farm enterprise.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fish For The Aquarium Trade">
      <data key="d0">Fish For The Aquarium Trade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A product category referring to goldfish and tropical fish raised for sale to the aquarium market, listed as a type of farm enterprise.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roadside Stand">
      <data key="d0">Roadside Stand</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A direct-to-consumer marketing channel for farm products, mentioned as an option to consider during the farm planning phase.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of on-farm sales operation that requires a location near well-traveled roads to be successful.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sale Barn">
      <data key="d0">Sale Barn</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A venue for selling livestock and a place to meet and learn from experienced farmers. It serves as both a marketing channel and a networking location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Land">
      <data key="d0">Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A fundamental physical resource for farming that must be evaluated. The text discusses options like buying versus renting to manage capital.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary physical property for a farm, which the index indicates must be evaluated as part of the resource assessment process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Labor">
      <data key="d0">Labor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">In the context of farming, Labor is a critical human resource and the workforce required for various operations, including planting, harvesting, and marketing. It is considered a key component in the resource evaluation phase of farm planning, where available time and people are assessed. The management of Labor is crucial, with planning aiming to balance its requirements with machinery and to minimize overall work. This workforce can be furnished by the farmer, their family, or hired workers, and it represents a significant cost in both time and money that is calculated separately from machinery expenses. For instance, labor costs calculated at $10 per hour can substantially increase the total cost per acre. The profitability of a farming enterprise is often measured in dollars earned per hour of labor, which is analyzed by comparing the hours needed for production versus marketing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db06dd0bc574d59f7ffa8848b9ae23fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mentorship">
      <data key="d0">Mentorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method of skill acquisition in farming, which involves learning practical 'how-tos' from experienced or 'old' farmers, often in exchange for labor or volunteering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Educational Events">
      <data key="d0">Educational Events</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Formal learning opportunities for farmers, such as workshops, seminars, classes, and conferences, used to acquire the rudiments of necessary skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Elk Farming">
      <data key="d0">Elk Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of livestock farming mentioned as an example. It is noted as being similar to cattle farming but with very different skill requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of specialized livestock farming mentioned as an example to illustrate that different farming operations have unique skill requirements, such as knowledge of collecting velvet from antlers, which differs from cattle farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cattle Farming">
      <data key="d0">Cattle Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A traditional form of livestock farming used as a point of comparison to highlight the unique skill requirements of more specialized operations like elk farming.&lt;SEP&gt;The practice of raising cattle. This requires specific skills such as applying ID tags, castration, dehorning, hoof trimming, administering medication, delivering calves, handling and moving animals, and understanding their behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feed and Supply Stores">
      <data key="d0">Feed and Supply Stores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Commercial establishments that sell agricultural supplies. They are also mentioned as places where a new farmer can find and talk to experienced farmers for mentorship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farming Skills">
      <data key="d0">Farming Skills</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A comprehensive set of abilities required for various agricultural operations. The text provides detailed lists of skills needed for specific types of farming, including field crops, vegetable crops, vine crops, cattle, hogs, and sheep, as well as general hand-tool skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farmers' Market">
      <data key="d0">Farmers' Market</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Farmers' Market is a community or public market venue where farmers sell their products directly to consumers. It serves as a key venue for direct marketing and is listed as an important marketing channel to consider during farm planning. This direct-to-consumer sales approach enables farmers to develop a loyal customer base, allows new farmers to gather information from vendors regarding effective growing practices in the local area, and provides them with income. While offering a potentially better return in terms of customer dollars per hour compared to roadside stands, Farmers' Markets require transporting goods to town and may have restrictions on the types of products that can be sold, such as meat or crafts. Information about Farmers' Markets can be found through the Openair Market Network. They are a recurring economic and social outcome of successful community gardens and serve as a venue for gardeners to sell produce and connect with the community. For example, Lynchburg Grows operates a weekly market where it sells its fresh, organic produce directly to the community. DIG crews also sell the produce they have harvested at a weekly commercial event, providing teens with practical business experience. The Warren Wilson Garden sells its produce at a market on campus twice a week and another at a weekend market at a nearby fire department, allowing students to interact directly with customers. The Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative in Okmulgee established a local market, the first since the 1930s, to provide an outlet for local farmers and offer fresh produce to the community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8dfe2a17435f059c92535241799795b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e970626c7a63b367949cb5600957bcab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-736ea6711636125c220e214941062b61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Old Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Old Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of experienced agriculturalists who possess valuable skills and are suggested as a source of instruction and mentorship for new farmers, potentially in exchange for volunteer work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Field-Crop Farming">
      <data key="d0">Field-Crop Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farming focused on field crops. Key skills include knowledge of soils, plowing, planting, harvesting, equipment operation, pest control, irrigation, and marketing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vegetable-Crop Farming">
      <data key="d0">Vegetable-Crop Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farming focused on vegetables. Essential skills include field preparation, managing seeds and transplants, using structures like trellises and cages, harvesting by hand or machine, and using season extenders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vine- and Bramble-Crop Farming">
      <data key="d0">Vine- and Bramble-Crop Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized type of farming that requires skills in planting, propagation, pruning, trellising, harvesting, and winter preparation for vine and bramble crops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hog Farming">
      <data key="d0">Hog Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The practice of raising hogs. Necessary skills include breed knowledge, castration, ringing, managing birthing, handling, and understanding hog-specific behaviors, particularly aggression in boars and sows.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sheep Farming">
      <data key="d0">Sheep Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A major category of agricultural enterprise presented in the document, focused on raising sheep. It includes several distinct business models for producing meat, wool, and milk, each with a detailed financial breakdown of income, expenses, and labor.&lt;SEP&gt;The practice of raising sheep. Key skills involve breed knowledge, tagging, docking (tail removal), shearing, delivering multiple lambs, and managing the aggression of rams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a6d072f41d662cb326c1c528ac3c5c51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Labor">
      <data key="d0">Farm Labor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A limiting factor in farm management. The text discusses its strategic use, prioritizing family labor before considering hired help to manage cash flow and operational efficiency."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The central concept of the text, representing the human effort and time required for agricultural tasks. The text provides detailed charts estimating labor hours for the care of various crops and livestock.&lt;SEP&gt;The human effort required for farm operations, which is typically measured in 10-hour workdays. The text discusses assessing available labor and provides an example that a full-time farmer supplies about 3,000 hours of labor per year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8448fec2abb01962b8a2c019dd0efd50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Labor">
      <data key="d0">Family Labor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Labor provided by the farmer's family, especially children. It is presented as a way to teach responsibility, work ethic, and safety without a direct cash expense. The text emphasizes making farm work a positive and educational experience for children."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A specific type of farm labor sourced from family members. The text advocates for dividing tasks based on individual skills rather than stereotypes and involving children to teach them a work ethic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Useful Hand-Tool Skills">
      <data key="d0">Useful Hand-Tool Skills</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of practical skills beneficial for farming operations, including woodworking, concrete working, fencing, electrical work, and operating tools like chain saws and rototillers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pest Control, Weed Control, Fertilization">
      <data key="d0">Pest Control, Weed Control, Fertilization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of essential skills for crop-based farming (field, vegetable, vine) focused on managing pests, controlling weeds, and providing nutrients to ensure healthy crop growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Irrigation">
      <data key="d0">Irrigation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key skill for various types of crop farming, involving the artificial application of water to land to assist in the production of crops.&lt;SEP&gt;A method of watering crops, such as drip or standard systems, which is described as expensive. It is presented as an alternative to the more sustainable practice of water conservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Animal Husbandry Practices">
      <data key="d0">Animal Husbandry Practices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of core skills common to raising livestock like cattle, hogs, and sheep. This includes applying ID tags, castration, administering medication, assisting with births, handling manure, and performing maintenance like hoof trimming, dehorning, docking, and tagging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breed Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">Breed Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific area of expertise required for raising cattle, hogs, and sheep, which involves understanding different breeds and their characteristics for breeding purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Behavioral Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">Behavioral Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An essential skill for managing livestock (cattle, hogs, sheep) that involves understanding animal behavior related to aggression, birthing, and sickness to ensure safety and proper care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fencing">
      <data key="d0">Fencing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Fencing is a critical farm infrastructure detailed in the text, with specific instructions on materials like galvanized wire and staples, and construction methods such as creating braces and stretching wire."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Fencing is described as a critical piece of farm infrastructure for managing livestock. The text discusses different types, including perimeter and temporary electrical fences, and materials like woven wire and barbed wire, emphasizing that good fences save labor and increase farm value."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A practical skill required for managing cattle, hogs, and sheep, involving the construction and maintenance of enclosures to contain the animals and manage their grazing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b34a5b7dc86e95b65f36512f72980b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d72291c86cdc15cc9b42dccc6b106a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Missouri Cooperative Extension">
      <data key="d0">University of Missouri Cooperative Extension</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization, located in Columbia, Mo, that provides agricultural resources and data, including the 'Farm Business Planning Guide for Organization #6500' cited in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8448fec2abb01962b8a2c019dd0efd50</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
