    </node>
    <node id="Farm Business Planning Guide for Organization #6500">
      <data key="d0">Farm Business Planning Guide for Organization #6500</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific publication from the University of Missouri Cooperative Extension, dated 1965, used as a primary data source for the labor charts in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8448fec2abb01962b8a2c019dd0efd50</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Selected Fruit and Vegetable Planning Budgets">
      <data key="d0">Selected Fruit and Vegetable Planning Budgets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication from the University of Missouri-Columbia, authored by Charles DeCourley and Kevin Moore, from which the equipment cost calculation example was adapted.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific publication (Ec 959) from the Department of Agricultural Economics at the University of Missouri-Columbia, dated 1987, used as a data source for the text's labor estimates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8448fec2abb01962b8a2c019dd0efd50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Enterprise Budgets: Northeast Oklahoma 1985">
      <data key="d0">Enterprise Budgets: Northeast Oklahoma 1985</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific publication prepared by Bill Burton of Oklahoma State University Cooperative Extension, dated 1985, used as a data source for the labor charts in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8448fec2abb01962b8a2c019dd0efd50</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Goals">
      <data key="d0">Family Goals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A central concept in farm planning, where the goals of the farm should align with the goals of the family. This involvement also helps children learn responsibility."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A concept highlighted in the text as a crucial element of farm planning, emphasizing the need to balance work with family time, vacations, and other personal obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;The collective objectives of the entire family involved in the farm. The text emphasizes that for a small farm to be successful, its goals must be shared by all family members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8448fec2abb01962b8a2c019dd0efd50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nonfarm Activities">
      <data key="d0">Nonfarm Activities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Activities such as attending seminars and conferences, which are recommended for farmers' education, personal development, and gaining new perspectives for business management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8448fec2abb01962b8a2c019dd0efd50</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calf">
      <data key="d0">Calf</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">In the context of cattle farming, a 'calf' refers to a female animal that has not yet given birth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steer Calf">
      <data key="d0">Steer Calf</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'Steer calf' is a young male bull calf that has been castrated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stocker Calf">
      <data key="d0">Stocker Calf</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'Stocker calf' is a young bovine that is sold at the time of weaning, typically at 6 to 7 months of age.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wintered Only">
      <data key="d0">Wintered Only</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'Wintered only' calf is one that has been weaned from its mother and is fed a diet of hay or grain throughout the winter season.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wintered And Finished">
      <data key="d0">Wintered And Finished</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'Wintered and finished' calf is one that is fed hay and grain until spring, and then is fed as much grain as it desires to prepare it for market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wintered And Grazed">
      <data key="d0">Wintered And Grazed</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'Wintered and grazed' calf is fed hay or grain over the winter, then put on pasture to graze on spring grass until it is sold, usually in the autumn.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crops">
      <data key="d0">Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A general category for the various plants discussed in the text, central to the themes of farming difficulty, profitability, and regional suitability. Examples include hard squashes, tomatoes, and lettuce."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A general term for cultivated plants, with 'cover crops' listed as a specific sub-topic.&lt;SEP&gt;Crops represent a general category of agricultural plants cultivated for various purposes, including food, economic gain, and livestock sustenance. Farmers select crops based on several factors: resources like soil type, water availability, regional climate, temperature, and location, and also growth time, marketability, and integration with livestock. Examples of crops include corn, wheat, vegetables, berries, hay, potatoes, legumes like alfalfa and lespedeza, grasses, snap beans, pumpkins, Indian corn, and cover crops. Cultivating different types of crops, like legumes and grasses, has varying nutritional values that impact livestock health and productivity. Crops have significant water requirements during growth phases and are vulnerable to climate-related diseases. Farmers may use windbreaks to protect crops, conserving moisture and heat and allowing the crops to use nutrients more efficiently. Extending the growing season for crops can increase profitability. The costs of production include aspects like seed, fertilizer, and equipment, while labor estimates vary based on the specific crop. Crop byproducts may serve as a free energy source for the farm. Diversified farms grow more than one kind of crop, to generate income, improve soil health, and integrate with livestock.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-766988b011c2a15c7312ce3e9bb55cf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfed52d46f5bf9d5489c4d6b6dddd2cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8448fec2abb01962b8a2c019dd0efd50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wintered, Grazed, And Finished">
      <data key="d0">Wintered, Grazed, And Finished</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'Wintered, grazed, and finished' calf undergoes a three-stage process: it is fed hay and grain after weaning, grazes from spring to autumn, and is then finished on more grain until it is ready for market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yearling Steer">
      <data key="d0">Yearling Steer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'Yearling steer' is a castrated male bovine that is approximately one year old. The term can be specified as 'short' for just under a year or 'long' for just over a year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small-Grain-Crop">
      <data key="d0">Small-Grain-Crop</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of crops that includes wheat and oats. Their suitability for cultivation is based on soil types and land characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large-Grain-Crop">
      <data key="d0">Large-Grain-Crop</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of crops, including corn and soybeans, which typically require more fertile soils for successful cultivation compared to small grains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fruit Crops">
      <data key="d0">Fruit Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of crops that thrive in environments without extreme temperatures and in well-drained soil, not necessarily requiring level topography.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abstract Office">
      <data key="d0">Abstract Office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local office where prospective landowners can verify the property rights, such as water and mineral rights, that have been previously sold or are attached to a piece of land.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Title Company">
      <data key="d0">Title Company</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A company that provides services to check the rights associated with a real estate property, including easements and whether water or mineral rights have been sold.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Conservation Department">
      <data key="d0">Soil Conservation Department</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A government agency that provides resources to landowners, including maps detailing land capability, to aid in sustainable agriculture and land management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University Extension Office">
      <data key="d0">University Extension Office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A University Extension Office is a local, educational outreach office affiliated with a university that serves as a primary resource for farmers and the community. It provides expert advice, information, and resources on a wide range of agricultural topics to aid in farm planning. The University Extension Office offers consultations on land capability, general agricultural practices, and the selection of crops, livestock, and trees for windbreaks that are suitable for specific local conditions. It is also a key source for obtaining materials like soil maps. Additionally, the office may have specialists who can provide guidance on direct marketing and methods for adding value to farm products.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Department Of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">State Department Of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A state-level government agency mentioned as a potential source for agricultural statistics needed for market research."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"A state-level government agency that serves as a source for finding soil maps and other agricultural resources."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A state-level government agency that can be consulted for information on land capability and agricultural matters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Land Capability Classes">
      <data key="d0">Land Capability Classes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification system that divides land into eight classes (I-VIII) based on slope, water capacity, and soil drainage. Classes I-IV are suitable for cultivation, while V-VIII are generally used for pasture, woodland, or wildlife.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terracing">
      <data key="d0">Terracing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural technique used for soil conservation on sloped land. It involves creating a series of steps or ridges at right angles to the slope to prevent water runoff and soil erosion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contour Strip-Cropping">
      <data key="d0">Contour Strip-Cropping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A farming practice where alternating strips of different crops are planted following the natural contours of the land to reduce soil erosion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soils">
      <data key="d0">Soils</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A fundamental agricultural resource. The text emphasizes that the natural fertility and condition of soils are crucial for determining land use, crop suitability, and the viability of different farming enterprises, particularly on smaller farms."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A thematic category within the provided text that lists books and resources concerning soil science, sustainable soil management, ecological soil health, and alternative cultivation methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The medium in which crops grow, characterized by properties like depth, texture (e.g., clay, sand, loam), and drainage. The type of soil is a critical factor in determining suitable crops and land usage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a25021ab306ceaf5d58e192cc6fc0a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinton-Boone-Lindley Areas">
      <data key="d0">Clinton-Boone-Lindley Areas</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A soil region located in the states of Wisconsin, Minnesota, Iowa, Illinois, and Missouri. It is characterized by rolling hills, is mostly woodland or pasture, and is suggested to be excellent for orchard land.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wisconsin">
      <data key="d0">Wisconsin</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Wisconsin is a U.S. state located in the Midwestern United States. It contains part of the Clinton-Boone-Lindley soil region and is home to the Troy Gardens project, the cities of Madison and Milwaukee, and the base of operations for Growing Power. Growing Power diverts over 2,000 tons of waste from Wisconsin landfills each year and has developed methods to grow food throughout the cold Wisconsin winters, utilizing heat from compost. A worm farmer from Wisconsin was also invited to speak at a garden-planning event on the reservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iowa">
      <data key="d0">Iowa</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The state in the United States associated with the Practical Farmers of Iowa organization."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. state of Iowa contains part of the Clinton-Boone-Lindley soil region and was the site of USDA studies in the 1930s that demonstrated sod pastures significantly improved water absorption compared to corn fields. The state is also home to several agricultural organizations, including the Practical Farmers of Iowa, which conducts research for family farmers, and the Heartland Organic Marketing Cooperative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e64fb1fe152a62cd06403742bd6beac5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16e699b4bb77d0bdc27d883c3cafa88c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Illinois">
      <data key="d0">Illinois</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A U.S. state that contains part of the Clinton-Boone-Lindley soil region.&lt;SEP&gt;A state in the United States, which contains the city of Chicago.&lt;SEP&gt;Illinois is a US state mentioned as a location in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Missouri">
      <data key="d0">Missouri</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Missouri is a U.S. state that serves as a primary example for various agricultural and environmental topics. It is home to the University of Missouri Extension Service, which has published materials on subjects like fencing, and the state-level "AgriMissouri" agricultural program. The state's regulations can differ from federal ones, as illustrated by the fact that a farm in Missouri is permitted to sell up to 20,000 live chickens per year without a USDA inspection.

Noted for its cattle production, Missouri, along with Texas, has one of the largest numbers of small farms in the country. The state is the location for both the author's farm and Steve Salt's farm in Yarrow. Consequently, its specific climate and conditions dictate agricultural practices, such as planting corn in early May and varying rest periods for pastures; fescue, for example, requires 60-90 days of rest in the summer but less than 30 in the spring. The state is also used as an example for its custom rates for farming operations like moldboard plowing, disking, cultivating, and corn planting.

Geographically and environmentally, Missouri contains part of the Clinton-Boone-Lindley soil region, and its soil is sometimes formed from rocks that are not broken down into sufficiently small particles. The state is also cited for its history of land management, including an instance from the 1960s where timber ground was poorly managed by being bulldozed and planted with fescue, leading to erosion on steep hills.&lt;SEP&gt;Missouri is a US state mentioned as a location in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b34a5b7dc86e95b65f36512f72980b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c07830327394a77dc9e8ac1a53acd63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wheat">
      <data key="d0">Wheat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wheat is a small-grain crop, often contrasted with large-grain crops like corn, and is used as an example of regional specialization in areas such as Kansas. It is excellently suited for cultivation in Mollisol soils, along with corn and soybeans.

Ideal growing conditions for wheat include an annual rainfall of 15 to 25 inches, and it has a water requirement of 513 pounds per pound of dry matter. The crop benefits from cold temperatures and snow, which serves as an insulator and is important for a good harvest. In contrast, its harvest period in the Midwest is associated with hot, dry weather. However, if the weather turns hot and humid, wheat is highly susceptible to a disease called rust, which can cut yields in half.

To improve yields, protective measures such as windbreaks can be implemented. A University of Nebraska study showed that windbreaks increased wheat yields by 22%, and wheat grass can be planted within the fields to serve this purpose. Wheat is also a versatile crop in agricultural systems, used in both the long-term Norfolk rotation and in short-term rotations, such as being planted in the fall and overseeded with clover.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740499</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oats">
      <data key="d0">Oats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of small-grain crop mentioned as an example, suitable for certain soil types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740499</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soybeans">
      <data key="d0">Soybeans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Soybeans are a cultivated, large-grain row crop and a type of legume that generally requires fertile soils, such as Mollisols, for cultivation. They are characterized by a low biomass root system and are noted for their ability to fix and replace nitrogen in the soil. Because of this nitrogen-fixing capability, soybeans are ideal for agricultural systems like crop rotation, where they are recommended to precede nitrogen-using crops like corn. For instance, a common rotation involves planting soybeans to follow a corn harvest, thereby replenishing the soil's nitrogen. Despite their nitrogen-fixing properties, it is noted that farmers may still add N-P-K fertilizers in rotations involving soybeans. Additionally, they can be used as a partner crop to corn in a strip-cropping system on gentler slopes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740499</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pasture">
      <data key="d0">Pasture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An area of land covered with grass and other low plants suitable for grazing animals. The text discusses using pasture for livestock feed, either through direct grazing or by mowing it for hay.&lt;SEP&gt;Land used for grazing livestock. The suitability of land for pasture is determined by its Capability Class, with Classes V-VIII often being used for this purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740499</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Water Rights">
      <data key="d0">Water Rights</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A legal concept referring to the right to access and use water that flows across or underneath a property. The text stresses the importance of securing these rights, especially in arid regions like the West, and recommends checking them at the county courthouse."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A legal right associated with a piece of property that grants the owner the use of water. The text advises buyers to verify these rights at an Abstract Office or Title Company.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal and practical concept concerning the right to use water from a specific source, listed as a topic in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mineral Rights">
      <data key="d0">Mineral Rights</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A legal right to exploit an area for the minerals it harbors. Property buyers are advised to ensure they have these rights by checking with an Abstract Office or Title Company.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740499</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crop Rotation">
      <data key="d0">Crop Rotation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural technique practiced by Williams, learned from her family, which involves changing the type of crop grown in a particular field each season to avoid depleting the soil's nutrients and killing the land.&lt;SEP&gt;Crop rotation is a vital and central agricultural practice, dating back to Roman times, that involves growing a series of dissimilar types of crops sequentially on the same land. It is a sustainable farming technique and management tool employed with both long-term and short-term strategies. The practice is described as the main method for improving soil fertility and health, preventing nutrient depletion (e.g., following nitrogen-using corn with nitrogen-fixing soybeans), managing organic matter, controlling plant diseases, weeds, and pests, improving soil structure, providing better ground cover, and evening out the distribution of labor. Rotating legumes and non-legumes is specifically mentioned to increase organic matter and soil fertility, and to provide feed for livestock. Crop rotation also involves the alternation of heavy- and light-feeding crops. It is promoted as a method to increase yields, with an average increase of 10% cited.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Easements">
      <data key="d0">Easements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A legal right for a person or entity to use land they do not own for a specific purpose. The text advises property buyers to be aware of any easements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740499</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Class I Land">
      <data key="d0">Class I Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The highest capability class of land, characterized by a 0-2% slope, deep, fertile soil, good water retention, and no rocks. It is suitable for any use, including cultivated crops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740499</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Class II Land">
      <data key="d0">Class II Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A high-quality land class with a 2-5% slope. It is very productive but may require some conservation practices to mitigate issues like erosion or wetness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Class III Land">
      <data key="d0">Class III Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Land with a 5-10% slope that requires intensive erosion control if regularly cultivated. It may also be excessively wet or poorly aerated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Class IV Land">
      <data key="d0">Class IV Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Land suitable for limited cultivation due to factors like its 10-15% slope. It is often better utilized as pasture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Class V Land">
      <data key="d0">Class V Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Land generally unsuitable for cultivation, with a 15-20% slope. It is limited by extremely wet, poorly aerated, or rocky soils but can be used as pasture or woodland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Class VI Land">
      <data key="d0">Class VI Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Land suitable for pastureland or woodland, characterized by limitations such as shallow soil, steep slopes, high erosion, or numerous rocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Class VII Land">
      <data key="d0">Class VII Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Land with severe limitations for use as pasture or woods, but which is considered good for wildlife habitats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Class VIII Land">
      <data key="d0">Class VIII Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The lowest capability class of land, described as being unable to grow much of anything and best suited for wildlife or recreation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Fertility">
      <data key="d0">Natural Fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An intrinsic quality of soil referring to its ability to supply nutrients for plant growth. The text highlights it as a critical factor for small farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a748f8974ea30d2229e5c6357765d4da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Geological Survey">
      <data key="d0">State Geological Survey</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A state agency that provides technical information and data on well water, including its flow rate (gallons per minute), quantity, and reliability."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The West">
      <data key="d0">The West</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A region of the United States characterized by arid conditions where more land is required for livestock, water rights are critically important, and accessing resources like breeding stock can be challenging due to distance."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A geographic region in the United States that is home to the nearly one hundred ranches that constitute the Country Natural Beef cooperative.&lt;SEP&gt;A region in the U.S. mentioned as a place where public lands have historically been leased to private ranchers, contrasting with the newer model of community-based farming for private gain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kansas">
      <data key="d0">Kansas</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A U.S. state used as an example of regional agricultural specialization, specifically known for its wheat production."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A US state that is the location of the Kansas Rural Center, an organization focused on sustainable farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorado">
      <data key="d0">Colorado</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A U.S. state where the city of Boulder is located."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"A U.S. state where the data for the 'Water Requirements Of Crops' table was gathered, linking it to agricultural research."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The state where Amory and Hunter Lovins founded the Rocky Mountain Institute in 1982 to focus on whole-system energy efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f712d3550c8612228f9fb4dc999ba9b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ae240ee8f1e0681df991703ff2ca94b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern U.S.">
      <data key="d0">Eastern U.S.</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A region of the United States mentioned in a study that found different water requirements for corn compared to data gathered in Colorado, illustrating regional variations."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agritourism">
      <data key="d0">Agritourism</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Agritourism is a business strategy and a form of commercial enterprise where farms attract visitors for educational or recreational activities. This involves linking agricultural production with tourism, opening farm gates to the public for activities like hay rides, corn mazes, or farm-to-table dinners. Agritourism's success often depends on the farm's location near well-traveled roads, population centers, or tourist sites. It serves as an effective method for farms to diversify income, build customer relationships, and educate the public about food sources. Agritourism can take various forms, such as U-pick operations or roadside stands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d53cc613c52fd5080e556f9abb248a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cow-Calf Operation">
      <data key="d0">Cow-Calf Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of livestock enterprise that involves raising a cow and its calf. The text notes that this operation requires significant land (e.g., 3 acres per pair, up to 10 in the West), the amount of which depends on soil quality."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U-Pick Berries">
      <data key="d0">U-Pick Berries</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop enterprise where customers harvest their own produce. The text presents this as a potentially high-income option on small acreage, provided there is adequate water."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Well">
      <data key="d0">Well</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A private water source drilled to access groundwater. The text advises investigating a well's history, water flow (gallons per minute), reliability during dry weather, and quality (mineral content, pollution, taste) before purchasing a farm."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Location/Access">
      <data key="d0">Location/Access</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A critical factor in farming that encompasses topography, soil types, climate, and proximity to markets and population centers. It influences the types of crops and livestock that can be raised and the viability of direct-market or agritourism businesses."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Climate">
      <data key="d0">Climate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the descriptions provided, climate is a key environmental factor and a central natural resource that significantly influences agriculture. It is defined by the prevailing weather conditions of a region, including rainfall, temperature, general weather patterns, and the length of the growing season. Climate directly impacts soil fertility and determines which plants and animals are suitable for an area, influencing farm type selection, crop choices, and livestock health. For instance, it sets specific requirements for crops, such as rainfall amounts for wheat and growing season temperatures for corn. Furthermore, climate affects species evolution, vegetation growth, the water requirements for plants, and the prevalence of parasites and diseases, making it a critical consideration in farm planning and pest management. Therefore, it is advised to match farming types to the local climate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Markets">
      <data key="d0">Markets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The commercial outlets for farm products. The text emphasizes considering the distance to markets for both livestock and direct-market produce, suggesting a 40-mile radius from a large population is ideal for the latter."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U-Pick Operation">
      <data key="d0">U-Pick Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A form of agritourism and direct marketing where customers harvest produce themselves. Its success is heavily dependent on location, requiring proximity to well-traveled roads or tourist destinations."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A U-pick operation is a type of farm enterprise where customers harvest their own produce. It is used as an example to illustrate the need for specific risk management plans, such as liability for customer safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a7d4e49c6e48a2727071f21a879ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Usda">
      <data key="d0">Usda</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The United States Department of Agriculture (USDA) is a federal regulatory body and governmental organization involved in various aspects of agriculture and food production. As a regulatory body, it sets guidelines and provides inspections for food processing, including the approval of kitchens and the regulation of meat products like pork, beef, lamb, and chicken. The USDA is also heavily involved in research; it employs researchers, such as Henry Hopp who studied earthworms in the 1940s, and provides increasing funding for aquaculture research, though this amount is still less than its funding for crop research. The department also collaborates with private entities, such as its partnership with the Delta Land and Pine Company to develop a "terminator" gene for seeds. To support farmers and growers, the USDA has issued a map of climate zones for the United States, which helps in determining plant hardiness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb9d28e4a28ca972c9e289441c78c0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arizona">
      <data key="d0">Arizona</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Arizona is a U.S. state located in the northern region of the United States. Phoenix is a city located in Arizona. The activities of Diablo Burger and the Diablo Trust take place in Arizona. The author visited the Navajo reservation in Arizona to speak with farmers and ranchers about their relationship with land and food. Major water pipelines extract water from Arizona, which impacts local groundwater availability. Cameron and Mary Seton Corboy are associated with Arizona. The North Leupp Family Farm is located in the northwest region of Arizona. Hotevilla and Moenkopi are located in Arizona. The southern part of Arizona is located in climate Zone 9.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ae240ee8f1e0681df991703ff2ca94b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1fa8277818b65f8d3d6665242021ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="West">
      <data key="d0">West</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A region of the United States where traditional cattle ranching is still practiced, typically in areas remote from population centers.&lt;SEP&gt;The West is a region in the United States mentioned as being part of climate Zone 6, where winter temperatures do not typically go below –10 degrees Fahrenheit.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U-pick Operation">
      <data key="d0">U-pick Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A U-pick operation is a specific type of on-farm business where customers harvest their own produce. The text notes that this model requires a location with good access near populated areas.&lt;SEP&gt;A business model for farming enterprises, particularly those with labor-intensive crops like berries, where customers perform the harvesting.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of direct-to-consumer sales event where customers harvest the produce themselves. This model significantly reduces the farmer's harvest labor but requires good liability insurance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfed52d46f5bf9d5489c4d6b6dddd2cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Climate Zones">
      <data key="d0">Climate Zones</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Climate Zones are a classification system created by the Usda for the United States, designating eleven zones based on minimum winter temperatures to determine the hardiness of plants that can be grown there.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microclimates">
      <data key="d0">Microclimates</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microclimates are localized climate conditions within a larger area that can be created naturally by land formations or artificially by structures like windbreaks and greenhouses. They can enable the growth of plants not typically suited to the broader region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Free Energy Sources">
      <data key="d0">Free Energy Sources</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Free Energy Sources is a concept describing natural resources and processes on a farm that reduce costs and labor. Examples include the sun, water, soil enhancers like crop rotation and nitrogen-fixing bacteria, and energy from livestock.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crop Rotations">
      <data key="d0">Crop Rotations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Crop rotations are an agricultural practice where different types of crops are planted in the same area in sequential seasons. The text describes it as a free energy source that nourishes the soil and improves yields without commercial fertilizers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct-marketing">
      <data key="d0">Direct-marketing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method of selling farm produce directly to consumers or businesses. The text suggests it is viable if the farm is within 40 miles of a large population and mentions selling to restaurants and grocery stores as examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740502</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Restaurants">
      <data key="d0">Restaurants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Restaurants are a significant business category and a potential market for farmers, offering prices slightly better than wholesale. They represent a valuable sales channel for agripreneurs who are direct-marketing. Specialty restaurants are particularly important targets, though they are very particular regarding produce quality, quantity, and consistent delivery schedules. While some large-volume food-service companies, like Chipotle Mexican Grill, may find it challenging to source directly from individual small farmers due to quantity requirements, restaurants in general, possess immense buying power and can be powerful change agents within the local food system. They can support farmers significantly by committing to practices like the whole-animal approach and developing innovative cuisine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07373ff8a8e2c42149614780aa736a26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grocery Stores">
      <data key="d0">Grocery Stores</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A retail outlet where the author's catfish sold much better than trout, influencing the decision to focus on raising catfish."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A type of retail business that can purchase produce directly from farms, mentioned as a potential sales channel for direct-marketing.&lt;SEP&gt;Retail organizations that sell food and other goods. They are mentioned as a potential sales channel for mushrooms, small fruits, fruit and nut trees, and flowers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Location">
      <data key="d0">Farm Location</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key strategic factor for a farm's success, influencing marketing opportunities (direct-marketing, agritourism), logistics (hauling crops), and access for family and visitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Machinery">
      <data key="d0">Machinery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Machinery, in a farm context, refers to any piece of equipment or tool, ranging from hand tools to larger items like tractors and tillers, whose primary purpose is to save time, reduce labor, and allow more work to be done faster. The specific machinery needs and costs for a farm are determined by its unique crop and livestock plans. For machinery to be a worthwhile investment, its cost must be balanced against the time it saves, and that saved time should be used for other profitable activities, such as planning or marketing.

It is crucial to match machinery capacity with available labor to ensure timeliness in farm operations. Efficiency can also be enhanced through a smart farm layout. To reduce costs and prolong its lifespan, machinery requires proper maintenance and protection from rain and sun, which can cause rust and weather wear. For this reason, it is typically stored in a machine shed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Enhancers">
      <data key="d0">Soil Enhancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of natural inputs and processes that improve soil health. Examples include nitrogen fixation, crop rotations, organic matter from plants, and beneficial organisms like worms and bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nitrogen Fixation">
      <data key="d0">Nitrogen Fixation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The biological process by which legumes, with the help of Rhizobia bacteria, convert atmospheric nitrogen into a form that can be used by plants, storing it in their roots."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A natural process, performed by leguminous plants and free-living bacteria like Azotobacters, that converts atmospheric nitrogen into a form usable by plants, reducing the need for fertilizer.&lt;SEP&gt;The biological process of converting atmospheric nitrogen into a form usable by plants. The text details how this is achieved by legumes and Rhizobia bacteria and is influenced by soil conditions like pH, temperature, and mineral content.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trees">
      <data key="d0">Trees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of plants mentioned for their ability to create protective microclimates on a farm, which can enhance the productivity of crops and livestock by shielding them from extreme weather.&lt;SEP&gt;A multi-purpose free energy source on a farm. They can be used as windbreaks, provide cooling shade for livestock, and serve as a fuel or building source.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Energy From Livestock">
      <data key="d0">Energy From Livestock</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept describing the benefits livestock provide beyond their primary products. This includes naturally plowing pastures and gardens and producing manure to enrich the soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Good Farming Principles">
      <data key="d0">Good Farming Principles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A set of fundamental guidelines for successful farming. Key principles mentioned include careful planning, making the farm profitable and sustainable, avoiding debt, keeping costs low, and timeliness."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A set of universal rules for effective farming that apply globally, such as conserving soil and improving fertility. These are presented as distinct from site-specific practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Debt">
      <data key="d0">Debt</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A financial principle for farmers. The text strongly advises avoiding debt, comparing it to a middleman that reduces profit. Start-up debt is considered acceptable only under strict conditions, with an emphasis on growing into an enterprise rather than borrowing."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A financial concept concerning borrowed capital, listed in the index as a critical topic in farm business management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Author">
      <data key="d0">Author</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"The narrator of the text, who provides advice and outlines principles for new farmers based on their experience and knowledge."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Farmer">
      <data key="d0">New Farmer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"The target audience of the text, referred to as 'you'. This individual is planning to start a farm and is seeking guidance on how to succeed."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hogs">
      <data key="d0">Hogs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hogs are a type of livestock used as a primary example throughout the text to illustrate various agricultural and business concepts. They are central to discussions on profitability, where the author details making a significant net profit by raising them pasture-fed without antibiotics or hormones and selling them directly to consumers as value-added products like sausage or meat cuts. This direct-to-consumer, niche marketing approach avoids low market prices from packers and is suggested as a path to success, along with focusing on purebred or rare breeds like the Mulefoot and Tamworth.

From a husbandry perspective, Hogs are multiple-birth animals whose ovulation and number of offspring can be enhanced by an increasing level of nutrition. Their management requires providing a source of freestanding water in the winter and ensuring perimeter fences, such as hog panels, are strong enough to contain them. They are susceptible to roundworm larvae, an infection process that requires moisture and rainfall. Hogs can also be integrated into farm rotation systems, where they pasture, turn up the soil for future crops like potatoes, and contribute manure, thereby improving soil fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e2a281158355949bfa9c35a51c5622a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e9fc423425c82be2483c04c11e6577f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Legumes">
      <data key="d0">Legumes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, Legumes are a class of high-protein, nitrogen-fixing plants that includes varieties such as clover, alfalfa, soybeans, vetch, peas, and lespedeza. They play a crucial role in agriculture due to their ability to enrich the soil. This is achieved through a symbiotic relationship with Rhizobia bacteria in their root nodules, which allows them to "fix" atmospheric nitrogen, replenishing this key nutrient in the soil. Consequently, legumes are widely used as cover crops, green manure, and are a vital component in crop rotation, often alternated with crops that have low biomass root systems, like corn.

In addition to their soil-enhancing properties, legumes are also valuable as feed for livestock. They are recommended for "flushing" ewes to increase ovulation. When harvested for hay, particularly plants like alfalfa, the timing is critical; legumes have the highest nitrogen content and quality at their early bloom stage. After this point, the quality of the hay declines even as the overall yield may increase.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grasses">
      <data key="d0">Grasses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop that generally has a lower relative feed value (Rfv) than legumes. However, the text states that a sufficient quantity of grass can compensate for its lower quality in feeding livestock."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A type of plant frequently mentioned in the text. Many grasses are xerophytes tolerant of low rainfall and are a primary food source for livestock in regions like the western United States and Southwestern Texas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Research">
      <data key="d0">Research</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key activity recommended for new farmers. It involves reading, visiting libraries, talking to other farmers, and consulting experts to make informed decisions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Soundness">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Soundness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A core component of sustainable farming. It involves using cropping and livestock systems that do not harm the environment, such as by preventing soil erosion."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740503</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Acceptability">
      <data key="d0">Social Acceptability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A core component of sustainable farming, referring to farming practices that are not opposed by the community. The text uses large, concentrated feedlots as an example of a practice that may lack social acceptability."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Costs">
      <data key="d0">Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A financial aspect of farming that the text advises to keep low. It is presented as a principle of good farming, with the suggestion to start new ventures on a small scale to save money."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Inputs">
      <data key="d0">Low Inputs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farming principle and strategy that involves minimizing expenditures. This approach is recommended to save money, improve soil, and enhance sustainability."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A farming principle focused on minimizing expenditures to save money, improve soil quality, and enhance operational sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Timeliness">
      <data key="d0">Timeliness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An important operational principle of farming that involves accomplishing tasks on time. It requires a match between the farm's labor and machinery capabilities."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time Management">
      <data key="d0">Time Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core farming principle that involves synchronizing labor and machinery with tasks and adhering to the seasonal and cyclical nature of farming. Examples include timing calving seasons and sizing fields for a day's work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Layout">
      <data key="d0">Farm Layout</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The physical and strategic arrangement of a farm's components, including travel lanes, fields, pastures, sheds, and gardens. A well-planned layout is designed to minimize work and maximize the efficiency of moving livestock and machinery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sheep">
      <data key="d0">Sheep</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sheep are an agricultural enterprise raised for meat, dairy, and wool. The wool market requires value-adding. Meat breeds such as Katahdin Hair, dairy breeds such as East Friesian, and trophy hunting breeds such as Barbados Blackbelly, are all types of sheep. Sheep farming operations are often based on one ewe with a spring lambing schedule, assuming a 150% lamb crop and a 20% replacement rate, although complete financial and labor details are not always available. Ewes (female sheep) have high nutritional needs, especially for ovulation and nursing, which can be met with high-quality pasture like legumes. Sheep can thrive with little or no free-standing water if they have access to good pasture, and along with cattle, are grazed in arid regions like Southwestern Texas. While they can often subsist on water provided by nature, they still require some access to it. The Katahdin Hair breed exemplifies their hardiness. As small livestock, sheep are particularly susceptible to worm parasites in warm, humid weather. They also use cedar groves for shelter during lambing and are often a focus of pasture rotation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sheep are described as grazing animals that 'look down' to eat grass, contrasting with goats who are 'browsers'. They were used alongside goats in Dr. Gelbard's anecdote about his lawn.&lt;SEP&gt;Sheep were part of a failed experiment at Polyface Farm. Joel Salatin concluded they were not a good fit for his system because they competed with the cows for resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e9fc423425c82be2483c04c11e6577f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd285deff310269197e8718b28f6076b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil">
      <data key="d0">Soil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical natural resource in the text, subject to quality and contaminant testing by Growing Gardens. The organization has a protocol to test for issues like lead before installing a garden.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil is a fundamental natural resource and a vital component of the farm environment, serving as the foundation for plant growth and overall farm sustainability. It is described as both a living system and a physical structure, composed of organisms, particles, water, and air. The health and fertility of soil, particularly in the topsoil or plow layer, are crucial for farming. Different types of soil, such as sandy or clay soils, impact water retention and ground warming. Maintaining and improving soil health involves various agricultural practices, including preventing erosion, crop rotation, increasing organic matter, using cover crops, and careful rotation with livestock to build nutrient reserves. The presence of organisms like worms and Rhizobia is essential for soil health. Water is also vital, as it supports microorganisms and facilitates the transfer of nutrients to plants. Practices like utilizing poultry droppings, cover crop root systems, and encouraging earthworm activity can enhance water retention and fertility, ultimately contributing to sustainable farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Record Keeping">
      <data key="d0">Record Keeping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Record Keeping is a crucial and fundamental practice within farming, identified as a core principle of agripreneurship and an essential farm management activity. Considered a short, intermediate, and long-term goal, its main purpose is to enable profitable long-term decisions. This is achieved by understanding the cost of production for each enterprise, which is vital for effective planning and pricing. The practice involves tracking and maintaining a wide range of key data, including detailed financial records like expenditures and sales tax records, animal performance data such as livestock breeding schedules and bloodline papers for purebred stock, crop yields, and environmental factors like weather for managing microclimates. Maintaining a diary is also a key component of comprehensive record keeping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-766988b011c2a15c7312ce3e9bb55cf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farmer Skills">
      <data key="d0">Farmer Skills</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of practical abilities that are important for a farmer to learn to reduce costs and operate sustainably. These include veterinary skills, carpentry, electrical work, machinery repair, and stockman skills for handling animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gentle Livestock">
      <data key="d0">Gentle Livestock</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of livestock characterized by a calm and non-nervous temperament. The text states they reproduce and grow better, are less stressed, and can be used to help manage the entire herd.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calving">
      <data key="d0">Calving</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The event of cows giving birth. It is used as an example of time management, where creating a close calving season leads to a uniform-sized calf crop and more focused attention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Culling">
      <data key="d0">Culling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The important management practice of selectively removing certain animals from a herd, mentioned as a key aspect of raising herd-type animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Acreages">
      <data key="d0">Small Acreages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farm characterized by its small size. This size is presented as an advantage for good timing, allowing farmers to complete tasks quickly and efficiently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cost Of Production">
      <data key="d0">Cost Of Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical financial metric that a farmer must know for each enterprise. Calculating it requires careful record-keeping of all inputs like feed, pasture, and equipment costs.&lt;SEP&gt;A critical financial metric that a farmer must know on a per-unit basis (bushel, plant, animal, acre). It is determined through consistent record keeping and is essential for setting prices and making profitable decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Water System">
      <data key="d0">Water System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The infrastructure for providing and storing water on a farm. A good system, such as a pond with a pump and pipes, is considered essential for livestock and minimizes labor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buildings">
      <data key="d0">Buildings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Farm structures like sheds. The text advises on their strategic placement within the farm layout for efficiency and the importance of their maintenance to reduce long-term costs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b7a7522a05ce3cf5aba22a772ae6607</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Fertility">
      <data key="d0">Soil Fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Soil Fertility is a central and foundational concept representing the health, nutrient content, and overall capacity of the soil to sustain agricultural plant growth. Described as the nutritional basis of a farm, it is created by a combination of weathered rock and organic matter from dead plants and animals, and its development is directly influenced by the climate.

Considered a crucial, lifetime goal for a farm, as well as a core principle of agripreneurship and a tenet of sustainable agriculture, maintaining and improving soil fertility is vital for farm productivity, success, and profitability. This is especially true on smaller acreages, where a high level of fertility becomes more important to produce the necessary volume. The management of soil fertility is presented as a delicate balance between taking nutrients from the soil through harvesting and replenishing them, as every crop consumes these resources. This improvement is a long, slow process achieved through various methods, including the primary technique of crop rotation. Other key practices involve adding organic matter through composting, applying animal and green manures, and utilizing cover crops and legumes, with livestock also playing a role. The text notes that some contributing factors for its maintenance are sourced from Clyde E. Leighty. Beyond crop support, healthy, fertile soil offers the additional benefits of being able to absorb more water and prevent erosion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Animal Manures">
      <data key="d0">Animal Manures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A source of organic matter used to improve soil fertility. The text advocates for the natural method of having animals spread their own manure on pasture as they graze, which is more efficient than mechanically spreading it."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diversified Farming">
      <data key="d0">Diversified Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farming strategy where a variety of crops and/or animals are raised. This approach is presented as essential for sustainability, as it provides protection from adverse weather or price fluctuations affecting a single product and allows for year-round income."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organic Farming">
      <data key="d0">Organic Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Organic farming is a certified method of agriculture that avoids the use of all synthetic chemicals, pesticides, fertilizers, and additives. To achieve certification, the land must be free of chemical fertilizers or sprays for a minimum of three years, a process that allows the soil to regenerate and become fertile. A central focus of this farming approach is soil renewal and health management, with practices like crop rotation being a key principle; a book by Nicolas Lampkin is cited as an authoritative source on modern rotation design. As practitioners avoid chemical inputs, they must incorporate alternative solutions for pest and disease control.

Economically, organic farming is often characterized by a low volume of production, with goods typically sold at premium prices to direct or niche markets. The practice is supported by a network of dedicated resources, including organizations like the Maine Organic Farmers and Gardeners Association, publications such as 'The Natural Farmer' and 'The Maine Organic Farmer &amp; Gardener', online resources like the Organic Pages Online, and specialized suppliers like Peaceful Valley Farm &amp; Garden Supply.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c07830327394a77dc9e8ac1a53acd63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfed52d46f5bf9d5489c4d6b6dddd2cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Student Garden">
      <data key="d0">Student Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A 4-acre garden in Santa Cruz, California, which serves as a case study for soil improvement. Starting with poor clay soil, students used organic methods to create an excellent fertile soil within three years."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A 4-acre student-run project in Santa Cruz, California, which successfully transformed a hillside with poor clay soil into fertile land using organic methods over a 3-year period. It serves as an example of successful soil improvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Santa Cruz, California">
      <data key="d0">Santa Cruz, California</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The location of the 4-acre Student Garden, which is used in the text as an example of successful soil rehabilitation using organic methods."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A location in California that is home to the 4-acre Student Garden, which was started on a hillside with clay soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Organic Matter">
      <data key="d0">Soil Organic Matter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key component of soil health that determines the soil's ability to absorb rainwater and prevent erosion. The text specifies that soil with 4-5% organic matter is significantly more absorbent than soil with only 2%."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Livestock Management">
      <data key="d0">Livestock Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A central theme concerning the principles of efficient and natural animal husbandry. This includes practices like having animals harvest their own feed (pasture grazing) and spread their own manure, as well as planning for feed reserves."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pasture Grazing">
      <data key="d0">Pasture Grazing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An efficient livestock management technique and principle where animals are kept on pasture to spread their own manure and harvest their own feed. This method saves significant labor, fuel, and machinery costs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feed Storage">
      <data key="d0">Feed Storage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key principle of farm preparedness. The text advises storing two years' worth of hay and grain to protect the farm against risks like prolonged winters, droughts, and the resulting high feed prices."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Marketing">
      <data key="d0">Farm Marketing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A crucial skill and principle for a sustainable farm. It involves strategies such as diversifying products for year-round sales, offering unique items, diversifying marketing methods, and controlling prices."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A specific business topic concerning how to market farm-fresh goods, which is covered in the book 'Starting &amp; Running Your Own Small Farm Business'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-516da735be61616733781bd48e941fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Least-Cost Production">
      <data key="d0">Least-Cost Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A fundamental business principle highlighted as essential for farms to survive market volatility and achieve sustainability and profitability."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Profitability">
      <data key="d0">Farm Profitability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A primary goal of farming operations, presented as a direct result of being a sustainable, least-cost producer with healthy soil and effective management."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Composting">
      <data key="d0">Composting</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Composting is a crucial agricultural process and natural method for recycling organic matter like leaves and food scraps into valuable fertilizer. It is used to improve and maintain soil fertility on both large and small scales. Will Allen and Growing Power heavily emphasize composting, including vermicomposting, as the foundational step for creating healthy soil for healthy food production. Growing Power uses composting to transform over 100,000 pounds of weekly community waste into rich soil fertilizer, referring to it as "black gold." The heat generated during decomposition is also used as a green energy source to warm greenhouses during Wisconsin winters. Composting, which involves plowing organic matter back into the soil, is a practice related to urban agriculture and is highlighted on the Growing Power website and in BioCycle magazine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e0df78d09fb33ee041307abbce7d33e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Green Manures">
      <data key="d0">Green Manures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A soil amendment method, used in conjunction with cover crops, that is identified as a way to improve and help soil fertility."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemical Fertilizers and Sprays">
      <data key="d0">Chemical Fertilizers and Sprays</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Chemical inputs whose absence for three or more years is a core requirement for a farm's land to become organically certified. Their disuse is said to allow the soil to 'return to life'."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9bb17f477fad911f5e90130ee2a67840</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-P-K (Nitrogen, Phosphorus, Potassium)">
      <data key="d0">N-P-K (Nitrogen, Phosphorus, Potassium)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A trio of key chemical nutrients (nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium) commonly added to soil by farmers for specific crop rotations, such as corn and soybean. The text contrasts this simple approach with the greater complexity of truly healthy soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Types">
      <data key="d0">Soil Types</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification system for soils based on particle size, materials, and organic content. The text identifies sandy, clay, and loam as the three principal types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sandy Soils">
      <data key="d0">Sandy Soils</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A soil type characterized by up to 70% sand, known for being 'light,' draining readily, and having lower nutrient content. They can be improved with organic matter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clay Soils">
      <data key="d0">Clay Soils</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A soil type containing at least 35% clay, characterized by tightly packed particles, poor drainage, and being sticky when wet and hard when dry. It requires careful management to avoid damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Loam Soils">
      <data key="d0">Loam Soils</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A desirable soil type that is a mixture of sand (45%), silt (40%), and clay (15%). It combines the best characteristics of sand and clay, having good aeration, drainage, and water-holding capacity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultisols">
      <data key="d0">Ultisols</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A major soil order found in the southeastern United States. It is a highly weathered soil with subsurface clay that stores water and nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mollisols">
      <data key="d0">Mollisols</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A major soil order found primarily in the midwestern United States. It is characterized by a fertile surface layer with high organic content, making it excellent for crops like corn, soybeans, and wheat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alfisols">
      <data key="d0">Alfisols</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A productive soil order found in the mideastern states of the United States that requires careful management to maintain its fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mideastern States">
      <data key="d0">Mideastern States</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A region in the United States where Alfisols, a type of productive soil, are commonly found. These soils require careful management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Orders">
      <data key="d0">Soil Orders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A high-level classification system for soils. The text mentions there are eighteen different orders in the United States, with ultisols, mollisols, and alfisols being major examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organic Matter">
      <data key="d0">Organic Matter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Organic matter is a crucial component of healthy and fertile soil, composed of decomposed biological material derived from dead and decaying plants and animals. It is most concentrated in the topsoil, where it improves the soil's structure and nutrient content, proving especially beneficial for sandy soils. Organic matter serves as a primary food source for soil organisms like earthworms, bacteria, and fungi, and its presence is key to increasing populations of beneficial microbes, thereby enhancing overall soil fertility. The supply of organic matter can be depleted when soil is worked, as this increases microbial action. However, its levels can be maintained or increased through practices like crop rotation, particularly with sod crops, and by tilling green-manure crops into the soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Layers">
      <data key="d0">Soil Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The natural stratification of soil into distinct levels. The text describes a structure of three layers: the top layer (high in organic matter), the subsoil, and the substratum (parent rock).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-498630940c856e5feff77c3e963f5210</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Living Soil">
      <data key="d0">Living Soil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Living Soil is described as a complex ecosystem teeming with life, including bacteria, worms, and fungi, primarily in the topsoil. It is more than just mineral particles and requires food, water, and air to function and reproduce."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Earthworms">
      <data key="d0">Earthworms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Earthworms are beneficial organisms with a significant ecological role and are also the subject of culture and farming. As the largest of the soil's creatures, earthworms perform several crucial tasks to improve soils. These tasks include aerating the soil, moving organic matter, and helping the soil hold water, making a worm-friendly environment a key strategy for on-farm water conservation. Earthworms also fertilize the soil through their castings, which are rich in nutrients such as nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium.

In addition to their natural role, earthworms can be raised in farming operations. A model for such an operation estimates a production of 4 pounds per square foot of worm bed. This model shows a potential net profit of $760.00 from an income of $840.00 (generated by selling 840 pints), with variable costs amounting to $80.00 and requiring 112 hours of labor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2920f4362eb05a4692fa915ca812b425&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Henry Hopp">
      <data key="d0">Henry Hopp</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A USDA researcher who conducted extensive studies on worms in the 1940s, noting how they move through soil and deposit fertilizing castings underground."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Night Crawlers (Lumbricus Terrestris)">
      <data key="d0">Night Crawlers (Lumbricus Terrestris)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A species of earthworm that is not native to the United States but has adapted well to cultivated fields."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bacteria">
      <data key="d0">Bacteria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Microscopic soil organisms, including aerobic and anaerobic types, that are crucial for soil fertility. They can number up to 2 tons per acre and are responsible for making nitrogen and sulfur compounds usable by plants."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Azotobacter">
      <data key="d0">Azotobacter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A free-living bacterium found in soil that fixes nitrogen from the soil and air into its body tissue. Its population can be increased by adding organic matter."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rhizobium">
      <data key="d0">Rhizobium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A nitrogen-fixing bacterium that occurs naturally on leguminous plants like peas and beans. It converts atmospheric nitrogen into a form that plants can use."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actinomycete">
      <data key="d0">Actinomycete</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of bacteria that also shares similarities with fungi. They act as decomposers of organic matter and aid in humus formation, thriving in neutral pH soils."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fungi">
      <data key="d0">Fungi</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of soil organisms that includes mold, yeasts, and mushrooms. They obtain nutrients by decomposing organic material, with some, like mycorrhiza, being beneficial to plants."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mycorrhiza">
      <data key="d0">Mycorrhiza</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of mycelium fungus that forms a mutually beneficial (symbiotic) relationship with the roots of about 80% of agricultural plants, helping them absorb nutrients and water."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between a fungus and plant roots, found in about 80% of agricultural plants. This association is beneficial as the fungus helps the plant absorb nutrients and water from the soil."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Zealand">
      <data key="d0">New Zealand</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A country mentioned alongside Australia where pasture production significantly increased after the introduction of earthworms."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A country where farmers using management-intensive grazing are reportedly achieving high yields of 700 pounds of beef per acre without irrigation or fertilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topsoil">
      <data key="d0">Topsoil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Also referred to as the 'plow layer,' it is the upper 6 or 7 inches of soil. This layer is where most soil organisms and the majority of a plant's roots are concentrated."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"The top 6 or 7 inches of soil, which is where the vast majority of soil organisms, such as insects, earthworms, bacteria, and fungi, inhabit."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nitrogen">
      <data key="d0">Nitrogen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nitrogen is a crucial chemical element and an essential nutrient for plant growth and overall soil fertility, often acting as the limiting factor. Good nitrogen levels are necessary for promoting deep root growth in plants. The management of nitrogen is critical, as it can be depleted by nitrogen-using crops like corn. Its levels can be replenished through several methods, including the application of manure and the practice of crop rotation, which involves alternating nitrogen-using crops with nitrogen-fixing crops like legumes (e.g., soybeans). This replenishment relies on biological nitrogen fixation, a process where vital soil bacteria, such as Azotobacter and Rhizobium, convert nitrogen into a usable form that plants can absorb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plant Roots">
      <data key="d0">Plant Roots</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Plant roots are the part of the plant that connects it to the soil, absorbing nutrients and water. They can have a mass up to three times that of the aboveground plant and also contribute to soil health by providing dead tissue as food for microorganisms and creating a structure that prevents erosion. The area around them is known as the rhizosphere."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"The part of a plant that anchors it and absorbs water and nutrients. Plant roots form a critical symbiotic relationship with Mycorrhiza fungi and contribute to the health of the living soil ecosystem."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leguminous Plants">
      <data key="d0">Leguminous Plants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of plants, such as peas and beans, that host Rhizobium bacteria on their roots. This relationship allows for the fixation of atmospheric nitrogen, making it available to the plant."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Worm Castings">
      <data key="d0">Worm Castings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The manure produced by earthworms, which acts as a powerful natural fertilizer. It is significantly richer in nitrogen, phosphorus, magnesium, potassium, and calcium than the surrounding soil."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cover Crop">
      <data key="d0">Cover Crop</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop planted to protect and improve the soil. It is specifically mentioned as a method to maintain a high population of earthworms by insulating the ground during cold weather."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A crop planted to keep the soil covered, preventing erosion and reducing weeds. It is listed as a factor in maintaining soil fertility. Rye and hairy vetch serve as examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plants">
      <data key="d0">Plants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"General flora whose growth is highly dependent on soil health and the availability of nutrients like nitrogen. About 80% of agricultural plants engage in a beneficial relationship with Mycorrhiza fungi."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Humus">
      <data key="d0">Humus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A stable organic component of soil formed by the decomposition of organic matter. Its presence encourages the growth of beneficial bacteria like Rhizobium and Azotobacter, and its formation is aided by Actinomycetes."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sulfur">
      <data key="d0">Sulfur</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A chemical element and plant nutrient. The text notes that soil bacteria are responsible for making sulfur compounds available for plant uptake."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phosphorus">
      <data key="d0">Phosphorus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key plant nutrient mentioned alongside nitrogen as being necessary to activate root growth."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"An essential plant nutrient that is highly concentrated in worm castings, where it is found at 7 times the level of the surrounding soil."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Potassium">
      <data key="d0">Potassium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An essential plant nutrient that is highly concentrated in worm castings, where it is found at 11 times the level of the surrounding soil."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calcium">
      <data key="d0">Calcium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An essential plant nutrient that is highly concentrated in worm castings, where it is found at 1.5 times the level of the surrounding soil."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnesium">
      <data key="d0">Magnesium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An essential plant nutrient that is highly concentrated in worm castings, where it is found at 3 times the level of the surrounding soil."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H. Dittmer">
      <data key="d0">H. Dittmer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"H. Dittmer is a researcher cited in the text for work published in the American Journal of Botany. Dittmer's research quantified the vastness of a single rye plant's root system, finding it had a length of 377 miles and a surface area of over 1.5 acres."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Journal Of Botany">
      <data key="d0">American Journal Of Botany</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The American Journal of Botany is a scientific publication mentioned in the text for publishing H. Dittmer's research on plant root systems in 1937–38."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roman Times">
      <data key="d0">Roman Times</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Roman Times is a historical period mentioned as the origin of the agricultural practice of crop rotation, when farmers began using rotations to replenish the fertility of their fields."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrients">
      <data key="d0">Nutrients</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Chemical elements and compounds essential for plant growth and soil organism function. They are cycled through the soil, absorbed by plants, and can be replenished or depleted by different agricultural practices like crop rotation."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Essential substances for plant growth, such as nitrogen and phosphorus. Cover crops help retain these in the soil, while excessive microbial action can deplete them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Microorganisms">
      <data key="d0">Soil Microorganisms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A collective term for the living organisms within the soil, including bacteria and fungi. They are essential for breaking down dead tissue (like from plant roots) into nutrients and are most active in the rhizosphere."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rhizosphere">
      <data key="d0">Rhizosphere</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific, biologically active area immediately surrounding plant roots where many 'food transactions' between the plant and soil microorganisms occur, making it a hub of nutrient exchange."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rye Plant">
      <data key="d0">Rye Plant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific type of plant cited from H. Dittmer's research to illustrate the massive, extensive nature of a plant's root system, which can span hundreds of miles in length."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Air">
      <data key="d0">Air</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An essential gaseous component within soil, filling pore spaces not occupied by water. It is necessary for root function and the survival of soil organisms; a lack of air in waterlogged soils can kill plants."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Continuous Cropping">
      <data key="d0">Continuous Cropping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An agricultural practice of growing the same crop repeatedly on the same land. It is described as a detrimental method that leads to a rapid loss of soil organic matter and overall fertility."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subsoil">
      <data key="d0">Subsoil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The layer of soil beneath the topsoil. Deep-rooted plants can access the subsoil to draw up minerals, bringing them to the upper, more accessible soil layers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sod Crop">
      <data key="d0">Sod Crop</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop, typically grasses or legumes, used in a rotation to maintain the supply of organic matter in the soil and provide raw food for soil bacteria."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grain Crops">
      <data key="d0">Grain Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A class of crops that includes wheat and barley. They are identified as one of the three main types of crops to consider when planning an effective agricultural rotation."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Grain crops are a type of agricultural product that may require protection. The text suggests using temporary electrical fencing for this purpose."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cultivated Crops">
      <data key="d0">Cultivated Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A class of crops that includes corn and soybeans. They are identified as one of the three main types of crops to consider when planning an effective agricultural rotation."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insect Pests And Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Insect Pests And Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Harmful organisms and conditions that can damage crops. The text presents crop rotation as a natural method for breaking their life cycles and reducing their impact on a farm."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weed Species">
      <data key="d0">Weed Species</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Unwanted plants that compete with agricultural crops for resources like nutrients and water. Crop rotation is mentioned as a farming practice that helps in their elimination."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cardiff, United Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">Cardiff, United Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f1d34c0baad55bf46a357beac030d04</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Bee Research Association, a key source for beekeeping books, is located in Cardiff, United Kingdom.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Short-Term Rotation">
      <data key="d0">Short-Term Rotation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of crop rotation lasting one year or less, with examples including wheat/clover and rye/vetch. It also includes minor rotations for small, specific areas like a hog pasture or truck garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-Term Crop Rotations">
      <data key="d0">Long-Term Crop Rotations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of crop rotation that lasts from 2 to 5 years, described as more complex than short-term rotations and often including hay and sod crops for livestock.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clyde E. Leighty">
      <data key="d0">Clyde E. Leighty</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Author of 'Soils and Men' from the 'Usda yearbook of Agriculture, 1938'. His work is cited as the source for lists of crop rotation advantages and factors for maintaining soil fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Department of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Department of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A U.S. federal department that has 'Farm to School Initiatives' and published a fact sheet on the topic.&lt;SEP&gt;A U.S. federal department whose Agricultural Research Service (ARS) and National Agricultural Statistics Service (NASS) publish data on food nutrients, Colony Collapse Disorder, and honey production.&lt;SEP&gt;A U.S. government organization that published the 'Usda yearbook of Agriculture, 1938', which is a cited source in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soils and Men">
      <data key="d0">Soils and Men</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication by Clyde E. Leighty featured in the 1938 USDA yearbook. It is cited as the source for the lists of advantages of crop rotation and factors in maintaining soil fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Washington, Dc">
      <data key="d0">Washington, Dc</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Washington, Dc is the location of several key institutions. It is home to the US Department of Agriculture and serves as the publication city for "The Yearbooks of Agriculture," including the "Usda yearbook of Agriculture, 1938." Additionally, the city is where both the National Academy Press and D.C. Central Kitchen are located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a25021ab306ceaf5d58e192cc6fc0a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea1bfb736a960a0ceee22d5eb6f8e955</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nicolas Lampkin">
      <data key="d0">Nicolas Lampkin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Author of 'Organic Farming', cited as an expert on the principles of crop rotation for maintaining soil fertility, organic matter, and nutrient levels."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Author of 'Organic Farming', a book focusing on organic farming practices in Europe.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of the book 'Organic Farming', whose rotation design principles from England are adapted for U.S. circumstances in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2920f4362eb05a4692fa915ca812b425&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Norfolk Rotation">
      <data key="d0">Norfolk Rotation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific historical, long-term rotation from the 18th and 19th centuries, consisting of a root crop, barley, a legume, and wheat. It is noted for allowing the integration of livestock.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minor Rotations">
      <data key="d0">Minor Rotations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of short-term crop rotation where a small area is set aside for a specific short-term use, such as a truck garden, hog pasture, or lambing area. It can be part of a larger rotation or managed separately.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Clover">
      <data key="d0">Red Clover</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A slow-growing legume crop used in rotations and as a cover crop. It provides significant dry matter and fixes 70 to 150 pounds of nitrogen per acre."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A legume crop used in a short-term rotation example, where it is overseeded in spring into a fall-planted wheat crop and can be grazed or incorporated as green manure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rye">
      <data key="d0">Rye</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A cover crop, specifically mentioned as winter rye, that has an allelopathic effect, meaning it can suppress the germination and growth of weeds. It is often planted with hairy vetch to create biomass, improve soil tilth, and absorb leftover nitrogen for the next crop."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A grain crop used in short-term rotations, often planted with hairy vetch as a winter cover crop to be plowed under as green manure before planting a summer crop.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific non-leguminous cover crop highlighted for its ability to capture soil nitrogen, produce biomass, and prevent nutrient leaching and erosion, particularly in the Southern United States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hairy Vetch">
      <data key="d0">Hairy Vetch</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hairy Vetch is a legume used as both a cover crop and a green-manure crop, often sown with winter rye. It grows through the winter and is plowed under in the spring to enrich the soil for subsequent crops, such as tomatoes. Its primary function is fixing atmospheric nitrogen, a process at which it is highly effective, capable of adding up to 100 pounds of nitrogen per acre back into the soil. Additionally, Hairy Vetch is noted for its ability to retain phosphorus, which is also released into the soil when the plant is plowed under.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Potatoes">
      <data key="d0">Potatoes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crop with agricultural economic data provided per acre, including a gross income of $2,150.00, total variable costs of $1,086.00, a net profit of $1,064.00, and requiring 105 labor hours.&lt;SEP&gt;A vegetable crop planted in a minor rotation example, specifically after hogs have pastured on the land, benefiting from the tilled soil.&lt;SEP&gt;Potatoes are a specific type of crop that the text dedicates a section to, providing instructions on planting, hilling, and using straw as mulch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alfalfa">
      <data key="d0">Alfalfa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A legume crop mentioned in a minor rotation example. It contributes to soil fertility, similar to other legumes.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of forage that grows in bunches, contrasting with sod-forming grasses. It is also mentioned as a crop that, when irrigated in a management-intensive system, can produce very high beef yields of up to 2,000 pounds per acre.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Legume">
      <data key="d0">Legume</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A class of nitrogen-fixing plants, including clover, vetch, alfalfa, and soybeans, that are a critical component of crop rotations for introducing nitrogen into the soil and allowing for the integration of livestock.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barley">
      <data key="d0">Barley</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A grain crop that is a component of the four-year Norfolk rotation and other common three-year rotations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Manures">
      <data key="d0">Farm Manures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key factor in maintaining soil fertility. The text notes that crop rotation provides a practical system for using farm manures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Usda yearbook of Agriculture, 1938">
      <data key="d0">Usda yearbook of Agriculture, 1938</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific publication from the U.S. Department of Agriculture that contains Clyde E. Leighty's work, 'Soils and Men', which is cited multiple times in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b320d4bf5bae3f28f4bca97246cd735</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Green Manure">
      <data key="d0">Green Manure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Green manure refers to crops, often cover crops like winter rye and hairy vetch, that are grown specifically to be plowed, tilled, or disked back into the soil while they are still green. The primary purpose of this practice is to improve soil health and fertility. As the crops decompose, they add organic matter, which improves the soil's structure, and release stored nutrients, acting as a type of fertilizer. Legumes are particularly emphasized for their use as green manure because they effectively release nitrogen into the soil. This process can reduce the need for external fertilizers and also helps to control erosion and suppress weeds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rhizobia">
      <data key="d0">Rhizobia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of bacteria that forms a symbiotic relationship with legumes, living in nodules on the plant's roots. This connection allows the plant to take nitrogen from the air and convert it into a usable form."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A type of bacteria that lives in nodules on the roots of legume plants and is responsible for nitrogen fixation. An inoculant containing these bacteria may be needed to start the process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Orchard Grass">
      <data key="d0">Orchard Grass</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A plant with a high biomass of roots, mentioned alongside red clover as a crop to be alternated with low-biomass root crops like corn."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sun Hemp">
      <data key="d0">Sun Hemp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An example of an extremely fast-growing crop that should be alternated with slow-growing crops in a rotation strategy."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buckwheat">
      <data key="d0">Buckwheat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An example of an extremely fast-growing crop that should be alternated with slow-growing crops in a rotation strategy."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radishes">
      <data key="d0">Radishes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An example of an extremely fast-growing crop that should be alternated with slow-growing crops in a rotation strategy."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A specific crop enterprise highlighted as being the fastest-growing crop, yielding a harvest in only 21 days. They are used as an example for generating quick returns on investment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-766988b011c2a15c7312ce3e9bb55cf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Winter Wheat">
      <data key="d0">Winter Wheat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A slow-growing crop that is described as being more susceptible to weed pressure and should ideally follow a weed-suppressing crop."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740508</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allelopathic Effect">
      <data key="d0">Allelopathic Effect</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A biological ability possessed by some plants, like winter rye, to produce biochemicals that suppress the germination and growth of competing weeds."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weeds">
      <data key="d0">Weeds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Unwanted plants that compete with crops for resources. The text discusses their suppression through methods like cover crops, allelopathy, crop shading, and mechanical cultivation."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Weeds are described as plants that can be controlled either by mowing or, more efficiently, by using multiple-species grazing. The text notes that weeds are often high in trace minerals, making them a beneficial food source for grazing animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Tilth">
      <data key="d0">Soil Tilth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The physical condition of soil, particularly its suitability for crop cultivation. The text states that turning under the biomass from rye and vetch improves soil tilth."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microbiological Activity">
      <data key="d0">Microbiological Activity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The level of activity of microorganisms in the soil, which is a key indicator of soil health. It is improved by the use of cover crops and green manure."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to the processes carried out by microorganisms, like bacteria, in the soil. The text notes that plowing under a green-manure crop causes a large increase in this activity, aiding decomposition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beneficial Insects">
      <data key="d0">Beneficial Insects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Insects that are advantageous to agriculture. Cover crops are noted to provide a haven for them, potentially reducing the need for pesticides."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dr. C. J. Willard">
      <data key="d0">Dr. C. J. Willard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An expert on legumes from Ohio who conducted research on the nitrogen content of sweet clover, noting that the amount of nitrogen varies depending on the time of cutting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ohio">
      <data key="d0">Ohio</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Ohio is a state located in the Midwestern United States. It is a location for farming and other food-related activities. Dr. C.J. Willard conducted his research on legumes in Ohio. The Appalachian Center for Economic Networks (ACEnet) is geographically located in Ohio. Furthermore, Oberlin College, a pioneer in buying local food, is located in the state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2d3545603e988fef215052ef7f681c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cover Crops">
      <data key="d0">Cover Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cover crops are plants strategically sown, not for harvest, but primarily to manage soil health, prevent erosion, and manage nutrients. This specific agricultural practice is used to conserve soil and improve fertility, contributing to a farm's resilience and mitigating weather-related problems by building up organic matter in the soil. They are planted to cover the soil, suppress weeds, protect the soil, and can also provide a habitat for beneficial insects and break disease cycles. Both leguminous (e.g., vetch, cowpeas) and non-leguminous plants like winter rye and hairy vetch are utilized. These crops improve water absorption, and stop nutrient leaking by holding nitrogen and phosphorus in the soil. Cover crops can also provide grazing and hay for livestock within an integrated and diversified farming system, and be incorporated into the soil as green manure. The choice of cover crop is site-specific and depends on the region.&lt;SEP&gt;Crops planted to cover the soil rather than for the purpose of being harvested, listed as a sub-entry under 'crops'.&lt;SEP&gt;Plants, such as winter rye or vetch, that are planted to cover the soil rather than for harvest. They are used in no-till farming at Radical Roots to protect the soil and are sometimes winter-killed before planting the main crop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-legumes">
      <data key="d0">Non-legumes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A class of plants (such as rye, barley, and oats) that do not fix nitrogen but are valuable for producing large amounts of organic matter (biomass) and capturing existing soil nutrients. They are often planted with legumes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inoculant">
      <data key="d0">Inoculant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A substance containing Rhizobia bacteria that is applied to legume seeds to initiate the nitrogen-fixation process in the plant's roots. Obtaining the correct inoculant from a seed dealer is recommended.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organic Matter / Biomass">
      <data key="d0">Organic Matter / Biomass</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Decomposed plant and animal material in the soil. The text describes how cover crops, particularly non-legumes like rye, contribute large amounts of organic matter or biomass, which improves soil structure and nutrient-holding capacity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mowing Or Grazing">
      <data key="d0">Mowing Or Grazing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Agricultural practices used to manage plant growth. The text mentions them as ways to control the growth stages of legumes and non-legumes in a mixed stand, and as methods of harvesting pasture for livestock feed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seed Dealer">
      <data key="d0">Seed Dealer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Seed Dealer is mentioned, along with universities, as a source of new crop varieties whose innovations tend to provide more significant benefits to large-scale farmers than to small farmers.&lt;SEP&gt;A local commercial entity that supplies agricultural products. The text advises farmers to consult their local seed dealer to obtain the correct type of rhizobial inoculant for their specific legume crop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e64fb1fe152a62cd06403742bd6beac5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time Of Cutting">
      <data key="d0">Time Of Cutting</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the specific point in a plant's growth when it is cut or plowed under. The text highlights this as a critical factor that determines the amount of nitrogen a legume crop will yield.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Bloom">
      <data key="d0">Early Bloom</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific growth stage of a plant when it is beginning to flower. The text identifies this as the best time to incorporate (plow under) a legume for green manure because the plant is most succulent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil PH">
      <data key="d0">Soil PH</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A measure of the acidity or alkalinity of the soil. The text states that a pH level between 6.0 and 8.0 is optimal for the soil bacteria that perform nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Nutrients (Iron, Sulfur, Molybdenum)">
      <data key="d0">Soil Nutrients (Iron, Sulfur, Molybdenum)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Specific chemical elements required for healthy soil and plant processes. The text explicitly states that iron, sulfur, and molybdenum are necessary for nitrogen fixation to function properly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jim Gerrish">
      <data key="d0">Jim Gerrish</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A 'grassman' who, along with Allan Nation, changed the term 'intensive grazing' to 'management-intensive grazing' (Mig) to emphasize the importance of management in farming.&lt;SEP&gt;A farming expert, described as a 'grassman,' who co-created the term 'management-intensive grazing' (Mig) to emphasize the importance of management over simple intensity.&lt;SEP&gt;An intensive-grazing expert at the University of Missouri who provides specific guidelines for grazing, such as grazing grasses from 6-10 inches down to half their height.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Management-Intensive Grazing">
      <data key="d0">Management-Intensive Grazing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A grazing method involving a minimum of sixteen paddocks with frequent livestock rotation (every one or two days). It significantly increases forage use to 80-85% and allows for long rest periods for the pasture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sod Pastures">
      <data key="d0">Sod Pastures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of pasture with a thick, even covering of interlocked grasses, such as Kentucky bluegrass and perennial ryegrass. They are highlighted as being crucial for controlling soil erosion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Erosion Control">
      <data key="d0">Erosion Control</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key agricultural benefit discussed in the text. It is the process of preventing soil loss, which is effectively achieved through the use of sod pastures that reduce water runoff.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weaver and Noll">
      <data key="d0">Weaver and Noll</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers who conducted a study in 1935 that demonstrated the effectiveness of sod quality in preventing water runoff and soil erosion, quantifying soil loss on poor pasture at 4.6 tons per acre.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="USDA">
      <data key="d0">USDA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) is a federal government agency involved in various aspects of agriculture and food systems. It collaborates with organizations like the American Grassfed Association on grass-fed standards. The USDA's regulations concerning local food procurement can pose challenges for Farm to School programs. The USDA reimburses schools for meals served, but the reimbursement rate can leave limited funds for actual food costs. The USDA also provides nutritional data, such as the lower fat content of goat meat compared to chicken and beef. They offer grants, such as the one that funded the St. Johns Woods garden and its successor program, Village Gardens, and another awarded to Janus Youth for a community gardening project. The USDA inspects processing plants for large stock and approves kitchens for crop processing, a requirement for selling these products to the public. Meat sold to the public must be processed in a USDA-inspected facility. The USDA conducted studies in the 1930s that found crop rotation with sod-farming pastures increased usable rainfall. In 2009, the USDA created a Farm to School Team to connect schools with local producers. They also provide economic estimates on honeybee pollination, track honey production statistics, report on pollination needs for crops like California almonds, and have documented the decline in managed honey bee colonies since the 1940s through its Agricultural Research Service. While some gardens, like the Warren Wilson Garden, grow food organically, they may choose not to pursue the USDA's official organic certification. The USDA is also the parent department of the National Institute of Food and Agriculture (NIFA).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea6dd73e41a475c9933ac4783be5f57a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fef6f9e3cdaa27cfe32361bcab20e464&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16e699b4bb77d0bdc27d883c3cafa88c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27bb80a716045bb643a01fd3aae1d84b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alaska">
      <data key="d0">Alaska</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A US state used as a hypothetical example to illustrate the importance of matching livestock to climate, noting that raising catfish there would be cost-prohibitive due to the need for constant water heating.&lt;SEP&gt;A geographical location used as an example to illustrate that the principles of good farming are universal and work anywhere in the world.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hay">
      <data key="d0">Hay</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of harvested forage made from pasture grass that has grown to 12 inches or more. The text specifies that the highest-quality hay is produced when plants are mown at the early bloom stage.&lt;SEP&gt;A thick crop used in strip-cropping to prevent water flow and erosion. It can also be grazed by livestock and, when plowed under, adds organic matter to the soil.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of stored crop mentioned as essential for feeding livestock during the winter when pasture grasses are not sufficient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cattle, Sheep, or Goats">
      <data key="d0">Cattle, Sheep, or Goats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Livestock that are described as the 'combines' or harvesting machines in a management-intensive grazing system. Their genetics, along with management and weather, determine the upper limits of production in such a system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fescue">
      <data key="d0">Fescue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a cool-season grass, which is a type of mesophyte.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of grass used as an example in Missouri to illustrate how pasture rest times must be adjusted seasonally. It requires 60-90 days of rest in the summer but much less in the spring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kentucky Bluegrass">
      <data key="d0">Kentucky Bluegrass</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of grass that forms a thick, interlocking sod. It is presented as an example of a grass that is ideal for creating sod pastures to control soil erosion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perennial Ryegrass">
      <data key="d0">Perennial Ryegrass</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of grass, mentioned alongside Kentucky bluegrass, that is known for growing into sod pastures, which are important for effective erosion control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740511</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catfish">
      <data key="d0">Catfish</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, Catfish are a type of fish commonly raised in aquaculture systems for meat, personal consumption, and breeding. They are described as a highly profitable product that can be successfully raised in on-farm ponds and processed in on-farm facilities before being sold. Due to high demand from 'home-style' restaurants and local grocery stores, Catfish can sell in much larger quantities than other fish like trout, with one account noting sales were ten times higher. A critical factor for their efficient growth is the need for warm water, approximately 80 degrees. This requirement makes heating costs a potentially prohibitive factor in some climates, and as a result, raising Catfish in a cold region like Alaska would be cost-prohibitive, illustrating the importance of matching agricultural practices to the local climate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9d28e4a28ca972c9e289441c78c0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1935 Weaver and Noll Study">
      <data key="d0">1935 Weaver and Noll Study</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific scientific test conducted in 1935 by Weaver and Noll on an upland silt loam soil. The study provided quantitative evidence that poor pasture sod resulted in 75% water runoff and significant soil loss, while excellent sod had almost no erosion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1930s USDA Studies">
      <data key="d0">1930s USDA Studies</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A series of studies conducted by the USDA in the 1930s, cited by Allan Nation. These studies demonstrated that rotating cropland through sod-farming pastures significantly increased usable rainfall in Iowa compared to continuous corn cultivation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quality Pasture">
      <data key="d0">Quality Pasture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A book authored by Allan Nation. It is cited in the text as the source for information about the 1930s USDA studies on the benefits of sod-farming for water retention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Corn Belt">
      <data key="d0">Corn Belt</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Corn Belt is a nickname for a region, likely in the United States, where the temperature and climate are optimal for growing corn.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cotton Belt">
      <data key="d0">Cotton Belt</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cotton Belt is a nickname for a region where the climate is suitable for growing cotton.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southwestern Texas">
      <data key="d0">Southwestern Texas</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific arid region where sheep and cattle are grazed year-round on grasses that cure on the stem, demonstrating agricultural adaptation to climate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allan Nation">
      <data key="d0">Allan Nation</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"An author for the Stockman Grass Farmer who wrote an article quoting research from Dr. Tilak Dhiman."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A writer for 'The Stockman Grass Farmer' who has documented and reported on Joel Salatin's innovative farming techniques, including pastured egg production and pig-based land clearing.&lt;SEP&gt;Allan Nation is the author of the book "Quality Pasture," which focuses on creating, managing, and profiting from quality pasture. He is also the editor of the 'Stockman Grass Farmer' newspaper. Allan Nation, along with Jim Gerrish, is credited with redefining 'intensive grazing' as 'management-intensive grazing' (MIG). He is quoted regarding USDA studies on the benefits of sod-farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d6381862167ee0d108723409eae36d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52de62eaf5c15e94a0f74fc247e02ae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea1bfb736a960a0ceee22d5eb6f8e955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214f4e9d9f3d3fed74243c9bdf50bf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temperature">
      <data key="d0">Temperature</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key climatic factor that dictates which plants are suitable for a region, influences plant life cycles (annuals vs. perennials), and affects the rate of water loss through transpiration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Xerophytes">
      <data key="d0">Xerophytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of plants, such as cactus and gama grass, that are adapted to grow with very limited water. They are common in arid regions and are highly water-efficient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hydrophytes">
      <data key="d0">Hydrophytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of plants, such as water lilies, that grow in or near water. They represent the opposite environmental adaptation to xerophytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mesophytes">
      <data key="d0">Mesophytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of plants that require a moderate amount of water, fitting between the extremes of xerophytes and hydrophytes. This group includes most commonly grown crops like corn.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annual Plants">
      <data key="d0">Annual Plants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of plants whose life cycle is tied to temperature. They live for only one year, producing seeds and then dying when the temperature drops in the cold season.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perennials">
      <data key="d0">Perennials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of plants whose life cycle is adapted to seasonal temperature changes. They die back to the ground after a hard freeze but their root systems survive the winter to grow again the next year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Photosynthesis">
      <data key="d0">Photosynthesis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The biological process by which plants use sunlight to create energy. The text states that water is a necessary component for photosynthesis to occur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microorganisms">
      <data key="d0">Microorganisms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Organisms living in the soil that require water to survive. They can be beneficial (like teramycin) or harmful (like apple scab fungus).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Teramycin">
      <data key="d0">Teramycin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a beneficial microorganism found in soil, noted for its use as an antibiotic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740512</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apple Scab Fungus">
      <data key="d0">Apple Scab Fungus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a harmful microorganism whose spores are loosened and spread by large raindrops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Succulents">
      <data key="d0">Succulents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of xerophyte, such as cactus and milkweed, that has specialized water-storage cells to sustain them in arid environments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cactus">
      <data key="d0">Cactus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a succulent and xerophyte. It is also mentioned as one of the most water-efficient plants, using 250-300 pounds of water to produce one pound of dry matter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Milkweed">
      <data key="d0">Milkweed</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a succulent plant that contains water storage cells to survive in dry conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gama Grasses">
      <data key="d0">Gama Grasses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of grass mentioned as an example of a xerophyte with arid-tolerant modifications. It is also listed as one of the most water-efficient plants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curly Mesquite">
      <data key="d0">Curly Mesquite</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of plant mentioned as having xero-graphic (arid-tolerant) modifications, allowing it to survive in regions with little rain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saltbush">
      <data key="d0">Saltbush</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of plant mentioned as having xero-graphic (arid-tolerant) modifications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cattle">
      <data key="d0">Cattle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of livestock raised at Polyface farm. Joel Salatin mentions that fresh grass is a critical component for their health, similar to chickens and pigs.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of livestock raised on the Warren Wilson Farm for beef. The cattle are managed by a student crew, which includes Julius Stuart, and are grazed in pasture fields using techniques like mob-stocking.&lt;SEP&gt;Cattle are a major type of large livestock raised as an agricultural enterprise for beef or dairy. Perimeter fences, potentially made of woven or barbed wire, are necessary to contain them. Their raising and management includes consideration of feed, profiting from small beef operations, and handling and breeding. As ruminant livestock with a four-stomach digestive system, they can be raised to a marketable weight on forage alone. Their water requirements are significant, ranging from 50 to 90 gallons per day for a mature cow, and they require a freestanding source of water to drink. Certain breeds are adapted to specific climates, such as arid regions, and they are grazed on grasses in places like Southwestern Texas. In northern areas, cattle can learn to graze through snow. When considering cattle as a farm enterprise, options include focusing on direct markets, small breeds like Dexter and Miniature Herefords, or organic raising methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab7679e841cce082d6f5f864896c9afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e9fc423425c82be2483c04c11e6577f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2920f4362eb05a4692fa915ca812b425</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shrub Oak">
      <data key="d0">Shrub Oak</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of plant mentioned as having xero-graphic (arid-tolerant) modifications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Water Lilies">
      <data key="d0">Water Lilies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a hydrophyte, a plant that grows in or near water.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warm-season Grasses">
      <data key="d0">Warm-season Grasses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of mesophyte that grows in moderate water conditions. Big bluestem is given as an example.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Big Bluestem">
      <data key="d0">Big Bluestem</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a warm-season grass, which is a type of mesophyte.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cool-season Grasses">
      <data key="d0">Cool-season Grasses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of mesophyte that grows in moderate water conditions. Fescue is given as an example.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transpiration">
      <data key="d0">Transpiration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of water evaporation from plants. Its rate is affected by temperature and light intensity, and it is a major factor in a plant's overall water consumption.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pineapple">
      <data key="d0">Pineapple</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crop cited as an example of a highly water-efficient plant, using only 250 to 300 pounds of water to produce one pound of dry matter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Milo">
      <data key="d0">Milo</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crop cited as an example of a highly water-efficient plant, alongside pineapple and cactus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pumpkin">
      <data key="d0">Pumpkin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crop cited as an example of a highly inefficient plant in terms of water use, potentially requiring 1,000 pounds of water to produce one pound of dry matter.&lt;SEP&gt;A crop with agricultural economic data provided per acre, including a gross income of $2,800.00, total variable costs of $1,419.00, a net profit of $1,381.00, and requiring 25 labor hours.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squash">
      <data key="d0">Squash</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crop cited as an example of a highly water-inefficient plant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cucumber">
      <data key="d0">Cucumber</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crop cited as an example of a highly water-inefficient plant.&lt;SEP&gt;A crop enterprise that yields 12,500 lbs. per acre. It results in a net profit of $895.00 per acre and requires 153 hours of labor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a6d072f41d662cb326c1c528ac3c5c51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aquatic Plants">
      <data key="d0">Aquatic Plants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of plants that grow in water and have very different water needs compared to terrestrial plants, requiring about 10 pounds of water per pound of growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parasites">
      <data key="d0">Parasites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Parasites are organisms and diseases that negatively impact crops and livestock. The text mentions specific examples like tomato blight, rust (a wheat disease), and roundworms in animals, noting that they thrive in warm, humid weather."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Animal Shelter">
      <data key="d0">Animal Shelter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Animal Shelter refers to structures designed to protect livestock from harsh weather. The text describes the ideal characteristics of a shelter, suggesting they should be three-sided, portable, open to the south, and well-bedded, with specific height recommendations for smaller animals."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wheat Harvest">
      <data key="d0">Wheat Harvest</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Wheat Harvest is a seasonal agricultural event, specifically mentioned as occurring in the Midwest during the hot, dry days of July and August. Its success is threatened by a disease called rust, which is exacerbated by hot and humid weather."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="I">
      <data key="d0">I</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The author of the text, who is a farmer that shares personal experiences and practices, such as planning calf births for April and May and managing pastures with cedar groves for lambing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Santa Gertrudis Cattle">
      <data key="d0">Santa Gertrudis Cattle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific breed of cattle mentioned as an example of climate adaptation. They were developed for hot-weather areas and have shaker muscles to help them get rid of flies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Irish Dexter Cattle">
      <data key="d0">Irish Dexter Cattle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A dual-purpose (meat and milk) breed of small cattle. They were developed in mountainous areas with sparse vegetation and short growing seasons, demonstrating indirect climate adaptation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpacas">
      <data key="d0">Alpacas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A species of South American camelid mammal, which is the sole focus of 'Alpacas Magazine'.&lt;SEP&gt;A woolly animal that is well-suited for colder regions but finds it difficult to cope with high temperatures, illustrating climate-specific adaptation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goats">
      <data key="d0">Goats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on information from sources like Langston University Research &amp; Extension, Goats are a type of livestock animal with numerous uses. They are described as browsing animals that enjoy climbing but dislike rain, which makes them highly effective for land management tasks like clearing brush and invasive species.

As a significant agricultural enterprise, goats are raised for their healthy meat (known as cabrito, with suggestions to target ethnic markets and crossbreed with Boer goats), dairy products, and fiber from angora varieties. Additionally, they are used to create value-added products such as goat soaps. Beyond traditional farming, goats are also kept as backyard pets in urban settings. It is noted that goats, much like sheep, are prone to worm problems, particularly when warm temperatures are combined with rain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e9fc423425c82be2483c04c11e6577f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Turkeys">
      <data key="d0">Turkeys</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of livestock mentioned alongside hogs as being susceptible to infection by roundworm larvae.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tomato Blight">
      <data key="d0">Tomato Blight</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A plant disease that thrives in warm, humid weather and specifically attacks tomatoes. It causes leaves to yellow and fall off, reducing the plant's production or killing it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rust">
      <data key="d0">Rust</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A disease that affects wheat, particularly in hot and humid conditions. Its spores are carried by the wind over long distances, from Canada to Texas, infecting plants along the way.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roundworms">
      <data key="d0">Roundworms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of livestock parasite whose larvae require moisture and rainfall to be able to infect hosts such as hogs and turkeys.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cedars">
      <data key="d0">Cedars</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of tree that forms groves which serve as excellent natural shelter for calving or lambing sheep. They provide bedding from fallen needles and protection from snow and rain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Western Regions">
      <data key="d0">Western Regions</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical area characterized by a drier climate, where worm parasites in livestock are noted to be less of a problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northern Regions">
      <data key="d0">Northern Regions</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical area characterized by a cold climate, where worm parasites in livestock are mentioned as being less problematic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tomatoes">
      <data key="d0">Tomatoes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop described as risky and labor-intensive, requiring staking and pruning. Michael Porterfield recommends them for beginners to build confidence."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Tomatoes are a vegetable crop that the author planted, benefiting from the nitrogen provided by a preceding hairy vetch cover crop. This crop is vulnerable to tomato blight, a disease that strikes in hot, humid weather, causing leaf yellowing and drop, which reduces production. A research study at the Texas Agricultural Experiment Station demonstrated that creating a microclimate for tomato plants decreased losses and significantly increased growth and yield. Economically, tomatoes have a gross income of $1,360.00 per acre, with total variable costs of $623.00, resulting in a net profit of $737.00, and require 76 labor hours per acre.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mountainous Areas">
      <data key="d0">Mountainous Areas</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical environment with short growing seasons and sparse vegetation. It is mentioned as the origin environment for Irish Dexter cattle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50ae2b642515d199b40d344346490776</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Animal Shelters">
      <data key="d0">Animal Shelters</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Structures for housing livestock that are ideally portable, three-sided, open to the south, and well-bedded with straw. They are critical for protecting animals, especially poultry, from predators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chicken Tractor">
      <data key="d0">Chicken Tractor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A portable, open-bottomed cage used to house poultry. It allows the chickens to be moved daily across fields or gardens to eat insects and weeds while fertilizing the soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sheds">
      <data key="d0">Sheds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Essential farm buildings used to shelter machinery from weather damage like rust and wear, and also for storing feeds in a sealed, rodent-proof environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feeds">
      <data key="d0">Feeds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Animal food that is stored on the farm in a dedicated, sealed shed with a raised floor to protect it from rodents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conserving Water">
      <data key="d0">Conserving Water</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sustainable and inexpensive farming practice focused on retaining every drop of rain. Methods include using worm-friendly soil, cover crops, and building ponds to capture runoff.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ponds">
      <data key="d0">Ponds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Ponds are a farm feature that can be created as a water source for livestock or for other agricultural purposes."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An effective method for storing water runoff for later use in irrigation, livestock watering, and other purposes. They can also support aquaculture, such as raising catfish or freshwater shrimp, to provide additional farm income.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kentucky">
      <data key="d0">Kentucky</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A U.S. state mentioned as a northern location where raising freshwater shrimp in on-farm ponds is feasible.&lt;SEP&gt;Kentucky is a U.S. state where the organization Faith Feeds was founded and operates with the mission of alleviating hunger.&lt;SEP&gt;Kentucky is a US state mentioned as a location in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Winter Rye">
      <data key="d0">Winter Rye</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A cover crop used by the author and in Dick Raymond's rotation. It is valued for covering the ground to prevent erosion, retaining nitrogen, providing mulch, and suppressing weeds through its allelopathic properties."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A specific type of cover crop mentioned for its ability to absorb excess nitrogen in the soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740517</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smedley Unit">
      <data key="d0">Smedley Unit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of small, portable coop, originally designed for hogs, that can be adapted for use as a shelter for poultry to protect them at night.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insects And Weeds">
      <data key="d0">Insects And Weeds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pests and unwanted plants found in gardens and fields that can be managed naturally by poultry, such as chickens housed in a chicken tractor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rodents">
      <data key="d0">Rodents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pests that are a threat to stored farm feeds. Feed sheds must be carefully sealed to prevent them from entering and contaminating the supply.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Foliar Feeding">
      <data key="d0">Foliar Feeding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural practice of spraying liquid fertilizer directly onto the leaves of plants. This practice is facilitated by having an irrigation system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertilizer">
      <data key="d0">Fertilizer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Substances added to soil or plants to supply nutrients. It can be applied to crops through irrigation water or via foliar feeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Poultry">
      <data key="d0">Poultry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key type of livestock raised at Polyface farm. The summer internship program is heavily focused on teaching all aspects of raising poultry, from day-old chicks to marketing the final product.&lt;SEP&gt;Poultry are a type of livestock encompassing a broad range of farm birds, including chickens, turkeys, ducks, emus, guineas, geese, and peafowl. Raising poultry can be a comprehensive agricultural enterprise, with markets existing for purebreds, chicks, eggs, and meat, as well as niche opportunities like fly-tying feathers and ethnic markets. As simple, one-stomach animals, poultry benefit from pasturing, which reduces feed costs and promotes a healthier product. Due to their vulnerability to predators like coyotes, owls, and raccoons, poultry require secure, predator-proof shelters, such as a poultry house or a chicken tractor. Dedicated shelters are also important, and the livestock require a source of freestanding water to drink during the winter. State regulations apply to the home butchering of poultry. Information on raising various types of domestic poultry can be found in dedicated books, which cover topics such as breeds, rotational grazing, and portable pens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab7679e841cce082d6f5f864896c9afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e9fc423425c82be2483c04c11e6577f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea1bfb736a960a0ceee22d5eb6f8e955</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740870</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Freshwater Shrimp">
      <data key="d0">Freshwater Shrimp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of crustacean that can be raised in on-farm ponds through aquaculture, even as far north as Kentucky.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crayfish">
      <data key="d0">Crayfish</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of crustacean that can be farmed in on-farm ponds through aquaculture, viable even in northern climates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Planting And Cultivation">
      <data key="d0">Planting And Cultivation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The agricultural activities of sowing seeds and preparing the soil. These actions can lead to moisture loss and increase microbial action, which burns up nutrients and organic matter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microbial Action">
      <data key="d0">Microbial Action</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The activity of microorganisms within the soil. While part of a healthy ecosystem, stirring the soil can increase this action, causing it to burn up valuable nutrients and organic matter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Green-Manure Crops">
      <data key="d0">Green-Manure Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of plants, such as hairy vetch, that are plowed into the soil to release nutrients like phosphorus. They serve as a winter storage for plant nutrients, which become available to the next crop after being turned under in the spring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microclimate">
      <data key="d0">Microclimate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific area that is protected from the adverse effects of wind, weather, and temperature. Microclimates can occur naturally or be created artificially using techniques like windbreaks, raised beds, or protective coverings to enhance plant growth and extend the growing season.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Windbreak">
      <data key="d0">Windbreak</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A barrier, typically made of trees, designed to reduce wind velocity. Windbreaks prevent soil erosion, conserve heat and moisture for crops and livestock, and are a primary method for creating a larger microclimate. Their placement is based on prevailing wind directions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D. R. Paterson">
      <data key="d0">D. R. Paterson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher at the Texas Agricultural Experiment Station who co-authored a study on methods for protecting tomato plants. The research showed that creating a microclimate for the plants significantly decreased losses and increased growth and yield.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D. R. Earhart">
      <data key="d0">D. R. Earhart</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher at the Texas Agricultural Experiment Station who, along with D. R. Paterson, reported on a study involving the protection of tomato plants from wind and hail, which resulted in significant improvements in plant growth and fruit yield.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Texas Agricultural Experiment Station">
      <data key="d0">Texas Agricultural Experiment Station</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A research institution where studies were conducted by D. R. Paterson and D. R. Earhart on creating microclimates for individual plants, specifically tomatoes, to improve their resilience and yield.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Nebraska's Mead Research Station">
      <data key="d0">University of Nebraska's Mead Research Station</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A research facility where studies on permanent windbreaks were conducted. The research demonstrated that windbreaks significantly increased soybean yields by 18% and also led to substantial fuel savings for protected homes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soybean">
      <data key="d0">Soybean</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crop whose yields were shown to increase by 18 percent in fields with permanent windbreaks, according to research conducted at the University of Nebraska's Mead Research Station.&lt;SEP&gt;A crop whose yields were shown to increase by 18% when protected by windbreaks in a University of Nebraska study. Soybeans can also be protected by corn rows acting as a windbreak.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Raised Bed">
      <data key="d0">Raised Bed</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural technique for creating a microclimate. Raised beds can keep plants away from frost and provide warmer soil temperatures in the spring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Nebraska">
      <data key="d0">University of Nebraska</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The parent institution of the Mead Research Station, which conducted research on the positive effects of windbreaks on crop yields and fuel savings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cbfa7273b78ee011a6cb142bb82745a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University Of Nebraska">
      <data key="d0">University Of Nebraska</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An educational and research institution that conducted studies showing the economic and agricultural benefits of windbreaks, such as increased crop yields. Its research is cited multiple times in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mead Research Station">
      <data key="d0">Mead Research Station</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A research facility associated with the University of Nebraska. Studies conducted here showed that windbreaks increased soybean yields by 18%, corn yields by 20%, and wheat yields by 22%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="James R. Brandle">
      <data key="d0">James R. Brandle</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher and windbreak expert, associated with a University of Nebraska study, who found that windbreaks are economically viable. He also provides recommendations on windbreak length and, with Laurie Hodges, on planting patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bruce B. Johnson">
      <data key="d0">Bruce B. Johnson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who co-authored a University of Nebraska study on the economic viability of windbreaks alongside James R. Brandle and Terry Akeson.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terry Akeson">
      <data key="d0">Terry Akeson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who co-authored a University of Nebraska study on the economic viability of windbreaks alongside James R. Brandle and Bruce B. Johnson.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Department Of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Department Of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A state-level government agency that individuals can consult to determine the best kinds of trees for creating windbreaks in their local area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Red Cedar">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Red Cedar</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of evergreen tree mentioned as a good choice for windbreaks because it is fairly dense and does not lose its needles in winter, providing year-round protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laurie Hodges">
      <data key="d0">Laurie Hodges</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who, along with James R. Brandle, recommends specific planting patterns for annual windbreaks in areas without a predominant wind direction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wildlife Habitat">
      <data key="d0">Wildlife Habitat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A benefit provided by windbreaks, offering spaces for animals like birds to nest, which in turn can help control insect pests in fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insects">
      <data key="d0">Insects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pests in agricultural fields that can be controlled by birds nesting in windbreaks. They can also be lured away from cash crops by strips of small grains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medicinal Herbs">
      <data key="d0">Medicinal Herbs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of high-value plants, such as goldenseal and ginseng, that can be grown in the protected areas provided by windbreaks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goldenseal">
      <data key="d0">Goldenseal</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a high-dollar medicinal herb that can be cultivated in the suitable areas created by windbreaks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ginseng">
      <data key="d0">Ginseng</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a high-dollar medicinal herb that can be cultivated in the suitable areas created by windbreaks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brambles">
      <data key="d0">Brambles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of plants, including blackberries and raspberries, mentioned as a type of product that can be grown in the shelter of windbreaks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evergreens">
      <data key="d0">Evergreens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of tree recommended for windbreaks because they are dense and do not shed their needles, providing consistent, year-round protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deciduous Trees">
      <data key="d0">Deciduous Trees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of tree, also called broadleafs, which are noted as being less effective for windbreaks in winter because they lose their leaves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oak">
      <data key="d0">Oak</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a deciduous tree, which is contrasted with evergreens for use in windbreaks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perennial Grasses">
      <data key="d0">Perennial Grasses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of plant, along with legumes, that can be planted in strips to serve as an alternative to tree-based windbreaks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Grains">
      <data key="d0">Small Grains</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of crops, including rye, wheat, and oats, that can be planted in strips to function as windbreaks or as lure crops to protect cash crops from insects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vegetables">
      <data key="d0">Vegetables</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vegetables are a type of crop and produce that serve as a primary example in farm planning, where ideal growing conditions related to slope, soil, and moisture are crucial for success. Certain kinds of vegetables, such as onions and early-planted cole crops, benefit from the protected microclimate that can be created by windbreaks made from small grains. The peak harvest for vegetables can be very labor-intensive, sometimes requiring hired help in addition to family labor to maintain product quality for sale. Additionally, vegetables are a major topic in gardening literature, with entire bibliographic sections dedicated to books on subjects like growing specific varieties, home plant breeding, and sustainable biointensive organic horticulture. Highlighting the extensive variety within this category, one publication, "'s A-Z Guide to Growing Organic Food," is mentioned for covering 765 different types of vegetables.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a25021ab306ceaf5d58e192cc6fc0a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-516da735be61616733781bd48e941fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740520</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wheat Grass">
      <data key="d0">Wheat Grass</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of grass, growing to about 5 feet tall, that is planted in double rows within wheat fields to serve as a windbreak.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Season Extenders">
      <data key="d0">Season Extenders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of farm equipment or infrastructure, such as greenhouses, tunnels, or cloches, that allows farmers to grow crops outside the normal season to produce early and late crops.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of tools and structures, such as greenhouses and tunnels, used to start crops earlier or lengthen their growing season to increase profits.&lt;SEP&gt;Season Extenders are tools and techniques, such as greenhouses, cloches, and tunnels, used to start crops growing early or lengthen their growing period late in the year to increase profitability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfed52d46f5bf9d5489c4d6b6dddd2cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shane Smith">
      <data key="d0">Shane Smith</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Author of 'The Bountiful Solar Greenhouse', a book about growing food year-round with solar greenhouses.&lt;SEP&gt;Shane Smith is the author of 'The Bountiful Solar Greenhouse' and a founder of the Cheyenne Community Greenhouse in Wyoming. He is presented as an expert on solar growing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tiberius Caesar">
      <data key="d0">Tiberius Caesar</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Tiberius Caesar was a Roman emperor whose doctor prescribed him to eat a fresh cucumber daily, leading his workers to create a precursor to the modern greenhouse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cheyenne Community Greenhouse">
      <data key="d0">Cheyenne Community Greenhouse</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cheyenne Community Greenhouse is a 5,000 square foot, 100% passive solar-heated community project in Cheyenne, Wyoming. It successfully operates without backup heating despite the cold and windy climate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wyoming">
      <data key="d0">Wyoming</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Wyoming is a US state where the city of Cheyenne is located. The text notes it is a cold place with high average wind speeds, highlighting the success of the greenhouse located there.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cheyenne">
      <data key="d0">Cheyenne</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Cheyenne is a city in Wyoming and the location of the Cheyenne Community Greenhouse. It is described as cold and ranking fourth highest in the nation for yearly average wind speed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anna Edey">
      <data key="d0">Anna Edey</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Anna Edey is the owner of the Solviva solar greenhouse on Martha's Vineyard. She grosses between $70,000 to $100,000 per year from her operation.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of 'Solviva', a book about using a biodynamic solar greenhouse for saving money on crops and livestock.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Solviva">
      <data key="d0">Solviva</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Solviva, which means 'sun life' in Swedish, is a year-round solar greenhouse on Martha's Vineyard owned by Anna Edey. It houses both crops and livestock and uses only the sun and animal body heat for warmth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Martha's Vineyard">
      <data key="d0">Martha's Vineyard</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Martha's Vineyard is a small island off Cape Cod, Massachusetts, and is the location of Anna Edey's Solviva greenhouse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Solar Growing">
      <data key="d0">Solar Growing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Solar Growing is a method of agriculture that uses solar energy, particularly through structures like greenhouses, to produce food. The text discusses its history, benefits, and provides several examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greenhouse">
      <data key="d0">Greenhouse</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A greenhouse is a structure used for growing plants under controlled, protected conditions. The text describes various types, from large passive solar installations to small, cheap DIY versions, as a key tool for season extension.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Bountiful Solar Greenhouse">
      <data key="d0">The Bountiful Solar Greenhouse</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">'The Bountiful Solar Greenhouse' is a book written by Shane Smith about using solar greenhouses for food production. It is cited as a source for the history of greenhouses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cape Cod">
      <data key="d0">Cape Cod</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographic location in Massachusetts where John and Nancy Todd founded the New Alchemy Institute in 1969 to pioneer self-sustaining food systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Cape Cod is a geographic peninsula in Massachusetts, mentioned as being the mainland area near which the island of Martha's Vineyard is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f712d3550c8612228f9fb4dc999ba9b7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Massachusetts">
      <data key="d0">Massachusetts</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Massachusetts is a U.S. state that contains Cape Cod and the island of Martha's Vineyard, as well as the cities of Boston, Holyoke, and Lincoln. It is home to several urban agriculture organizations, including Nuestras Raíces and The Food Project, the latter of which operates specifically in the Boston area. One of the Northeast Organic Wheat consortium's teams is located in Massachusetts. The state is also home to Hampshire College, where the La Finquita community garden was established. Massachusetts' six-month growing season presents a significant challenge for farmers, requiring careful seasonal planning. Massachusetts is cited as an example of the economic benefits of Farm to School programs, where 60 participating farms are generating over $700,000 in additional annual revenue. The Solviva greenhouse is also located in Massachusetts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fef6f9e3cdaa27cfe32361bcab20e464&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ee782c1d8a631cff4fafc639994eb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04be01c6169d0bdd92b10dca615a3936&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8dfe2a17435f059c92535241799795b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aaeba6a87de6589129068300a4f26db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d076831b7a91bcd605c1e387112d81e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cloches">
      <data key="d0">Cloches</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cloches are a type of season extender, mentioned as a tool that can be used to start crops growing early or lengthen their growing period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tunnels">
      <data key="d0">Tunnels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tunnels are a type of season extender, listed alongside greenhouses and cloches as a method to extend the growing season for crops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Floating Row Covers">
      <data key="d0">Floating Row Covers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Floating row covers are a type of season extender, listed as a tool that can be used to help crops grow outside of their normal season.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cold Frame">
      <data key="d0">Cold Frame</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A cold frame is described as a transportable mini-greenhouse, made from a wood frame and a window pane, used as a cheap method to extend the growing season.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Raised-bed Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Raised-bed Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Raised-bed gardens are a gardening structure consisting of frames filled with soil. The text notes they can be converted into season extenders by adding PVC hoops and a plastic covering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419d5c5d2a7c161df014b3fadb6e4529</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Katahdin Hair Sheep">
      <data key="d0">Katahdin Hair Sheep</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Katahdin Hair Sheep are a specific breed of hair sheep raised exclusively for meat. They are characterized as a particularly low-maintenance, hardy, and self-sufficient breed, making them a prime example of a sustainable livestock enterprise. A key advantage is that they are hair sheep and do not require shearing, which significantly reduces the labor associated with wool production.

The breed is well-suited for a low-labor, pasture-based system. They are selected for their ability to lamb on grass pastures, which saves on labor and veterinary bills, and they have few lambing problems and good mothering skills. They thrive on an all-grass, grass-fed system and possess a natural resistance to parasites. Their hardiness is further highlighted by their ability to go without provided water for up to a week in winter. These characteristics result in low management costs and a steady income, integrating well with a farm's labor schedule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e9fc423425c82be2483c04c11e6577f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Earnie Bohner">
      <data key="d0">Earnie Bohner</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farmer in Lampe, Missouri, who successfully operates a U-pick blueberry farm called Persimmon Hill Berry Farm. He exemplifies the niche market strategy by processing surplus berries into high-value products like sauces and dried blueberries (blazons)."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"The operator of Persimmon Hill Farm, presented as a case study of a successful U-pick farmer. He overcomes the challenge of a remote location by creating a tourist-friendly destination with unique, value-added products."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A farmer who, along with his wife Martha, co-founded Persimmon Hill Berry Farm. He is presented as an example of meticulous planning, having spent 3 years researching and developing plans before starting the farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df880ab4353881f2fe1a61ec533c5679&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3eba3a070130b55e2912440d8aaca3d2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Martha Bohner">
      <data key="d0">Martha Bohner</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A farmer who, along with her husband Earnie, co-founded Persimmon Hill Berry Farm. She is used as an example of diligent planning, attending conferences and talking to other farmers to prepare.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persimmon Hill Berry Farm">
      <data key="d0">Persimmon Hill Berry Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The U-pick blueberry farm owned and operated by Earnie and Martha Bohner in Lampe, Missouri. The farm is an example of a successful niche operation that adds value by creating products like jams and sauces under its own label."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A farm co-founded by Earnie and Martha Bohner. It started with U-pick berries and shiitake mushrooms and is used as a case study for extensive farm planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df880ab4353881f2fe1a61ec533c5679&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Depression">
      <data key="d0">Depression</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical period of economic hardship mentioned by an elderly farmer in an anecdote about extremely low prices for hogs, used to illustrate a point about the business of farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="South">
      <data key="d0">South</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical region mentioned as a place where it's possible for livestock to graze all winter due to its climate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pasture Grasses">
      <data key="d0">Pasture Grasses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural feed source for livestock whose nutritive value declines during the winter, necessitating supplemental feeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ear Corn">
      <data key="d0">Ear Corn</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of stored crop mentioned alongside hay as a necessary feed for livestock during the winter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cold-hardy Forage Crops">
      <data key="d0">Cold-hardy Forage Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of crop that can be grown to allow livestock to graze late into the winter, especially in the South, as part of a sustainable feeding strategy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goals">
      <data key="d0">Goals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Goals are a fundamental part of farm management and planning, categorized into long-term, medium-term, and short-term objectives. They include profitability and gaining knowledge."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A central concept in the text, described as essential for farm planning. Goals can be short-term, long-term, personal, family, or business-related, and they provide direction for the farm's development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U-pick Berries">
      <data key="d0">U-pick Berries</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural enterprise chosen by Earnie and Martha Bohner for their Persimmon Hill Berry Farm after extensive research and planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shiitake Mushrooms">
      <data key="d0">Shiitake Mushrooms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural enterprise, along with U-pick berries, that Earnie and Martha Bohner decided to start on their Persimmon Hill Berry Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Elderly Farmer">
      <data key="d0">Elderly Farmer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An elderly farmer who owned a bluegrass sod farm, shared stories about the Depression, and demonstrated shrewdness by taking his hogs to market during an ice storm for a higher profit.&lt;SEP&gt;An unnamed character who shared an anecdote with the author about the low price of hogs during the Depression, illustrating a point about farming economics and history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northern Areas">
      <data key="d0">Northern Areas</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical region mentioned where cattle will learn to graze through snow, highlighting animal adaptation to harsh winter conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St. Louis">
      <data key="d0">St. Louis</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">St. Louis is mentioned as a market city, 125 miles away, where a farmer successfully sold hogs for a higher price during an ice storm due to a supply shortage.&lt;SEP&gt;St. Louis is the city where Gateway Greening supports numerous community gardens and an urban farm.&lt;SEP&gt;The city where Gateway Greening operates. The organization aims to beautify the city, stabilize its neighborhoods, and make use of its vacant lots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aab30ee2b8630558ddd6575d42c69264&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northern Hemisphere">
      <data key="d0">Northern Hemisphere</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The northern hemisphere is used as a geographical example to explain how the sun's angle affects land, noting that trees typically grow on northern slopes and grasses on warmer, drier southern slopes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Depression">
      <data key="d0">The Depression</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical economic event that, along with dust storms, caused Americans to become concerned about their food supply and soil conservation, leading to a shift in attitude towards land.&lt;SEP&gt;The Depression is a historical economic event recalled by an elderly farmer, characterized by extremely low market prices, such as hogs selling for 3 cents per pound.&lt;SEP&gt;A historical period in the 1930s when community gardens, funded by the Federal Emergency Relief Administration, were used as a crucial stopgap measure to alleviate hardship before larger relief efforts were implemented.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ice Storm">
      <data key="d0">Ice Storm</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A severe ice storm is described as an event that created a market opportunity. It prevented most farmers from getting to market, allowing one who did to sell his hogs for a significantly higher price.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sustainable, Diversified Farming">
      <data key="d0">Sustainable, Diversified Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A farming philosophy described as the best approach for earning a living from a farm. It aims to mitigate risks like monetary cycles and cash-flow shortages through variety and stability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Strip-Cropping">
      <data key="d0">Strip-Cropping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural technique used on moderately sloping land to prevent soil erosion. It involves planting alternating strips of different crops, such as a row crop and a thicker hay crop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goal Setting">
      <data key="d0">Goal Setting</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A strategic practice for farmers involving the creation and regular review of long-term goals (10+ years) to ensure the farm's operations are aligned with its intended direction and to adapt to new plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monetary Cycles">
      <data key="d0">Monetary Cycles</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurring economic fluctuations that can create financial hardship and stress for a farm enterprise. Diversified farming is presented as a way to mitigate their impact.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cash-flow Shortages">
      <data key="d0">Cash-flow Shortages</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A financial challenge that can make any farm enterprise difficult. The text emphasizes that planning and flexibility are key to maintaining a steady cash flow and avoiding these shortages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Author/Narrator">
      <data key="d0">Author/Narrator</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The narrator of the text, who worked as a foreman on a sod-laying crew after college and uses personal anecdotes and farming examples to explain principles of farm planning and success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buyers">
      <data key="d0">Buyers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The customers or companies that purchase agricultural products from farmers. Market groups and cooperatives act as intermediaries to connect farmers with buyers."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A group of purchasers at the St. Louis market. On the day of the ice storm, their demand was not met due to a low supply of hogs, which drove up the price.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Slope">
      <data key="d0">Slope</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key land feature considered in farm planning. Its angle affects soil temperature, moisture levels, and the potential for erosion, influencing crop selection and planting methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moisture">
      <data key="d0">Moisture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical environmental factor for crops, influenced by the slope of the land and soil type. The text advises choosing crops based on moisture availability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Planting">
      <data key="d0">Planting</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crucial agricultural practice described as an art form. It involves specific techniques adapted to the desert, such as planting seeds very deep (8-12 inches) in clumps to protect them from wind and drought.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of sowing crops, which must be adapted to the land's characteristics. The text describes specific methods, like running rows perpendicular to a slope, to manage water and prevent erosion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Management">
      <data key="d0">Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Management encompasses the principles and tools for running a farm, including planning, labor management, and goal setting."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A component of farm planning that involves considering the effects of all farm operations on each other, such as using livestock to fertilize a future planting area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Manure">
      <data key="d0">Manure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Manure is animal waste used as a natural fertilizer to enrich the soil, often as part of a crop rotation system."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Animal waste product described as a valuable, virtually free source of fertilizer that can be concentrated on a slope by feeding hay to livestock in that area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Erosion">
      <data key="d0">Erosion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of soil being washed away by water. The text describes planting methods like contour farming and strip-cropping specifically to combat erosion on slopes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fee-hunting Ranch">
      <data key="d0">Fee-hunting Ranch</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An alternative type of agricultural enterprise mentioned as an example of a business that would have very different long-term goals compared to a traditional crop or livestock farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e383e7fee90d89413a8ba4dadf9296</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diversified Crop And Livestock Farm">
      <data key="d0">Diversified Crop And Livestock Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farm where a combination of crops and livestock complement each other. This approach can help reduce input costs, such as using homegrown corn to feed hogs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organic Market Niche">
      <data key="d0">Organic Market Niche</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific market segment for farm products. The text suggests that targeting this niche is a specific goal that requires understanding certification requirements and potential impacts on crop yields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sheila">
      <data key="d0">Sheila</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sheila is a hypothetical person used as an example to differentiate between a short-term goal (planting blueberries) and a long-term goal (planning the family's overall operational direction).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Management">
      <data key="d0">Risk Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A business strategy mentioned in the index in direct relation to achieving and maintaining profitability in a farm enterprise.&lt;SEP&gt;A key long-term goal in farming focused on reducing or eliminating operational risks that affect profitability. Diversification is presented as one method for risk management.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk Management is a key strategy for farm operations, focusing on reducing or eliminating major agricultural risks. This includes planning for contingencies like illness or drought and utilizing tools like crop, livestock, or gross revenue insurance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The City">
      <data key="d0">The City</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical origin for potential new farmers who may lack practical skills. The text suggests that individuals from the city should seek hands-on experience by working for a farmer to learn.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to the municipal government of Seattle. It manages the P-Patch program, and its budget and political shifts have historically affected the program's stability and support.&lt;SEP&gt;The municipal government entity in Kensington. Initially skeptical of Greensgrow's business plan, it eventually worked with the CDC to purchase the lot and supported the venture by providing a lease after its officials were laughed at for incorrectly typing 'hydrophonics' on the business permit.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6fd2b31420a632f31dfe7120da040d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07f097aee6be0e05076e0e695419438a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Machinery Management">
      <data key="d0">Machinery Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A goal focused on purchasing only absolutely necessary machinery to control costs, as equipment depreciates in value. It is noted that buying used machinery can be a good option for those with tool skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Budgeting">
      <data key="d0">Budgeting</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A financial planning goal that involves using 5-year averages for budgets and aiming for a cash flow of 120 percent to ensure profitability and cover living expenses, savings, and farm upgrades.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-Term Goals">
      <data key="d0">Long-Term Goals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The central concept of the text, defined as objectives spanning 10 years or more. They are described as broad-scope goals that should be written down and reviewed monthly to ensure a farming operation is on the right track.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pasture-Based Farm">
      <data key="d0">Pasture-Based Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of farming operation mentioned as an example of a long-term, directional goal. This choice helps define the farm's focus before deciding on specific crops or livestock.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Raising Purebred Animals">
      <data key="d0">Raising Purebred Animals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized agricultural focus presented as an example of a long-term goal. Choosing this path helps to set a specific direction for a farm's development and operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Unit">
      <data key="d0">Family Unit</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The collaborative group (e.g., family, partners, neighbors) responsible for a farming operation. The text emphasizes that total agreement on monetary and labor goals within this unit is critical for success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reducing Input Costs">
      <data key="d0">Reducing Input Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key long-term financial goal for a farm. The text suggests this can be achieved by improving soil fertility or increasing diversity with complementary crops and livestock to enhance profitability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Improving Soil Fertility">
      <data key="d0">Improving Soil Fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A long-term agricultural goal, also referred to as improving soil health. It is presented as a primary method for reducing input costs, as healthier soil is more productive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Marketing">
      <data key="d0">Direct Marketing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, Direct Marketing is a key business strategy, sales model, and management tool that is considered essential, and even mandatory, for the success of small and family farms, particularly those in alternative agriculture. Described as the "profit equalizer," this approach involves farmers selling their products directly to consumers, bypassing intermediaries to receive higher retail prices and significantly increase their net profit and profit margins.

Common methods of Direct Marketing include sales at farmers' markets, U-pick operations, and the creation of value-added products, such as turning hogs into sausage. The strategy is often linked to niche markets and involves marketing both the product and the farmer. To be successful, farmers must engage in practices such as market identification, niche development, and advertising. A critical component is knowing the precise cost of production to determine appropriate pricing, as newcomers in this model often make the mistake of underpricing their goods. Ultimately, Direct Marketing is a crucial tool used by farmers, alongside sustainable agriculture, to achieve financial security.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df880ab4353881f2fe1a61ec533c5679&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-766988b011c2a15c7312ce3e9bb55cf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473f8348a15ba74f2e21ca4cc96c583e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Newspapers">
      <data key="d0">Newspapers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of media outlet where the farm can buy classified and display advertising. It is noted that their prices are often 'fairly set'.&lt;SEP&gt;Mentioned as a source of information, hearsay, or hype about new farming techniques or projects. The text advises farmers to be cautious and test information from newspapers on their own farms first.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2afc9b5400cf61bfbec7b607b711ba96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magazines">
      <data key="d0">Magazines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of media outlet for advertising that may offer 'bargaining room' for the price of ads, unlike newspapers.&lt;SEP&gt;Listed alongside newspapers and universities as a source of potentially unreliable hype or information about farming projects. Farmers are advised to test ideas from magazines before full-scale implementation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2afc9b5400cf61bfbec7b607b711ba96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quality Of Life">
      <data key="d0">Quality Of Life</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant personal goal and desired outcome of a successful farming operation. It is listed as a long-term objective alongside improving financial metrics like net worth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Net Worth">
      <data key="d0">Net Worth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key financial goal for a farming operation. The text mentions improving net worth as a long-term objective that is part of a successful farm plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Profit">
      <data key="d0">Profit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The financial gain a farmer aims to achieve. It is calculated by setting a price that covers all costs of production and marketing, with a margin left over."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The financial gain from a farming enterprise. The text illustrates profit as the surplus revenue (e.g., the extra 20% in the budget example) available for living expenses, savings, and farm upgrades.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Agriculture is a central theme and activity across multiple projects and discussions. It encompasses the practice of farming, food production, and the cultivation of land, even being described as having notable "power". It plays a key role in food systems and economics, while also serving as a powerful engine for community building, particularly through community gardening, identified as the 'heart and soul' of the Troy Gardens project. Integrating agriculture into new housing developments is becoming increasingly common as a primary community amenity. However, agriculture is also characterized by major risks, including weather, prices, government regulations, disease, and soil fertility, all of which affect profitability. The Food Project also recognizes agriculture as a central activity, one which crew leaders seek to connect with on a deeper level.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aaeba6a87de6589129068300a4f26db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f1544f96bf3906c3ffd91d3f551a278</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct-Marketing">
      <data key="d0">Direct-Marketing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Direct-Marketing is a sales strategy where farm production is sold directly to consumers, such as at a farmers' market. It is promoted as a method to achieve higher profits, build customer relationships, and reduce overall farming risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Government">
      <data key="d0">The Government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Government is an entity that influences farm operations through regulations and laws that can affect profitability. It also provides support programs, such as the gross revenue insurance plan introduced in 1999.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Short-Term Debt">
      <data key="d0">Short-Term Debt</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Short-Term Debt is a financial liability that the text strongly advises avoiding. It accumulates quickly and makes it difficult to maintain financial stability, contrasting with more manageable long-term loans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-Term Loan">
      <data key="d0">Long-Term Loan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Long-Term Loans are financial tools with 3- to 5-year terms used to purchase major assets like breeding stock, machinery, and buildings. They are presented as a more strategic form of debt compared to short-term debt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Bank">
      <data key="d0">The Bank</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bank is a financial institution that provides loans for farm operations. The text suggests maintaining a 2:1 asset-to-liability ratio to be in good standing with the bank.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blueberries">
      <data key="d0">Blueberries</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A long-term fruit crop enterprise, productive from years 3 to 15. It yields 4,000 lbs. per acre and has a very high net profit of $5,200.00 with 200 hours of labor.&lt;SEP&gt;Blueberries are presented as an example of a medium-term crop enterprise. They require a significant time investment (3-6 years) to reach full production and necessitate long-term financing and pre-developed markets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6d072f41d662cb326c1c528ac3c5c51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gross Revenue Insurance">
      <data key="d0">Gross Revenue Insurance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gross Revenue Insurance is a specific risk management tool introduced by the government in 1999. It guarantees a certain gross revenue or yield for crops and livestock in exchange for a higher premium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Adding">
      <data key="d0">Value Adding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Value adding is a key strategy for farm profitability, involving processing or packaging raw farm products to increase their market value. The text suggests that 80 percent of income can come from value-added direct marketing efforts, which also helps reduce overall farming risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Continuing Education">
      <data key="d0">Continuing Education</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Continuing Education is described as an important annual goal for farmers. It involves attending conferences, seminars, and reading research to stay informed about crops and livestock, and using this new information to update farm goals and practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conferences and Seminars">
      <data key="d0">Conferences and Seminars</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Conferences and Seminars are specific events that farmers are encouraged to attend and participate in every year as part of their continuing education to gain new information and insights for their operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marketing Plan">
      <data key="d0">Marketing Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Marketing Plan is a crucial business tool for a farm, designed to ensure sales every month of the year to cover monthly bills. It is considered a short-term, intermediate, and long-term goal that requires periodic reevaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil-Conserving Tillage Methods">
      <data key="d0">Soil-Conserving Tillage Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Soil-Conserving Tillage Methods are specific techniques used to achieve the lifetime goal of building soil fertility. Examples given include using cover crops, green manures, livestock, and strip-cropping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Assets">
      <data key="d0">Farm Assets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Farm Assets are long-term physical items required for a farm operation. The text lists breeding stock, machinery, and buildings as examples of assets that are typically purchased using 3- to 5-year term loans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Operating Loans and Lines of Credit">
      <data key="d0">Operating Loans and Lines of Credit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Operating Loans and Lines of Credit are financial instruments used for short-term expenses on items that are used up within one year. Revolving credit is mentioned as a specific type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Operating Supplies">
      <data key="d0">Operating Supplies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Operating Supplies are consumable items necessary for the day-to-day running of a farm, which are paid for within a single year. The text provides seed, fertilizer, and veterinary supplies as primary examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Life and Health Insurance">
      <data key="d0">Life and Health Insurance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Life and Health Insurance is presented as a personal risk management tool for farmers. It helps mitigate the financial impact of the farmer getting sick and being unable to work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gross Margin">
      <data key="d0">Gross Margin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gross Margin is a financial metric used to compare the direct profitability of different farm enterprises. It is calculated as the gross income per acre minus the direct cash costs per acre, excluding fixed costs and mortgage payments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Politicians">
      <data key="d0">Politicians</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Politicians are identified as an uncontrollable external factor that can affect a farm's bottom line by passing new laws and regulations. They represent a source of risk that farmers must plan for.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9d3e2ae9649f6dc7e9301ed98bc01d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bob Smith">
      <data key="d0">Bob Smith</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual identified in a sample short-term farm goal as a potential seller of crossbred (Yorkshire x Hampshire) feeder pigs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Business Development office">
      <data key="d0">Small Business Development office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local resource recommended for farmers to consult when creating a formal business plan, which is essential for securing loans and for strategic planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Masanobu Fukuoka">
      <data key="d0">Masanobu Fukuoka</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Japanese farmer and author famous for his natural farming methods, as described in his books 'One Straw Revolution' and 'Natural Farming'. He advocates for a 'do-nothing' or 'thinking-type' agriculture and categorizes farming into Mahayana, Hinayana, and Scientific types.&lt;SEP&gt;A Japanese farmer known for his 'do-nothing method' of farming, which emphasizes reducing costs to make the farm sustainable and profitable.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of 'The Natural Way of Farming', which describes the 'do-nothing' organic farming method practiced in Japan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a25021ab306ceaf5d58e192cc6fc0a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stockman Grass Farmer newspaper">
      <data key="d0">Stockman Grass Farmer newspaper</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication edited by Allan Nation, focused on grass-fed livestock farming and influential in promoting the concept of management-intensive grazing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tanya Denckla Cobb">
      <data key="d0">Tanya Denckla Cobb</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Author of the book being discussed, who has documented the diversity of the grassroots food movements across cultures, faiths, races, and classes.&lt;SEP&gt;Tanya Denckla Cobb is a writer, environmental mediator, and a teacher of food-system planning at the University of Virginia. She has founded and directed several environmental and food-related organizations in Virginia.&lt;SEP&gt;Tanya Denckla Cobb is the author of the book 'Reclaiming Our Food,' which focuses on the grassroots food movement. Her work documents and describes community-based food initiatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cfeabc3f21e3329484ec062ac1c4b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gary Paul Nabhan">
      <data key="d0">Gary Paul Nabhan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Gary Paul Nabhan wrote the foreword for 'Reclaiming Our Food.' He is presented as an expert who provides context on the American food system and the significance of the grassroots food movement.&lt;SEP&gt;The author of the book 'Where Our Food Comes From', as indicated by the parenthetical reference '(Nabhan)' in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cfeabc3f21e3329484ec062ac1c4b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jason Houston">
      <data key="d0">Jason Houston</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jason Houston is a photographer who explores social and environmental issues and works as the picture editor for Orion magazine. For the book 'Reclaiming Our Food,' he provided the compelling photo essays that accompany the text, which have been described as manifesting the book's "heart and soul." His work on the book captures the positive emotions and experiences within the alternative food system. Jason Houston lives in western Massachusetts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cfeabc3f21e3329484ec062ac1c4b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grassroots Food Movement">
      <data key="d0">Grassroots Food Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Grassroots Food Movement is a broad, diverse, and growing social movement in the United States focused on creating a new paradigm for food. It serves as a community-based alternative to the industrial food system, aiming to establish more localized, self-reliant, and sustainable food systems. Driven by a wide range of environmental, social, health, and economic motivations, the movement arises from a collective feeling of disconnection, seeking to restore harmony between people, the land, and their food.

Key goals of the Grassroots Food Movement include rebuilding local food systems to enhance food security, transitioning to a less petroleum-dependent model, and combating issues like the obesity epidemic, hunger, and food waste. It is characterized by ground-level, community-led projects and advocates for regional, smaller-scale food production. The movement's initiatives focus on the entire food cycle, including growing, distributing, and accessing food. Specific activities include supporting farmers, promoting renewable energy in farming, regenerating the environment, and popularizing practices like gleaning. It has fostered innovations such as farmers' markets and Community Supported Agriculture (CSAs), and operates through various venues like schools and after-school programs to change the eating habits of children and parents. The local grain movement is also described as an extension of this larger movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d4150698732f393c235b8b3e1cb2d88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea6dd73e41a475c9933ac4783be5f57a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cfeabc3f21e3329484ec062ac1c4b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cecil">
      <data key="d0">Cecil</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Cecil is the individual to whom the book is dedicated by the author, Tanya Denckla Cobb, for their sacrifice, support, and encouragement.&lt;SEP&gt;The author's friend and life partner, acknowledged for providing significant personal support, encouragement, and sacrificing weekends for the project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cfeabc3f21e3329484ec062ac1c4b2</data>
