    <node id="Foodshed Consciousness">
      <data key="d0">Foodshed Consciousness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">'Foodshed Consciousness' is a term used by Gary Paul Nabhan to describe the awareness and appreciation for the people and processes that bring food to consumers.&lt;SEP&gt;A concept and awareness of local and regional food systems, which is adopted by the 'agricultural creatives' leading the food movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cfeabc3f21e3329484ec062ac1c4b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Farming">
      <data key="d0">Urban Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the provided descriptions, Urban Farming is a central concept referring to the practice of cultivating, processing, and distributing food within or around cities and suburban areas. It is presented as a multifaceted movement, profession, and business model that is a key component of 21st-century urban revitalization.

Urban Farming focuses on innovative strategies for utilizing urban land, including vacant lots and contaminated brownfield sites, with the goal of rehabilitating the land. It is positioned as a solution for a wide range of urban issues, contributing to job creation, economic development, equity, and social justice. As a business model, it is characterized by low start-up costs and requires an entrepreneurial mindset that differs from traditional rural farming.

The practice faces challenges such as leasing land and the need for written agreements, but successful models like The Intervale and Greensgrow Farm demonstrate its potential. Ultimately, Urban Farming is considered a replicable and essential practice for the future that builds community resilience, provides educational opportunities, and reconnects people with their food sources.&lt;SEP&gt;The practice of agriculture in urban areas, with information provided by the 'City Farmer' organization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cfeabc3f21e3329484ec062ac1c4b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd12c93ca420ae0dfd8107f51eec1886</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740840</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Heritage">
      <data key="d0">Food Heritage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept referring to the preservation of cultural identities through place-based foods that are suited to local climates and soils. It is presented as a crucial component for achieving food security and community resilience.&lt;SEP&gt;Food Heritage is a central concept representing the traditions, methods, and cultural significance of foods specific to a landscape, seascape, or culture. The text emphasizes the importance of documenting and preserving it.&lt;SEP&gt;Food Heritage is a key topic and a chapter title in the book 'Reclaiming Our Food', focused on the preservation of cultural identities through food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cfeabc3f21e3329484ec062ac1c4b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
