    <node id="Commodity Food Giants">
      <data key="d0">Commodity Food Giants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Large-scale, industrial food corporations that the grassroots food movement contrasts with. They are associated with the impersonal, opaque, and profit-driven aspects of the food system, likened to the 'Titanic' heading for disaster.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Food">
      <data key="d0">Local Food</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local Food is a food system model and central concept that refers to the production, sale, and consumption of food within a specific geographic region. The definition of "local" is a key point of discussion and can be based on various criteria, such as mileage (with a "100-mile criterion" being a specific example), state lines, or driving time; establishing a clear definition is often a primary goal for stakeholder committees.

As a central theme in grassroots movements, Local Food is characterized as being fresh, personal, transparent, accessible, and nutrient-rich. It is promoted for its benefits to nutrition, community health, the local economy, community value, and environmental sustainability, and is seen as a key component in rebuilding communities. Building a local food system involves creating strategies that foster trust between producers and buyers.

The concept is integral to various initiatives. It is the central commodity in the "Farm to School" movement, which aims to incorporate fresh, locally sourced food into school cafeterias by overcoming procurement challenges and building supply chains. It is also a central theme in JVUF's mission, taught during "Weekend Workshops" to emphasize its importance for community health and sustainability. Furthermore, the concept is discussed in the context of merging with composting in urban gardens as part of a sustainable food model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d31a2f89a77b64e0ae04120df1fdb3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e0df78d09fb33ee041307abbce7d33e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2d3545603e988fef215052ef7f681c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrial Foodstuffs">
      <data key="d0">Industrial Foodstuffs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Products of the mainstream food industry, described negatively as 'toxic,' 'highly processed,' 'nutrient-poor,' 'tasteless,' 'rootless,' and 'fructose-laden crud.' The grassroots movement aims to provide alternatives to these products.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Titanic">
      <data key="d0">The Titanic</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A metaphor used to represent the massive, unwieldy, and imperiled industrial food system controlled by commodity food giants. The 'agricultural creatives' are depicted as building 'lifeboats' as an alternative to this sinking ship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Foodshed Design Team">
      <data key="d0">Foodshed Design Team</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept for a local group that individuals are inspired to join after reading the book. These teams work to change how their community accesses food, aiming for a more efficient, equitable, resilient, and diverse system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Low-income, Incarcerated, Homeless, Homebound, Disabled, And Hungry">
      <data key="d0">The Low-income, Incarcerated, Homeless, Homebound, Disabled, And Hungry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group described as 'often forgotten or overlooked members of our society.' Providing healthy, fresh food to this group is a primary altruistic and faith-driven motivation for many participants in the grassroots food movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d86135a3ecce951cb211b81554dd41c0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Food Movement">
      <data key="d0">Local Food Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Local Food Movement is a cultural, social, and economic trend focused on connecting food producers and consumers within the same geographic region to increase the production and consumption of locally sourced food. The movement aims to build a sustainable and resilient economy, nurture community spirit, and fill gaps in food knowledge and access by making fresh, healthy food affordably accessible to all Americans. Key strategies include urban farming and whole-animal purchasing, which is a vital component for the success of participating restaurants.

This overarching trend involves individuals, colleges, businesses, and institutions like hospitals, senior centers, and schools purchasing from local sources, driven by benefits to local economies, public health, sustainability, and community relations. Contrary to perceptions of it being elitist, the movement resonates at all levels of society. Key figures and organizations associated with the Local Food Movement include leading figure Gary Nabhan, participant Oren Molovinsky, and the facilitator ASAP, with Diablo Burger serving as a prime example of the movement in action. Ultimately, the movement's goal is to be so successful that it is no longer needed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1fa8277818b65f8d3d6665242021ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Beauty Way">
      <data key="d0">The Beauty Way</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Navajo belief system centered on achieving harmony, balance, and relationship. The author uses this concept as the 'connecting thread' to understand the motivations behind the grassroots food movement."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Food Gardening">
      <data key="d0">Home Food Gardening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An increasingly popular activity in America, practiced by over a third of households. Motivations include better quality food, economic returns, destressing, community building, and connecting with nature."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Gardening Association (NGA)">
      <data key="d0">National Gardening Association (NGA)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An organization that provides data and surveys on food gardening in America. According to the NGA, a well-maintained garden can yield a $500 return, and gardeners are primarily motivated by the desire for better quality, taste, and nutrition."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The National Gardening Association (NGA) is an organization mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Reservation">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Reservation</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A location in Arizona where the author visited farmers and ranchers. It serves as a key example where the community is actively restoring traditional relationships with land and food, and where the author learned about the Beauty Way."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The Navajo Reservation is a geographical area mentioned as the location of a farm that came back to life in 2011 and is home to communities involved in local farming initiatives.&lt;SEP&gt;The homeland of the Navajo Nation where young people have been leaving and where Jonathan Yazzie's farm is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World War II Victory Garden Era">
      <data key="d0">World War II Victory Garden Era</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A historical period used as a point of comparison to modern gardening. During this era, gardening was a necessity, whereas today it is often viewed as a hobby, sport, or social activity."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pragmatic Leaders">
      <data key="d0">Pragmatic Leaders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of individuals described as the drivers of the local food movement, encompassing social innovators, shrewd businesspeople, technical inventors, and systems thinkers. They work as farmers, chefs, and community organizers to advance the movement's goals economically and sustainably."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo">
      <data key="d0">Navajo</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Navajo are an Indigenous Native American people of the American Southwest, with communities on the Navajo reservation in areas including northern Arizona and Tuba City. They possess a long and deep heritage of farming and ranching, with their culture, ceremonies, and beliefs being intricately rooted in traditional agriculture. The Navajo perfected traditional farming techniques and have a notable connection to corn. Their belief system, known as the Beauty Way, emphasizes restoring harmony with the land and food.

Historically, the Navajo, alongside the Hopi, were highly successful in their agricultural practices, maintaining up to 90% food self-sufficiency with a diverse set of crops until World War II, which serves as a lesson in food security. A specific cultural tradition mentioned is the passing of land down through women.

In contemporary times, the Navajo community faces challenges such as water scarcity and the loss of traditional agricultural knowledge. In response, a grassroots movement is underway to reclaim their food and cultural traditions. Efforts to preserve and reestablish traditional farming, culinary practices, and cultural heritage are being led by individuals like Rose Marie Williams, a Navajo farmer and rancher, and organizations such as the North Leupp Family Farm. The Navajo's desire to restore their traditional ways provides a key insight into the modern food movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Highly Processed Food">
      <data key="d0">Highly Processed Food</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of food described as calorie-rich but nutrient-poor. The text states it benefits the corporations that produce it, not consumers, and links its consumption to epidemics of obesity, diabetes, and heart disease."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Epidemics (Obesity, Diabetes, Heart Disease)">
      <data key="d0">Health Epidemics (Obesity, Diabetes, Heart Disease)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A series of major health crises mentioned in the text as a direct result of decades of consuming highly processed food. These epidemics are described as stressing the national health-care system."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lower-income People">
      <data key="d0">Lower-income People</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A demographic group mentioned in the context of the debate over whether food gardening is elitist. The argument suggests they may lack the time or money to participate in growing their own food."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grassroots Food Leaders">
      <data key="d0">Grassroots Food Leaders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Described as the pioneers of the grassroots food movement. The book shares their stories to inspire action and provide lessons for future leaders in the movement to reclaim food systems."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28d8a78f075f0eccbcec19198a69d0c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food System">
      <data key="d0">Food System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A comprehensive concept that includes agriculture, policy, labor, business, and distribution. The Food Project educates teens on how to understand and influence the food system.&lt;SEP&gt;The modern, industrial food system is described as a 'double-edged testimony to the amazing power of efficiencies.' It successfully feeds billions of people cheaply, but this is achieved at a significant long-term cost to land, water, habitat, health, communities, and spirit. It is contrasted with the personal and community-focused approach of home gardening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Home Gardening Project Foundation">
      <data key="d0">The Home Gardening Project Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A foundation located at 8060 Upper Applegate Road, Jacksonville, OR. Its representative is Dan Barker."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Based in southern Oregon, The Home Gardening Project Foundation (HGPF) was started to provide free raised-bed vegetable gardens to people in need, including the disabled, the elderly, single-parent mothers, and large families. It now also assists other community garden groups across the United States with similar goals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dan Barker">
      <data key="d0">Dan Barker</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dan Barker is the founder, representative, and a key figure in the Home Gardening Project Foundation. Described as the "real deal" and a modern "Johnny Appleseed of home gardens," he is a green activist who was motivated to start the project to help others after experiencing a traumatic convenience-store robbery. As a Vietnam veteran who suffers from PTSD, Barker is deeply committed to his work of giving away gardens, which he views as a spiritual act. He developed a specific methodology for building and distributing free gardens to people in need, including the elderly, disabled, and single mothers.

Beyond his activism, Dan Barker is a multifaceted individual, described as a philosopher, poet, writer, artist, student of the Tao, and humorist. He is the author of books such as "Queen Jane" and "Building Free Vegetable Gardens for People in Need," and his work has been recognized in a Smithsonian article.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southern Oregon">
      <data key="d0">Southern Oregon</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Southern Oregon is the rural region where Barker moved in 1996 for a safer and slower lifestyle after leaving Portland.&lt;SEP&gt;The geographical location where The Home Gardening Project Foundation is based.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michael Pollan">
      <data key="d0">Michael Pollan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Michael Pollan is a prominent and influential author, journalist, analyst, speaker, and activist known for his extensive work on food systems, agriculture, and sustainability. As a noted critic of the industrial food system, he has written several key works, including the book "The Omnivore's Dilemma," the 2008 New York Times Magazine article "Farmer in Chief" which discussed food policy, and a piece on the "Organic-Industrial Complex."

In his analysis, Pollan carefully explains the systemic link between federal farm incentives for crops like corn, the mass production of cheap processed foods, and resulting public health issues such as obesity. He is also credited with making the connection between food production and fossil fuel consumption visible. Through "The Omnivore's Dilemma," he brought national attention and acclaim to Joel Salatin and the innovations at Polyface Farm, and the book is also cited as an effective counter to the argument that gardening is elitist. As an activist, Pollan wrote critically about Gene Kahn in 2001, framing his actions as a "takeover of the movement by agribusiness." He has engaged in public advocacy by speaking at events like the 2009 Bioneers conference and by writing directly to political leaders, such as his 2008 letter to then-President-elect Obama.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d6381862167ee0d108723409eae36d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8b5f0f35b6bfccad911410ecdcdb7c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e0df78d09fb33ee041307abbce7d33e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63208f55f5e282db2dc1514f989da622&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd285deff310269197e8718b28f6076b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barbara Kingsolver">
      <data key="d0">Barbara Kingsolver</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Author of 'Animal, Vegetable, Miracle', whose work is cited as effectively countering the argument that gardening is elitist by highlighting the negative consequences of the industrial food system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
