    </node>
    <node id="Johnny Appleseed">
      <data key="d0">Johnny Appleseed</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical figure used as a point of comparison for Dan Barker, signifying Barker's extensive work in establishing a large number of home gardens for others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Elitism Argument">
      <data key="d0">The Elitism Argument</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A central argument critiqued in the text, which posits that home gardening is elitist. The rationale is that lower-income individuals lack the necessary time and money for gardening, and engaging in it only widens the gap between socioeconomic classes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Productivity (Economic Definition)">
      <data key="d0">Productivity (Economic Definition)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept of productivity, heavily criticized in the text, that is measured strictly in units of time and output. This mindset is identified as the foundation for the modern industrial food system and the argument that gardening is 'unproductive' for low-income families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community">
      <data key="d0">Community</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">In the provided text, community is a central and recurring theme that encompasses various interconnected topics. It represents the social groups and collective well-being that featured projects, such as Janus Youth and Lynchburg Grows, aim to strengthen and revitalize. The text describes several methods for achieving this, including coalition building, organizing, and utilizing food as a tool for transformation. Gardening is highlighted as a specific activity that serves as a tool for renewing a sense of community, with community garden groups and foundations being noted for their service. The concept of community also extends to the broader group of people connected to a school, where long-term community commitment is identified as a necessary component for an effective and sustainable school garden program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spirituality">
      <data key="d0">Spirituality</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A quality discussed in relation to gardening and the work of Dan Barker. It represents the internal joy and connection people feel with the soil, plants, and the world, and is presented as a core motivation for Barker's work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Candide">
      <data key="d0">Candide</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The protagonist of Voltaire's novel, who is cited as a philosophical example. After despairing over the human condition, Candide's ultimate decision to 'cultivate his own garden' is used as a metaphor for finding a practical and meaningful solution to overwhelming problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder">
      <data key="d0">Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A condition mentioned in relation to Dan Barker. The text notes that he is a Vietnam veteran still suffering from PTSD, which provides context for his directness and deep commitment to his work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Tao">
      <data key="d0">The Tao</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A philosophy that Dan Barker is a student of. This detail contributes to the description of his multifaceted character, portraying him as a philosopher and a deep thinker in addition to his other roles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nation">
      <data key="d0">Nation</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The broader geographical scope of The Home Gardening Project Foundation's influence. The text states that the foundation now assists community garden groups 'across the nation', indicating its expansion beyond its local origins in southern Oregon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Gardening Project">
      <data key="d0">Home Gardening Project</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Home Gardening Project is a nonprofit initiative started by Dan Barker in 1983. Its mission is to build and give away free vegetable gardens to alleviate suffering and provide a 'significant occupation' for recipients. The project requires an estimated $25,000 in start-up funds and focuses on providing raised beds, soil, seeds, and ongoing mentorship.&lt;SEP&gt;The Home Gardening Project was the original organization founded by Barker in Portland. After his departure, it evolved into the nonprofit known as Growing Gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="City of Portland">
      <data key="d0">City of Portland</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The municipal government and geographic setting for Abernethy Elementary School. The text mentions perceptions within the city regarding the school's budget and resources."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The City of Portland is the location where the Home Gardening Project was founded. Its government provided the initial $5,000 grant that enabled Dan Barker to build the first 20 gardens in 1983.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c16ea96bc625614680cc6d8276e6ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convenience-Store Robbery">
      <data key="d0">Convenience-Store Robbery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Convenience-Store Robbery was a pivotal, traumatic event in Dan Barker's life where a gun was held to his head. This experience served as the primary catalyst that propelled him to start the Home Gardening Project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Free Vegetable Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Free Vegetable Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Free Vegetable Gardens are the core offering of the Home Gardening Project. They consist of 5-foot-by-8-foot raised beds, quality soil, seeds, and trellises, given to people in need. They are designed to provide food, a new occupation, and a sense of productivity for recipients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Support Organizations">
      <data key="d0">Community Support Organizations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Community Support Organizations represent a category of potential partners for the Home Gardening Project. The text suggests contacting entities like senior citizen organizations, the Multiple Sclerosis Society, housing authorities, mental health services, food banks, and churches to find eligible recipients for the gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen Jane">
      <data key="d0">Queen Jane</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">'Queen Jane' is a book written by Dan Barker that tells the story of his experiences with giving away home gardens. In it, he discusses the spiritual aspects of his work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Building Free Vegetable Gardens for People in Need">
      <data key="d0">Building Free Vegetable Gardens for People in Need</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A booklet written by Dan Barker that provides instructions on how to start a project for giving away free vegetable gardens. It is available as a free download from the project's website.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1970s Community Gardens">
      <data key="d0">1970s Community Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical movement of community gardening that took off in the 1970s. Dan Barker believed this model did not adequately cater to all people who could benefit, such as the elderly, single mothers, and the disabled, which influenced his own approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Garden Recipients">
      <data key="d0">Garden Recipients</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The target demographic for the Home Gardening Project, specifically identified as the elderly, people in wheelchairs, the disabled, single mothers, and those impoverished by medical care costs. In Portland, 90% were women.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Executive Builder">
      <data key="d0">Executive Builder</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A key paid role within the Home Gardening Project, described as the person who 'picks up the shovel' and does the arduous physical labor. Dan Barker suggests this person should be paid at least three times the minimum wage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Sclerosis Society">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Sclerosis Society</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific example of a community organization that can be contacted to find potential recipients for the Home Gardening Project, particularly those with disabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multiple Sclerosis Society is mentioned as an example of a local organization to contact for support when starting a community garden project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cca03f696c02b7f1dcaa2eb85e1bf380</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barker">
      <data key="d0">Barker</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A long-time advocate for community gardening, having started his work in 1983. He views gardening as a form of community preventive medicine and a way to improve quality of life. He was interviewed by Biography Magazine in 2003.&lt;SEP&gt;Barker is the founder of the Home Gardening Project, a movement focused on building free gardens for those in need. He started his work in Portland, operated for 15 years, and inspired hundreds of similar projects across the U.S. and elsewhere. After experiencing threats and stress, he moved to southern Oregon and now focuses on raising and distributing funds to other grassroots garden projects. He has been featured in Smithsonian and Biography Magazine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richard Doss">
      <data key="d0">Richard Doss</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A former undergraduate student at Evergreen College who was inspired to start the Kitchen Garden Project, which later grew into the nonprofit GRuB.&lt;SEP&gt;Richard Doss was a student at Evergreen College who, inspired by Barker, founded the Kitchen Garden Project in Olympia, Washington. He successfully developed this project into a nationally acclaimed nonprofit organization called Garden-Raised Bounty (GRuB).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aaeba6a87de6589129068300a4f26db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kitchen Garden Project">
      <data key="d0">Kitchen Garden Project</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A college-based project started in 1992 by Richard Doss at Evergreen College that evolved into the larger nonprofit organization, GRuB.&lt;SEP&gt;The Kitchen Garden Project was founded by Richard Doss at Evergreen College. It provides free gardens to low-income people and operates as a course for college credit. Since 1993, it has built over 2,200 gardens and evolved into the nonprofit GRuB.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aaeba6a87de6589129068300a4f26db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Garden-Raised Bounty (GRuB)">
      <data key="d0">Garden-Raised Bounty (GRuB)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Garden-Raised Bounty (GRuB) is a successful nonprofit organization in Olympia, Washington, directed by Richard Doss. It grew out of the Kitchen Garden Project and has gained national acclaim for its work in providing free gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flint Urban Gardening and Land Use Corporation">
      <data key="d0">Flint Urban Gardening and Land Use Corporation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Flint Urban Gardening and Land Use Corporation is an organization in Flint, Michigan, highlighted by Barker as a successful example of adapting his model to transform abandoned yards into block gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Growing Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Growing Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Growing Gardens is a nonprofit organization based in Portland, Oregon, that was founded in 1996. Evolving from Barker's original Home Gardening Project, its mission is to promote home-scale organic food gardening to improve health, self-reliance, and access to healthy food, primarily for low-income populations, schools, and urban communities.

The organization's central program involves installing free home gardens for low-income participants, who are required to make a three-year commitment. To ensure their success, Growing Gardens provides comprehensive, ongoing support through a large volunteer base, which includes seeds, tools, supplies, workshops, and personal mentors. In addition to its home gardening focus, the organization works with neighborhoods and schools to establish gardens and provide fresh food. It runs several key initiatives, including the "Youth Grow" after-school program to educate children, the School Garden Coordinator Certificate Training, and the "Tour de Coops" fundraiser.

Growing Gardens is located at 2003 NE 42nd Avenue, #3, Portland, OR. It has produced a video titled "Digging at the Root of Hunger" to highlight its work. Key individuals associated with the organization include representatives Rodney Bender, Caitlin Blethen, Debra Lippoldt, and Nell Tessman, as well as Anthony Flaccovento.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9297eb734555e7b850045590e4fc5cb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
