    <node id="Biography Magazine">
      <data key="d0">Biography Magazine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication that interviewed Barker in 2003, where he discussed his plan to distribute funds for home gardens in two hundred cities if he were given $5 million.&lt;SEP&gt;Biography Magazine is a media publication that interviewed Barker in 2003 about his philanthropic goals and grassroots approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Biography Magazine is a publication that featured an article by Bob Frost in May 2003, as cited in the notes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Portland">
      <data key="d0">Portland</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Portland is a city in Oregon that is home to several notable community programs, educational institutions, and progressive urban agriculture policies. It is the location of Portland State University and Portland Community College.

The city is a hub for non-profit and community-based initiatives. The organization Growing Gardens operates throughout Portland, continuing the work of Richard Barker's Home Gardening Project, which he began and ran for 15 years. The city was also a recipient of Barker's 2003 funding. Growing Gardens runs programs such as the school-based "Youth Grow" in partnership with four local elementary schools, and hosts events like the "Tour de Coops" in east Portland. The city government and its Water Bureau assist with soil testing, as lead contamination is a recognized issue in the area's soil. Other key projects in the city include the Village Gardens project, which aims to expand across its communities, and the successful garden and kitchen program at Abernethy Elementary School. These community garden initiatives are active in neighborhoods like New Columbia and St. Johns Woods.

In addition to gardening programs, Portland provides social services through organizations like Janus Youth, which operates a significant 60-bed shelter for homeless youth in the downtown area.

Portland is also noted for its policies regarding urban animals. The city has approved the keeping of backyard poultry, and unlike other municipalities, it never made keeping goats illegal. An anecdote from Dr. Jon Gelbard mentions an observation in Portland where sheep and goats were used for lawn maintenance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d7d0c8754f5ccddfb78068c25f0fa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edff28cc5d3b6f311f02cd2481c28fb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c16ea96bc625614680cc6d8276e6ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
