    <node id="Boston">
      <data key="d0">Boston</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Boston is a major city in the United States, located in the state of Massachusetts. It serves as the primary geographic setting for various community and environmental initiatives.

The city is the base of operations for The Food Project, a nonprofit with a diverse funding model that maintains urban plots and conducts work on soil contamination, specifically focusing on the Roxbury and Dorchester neighborhoods. Boston is also home to the South End/Lower Roxbury Open Space Land Trust (SELROSLT) and the site of numerous community gardens, including the Worcester Street Garden and the Bessie Barnes Garden, which are located in the South End and Lower Roxbury neighborhoods.

Several specific projects and studies have taken place in Boston. A soil remediation project occurred in the Jamaica Plain neighborhood near the Orange Line, and the city government contributed to remediation efforts by donating tested, clean compost. The city's community gardens were also the subject of a study by the Boston University School of Public Health regarding soil contamination. In 2003, a local garden project received start-up funds from Barker, made possible by a grant from the Wallace Genetics Foundation.

Furthermore, Boston is cited as an example of a community that has changed its laws to permit residents to raise backyard poultry.&lt;SEP&gt;The city in Massachusetts where Little, Brown and Company is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc825bb5fbec77abff4a02be6fc5fcec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fec31f78ae747d4c7614c6c15fff42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a25021ab306ceaf5d58e192cc6fc0a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16e699b4bb77d0bdc27d883c3cafa88c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8965f3e3965322fdf5c53aebddde1e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Gardening">
      <data key="d0">Community Gardening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the provided descriptions, Community Gardening is the central theme and practice of residents collectively gardening on shared plots of land. It is presented as a movement that focuses on building free gardens, particularly in disenfranchised and high-risk neighborhoods, to provide food for low-income individuals. Initially an unconventional project for an organization called Janus Youth, Community Gardening evolved into a powerful tool that serves as a platform for building relationships, strengthening the community, and fostering social integration. Its benefits extend to empowering residents and immigrants, engaging youth, reducing crime, and offering therapeutic value to participants. Despite these positive outcomes, the practice faces management challenges and requires support from the city or the wider community to be successful.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc825bb5fbec77abff4a02be6fc5fcec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a815073ae837fa29833af41e55990ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="City Government">
      <data key="d0">City Government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A municipal governing body suggested as a resource for obtaining maps of disenfranchised neighborhoods and potentially securing government-sponsored self-help funding for community projects.&lt;SEP&gt;City Government is identified as a key stakeholder and potential source of support for urban farms. Gaining its backing often requires the submission of a solid business plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neighborhood Association">
      <data key="d0">Neighborhood Association</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local community organization whose newsletters and meetings are recommended as a channel for announcing and promoting new gardening projects to the public, ideally starting in mid-January.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Threats Against Barker">
      <data key="d0">Threats Against Barker</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A series of dangerous and stressful incidents, including being threatened, shoved, shot at, and insulted, that Barker experienced while working in distressed Portland neighborhoods, ultimately leading to his relocation in 1996.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barker's 2003 Funding Initiative">
      <data key="d0">Barker's 2003 Funding Initiative</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An initiative in 2003 where Barker, using a grant from the Wallace Genetics Foundation, distributed start-up funds to grassroots garden projects in various cities, including Boston, Portland, and Flint.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Income People">
      <data key="d0">Low-Income People</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary demographic and beneficiaries of the free garden projects. The Kitchen Garden Project specifically focuses on providing its services to this group.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Disenfranchised Neighborhoods">
      <data key="d0">Disenfranchised Neighborhoods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Identified as the optimal locations for establishing community garden projects to maximize social impact and qualify for potential government funding. These areas are characterized by a lack of resources and social challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Therapeutic Value of a Garden">
      <data key="d0">Therapeutic Value of a Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core concept promoted by Barker, who believes that gardening provides significant mental and social benefits. He claims it gives agoraphobic people a reason to go outside and helps to quiet neighborhoods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grassroots Level Action">
      <data key="d0">Grassroots Level Action</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The fundamental philosophy guiding Barker's work, which emphasizes direct, community-based action (building gardens) over the creation and management of large, bureaucratic nonprofit organizations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82372fd5a606e148caa2a0647673892b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Gardening Project Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Home Gardening Project Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A foundation dedicated to promoting home gardening, closely associated with Sally Eason."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An organization that publishes materials on how to start and manage home gardening projects. The checklist in the text is adapted from one of its publications.&lt;SEP&gt;The Home Gardening Project Foundation is an organization focused on home gardening, mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Gardening">
      <data key="d0">Home Gardening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The central concept of the text, described as an activity to improve nutrition, health, self-reliance, and quality of life. It encompasses fundraising, building garden beds, teaching, and community support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robin Proebsting">
      <data key="d0">Robin Proebsting</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Robin Proebsting is a researcher and writer who contributed to the work this text is an index for. As a graduate student at the University of Virginia, she earned a master's degree in urban and environmental planning. Proebsting is frequently mentioned in connection with locations like Seattle and topics such as urban farming. She is credited with conducting research for and contributing to numerous sections of the text, specifically on the Seattle Urban Farm Company, Seattle's P-Patch Community Gardening Program, the 'Growing Gardens' organization, The Goat Justice League, Greensgrow Farm, The Food Project, the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project, and Janus Youth Programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07d81959e2e7dfd429fb34ce74beae5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc825bb5fbec77abff4a02be6fc5fcec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7ecda741e6aebaebfb4bbcd928bb80b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aab30ee2b8630558ddd6575d42c69264&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd12c93ca420ae0dfd8107f51eec1886</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Portland, Oregon">
      <data key="d0">Portland, Oregon</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Portland, Oregon is a major city known for its progressive food culture and urban agriculture initiatives. It is the operational base for several non-profit organizations, including Janus Youth and Growing Gardens. A small garden at a youth shelter called 'Harry's Mother' evolved into a large-scale initiative managed by Janus Youth, and a community meeting in response to a USDA grant also led to a new garden project in the city. The nonprofit Growing Gardens works in Portland by installing home food gardens for low-income communities and partnering with local neighborhoods and schools, such as Kelly Elementary School and Abernethy Elementary School. The city has seen various gardening projects, including one where residents of a housing development started small plots to supplement their food supply. Additionally, a study in Portland found that a Farm to School program providing a farm-fresh salad bar resulted in students consuming roughly one additional serving of fruits and vegetables per day.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fef6f9e3cdaa27cfe32361bcab20e464&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45291ea55455adae718c5031c0e8dfff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04be01c6169d0bdd92b10dca615a3936&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9297eb734555e7b850045590e4fc5cb6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Debra Lippoldt">
      <data key="d0">Debra Lippoldt</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Debra Lippoldt is the executive director and a representative for the organization Growing Gardens. She articulates the organization's mission, which is to help people, particularly those in low-income populations, access fresh food and resources. Lippoldt provides insights into the high demand for Growing Gardens' services and explains the rationale for creating a certificate training program. She also comments on the success of both the garden clubs and the volunteer program for garden installations. Regarding the organization's work with schools, Lippoldt explains their strategy of targeting schools where at least 50% of students qualify for free or reduced-price lunch. To ensure the long-term success and sustainability of these school gardens, she emphasizes the necessity of a long-term budget, administrative buy-in, and a designated team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monique">
      <data key="d0">Monique</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A participant in the 'Growing Gardens' program, described as a single mother of seven who finds gardening to be a significant stress reliever.&lt;SEP&gt;A participant in the Growing Gardens program who highlights the value of the mentorship component, noting she has a wonderful mentor who communicates frequently and provides support throughout the year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Isabel">
      <data key="d0">Isabel</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A program participant who finds emotional support and happiness from her garden, stating that it cheers her when she is sad.&lt;SEP&gt;An immigrant from Mexico City and a participant in the 'Growing Gardens' program. She grows food for her family and neighbors and finds emotional comfort in her garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mexico City">
      <data key="d0">Mexico City</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The former home of Isabel, a 'Growing Gardens' participant. She contrasts the food she grows now with the chemically-treated produce she used to buy there.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Violet">
      <data key="d0">Violet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An elderly participant in the 'Growing Gardens' program whose backyard garden is so productive that it greatly reduces her need to shop for groceries.&lt;SEP&gt;An elderly woman who is a participant and beneficiary of the Growing Gardens program. Her backyard was transformed into a productive garden that supplies most of the groceries for her and her husband.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Preventive Medicine">
      <data key="d0">Community Preventive Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept articulated by Barker, who believes that providing home gardens to people in need is a form of proactive health care for the community that raises the quality of life without causing harm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deflationary Pickle">
      <data key="d0">Deflationary Pickle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An economic condition mentioned by Barker, describing a long-term period of deflation that he believes the country is experiencing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Well-being">
      <data key="d0">Well-being</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A central theme representing the quality of life in a country. Barker notes that the general attitude towards what constitutes well-being is changing, and he connects gardening to this concept.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Democratic World">
      <data key="d0">Democratic World</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geopolitical concept representing countries with democratic governments. Barker hopes the positive cultural changes happening in the US will be disseminated throughout this region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Places Where Tyranny Reigns">
      <data key="d0">Places Where Tyranny Reigns</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geopolitical concept for non-democratic countries. Barker expresses a desire for the positive impact of gardening to also reach these areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Garden Project Checklist">
      <data key="d0">Home Garden Project Checklist</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A detailed list of practical questions and steps for starting a home garden project. It covers key areas like funding, labor, materials, insurance, and participant recruitment. The text notes it is adapted from a publication of the Home Gardening Project Foundation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-income Populations">
      <data key="d0">Low-income Populations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary demographic group served by the 'Growing Gardens' organization. The text highlights that this group has a high demand for home gardens, contrary to stereotypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bc7bd57341c3dd9d82ef3889268cc30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caitlin Blethen">
      <data key="d0">Caitlin Blethen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Caitlin Blethen is a representative for Growing Gardens and the manager of its school-based youth program, known as the Youth Grow program. In her role, she emphasizes the importance of connecting children to their food sources to encourage them to eat vegetables they have grown themselves. Blethen also highlights that building trust with students, teachers, and parents over time is crucial, and believes in starting with fun, engaging activities to establish this connection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rodney Bender">
      <data key="d0">Rodney Bender</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Growing Gardens."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The garden programs manager for Growing Gardens. He reports on survey data indicating that program participants save significant amounts of money and eat healthier by growing their own food and thinking more about their food choices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Youth Grow Program">
      <data key="d0">Youth Grow Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A program operated by Growing Gardens that focuses on educating elementary school children through after-school garden clubs and summer camps. It aims to create positive connections between kids and fresh food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Gardening Program">
      <data key="d0">Home Gardening Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary program of Growing Gardens for individuals and families, which requires a three-year commitment. It provides participants with a complete support system, including a garden installation, a mentor, and educational resources, to ensure long-term gardening success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Schools Uniting Neighborhoods Program">
      <data key="d0">Schools Uniting Neighborhoods Program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A community partnership program that provides resources to schools for extracurricular services. Growing Gardens leverages this existing program to implement its after-school garden clubs in Portland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Gardeners">
      <data key="d0">Home Gardeners</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary beneficiaries of Growing Gardens' main program. They are individuals and families, often from low-income populations, who commit to a three-year program to learn to grow their own food with support, materials, and mentorship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Personal Mentor">
      <data key="d0">Personal Mentor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key support role within the Growing Gardens Home Gardening Program. Each participant is assigned a personal mentor who visits multiple times during the growing season to provide personalized guidance and support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="After-school garden clubs">
      <data key="d0">After-school garden clubs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An activity run by Growing Gardens as part of the Youth Grow program. These clubs meet weekly at elementary schools, teaching children hands-on gardening skills like planting, composting, and harvesting in an extracurricular setting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summer garden camps">
      <data key="d0">Summer garden camps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A school-based program developed by Growing Gardens as an extension of its successful after-school garden clubs, providing gardening education and activities for children during the summer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Portland elementary schools">
      <data key="d0">Portland elementary schools</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of four schools in Portland that partner with Growing Gardens for the Youth Grow program. These schools are selected based on serving a high percentage of low-income students and participating in the Schools Uniting Neighborhoods program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Active parent programs">
      <data key="d0">Active parent programs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An existing program structure within partner schools that Growing Gardens leverages to connect with parents, understand their needs, and hear suggestions. These are encouraged by the Schools Uniting Neighborhoods program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parent-child workshops">
      <data key="d0">Parent-child workshops</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A series of workshops developed by Growing Gardens focusing on summer gardening activities. They are designed to engage both parents and children together in topics like planting, harvesting, and composting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e70107854e0f943a28c957596ea5c0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blethen">
      <data key="d0">Blethen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"An individual associated with Growing Gardens who articulates the organization's philosophy that food and gardening are powerful tools for binding people together and promoting community health."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="School Garden Coordinator Certificate Training">
      <data key="d0">School Garden Coordinator Certificate Training</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A five-day, 35-hour certificate program created by Growing Gardens to train individuals in creating and sustaining quality garden-based education programs in schools. It was developed to meet overwhelming demand."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tour de Coops">
      <data key="d0">Tour de Coops</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"An annual self-guided fundraising tour started by Growing Gardens in 2003. Participants visit backyard chicken coops in east Portland, with the event serving as both a fundraiser and a community-building activity."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Community Gardening Association">
      <data key="d0">American Community Gardening Association</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Community Gardening Association (ACGA) is a national organization focused on providing extensive information and resources for community gardens. It offers support such as start-up guides, sample forms, a database of existing gardens, and training for starting sustainable community gardens, with a shared emphasis on the importance of beginning with community support. Betsy Johnson has held leadership roles within the organization, having served as its former president and former interim executive director.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b1f6adc63dbe7f3160ab5bd7b25233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Food Project">
      <data key="d0">The Food Project</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based in the Boston, Massachusetts metropolitan area, The Food Project is a non-profit organization established in 1991 that is recognized as a premier national model for youth empowerment and community-focused urban agriculture. Its mission is to build a sustainable food system, provide youth leadership opportunities, and inspire community change by connecting young people with land and community through food.

Operationally, The Food Project is a large organization with a budget of approximately $3 million to $3.3 million (as of 2009), 35 full-time staff members, and a diversified funding model that relies on individual donations, foundations, and corporate revenue, with very little dependence on government grants. It operates multiple farm and garden sites, including the Baker Bridge Farm at 10 Lewis Street in Lincoln, MA, as well as plots in Ipswich, Beverly, and the Boston neighborhoods of Roxbury and Dorchester. The organization produces a significant amount of food, with annual harvests reported between 200,000 and over 350,000 pounds, which is then sold and distributed to the community. To improve food access, it runs initiatives like the "Bounty Bucks" program in low-income communities.

A central component of The Food Project is its highly structured youth programs, which employ teenagers from diverse urban and suburban backgrounds in paid jobs. Through a tiered system of opportunities including the DIG, Internship, and BLAST (The Blast Youth Initiative) programs, teens learn about sustainable production farming, efficient agricultural work, social justice, and environmentalism, empowering them to become leaders.

The Food Project is also a valuable resource and research hub. It provides free online manuals, including the 'Rural Grower's Manual,' 'Urban Grower's Manual,' 'Farmers' Market Manual,' and a 'Volunteer Program Manual' that showcases its effective strategies for managing its nearly 3,000 annual volunteers. The organization has also authored the book 'French Fries and the Food System,' written by Sara Coblyn. Furthermore, it actively researches soil lead contamination, providing guidelines for safe urban gardening and partnering with Wellesley College to study phytoremediation techniques. The organization and its programs have been the subject of multiple academic studies, including theses by Iris Ahronowitz and a doctoral study by Lianne Fisman. Its representative has been noted as Jen James.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aab30ee2b8630558ddd6575d42c69264&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aaeba6a87de6589129068300a4f26db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8965f3e3965322fdf5c53aebddde1e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16e699b4bb77d0bdc27d883c3cafa88c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e0df78d09fb33ee041307abbce7d33e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d076831b7a91bcd605c1e387112d81e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb8186cf72c91110756ec67bcc379a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07d81959e2e7dfd429fb34ce74beae5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Garden-Based Education">
      <data key="d0">Garden-Based Education</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An educational approach that uses gardens as a teaching tool. Growing Gardens actively promotes this by working with schools and offering a specialized training program to create sustainable programs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community-Building">
      <data key="d0">Community-Building</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key outcome and intentional goal of Growing Gardens' activities. The text highlights how gardening, volunteer work, and events like the 'Tour de Coops' are used to connect neighbors and strengthen community bonds."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Income Communities">
      <data key="d0">Low-Income Communities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The primary demographic group that Growing Gardens focuses its efforts on, particularly for its home garden installation program and school-based initiatives."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="School System">
      <data key="d0">School System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The broader institutional framework within which Growing Gardens aims to foster sustainable, long-term change through its garden-based education programs and coordinator training."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Volunteer Program">
      <data key="d0">Volunteer Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The operational arm of Growing Gardens responsible for installing home gardens. It is described as a highly popular activity that mobilizes hundreds of community members."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A program within Growing Gardens that encourages graduates of the three-year home gardener program to become volunteers, creating a sustainable cycle of community support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Security">
      <data key="d0">Food Security</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Food security is defined as the state of having reliable access to a sufficient quantity of affordable, nutritious food. This concept is a central theme and primary mission for the Beardsley Community Farm, which aims to address the lack of food access in Knoxville's low-income communities. Similarly, it is a key objective of the Troy community gardens, an antipoverty program that provides a reliable food source for low-income families. For the organization Growing Gardens, food security is the overarching goal and context for its mission, describing itself as a piece of the "bigger food-security puzzle." An example of achieving food security is the historical success of the Hopi and Navajo, who used agricultural biodiversity to ensure a reliable and sufficient food source.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-488bc663aa4ded0ca40ac601222c065d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb8186cf72c91110756ec67bcc379a67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Garden">
      <data key="d0">Community Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of garden that serves a neighborhood or community. The text mentions that some schools are located near them and that expertise in starting them is a key practice."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A shared community project that acts as a catalyst for broader community awareness, economic opportunities, and social justice. Its success is fundamentally dependent on being initiated, owned, and led by the community itself.&lt;SEP&gt;The central concept of the text, representing a shared space for growing food that also functions as a powerful tool for building social capital, developing skills, improving health, and fostering community connections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ed0d32c6d653b34677909bb2a4df08b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d7d0c8754f5ccddfb78068c25f0fa0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Portland's Water Bureau">
      <data key="d0">Portland's Water Bureau</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A government bureau in Portland that provides funding to Growing Gardens to test the soil at its garden sites for potential contaminants like lead.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SNAP (Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program)">
      <data key="d0">SNAP (Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A federal assistance program whose income-level requirements are used by Growing Gardens as a benchmark to determine eligibility for its home gardener program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WIC (Women, Infants and Children) Program">
      <data key="d0">WIC (Women, Infants and Children) Program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A federal assistance program whose income-level requirements are also used by Growing Gardens to establish eligibility for participation in its gardening initiatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lippoldt">
      <data key="d0">Lippoldt</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A person associated with Growing Gardens who is quoted in the text, providing insight into the varying levels of expertise among new home gardeners.&lt;SEP&gt;Lippoldt is an individual who suggests that school systems should hire full-time garden program staff to provide consistency and support for system-wide gardening initiatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9297eb734555e7b850045590e4fc5cb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learn and Grow Workshops">
      <data key="d0">Learn and Grow Workshops</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A series of educational workshops hosted by Growing Gardens, some offered in Spanish, designed to teach gardening skills. They are open to the broader community on a sliding-scale donation basis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seattle Urban Farm Company">
      <data key="d0">Seattle Urban Farm Company</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the provided descriptions, the Seattle Urban Farm Company is a for-profit enterprise located at 11550 North Park Avenue North in Seattle, Washington. Founded in 2007 by Colin McCrate, with Brad Halm and Hilary Halm also identified as co-founders or representatives, the company specializes in urban farming. It provides a range of services for both private and commercial clients, including homeowners, businesses, and restaurants. These services include the design, installation, and maintenance of urban food gardens, as well as the construction of backyard chicken coops. The company's mission is to create a profitable and sustainable small-scale farming model, emphasizing the use of quality materials, operational efficiency, and a diverse customer base.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seattle">
      <data key="d0">Seattle</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Based in Washington state, Seattle is a major U.S. city recognized as a pioneer in local food matters and urban agriculture. The city demonstrates a strong municipal commitment to these initiatives through its P-Patch Community Gardening Program, which has operated for over four decades. Although this program includes more than 70 garden sites, demand for plots consistently outstrips supply, and its operations have been impacted by budget and political shifts over the years. The program is a central topic in Jim Diers' book, 'Neighborhood Power: Building Community the Seattle Way'.

Seattle is home to several key organizations supporting urban food systems. These include the for-profit Seattle Urban Farm Company, which meets significant local demand for gardening services, and the nonprofit community development organization Solid Ground, which runs the Community Fruit Tree Harvest gleaning program in neighborhoods like the University District. Other initiatives include Marra Farm, which serves as a model for community gardens in public parks, and a pioneering market-gardening project on public land.

The city has also enacted progressive laws regarding urban livestock. It is one of the major U.S. cities that permits backyard poultry and has legalized the keeping of miniature goats as pets. A city council ordinance reclassified the goats as small animals, a change spurred by residents like Jennie Grant, who founded the Goat Justice League and successfully raised goats at her home. The city now hosts over 100 urban goats. This environmental focus also extends to pollinators, with bees kept at city hall and "bee condos" installed in parks. Furthermore, Seattle is home to organizations like Seattle Tilth and the Municipal Research and Services Center (MRSC), and its landscape is noted for its prevalent wild blackberry bushes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc825bb5fbec77abff4a02be6fc5fcec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07f097aee6be0e05076e0e695419438a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6db795582f52e35a0bb9aea86e66f7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colin Mccrate">
      <data key="d0">Colin Mccrate</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for the Seattle Urban Farm Company."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Founder of the Seattle Urban Farm Company in 2007. He attended Denison University's 'Homestead' and is a pioneer in the business of providing urban gardening services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brad Halm">
      <data key="d0">Brad Halm</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for the Seattle Urban Farm Company."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A co-pioneer with Colin McCrate in the urban farming business. He also attended Denison University's 'Homestead', where he learned about ecological sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Denison University">
      <data key="d0">Denison University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A university that hosts the 'Homestead,' a student-run intentional community focused on ecological sustainability, which was attended by both Colin McCrate and Brad Halm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homestead">
      <data key="d0">Homestead</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A student-run intentional community at Denison University with a focus on ecological sustainability. It provided formative experiences in gardening and sustainable living for Colin McCrate and Brad Halm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Gardener Program">
      <data key="d0">Home Gardener Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Home Gardener Program is the primary initiative of Growing Gardens, designed to assist low-income families by providing them with household gardens, tools, seeds, and ongoing support for a three-year period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Household Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Household Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Household gardens are the physical product provided by Growing Gardens' program, consisting of two 4-foot-by-8-foot garden beds installed at participants' homes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Double-dug Beds">
      <data key="d0">Double-dug Beds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific gardening technique used by Growing Gardens to install new gardens. It is preferred over raised beds for its long-term sustainability, as it avoids the need to add more soil later.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Raised Beds">
      <data key="d0">Raised Beds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of garden installation that Growing Gardens uses as a contingency plan. They are installed if soil tests reveal contaminants like lead, and are lined with plastic and filled with clean soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lead Contamination">
      <data key="d0">Lead Contamination</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific soil issue common in Portland that poses a health risk. Growing Gardens' protocol is designed to detect and mitigate this problem by using raised beds.&lt;SEP&gt;Lead Contamination is a specific, severe form of soil contamination frequently found in urban areas. It poses a significant health risk, disproportionately affecting poor and minority communities, and is a primary focus of The Food Project's work in Boston.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Governments">
      <data key="d0">Local Governments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of governing body mentioned as a good source of assistance for soil testing, as they often have access to federal funding for such purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Governing bodies that communities and nonprofits work with to change laws and regulations in order to permit the raising of backyard chickens in urban and suburban areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Orientation">
      <data key="d0">Orientation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A mandatory meeting for all participants of Growing Gardens' Home Gardener Program, designed to ensure they understand their responsibilities in maintaining their new garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
