      <data key="d2">A client category for the Seattle Urban Farm Company, which also offers its urban gardening services to commercial entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Backyard Chicken Coops">
      <data key="d0">Backyard Chicken Coops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific service offered by the Seattle Urban Farm Company, involving the design and installation of coops for raising chickens in a backyard setting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="City Folk">
      <data key="d0">City Folk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A term describing the target market of the Seattle Urban Farm Company: urban dwellers who desire to grow their own food but may lack the time or knowledge to do so.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="McCrate">
      <data key="d0">McCrate</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-founder of the Seattle Urban Farm Company. He is portrayed as an enthusiastic entrepreneur who pushed to start the business immediately, believing the timing was right. He manages client relationships and business operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Halm">
      <data key="d0">Halm</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-founder of the Seattle Urban Farm Company. He shares a background in gardening and farming with McCrate and helped establish the business.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Urban Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary product and service offered by the Seattle Urban Farm Company. The text details the process of designing, installing, and maintaining these gardens for clients, customized to their budgets and yard conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Breed of Farmer">
      <data key="d0">New Breed of Farmer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept describing the type of entrepreneur who runs an urban farming business like Seattle Urban Farm Company. This farmer is characterized as enjoying working with people, preferring urban life, and having low start-up costs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clients">
      <data key="d0">Clients</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The customers of the Seattle Urban Farm Company. They have varying levels of knowledge and specific desires for their gardens and chicken coops, and often have budgets that require careful planning and consultation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Business Plan">
      <data key="d0">Business Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Business Plan is a formal document that outlines a business's goals and the strategies it will use to achieve them. Specifically, it explains how the business will manage its revenue, expenses, loan repayment, and market analysis. A farm plan is considered a specific type of business plan. For urban farming projects, a Business Plan is a critical document for seeking support and backing from city governments, as a solid plan can build confidence in a farm's chances of success. While it is a key document for outlining strategies, some, like McCrate and Halm, have chosen not to create one initially. They later acknowledged that a formal business plan would have been helpful during difficult times, but also felt that creating one at the very beginning might have dampened their enthusiasm and initiative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chickens">
      <data key="d0">Chickens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of livestock whose meat can generally be butchered at home. The text uses chickens to illustrate state-level regulatory exemptions, such as in Missouri where 20,000 live chickens can be sold annually without USDA inspection."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A subject category focusing on the practice of raising backyard chickens. The text provides several online resources for enthusiasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Animals raised in backyards as part of the company's supplementary service. They are described as rewarding and changing a yard's atmosphere, but also more complicated than gardens due to needs for proper food storage and protection from predators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Salvage Companies">
      <data key="d0">Local Salvage Companies</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Businesses from which the Seattle Urban Farm Company sources some materials for building chicken coops, in addition to environmentally responsible store-bought materials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rats">
      <data key="d0">Rats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pests that can be attracted by spilled chicken feed, presenting a complication and a problem that needs to be managed when raising chickens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17659302dc2bf4db1dc4216f4ab25fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colin McCrate">
      <data key="d0">Colin McCrate</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-founder of the Seattle Urban Farm Company. He is described as entrepreneurial and provides business advice on using quality materials, managing projects efficiently, and expanding into commercial work with restaurants and developers. He has a vision for integrating gardens into all new developments in Seattle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hilary Halm">
      <data key="d0">Hilary Halm</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-founder of the Seattle Urban Farm Company. Halm emphasizes the importance of finding a job one loves and being spiritually satisfied with daily work. He acknowledges the necessity of robust record-keeping and systematization for the business to be successful and reliable, despite a personal dislike for the task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Backyard Chickens">
      <data key="d0">Backyard Chickens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Backyard Chickens refers to the practice of raising small flocks of chickens, particularly hens, in residential, urban, or home garden settings. This growing movement across the United States is promoted for its various benefits, which include providing fresh eggs, pest control, creating healthier yards, and serving as an educational tool for children. The practice has spurred citizen campaigns and led to changes in local laws in communities like Salem, Oregon. However, the topic is also a subject of debate, with arguments against it centered on public health risks like avian flu, concerns about smell and noise, and issues of egg safety. The humane treatment of the animals, often discussed in the context of the 'five freedoms', is also a key consideration. For those interested in this non-farm practice, resources such as learning centers and advocacy sites are available.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Garden Installation Business">
      <data key="d0">Garden Installation Business</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of business that is the focus of the 'Lessons Learned' section. It is characterized by short-term projects (one or two days), requiring a constant stream of new work to remain solvent. The Seattle Urban Farm Company is an example of this business model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Record-keeping System">
      <data key="d0">Record-keeping System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crucial business tool identified by Hilary Halm as essential for success and reliability in a garden installation business. It includes managing a seeding schedule, tracking tools, billing, and job hours.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commercial Businesses">
      <data key="d0">Commercial Businesses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key customer base for the Seattle Urban Farm Company, including restaurants and developments. These clients provide larger, longer-term projects that offer financial stability, free marketing, and new customer referrals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Communities And Nonprofits">
      <data key="d0">Communities And Nonprofits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Groups and organizations across the United States that are actively working and campaigning to make backyard chickens a legal and accessible option for all people by collaborating with local governments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New York City">
      <data key="d0">New York City</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A major U.S. city mentioned via a newspaper headline, 'City Facing First Famine in Our History,' which highlighted concerns about the nation's food supply.&lt;SEP&gt;New York City is a major metropolitan city in the United States involved in various urban food system initiatives. The city has enacted several legal changes to support urban agriculture; in 2010, it legalized urban beekeeping by removing honeybees from its health code's list of "venomous insects," leading to a surge in local interest. New York City also changed its laws to permit residents to raise backyard poultry. Historically, in 1999, a plan to auction 114 community gardens was met with public outrage, resulting in a deal brokered by The Trust for Public Land and the New York Restoration Project to save them. Furthermore, the city is home to the organization Just Food, which began its initial projects in the Bronx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b1f6adc63dbe7f3160ab5bd7b25233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Los Angeles">
      <data key="d0">Los Angeles</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A city where the Los Angeles Neighborhood Land Trust was founded in 2004 to create new community gardens in underserved neighborhoods to help revitalize them.&lt;SEP&gt;A major U.S. city cited as an example of a community that has changed its laws to permit residents to raise backyard poultry.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the major US cities listed as having said 'yes' to backyard poultry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0b1f6adc63dbe7f3160ab5bd7b25233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="San Francisco">
      <data key="d0">San Francisco</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">San Francisco is a major U.S. city in California that serves as a geographical reference for the location of Marin County and Marin Organic. The city is the base for several organizations, including the Center for Urban Education about Sustainable Agriculture (CUESA), the Trust for Public Land, and ForageSF. ForageSF, which operates primarily in the broader San Francisco Bay Area, was founded in the city by Iso Rabins, who also established the Underground Market, first held in the Mission District. Additionally, San Francisco is a community that has changed its laws to permit residents to raise backyard poultry.&lt;SEP&gt;The location of the publisher Chronicle Books.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6db795582f52e35a0bb9aea86e66f7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16e699b4bb77d0bdc27d883c3cafa88c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dbce12e45fe13ef4f6973f92e57e779&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chicago">
      <data key="d0">Chicago</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Chicago is a major city in the state of Illinois that has actively supported urban agriculture. In 1996, the city created the NeighborSpace land trust to protect its community gardens from development pressures. Later, in 2002, the organization Growing Power expanded its operations to Chicago, where the local branch, run by Erika Allen, established community gardens, model farms, and educational programs in low-income neighborhoods. The city also pioneered urban beekeeping, installing beehives on its city hall rooftop in 2003, and is a known location for rooftop garden beekeeping. Its climate is beneficially affected by Lake Michigan, which helps to extend the beekeeping season. Additionally, Chicago is one of the major U.S. cities that has changed its laws to permit residents to raise backyard poultry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b1f6adc63dbe7f3160ab5bd7b25233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-123973bb8732b383c984f14c89a9aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
