      <data key="d0">South Portland</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the US cities listed as having said 'yes' to backyard poultry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Everett">
      <data key="d0">Everett</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Everett is a city mentioned alongside Portland as a place that did not have ordinances prohibiting farm animals like goats.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the US cities in Oregon listed as having said 'yes' to backyard poultry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dave Cieslewicz">
      <data key="d0">Dave Cieslewicz</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual mentioned in the index.&lt;SEP&gt;Dave Cieslewicz is identified as the Mayor of Madison through his 'Mayor Dave's Blog'. He wrote posts on topics like 'Urban Agriculture' and 'A Game of Chicken', showing his involvement in local food policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The mayor of Madison, Wisconsin, who is a vocal supporter of backyard chickens. He uses his blog to promote the practice, linking it to community building, economic savings ('eggonomics'), and food justice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Justice">
      <data key="d0">Food Justice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Food Justice is a concept and social movement focused on ensuring equitable, affordable, and accessible access to fresh, healthy, and locally produced food for all residents. The movement aims to address both health and economic inequities, often through the promotion of local food systems. Organizations such as Growing Power and People's Grocery, a key player in West Oakland, are associated with this movement. The concept of Food Justice has also served as a motivation for public policies, such as Madison's pro-chicken initiatives. In a different context, Food Justice is also the name of a publication where Debra Eshmeyer served as an editor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e0df78d09fb33ee041307abbce7d33e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb8186cf72c91110756ec67bcc379a67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Urban Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urban Agriculture is a field of practice, study, and a specific method of farming focused on growing and cultivating food within cities and urban areas. This practice encompasses activities such as community gardening and raising backyard chickens. As a central concept, Urban Agriculture is presented as a crucial and effective tool for addressing various community issues, including hunger, revitalization, and economic development. It aims to provide communities with access to fresh, healthy food, serve an educational purpose, and reconnect urban populations with their food sources. While the practice has distinct advantages like proximity to markets and access to labor, it also faces significant challenges, such as expensive land, potential soil contamination, and regulatory hurdles. Various organizations and individuals have engaged with this field; resources are provided by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, The Food Project, and the Community Food Security Coalition, and it has been studied in academic work like Iris Zippora Ahronowitz's thesis. It is a key activity at places like Troy Gardens, used by organizations such as Janus Youth Programs, and discussed by public figures like Mayor Dave Cieslewicz, with cities like Madison being described as well-positioned to be a leader in the movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16e699b4bb77d0bdc27d883c3cafa88c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robin Ripley">
      <data key="d0">Robin Ripley</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A chicken enthusiast quoted in the text who highlights the social networking and entertainment benefits of owning backyard chickens.&lt;SEP&gt;Robin Ripley is an author for the Gardening Examiner who wrote an article on January 18, 2010, outlining 'Eight Benefits of Raising Backyard Chickens'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Avian Influenza">
      <data key="d0">Avian Influenza</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of bird flu that is a major public health concern associated with backyard chickens. The text notes that the kind contagious to humans has not been found in North America, mitigating one of the main arguments against the practice.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as bird flu, it is a public health concern discussed in the text. The document argues that backyard chickens pose a low risk of transmission to humans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Twentieth Century">
      <data key="d0">Twentieth Century</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The historical period, specifically the last half, in which the Green Revolution took place and transformed global agriculture."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A historical period described as an anomaly for its 'denaturation' of cities, where nature and agriculture were removed in favor of sanitation, sterile landscapes, and car-centric infrastructure, leading to a disconnect from food sources.&lt;SEP&gt;A historical period mentioned in the text, during which the trend of keeping backyard fowl declined before seeing a resurgence in the present day.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Poultry Ban Reversal in Madison">
      <data key="d0">Poultry Ban Reversal in Madison</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific event that occurred in Madison in 2004, where the city's ban on keeping backyard poultry was overturned. This is a key event highlighted in the text as a turning point for the community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d94881bd64e92c01d329307d5d40738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRAIN">
      <data key="d0">GRAIN</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A sustainable agriculture advocacy group that concluded in 2006 that diverse, small-scale poultry farming is a solution rather than a problem concerning bird flu.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consumer Reports">
      <data key="d0">Consumer Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A consumer-focused organization with a health blog that published an article on egg safety and home-grown eggs in July 2009.&lt;SEP&gt;An organization that supported the idea that salmonella infection is less likely in backyard chicken runs compared to factory farms.&lt;SEP&gt;Consumer Reports is an organization whose Health Blog is cited as a source in the document's notes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Animal Welfare Council">
      <data key="d0">Farm Animal Welfare Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An advisory body concerned with the welfare of farm animals, likely addressing issues found in industrial agriculture."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An advisory body to the UK government that developed the 'five freedoms' as a standard for the humane treatment of farm animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="City of Vancouver">
      <data key="d0">City of Vancouver</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The municipal government that produced a 2010 study titled 'Guidelines for Keeping of Backyard Hens', which is referenced in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Goat Justice League">
      <data key="d0">The Goat Justice League</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An organization whose representative is Jennie Grant."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An advocacy group founded by Jennie Grant to legalize backyard goats in Seattle. It now offers education and support to urban goat owners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jennie Grant">
      <data key="d0">Jennie Grant</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for The Goat Justice League."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Jennie Grant is the founder of the Goat Justice League and an advocate for raising goats in urban environments like Seattle. She provides practical advice on community relations, livestock care, and navigating local laws.&lt;SEP&gt;The founder of The Goat Justice League, referred to as the 'godmother of urban goats'. She successfully campaigned to legalize goats in Seattle and raises them herself.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UK">
      <data key="d0">UK</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A country mentioned as a source of meat imported into the U.S., which Molovinsky's local system aimed to make unnecessary.&lt;SEP&gt;A country mentioned as an example of a distant, unverifiable source for meat, contrasting with the local sourcing model of the cooperative.&lt;SEP&gt;The country whose government is advised by the Farm Animal Welfare Council.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seattle, Washington">
      <data key="d0">Seattle, Washington</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A major city in Washington state, home to several urban agriculture initiatives like the P-Patch program and the Seattle Urban Farm Company."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The city where The Goat Justice League successfully campaigned to legalize backyard goats, and where its founder Jennie Grant resides.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Five Freedoms">
      <data key="d0">Five Freedoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of principles for the humane treatment of farm animals, which are applied in the text as basic requirements for keeping backyard chickens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Backyard Goats">
      <data key="d0">Backyard Goats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of urban livestock whose legalization in Seattle was championed by The Goat Justice League. The text discusses the arguments for and against raising them in cities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lake Washington">
      <data key="d0">Lake Washington</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A lake in Seattle overlooked by the backyard pen of Jennie Grant's goats, Snowflake and Maple.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pest Animals">
      <data key="d0">Pest Animals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of animals including coyotes, hawks, raccoons, and rodents, discussed as potential predators of backyard chickens. The text also notes that chickens can help control insect pests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guidelines for Keeping of Backyard Hens">
      <data key="d0">Guidelines for Keeping of Backyard Hens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 2010 study by the City of Vancouver that established guidelines for backyard hen owners, from which the text is adapted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Legalizing Livestock in Urban Environments">
      <data key="d0">Legalizing Livestock in Urban Environments</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A social and legal movement to allow the keeping of animals like goats and chickens in cities, exemplified by the efforts of The Goat Justice League in Seattle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-923ecd82dafb6f5a01be8c322b21fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grant">
      <data key="d0">Grant</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Grant is a goat owner and advocate in Seattle. She founded the Goat Justice League to legalize backyard goats, teaches a 'City Goats 101' workshop for Seattle Tilth, and was interviewed by the Seattle Times. She raises two dairy goats for milk, which her family consumes and she uses to make cheese.&lt;SEP&gt;Grant is a resident of Seattle and a miniature goat owner who faced a legal challenge when her goats were classified as illegal farm animals. She launched and led a successful campaign to have the city legalize miniature goats, which involved collecting petition signatures and collaborating with a city councilor. She now advises others on strategies for legalizing urban goats.&lt;SEP&gt;Grant is an expert on goats who warns about poisonous ornamental plants but is generally supportive of the idea of using goats to clear urban landscapes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richard Conlin">
      <data key="d0">Richard Conlin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual mentioned in the index.&lt;SEP&gt;Richard Conlin is a city councilor in Seattle and a neighbor of Grant. He was sympathetic to her cause, and his office investigated the matter with the Health Department and Seattle Animal Control, finding no significant issues. He was instrumental in the unanimous council vote for the new ordinance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Council President of Seattle who authored a newsletter article titled 'One Small Step For Man, One Giant Leap For Goatkind' in October 2007.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Miniature Goats">
      <data key="d0">Miniature Goats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Miniature Goats are the central subject of the text. Initially classified as illegal 'farm animals' in Seattle, they were reclassified as 'small animals' following a campaign. They are described as being quiet, clean, and requiring minimal space. The city of Seattle now permits up to three per lot and even uses them to clear blackberry brambles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goat Legalization Campaign">
      <data key="d0">Goat Legalization Campaign</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Goat Legalization Campaign was an initiative launched by Grant in Seattle to change the law prohibiting miniature goats on city lots. The campaign successfully used a petition with nearly a thousand signatures and political support from a city councilor to achieve a new city ordinance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Animals">
      <data key="d0">Farm Animals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">'Farm Animals' is a legal and cultural classification that initially applied to miniature goats in Seattle, making them illegal on small city lots due to land size requirements. The text argues this distinction is culturally biased and was a key hurdle to overcome for legalization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Department">
      <data key="d0">Health Department</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A government regulatory body that sets standards for the handling and sale of food products. The narrator consulted this agency to learn the requirements for selling fish, and had to re-consult them to clarify specific packaging rules required by stores."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"A local government agency responsible for health regulations. It is a crucial resource for farmers who need to find or get approval for a processing kitchen for value-added foods and can provide leads on rentable facilities."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The Health Department is a city agency in Seattle that was consulted by Councilor Richard Conlin's office. It reported that there were no significant issues associated with keeping miniature goats in the city.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seattle Animal Control">
      <data key="d0">Seattle Animal Control</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Seattle Animal Control is a city agency that was consulted by Councilor Richard Conlin's office regarding miniature goats. Along with the Health Department, it found no significant issues with keeping them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seattle Post-Intelligencer">
      <data key="d0">Seattle Post-Intelligencer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A news publication that reported on the legalization of keeping miniature goats as pets in Seattle in September 2007.&lt;SEP&gt;The Seattle Post-Intelligencer is a newspaper that quoted Councilman Richard Conlin on the new goat ordinance, highlighting its role in the city's sustainability efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Neighbor">
      <data key="d0">Distant Neighbor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neighbor of Grant who accused Grant's goats, Snowflake and Brownie, of causing her daughter's serious illness. This accusation, though false, triggered the discovery that the goats were illegal under city law.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Factory Farming Industry">
      <data key="d0">Factory Farming Industry</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An industry mentioned by Grant in her argument against the culturally biased distinction between pets and farm animals. It is criticized for treating animals as commodities and obscuring their lives from consumers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goat Illness Accusation">
      <data key="d0">Goat Illness Accusation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An event where a neighbor falsely accused Grant's goats of making her daughter ill. This incident was the catalyst that led Grant to discover the illegality of her goats and launch her legalization campaign.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Goat Ordinance">
      <data key="d0">New Goat Ordinance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The law passed unanimously by the Seattle City Council that legalized miniature goats. It reclassified them from 'farm animals' to 'small animals' and established new licensing requirements similar to those for dogs and cats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Animals">
      <data key="d0">Small Animals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The legal classification created by the new Seattle ordinance to categorize miniature goats, removing them from the 'farm animals' category. This classification treats them similarly to other common pets like dogs, cats, and potbellied pigs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pets">
      <data key="d0">Pets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A cultural and legal classification of animals that is contrasted with 'farm animals'. Grant argues that the distinction is culturally biased and has been shaped by the industrialization of meat production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Personal Liberty">
      <data key="d0">Personal Liberty</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary strategic argument Grant advocates for legalizing urban goats. It posits that city laws should not prevent people from engaging in activities that do not harm others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrialization of Meat Production">
      <data key="d0">Industrialization of Meat Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical and economic process that Grant argues is responsible for creating the modern, rigid, and culturally-based distinction between a 'pet' and a 'farm animal'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3274fdc75290435635fca543d6b0957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goat Justice League">
      <data key="d0">Goat Justice League</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An advocacy group that promotes the legal keeping of urban goats and provides educational resources such as an 'Urban Goats 101' class.&lt;SEP&gt;The Goat Justice League is an organization founded by Grant in Seattle. It successfully advocated for the legalization of backyard goats, using constituent passion and humor to achieve its goal.&lt;SEP&gt;The Goat Justice League is an organization founded by Jennie Grant, likely to advocate for and support the practice of keeping goats in urban areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seattle Tilth">
      <data key="d0">Seattle Tilth</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Seattle Tilth is a local gardening and conservation organization in Seattle. It hosts Grant's popular 'City Goats 101' workshop for aspiring urban goat owners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Didi">
      <data key="d0">Didi</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Didi is a friend of Grant who attended the 'City Goats 101' workshop intending to be dissuaded from getting goats, but was instead convinced to get them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seattle Times">
      <data key="d0">Seattle Times</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A news publication that covered the story of Seattle giving pet status to miniature goats in September 2007.&lt;SEP&gt;The Seattle Times is a newspaper that interviewed Grant, in which she expressed her vision for Seattle to become a 'charming city' with minifarms featuring chickens, goats, and gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cooperative Extension office">
      <data key="d0">Cooperative Extension office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Cooperative Extension office is a type of local resource recommended by Grant for goat owners who need to find a stud buck for breeding purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goat Milk">
      <data key="d0">Goat Milk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Goat milk is a product from dairy goats, noted for its nutritional benefits, including being easier to digest than cow milk and higher in various vitamins and minerals. It is used for drinking and making cheese like chèvre and mozzarella, but is low in folic acid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goat Patrol">
      <data key="d0">Goat Patrol</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Goat Patrol is a company in North Carolina that specializes in using goats to clear and eradicate invasive plant species such as poison ivy, honeysuckle, kudzu, and privet hedges.&lt;SEP&gt;Goat Patrol is a rent-a-goat business located in North Carolina. It advertises its goats for eradicating invasive species such as poison ivy, honeysuckle, kudzu, and privet hedges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="North Carolina">
      <data key="d0">North Carolina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">North Carolina is a state in the Southeastern United States and the Southern Appalachian region, which serves as the primary location for numerous farms, organizations, and agricultural initiatives. Specifically, the western part of the state is a focal point, noted for undergoing a transformation from a region with a declining heritage of tobacco farming into a hub for small, sustainable food farms. This shift is heavily influenced by the work of the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP), which is located in North Carolina and is the home of its key figure, Charlie Jackson.

The state is home to several specific farms and businesses, including Gladheart Farms, Hickory Nut Gap Farm (located in Western North Carolina) along with its partner Sam Dobson's farm, and the rent-a-goat operation Goat Patrol. It is also the location of Warren Wilson College. North Carolina hosts various key initiatives such as the North Carolina Organic Bread Flour Project, which aims to create a viable local grain market, and the Walking Fish cooperative, with activities in the coastal town of Beaufort and the city of Durham. Other mentioned locations within the state include Asheville and Canton. Overall, North Carolina is characterized as having a robust food distribution infrastructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d53cc613c52fd5080e556f9abb248a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d4150698732f393c235b8b3e1cb2d88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7ecda741e6aebaebfb4bbcd928bb80b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e970626c7a63b367949cb5600957bcab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214f4e9d9f3d3fed74243c9bdf50bf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5944a88ca415780f3d4838a47d068512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c8f3e81fd6708a9852f46f0d4ac0a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e3df5ce7be88ffd8bdf29e5b896da3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richard Graham">
      <data key="d0">Richard Graham</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Richard Graham is a writer for the TreeHugger website who reported on the benefits and cost-effectiveness of using goats for land clearing, especially for construction companies.&lt;SEP&gt;Richard Graham is a writer for the TreeHugger website who reported on the increasing use of goats by construction companies for cost-effective and sustainable land clearing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TreeHugger">
      <data key="d0">TreeHugger</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A media outlet focused on travel, nature, and sustainability that published an article on using goats for land management.&lt;SEP&gt;TreeHugger is a website that published an article by Richard Graham about the growing popularity of using goats for land management in construction.&lt;SEP&gt;TreeHugger is a website that published an article by Richard Graham discussing the use of goats in land management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="City Goats 101 Workshop">
      <data key="d0">City Goats 101 Workshop</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'City Goats 101' Workshop is an educational event taught by Grant and hosted by Seattle Tilth. It is designed to teach people about raising backyard goats and is noted for being very popular.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="County Fair">
      <data key="d0">County Fair</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A county fair is a type of event mentioned as a place for goat owners to network to find a stud buck for breeding purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chevre">
      <data key="d0">Chevre</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chevre is a type of soft goat cheese that Grant makes from her goats' milk. It has become an everyday commodity for her, and she contrasts its cost with store-bought versions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mozzarella">
      <data key="d0">Mozzarella</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mozzarella is a type of cheese that Grant makes from her goats' milk. She makes enough to stock her freezer and share with others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cow Milk">
      <data key="d0">Cow Milk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cow milk is a type of milk used in the text as a nutritional benchmark to highlight the benefits and one drawback (lower folic acid) of goat milk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Construction Companies">
      <data key="d0">Construction Companies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of business that increasingly uses goats for cost-effective, environmentally friendly, and quiet land clearing before survey work can be done, finding it a 'win/win' situation.&lt;SEP&gt;Construction companies are a category of business that are increasingly using goats to clear brush and other vegetation from land, finding it to be a cost-effective and win-win solution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06b664b68583390f1a748abc957021ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tammy Dunakin">
      <data key="d0">Tammy Dunakin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Tammy Dunakin is the owner of Rent-A-Ruminant in Vashon, Washington. She was interviewed by 'Living the Country Life Radio' about the logistics of using goats to clear dense vegetation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rent-A-Ruminant">
      <data key="d0">Rent-A-Ruminant</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Rent-A-Ruminant is a business in Vashon, Washington, owned by Tammy Dunakin, that rents out goats for clearing land.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vashon, Washington">
      <data key="d0">Vashon, Washington</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Vashon, Washington is the location of Tammy Dunakin's goat rental business, Rent-A-Ruminant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Living the Country Life Radio">
      <data key="d0">Living the Country Life Radio</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">'Living the Country Life Radio' is a media outlet that conducted an interview with Tammy Dunakin about her goat rental business.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dr. Jon Gelbard">
      <data key="d0">Dr. Jon Gelbard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dr. Jon Gelbard is a scientist at the Conservation Initiative who argues against the universal benefits of goats for land clearing, suggesting they can favor weed invasion and harm native plants, particularly west of the Rockies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conservation Initiative">
      <data key="d0">Conservation Initiative</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Conservation Initiative is an international nonprofit organization focused on preserving biodiversity and global sustainability, where Dr. Jon Gelbard works.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rockies">
      <data key="d0">Rockies</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Rockies are a mountain range mentioned as a geographical dividing line, with native plants to the west being less tolerant of heavy grazing by animals like goats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cornell Cooperative Extension">
      <data key="d0">Cornell Cooperative Extension</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cornell Cooperative Extension is an agricultural institution whose specialists have identified meat-goat production as the fastest-growing livestock industry in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Management-Intensive Grazing (Mig)">
      <data key="d0">Management-Intensive Grazing (Mig)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural practice for cattle operations that focuses on strategic management to improve profitability and sustainability. It is presented as a long-term goal requiring significant planning for infrastructure like fences and water lines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Hog Sausage Production">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Hog Sausage Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sample short-term farm goal involving the purchase of feeder pigs to be raised and sold as whole-hog sausage, requiring decisions on sourcing, feed, and marketing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Portable Chicken Tractor Operation">
      <data key="d0">Portable Chicken Tractor Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A short-term farm goal to raise meat birds in portable cages, known as chicken tractors, which allow them to graze and fertilize a garden without damaging plants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egg-Laying Operation">
      <data key="d0">Egg-Laying Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farm business, presented as a goal, that involves buying ready-to-lay pullets and requires specific decisions about feed, production levels, and pricing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Specialty Popcorn">
      <data key="d0">Specialty Popcorn</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific crop used as an anecdote by 'Small Farm Today' to illustrate the failure that results from a lack of market planning before production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weed, Disease, and Insect Problems">
      <data key="d0">Weed, Disease, and Insect Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Common agricultural challenges that a farm's business plan should address through a production strategy, preferably one that lowers purchased inputs and builds soil fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drip Irrigation">
      <data key="d0">Drip Irrigation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A limited water usage technique mentioned as a method for farms to counter the negative effects of weather, demonstrating a proactive planning component.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yorkshire">
      <data key="d0">Yorkshire</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A breed of pig mentioned in a sample short-term goal. A crossbreed of Yorkshire and Hampshire pigs is suggested for purchase from Bob Smith for sausage production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hampshire">
      <data key="d0">Hampshire</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A breed of pig mentioned in a sample short-term goal. A crossbreed of Hampshire and Yorkshire pigs is suggested for purchase for a whole-hog sausage operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a17c911f4ce144036087ad77f694974d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stockman Grass Farmer">
      <data key="d0">Stockman Grass Farmer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An agricultural publication that featured an article by Allan Nation on the benefits of grass-fed products."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A newspaper focused on farming, for which Allan Nation serves as the editor. It was instrumental in popularizing the term 'management-intensive grazing'.&lt;SEP&gt;The Stockman Grass Farmer is a publication in which Joel Salatin was interviewed in 2006, discussing his economic goals and farming systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214f4e9d9f3d3fed74243c9bdf50bf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd285deff310269197e8718b28f6076b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740870</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Japan">
      <data key="d0">Japan</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Japan is the destination for about 10 percent of Curtis Bennett's high-protein organic soybeans, representing his overseas market.&lt;SEP&gt;The country associated with Masanobu Fukuoka and his 'do-nothing' organic farming method, as detailed in his book 'The Natural Way of Farming'.&lt;SEP&gt;The home country of Masanobu Fukuoka and the origin of his natural farming philosophy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a25021ab306ceaf5d58e192cc6fc0a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e64fb1fe152a62cd06403742bd6beac5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Management-intensive grazing (Mig)">
      <data key="d0">Management-intensive grazing (Mig)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A farming term that re-frames 'intensive grazing' to highlight the crucial role of management and thought processes in achieving farm profitability and success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Farming">
      <data key="d0">Natural Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A farming method that avoids artificial inputs like antibiotics and chemical pesticides. It can be aimed at either commodity or direct markets and may be sustainable.&lt;SEP&gt;A farming philosophy developed by Masanobu Fukuoka based on a 'do-nothing' approach that works in harmony with nature. It has five principles: no tillage, no fertilizer, no pesticides, no weeding, and no pruning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfed52d46f5bf9d5489c4d6b6dddd2cb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mahayana natural farming">
      <data key="d0">Mahayana natural farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of Fukuoka's three types of farming. It is described as a philosophical ideal of perfection, representing the farming of Nature itself, which is considered unattainable by humans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hinayana natural farming">
      <data key="d0">Hinayana natural farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of Fukuoka's three types of farming. It is the practical method used by Fukuoka, which involves working with nature's pure forces and allowing for necessary human intrusions like planting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scientific Farming">
      <data key="d0">Scientific Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of Fukuoka's three types of farming. It is characterized by the use of human knowledge and action to improve agriculture, but Fukuoka believes it is limited by short-term goals and is ultimately inferior to natural farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value-added products">
      <data key="d0">Value-added products</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A business strategy for farmers to increase profitability and reduce risk by transforming a basic product into one with additional value, such as turning corn into cornmeal or making jams and sausages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Government regulations">
      <data key="d0">Government regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant external factor and challenge for small farmers. The text stresses the importance for farmers to be involved in legislative meetings to protect their ability to compete and operate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Katahdin Hair sheep">
      <data key="d0">Katahdin Hair sheep</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A breed of sheep raised by the author. They are highlighted as being efficient at converting pasture to profit and are bred specifically for the author's farm conditions. They are classified as ruminants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sausage hogs">
      <data key="d0">Sausage hogs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hogs raised by the author for sausage. They are described as simple, one-stomach animals fed with farm-grown corn and are considered less efficient at converting pasture to profit compared to ruminants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ruminants">
      <data key="d0">Ruminants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A class of animals, including sheep and cattle, characterized by having four stomachs. This digestive system allows them to consume large amounts of roughage and thrive on forage alone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">American Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The conventional farming tradition in the United States. Masanobu Fukuoka critiques it for being steeped in tradition rather than critical thinking about the 'how' and 'why' of its practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S.">
      <data key="d0">U.S.</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The United States is identified as a country where goat populations and the demand for goat meat are significantly increasing, driven by a growing ethnic diversity.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is mentioned as the location of organic farmers who risk being squeezed out by the economic pressures of large-scale industrial organic operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d31a2f89a77b64e0ae04120df1fdb3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Species">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Species</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive species are unwanted plants like poison ivy, honeysuckle, and kudzu that goats are used to control and eradicate, representing a key use for the animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goat Meat">
      <data key="d0">Goat Meat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Goat meat is described as a healthy food product with less fat than chicken or beef. Its popularity is growing in the U.S. as American palates expand to cuisines where it is common, such as Caribbean and Southeast Asian.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interview with 'Living the Country Life Radio'">
      <data key="d0">Interview with 'Living the Country Life Radio'</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A media event where Tammy Dunakin, owner of Rent-A-Ruminant, was interviewed about the logistics and timeline for using goats to clear dense vegetation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Laws">
      <data key="d0">Community Laws</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local regulations and ordinances that govern the keeping of livestock in communities. The text advises engaging with local representatives to understand or change these laws.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ethnic Consumers">
      <data key="d0">Ethnic Consumers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group described as the 'backbone of the meat-goat industry in the U.S.' whose demand for goat meat is increasing as the population becomes more diverse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Organic Farming">
      <data key="d0">American Organic Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of farming in the U.S. which Masanobu Fukuoka dismisses as just another form of 'scientific farming', not true 'natural farming'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="One Straw Revolution">
      <data key="d0">One Straw Revolution</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A book written by Masanobu Fukuoka which details his philosophy and methods of natural farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Legislators">
      <data key="d0">Legislators</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lawmakers who are described as being four to five generations removed from farming. Their unfamiliarity with the agricultural industry poses a risk to farmers through uninformed legislation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-551c4a3f4f76cd87581e1acc8d4a5d71</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alternative Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Alternative Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farming methodology focused on identifying a market's needs first and then growing what is in demand. It emphasizes direct marketing, adding value to products, and achieving greater profits by selling at retail prices."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A farming approach contrasted with traditional agriculture, focusing on diverse or non-conventional crops and enterprises. The text implies it may have more resilience against certain market crises.&lt;SEP&gt;A form of agriculture that is described as being less tied to climate compared to traditional farming. The text provides guidance on how and when to start an alternative agriculture operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bfed52d46f5bf9d5489c4d6b6dddd2cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-766988b011c2a15c7312ce3e9bb55cf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value-Added Products">
      <data key="d0">Value-Added Products</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Products created by processing a raw commodity to increase its value and appeal to a different market. Examples include turning corn into cornmeal, livestock into freezer beef or sausage, and berries into jams or sauces. This is a key strategy for profitability in niche markets."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Retail consumer items created by processing raw farm commodities, such as turning corn into cornmeal or berries into jam. This strategy drastically reduces the volume of product needed to achieve high gross revenues and adds a layer of protection against market fluctuations."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df880ab4353881f2fe1a61ec533c5679&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vermont">
      <data key="d0">Vermont</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Vermont is a state located in the northeastern United States. It is home to various locations and organizations, including South Village, Burlington, The Intervale, Middlebury College, and the Northern Grain Growers Association, which collaborates with the University of Vermont. Vermont is part of the Northeast Organic Wheat consortium. The state's northern climate makes it suitable for certain types of agriculture. Vermont has a unique law that allowed South Village to organize as a low-profit limited liability corporation (LLLC). A roadside pumpkin display on U.S. Route 7 in Vermont exemplifies marketing strategies. Middlebury College, located in Vermont, is notable for its local food purchasing policies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f1544f96bf3906c3ffd91d3f551a278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d4150698732f393c235b8b3e1cb2d88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Route 7">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Route 7</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A highway in southwestern Vermont where a roadside pumpkin display attracts tourists, photographers, and buyers, serving as an example of effective marketing."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caribbean">
      <data key="d0">Caribbean</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Caribbean is a geographical region with several distinct characteristics. It is noted for its year-round growing season, which simplifies farming and is used as a point of contrast to Massachusetts. In its local cuisine, goat meat is a prominent feature, a factor that may contribute to its rising popularity in the U.S. Additionally, the Caribbean is home to coral reefs that an individual named Gerard Ramos aims to repopulate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ee782c1d8a631cff4fafc639994eb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8dfe2a17435f059c92535241799795b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27f0b8018ca6a374ee44c20b082d0b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Goats">
      <data key="d0">Community Goats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Community Goats is a concept discussed in the text that involves raising goats in urban landscapes for clearing brush and bramble.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coogan Brennan">
      <data key="d0">Coogan Brennan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Coogan Brennan is a contributor to a book who served as a researcher and writer. At the time of his work on the text, he was an undergraduate student from Columbia University who was taking a hiatus from his studies. Brennan is credited as the co-author of the "Community and Home Beekeeping" section and also conducted research for the section about Seeds of Solidarity. He is also mentioned in the book's index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Beekeeping">
      <data key="d0">Urban Beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of beekeeping practiced by a new demographic of professionals in cities, often as a hobby. Hives are set up in available spaces like rooftops and vacant lots, and the practice is supported by a growing number of blogs and websites.&lt;SEP&gt;Urban Beekeeping is a grassroots movement involving the practice of keeping bees in urban environments for honey production and pollination services. The text details its rise in several North American cities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27bb80a716045bb643a01fd3aae1d84b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mayor Richard Daley">
      <data key="d0">Mayor Richard Daley</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mayor of Chicago who initiated the city hall rooftop beehive project in 2003 after being inspired by a trip to Germany.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germany">
      <data key="d0">Germany</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A country where rooftop gardening and apiculture are common, which served as the inspiration for Chicago's rooftop beehive project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dwight James">
      <data key="d0">Dwight James</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A banker-turned-farmer from Alabama who identified that his state was importing pumpkins. He capitalized on this market gap, became a successful pumpkin grower, and is now the president of a pumpkin association. He is presented as an example of an 'agripreneur.'"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tennessee">
      <data key="d0">Tennessee</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Tennessee is a state in the southeastern United States and the Appalachian region. It is one of the states where Appalachian Sustainable Development (ASD) operates to support the local food system. Tennessee is also considered part of the Southern Appalachian region and falls within the 100-mile "local" definition established by ASAP. Notably, Tennessee was once an external source of pumpkins for the Alabama market before local farmers in Alabama began cultivating them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497b3487d00eabefe426a10fa32f8010</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Mexico">
      <data key="d0">New Mexico</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">New Mexico is a state in the Southwestern United States. It is part of the region where the Navajo Nation is located, and the state where an elderly Navajo woman grew up farming. It was also the location of Fort Sumner, where the Diné people were forcibly interned in the 1800s. New Mexico is the state where both Holistic Management International and the Savory Institute are based. It is also a geographical location used as an example to illustrate that the principles of good farming are universal and work anywhere in the world. New Mexico is mentioned as an external source of pumpkins for the Alabama market before local farmers started growing them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16e699b4bb77d0bdc27d883c3cafa88c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pumpkin Association">
      <data key="d0">Pumpkin Association</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A sixty-member organization in Alabama, with Dwight James as its president. The association was formed to promote and market many varieties of pumpkins grown locally, demonstrating a successful application of alternative agriculture principles."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740807</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Agriculture Statistics Office">
      <data key="d0">Local Agriculture Statistics Office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An office that provides data on local populations and agricultural activities. It is cited as a resource for farmers conducting market research as part of the 'Eight Steps to Identifying the Market.'"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740807</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Extension Office">
      <data key="d0">Local Extension Office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A local resource for farmers, suggested as a point of contact for finding the Local Agriculture Statistics Office and for gathering information about other local farmers and their products."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"An advisory body that farmers are encouraged to consult for information. They can provide details on available local events and offer guidance on the specific requirements and regulations for establishing a U-pick farm."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A local resource that provides custom rate sheets for various farming operations, enabling farmers to compare costs and make economic decisions about equipment ownership versus hiring services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3eba3a070130b55e2912440d8aaca3d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740807</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stephanie Averill">
      <data key="d0">Stephanie Averill</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A local beekeeper who, along with Michael Thompson, installed the beehives on Chicago's city hall rooftop. She notes the benefit of Chicago's longer growing season for bees.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual mentioned in the index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52b5200e77354191139029d689fb62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740807</data>
    </node>
