    <node id="Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA)">
      <data key="d0">Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) is a food production, distribution, and financial business model that directly connects local farmers with consumers. In this subscription-based system, customers buy "shares" in a farm's upcoming harvest by paying an upfront fee before the planting season begins. This pre-payment provides farmers with crucial capital and a reliable, steady income stream, which helps ensure farm stability and build strong customer relationships.

In return for their investment, shareholders receive a portion of the farm's output, typically in the form of weekly food baskets. A key principle of the CSA model is that members share in the farm's risks alongside the farmer. In some cases, members may also provide labor on the farm, which can help reduce the farmer's individual labor requirements. The CSA model is considered a well-established counterpart to the Community-Supported Fishery (CSF) and is explicitly stated as the basis for the CSF program, playing a primary role within the broader local and organic foods movement. Examples of its implementation include Kipp Nash’s 'Community Roots', the Warren Wilson College garden, and the nonprofit Lynchburg Grows, which successfully expanded its program and partners with other producers to ensure a reliable supply for its members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ae240ee8f1e0681df991703ff2ca94b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0ebf31ca8cf0e8a5dbe6d09c3efa272&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dbce12e45fe13ef4f6973f92e57e779</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
