    <node id="Ratites">
      <data key="d0">Ratites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of large flightless birds, including ostrich, emu, and rhea. They are the central subject of the 'True Story' section, illustrating how a collapsed market can be turned into a profitable niche with add-on value products."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"A category of large, flightless birds. They are presented as an example of an animal whose parts can be processed into a wide variety of value-added products, including meat, emu oil, carved eggs, leather, and feather crafts."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Ratites are an enterprise category including ostriches, emus, and rheas. Although a past speculation market collapsed, they have sales potential for low-fat meat, hides, decorative eggs, ostrich feathers, and emu oil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e9fc423425c82be2483c04c11e6577f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e2a281158355949bfa9c35a51c5622a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emus">
      <data key="d0">Emus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific type of ratite that Chuck DeCourley purchased at low prices, bred, and used to create various add-on value products like meat sticks, sausage, oil, and carved eggs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e2a281158355949bfa9c35a51c5622a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Elk">
      <data key="d0">Elk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Elk are a large species of deer that are considered both a potential livestock enterprise and a wildlife management concern. As farmed livestock, Elk have unique management needs, requiring specialized, heavy, 8-foot-tall fencing. They are valued as a good "add-on value" product because various parts, including meat, hides, and antlers (such as velvet), have commercial purposes. A specific example of a farming operation with a breeding herd of 25 cows and 2 bulls yielded a net profit of $3,566.00. This was from an income of $4,144.00 generated by selling breeding stock and velvet, with expenses totaling $429.00 for feed and $149.00 for other costs, requiring 9 hours of labor. Additionally, Elk are the subject of the "North American Elk" periodical.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e2a281158355949bfa9c35a51c5622a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740810</data>
    </node>
