    <node id="Community-Supported Foraging (CSF)">
      <data key="d0">Community-Supported Foraging (CSF)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A program model created by ForageSF where subscribers pre-pay for a monthly share of wild foods collected by independent foragers. It is based on the Community-Supported Agriculture model."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A food distribution model created by Iso Rabins, where members receive a monthly box of wild foraged foods, analogous to community-supported agriculture.&lt;SEP&gt;A model for sourcing wild, foraged foods, linked to 'ForageSF' and 'gleaning'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6db795582f52e35a0bb9aea86e66f7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dbce12e45fe13ef4f6973f92e57e779</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Food System">
      <data key="d0">Local Food System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, a Local Food System is a concept that refers to the entire, complex, and interconnected network of food production, distribution, and consumption within a geographically localized area. This system encompasses a wide range of participants, including local and often organic or sustainable farmers, producers, processors, distributors, consumers, retailers, and institutions like hospitals and schools.

The primary goal of building a Local Food System is to strengthen the regional economy and community, build relationships, promote health, and foster resilience. It is presented as a more sustainable, community-focused alternative to the industrial food system, and in some cases, serves to create a new agricultural economy as an alternative to previous industries like tobacco.

Organizations such as Appalachian Sustainable Development (ASD) and the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP) are central to establishing and strengthening these systems. Their work involves analyzing the network, educating the public, and actively connecting its various participants to identify and fill major gaps. Strategies to support the system include exploring sustainable business models, creating efficient sourcing through initiatives like pooled delivery partnerships (as seen in Flagstaff), and fostering connections between farmers and local markets. Foundational elements for creating such a system include community gardens and farms, with businesses like Diablo Burger serving as examples of the system in action. However, the development of these systems can face challenges, such as a lack of local processing facilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fee18f76430d121ab613984069f209e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5944a88ca415780f3d4838a47d068512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-087cef03c5ab256c59a3f536c4533b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efae0d6c5dabc751b9ceb56518857dff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d67ba0430fa075f3a4e6609250b9a01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497b3487d00eabefe426a10fa32f8010&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dbce12e45fe13ef4f6973f92e57e779</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
