    <node id="Community GroundWorks">
      <data key="d0">Community GroundWorks</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, Community GroundWorks is a nonprofit organization and community partnership located in Madison, Wisconsin. It is responsible for managing Troy Gardens, a mixed-use greenspace of approximately 26 to 31 acres, which it stewards under a long-term lease. The organization's mission is to foster a meaningful relationship between people and the land.

Led by Greg Rosenberg, Community GroundWorks evolved from the original development coalition for the project and is cited as a successful example of supporting community gardening. It facilitates various programs, including community gardens and educational initiatives. With a budget of around $350,000, the organization relies heavily on volunteers and has received significant funding, such as a large grant from the W.K. Kellogg Foundation. To help generate income, Community GroundWorks also created a social enterprise named Madison FarmWorks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc825bb5fbec77abff4a02be6fc5fcec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Troy Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Troy Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Troy Gardens is a 31-acre, multifaceted sustainable development project and "accidental ecovillage" located in Madison, Wisconsin, near Lake Mendota. The site was saved from development by a broad community coalition and created through extensive organizing, partnerships, and significant government support. It is managed by the nonprofit organization Community GroundWorks.

The project uniquely integrates a cohousing development with urban agriculture. This includes 30 housing units, two-thirds of which are designated as permanently affordable, that were built using green building techniques. The land also features extensive community gardens, a CSA farm (which is part of the larger Troy Community Farm), restored prairie, and conservation areas. The mission of Troy Gardens emphasizes accessibility to home ownership, green spaces, healthy food, and public transportation.

Having received multiple awards for its design and affordability, Troy Gardens is often presented as a model case study. It exemplifies the success of involving partners early in a collaborative process to manage funding and governance, ensuring shared investment and long-term success. However, it also serves as a cautionary tale in fundraising, as an early reliance on a large grant from the W.K. Kellogg Foundation led the organization to neglect the development of a broader, more sustainable funding base.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Coalition">
      <data key="d0">The Coalition</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A coalition of individuals and nonprofits (including a community land trust, a conservation land trust, a community garden coalition, a local university, and an antipoverty agency) formed in 1995 to save the Troy Gardens site from being sold for development."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Madison, Wisconsin">
      <data key="d0">Madison, Wisconsin</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the descriptions provided, Madison, Wisconsin, is the city and state that is home to several community organizations and projects, including Troy Gardens, Troy Community Farm, and Community GroundWorks. The city government of Madison actively supported the Troy Gardens project by providing a long-term, low-interest loan sourced from its community development block grants. This financial support enabled a coalition to purchase the land for the project.&lt;SEP&gt;Madison, Wisconsin is the location of Gempler's.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc825bb5fbec77abff4a02be6fc5fcec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57c24510274e9c9fcb046e6b604285fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marcia Caton Campbell">
      <data key="d0">Marcia Caton Campbell</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Marcia Caton Campbell is the Milwaukee director of the Center for Resilient Cities and a representative of Community GroundWorks. Formerly with the Department of Urban and Regional Planning at the University of Wisconsin, Campbell was a key figure in a coalition where she established the principle that a site must benefit its neighborhood. She is the co-author of the academic article "Community and Conservation Land Trusts as Unlikely Partners? The Case of Troy Gardens, Madison, Wisconsin," and has also positively commented on the success and flourishing state of the Community GroundWorks organization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1fa8277818b65f8d3d6665242021ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Christie Ralston">
      <data key="d0">Christie Ralston</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"The associate director and natural areas coordinator at Community GroundWorks. She provides details on the organization's budget and the significant role of volunteers."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A representative of Community GroundWorks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Wisconsin in Madison">
      <data key="d0">University of Wisconsin in Madison</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The local university that was a key partner in the coalition formed to save and repurpose the Troy Gardens site."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"The university that houses the Great Lakes WATER Institute (GLWI), where Allen collaborates with scientists."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The university where the Great Lakes WATER Institute (GLWI) is located. It is a center for research relevant to Growing Power's aquaculture efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regine Kennedy">
      <data key="d0">Regine Kennedy</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Regine Kennedy is an interpretive planner and designer at the 106 Group in St. Paul, Minnesota. An alumna of the University of Virginia, where she was a graduate student, Kennedy also served in the Peace Corps. She is credited as a researcher, writer, and co-author of a text, contributing to several specific sections. Her co-authored works include the sections titled 'Growers, Millers, Bakers: Rebuilding a Local Grain Economy,' 'Local and Heritage Grains,' and 'Growing Power,' as well as the sections describing the White Earth Land Recovery Project and Community GroundWorks and Troy Gardens. Additionally, Regine Kennedy is associated with other organizations and locations, including Jones Valley Urban Farm and Milwaukee.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6824e03de6d996c4161ce46d959096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d4150698732f393c235b8b3e1cb2d88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
