    <node id="Fish">
      <data key="d0">Fish</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of animal whose meat is mentioned as generally being permissible to butcher at home for direct sale, alongside chickens and rabbits."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Fish are mentioned as another type of animal that will require some of the processing equipment listed for poultry when processed at home."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A major category of aquatic animal produced through aquaculture for protein needs and commercial sale. The text states that at least 90 species are farmed worldwide.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9d28e4a28ca972c9e289441c78c0be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rabbits">
      <data key="d0">Rabbits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of animal whose meat is mentioned as generally being permissible to butcher at home for direct sale. The text also notes that specific processing equipment is available for rabbits."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Rabbits are a type of small animal that, like poultry, can be butchered at home. They require some, but not all, of the same processing equipment as poultry."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Rabbits are an agricultural enterprise raised for meat, pelts, fiber (angora), and as pets. The text suggests value-added products like jerky and sausage and sales to restaurants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e9fc423425c82be2483c04c11e6577f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pork, Beef, Lamb, or Cabrito (Goat)">
      <data key="d0">Pork, Beef, Lamb, or Cabrito (Goat)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A group of meat animals that the text collectively identifies as requiring processing in a state-inspected or USDA plant in order to be sold as meat. Producers can circumvent this by selling the animals live."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leather Products">
      <data key="d0">Leather Products</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of value-added goods made from animal hides, mentioned under 'Other ideas' and also as a product derived from Ratites."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oils">
      <data key="d0">Oils</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of value-added products listed under 'Other ideas.' The text also specifies emu oil as a product derived from Ratites."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Center for Resilient Cities">
      <data key="d0">Center for Resilient Cities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Wisconsin-based organization where Marcia Caton Campbell currently serves as the Milwaukee director."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greg Rosenberg">
      <data key="d0">Greg Rosenberg</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Greg Rosenberg is the academy director of the National Community Land Trust Network and was formerly the executive director of the Madison Area Community Land Trust. He has also served as the executive director and a representative for Community GroundWorks. Greg Rosenberg was a key figure in the design, development, and construction of the 30 cohousing units at Troy Gardens, providing insights on the project's unique features, including its affordability, green building practices, and site layout. Additionally, he is the author of an excerpt about Troy Gardens published in 'The Community Land Trust Reader.'</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lake Mendota">
      <data key="d0">Lake Mendota</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A lake mentioned for its proximity to the Troy Gardens site in Madison, Wisconsin."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The State Of Wisconsin">
      <data key="d0">The State Of Wisconsin</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The government entity that owned the 31-acre site of Troy Gardens. It announced its decision to sell the land in 1995 but was persuaded by the community coalition to instead provide a long-term lease and eventually sell the property to them."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Affordable Housing">
      <data key="d0">Affordable Housing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key, though initially disputed, component of the Troy Gardens plan. It was a required element for some coalition members, resulting in 5 acres being allocated for 30 cohousing units, two-thirds of which are permanently affordable."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A central theme and a key feature of the Troy Gardens development, where 20 of the 30 housing units are designated as permanently affordable.&lt;SEP&gt;A key issue in the South End/Lower Roxbury community. The city's plan to build an affordable housing project on a garden site sparked conflict, leading to a coalition that advocated for the preservation of both affordable housing and community gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b1f6adc63dbe7f3160ab5bd7b25233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Department of Urban and Regional Planning at the University of Wisconsin">
      <data key="d0">Department of Urban and Regional Planning at the University of Wisconsin</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The academic department where Marcia Caton Campbell was employed at the time of her involvement with the coalition that planned and created Troy Gardens."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Native Americans">
      <data key="d0">Native Americans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Native Americans, also referred to as First Nation people, are the indigenous inhabitants of lands such as the site of Troy Gardens, which they considered sacred ground. As a cultural group, they are focused on preserving their cultural identities, food heritage, and agricultural knowledge. This is partly in response to being depicted on misleading marketing for products like paddy rice and suffering from the world's highest rates of diet-induced health epidemics, such as type 2 diabetes and obesity. To combat these health issues and enhance community resilience, Native Americans are working to restore health through the use of traditional foods, cultural practices, and the preservation of biodiversity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summer Festival">
      <data key="d0">Summer Festival</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"An event held at Troy Gardens as an example of a 'fun day' to bring the community together. It features activities like music, hayrides, and food."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Processing Equipment">
      <data key="d0">Processing Equipment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Processing Equipment is a category of tools and machinery used by farmers to process their own farm goods, such as large stock, poultry, or crops, in order to create value-added products. This equipment can range from fruit and wine presses, grinding mills, and food choppers to poultry shears, knives, sausage stuffers, and various wrappers, bags, and egg cartons. For the specific task of butchering poultry, the equipment includes items like killing cones, scalders, pluckers, and cooling tanks. Before making a purchase, it is advised that farmers evaluate the costs and potential break-even points of investing in such equipment. It is also important to note that if these processed goods are intended for public sale, the use of the equipment is subject to regulations, often requiring the work to be done in USDA-inspected facilities or approved kitchens. This equipment can be acquired from various purchasing locations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5deb38ed9dc0a60bcac768fbb7955c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Department of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">State Department of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The State Department of Agriculture is a state-level government agency that provides comprehensive support and resources for farmers and farms. This government body is a recommended point of contact for a variety of services, including financial aid, grants, and other support programs. Additionally, the State Department of Agriculture offers legal information, details on local offices, lists of farmers' markets within the region, and information on advertising opportunities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c07830327394a77dc9e8ac1a53acd63</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Family Farms">
      <data key="d0">Small Family Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A model of agriculture presented as a potential, though perhaps impractical, solution to the problems of industrial food. They focus on selling to local communities but have been largely forced out by the 'National Cheap Food Policy'."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The primary focus of the text, Small Family Farms are agricultural operations for which direct marketing is presented as a 'profit equalizer' to achieve financial sustainability and increase net profits over selling raw products.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Narrator">
      <data key="d0">The Narrator</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"An agricultural entrepreneur who recounts their experience starting an aquaculture business raising and selling catfish and trout. Their story serves as a case study on market research, understanding customer needs, and adapting business strategy based on sales data from restaurants and grocery stores."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An unnamed farmer who provides a personal account of successfully selling hogs and sausage directly to customers at a premium price. This individual emphasizes product quality (pasture-raised, no antibiotics) and clear customer communication as keys to success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Friends and Neighbors">
      <data key="d0">Friends and Neighbors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific group of potential customers recommended as the best starting point for farmers new to direct marketing. The text highlights both the ease of contact and the potential challenges, such as expectations for lower prices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roadside Stands">
      <data key="d0">Roadside Stands</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Roadside Stands are a direct-to-consumer sales channel and marketing method where farmers sell farm products, such as produce, directly to passersby. These stands are typically located on or near the farm and can range from simple setups, like the bed of a pickup truck, to more permanent shop-like structures. The success of a Roadside Stand is highly dependent on its location on a well-traveled road with adequate parking to ensure sufficient customer traffic. As a sales venue, it relies heavily on effective advertising through roadside signs, which should be simple, large, and well-placed to attract passing motorists. While this model has the benefit of low expenses, it is also necessary for the enterprise to have liability insurance to cover potential customer injuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-766988b011c2a15c7312ce3e9bb55cf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Processors">
      <data key="d0">Food Processors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of business that consistently receives a high return on investment (20-25%) by processing raw agricultural products. They are contrasted with farmers who receive low returns (5%) for selling unprocessed goods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Madison's Community Development Block Grants">
      <data key="d0">Madison's Community Development Block Grants</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A city-level funding program in Madison that provided the crucial long-term, low-interest loan which enabled the coalition to purchase the Troy Gardens property from the state."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Federal Food Policies">
      <data key="d0">Federal Food Policies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A high-level concept mentioned in the introductory paragraph as part of the broader context influencing local food systems and community agriculture efforts."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cohousing Development">
      <data key="d0">Cohousing Development</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The specific model of housing chosen for the residential component of Troy Gardens. The development consists of 30 units, which were designed and built before recruiting homeowners."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-463bc71b9c03d72246d0c3904090b54a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Madison FarmWorks">
      <data key="d0">Madison FarmWorks</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An innovative business created by Community GroundWorks. For a fee, it designs and installs vegetable gardens for residents and institutions throughout Madison, with the goal of generating income to support the nonprofit's work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nathan Larson">
      <data key="d0">Nathan Larson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A representative of Community GroundWorks.&lt;SEP&gt;The education director for Community GroundWorks. He oversees youth programs like the Kids' Garden, speaks to their transformative impact on children, and highlights funding as the primary challenge for these programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Madison Area Community Land Trust">
      <data key="d0">Madison Area Community Land Trust</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The former organization of Greg Rosenberg, where he served as executive director. It was involved in the initial stages of the Troy Gardens project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Community Land Trust Network">
      <data key="d0">National Community Land Trust Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The organization where Greg Rosenberg currently works as the academy director.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oakhill Correctional Institution">
      <data key="d0">Oakhill Correctional Institution</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A correctional facility that supports the Troy Gardens' Kids' Garden by donating seedling plants through its horticulture vocational program.&lt;SEP&gt;Oakhill Correctional Institution is a facility with a horticulture vocational program that partners with Troy Gardens by donating seedling plants for the Kids' Garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Raw Products">
      <data key="d0">Raw Products</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unprocessed agricultural goods, such as corn, which farmers sell for a very low return on their investment (around 5%). The text encourages farmers to process these goods to increase profitability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Add-on Value">
      <data key="d0">Add-on Value</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core business concept referring to the increased income and net profits generated by processing raw farm products into finished goods (e.g., turning hogs into sausage). It is presented as a key to financial success for small farms.&lt;SEP&gt;A key marketing concept, listed as part of a direct marketing strategy, which involves enhancing the primary product or service to increase sales and profitability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-766988b011c2a15c7312ce3e9bb55cf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Retail Outlets">
      <data key="d0">Retail Outlets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sales channel that is not part of direct marketing. It is mentioned as an alternative way to sell farm products, likely involving intermediaries, in contrast to the direct-to-consumer methods the text focuses on.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Special Events">
      <data key="d0">Special Events</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Activities such as bake sales, festivals, or concerts that can be held at a farmers' market. They are suggested as an additional way to draw revenue and attract customers to the market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sam And Elizabeth Smith">
      <data key="d0">Sam And Elizabeth Smith</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Organic farmers who run Caretaker Farm in Williamstown, Massachusetts. They transitioned their business into a CSA model to foster a closer connection with the local community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Williamstown, Massachusetts">
      <data key="d0">Williamstown, Massachusetts</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The town where Sam and Elizabeth Smith's Caretaker Farm is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AARP">
      <data key="d0">AARP</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization that, along with the National Association of Homebuilders, gave Troy Gardens the '2007 Livable Communities Award'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Depot Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Home Depot Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A foundation that recognized Troy Gardens with its 2008 'Award for Affordable Housing Built Responsibly'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Youth Programs">
      <data key="d0">Youth Programs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of programs at Troy Gardens focused on engaging young people. These programs are considered to have a powerful transformative effect but face significant funding challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Educational programs run by Community GroundWorks, such as the Kids' Garden, aimed at teaching children about food, nature, and cooking. These programs are a core part of the organization's mission but face funding challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Youth Programs are a core component of Lynchburg Grows' outreach. They include 'Farmer for a Day' field trips, the 'Roots and Shoots' intergenerational program, the 'Grasshopper' mobile learning bus, the 'Elementary Education Garden' program, and 'Farm Camp', all designed to teach children about food, farming, and healthy eating.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cee86d7056c73fbe7bdaa432b2abe64</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Green Building">
      <data key="d0">Green Building</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A design principle applied to the Troy Gardens housing development. Techniques were used as much as possible within budgetary limits, with upgrades like solar panels available to homeowners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Association of Homebuilders">
      <data key="d0">National Association of Homebuilders</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A professional association that, in partnership with AARP, recognized Troy Gardens with the '2007 Livable Communities Award'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ralston">
      <data key="d0">Ralston</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual associated with Community GroundWorks who noted the communication challenges involved in managing the diverse, multilingual group of gardeners at Troy Gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Capitol">
      <data key="d0">State Capitol</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A prominent landmark in Madison where a Madison FarmWorks vegetable garden has been installed, demonstrating the program's visibility and integration into the city.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2007 Livable Communities Award">
      <data key="d0">2007 Livable Communities Award</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An award presented to Troy Gardens by AARP and the National Association of Homebuilders, recognizing its successful community design and livability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caretaker Farm">
      <data key="d0">Caretaker Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organic farm in Williamstown, MA, run by the Smiths that operates as a thriving CSA. It serves as a community hub, hosting apprentices, collaborating with a local baker and a neighboring farm, and organizing seasonal festivals for its members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seasonal Festivals">
      <data key="d0">Seasonal Festivals</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Community events hosted by Caretaker Farm for its CSA members, such as potato and pumpkin harvests, which include activities like square dances and potluck dinners to strengthen community ties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steering Committee">
      <data key="d0">Steering Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A group that helps manage the membership administration for the Caretaker Farm CSA, demonstrating a structured approach to its operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neighboring Farm">
      <data key="d0">Neighboring Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A farm that collaborates with Caretaker Farm by supplying corn to its CSA members, illustrating a local partnership.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catalog Sales">
      <data key="d0">Catalog Sales</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A direct marketing method for value-added products, requiring careful market research, experience in farming and marketing, and the ability to manage supply and demand. It is described as a high-volume business that is difficult to start.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Customers">
      <data key="d0">Customers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Customers are the end-users and buyers of agricultural products and the target audience for farm products. Farmers should ask questions to understand customer preferences regarding product, packaging, and availability. It is important to understand and manage their expectations, particularly concerning yields of processed meat. Farmers need to consider methods for attracting and interacting with customers, using techniques such as advertising. Customers' expectations regarding price and quality should also be considered. Furthermore, customer retention and loss have a significant financial impact on the farm. In some cases, customers can even be a source of labor, such as in U-pick operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The buyers who purchase produce from the garden at the farmers' markets. The relationship between the student-farmers and customers is considered critical for the students' future success.&lt;SEP&gt;The patrons of restaurants implementing whole-animal cuisine. Their support and understanding are crucial, especially when specific cuts like steak are unavailable. The text notes they are supportive when the reasons for menu limitations are explained.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a603ecfe84d863d026d6d7309cc4add3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shows And Fairs">
      <data key="d0">Shows And Fairs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A marketing strategy involving setting up booths at trade shows and community events to promote a farm and its products, tapping into local markets to find new customers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Apprentices">
      <data key="d0">Apprentices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of individuals who live and work on Caretaker Farm from spring to harvest. They come from all over the country to learn about organic farming and the CSA model, and they assist with various farm tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Award for Affordable Housing Built Responsibly">
      <data key="d0">Award for Affordable Housing Built Responsibly</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An award presented to Troy Gardens in 2008 by the Home Depot Foundation, highlighting its success in creating responsible and affordable housing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cohousing">
      <data key="d0">Cohousing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A model of intentional community development that is the foundational structure for the Troy Gardens housing project, emphasizing shared spaces and community interaction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kids' Garden">
      <data key="d0">Kids' Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific educational program within the Troy community garden, occupying about 10 plots, where children learn to plant, grow, and cook their own food under the guidance of Community GroundWorks.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific youth program within Troy Gardens where children garden. It receives support in the form of donated seedling plants from the Oakhill Correctional Institution's horticulture program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6524af1074c6309e3cd8d13cbf621c2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Larson">
      <data key="d0">Larson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Larson is a person associated with the youth programs at Troy Gardens who highlights the challenges of funding and the positive impact of the programs on young people.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Wisconsin School of Medicine and Public Health">
      <data key="d0">University of Wisconsin School of Medicine and Public Health</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The University of Wisconsin School of Medicine and Public Health is an academic institution whose pediatric clinic obesity prevention specialists partnered with Troy Gardens to create the 'Garden Fit' pilot program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Garden Fit">
      <data key="d0">Garden Fit</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">'Garden Fit' is a pilot program created in partnership between Troy Gardens and a pediatric clinic. It aims to improve children's physical fitness during the summer through gardening and other outdoor activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Claire Strader">
      <data key="d0">Claire Strader</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A representative of Community GroundWorks.&lt;SEP&gt;Claire Strader is the manager of Troy Community Farm and was the first person hired at Troy Gardens. She manages the farm's operations, teaches organic gardening classes, and is leading its expansion into an urban-yard farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Troy Community Farm">
      <data key="d0">Troy Community Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A community-based farm project.&lt;SEP&gt;Troy Community Farm is a 5-acre, certified organic farm within Troy Gardens. It operates as a community-supported agriculture (CSA) project, sells produce to local venues, and serves as a key component of Community GroundWorks' mission.&lt;SEP&gt;Troy Community Farm is an urban farm in Madison, Wisconsin. It is highlighted as a model for leveraging both the advantages (large labor pool of students and volunteers) and disadvantages of an urban location, and for its focus on education through various programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Baker">
      <data key="d0">Local Baker</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local business that has partnered with Caretaker Farm. This baker provides fresh bread, rolls, and desserts for sale at the farm and also conducts baking classes, expanding the farm's offerings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Children's Garden">
      <data key="d0">Children's Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A designated section within the Seeds of Harmony garden specifically created for children to learn about and participate in gardening.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific feature on Caretaker Farm, offered as a community amenity. It is an area for children, complemented by classes on craft projects, enhancing the farm's family-friendly appeal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a815073ae837fa29833af41e55990ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aunt Sally">
      <data key="d0">Aunt Sally</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hypothetical person whose product, 'Aunt Sally's Special Mustard', is used as an example to illustrate how marketing at shows and fairs can tap into existing brand recognition among customers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca2b88e7e8ecb62ee9eae1efc377690c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shows and Fairs">
      <data key="d0">Shows and Fairs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of promotional events where farmers can set up booths to market their farm and sell products. The text details a range of events from small community gatherings and craft shows to major state fairs and expensive gourmet food festivals, highlighting associated costs and profit considerations."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3eba3a070130b55e2912440d8aaca3d2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chambers of Commerce">
      <data key="d0">Chambers of Commerce</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"Business network organizations located in nearby communities. The text recommends that farmers contact them to find information on local events like shows and fairs where they can promote their products."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3eba3a070130b55e2912440d8aaca3d2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U-Pick Farms">
      <data key="d0">U-Pick Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farm business model where customers harvest their own produce. This approach reduces farmer labor for harvesting and transport but requires significant customer interaction skills and investment in infrastructure like parking, bathrooms, and insurance."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3eba3a070130b55e2912440d8aaca3d2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Circles">
      <data key="d0">Food Circles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A cooperative marketing model described as a hybrid between a CSA subscription and a cooperative. In this system, a limited group of farmers pools their produce to supply a corresponding group of nonfarm families."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3eba3a070130b55e2912440d8aaca3d2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persimmon Hill Farm">
      <data key="d0">Persimmon Hill Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific U-pick farm located in Lampe, Missouri, and operated by Earnie Bohner. It is cited as a successful example that attracts many visitors, including tour buses, by offering special products like muffins, smoothies, and sauces, turning it into a tourist magnet."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3eba3a070130b55e2912440d8aaca3d2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Madison Area Community Land Trust (MACLT)">
      <data key="d0">Madison Area Community Land Trust (MACLT)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Madison Area Community Land Trust (MACLT) is a non-profit that focuses on acquiring land for community benefit, particularly affordable housing. It purchased the Troy Gardens site and leases the greenspace to Community GroundWorks.&lt;SEP&gt;The Madison Area Community Land Trust (MACLT) is an organization focused on land trusts in the Madison area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Center for Resilient Cities">
      <data key="d0">The Center for Resilient Cities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Center for Resilient Cities, formerly the Urban Open Space Foundation, is a land trust that holds a conservation easement on the 26 acres of greenspace at Troy Gardens to ensure its permanent protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="City of Madison's Community Development Block Grant program">
      <data key="d0">City of Madison's Community Development Block Grant program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a municipal program in Madison that provided a long-term, low-interest loan to the Madison Area Community Land Trust (MACLT) to finance the purchase of the Troy Gardens site from the state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Troy Gardens Advisory Council">
      <data key="d0">Troy Gardens Advisory Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Troy Gardens Advisory Council was a body within the Troy Gardens coalition that created a set of 'Guiding Principles' in 1999 to maintain focus on the project's original goals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynchburg Grows">
      <data key="d0">Lynchburg Grows</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, Lynchburg Grows is a non-profit urban farm and community organization located at 1339 Englewood Street in Lynchburg, Virginia. Its core mission is to provide healthy food, educational outreach, job training, life skills, and community integration for people with special needs, disabilities, low-income individuals, and at-risk youth.

Founded by four partners, including co-founders Scott Lowman and Michael Van Ness, the organization transformed a 6.8-acre abandoned industrial site and former brownfield (the Schenkel property) into a productive farm. Before breaking ground, its founders took a patient, organic approach, spending four years engaging with the neighborhood to ensure strong community buy-in. A critical step in this transformation involved excavating and replacing contaminated soil to make the land suitable for agriculture.

The farm's diverse operations include growing organic produce, raising fish and chickens, keeping bees, and running a large-scale composting operation. Lynchburg Grows operates out of nine restored historic greenhouses, which are used not only for growing food and roses but also for alternative fuel research. The organization runs a Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) program, sells surplus produce at farmers' markets, donates food to local pantries, and establishes community gardens on vacant lots. Its educational and vocational programs include "Farmer for a Day" and the "Post-D" program. In May 2009, Lynchburg Grows hosted talks and a workshop by urban farming pioneer Will Allen.

The organization's success, marked by a budget of over $260,000 and the accumulation of 25,000 volunteer hours in its first five years, is heavily reliant on community support and strategic partnerships. All volunteers are required to sign liability forms, and board members must have at least six months of volunteer experience. A key partnership with Appalachian Sustainable Development (ASD) allows Lynchburg Grows to source high-quality second-grade produce for its CSA. It also partners with local entities like the Community Court system and the parks and recreation department. Key individuals associated with the organization include Michael Van Ness, Scott Lowman, Meade Anderson, Dereck Cunningham, and John Matheson. Lynchburg Grows is often cited as a model of a successful urban farm with a readily available local market, drawing customers from across the city, and it maintains a blog to share its story.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e0df78d09fb33ee041307abbce7d33e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d076831b7a91bcd605c1e387112d81e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0ebf31ca8cf0e8a5dbe6d09c3efa272&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475015ec5f08648d774910da2fb23967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f4b1f56482ca88dc86d76611131fea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cee86d7056c73fbe7bdaa432b2abe64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a815073ae837fa29833af41e55990ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497b3487d00eabefe426a10fa32f8010</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virginia">
      <data key="d0">Virginia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A US state that is the location of the Virginia Biological Farmers Association, which focuses on organic farming.&lt;SEP&gt;Located in the Mid-Atlantic and Southeastern United States, Virginia is a state with regions in rural and Southern Appalachia and is recognized by ASAP within its 100-mile "local" definition. The state has a strong farming community and is a hub for numerous organizations and initiatives focused on sustainable agriculture and local food systems.

Virginia is home to several key non-profits and farms. The Appalachian Sustainable Development (ASD) is a non-profit organization based in southwestern Virginia that operates throughout the state's Appalachian region to support the local food system and distribute produce. The city of Lynchburg is the site of Lynchburg Grows, a successful urban community farm. Nearby, in the Blue Ridge mountains, is the headquarters for the Society of St. Andrew (SoSA), which was founded in the small Virginia community of Big Island. The state is also the location for the renowned Polyface Farm, owned by grazier Joel Salatin, who uses pigs to create pasture from forest lands and has supplied to national chains like Chipotle. Salatin envisions a future food system for Virginia composed of five hundred "food clusters." Additionally, the Central Virginia Grain Project is working to establish a local grain economy, and the Southern Exposure Seed Exchange maintains its seed bank facility in the state. Historically, Virginia was home to the former artisanal producer Buffalo Ridge and Schenkel's business, which once accounted for a quarter of the state's flower industry.

The state is a crucial source for local supply chains. Oren Molovinsky established a supply chain for D.C. restaurants by sourcing whole animals directly from Virginia farmers after visiting numerous farms and processing plants. Virginia is the source of local meat for cooperatives, supporting farmers like Catherine Tatman and local slaughterhouses. It is also home to the Grayson Natural cooperative, which supplies local meat and received assistance from the Virginia Department of Agriculture.

Several influential figures and institutions are based in Virginia. Tanya Denckla Cobb, a Virginia resident, was instrumental in founding organizations like the Virginia Food System Council. The state is also the location of Virginia Tech.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e930a3eb466e9bb1dfa38b0df50bfd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d4150698732f393c235b8b3e1cb2d88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214f4e9d9f3d3fed74243c9bdf50bf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07373ff8a8e2c42149614780aa736a26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3699752bc52dee1358ad76a26a9c6fb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d6381862167ee0d108723409eae36d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e970626c7a63b367949cb5600957bcab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b16258e5de8192443e482a18e2beeb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d53cc613c52fd5080e556f9abb248a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f4b1f56482ca88dc86d76611131fea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aaa683758c365cd82a3b03895da095e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497b3487d00eabefe426a10fa32f8010</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
