    <node id="Janus Youth">
      <data key="d0">Janus Youth</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, here is a comprehensive summary:

Janus Youth is a large, regional non-profit social services organization based in Portland, Oregon, that serves communities in both Oregon and Washington. Under the direction of Dennis Morrow, the organization is primarily dedicated to serving at-risk populations, including runaway and homeless youths, teen parents, and immigrants.

Janus Youth has expanded its focus to include community development, running notable programs such as Village Gardens, Food Works, and St. Johns Woods. These initiatives include stewarding neighborhood gardens, operating a certified organic farm, and establishing new gardens like Seeds of Harmony in New Columbia through a leadership program. The success of these programs has led to the creation of a community-run store selling produce, job creation for local residents, and an expansion into poultry production.

The organization's core philosophy, championed by employees like Tera Wick, is rooted in humility, collaboration, and empowerment. It employs a "strength-based" approach, focusing on community assets rather than deficits, and aims to support a community's vision rather than impose outside solutions. Janus Youth actively forms partnerships with local governments, universities, public health institutions, and other community groups to achieve its goals. Cited as an example of effective non-profit management, it is known for using a modular system for its programs and for hiring from within the communities it serves, thereby creating safe and empowered environments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45291ea55455adae718c5031c0e8dfff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d7d0c8754f5ccddfb78068c25f0fa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edff28cc5d3b6f311f02cd2481c28fb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a815073ae837fa29833af41e55990ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Circle">
      <data key="d0">Food Circle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A food distribution model described as a hybrid of a CSA subscription farm and a cooperative. It involves a limited group of farmers and non-farm families pooling produce. Customers pay a fee to join and buy produce at a distribution site, making it easier to attract customers than a CSA due to lower upfront costs. It requires significant organizational time from farmers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Csa Subscription Farm">
      <data key="d0">Csa Subscription Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of agricultural business model, also known as Community Supported Agriculture (CSA), where customers pay an upfront fee for a share of a farm's harvest. It is mentioned as a point of comparison to Food Circles, which require less upfront money from customers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grocery And Health-food Stores">
      <data key="d0">Grocery And Health-food Stores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Wholesale outlets for farm products. They offer lower prices to the farmer (wholesale) and have specific requirements for packaging and handling, as illustrated by the narrator's experience selling fish. Selling to them should ideally be a small portion of a farmer's total sales."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chefs">
      <data key="d0">Chefs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chefs are key decision-makers within restaurants who purchase fresh produce. While they can be particular and sometimes temperamental, they are often open to trying new products. Building direct relationships with chefs is crucial for farmers selling to restaurants. Chefs wield significant influence in the food industry, and their egos and desire for unique, high-quality products can be leveraged to promote local sourcing and the whole-animal movement, as they have the power to dictate what purveyors supply. Molovinsky aims to help more chefs learn how to cook whole animal, especially as the cooperative grows, and mentors chefs, encouraging them to adopt whole-animal buying. Many contemporary chefs lack traditional slow-cooking skills, which hinders the adoption of this model, but learning these techniques can unleash their creativity. Chefs, including those in university dining halls, are connected with local growers through collaboratives like Farm Fresh Rhode Island to source local ingredients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ba8e532ec9e7db3fd3049537a06db80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Market Pools">
      <data key="d0">Market Pools</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A collaborative, often retail-focused group where local farmers agree to promote each other's businesses and refer sales to one another."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Market pools are a form of retail marketing where a group of local farmers agree to refer sales to each other and promote each other's businesses, offering customers more choice."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large City">
      <data key="d0">Large City</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A significant urban area located about 30 minutes from the narrator's farm. The narrator targeted grocery stores in this city as a market for their catfish."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Manager">
      <data key="d0">Manager</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative of a grocery store who makes purchasing decisions. The narrator's conversation with a store manager was pivotal in understanding the specific packaging requirements for their fish."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aquaculture Business">
      <data key="d0">Aquaculture Business</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The specific type of alternative agriculture venture the narrator started, focused on raising and marketing fish like catfish and trout."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tera Wick">
      <data key="d0">Tera Wick</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Tera Wick is an innovator and supervising staff person at Janus Youth Programs who was instrumental in the growth of its gardening programs. Considered the founder of these projects, she is described as both the "mother" and "midwife" of the Village Gardens program. This initiative began after Wick organized a community-wide brainstorming session in the St. Johns Woods community to respond to a USDA grant opportunity. Another of her key achievements was securing the land for the Food Works farm on Sauvie Island.

Wick's work is guided by a core belief that real change must come from the community itself. She champions a strength-based approach, believing that progress comes from identifying a community's strengths rather than focusing on "what's wrong." In line with this philosophy, she is lauded for her humble method of asking communities what they want and was planning to create a formal community advisory council to ensure accountability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45291ea55455adae718c5031c0e8dfff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a815073ae837fa29833af41e55990ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dennis Morrow">
      <data key="d0">Dennis Morrow</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dennis Morrow is the executive director of Janus Youth Programs, Inc., an organization he has led for over 30 years. A former Vista volunteer, he also teaches at Portland State University and Portland Community College. As a key figure and representative for Janus Youth, Morrow is a strong advocate for a community-centric approach, believing that the foundation for any successful project is the development and cultivation of relationships within that community. He emphasizes that this approach must be rooted in authentic respect and a genuine voice for the community, rather than a traditional social services mentality. Specifically, Morrow recounts the history and success of the organization's gardening programs, expressing a sense of wonder at how a small project evolved into a significant community-feeding initiative. He stresses that these programs are not just about food, but are a means of transforming kids, families, and communities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45291ea55455adae718c5031c0e8dfff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St. Johns Woods">
      <data key="d0">St. Johns Woods</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">St. Johns Woods is a public housing community in North Portland that was formerly characterized by significant challenges, including crime, hunger, and the city's highest fifth-grade dropout and police call rates. A transformative change was initiated when Janus Youth, along with other community partners, established a community garden program in the area. This highly successful and transformative project led to significant positive social changes, including a reduction in crime and a stronger connection among residents. The garden at St. Johns Woods served as a key example of Janus Youth's community-led approach and became the foundational, original garden for the larger Village Gardens program. Additionally, experienced gardeners from the St. Johns Woods project served as mentors for the New Columbia project, creating a valuable inter-community link.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45291ea55455adae718c5031c0e8dfff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d7d0c8754f5ccddfb78068c25f0fa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edff28cc5d3b6f311f02cd2481c28fb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="USDA Community Food Projects grant program">
      <data key="d0">USDA Community Food Projects grant program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The USDA Community Food Projects grant program is a federal funding opportunity that served as a catalyst for Tera Wick and the Portland community to develop a new food-related project after Janus Youth lost its previous garden property."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World Health Organization">
      <data key="d0">World Health Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The World Health Organization (WHO) is cited for its work on the social determinants of health, which provides a theoretical basis for the importance of reconnecting people and community organizations to improve quality of life, a principle embraced by Janus Youth."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An international public health agency that established the Commission on Social Determinants of Health (CSDH) in 2005 to address health inequities. Its work emphasizes empowering civil society and tackling inequitable distribution of resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Partnership Principles">
      <data key="d0">Community Partnership Principles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"This is a set of guiding principles for successful community engagement, articulated by Lynchburg Grows. Key tenets include not duplicating services, filling a need, sharing credit, making the project community-owned, building diverse partnerships, and effective storytelling."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Village Gardens program">
      <data key="d0">Village Gardens program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Village Gardens program is a specific initiative at Janus Youth, founded by Tera Wick. It serves as a prime example of building new relationships and partnerships to create successful, long-term community projects."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trout">
      <data key="d0">Trout</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Trout are a type of fish that can be raised through aquaculture for various purposes, including meat production, breeding stock, or fee fishing, with potential for good sales in grocery stores and restaurants. They come in varieties such as rainbow and golden. The narrator of one account initially raised trout for upscale restaurants but discontinued doing so to focus on catfish due to low weekly sales of around 25 pounds. Chef Hudson utilizes trout to create innovative products, while Sally Eason farms them sustainably.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16c3fe8c089550fd16987a1c4f301a2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9d28e4a28ca972c9e289441c78c0be</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740870</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tourist Area">
      <data key="d0">Tourist Area</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A location type recommended for restaurant customers, as restaurants in these areas are more likely to have a high volume of business, making them worthwhile clients for farmers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wholesale">
      <data key="d0">Wholesale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A sales model characterized by selling products in bulk to a retailer (like a grocery store) rather than directly to the consumer, resulting in lower prices per unit for the farmer."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consignment Sales">
      <data key="d0">Consignment Sales</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A sales arrangement mentioned as a way for farmers to get their products into stores. It offers higher profit but requires more time from the farmer for arranging displays and accounting."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Herbs And Vegetables">
      <data key="d0">Herbs And Vegetables</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Types of produce mentioned as examples of what a farmer can offer to restaurants. The text suggests growing new varieties as samples for receptive chefs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4fa81fea1066bfc4f1fbbd063489245c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Market Groups">
      <data key="d0">Market Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Market groups are typically regional or national associations that connect buyers and farmers, handling the sales aspect in exchange for a commission. They are particularly common in the herb and flower industries."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cooperatives">
      <data key="d0">Cooperatives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Cooperatives are a business model where small producers raising the same product pool their goods to meet the volume demands of major buyers. A co-op salesperson handles marketing, but farmers receive less than retail price and pay fees."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A cooperative is a type of business structure recommended in the text for farmers who wish to purchase machinery together. The text advises that all arrangements should be formalized in writing to avoid problems.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of business structure where farmers work together, mentioned in the index as a relevant topic for farm operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d20bcb040d48598530ec2b7c42edba7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sugar Beet Cooperatives In The Dakotas">
      <data key="d0">Sugar Beet Cooperatives In The Dakotas</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The sugar beet cooperatives in the Dakotas are mentioned as an example of large-scale cooperatives where farmers sign strict production contracts."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Government">
      <data key="d0">Local Government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"Identified as a powerful resource and ally for community projects. The text recommends finding the right department and person within the local government to help navigate the system and support the project, citing community planning or development as examples."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Economic Development Organizations">
      <data key="d0">Local Economic Development Organizations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of potential partners, including chambers of commerce and economic development commissions, that may offer resources to assist community projects."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Public Housing Advocacy Group">
      <data key="d0">Public Housing Advocacy Group</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An unnamed advocacy group that attended Tera Wick's community meeting and suggested creating a positive, long-term project in a public housing community, which directly led to the establishment of the St. Johns Woods program."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An unnamed advocacy group that attended Wick's first meeting and proposed the idea for a long-term community project, which led to the creation of the St. Johns Woods program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Portland Community Meeting">
      <data key="d0">Portland Community Meeting</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A meeting organized by Tera Wick of Janus Youth in response to a USDA grant opportunity. This event served as a brainstorming session for Portland residents, leading to the conception of the St. Johns Woods garden program."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Relationship Building">
      <data key="d0">Relationship Building</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A core concept and activity identified as a crucial task and deliverable for successful community projects. It is presented as key to improving quality of life and is supported by social science theories from the World Health Organization."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Capital and Networks">
      <data key="d0">Social Capital and Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A social science theory cited in the text that supports the practice of 'relationship building.' It suggests that reconnecting people and community organizations is a key factor in improving quality of life."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Determinants of Health">
      <data key="d0">Social Determinants of Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A concept developed by the World Health Organization and cited in the text. It provides a theoretical basis for why relationship building is crucial for fighting poverty, food insecurity, and health inequities."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A concept central to the work of a World Health Organization commission, referring to the conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work, and age, which affect health status and equity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
