      <data key="d0">The Dakotas</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A region in the United States mentioned as the location for large sugar beet cooperatives."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Missouri Farmers' Association">
      <data key="d0">Missouri Farmers' Association</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Missouri Farmers' Association is cited as an example of an older cooperative model."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Generation Cooperatives">
      <data key="d0">New Generation Cooperatives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A newer model of cooperative that sets strict limits on the number of members, differentiating it from older models like the Missouri Farmers' Association."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Herb And Flower Associations And Magazines">
      <data key="d0">Herb And Flower Associations And Magazines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"These are mentioned as resources for farmers to find and connect with market groups."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pricing">
      <data key="d0">Pricing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Pricing is described as the critical process of setting a value on farm products, which determines income and must account for all production costs, labor, and a desired profit margin."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The strategic process of setting a product's sale price. It must account for all costs, profit margin, market demand, uniqueness, and what the competition is charging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="\&quot;Home-style\&quot; Restaurants">
      <data key="d0">\"Home-style\" Restaurants</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific market segment that purchased 50 to 100 pounds of catfish per week from the author."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Herb And Flower Businesses">
      <data key="d0">Herb And Flower Businesses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An industry sector mentioned where market groups are a particularly common method for connecting farmers and buyers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Co-op Salesperson">
      <data key="d0">Co-op Salesperson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"An individual employed by a cooperative whose function is to handle the selling of members' pooled products, allowing farmers more time for production."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Conflict Resolution">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Conflict Resolution</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A field of work mentioned by the author. The author uses their personal experience in this field to attest to the importance of early collaboration and engagement with partners to build trust and community buy-in."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c331074eb4b375b2a5a5a014411e2c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wick">
      <data key="d0">Wick</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"An individual familiar with the Food Works program who provides an anecdotal story about how asking youth for solutions, rather than telling them, led to their deep involvement and investment in creating a cashiering program."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An individual who listened to a public housing advocacy group and co-founded the St. Johns Woods program to provide a positive, long-term community resource.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edff28cc5d3b6f311f02cd2481c28fb7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St. Johns Woods program">
      <data key="d0">St. Johns Woods program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A community program initiated by Wick and a public housing advocacy group, created in response to a perceived need for a positive, long-term community engagement project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rachel Reinhart">
      <data key="d0">Rachel Reinhart</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Rachel Reinhart is the program director at Jones Valley Urban Farm (JVUF) in Birmingham, Alabama, and also serves as a representative for Kelly Elementary School. In her role, she teaches the ASAP classes and manages the student interns, whom she describes as "amazing." Reinhart emphasizes the importance of community engagement and relationship-building as a means to secure support and funding for programs like Seed 2 Plate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jones Valley Urban Farm (JVUF)">
      <data key="d0">Jones Valley Urban Farm (JVUF)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An urban farm located in Birmingham, Alabama, that runs hands-on educational programs for children, such as the Seed 2 Plate program.&lt;SEP&gt;JVUF is an organization in Birmingham focused on urban agriculture and food education. It runs the 'Arts and Science of Agriculture' (ASAP) course for high school students, hires graduates as interns, and offers numerous community programs like 'Growing Together' and 'Seed 2 Plate'. JVUF is also a core partner in the local Obesity Task Force, advocating for healthier school food.&lt;SEP&gt;Jones Valley Urban Farm (JVUF) is an urban farm that is a key subject in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Birmingham, Alabama">
      <data key="d0">Birmingham, Alabama</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Birmingham, Alabama, is the largest city in its state. Historically an industrial center, it is now a banking hub. The city is the location of the Jones Valley Urban Farm, which operates by farming on abandoned lots. Birmingham faces significant public health challenges, including a high obesity rate, which has led to it being identified by a Forbes.com article as one of "America's Most Obese Cities" and becoming the subject of news commentary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9297eb734555e7b850045590e4fc5cb6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seed 2 Plate program">
      <data key="d0">Seed 2 Plate program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A hands-on, on-farm workshop program for kids run by the Jones Valley Urban Farm. Its funding was secured after a key 'person of influence' was invited to a meeting about it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Julia Rivera">
      <data key="d0">Julia Rivera</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Julia Rivera is the organizing director and board president for the organization Nuestras Raíces, located in Holyoke, Massachusetts. A key community leader and Puerto Rican immigrant, she is also the founder of the women's support group, Raíces Latinas. Rivera advocates for creating community gardens on available land, even without ownership, arguing that it transforms the community and empowers residents politically. She also stresses the importance of understanding community needs before initiating a project in order to build a strong base of support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69499413d16357f9a2d75749d72ecca8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ranchers">
      <data key="d0">Ranchers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of agricultural producer, mentioned as an example of a small group (eight to ten) who might form a cooperative to pool their calves for sale."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Delis">
      <data key="d0">Delis</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of specialty food retailer identified as a market for high-value or niche products, such as the heirloom tomatoes sold by the author."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heirloom Tomatoes">
      <data key="d0">Heirloom Tomatoes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of specialty crop used as an example of a product that farmers can specialize in to sell through cooperatives or directly to premium markets like delis."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A key crop grown at Greensgrow. The farm cultivates 30 different varieties of heirloom tomatoes specifically because there is high demand for them from the community.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of fresh, soft tomato used as an example to illustrate the challenges of switching to a fresh food system. Cafeteria staff at a Virginia university were unequipped to process them with their automatic slicers, which were designed for harder, industrially grown tomatoes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d971c6976ab8e71370d85b346b00f467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82a428e9ecd2ac447a8293c4680becb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Production Contracts">
      <data key="d0">Production Contracts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Formal agreements that large cooperatives, such as the sugar beet cooperatives, require their members to sign, dictating strict production terms and quantities."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rural Electric Co-ops">
      <data key="d0">Rural Electric Co-ops</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An example of an older, traditional cooperative model, used as a point of contrast with the newer 'New Generation' cooperatives."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value-added Products">
      <data key="d0">Value-added Products</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Products that have been enhanced or processed from their raw state to increase their market price. Pricing for these products must cover all costs."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Value-added products are goods that have been processed to increase their market value. The text discusses their benefits, production, and marketing as part of a farm plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Records">
      <data key="d0">Farm Records</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Described as an essential tool for farmers to accurately track all expenses and production data, which is necessary for calculating costs and setting profitable prices."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Costs Of Production">
      <data key="d0">Costs Of Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The total expenses required to raise a product, including direct costs, labor, and depreciation. The text stresses that knowing this is fundamental to pricing."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuestras Raíces">
      <data key="d0">Nuestras Raíces</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Nuestras Raíces ("Our Roots") is a nationally recognized, grassroots nonprofit organization located at 329 Main Street in Holyoke, Massachusetts. Founded in 1992 by Puerto Rican immigrants, its mission is to promote economic, human, and community development through projects related to food, agriculture, and the environment. The organization operates with a budget near $1 million and a staff of 25, with representatives including Kevin Andaluz, Jesus Espinosa, Gerard Ramos, Julia Rivera, Dan Ross, and Tito Santana.

Originally starting as a single community garden project called La Finquita, which was established based on direct community needs, Nuestras Raíces rapidly expanded. It is known for converting vacant and abandoned urban lots into productive community gardens, ultimately managing around ten gardens, including the Centro Agricola. The organization's work addresses environmental and community issues while celebrating Puerto Rican cultural identity and food heritage.

Nuestras Raíces has grown into a large organization that offers a variety of programs aimed at empowering residents. It runs the La Finca farm incubator and other small-enterprise incubator programs to grow new farmers and businesses, offers training courses, and operates a farm stand. Its projects have been cited as successful examples of youth and women's empowerment. An economic impact study confirmed that the organization has created measurable positive economic and social benefits for the low-income community, with projections for significant future growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ee782c1d8a631cff4fafc639994eb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aab30ee2b8630558ddd6575d42c69264&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69499413d16357f9a2d75749d72ecca8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8dfe2a17435f059c92535241799795b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aaeba6a87de6589129068300a4f26db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c38b37fe24e15bfa43a713c9e38509&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d076831b7a91bcd605c1e387112d81e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Holyoke, Massachusetts">
      <data key="d0">Holyoke, Massachusetts</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Holyoke, Massachusetts, is a city in the western part of the state that serves as the primary location for the nationally recognized organization, Nuestras Raíces. The organization originated from an initiative by the city's poor and unemployed Puerto Rican population, who, after local factories closed down, began transforming abandoned lots into food gardens for subsistence. This effort evolved into Nuestras Raíces, which now turns abandoned lots into community gardens and runs a farmer incubator program. Holyoke has the highest concentration of Puerto Ricans in the United States outside of Puerto Rico and faces significant economic and social challenges, including high rates of poverty and diabetes, making its demographic and poverty data the subject of various articles and studies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69499413d16357f9a2d75749d72ecca8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c38b37fe24e15bfa43a713c9e38509&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d076831b7a91bcd605c1e387112d81e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Puerto Rico">
      <data key="d0">Puerto Rico</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Puerto Rico is the homeland of Gerard Ramos and the location of the coral reefs he is working to restore. His connection to the area, where he observed the degradation of the reefs, serves as the motivation for his business.&lt;SEP&gt;The homeland of the immigrants who founded Nuestras Raíces. The organization's work is deeply connected to preserving and celebrating Puerto Rican rural heritage and culture for the community in Holyoke.&lt;SEP&gt;The place of origin for immigrant farmers in Holyoke whose need for food security led to the creation of the La Finquita community garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ee782c1d8a631cff4fafc639994eb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69499413d16357f9a2d75749d72ecca8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="La Finquita">
      <data key="d0">La Finquita</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">La Finquita is a farm or agricultural project mentioned in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The first community garden established in 1992 that was so successful it led to the formal creation of the Nuestras Raíces organization to preserve it and create more gardens.&lt;SEP&gt;The first community garden started by Nuestras Raíces, created because immigrant farmers from Puerto Rico needed a place to grow their own food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69499413d16357f9a2d75749d72ecca8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Raíces Latinas">
      <data key="d0">Raíces Latinas</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A support group for women in Holyoke founded by Julia Rivera as an initiative of Nuestras Raíces. The group focuses on health, nutrition, financial literacy, and job training to empower local women.&lt;SEP&gt;An educational and training program for women created by Nuestras Raíces in response to the community's expressed desire for help in finding jobs and starting businesses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69499413d16357f9a2d75749d72ecca8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tim Carlisle">
      <data key="d0">Tim Carlisle</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An organizer from the Northside Planning Council whose diligent community organizing was crucial in the creation of the Troy Gardens project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northside Planning Council">
      <data key="d0">Northside Planning Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The organization that employed Tim Carlisle and played a key role in the community organizing efforts that led to the Troy Gardens project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Organizing">
      <data key="d0">Community Organizing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core skill and process identified as the key to a successful community garden. It involves meeting with community members, facilitating discussions, building coalitions, and fundraising, and is the primary focus of the ACGA's leadership workshop.&lt;SEP&gt;A foundational process for community development, described as the first 'ingredient' for the Troy Gardens project, essential for creating a shared community vision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Competitors">
      <data key="d0">Competitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Other producers or sellers of similar goods. The text advises checking their prices as a crucial step in determining the right price for one's own products."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="'Red Cup' Heirloom Stuffing Tomato">
      <data key="d0">'Red Cup' Heirloom Stuffing Tomato</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific, named variety of heirloom tomato that the author successfully sold at a premium price to a small deli, illustrating a successful pricing strategy for quality products."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb88af633c57e852629d8c72ce59e087</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pricing Strategy">
      <data key="d0">Pricing Strategy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pricing Strategy is a central theme, discussing how farmers should price products based on quality and rarity, the negative consequences of underpricing at farmers' markets, and methodical approaches to increasing prices over time, such as using small increments and calculating potential profit vs. customer loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advertising">
      <data key="d0">Advertising</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advertising is a marketing method used to promote farm products and services through various channels. These channels include classified and display ads in newspapers and magazines, online advertising through websites, word-of-mouth, flyers, community engagement, simple roadside signs, and media outreach. It is considered a necessary practice for farmers to attract customers and can be done cost-effectively. However, some farms, like Polyface Farm, deliberately avoid advertising, arguing that it runs counter to their mission. Instead, they rely on word-of-mouth to ensure they maintain high quality and customer satisfaction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8b5f0f35b6bfccad911410ecdcdb7c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afc9b5400cf61bfbec7b607b711ba96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e0f884a6200b04603c0d37b1e67fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740871</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Cup">
      <data key="d0">Red Cup</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Cup is an heirloom stuffing tomato used as an example of a rare, high-quality product that should be sold at a premium price. Its value was demonstrated by a deli that sold it as a stuffed special.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CSA">
      <data key="d0">CSA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CSA, which stands for Community Supported Agriculture, is a business and distribution model utilized by farms like Greensgrow Farm, Gladheart Farms, Radical Roots, and Jones Valley Urban Farm. In this model, community members can purchase "shares" of the farm's harvest. Greensgrow Farm uses its nursery to attract customers to its CSA program, which boosts overall sales. Radical Roots serves a one-hundred-member CSA. CSA is also used as an example to illustrate the potential net profit gain from raising share prices, even with some customer attrition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d53cc613c52fd5080e556f9abb248a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9297eb734555e7b850045590e4fc5cb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kelly Klober">
      <data key="d0">Kelly Klober</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Author of 'Storey's Guide to Raising Pigs', which is described as an excellent resource for small-scale pig raising.&lt;SEP&gt;Kelly Klober is an expert direct marketer and author cited for his promotional advice, which includes sending sample products to media personalities and maintaining a customer mailing list for regular updates and press releases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a25021ab306ceaf5d58e192cc6fc0a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-H">
      <data key="d0">4-H</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">4-H is a children's club mentioned as an example of a community group where farmers can give talks or presentations about their operations to get free publicity and build relationships.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific example of a children's organization that the farm can partner with to host a fundraiser, such as selling pumpkins or Christmas trees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afc9b5400cf61bfbec7b607b711ba96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Partnership">
      <data key="d0">Partnership</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key strategy identified as essential for complex projects like Troy Gardens, as it expands capacity and brings diverse skills together.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Land Trust Model">
      <data key="d0">Community Land Trust Model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A legal and ownership model used at Troy Gardens to ensure the permanent protection of green space and long-term housing affordability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Government Support">
      <data key="d0">Government Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Financial and regulatory assistance from federal, state, county, and city governments, described as essential for the Troy Gardens project to succeed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wick's First Meeting">
      <data key="d0">Wick's First Meeting</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A meeting hosted by Wick where a public housing advocacy group suggested the need for a community program, leading to the creation of the St. Johns Woods program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Inequities">
      <data key="d0">Social Inequities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category representing the root problems of poverty, food insecurity, and health disparities that community projects, such as those by Nuestras Raíces, aim to combat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tenacity">
      <data key="d0">Tenacity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Described as the second essential 'ingredient' in the Troy Gardens recipe, representing the perseverance needed to overcome challenges in complex community projects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patience">
      <data key="d0">Patience</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Described as the third essential 'ingredient' in the Troy Gardens recipe, highlighting the need to accept that multifaceted, innovative projects take a long time to complete.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chamber of Commerce">
      <data key="d0">Chamber of Commerce</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Chamber of Commerce is a local business organization suggested as a resource for farmers needing help to develop a business plan and marketing tools.&lt;SEP&gt;The Chamber of Commerce is a local business organization that farmers can use for free advertising by submitting information about seasonal or one-time farm events to their community calendars.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extension Office">
      <data key="d0">Extension Office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An extension office is a local resource that farmers can consult for services such as the analysis of oils and lubricants to assess the condition of used equipment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Extension Office is a state or local entity that farmers are advised to contact to inquire about potential advertising opportunities and resources they may offer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quality">
      <data key="d0">Quality</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Quality is a key attribute of farm products that directly influences pricing strategy. The text states that high quality justifies a premium price and that low prices can undermine the perception of quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heirloom Variety">
      <data key="d0">Heirloom Variety</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An Heirloom Variety is a type of rare produce, exemplified by the 'Red Cup' tomato, that the text advises should be sold at a higher price due to its uniqueness and unavailability in common stores.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Network">
      <data key="d0">Community Network</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Community Network is the web of relationships a farmer is encouraged to build with friends, relatives, neighbors, businesses, clubs (like 4-H), and community groups to enhance their business through word-of-mouth advertising and free publicity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Signs">
      <data key="d0">Signs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Signs are a key advertising tool, especially for roadside stands and farmers' markets. The text provides detailed guidelines on their design, including simplicity, color contrast (black on neon), letter size relative to traffic speed, and strategic placement for visibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Word of Mouth">
      <data key="d0">Word of Mouth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Word of Mouth is identified as the 'first and best way to advertise.' It is a cost-free promotional method that involves leveraging one's Community Network, including friends, neighbors, and local business contacts, to spread the word about the farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flyers and Notices">
      <data key="d0">Flyers and Notices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Flyers and Notices are described as the 'next step in advertising.' This low-cost method involves placing promotional literature on community bulletin boards in locations like hardware stores, restaurants, and doctors' offices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clustering of Housing">
      <data key="d0">Clustering of Housing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The sixth 'ingredient' or design strategy used at Troy Gardens, where homes are grouped densely in one area to preserve large amounts of open space for community use like gardens and prairies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Accessibility">
      <data key="d0">Accessibility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The eighth 'ingredient' of the Troy Gardens project, defined as a multi-dimensional principle ensuring access to housing, home ownership, green space, healthy food, and transportation for all community members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Belief">
      <data key="d0">Belief</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The tenth 'ingredient' for the Troy Gardens project, representing the fundamental conviction and confidence required to undertake and complete such an ambitious community endeavor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b14ac130219733cde2f806a475527525</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sol Levin">
      <data key="d0">Sol Levin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Sol Levin was one of the original community leaders in the development of Troy Gardens. He is credited with having infectious faith that helped the project succeed through difficult times."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="John Emmeus Davis">
      <data key="d0">John Emmeus Davis</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"John Emmeus Davis is the editor of 'The Community Land Trust Reader,' a book that features an excerpt about Troy Gardens."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lincoln Land Institute">
      <data key="d0">Lincoln Land Institute</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Lincoln Land Institute is the publisher of 'The Community Land Trust Reader,' a book published in 2010."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indian Line Farm">
      <data key="d0">Indian Line Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Indian Line Farm is a historically significant farm located in South Egremont, Massachusetts, with a specific address at 57 Jug End Road, Great Barrington, MA. It is recognized as one of the founding Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) farms in the United States and one of the first two CSA programs in North America, established in 1986. The farm's representatives are Elizabeth Keen and Al Thorpe. Cited as a successful example of a community partnership in agriculture, Indian Line Farm operates under a unique ownership model that involves a land trust, the farmers, and The Nature Conservancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc825bb5fbec77abff4a02be6fc5fcec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f4b1f56482ca88dc86d76611131fea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f1544f96bf3906c3ffd91d3f551a278</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Media Outreach">
      <data key="d0">Media Outreach</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Media Outreach is a promotional strategy focused on gaining publicity through local media channels. Tactics discussed include trying to get feature stories, sending sample products to radio or TV personalities, and distributing press releases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mailing List">
      <data key="d0">Mailing List</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Mailing List is a direct marketing tool recommended by Kelly Klober for customer communication. It is suggested for farmers with 200 or more names to share updates, news on new produce, and special opportunities, potentially using a bulk mailing permit.&lt;SEP&gt;A key marketing tool for farms, used to send press releases, newsletters, or postcards to a list of contacts, including customers and local media. It is expanded by collecting addresses from sign-up sheets and checks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afc9b5400cf61bfbec7b607b711ba96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Press Releases">
      <data key="d0">Press Releases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific marketing activity recommended for the farm. It involves sending single-page, double-spaced documents to local media to generate publicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Press Releases are a formal communication tool for Media Outreach. The text specifies their format should be limited to a single, double-spaced page and they should be sent regularly to contacts on the mailing list, including local media.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afc9b5400cf61bfbec7b607b711ba96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Media">
      <data key="d0">Local Media</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of organizations, including newspapers, that should be on the farm's mailing list to receive regular press releases and newsletters for publicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Local Media refers to the collective group of local newspapers, magazines, ad gazettes, radio, and television stations. They are presented as a channel for both expensive paid advertising and free publicity through feature stories and press releases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ca358634d91f845f6dd19689edaf7b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afc9b5400cf61bfbec7b607b711ba96</data>
