    </node>
    <node id="Mary Seton Corboy">
      <data key="d0">Mary Seton Corboy</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mary Seton Corboy is a prominent leader in the local food movement and the co-founder, CEO, and visionary leader of Greensgrow Farm in Philadelphia, which she established with Tom Sereduk. A chef by background, her initial motivation was to find cheap land and grow better tomatoes, leading her to adapt a farming model that could thrive on a contaminated urban site. Under her leadership, Greensgrow grew into a successful enterprise with over $1 million in revenue, in part by making strategic decisions such as transforming its greenhouses into a full-fledged nursery to extend its year-round income.

Corboy is cited as an expert on running a successful Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) program, emphasizing that CSAs are about building relationships with both customers and suppliers. She advocates for complete transparency, especially during crop failures, and encourages cooperative partnerships among farmers. She also serves as the farm's beekeeper and is the founder of Philly LIFE, championing hands-on learning and innovative solutions to bring fresh, nutritious food to people of all income levels.

Drawing from her experience, Corboy offers extensive advice for aspiring urban farmers. She recommends starting with minimal capital, leasing land instead of buying it to avoid high costs and liability, and forgoing expensive equipment like tractors unless absolutely necessary. She also highlights the importance of new farms identifying their customers, securing trained labor, and building trust with the community over time. Her ultimate vision is for healthy, local food to become so accessible and commonplace that the "local food movement" itself becomes obsolete, a goal for which she often uses the successes of the feminist movement as an analogy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f4b1f56482ca88dc86d76611131fea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd12c93ca420ae0dfd8107f51eec1886</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greensgrow Farm">
      <data key="d0">Greensgrow Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, here is a comprehensive summary of Greensgrow Farm:

Greensgrow Farm is a profitable and pioneering urban farm located at 2501 East Cumberland Street in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. Founded in 1997 by Mary Seton Corboy and Tom Sereduk, the farm is notable for its innovative operation on a leased Superfund (brownfield) site, where it uses raised beds to safely grow produce over the contaminated soil.

The farm began by selling hydroponic greens and has since diversified its income streams significantly. It established a successful nursery business selling a wide range of plants, which now accounts for 30% of its revenues in the early part of the year. In addition to its nursery, Greensgrow Farm serves its community through a farmers' market and a successful Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) program. It also engages in agritourism, using its popular nursery to attract customers to its other business operations.

With a budget nearing $1 million and a staff of 19, Greensgrow Farm is often cited as a case study for a successful, lean business model. It has avoided overcapitalization by employing cost-effective solutions, such as using rescued trailers for offices and building its own custom coolers instead of purchasing expensive commercial models. The farm, represented by figures like Cody Richter and the late Mary Seton Corboy, is also used as an example for demonstrating the importance of transparency and customer relationships, particularly in navigating challenges like a drought. Overall, Greensgrow Farm is a key model for successful local food production and community engagement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d53cc613c52fd5080e556f9abb248a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f4b1f56482ca88dc86d76611131fea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd12c93ca420ae0dfd8107f51eec1886</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the provided information, Philadelphia is the city where the urban farm Greensgrow Farm is located. This farm, often cited as an example of a successful Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) operation, aims to feed the people of Philadelphia and actively engages with its community. To grow its business, Greensgrow targeted influential horticulture clubs within the city and supports a local ecosystem that includes restaurants, which supply used oil for biodiesel production. Greensgrow Farm successfully overcame the challenge of utilizing available, affordable land that had been blighted by former industry.

In addition to Greensgrow, Philadelphia is also home to the Neighborhood Gardens Association, a prominent community garden land trust established in 1986. The city has also seen success with educational initiatives, such as a Farm to School program that increased the percentage of kindergarteners who knew where their food came from from 33 percent to 88 percent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d53cc613c52fd5080e556f9abb248a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fef6f9e3cdaa27cfe32361bcab20e464&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75f4b1f56482ca88dc86d76611131fea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-951f3a20705ad68da0aafd6377f51f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e143d750d8e9b45fec3f4d1a9ec2a04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b1f6adc63dbe7f3160ab5bd7b25233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82a428e9ecd2ac447a8293c4680becb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd12c93ca420ae0dfd8107f51eec1886</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740817</data>
    </node>
