    </node>
    <node id="Extension Service">
      <data key="d0">Extension Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An advisory body, mentioned alongside the Forestry Department, that farmers are encouraged to contact for an evaluation before clearing trees.&lt;SEP&gt;An extension service is an advisory organization that farmers can contact for an evaluation of their resources, such as timber.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db06dd0bc574d59f7ffa8848b9ae23fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Forestry Department">
      <data key="d0">Forestry Department</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A government agency that provides expert evaluations on trees, which farmers are advised to consult before clearing land.&lt;SEP&gt;The Forestry Department is a governmental or official body that can provide evaluations on the value and potential of tree crops before they are cleared.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db06dd0bc574d59f7ffa8848b9ae23fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Draft Animal Activities">
      <data key="d0">Draft Animal Activities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of farm enterprise that includes breeding and racing draft animals, as well as running schools for them. Plow Days are used as a promotional event for these activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pasture, Hay, And Cover Crops">
      <data key="d0">Pasture, Hay, And Cover Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of crops that are essential for integrating livestock and crop enterprises on a farm. While their direct market potential is low, they can be used for trade or value-added products like miniature bales for craft events.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCLT">
      <data key="d0">SCLT</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">SCLT is an organization that runs a productive urban farm, founded the Greater Providence Urban Agriculture Task Force, and created the Urban Agriculture Resource Center website. It has plans to expand its network of gardens and its educational programs for schoolchildren.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carol Bonnar">
      <data key="d0">Carol Bonnar</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Carol Bonnar is the volunteer manager of the Worcester Street community garden, which is located in the South End/Lower Roxbury area of Roxbury, Massachusetts. Leveraging her background in project management, she navigates the challenges of diversity and governance within the garden. Bonnar is a strong advocate for establishing garden rules in collaboration with the gardeners. She has actively addressed the issue of soil contamination from old railroad ties and successfully solicited a donation from a local business, McVey Stone Company, to improve the garden's infrastructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07f097aee6be0e05076e0e695419438a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Worcester Street">
      <data key="d0">Worcester Street</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Worcester Street is the location of the second largest community garden in the South End/Lower Roxbury area. The garden is managed by Carol Bonnar and has a diverse membership, including Haitian immigrants and residents from suburbs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Orange Line">
      <data key="d0">Orange Line</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Orange Line was a train line that ran through Boston and Roxbury. After it was moved underground in the mid-1980s, its creosote-treated railroad ties were given to community gardens, causing significant soil contamination with arsenic and other toxins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roxbury">
      <data key="d0">Roxbury</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Roxbury is an urban, inner-city neighborhood in Boston, Massachusetts, that is part of the South End/Lower Roxbury area. The neighborhood serves as a key site for The Food Project's city-based activities, where the organization operates several urban plots, including three city farm lots, the Urban Learning Farm at the Shirley-Eustis House, and other neighborhood gardens. These locations are central to The Food Project's mission of exposing suburban and urban youths to each other's home environments and demonstrating a commitment to working in diverse community settings. Historically, the Orange Line formerly ran through Roxbury, and its community gardens were affected by contaminated railroad ties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07d81959e2e7dfd429fb34ce74beae5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jamaica Plain Project">
      <data key="d0">Jamaica Plain Project</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Jamaica Plain Project was an expensive urban renewal effort in Boston where several organizations collaborated to remove, clean, and return toxic soil from a community garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boston Natural Areas Network">
      <data key="d0">Boston Natural Areas Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Boston Natural Areas Network was a key partner in the Jamaica Plain Project, responsible for hauling out and cleaning the toxic soil from a community garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boston University School of Public Health">
      <data key="d0">Boston University School of Public Health</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An academic institution that conducted soil sample analysis at the Worcester Street Garden. Its faculty, including Wendy Heiger-Bernays, identified the contamination and developed a remediation strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;An academic institution that published a news item about using low-cost compost to remedy soil contamination in Boston's community gardens.&lt;SEP&gt;The Boston University School of Public Health was a collaborator on the Jamaica Plain Project. They later tested soil samples at the Worcester Street Garden at Carol Bonnar's request and provided an expert opinion on the contamination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Grains">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Grains</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of crops including corn, wheat, and soybeans, which are hard to profit from without adding value. The text suggests focusing on open-pollinated corn for its feed value and seed-saving qualities, or unique soybeans for tofu. They also have potential for decorative sales (e.g., for Halloween) and other products like flours and breads.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vegetables And Melons">
      <data key="d0">Vegetables And Melons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of traditional crops for direct marketing. The text suggests increasing sales by offering rare, exotic, or 'baby' varieties, and extending the growing season. Value-added products include sauces and wreaths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Herbs And Medicinal Herbs">
      <data key="d0">Herbs And Medicinal Herbs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of intensive crops with growing market potential. The text advises finding buyers before growing them, especially for high-investment herbs like ginseng. Their best use is on a small scale or in value-added products like soup mixes, oils, and soaps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mushrooms">
      <data key="d0">Mushrooms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of produce that can be lucrative but requires a few years to establish. They can be sold to restaurants and stores, or as mushroom-growing kits. Value-added items include dried mushrooms and soup mixes.&lt;SEP&gt;A specialized product category, including gourmet and medicinal types, supplied by Fungi Perfecti Llc.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c07830327394a77dc9e8ac1a53acd63</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gourds And Pumpkins">
      <data key="d0">Gourds And Pumpkins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of good seasonal items, particularly when value is added. Examples include carved pumpkins, specialty varieties, painted gourds, and products like pumpkin pie.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrial Crops">
      <data key="d0">Industrial Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of nonfood crops like crambe and jojoba used for industrial applications such as lubricants. While potentially profitable, they require a large investment and rely on a very small number of buyers, making them a risky, non-sustainable option at present.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Fruits">
      <data key="d0">Small Fruits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of crop grown by Steve Salt on his farm, which he sells at farmers' markets and are noted as being enjoyed by everyone.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of crops including strawberries, raspberries, and gooseberries with great potential for U-pick farms and value-added markets. The text recommends planting varieties that mature at different times to ensure a steady flow of customers. Value-added products include jams, jellies, and syrups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fruit And Nut Trees">
      <data key="d0">Fruit And Nut Trees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of orchard crops like apples, peaches, walnuts, and pecans. They are suitable for U-pick farms, farmers' markets, and grocery sales. The text suggests offering varieties with different flavors and maturity times, and considering value-added options like a subscription service.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Worcester Street Garden">
      <data key="d0">Worcester Street Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Worcester Street Garden is a community garden located on Worcester Street in the South End/Lower Roxbury area of Boston, Massachusetts. It is the second largest community garden in the neighborhood and is managed by the South End/Lower Roxbury Open Space Land Trust (SELROSLT), with Carol Bonnar serving as its manager. The garden is home to a culturally diverse group of gardeners, including members of the Haitian community.

Historically, the garden suffered from extensive soil contamination caused by creosote and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) leaching from old railroad ties that were used for its raised beds. Due to these concerns, it underwent a major remediation project and complete redesign between 2008 and 2009. During this process, the toxic soil was replaced, and the old railroad tie beds were removed and replaced with new raised beds made from bluestone, which was donated by a local company. For plot maintenance, the garden now utilizes a flag system to issue warnings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07f097aee6be0e05076e0e695419438a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Haitians">
      <data key="d0">Haitians</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of gardeners at the Worcester Street Garden, many of whom live in subsidized housing. They contribute to the garden's diversity, for example by bringing seeds from their homeland, and present cultural and linguistic challenges for governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The State">
      <data key="d0">The State</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A government body that worked in partnership with the Boston Natural Areas Network and the Boston University School of Public Health on the Jamaica Plain project to remove and clean toxic soil from a community garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-603aed086b5eda0ec62eae7fa8c0f1b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wendy Heiger-Bernays">
      <data key="d0">Wendy Heiger-Bernays</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An associate professor of environmental health at Boston University School of Public Health. She investigated soil contamination in community gardens, identified the presence of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) leaching from creosote-treated railroad ties, and developed an affordable soil remediation recipe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bonnar">
      <data key="d0">Bonnar</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A community gardener at the Worcester Street Garden who was instrumental in the garden's rebuilding effort. Bonnar successfully raised funds and negotiated with a company to donate bluestone for the new garden beds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bessie Barnes Garden">
      <data key="d0">Bessie Barnes Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A community garden in a historic African-American neighborhood contaminated by PAHs from urban pollution. The EPA remediated the site by capping the soil with concrete, on top of which a safe food garden with tall raised beds was built.&lt;SEP&gt;A small but abundant community garden in Lower Roxbury, built on a concrete cap. It serves as an example of a successful grassroots project where gardeners want to sell surplus produce, leading to the creation of a local farmers' market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St. Paul, Minnesota">
      <data key="d0">St. Paul, Minnesota</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A city that serves as a case study where a children's garden evolved into a community garden for Hmong immigrants, eventually leading to a farmers' market and a youth education program.&lt;SEP&gt;A city used as an example by Betsy Johnson where a simple children's garden evolved into a larger community garden and eventually a farmers' market, driven by the needs of Hmong immigrants.&lt;SEP&gt;St. Paul, Minnesota is the city where Regine Kennedy resides and works at the 106 Group.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SELROSLT gardens">
      <data key="d0">SELROSLT gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of community gardens in Boston, including the Worcester Street and Bessie Barnes gardens, that shared a legacy of soil contamination, particularly from PAHs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flowers, Cut And Dried">
      <data key="d0">Flowers, Cut And Dried</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A growing market category. Sales can be year-round with a greenhouse. The text suggests combining this with a nursery tree operation and targeting value-added markets (baskets, arrangements) and seasonal events.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Timber">
      <data key="d0">Timber</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of tree crops that can be raised for timber, firewood, or nursery stock. The text notes that quick-growing varieties like Pawlonia or bamboo can be highly profitable, and advises getting an evaluation from an extension service or Forestry Department before clearing land.&lt;SEP&gt;A farm resource identified as a valuable long-term reserve that can be used for future sales or as firewood.&lt;SEP&gt;Timber is described as a valuable and manageable farm resource that can produce posts, firewood, wildlife, light grazing, and lumber. The author advocates for managing existing timber rather than clearing it, especially on steep, erodible hills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Christmas Trees">
      <data key="d0">Christmas Trees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of tree crop highlighted for its excellent seasonal sales potential, providing an income source outside of the traditional growing season.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific type of tree crop with excellent seasonal sales potential, mentioned as a profitable option within the broader category of timber.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db06dd0bc574d59f7ffa8848b9ae23fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U-pick">
      <data key="d0">U-pick</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A direct-to-consumer sales model where customers harvest their own produce from the farm. It is mentioned as a high-potential market for small fruits and fruit/nut trees, and as a venue for agritourism attractions like hay-bale mazes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organic Markets">
      <data key="d0">Organic Markets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A niche market channel focused on selling certified organic products. The text suggests this as a potential outlet for traditional grains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Specialty Restaurants">
      <data key="d0">Specialty Restaurants</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Food service businesses that are identified as lucrative buyers for high-value or unique agricultural products, specifically specialty mushrooms and fresh herbs like lemongrass.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Stores">
      <data key="d0">Health Stores</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Retail businesses that specialize in health-related products. The text mentions them as a potential sales channel for small fruits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flower Shops">
      <data key="d0">Flower Shops</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Retail businesses specializing in the sale of flowers and floral arrangements. They are mentioned as a potential sales outlet for farmers growing cut flowers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1923a02fc73ab559e6ae4a61b5e1218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPA">
      <data key="d0">EPA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The EPA (Environmental Protection Agency) is a government agency that, in 2001, estimated the annual cost to create cleanup plans for nutrient-polluted waterways to be between $900 million and $4.3 billion."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The Environmental Protection Agency, a US government agency. It discovered the very low air quality in Holyoke, which prompted educational efforts like the 'Healthy Homes Workshops'.&lt;SEP&gt;The Environmental Protection Agency, a government body that decided to 'cap' the contaminated soil at the Bessie Barnes garden with concrete to ensure the safety of the new food garden built on top.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8dfe2a17435f059c92535241799795b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons (PAHs)">
      <data key="d0">Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons (PAHs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of over one hundred toxic chemicals that can cause cancer and harm immune and reproductive systems. They were found contaminating the soil in Boston community gardens, originating from both railroad ties and general urban pollution like burning coal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Creosote">
      <data key="d0">Creosote</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A carcinogenic substance used to preserve railroad ties. It was identified as the source of soil contamination in the Worcester Street Garden, as it leached toxins into the surrounding soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Worcester Street Community Gardeners">
      <data key="d0">Worcester Street Community Gardeners</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The collective group of gardeners and volunteers who manage the Worcester Street Garden. They were responsible for carrying out the massive remediation project in 2008-2009, which involved removing old railroad ties and toxic soil and redesigning the garden layout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Remediation 'recipe'">
      <data key="d0">Remediation 'recipe'</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An affordable soil treatment plan developed by Wendy Heiger-Bernays to decontaminate the soil at the Worcester Street Garden. It consists of mixing two parts donated clean city compost with one part of the old, contaminated soil to help break down remaining PAHs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="African-Americans">
      <data key="d0">African-Americans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group central to the program's focus. The text notes that many teens in the program are African-American and discusses the cultural stigma attached to farming within this community, linking it to a history of slavery and oppression. The program aims to redefine farming for them and connect them with their agricultural heritage.&lt;SEP&gt;A demographic group mentioned in the text as being the historical residents of the middle-class neighborhood where the Bessie Barnes garden is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Puerto Rican Population">
      <data key="d0">Puerto Rican Population</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A community in Holyoke, Massachusetts, cited as an example of a group that turned to food gardening for subsistence after local textile and tobacco factories shut down, leaving them unemployed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e04d6e50411798e18bdf72f9c1734a84</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hmong Immigrants">
      <data key="d0">Hmong Immigrants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A community in St. Paul, Minnesota, used as an example by Betsy Johnson. Their involvement in a community garden led to its expansion, more intensive food production, and the eventual creation of a farmers' market to sell their produce.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific community in St. Paul who, despite being deaf from bombing in Southeast Asia, utilized a community garden for intensive food production, economic opportunity, and social integration.&lt;SEP&gt;Hmong immigrants are a cultural group mentioned as a topic in the text.</data>
