    <node id="Progressive Era">
      <data key="d0">Progressive Era</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical period from the 1890s through World War I, marking the origin of the community garden tradition in the U.S., where they were seen as tools for aiding the poor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World War I">
      <data key="d0">World War I</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A period during which 'war gardens' were promoted to support the war effort, leading to the creation of the National War Garden Commission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World War II">
      <data key="d0">World War II</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical global conflict, after which the increased use of the automobile in the U.S. led to people moving from cities to the country, contributing to the conversion of farmland into developed areas.&lt;SEP&gt;World War II was a historical global conflict that had several significant impacts on technology and agriculture. During the war, the pesticide DDT was considered a "miracle drug" used for delousing soldiers and killing mosquitoes, long before its carcinogenic properties were understood. The period was also renowned for its "victory gardens," which produced over 40 percent of the vegetables grown in the United States. Furthermore, World War II serves as a crucial time marker, as the Hopi and Navajo maintained 90 percent food self-sufficiency up to this point before shifting towards commercial food. The conflict also acted as the catalyst for the Green Revolution, which was initiated in its aftermath to address world hunger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f712d3550c8612228f9fb4dc999ba9b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National War Garden Commission">
      <data key="d0">National War Garden Commission</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A federal commission established by the U.S. government in 1917 to promote and support 'war gardens' during World War I.&lt;SEP&gt;The National War Garden Commission is a historical organization mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56</data>
