    <node id="Youth Education Program">
      <data key="d0">Youth Education Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A program that emerged in St. Paul as a solution to help deaf, non-English-speaking Hmong gardeners sell their produce at the farmers' market by training youth to handle sales. It illustrates the role of community gardens in spurring educational and social initiatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Detroit">
      <data key="d0">Detroit</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Detroit is a U.S. city frequently used as an example to illustrate the potential of urban agriculture in post-industrial cities. It is noted for having a significant amount of affordable, abandoned land, including approximately 6,000 acres of vacant lots, which could be transformed for farming. Activist Malik Yakini specifically cites Detroit to show how this land could be used to produce a substantial amount of food for its residents. As a testament to this movement, the city is home to The Greening of Detroit, a comprehensive food project that evolved from a community garden. Additionally, Detroit is referenced as the location for the publication 'Detroit Metro Times'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c38b37fe24e15bfa43a713c9e38509&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d076831b7a91bcd605c1e387112d81e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="War Gardens">
      <data key="d0">War Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical type of garden promoted during the World Wars. They were framed as a patriotic duty for citizens to contribute to the war effort through home food production and canning.&lt;SEP&gt;A term for gardens planted during wartime to supplement food supplies, which the text explicitly cross-references with 'victory gardens'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Victory Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Victory Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A highly successful community gardening initiative during World War II. These gardens, totaling over 20 million, produced more than 40% of the vegetables grown in the United States, demonstrating the massive potential of community-based food production.&lt;SEP&gt;Food gardens planted at private residences and public parks during wartime (WWI and WWII) to reduce pressure on the public food supply. The text links this to 'war gardens'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43ebc646508b0f93fb43f529856b4d56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740819</data>
    </node>
