    </node>
    <node id="ACGA">
      <data key="d0">ACGA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Community Gardening Association (ACGA) is an organization that offers a two-day workshop focused on developing leadership for community gardens. The workshop emphasizes community organizing, fundraising, and coalition building rather than horticultural skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DINÉ Inc.">
      <data key="d0">DINÉ Inc.</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization for which Jonathan Yazzie works as a mentor farmer, helping other Navajo families to begin growing their own food.&lt;SEP&gt;An organization involved in restoring traditional farming on the Navajo reservation, for which Jonathan Yazzie serves as a mentor farmer.&lt;SEP&gt;An organization that operates the Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach program. It supports the development of community gardens by assisting communities in creating their own vision and goals, with an emphasis on internal leadership.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P-Patch Program">
      <data key="d0">P-Patch Program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A community gardening program, prominently associated with Seattle, that allows residents to cultivate garden plots."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A community garden program in Seattle, managed by Richard MacDonald, that requires a group of at least five or six committed community leaders to propose a new garden, ensuring collective stewardship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jamescita Peshlakai">
      <data key="d0">Jamescita Peshlakai</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jamescita Peshlakai is the lead coordinator for the Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach (NNTAO) program at DINÉ Inc. and is a U.S. Army combat veteran of the Persian Gulf War. As a representative and community figure for the Navajo Nation, she leads the effort to restore traditional Navajo agriculture to combat health epidemics like obesity and diabetes. Peshlakai is also facilitating the formation of the Navajo Nation Food Policy Council. She believes her role is not to impose a vision, but to support the community in developing its own plans for projects like gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chickens, Broilers">
      <data key="d0">Chickens, Broilers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farming operation for raising broiler chickens for meat. The model starts with 1000 chicks, assumes a 10% mortality rate, and takes 8-9 weeks to reach a market weight of 4 lbs per bird. For 900 surviving chicks, this yields a net profit of $4,688.00 from $7,200.00 in income, with expenses of $1,512.00 for feed and $1,000.00 for other costs. The operation requires 500 hours of labor."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chickens, Purebred">
      <data key="d0">Chickens, Purebred</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farming operation for raising purebred chickens to sell chicks. The model is based on 20 hens with a 60% production rate and a 60% hatching rate, yielding 2,640 chicks. This generates a net profit of $7,440.00 from $7,920.00 in income, with expenses of $280 for feed and $200 for other costs, and requires 40 hours of labor."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dairy Goat">
      <data key="d0">Dairy Goat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farming operation involving dairy goats, with does averaging two kids. The operation generates a net profit of $224.50 from $586.00 in income (from milk sales, culls, and replacements). Expenses are $234.00 for feed and $127.50 for other costs, requiring 22 hours of labor."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fallow Deer">
      <data key="d0">Fallow Deer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farming operation for raising fallow deer, based on a model of 100 does and 5 bucks. The provided figures, based on one doe, show a net profit of $131.00 from $253.00 in income (from venison, breeding stock, and hides). Expenses are $59.00 for feed and $63.00 for other costs, requiring 3 hours of labor per doe."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hogs, Feeder Pig Purchased">
      <data key="d0">Hogs, Feeder Pig Purchased</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A hog farming operation where a 40-lb. feeder pig is purchased and raised to 250 pounds, then direct-marketed as sausage. This model generates a net profit of $170.00 from $250.00 in income. Expenses include $48.00 for feed and $32.00 for the pig cost, and it requires 8 hours of labor."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hogs, Sow and 16 Pigs Raised (Direct Market as Half Carcasses)">
      <data key="d0">Hogs, Sow and 16 Pigs Raised (Direct Market as Half Carcasses)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A hog farming operation based on a sow producing 16 pigs over two litters per year, which are then direct-marketed as half carcasses. This model generates a total gross income of $3,500.00 and a net profit of $2,352.00. Expenses are $1,116.00 for feed and $32.00 for other costs, requiring 20 hours of labor."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hogs, Sow and 16 Pigs Raised (Direct Market as Sausage)">
      <data key="d0">Hogs, Sow and 16 Pigs Raised (Direct Market as Sausage)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A hog farming operation based on a sow producing 16 pigs over two litters per year, which are then direct-marketed as sausage. This model generates a total gross income of $5,187.50 and a net profit of $4,039.50. Expenses are $1,116.00 for feed and $32.00 for other costs. It requires 20 hours of labor plus an additional 20 hours for market labor."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eca Etabo Wasongolo">
      <data key="d0">Eca Etabo Wasongolo</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A community organizer for Village Gardens, a Janus Youth program in Portland, Oregon. He advocates for first understanding a community's needs and guiding them toward the idea of a garden, rather than proposing it directly.&lt;SEP&gt;A political refugee from Congo with a degree in social work. He is a leader in the Seeds of Harmony garden and was hired as a community organizer by Village Gardens, allowing him to use his professional skills.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual named in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d7d0c8754f5ccddfb78068c25f0fa0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michael Van Ness">
      <data key="d0">Michael Van Ness</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Michael Van Ness is a co-founder, leader, and the executive director of Lynchburg Grows in Virginia. In 2004, he discovered an abandoned greenhouse site and, inspired by the story of Paul Lam, envisioned its transformation into a community farm. Van Ness was a key partner in the organization's early, physically demanding projects, such as replacing the soil.

As a representative and the executive director, he champions a philosophy of building projects with the community rather than for it, advocating for a slow, community-driven approach to ensure success and broad acceptance. He emphasizes that marginalized people desire to contribute to society and believes that working with people with special needs teaches empathy. Additionally, Van Ness stresses the practical importance of having all volunteers sign a liability form to protect the organization from financial crisis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cee86d7056c73fbe7bdaa432b2abe64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Columbia, Portland, Oregon">
      <data key="d0">New Columbia, Portland, Oregon</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A low-income community in Portland, Oregon, that serves as the location for the Village Gardens program, which is operated by Janus Youth and employs Eca Etabo Wasongolo.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leadership Development">
      <data key="d0">Leadership Development</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical component for the success and sustainability of community gardens. It involves skills in community organizing, facilitation, and fundraising, and is presented as more important than gardening knowledge itself.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Village Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Village Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, here is a comprehensive summary:

Village Gardens is a community garden program and nonprofit project located in Portland, Oregon, and operated by the larger organization Janus Youth Programs. The program, directed by Amber Baker, is situated in the New Columbia community and grew out of an earlier garden at St. Johns Woods with funding from a USDA grant.

A core principle of Village Gardens is its model of deep community ownership and collaborative decision-making, which requires community members to approve all new staff, volunteers, and grant proposals, and is fostered through regular, intensive meetings. To engage local residents, the program employs community organizers such as Eca Etabo Wasongolo.

Village Gardens has become a successful model for other communities and has expanded its mission to include mentorship for new gardens; for example, its members from St. Johns Woods mentored the New Columbia gardeners. The program also includes a food farm for teens. Through its work, Village Gardens has learned the importance of staying focused on its core mission and serving its local community's needs rather than expanding indefinitely.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d7d0c8754f5ccddfb78068c25f0fa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edff28cc5d3b6f311f02cd2481c28fb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07f097aee6be0e05076e0e695419438a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Works">
      <data key="d0">Food Works</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Food Works is a teen employment and empowerment program created by and associated with Janus Youth. It emerged from the success of community gardening projects and was developed in response to high food insecurity and a community desire for teen-focused programs. Originally a tiny garden, Food Works has since grown into a 1-acre certified organic farm on Sauvie Island.

The program employs youth from the community, including Pascal Ananouko's children, providing them with valuable job skills and leadership opportunities. Participants are empowered by learning to plan, grow, and market produce, and all proceeds from sales are reinvested back into the program. Food Works serves as an example of community-led decision-making, as its expansion into poultry production was a result of natural evolution and community choice, not a top-down mandate. The program has reported positive outcomes, such as an increased consumption of fresh vegetables among its teen participants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45291ea55455adae718c5031c0e8dfff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d7d0c8754f5ccddfb78068c25f0fa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach Program">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach Program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A program operated by DINÉ Inc. and coordinated by Jamescita Peshlakai. It focuses on supporting the Navajo Nation community in developing its own garden projects by providing assistance rather than directives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
