    </node>
    <node id="Corporations">
      <data key="d0">Corporations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of for-profit entities that, according to the text, have shown interest in community gardens as part of the food movement. They are often criticized by experts like Johnson for attempting to fund gardens with a top-down approach and insufficient resources or community engagement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Nation">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Nation</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Navajo Nation is a semi-autonomous Native American territory covering 10 million acres that serves as the homeland for the Diné people. Functioning as both a governing body and a cultural region, it is the setting for various agricultural and community initiatives aimed at preserving its culture and health. These initiatives, such as DINÉ Inc.'s Traditional Agricultural Outreach program and a community farm, emphasize local leadership and reconnecting people with their land and food. Individuals like Rose Marie Williams contribute by conducting workshops and consulting at chapter houses throughout the nation. These efforts are particularly crucial as the Navajo Nation faces significant challenges, including food-related health issues, cultural exploitation, and the tension between traditional and "Americanized" lifestyles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACGA Two-day Workshop">
      <data key="d0">ACGA Two-day Workshop</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific training event offered by the ACGA. Its curriculum is intentionally focused on community organizing, meeting facilitation, fundraising, and coalition building, rather than on the technical aspects of gardening, to build sustainable leadership.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Movement">
      <data key="d0">Food Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A broad social and academic movement focused on food systems, which is being integrated into higher-education curricula to educate students on nutrition, agriculture, and local economies.&lt;SEP&gt;A societal trend that has gained steam, leading to increased interest in local food systems and community gardens. This movement has prompted larger organizations, including corporations, to get involved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Top-down Approach">
      <data key="d0">Top-down Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A project implementation strategy, heavily criticized in the text, where an external organization imposes a plan (like a community garden) on a community without its initial desire, ownership, or leadership. It is contrasted with a 'from the ground up' approach and is linked to project failure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Justice">
      <data key="d0">Social Justice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Social justice is a foundational concept for several entities, acting as a core principle, value, and high-level societal goal. For the organization ASD, social justice was a primary motivation behind its founding, and it addresses this by providing economic opportunities to struggling farmers and challenging the notion that the organic food movement is elitist. It is also a core component of the mission of Seeds of Solidarity, which connects environmental health with social equity, specifically through educational programs for low-income communities. For Johnny, a crew leader at The Food Project, social justice is a core value he actively worked on by integrating it with environmental and agricultural concerns. More broadly, social justice is presented as a high-level societal goal that can be advanced through community garden projects, which are seen as a way to use food as a means for empowerment and equity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aaeba6a87de6589129068300a4f26db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Youth And Women's Empowerment">
      <data key="d0">Youth And Women's Empowerment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific positive outcome that resulted from the community garden project at Nuestras Raíces, serving as an example of the broader social benefits of such initiatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-174591c0f94ab01a05c96941979500a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seattle's P-Patch Program">
      <data key="d0">Seattle's P-Patch Program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A city-managed community garden program in Seattle, Washington. It requires group participation for new gardens and has been sustained for decades with the help of a volunteer-driven nonprofit, the P-Patch Trust.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07f097aee6be0e05076e0e695419438a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bell Pepper">
      <data key="d0">Bell Pepper</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crop enterprise that yields 9,960 lbs. per acre. It generates a net profit of $1,023.00 per acre and requires 185 hours of labor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a6d072f41d662cb326c1c528ac3c5c51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
