    </node>
    <node id="Diversity">
      <data key="d0">Diversity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core farming principle stating that a successful farm should raise a variety of both crops and livestock. This approach provides economic stability by ensuring there are different products to sell throughout the year, especially if the market for one product drops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Enterprises">
      <data key="d0">Farm Enterprises</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The individual operational units of a farm, such as a specific crop or livestock type. The text's main argument is that these enterprises must be chosen and managed based on the principles of diversity and sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Economic Stability">
      <data key="d0">Economic Stability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary benefit achieved through the principle of farm diversity. It is the result of having multiple income sources from different enterprises that can buffer against market fluctuations for any single product.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integrated Farming Systems">
      <data key="d0">Integrated Farming Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept described under the Diversity principle where different farm enterprises support each other, such as using livestock waste to fertilize crops or using crop land for grazing, thereby strengthening the farm's overall system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YMCA">
      <data key="d0">YMCA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization that Will Allen collaborated with on a youth garden training project in 1995, which marked the beginning of the urban farm that would become Growing Power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Twenty-first Century">
      <data key="d0">Twenty-first Century</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The current historical period, characterized by a new sensibility for urban life that involves revitalizing cities by reintroducing nature, such as planting trees, 'daylighting' streams, creating greenways, and re-establishing local food systems like urban farms and farmers' markets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Resilience">
      <data key="d0">Community Resilience</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A community's capacity to withstand and recover from various shocks, such as economic crises or natural disasters. The text argues that food security, supported by heritage foods, is at the core of this resilience.&lt;SEP&gt;A quality that enables a community's economy, ecosystem, and social fabric to thrive in the face of expected and unexpected events. The text posits that food initiatives are a key strategy for building this resilience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Post-Katrina">
      <data key="d0">Post-Katrina</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The period following Hurricane Katrina in New Orleans, which served as a catalyst for the creation of the New Orleans Food and Farm Network and a broader focus on using local food systems to build community resilience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Farms">
      <data key="d0">Urban Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of agriculture that Greensgrow exemplifies. A central theme is Mary Corboy's mission to change how people, including rural communities and state authorities, perceive urban farms and to have them recognized as 'real' farms.&lt;SEP&gt;Farms located within cities or towns. The text presents them as a growing feature of the urban landscape, contributing to food system resilience, community education, and connection with nature, although not expected to feed a community entirely.&lt;SEP&gt;Urban Farms are farms located within cities. The text focuses on the establishment of Gladheart Farms as a case study for the opportunities and regulatory challenges faced by urban farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e143d750d8e9b45fec3f4d1a9ec2a04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CSA Market-basket Program">
      <data key="d0">CSA Market-basket Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A food distribution model where consumers receive regular shares of produce from a farm or cooperative. Growing Power operates such a program to deliver food to restaurants, schools, and institutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maryland">
      <data key="d0">Maryland</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Maryland is a U.S. state where The Capital Meat Company sources whole animals from local farms. It is also the state from which the first loyal customers who inspired the Polyface Buying Club originated. Additionally, Will Allen, the founder of the organization Growing Power, grew up on a farm in Maryland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab7679e841cce082d6f5f864896c9afe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oak Creek">
      <data key="d0">Oak Creek</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A suburb of Milwaukee where Will Allen commercially farmed on family land after his basketball and corporate careers, before he purchased the land for Growing Power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf60421969007759eb279ddb028ec67b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-input System">
      <data key="d0">Low-input System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural model where sustainable crops and livestock can thrive with minimal external inputs like expensive feed or medicine, reducing operational costs. The Katahdin sheep are an example of an enterprise suited to this system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intensive Grazing">
      <data key="d0">Intensive Grazing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A highly management-intensive practice mentioned as a method to increase land productivity by grazing multiple livestock species (such as cattle, sheep, and goats) on the same pasture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Free-Range Poultry and Pasture Hogs">
      <data key="d0">Free-Range Poultry and Pasture Hogs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Livestock systems predicted to become the norm in agriculture. Their value comes from their sustainable properties (low inputs, easy management) and their appeal to consumers seeking safe, high-quality food from transparent sources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ruth Stout">
      <data key="d0">Ruth Stout</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A writer known for her 'no-work gardens' technique. She is cited as an example of successful, large-scale vegetable production using minimal equipment by mulching the ground to prepare it for planting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Draft Animals">
      <data key="d0">Draft Animals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Draft Animals are a category of work animals, such as horses, mules, and oxen, used to provide labor and animal-based power for tasks like farming and logging. Described as the original "machinery" on farms, they are presented as a currently viable, economical, and sustainable alternative to tractors, particularly for small farms ranging from 2 to 80 acres. The use, selection, and care of these animals are central to the practice, which fits well within the sustainable agriculture model. While using draft animals requires a learning curve and an enjoyment of working with them, the topic is the specific focus of periodicals like 'Rural Heritage' and 'Small Farmer's Journal'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2920f4362eb05a4692fa915ca812b425&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Amish">
      <data key="d0">The Amish</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An agrarian society cited as a successful example of a community that uses draft animals for farming, demonstrating that non-mechanized, sustainable farming can be a successful model.&lt;SEP&gt;An agrarian society of over 100,000 people in North America that successfully uses traditional farming methods, primarily with draft animals. Their lifestyle is guided by values of tradition, nature, and sacrifice, leading to smaller farms (around 50 acres), a slower pace of life, and low input costs. They are presented as a case study in successful non-industrial farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="My Farm">
      <data key="d0">My Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The author's 80-acre farm, where he implements his sustainable, low-input agricultural principles. The farm's enterprises include raising sheep, hogs, poultry, and growing open-pollinated corn and a market garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hand Tools">
      <data key="d0">Hand Tools</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sub-category of machinery mentioned as the most basic level of equipment for small-scale farming. Examples include hoes and spades, which can be used to prepare ground for planting in lieu of larger machines.&lt;SEP&gt;Hand tools are non-powered farming instruments presented as a low-cost, precise, and environmentally friendly alternative to tractors. Examples include hoes, shovels, scythes, and wheel hoes. They are praised for giving farmers a closer perspective on their crops and soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allen">
      <data key="d0">Allen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"The founder and leader of Growing Power, described as the 'team captain.' He is a nationally recognized pioneer in the urban farming movement, using it as a tool to address issues of jobs, education, economic development, and social justice. In 2008, he was awarded a MacArthur Fellowship for his work."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jerry Kaufman">
      <data key="d0">Jerry Kaufman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jerry Kaufman is the President of the Board of Directors for Growing Power, Inc., and also acts as a representative for the organization. He advocates for nonprofits to quantify their community impact in financial terms to gain support and to diversify their income streams to avoid the "mission drift" caused by chasing grants. In his role, Kaufman speaks about the importance of gaining the trust of local residents and discusses the organization's experiences with community relations. He provides insight into the early career of Growing Power's founder, Allen, noting that the organization began not with a grand plan, but with a dedication to working with youth and cleaning up the greenhouse site. In a broader context, Jerry Kaufman's work and views are discussed in relation to social issues like homelessness and hunger, and he is also acknowledged for helping the author of the source text identify successful projects to serve as models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Erika Allen">
      <data key="d0">Erika Allen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Allen's daughter, who runs Growing Power's operations in Chicago. She shares her father's passion and views food as a powerful organizing tool for social transformation, impacting land, housing, transportation, and economics."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An individual mentioned in the index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MacArthur Foundation">
      <data key="d0">MacArthur Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A prestigious foundation that awarded Allen a MacArthur Fellowship, or 'genius grant,' in 2008, recognizing his practical and holistic approach to fighting inner-city hunger."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MacArthur Fellowship">
      <data key="d0">MacArthur Fellowship</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A prestigious 'genius grant' awarded to Allen in 2008 by the MacArthur Foundation. The award recognized his dedication and originality in developing a holistic urban farming model."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Lakes WATER Institute (GLWI)">
      <data key="d0">Great Lakes WATER Institute (GLWI)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A research institute at the University of Wisconsin that collaborates with Allen to raise yellow perch, a native Lake Michigan species."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An institute at the University of Wisconsin in Madison that collaborates with Will Allen to raise yellow perch, a species native to Lake Michigan. It focuses on aquaculture and sustainable food growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fred Binkowski">
      <data key="d0">Fred Binkowski</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A scientist at the Great Lakes WATER Institute (GLWI) who works with Allen on fish farming. He views this work as part of a trend toward growing safer, better food closer to home."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A scientist affiliated with the Great Lakes WATER Institute (GLWI) who provides expert commentary on fish farming as a trend for growing safer food locally and filling consumer demand.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual mentioned in the index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shannon Sloan-Spice">
      <data key="d0">Shannon Sloan-Spice</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A writer who commented on Allen's work, highlighting his dedication to providing healthy food to low-income areas and teaching sustainability as a way of fighting economic racism."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Author of the article 'Growing Hope, Growing Change, Growing Power' in Outpost Exchange.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e0df78d09fb33ee041307abbce7d33e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tractors">
      <data key="d0">Tractors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of mechanical power source considered indispensable for most modern farms. Tractors are used for a wide range of tasks and are more efficient than draft animals on large farms (over 80 acres). They are the preferred choice for those who lack the time or temperament to work with animals.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific type of heavy machinery used in farming, listed as a prominent topic within the broader category of Machinery and Equipment.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific type of heavy machinery used in farming. The text contrasts tractors with draft animals, noting that tractors are generally faster but that a used tractor can be a good purchase for a small farm if the price and fit are right.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="No-Work Gardens">
      <data key="d0">No-Work Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gardening technique developed by Ruth Stout, cited as an example of a method that requires minimal machinery. It involves mulching the ground a year in advance to soften the soil and kill vegetation before planting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Raised-Bed Gardening">
      <data key="d0">Raised-Bed Gardening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gardening method mentioned as an alternative to conventional tilling, where beds are loosened with a spade or special tine tool. It represents another form of small-scale agriculture that may not require heavy machinery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open-Pollinated Corn">
      <data key="d0">Open-Pollinated Corn</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A crop grown by the author on his farm as part of his diversified, sustainable operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Market Garden">
      <data key="d0">Market Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the enterprises the author manages on his farm, indicating he grows vegetables for sale to customers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8955ba3c4625c18d5ced8e174a4072e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Donald B. Kraybill">
      <data key="d0">Donald B. Kraybill</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An author and expert on Amish culture, cited for his book 'The Riddle of the Amish Culture'. He documents the growth of the Amish population and analyzes their ability to thrive in an industrialized world.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Riddle of the Amish Culture">
      <data key="d0">The Riddle of the Amish Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A book written by Donald B. Kraybill which examines the history, growth, and cultural success of the Amish society. It questions how they flourish in the midst of industrialization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vertical Farming">
      <data key="d0">Vertical Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific, high-yield farming method promoted by Allen as key to the profitability of urban farms. It involves growing crops in vertically stacked layers to maximize output on a small area of land."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A farming method described as a dynamic, integrated system used at Growing Power, particularly in its multilevel aquaponics systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aquaponics">
      <data key="d0">Aquaponics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A system that combines aquaculture (fish farming) with hydroponics (growing plants in water). Growing Power uses a low-cost, vertically integrated aquaponic system to raise fish and grow cash crops like tomatoes and greens simultaneously."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A food production system used and taught by Growing Power. The organization has evolved its methods from independent systems to integrated, multilevel, vertically integrated systems that combine aquaculture (raising aquatic animals) with hydroponics (cultivating plants in water).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Milwaukee's North Side Neighborhoods">
      <data key="d0">Milwaukee's North Side Neighborhoods</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The urban area in Milwaukee where Will Allen began his work, focusing on community engagement and teaching youth about food production."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-income Families/Areas">
      <data key="d0">Low-income Families/Areas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The primary demographic and community group that Growing Power aims to serve by providing affordable, healthy food and establishing community gardens and educational programs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Youths">
      <data key="d0">Local Youths</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key group involved with Growing Power, who staff the organization's warehouse, receive academic tutoring, and learn practical skills in farming and construction."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Economic Racism">
      <data key="d0">Economic Racism</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A systemic issue that Shannon Sloan-Spice identifies as being combated by Will Allen's work in providing food access and teaching sustainability in underserved communities."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a1f62a42d98fb088984d8c0a7a0d293</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heifer International">
      <data key="d0">Heifer International</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization that initiated Growing Power's vermicomposting (worm composting) systems in 1996 by providing the first red wiggler worms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Horse-drawn Equipment">
      <data key="d0">Horse-drawn Equipment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of farming tools and machinery designed to be powered by draft animals. The text notes that this equipment is rare but relatively cheap, contributing to the economic viability of using draft animals on small farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Horseman">
      <data key="d0">Horseman</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A role described as an experienced handler of horses. The text recommends that a novice farmer should seek assistance from a horseman when choosing draft horses and related equipment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oxen Handler (Drover)">
      <data key="d0">Oxen Handler (Drover)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A role described as an experienced handler of oxen. The text suggests enlisting the help of a drover for guidance when selecting oxen and equipment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Teamster">
      <data key="d0">Teamster</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An experienced driver or handler of a team of draft animals. The text highlights the importance of learning from an experienced teamster to overcome the steep learning curve associated with using draft animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steve Salt">
      <data key="d0">Steve Salt</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Steve Salt is a farmer who provides guidelines on the appropriate scale of tool and equipment usage for small farms, ranging from human-powered tools for small plots to tractors for larger acreages."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Steve Salt is a diversified market gardener and frequent contributor to Small Farm Today magazine, who lives on a 147-acre farm near Yarrow, Missouri. He primarily uses hand tools to grow vegetables, herbs, and small fruits, specializing in sweet sorghum and about 300 varieties of ethnic and heirloom vegetables. He sells his produce at farmers' markets, boards rodeo stock, and is writing a book on ethnic vegetables.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b34a5b7dc86e95b65f36512f72980b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yarrow">
      <data key="d0">Yarrow</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Yarrow is a location in northern Missouri where farmer Steve Salt and his family live.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tractor">
      <data key="d0">Tractor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key piece of farm equipment used as a primary example for calculating ownership and operating costs per acre. The text discusses its maintenance, life expectancy, and economic considerations for purchase.&lt;SEP&gt;A key piece of farm machinery used as the primary example for discussing equipment costs, lifespan (approx. 12,000 hours), maintenance, and purchasing decisions within the context of farm management.&lt;SEP&gt;A tractor is a type of farm vehicle. The text advises that a 30- to 50-horsepower tractor with a three-point hitch is suitable for most small farms and emphasizes the safety of wide-front-end models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tractor Implements">
      <data key="d0">Tractor Implements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tractor implements are tools attached to a tractor for specific jobs like tillage, planting, and cultivation. They are rated by category (e.g., Category I for smaller tractors) and include items like plows, seeders, mowers, and balers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biodigester">
      <data key="d0">Biodigester</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A system planned for use by Growing Power that converts organic waste into methane gas using microorganisms, which can then be used to generate electricity.&lt;SEP&gt;A system that breaks down organic material to produce biogas, listed as a topic.&lt;SEP&gt;A technology that Growing Power is experimenting with to make urban farming more energy-efficient. It's an airtight container that uses bacteria to convert organic waste (like food scraps) into biogas (methane), which can then be used to generate electricity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lakefront Brewery">
      <data key="d0">Lakefront Brewery</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local brewery in Wisconsin that is a community partner of Growing Power. It provides 20,000 pounds of brewery wastes each week to be used in the composting process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Wisconsin–Milwaukee">
      <data key="d0">University of Wisconsin–Milwaukee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A university that is a community partner of Growing Power, providing food waste from its residence halls for the organization's composting operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow Perch">
      <data key="d0">Yellow Perch</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A fish species native to Lake Michigan that has experienced a significant population decline. Growing Power, in collaboration with the Great Lakes WATER Institute, is raising this species as part of its aquaculture program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ethnic and Heirloom Vegetables">
      <data key="d0">Ethnic and Heirloom Vegetables</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ethnic and heirloom vegetables are a specialized category of crops grown by Steve Salt. He grows approximately 300 varieties, which are popular with his Hispanic and Asian customers at farmers' markets. He is also writing a book on this topic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="County and State Health Departments">
      <data key="d0">County and State Health Departments</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"County and State Health Departments are governmental bodies that regulate at-home animal slaughter. They provide information on the allowed amounts and sale conditions for processing animals like poultry."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;County and state health departments are governmental bodies responsible for providing information and setting regulations on the at-home processing and sale of poultry and rabbits, with rules varying by state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hispanic Customers">
      <data key="d0">Hispanic Customers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific customer demographic mentioned in the text who purchase produce from Steve Salt at farmers' markets. They particularly favor his sixty varieties of tomatoes and peppers, as well as Mexican herbs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asian Customers">
      <data key="d0">Asian Customers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific customer demographic mentioned in the text who purchase produce from Steve Salt at farmers' markets. They are noted as being fond of his Chinese greens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Motors">
      <data key="d0">Small Motors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of farm equipment representing a step up from hand tools. This includes two-wheeled tractors and walk-behind tillers, typically ranging from 5 to 20 horsepower, and are considered an excellent investment for small-scale market gardeners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Herbs">
      <data key="d0">Herbs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Herbs are a class of plants grown for culinary, medicinal, or other purposes. They are a crop category, with one example being the Mexican herbs grown by Steve Salt for his Hispanic customers. As a product category, information about herbs can be found through dedicated organizations like the Herb Growing and Marketing Network and the Herb Research Foundation. Further resources include specialized directories such as The Herbal Green Pages, suppliers like Richters Herbs, and books like the "A-Z Guide to Growing Organic Food," which covers 765 varieties of plants, including herbs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c07830327394a77dc9e8ac1a53acd63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-516da735be61616733781bd48e941fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rodeo Stock (Horses)">
      <data key="d0">Rodeo Stock (Horses)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of animal that Steve Salt boards on his farm as a sideline business. He cares for and feeds about eighty head of these horses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large Stock">
      <data key="d0">Large Stock</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of farm animals including beef, hogs, sheep, goats, bison, and elk. The text states that their processing for public sale must be done in a government-inspected plant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vermicomposting">
      <data key="d0">Vermicomposting</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific composting method using worms, which is a key soil-building technique at Growing Power. The text notes that letting the worms do the work reduces odors compared to frequent turning.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific method of composting that uses worms to break down organic matter. Growing Power's vermicomposting system was initiated by Heifer International and utilizes red worms to create nutrient-rich fertilizer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Worms">
      <data key="d0">Red Worms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific species of worm (Eisenia foetida), also known as red wigglers, used by Growing Power in its vermicomposting systems to process organic waste into castings (fertilizer).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biogas">
      <data key="d0">Biogas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A mixture of methane and carbon dioxide that is the product of a biodigester. Growing Power is experimenting with creating biogas from food waste to generate electricity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southeast">
      <data key="d0">Southeast</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical region in the United States where the Rainbow Farmer's Cooperative has expanded its network of small-scale farmers and sellers, in addition to the Midwest.&lt;SEP&gt;The Southeast is a geographical region in the United States for which the Southern Seed Exposure Exchange specializes in saving suitable heirloom seeds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Children's Hospital">
      <data key="d0">Children's Hospital</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local partner of Growing Power that provides a steady stream of food waste materials for the organization's large-scale composting initiatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Poultry and Rabbits">
      <data key="d0">Poultry and Rabbits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of farm animals including chickens, turkeys, ducks, emus, and rabbits. The text notes that they can often be processed at home for sale, subject to state and county regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d427f43ffc4c26161ccd06730b43ec9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Specialized Machinery">
      <data key="d0">Specialized Machinery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Specialized Machinery includes expensive equipment like hay balers, hay wrappers, and combines. The text suggests that these are often too costly for small operations and should be rented or borrowed rather than purchased."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Livestock Handling Equipment">
      <data key="d0">Livestock Handling Equipment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Livestock Handling Equipment is a category of tools and structures like loading chutes, headgates, and corrals. Their purpose is to ease the task of transferring and managing livestock, thus reducing labor and costs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barbed Wire Fence">
      <data key="d0">Barbed Wire Fence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Barbed Wire Fence is a specific type of fencing whose construction is detailed in a step-by-step guide. It is used to contain livestock and protect against predators like dogs and coyotes."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barbed Wire Fence Construction">
      <data key="d0">Barbed Wire Fence Construction</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"This is a detailed, multi-step process outlined in the text for building a barbed wire fence. The steps include clearing the area, setting corner and line posts, installing braces, and stretching the wires."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dogs and Coyotes">
      <data key="d0">Dogs and Coyotes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Dogs and coyotes are identified as predators. The instructions for building a barbed wire fence include adding a low wire specifically to help keep them out."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Killing Cone">
      <data key="d0">Killing Cone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A killing cone is a specific piece of equipment used in poultry processing to hold the bird still during slaughter, which prevents wing flapping from bruising the meat."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scalder">
      <data key="d0">Scalder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A scalder is a piece of equipment used in poultry processing. It is a tank of hot water used to loosen the feathers of a bird to make plucking easier."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rockwell Automation">
      <data key="d0">Rockwell Automation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local business partner that supplies Growing Power with food waste, which is then used to create compost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maglio Produce Wholesaler">
      <data key="d0">Maglio Produce Wholesaler</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local produce wholesaler that partners with Growing Power by providing food waste for its composting program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alterra Coffee">
      <data key="d0">Alterra Coffee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local coffee company that provides Growing Power with 300 pounds of used coffee grounds each week for its compost mixture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stone Creek Coffee">
      <data key="d0">Stone Creek Coffee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A local coffee company that partners with Growing Power by providing coffee grounds weekly for its composting operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa54079f022f9b869fe9a285cb16fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Food Systems">
      <data key="d0">Community Food Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary theme and goal of Growing Power's work. It refers to the network of suppliers, producers, institutions, and community members involved in a local food economy, which is seen as the ultimate outcome of a successful urban farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Growing Power workshops">
      <data key="d0">Growing Power workshops</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Educational events hosted by Growing Power where Will Allen and his team teach participants from diverse backgrounds about various topics, including urban gardening, renewable energy, aquaponics, and cheesemaking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greensgrow">
      <data key="d0">Greensgrow</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Greensgrow is a prominent urban farm and organization located in Philadelphia's Kensington neighborhood, established on a former Superfund site. Founded by Mary Seton Corboy (who also serves as CEO) and Sereduk, the farm is cited as a successful example of urban agriculture due to its readily available local market, community trust, and emphasis on transparency.

Greensgrow employs diverse growing methods, including hydroponics and raised beds, to cultivate a variety of crops. Its diversified business model includes a garden nursery, a Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) program, and a farmers' market where it sells honey from its own beehives and other products. To improve food access, the organization accepts FMNP coupons and created the "New LIFE Program" (also known as the Philly LIFE program) to offer healthy food options to the community.

A key focus for Greensgrow is sustainability and community engagement. The farm demonstrates this through its biodiesel production operation and its active outreach to neighbors, which includes testing water runoff to address local concerns. Functioning as an educational "outpost," Greensgrow aims to connect city dwellers with their food through "agri-edu-tainment," educational workshops, and by publishing its "Lessons Learned" online, all part of its broader mission to change the public's perception of urban farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6fd2b31420a632f31dfe7120da040d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e143d750d8e9b45fec3f4d1a9ec2a04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e0df78d09fb33ee041307abbce7d33e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d076831b7a91bcd605c1e387112d81e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82a428e9ecd2ac447a8293c4680becb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Milwaukee's North Side">
      <data key="d0">Milwaukee's North Side</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific, modest neighborhood in Milwaukee where Growing Power's main farm and composting operation is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plucker">
      <data key="d0">Plucker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A plucker is a machine used to remove feathers from poultry after they have been through a scalder, automating a key step in the processing."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wrapping Machine">
      <data key="d0">Wrapping Machine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A wrapping machine is a piece of equipment that becomes necessary if a farm begins selling processed birds to stores, as it is used for packaging the final product."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perimeter Fence">
      <data key="d0">Perimeter Fence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A perimeter fence is the fence built along the property lines of a farm. It is described as needing to be robust enough to hold any type of livestock and to keep neighbors' animals out."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lanes">
      <data key="d0">Lanes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Lanes are fenced corridors within a farm. They are designed to ease the movement of livestock and equipment between different fields and pastures."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrical Fencing">
      <data key="d0">Electrical Fencing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Electrical fencing is described as an economical and valuable type of temporary fencing, particularly useful for intensive grazing management and for protecting grain crops."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Woven Wire">
      <data key="d0">Woven Wire</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Woven wire is a type of fencing material recommended for building durable perimeter fences on most farms to contain livestock."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barbed Wire">
      <data key="d0">Barbed Wire</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Barbed wire is a fencing material consisting of twisted strands of wire with sharp barbs. It is recommended for perimeter and interior fences and is the subject of a detailed construction guide."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab58b310c0ccf38821b52ac8254aeaf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
