    <node id="Healthy Soil">
      <data key="d0">Healthy Soil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A foundational concept in Will Allen's philosophy, considered the most critical element for growing healthy food. It is created through methods like composting and vermicomposting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Youth Corps">
      <data key="d0">Youth Corps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A program component of Growing Power that involves establishing connections with local schools.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48fbe13ccbab1daf20dc4c993f911dd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gladheart Farms">
      <data key="d0">Gladheart Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Gladheart Farms is a certified-organic, urban vegetable farm in Asheville, North Carolina, located at 29 Lora Lane. Owned and operated by Michael Porterfield, the farm is situated on formerly unused, overgrown riverside property owned by the city, demonstrating a successful use of overlooked public land that was achieved through a solid business plan.

The farm has a multi-faceted business model. It operates a Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) program, which has been successfully marketed to corporate employees, and sells produce at a farm stand in a senior community. Additionally, Gladheart Farms functions as a wholesale distributor and has expanded to include a distribution center, marketing and selling products for other local farms in addition to its own.

To support its operations, the farm relies on interns and volunteers sourced from networks like WWOOF (World Wide Opportunities on Organic Farms) and a community of faith. Gladheart Farms is often cited as a success story, exemplifying a venture built on experiential learning and experimentation rather than formal education, and it has received support and connections from the organization ASAP.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d67ba0430fa075f3a4e6609250b9a01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-488bc663aa4ded0ca40ac601222c065d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e970626c7a63b367949cb5600957bcab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michael Porterfield">
      <data key="d0">Michael Porterfield</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Michael Porterfield is the owner, operator, and representative for Gladheart Farms, a 7.5-acre farm located near downtown Asheville. Supported by his wife, Michelle, and his community of faith, Porterfield established the farm and promotes a hands-on, intuitive approach to agriculture. He believes in learning through experience and from other successful farmers, advising beginners to start with easy crops to build confidence and to not be intimidated by book-learning.

As a key figure in the local farming community, Porterfield pioneered a corporate Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) model, arranging for weekly deliveries to a large manufacturer's employees, and suggests this is a promising model for other businesses. He emphasizes the importance of human resources, utilizing interns and volunteers, and stresses the need to set clear expectations and maintain a strong work ethic on the farm. He also advises that a strong business plan is essential for urban farming projects seeking to gain city support. Porterfield received significant help from the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP) with financial projections, grant applications, and business connections, and he highly values ASAP as a resource.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d67ba0430fa075f3a4e6609250b9a01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-488bc663aa4ded0ca40ac601222c065d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asheville, North Carolina">
      <data key="d0">Asheville, North Carolina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Based in North Carolina, the city of Asheville is a significant location for several agricultural and educational initiatives. It is home to Michael Porterfield's Gladheart Farms, which was established in 2006 and operates on 7.5 acres of city-owned, riverside property. The farm is also cited as an example for its reliance on interns and volunteers.

Additionally, Asheville is the base for the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP) and its associated Growing Minds program. The city served as the initial focus area for implementing local food programs in schools. Asheville, North Carolina is also the location of Warren Wilson College, which is highlighted as a primary example of a successful college farming program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04be01c6169d0bdd92b10dca615a3936&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-488bc663aa4ded0ca40ac601222c065d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e970626c7a63b367949cb5600957bcab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fee18f76430d121ab613984069f209e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project">
      <data key="d0">Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP) is a non-profit community organization founded in 1995 and centered in Asheville, North Carolina. Its primary mission is to foster a local food system by transforming the region's agriculture from tobacco to sustainable food farms. Functioning as a collaborative, ASAP connects local agricultural growers with buyers and serves as a model for successful regional food initiatives, similar to organizations like Farm Fresh Rhode Island.

To promote local products, ASAP initiated and utilizes the "Appalachian Grown" brand. The organization runs several key initiatives, including "Farm to Hospital," "The Family Farm Tour," and "Growing Minds." It also engages in community outreach, such as volunteering in schools with partners like Chef Charles Hudson to promote fresh, local food. Furthermore, ASAP provides direct support and resources to farmers, helping them with business plans and marketing tools, as it did for Michael Porterfield of Gladheart Farms. The organization also offers free downloadable publications from its website, such as "Best Practices for Accepting EBT at the Farmers Market" and "Farm Promotion and Support."

Representatives for the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project include Charlie Jackson, Peter Marks, and Emily Jackson, who has represented the organization in a statement to the U.S. Senate Committee on Agriculture, Nutrition, and Forestry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7ecda741e6aebaebfb4bbcd928bb80b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16e699b4bb77d0bdc27d883c3cafa88c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ba8e532ec9e7db3fd3049537a06db80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214f4e9d9f3d3fed74243c9bdf50bf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e3df5ce7be88ffd8bdf29e5b896da3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Environmental Protection Agency">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Environmental Protection Agency</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A U.S. government agency (EPA) that provides information on agricultural demographics through its 'Ag 101' guide."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A U.S. federal agency (EPA) that addresses environmental issues, including estimating the costs of its clean water programs.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is a federal agency that sets health and safety standards. It is mentioned for designating 400 parts per million as the harmful threshold for lead in soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214f4e9d9f3d3fed74243c9bdf50bf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
