    <node id="Wellesley College">
      <data key="d0">Wellesley College</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A private liberal arts college for women, mentioned as a relevant institution.&lt;SEP&gt;An educational institution that collaborates with The Food Project to research the use of hyperaccumulator plants to reduce lead in soil.&lt;SEP&gt;Wellesley College is an educational institution that collaborates with The Food Project on research into phytoremediation, experimenting with various plants to find a practical method for removing lead from contaminated soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb8186cf72c91110756ec67bcc379a67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roxbury and Dorchester">
      <data key="d0">Roxbury and Dorchester</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Roxbury and Dorchester are neighborhoods in Boston that are disproportionately affected by poverty and high levels of lead contamination in the soil. They are the focus of The Food Project's community and research efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Contamination">
      <data key="d0">Soil Contamination</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant challenge at the Lynchburg Grows site, which was contaminated with pesticides (like DDT and arsenic residues) and lead. Addressing this was a top priority for the founders to ensure the safety of the gardens.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil Contamination, specifically with lead, is identified as a major obstacle for urban farms. The text notes its disproportionate impact on poor and minority communities and discusses efforts to raise awareness and find remediation solutions like phytoremediation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phytoremediation">
      <data key="d0">Phytoremediation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phytoremediation is a biological soil-cleaning method being researched by The Food Project and Wellesley College. The process involves using 'hyperaccumulator' plants like mustard greens and sunflowers to absorb contaminants like lead from the soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Gap">
      <data key="d0">Training Gap</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Training Gap refers to the lack of available training for people who want to work in agriculture, a problem identified by Mary Seton Corboy as the biggest gap for urban agriculture. This issue is noted to be a challenge for both urban and rural farming initiatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626a48dbfe67b1f55d1d46c5b8d30097</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740827</data>
    </node>
