    </node>
    <node id="Michael Porterfield's Community of Faith">
      <data key="d0">Michael Porterfield's Community of Faith</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A faith-based community that provided Michael Porterfield with crucial support, including funding for investment and a core group of 10 to 15 people who assist with labor and management at Gladheart Farms."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Internships">
      <data key="d0">Internships</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A program offered by Gladheart Farms to provide hands-on experience in organic farming. Interns are sourced through networks like WWOOF and a community of faith, and clear, demanding work expectations are set for them."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A form of work experience or training program, linked in the text as a relevant activity for 'teens' and 'youth'.&lt;SEP&gt;Work experience opportunities, often for students, linked as a 'See also' for 'college students'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Filters">
      <data key="d0">Filters</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A consumable component of machinery that must be changed regularly, especially after harvest and during preseason start-up, as part of a preventive maintenance schedule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lubricants">
      <data key="d0">Lubricants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of materials, including oil and grease, used to maintain farm equipment. The text advises using quality lubricants from reputable suppliers and applying them to various parts to ensure smooth operation and prevent wear.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Batteries">
      <data key="d0">Batteries</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific component of farm equipment requiring maintenance. The text outlines a procedure for removing, cleaning terminals with baking soda, reinstalling, and coating them with grease to prevent corrosion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Corrosion">
      <data key="d0">Corrosion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A detrimental chemical process (rust) that can damage metal components of farm equipment. The text describes several preventive measures, such as cleaning residual crops, painting, and greasing terminals, to combat corrosion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interest Rate">
      <data key="d0">Interest Rate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A financial factor that directly influences the ownership costs of farm equipment. The text states that for every 1% increase in the interest rate, ownership costs increase by one-half of 1 percent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Preseason Start-Up">
      <data key="d0">Preseason Start-Up</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical period before the growing season begins, involving a series of checks and maintenance tasks on equipment, such as inspecting hoses for leaks, changing winter oil, and replacing filters to ensure readiness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plow">
      <data key="d0">Plow</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farm machinery for which ownership costs are provided (14% for new, 17% for used). Custom rates for plowing are also listed as an example from Missouri.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Planter">
      <data key="d0">Planter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A piece of farm machinery with specified ownership costs (14% for new, 17% for used). Maintenance tasks like greasing press wheels and clutch parts are mentioned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liability Forms">
      <data key="d0">Liability Forms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A legal document that organizations like Lynchburg Grows insist all volunteers sign. It clarifies risks and potential liabilities, protecting the organization from financial crises in case of accidents."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Volunteer Program Manual">
      <data key="d0">Volunteer Program Manual</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An invaluable, detailed guide created by The Food Project in Boston for recruiting and working effectively with volunteers. It is available for free on their website and contains numerous key lessons."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The 'Volunteer Program Manual' is a guide written by Greg Gale for The Food Project. It provides guidance on recruiting and working with volunteers and is available as a free PDF.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4daf18984831b8673c2be0060612bd34</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Porterfield">
      <data key="d0">Porterfield</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Porterfield is the founder of Gladheart Farms, an urban farm in Asheville. He is a businessman with previous experience in an organic tea business and is focused on making his farm a self-supporting, profitable enterprise. He successfully navigated city regulations to lease land and has expanded his business into wholesale distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP)">
      <data key="d0">Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP) is a non-profit community partner organization based in Asheville, North Carolina, dedicated to promoting and supporting sustainable agriculture and the local food system, primarily within the western North Carolina and southern Appalachian regions. Founded by its director, Charlie Jackson, in 1995, the organization has a long and successful track record of building local food economies and operates on a budget of over $1 million.

ASAP's work focuses on supporting small-scale farmers by creating local markets, developing farmers' markets, and connecting farmers with distributors like Carolina Produce and various customers. It provides direct assistance to farmers with services such as business planning, marketing, and negotiation support. A key strategic focus for the organization is supporting the next generation of farmers, specifically by encouraging farm heirs to return to farming.

The organization runs several programs, including the "Growing Minds" farm-to-school program, which connects local farms with school systems and involves volunteers like Chef Hudson. Other initiatives include the "Appalachian Grown" certification process, a farm-to-hospital program, and Project EMMA for seniors. To promote its mission, ASAP conducts community food system assessments and utilizes various marketing tools, such as publishing a Local Food Guide, distributing bumper stickers, and using the mantra, "Local Food: Thousands of Miles Fresher."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16c3fe8c089550fd16987a1c4f301a2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c38b37fe24e15bfa43a713c9e38509&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fee18f76430d121ab613984069f209e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5944a88ca415780f3d4838a47d068512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c16ea96bc625614680cc6d8276e6ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peter Marks">
      <data key="d0">Peter Marks</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, here is a comprehensive summary:

Peter Marks is the program director at the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP), where he works with his colleague Charlie Jackson. With a background in small business development, his role involves helping new and existing farmers build their business capacity. Marks is an expert who argues that food can serve as a profitable pathway for economic development, especially when comprehensive strategies are used to connect local farms with small businesses. He has provided direct assistance to farmers, such as helping Michael Porterfield write a business plan and develop marketing tools. Marks comments that ASAP's strategy builds upon the work of established farm-support groups like the Farm Bureau and Future Farmers of America. Despite his expertise, he maintains a humble, learning-oriented perspective on the complexity of the food system, stating that the organization is "still really beginners at understanding the distribution of food."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c38b37fe24e15bfa43a713c9e38509&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fee18f76430d121ab613984069f209e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d67ba0430fa075f3a4e6609250b9a01</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asheville">
      <data key="d0">Asheville</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Asheville is a city in western North Carolina that serves as the central hub for the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP) and is the geographic center of the "Appalachian Grown" 100-mile region. The city promotes its vibrant local food scene for economic development and tourism, marketing itself as "Foodtopia." It is home to the Asheville City Market, which is managed by ASAP, and is a key market for products from local farms like Hickory Nut Gap Farm. Demonstrating support for urban agriculture, the Asheville city council passed a resolution and leased unused property to establish Gladheart Farms within the city. This supportive environment has contributed to an increase in local farms serving schools, which in turn allowed programs like Growing Minds to engage with the larger county school system. Despite this thriving food scene, the limited pastureland available for lease in the Asheville area presents a challenge, influencing the strategic decisions of local farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7ecda741e6aebaebfb4bbcd928bb80b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5944a88ca415780f3d4838a47d068512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c8f3e81fd6708a9852f46f0d4ac0a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d67ba0430fa075f3a4e6609250b9a01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c16ea96bc625614680cc6d8276e6ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asheville City Council">
      <data key="d0">Asheville City Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Asheville City Council is the governing body of the city of Asheville. It unanimously passed a resolution to allow unused city land, like the property leased by Porterfield, to be used for farming organic vegetables.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fuel">
      <data key="d0">Fuel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary component of operating (variable) costs for farm machinery like tractors. It is described as a cost incurred every time the equipment is used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d2c2f40ce23609da02bb09b06c26efd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equipment Costs">
      <data key="d0">Equipment Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary subject of the document, detailing a methodology for calculating the total cost of machinery. It is broken down into fixed and variable costs, with specific formulas provided for each component.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fixed Costs">
      <data key="d0">Fixed Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A component of total machinery expenses that do not vary with usage. The document provides formulas to calculate fixed costs based on depreciation, insurance, housing, and interest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variable Costs">
      <data key="d0">Variable Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A component of total machinery expenses that vary with usage. The document provides formulas to calculate variable costs based on repairs, fuel, and lubrication.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Charles DeCourley">
      <data key="d0">Charles DeCourley</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-author of the publication 'Selected Fruit and Vegetable Planning Budgets', which is cited as the source for the cost calculation methodology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kevin Moore">
      <data key="d0">Kevin Moore</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-author of the publication 'Selected Fruit and Vegetable Planning Budgets', which is cited as the source for the cost calculation methodology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Missouri-Columbia, Department of Agricultural Economics">
      <data key="d0">University of Missouri-Columbia, Department of Agricultural Economics</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An academic institution and department credited with publishing the 'Selected Fruit and Vegetable Planning Budgets' in 1987, the source material adapted for the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wendell Bower">
      <data key="d0">Wendell Bower</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Author of the book 'Modern Concepts of Farm Machinery Management', cited as an expert on the operational costs and life cycle of farm machinery.&lt;SEP&gt;Wendell Bower is the author of the book 'Modern Concepts of Farm Machinery Management' and provides expert advice on when to trade farm tractors based on operational costs and usage hours.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swannanoa River">
      <data key="d0">Swannanoa River</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Swannanoa River is a river in the Asheville area. Porterfield was able to lease prime bottomland property adjacent to the river for his farm expansion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gladheart Farms Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Gladheart Farms Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Gladheart Farms Distribution is the wholesale distribution arm of Gladheart Farms, established by Porterfield. It operates from a converted bank and warehouse, distributing produce from Gladheart Farms and other farms across the country to support the organic wholesale market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="western North Carolina">
      <data key="d0">western North Carolina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A mountainous region where Asheville is located. The text notes that organic cultivation can be challenging in this area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wholesaling">
      <data key="d0">Wholesaling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The business practice of selling goods in large quantities, typically for resale. Listed as a topic in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Wholesaling is a business practice of selling goods in large quantities to retailers or other businesses rather than directly to consumers. It became the primary business model for Gladheart Farms, accounting for 95% of its sales.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tailgate Markets">
      <data key="d0">Tailgate Markets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of informal market where vendors, often farmers, sell goods directly from their vehicles.&lt;SEP&gt;Tailgate Markets are a type of direct-to-consumer sales channel where farmers sell produce from their vehicles. Gladheart Farms operates a highly successful tailgate market at an assisted living community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Faith Community">
      <data key="d0">Faith Community</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An unnamed faith community that provided Porterfield with crucial startup support, including funding for investment and 10 to 15 people for labor and management. This community had also supported his previous organic tea business.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Porterfield's Former Organic Tea Business">
      <data key="d0">Porterfield's Former Organic Tea Business</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An enterprise previously run by Porterfield, which was supported by his faith community and provided him with solid business experience before he started Gladheart Farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Auction">
      <data key="d0">Auction</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An event mentioned as a place to buy used farm equipment, with a note of caution that competitive bidding can inflate prices beyond their actual value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Growing Season">
      <data key="d0">Growing Season</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The period of agricultural activity during which preventive maintenance is emphasized as a critical and controllable aspect of farming operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oil and Hydraulic Fluid Analyses">
      <data key="d0">Oil and Hydraulic Fluid Analyses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic practice recommended to prolong equipment life by enabling the early detection of abnormal wear in farm machinery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buying Used Machinery">
      <data key="d0">Buying Used Machinery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A strategy discussed in the text for keeping equipment costs down. It is presented as a potentially wise investment if the equipment's age and usage are carefully considered.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tractor Dealerships">
      <data key="d0">Tractor Dealerships</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specified channel for purchasing both new and used farm equipment, noted for potentially offering warranties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Newspapers">
      <data key="d0">Local Newspapers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A media source mentioned as a place to find farm equipment listed for sale directly by the owner.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Magazines">
      <data key="d0">Farm Magazines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of publication mentioned as a place to find farm equipment listed for sale directly by the owner.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modern Concepts of Farm Machinery Management">
      <data key="d0">Modern Concepts of Farm Machinery Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A book authored by Wendell Bower that provides guidance on farm machinery management, specifically advocating for trading tractors after about 10 years or 6,000 to 7,000 hours of use to avoid rising repair costs.&lt;SEP&gt;A book written by Wendell Bower, cited for its analysis of the operational costs of a new tractor over its life, particularly the high cost in its first year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Food Market">
      <data key="d0">Local Food Market</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The strong demand within the local food market in the Asheville area was a primary driver for Porterfield's success and his decision to expand his farm operations. He noted he could have sold 'ten times more' in his first year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unused City Land">
      <data key="d0">Unused City Land</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This refers to the derelict, unused property owned by the city of Asheville, specifically prime bottomland along the Swannanoa River, which Porterfield successfully leased and restored for agricultural use by Gladheart Farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Land Lease Negotiation Process">
      <data key="d0">Land Lease Negotiation Process</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A six-month period described as a 'baptism by fire' during which Porterfield had to navigate city ordinances, regulations, and multijurisdictional decision-making to secure the lease for the city-owned land.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ordinances And Regulations">
      <data key="d0">Ordinances And Regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of legal and bureaucratic rules that Porterfield had to navigate to establish his farm on city land. Specific examples included a soil and water erosion ordinance, wetlands regulations, and a requirement for an endangered species study.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Farming Resolution">
      <data key="d0">Urban Farming Resolution</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A resolution unanimously passed by the Asheville City Council that officially permitted unused city land to be farmed for organic vegetables. This was a direct result of Porterfield's efforts and opened the door for other urban farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CSA (Community Supported Agriculture)">
      <data key="d0">CSA (Community Supported Agriculture)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) is a food production, distribution, and sales model where consumers buy "shares" in a farm's harvest in advance. This direct-to-consumer business model connects farmers with members, who then receive a portion of the produce, often on a weekly basis during the growing season, which can run from May through October.

CSAs are used by a variety of farms and organizations. For some, like Appalachian Sustainable Development, a CSA can be an initial, small-scale channel for selling organic produce, while for others, such as Gladheart Farms, it is a main sales channel for its 50 different varieties of vegetables. The model is scalable, ranging from the 120 shares delivered by Lynchburg Grows to being a key activity for The Intervale in Vermont, which hosts one of the largest CSAs in the state.

Beyond being a source of revenue, CSAs serve multiple functions. They create specific operational needs for farms, such as the walk-in cooler required by Greensgrow Farm. They can also have a community focus; The Food Project uses its CSA as a management opportunity for interns and an educational tool for teens, while Lynchburg Grows provides "scholarship" shares to community members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Assisted Living Community">
      <data key="d0">Assisted Living Community</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A senior retirement community that hosts a highly successful farm stand for Gladheart Farms. They preferred to deal with a single farmer, and the relationship was brokered by ASAP.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Stand">
      <data key="d0">Farm Stand</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A direct sales outlet, specifically a small stand set up by Gladheart Farms at a local assisted living community. It is described as being more profitable in two hours than a full day at other markets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Truck Farm">
      <data key="d0">Truck Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of small-scale vegetable farm mentioned as an example of an operation that has specific equipment needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crop and Livestock Farm">
      <data key="d0">Crop and Livestock Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farm mentioned as an example of an operation that has specific equipment needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hay Farm">
      <data key="d0">Hay Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farm mentioned as an example of an operation that has specific equipment needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8cfe86ed3c97ccb7c9629ee2dac08b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swine Handbook of Housing and Equipment">
      <data key="d0">Swine Handbook of Housing and Equipment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Swine Handbook of Housing and Equipment is a reference text cited for providing data on swine manure production, which is used in the author's example of nutrient cycling and soil fertility management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dealers and Auctioneers">
      <data key="d0">Dealers and Auctioneers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Businesses or individuals who sell farm equipment. The text advises farmers to seek out reputable dealers and auctioneers and to check their reputation with fellow farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrient and Usage Cycles">
      <data key="d0">Nutrient and Usage Cycles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sustainable farming practice where resources are repeatedly reused. The text provides a detailed example of using hogs to till land and provide manure, which is then followed by a cover crop to enrich the soil for a subsequent vegetable crop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hogs/Pigs">
      <data key="d0">Hogs/Pigs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Livestock used as a detailed example of a nutrient and usage cycle. They are used to till pasture, eat weeds and grubs, and provide a significant amount of manure (nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium) to fertilize the soil for a future vegetable crop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dairy Farms">
      <data key="d0">Dairy Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farming operation noted as being labor-intensive. The text suggests that management strategies like grass-based dairies and seasonal dairying can greatly reduce labor needs while maintaining adequate income.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cdeba8bee9942d43ed93441dcd109d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distributors">
      <data key="d0">Distributors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of business partners that the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project connected Porterfield with. Gladheart Farms Distribution sells produce wholesale to these entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wholesale Customers">
      <data key="d0">Wholesale Customers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary buyers for Gladheart Farms' produce, accounting for 95% of its business in 2009. This category includes restaurants, groceries, and catered events.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Organic Farms">
      <data key="d0">National Organic Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Farms located across the country that Gladheart Farms Distribution works with to source organic products that cannot be grown locally, such as pineapples, bananas, and citrus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ecc7c9cb359df6578917b9d87ffcd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Edwin Marty">
      <data key="d0">Edwin Marty</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Edwin Marty is the co-founder and executive director of Jones Valley Urban Farm (JVUF), located in Knoxville, Tennessee. With a background in agroecology from the University of California at Santa Cruz, he started JVUF to use urban farming as an educational tool. In addition to his primary roles, Marty has also served as a representative for Kelly Elementary School and is associated with The Intervale.

Drawing from his experience, Marty emphasizes several key aspects of running a successful urban farm. He believes a Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) model holds the "single biggest potential" for achieving financial stability. He also stresses the importance of paying a sufficient living wage and providing benefits to retain key staff, like a farm manager. Furthermore, Marty discusses the strategic challenges involved in determining the correct economy of scale for an urban farm and the decisions related to marketing products to different types of stores.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9297eb734555e7b850045590e4fc5cb6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jones Valley Urban Farm">
      <data key="d0">Jones Valley Urban Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, here is a comprehensive summary:

Jones Valley Urban Farm (JVUF) is a non-profit urban farming organization located in Birmingham, Alabama, with a farm situated at 701 25th Street North. Co-founded by Page Allison and Edwin Marty, who also serves as the Executive Director, the organization's representatives include Bryding Adams and Rachel Reinhart.

The farm has transformed over three acres in downtown Birmingham, operating on a main city block leased for a nominal fee as well as other abandoned city lots. It grows organic produce which it distributes through multiple channels, including farmers' markets, local restaurants, grocery stores, and a Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) model, which is seen as a key to its financial stability.

A core part of its mission is education; JVUF provides the community with extensive food and garden-based educational programs on sustainable agriculture and nutrition, such as its ASAP program and Weekend Workshops. The organization is also used as an example for its sound business practices, having learned the importance of written land-use agreements and improved staff retention by committing to paying a living wage, notably by once increasing a farm manager's salary by $10,000 and adding health insurance. Despite its successes, the farm also grapples with strategic challenges concerning its size, production scale, and marketing approach to balance revenue with market reach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c575c019780e689390cd685c690159f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9297eb734555e7b850045590e4fc5cb6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amy Ager">
      <data key="d0">Amy Ager</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Amy Ager is the co-founder, co-owner, and a key figure at Hickory Nut Gap Farm, a fifth-generation farm she runs with her husband and business partner, Jamie Ager. The couple co-wrote the initial business plan for the farm after Amy attended Warren Wilson College, where she gained experience in marketing local food. As part of her role, she co-leases the farm and co-runs the associated business, Hickory Nut Gap Meats, guided by a sustainable farming ethic focused on restoring natural efficiencies.

Ager is a prominent advocate for a business philosophy that directly opposes the "get big or get out" adage, believing that a farm's success is determined by creativity and not size. She encourages farmers to identify and build on their "unfair advantage" and to implement a diversified business plan to ensure long-term stability. She also provides advice to those in livestock production, emphasizing the need for business savvy, including an understanding of butchery and the specific needs of wholesale customers. Additionally, Ager offers guidance on managing agritourism safely and effectively and stresses the importance of food safety through knowing the source of one's food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d53cc613c52fd5080e556f9abb248a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f712d3550c8612228f9fb4dc999ba9b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e970626c7a63b367949cb5600957bcab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c8f3e81fd6708a9852f46f0d4ac0a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e930a3eb466e9bb1dfa38b0df50bfd3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hickory Nut Gap Farm">
      <data key="d0">Hickory Nut Gap Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Hickory Nut Gap Farm is a 350-acre, fifth-generation family farm located at 57 Sugar Hollow Road in Fairview, western North Carolina, operated and managed by Jamie and Amy Ager. The farm is protected by a conservation easement and serves as a model for sustainable, pasture-based animal farming that mimics natural systems and represents post-industrial agriculture. It specializes in producing grass-fed and pastured products, including beef, other meats, poultry, eggs, and produce.

As a business, Hickory Nut Gap Farm employs a diversified "three-legged stool" business plan, selling its products wholesale, to restaurants, and directly to consumers. To meet demand, it raises its own animals and also partners with other local farmers, capitalizing on its family heritage and name recognition for marketing. The farm is also a prominent example of using agritourism to diversify income, educate visitors, and build community relationships. It is connected with community initiatives such as the Durham Inner-City Gardeners (DIG) program, and the Ager family's experience is noted for highlighting the importance of business knowledge in the meat industry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4d53cc613c52fd5080e556f9abb248a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d6381862167ee0d108723409eae36d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f712d3550c8612228f9fb4dc999ba9b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e970626c7a63b367949cb5600957bcab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c8f3e81fd6708a9852f46f0d4ac0a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e930a3eb466e9bb1dfa38b0df50bfd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Western North Carolina">
      <data key="d0">Western North Carolina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Western North Carolina is a geographical region described as the lush, verdant southern gateway to the Blue Ridge Mountains. It is the location of several entities, including Warren Wilson College and Hickory Nut Gap Farm. The region is the primary area of operation for the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP) and its programs that support the local food movement. Additionally, Western North Carolina is the target region for Verve magazine, where an article by Mackensy Lunsford was published.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d6381862167ee0d108723409eae36d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c38b37fe24e15bfa43a713c9e38509&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e930a3eb466e9bb1dfa38b0df50bfd3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Missouri Extension Service">
      <data key="d0">Missouri Extension Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural service that ran a program called 'Balanced Farming' in the early 1940s, which promoted principles of resource management and soil conservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Balanced Farming">
      <data key="d0">Balanced Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A farming process and principle focused on planning and combining resources like land, labor, capital, and management to achieve optimum net income while conserving and improving soil fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World">
      <data key="d0">World</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical entity used to emphasize the universal applicability of good farming principles, which are said to work anywhere in the world.&lt;SEP&gt;A global entity used to frame the scale of the biodiversity crisis. The text notes that dozens of countries around the world have taken action against GMOs and that the 'Power of the World' is a concept in Sioux philosophy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Site-Specific Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Site-Specific Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core concept of sustainable farming, stating that agricultural practices must be tailored to the unique conditions of a farm, including its soil, climate, and financial situation, rather than following a generic recipe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrient Cycling">
      <data key="d0">Nutrient Cycling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ecological process of managing farm nutrients to maintain soil fertility. The text uses the example of feeding hay back to livestock on the farm to recycle nutrients via manure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Land Use Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Land Use Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A management principle within Balanced Farming that involves using different types of land (e.g., timber, pasture, level fields) for their most suitable and profitable purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monocultures">
      <data key="d0">Monocultures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Monocultures are a farming practice focused on a single product (e.g., all corn or all hogs). The text criticizes this practice for making farms vulnerable to price fluctuations and for depleting soil fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple-Species Grazing">
      <data key="d0">Multiple-Species Grazing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple-species grazing is a management practice where different animals (e.g., cattle, sheep, goats) graze together. It is presented as a natural and cost-effective alternative to mowing for weed control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southwestern Virginia">
      <data key="d0">Southwestern Virginia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Southwestern Virginia is the region where Appalachian Sustainable Development operates and where its farming advice is contextually applied.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Overcapitalization">
      <data key="d0">Overcapitalization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Overcapitalization is a key business pitfall for new farmers, defined as investing in expensive, unnecessary equipment. The text warns that it can ruin new farms by reducing flexibility and tying them to a specific production mode.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Economy of Scale">
      <data key="d0">Economy of Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An economic principle concerning the cost advantages that enterprises obtain due to their scale of operation, mentioned in the context of food systems."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Economy of scale is a business concept discussed as a major challenge for urban farms. It involves balancing production volume against price points and market capacity, forcing farmers to strategically decide how large they want their operation to become.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Livestock Production">
      <data key="d0">Livestock Production</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Livestock production is a farming sector that requires significant business and marketing knowledge beyond simply raising animals. Success depends on understanding the entire supply chain, from butchering to meeting the demands of wholesale clients.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of raising animals for food, which is governed by natural biological timelines and cannot be sped up like industrial manufacturing. It presents challenges for farmers trying to meet large-scale, consistent demand.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b16258e5de8192443e482a18e2beeb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Farm">
      <data key="d0">Urban Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A central concept in the text, representing farms located in cities. The document provides business strategies and warns against common pitfalls for this type of agriculture, using Greensgrow Farm and Jones Valley Urban Farm as examples.&lt;SEP&gt;A conceptual category of farm located within a city, often on re-purposed or derelict land. The text uses Greensgrow as a prime example to explore the challenges and strategies of creating a sustainable urban farm, from land acquisition and funding to business diversification.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific model of the project undertaken by the partners. They explicitly decided to turn the abandoned greenhouses into an urban farm, which is the identity and function of Lynchburg Grows.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6fd2b31420a632f31dfe7120da040d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Farmers">
      <data key="d0">New Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">New Farmers are a group that Joel Salatin and Polyface are actively trying to support. Polyface has developed several innovative, debt-free business models to help them become financially sustainable while using Polyface's land-healing farming methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary audience for the advice in the text. They are described as being susceptible to common pitfalls like overcapitalization and are advised to start small with easy crops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3699752bc52dee1358ad76a26a9c6fb9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Business Savvy">
      <data key="d0">Business Savvy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An essential skill for success in modern farming, particularly in livestock production. It includes knowledge of marketing, butchering, and the specific needs of wholesale customers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Starting Small">
      <data key="d0">Starting Small</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core business strategy recommended throughout the text. It is presented as the best way for new farmers to learn, avoid costly mistakes, and grow sustainably.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Author's Creek-Bottom Farm">
      <data key="d0">The Author's Creek-Bottom Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Author's Creek-Bottom Farm is a property the author purchased. It had been timbered for logs, and the author used the leftover treetops as a resource to cut and sell firewood, which helped make the first year's payment on the property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Firewood">
      <data key="d0">Firewood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Firewood is presented as a valuable resource that the author created from leftover treetops on a newly purchased farm. The author sold 100 cords of it for $75 a cord, demonstrating the principle of turning a perceived problem into a profitable opportunity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University Libraries">
      <data key="d0">University Libraries</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">University libraries are recommended as a source of valuable information for farmers, specifically for finding research papers and books from the period of 1910 to 1960 that cover diversified family farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extension Bulletins">
      <data key="d0">Extension Bulletins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extension bulletins, particularly from the 1910-1960 era, are cited as an important source of information on historical farming practices like diversified farms, cover crops, rotations, and multiple livestock species.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farming Magazines">
      <data key="d0">Farming Magazines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Farming magazines are recommended as a resource for farmers to stay informed on topics of interest and to find advertisements for used-book sources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Livestock (Cattle, Sheep, and Goats)">
      <data key="d0">Livestock (Cattle, Sheep, and Goats)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cattle, sheep, and goats are given as examples of animals used in multiple-species grazing. This practice helps control weeds naturally, with sheep being particularly effective, eating 90 percent of common weeds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exotic Animals (Emu and Elk)">
      <data key="d0">Exotic Animals (Emu and Elk)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emu and elk are used as an example of a misconception about diversification. The author argues that raising a single species, even an exotic one, is still a monoculture and makes the farm vulnerable to price fluctuations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heavy-Feeding Crops">
      <data key="d0">Heavy-Feeding Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Heavy-feeding crops, such as corn, are crops that use large amounts of soil nutrients (N-P-K). The text warns that continuously planting them in a monoculture depletes soil fertility and that they should be alternated with other crops in a rotation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Easy Crops">
      <data key="d0">Easy Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of agricultural products, such as hard squashes, recommended for new farmers. They are characterized by being less risky in terms of production challenges, pest issues, and perishability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcbabc72a18ceaedb14452609db7d256</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASD">
      <data key="d0">ASD</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"ASD is an organization that provides advice to farmers in Southwest Virginia, suggesting they choose crops based on their experience, soil conditions, and time commitment. They offer guidance on the difficulty and profitability of various crops like hard squashes and bell peppers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Intervale">
      <data key="d0">The Intervale</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Intervale is a major agricultural hub within Burlington, Vermont. It encompasses a wide range of activities, including one of Vermont's largest CSAs, an incubator program for new farmers, a nursery, community gardens, a retail store, and a composting facility. It is presented as a model for innovative urban agriculture."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A vibrant community farming center in Burlington, founded by Will Raap. It aims to provide 10% of Burlington's food, produces over 1 million pounds of food annually, incubates new farms and farmers, and operates a key composting operation to restore soil fertility. It is considered a catalyst for community farming initiatives nationwide.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd12c93ca420ae0dfd8107f51eec1886</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ben Epperson">
      <data key="d0">Ben Epperson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Ben Epperson is the former farm manager at Beardsley Community Farm. He advocates for new agricultural projects to collaborate with established ones in similar climates to share knowledge and solve common problems, such as volunteer recruitment and water procurement."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A representative of Beardsley Community Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southwest Virginia">
      <data key="d0">Southwest Virginia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Southwest Virginia is a rural, mountainous region that is home to the Appalachian Sustainable Development (ASD) organization and the farm cooperative Grayson Natural. The area's economy has historically been centered on tobacco, and its farmers are noted for having a reticence to new ideas due to past failed projects. In terms of agriculture, certain crops like hard squashes are relatively easy to grow in Southwest Virginia, while others, such as cucumbers and tomatoes, are considered riskier.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74e3df5ce7be88ffd8bdf29e5b896da3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efae0d6c5dabc751b9ceb56518857dff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2d3545603e988fef215052ef7f681c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Agricultural Projects">
      <data key="d0">Community Agricultural Projects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A central theme of the text, referring to initiatives like community gardens and farms. The text offers extensive advice on how to start and sustain these projects, covering topics like crop selection, collaboration, long-term planning, and soil remediation."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hard Squashes">
      <data key="d0">Hard Squashes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop identified as relatively easy to grow in Southwest Virginia, requiring little maintenance and having a long shelf life, but offering lower profits."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soil-Building Legume Crops">
      <data key="d0">Soil-Building Legume Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Soil-building legume crops are a key component of a true crop rotation. Their purpose is to improve and restore soil fertility, counteracting the effects of heavy-feeding crops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small-Grain Crops">
      <data key="d0">Small-Grain Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small-grain crops, such as wheat and oats, are mentioned as part of a crop rotation system. Planting them after a row crop can eliminate the need for plowing, thus evening out labor and machinery use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dick Raymond">
      <data key="d0">Dick Raymond</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Author of 'Joy of Gardening', who is credited with a 2-year rotation system that uses only green-manure crops without any external fertilizers or compost."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Legume Crops">
      <data key="d0">Legume Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific class of crops, such as peas, beans, and hairy vetch, that are highlighted for their crucial role in crop rotations due to their ability to fix atmospheric nitrogen in the soil, thereby enriching it for subsequent crops."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oscar Lorenz">
      <data key="d0">Oscar Lorenz</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Co-author of 'The Knotts Handbook for Vegetable Growers', which is mentioned as a comprehensive reference for information on vegetable crops."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Donald Maynard">
      <data key="d0">Donald Maynard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Co-author of 'The Knotts Handbook for Vegetable Growers', a go-to resource for details on vegetable cultivation."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Knotts Handbook for Vegetable Growers">
      <data key="d0">The Knotts Handbook for Vegetable Growers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A reference book co-authored by Oscar Lorenz and Donald Maynard, mentioned as a standby source for comprehensive lists and information about vegetable crops and their characteristics, such as nitrogen usage."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Joy of Gardening">
      <data key="d0">Joy of Gardening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A book written by Dick Raymond, from which a specific 2-year crop rotation system is described as an example of a self-sustaining plot using only green-manure crops."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cucumbers">
      <data key="d0">Cucumbers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop described as risky for the Southwest Virginia region, requiring daily picking to meet commercial grocery store standards."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zucchini">
      <data key="d0">Zucchini</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop described as risky for the Southwest Virginia region, which requires daily picking to meet commercial grading standards."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lettuce">
      <data key="d0">Lettuce</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop, specifically leaf lettuce, noted as being particularly challenging to grow due to susceptibility to pests like slugs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Onions">
      <data key="d0">Onions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of crop that Michael Porterfield grows successfully, used as an example of his philosophy of experimentation and defying conventional wisdom."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-Term Plantings">
      <data key="d0">Long-Term Plantings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of crops, including fruit trees, nut trees, grape vines, and berry bushes, recommended as an early investment for a project's long-term sustainability due to their long maturation period."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weekend Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Weekend Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of farmers who farm as a secondary activity and are advised to choose crops that do not require daily attention."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Project Challenges">
      <data key="d0">Project Challenges</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A category of common difficulties faced by new community gardens, including recruiting volunteers, dealing with city ordinances, and securing a water source."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peas and Beans">
      <data key="d0">Peas and Beans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Nitrogen-fixing legume crops used in Dick Raymond's rotation to provide nitrogen for the following year's sweet corn. They are also mentioned as examples of low nitrogen users in general."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annual Ryegrass">
      <data key="d0">Annual Ryegrass</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A fall crop used in Dick Raymond's rotation. It serves as a winter cover to reduce erosion and nutrient leaching, preserve earthworms, and add root biomass to the soil."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weed Control">
      <data key="d0">Weed Control</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key benefit and goal of crop rotation. The text describes achieving it through crop diversity, mulching (from winter rye), and the allelopathic tendencies of certain plants."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Labor Management">
      <data key="d0">Labor Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An important farm management principle discussed in the text. Both crop rotation and the integration of livestock are presented as methods to even out the peaks and valleys of labor use throughout the year."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee261677ef65b4f0bf53fae46bfcfb75</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amish">
      <data key="d0">Amish</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A cultural group used as an example of family togetherness. Their practice of heating only one room encourages the family to congregate, which facilitates children learning from the interactions of their parents and siblings."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-H Program">
      <data key="d0">4-H Program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A youth organization recommended as an excellent way for farm children to learn about agriculture by raising their own crops or livestock. It also allows them to interact with other farm families, though the author notes some programs can be overly competitive."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hired Labor">
      <data key="d0">Hired Labor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Hired Labor refers to the practice of employing outside help on a farm. The text discusses the challenges of hiring, the importance of contracts and performance reviews, legal obligations like taxes and insurance, and methods for finding and retaining good employees."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Labor employed from outside the family, which requires a cash payment. The author advises using it only when necessary, such as for peak harvest, and hiring the best available person for the job."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Author's Farm">
      <data key="d0">The Author's Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The central location and setting for the agricultural examples provided, where the author raises Katahdin Hair sheep and feeder pigs. The farm's operations are designed to be labor-efficient, especially during winter, and to positively involve family labor."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intervale Center">
      <data key="d0">Intervale Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A community farming center located in close proximity to Burlington. It hosts 12 farms, a compost facility, a conservation nursery, a community garden, and various businesses. It facilitates a collaborative environment for farmers, incubates agricultural enterprises, and engages the public through events and open access."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"An organization located at 180 Intervale Road, Burlington, VT."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A non-profit organization that owns and manages the 350-acre Intervale grounds. It runs various programs to foster new farms and sustainable agriculture, including the Farms Program, a multi-farm CSA, and offers research and consulting services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Winooski River">
      <data key="d0">Winooski River</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A river in Vermont whose floodplain is the location of the Intervale.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Missisquoi Abenaki">
      <data key="d0">Missisquoi Abenaki</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An indigenous people with a long history of agriculture in the Intervale area, dating back at least five thousand years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intervale Community Farm (ICF)">
      <data key="d0">Intervale Community Farm (ICF)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Vermont's first and one of its largest Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) farms, located in the Intervale. It operates as a consumer cooperative and requires members to assemble their own shares at the farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Andy Jones">
      <data key="d0">Andy Jones</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"The operator of the Intervale Community Farm. He emphasizes the value of having a peer community of farmers to share collective experience and wisdom."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The farm manager of the Intervale Community Farm (ICF). He advocates for the farm's on-site pickup process as a way to build a stronger connection between members and the farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intervale Community Farm Cooperative">
      <data key="d0">Intervale Community Farm Cooperative</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The consumer cooperative that owns and governs the Intervale Community Farm. Membership provides benefits such as profit-sharing and a role in the farm's governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farms Program">
      <data key="d0">Farms Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An incubator program run by the Intervale Center to support new farmers. It provides access to land, equipment, and mentorship under three categories: Incubator, Enterprise, and Mentor farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Incubator Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Incubator Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category for new farmers in the Intervale Center's Farms Program who receive subsidized land, technical assistance, and mentorship for up to three years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feeder Pigs">
      <data key="d0">Feeder Pigs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A type of livestock the author buys to raise. They serve a strategic purpose by consuming surplus corn, thereby reducing harvesting labor, and are sold as sausage for winter income. They are slaughtered before deep winter to minimize feeding costs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Machinery">
      <data key="d0">Farm Machinery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Refers to engine-powered equipment such as tractors and mowers. It is highlighted as a significant safety risk, and the author strongly advises keeping young children away from it until they can understand the dangers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f0a967dcad41868e71ff8891a86b69b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tax Accountant">
      <data key="d0">Tax Accountant</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Tax Accountant is a professional who must be consulted when hiring farm labor to ensure compliance with financial regulations, such as withholding taxes and Social Security."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Security">
      <data key="d0">Social Security</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"Social Security is a government program mentioned in the context of employer responsibilities, as farmers who hire labor may be required to withhold Social Security taxes from employee paychecks."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Efficiency">
      <data key="d0">Farm Efficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Farm Efficiency is a key principle of farm management focused on the optimal use of labor. The text highlights its connection to farmstead arrangement, building locations, and the size and shape of fields to minimize wasted time and travel."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Farm Management Handbook">
      <data key="d0">The Farm Management Handbook</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Farm Management Handbook is an authoritative publication cited in the text to support the importance of farmstead arrangement for labor efficiency, providing specific data on how distance impacts travel time."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Building Reserves">
      <data key="d0">Building Reserves</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Building Reserves is a financial safety strategy for farms. It involves methods like setting aside money in savings, accounting for equipment depreciation, and storing non-cash assets like crops and livestock to manage unsteady markets and financial periods."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crops and Livestock">
      <data key="d0">Crops and Livestock</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Crops and Livestock are identified as a primary farm resource that can be used to build reserves. This includes storing crops like hay for future use and retaining female livestock to lower replacement costs, thus providing a buffer against bad years."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Enterprise Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Enterprise Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category within the Intervale Center's Farms Program for farmers who have been in operation for at least three years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mentor Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Mentor Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category within the Intervale Center's Farms Program for experienced farmers (at least five years) who agree to provide guidance to newer farmers.&lt;SEP&gt;Mentor Farmers are four experienced individuals who teach traditional Navajo farming and ranching practices to the 50 beginning ranchers as part of the NNTAO's mentoring program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glenn McRae">
      <data key="d0">Glenn McRae</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Glenn McRae is the former executive director of the Intervale Center and a representative for Intervale Community Farm. He emphasizes the importance of mentorship and community interaction among farmers in the incubator program and highlights the benefits of farmer proximity, describing the Intervale's model as "farming in public."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ethan Allen">
      <data key="d0">Ethan Allen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical figure whose original farmstead is located within the Intervale.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Municipal Composting Facility">
      <data key="d0">Municipal Composting Facility</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A municipal facility located in the Intervale that composts waste and has significantly contributed to rebuilding the fertility of the Intervale's soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="City Power-Generating Station">
      <data key="d0">City Power-Generating Station</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A wood-fired power-generating station for the city of Burlington, located within the Intervale.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conservation Plant Nursery">
      <data key="d0">Conservation Plant Nursery</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A nursery located in the Intervale that specializes in conservation and native plants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Retail Garden Center">
      <data key="d0">Retail Garden Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A large retail store with demonstration gardens located in the Intervale, serving the public.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Families">
      <data key="d0">Farm Families</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Farm Families are a social and economic group mentioned as a community with whom a farmer's children can interact through various programs, fostering social connections within the agricultural community."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unemployment Insurance">
      <data key="d0">Unemployment Insurance</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"Unemployment Insurance is a government-mandated program that farmers may be required to pay into when they hire employees, providing a financial safety net for workers who lose their jobs."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Worker's Compensation Insurance">
      <data key="d0">Worker's Compensation Insurance</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"Worker's Compensation Insurance is a type of insurance farmers might have to pay for hired labor to cover potential work-related injuries or illnesses, protecting both the employee and employer."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fellow Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Fellow Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Fellow Farmers are peers in the agricultural community who can provide suggestions and recommendations for finding reliable hired help based on their own experiences."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Employees">
      <data key="d0">Employees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Employees are individuals hired to perform labor on the farm. The text details the process of hiring, managing, retaining, and the legal and financial responsibilities associated with them."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Money">
      <data key="d0">Money</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Money is described as the most obvious and essential reserve to build for a farm's financial safety, used to manage cash flow, cover depreciation, and seize opportunities like sales or training."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea8dd630733dc61f911a635a9b8f0c7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Louis Bromfield">
      <data key="d0">Louis Bromfield</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An author who wrote 'Malabar Farm' around 1943. He warned about future hunger caused by the disappearance of farmers, and his ideas are cited as precursors to the modern sustainable agriculture movement.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of 'From My Experience' and 'Malabar Farm', where he writes about his return to organic farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea1bfb736a960a0ceee22d5eb6f8e955</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ethan Allen's Farmstead">
      <data key="d0">Ethan Allen's Farmstead</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The original, historical farmstead of Ethan Allen, which is preserved and located within the Intervale.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equipment-sharing Cooperative">
      <data key="d0">Equipment-sharing Cooperative</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A cooperative that farmers in the Intervale can join to share access to tractors, greenhouse space, and other specialized equipment, significantly reducing their start-up and operational costs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d83e7f9383ec8baa65b1a2fc070b1acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adam Hausman">
      <data key="d0">Adam Hausman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"The owner and operator of Adam's Berry Farm at the Intervale. He provides a firsthand account of the benefits, such as reduced land costs and equipment sharing, and the strong sense of community."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adam's Berry Farm">
      <data key="d0">Adam's Berry Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A pick-your-own and wholesale berry farm, one of the 12 farms at the Intervale. It is run by Adam Hausman and specializes in perennial crops, benefiting from shared resources like freezers."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A farm focused on growing berries.&lt;SEP&gt;Adam's Berry Farm is a farm mentioned in the document's index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intervale Community Farm">
      <data key="d0">Intervale Community Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farm located at 128 Intervale Road, Burlington, VT. Its representatives include Adam Housmann, Glenn McRae, and Will Raap."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"A farm run by Andy Jones located at the Intervale. It is part of the close-knit community of farmers on the site."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burlington">
      <data key="d0">Burlington</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The city located near the Intervale. Its residents engage with the farms, and its organic waste is processed by Intervale Compost Products."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A city in the state of Vermont.&lt;SEP&gt;The city in Vermont where the Intervale is located. The city has a history of supporting sustainability, and its mayors have been key supporters of the Intervale. The city's organic waste is used for the Intervale's composting operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd12c93ca420ae0dfd8107f51eec1886</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intervale Compost Products">
      <data key="d0">Intervale Compost Products</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A facility at the Intervale that processes all organic waste from Burlington. It produces a salable compost product and is now operated by the Chittenden Solid Waste District."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chittenden Solid Waste District">
      <data key="d0">Chittenden Solid Waste District</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A state agency that now owns and operates the Intervale Compost Products facility after it encountered environmental permitting issues."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Edward Faulkner">
      <data key="d0">Edward Faulkner</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The author of 'Plowman's Folly,' who questioned the destructive effects of the plow on soil. His ideas from the 1940s are considered foundational to modern sustainable agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arthur Moore">
      <data key="d0">Arthur Moore</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The author of 'The Farmer and the Rest of Us,' who in 1945 analyzed the U.S. farm system, noting the high concentration of production among a small number of large farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Countryside">
      <data key="d0">American Countryside</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the rural areas of the United States, from which the text states that farmers and the agricultural way of life are disappearing due to development and changing societal values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reserves">
      <data key="d0">Reserves</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core farm management concept involving the storage and preservation of resources like crops (hay), livestock genetics, soil nutrients, and materials (timber, wire) to ensure stability and resilience against future challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Development">
      <data key="d0">Development</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of converting farmland into non-agricultural uses such as housing, shopping malls, and recreation centers. It is identified as the primary cause of the loss of American farmland, happening at a rate of 2 acres per minute.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dust storms">
      <data key="d0">Dust storms</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical environmental event in the U.S. which, along with the Depression, heightened national concern over soil conservation and food security.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="My grandmother">
      <data key="d0">My grandmother</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A personal figure quoted by the author to illustrate the importance of understanding the past ('where you have been') in order to plan for the future ('where you're going').</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homesteading">
      <data key="d0">Homesteading</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical practice in the United States where land was offered to settlers to encourage the population of certain areas, often promoted by businesses like railroads.&lt;SEP&gt;A lifestyle of self-sufficiency, which is the focus of publications like 'Countryside &amp; Small Stock Journal' and older issues of 'Mother Earth News'.&lt;SEP&gt;A subject category for books providing comprehensive practical information on country living and homecraft skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intervale Conservation Nursery">
      <data key="d0">Intervale Conservation Nursery</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A nursery owned by the Intervale Center that grows about 50,000 native trees and shrubs for wetland restoration projects."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
