    <node id="State">
      <data key="d0">State</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the wider geographical and political area, presumably Vermont, where Intervale-related activities such as environmental permitting and state-wide wetland restoration projects take place.&lt;SEP&gt;The governmental authority that Greensgrow had to lobby to receive a 'farm truck' license plate. This struggle highlights the broader issue of urban farms not being viewed as 'real' farms by regulatory bodies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e143d750d8e9b45fec3f4d1a9ec2a04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c977e0c22629a3b90f1214c2370c4394</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Railroads">
      <data key="d0">Railroads</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Businesses mentioned in a historical context for encouraging the practice of homesteading in order to create a customer base for their services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paul B. Frederic">
      <data key="d0">Paul B. Frederic</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical geographer who notes that a farm's location relative to markets is of critical importance in determining its characteristics and how its use changes over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="James Whitaker">
      <data key="d0">James Whitaker</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The editor of the book 'Farming in the Midwest 1840–1900', which is cited in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="John Deere">
      <data key="d0">John Deere</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An innovator who developed the first steel plows, which was a key part of the mechanization of agriculture and replaced horses as the main power source on farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Delta Land and Pine Company">
      <data key="d0">Delta Land and Pine Company</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A company that worked with the USDA to create the 'terminator' gene. It was later acquired by Monsanto.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monsanto">
      <data key="d0">Monsanto</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A major agricultural company that bought Delta Land and Pine Company. It chose not to market the initial 'terminator' gene due to bad publicity but has continued to develop similar technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="George Washington">
      <data key="d0">George Washington</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An early American politician and farmer who owned extensive landholdings and experimented with crops and soil improvement techniques, such as growing legumes for cover crops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fbeff72000d21039cdbac6ba65f75077</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740830</data>
    </node>
