    <node id="Federal Government">
      <data key="d0">Federal Government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Federal Government of the United States has a varied and significant impact across different sectors and communities. It administers programs aimed at supporting low-income populations, such as the Farmers Market Nutrition Program (FMNP) and the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP). However, its support is not universal across all industries; for example, the Federal Government does not provide a financial safety net, like subsidies or traditional crop insurance, to the fish farming industry, which increases the sector's financial risk. In terms of regulation, there are calls for changes at the federal level to streamline the inspection and certification process for smaller meat-processing facilities serving local food markets. Furthermore, the Federal Government's historical relationship with the Muscogee Nation is noted to have a continuing effect on the community's present-day conditions and health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-087cef03c5ab256c59a3f536c4533b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16c3fe8c089550fd16987a1c4f301a2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Newspaper">
      <data key="d0">Local Newspaper</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A media outlet that interviewed Mary Seton Corboy about Greensgrow's mission and the LIFE program, helping to disseminate her message about food availability and affordability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metropolanites">
      <data key="d0">Metropolanites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A term for people living in metropolitan areas, representing 84 percent of Americans. The text describes them as having little connection to or understanding of where their food comes from.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Older, Rural-Based Generation">
      <data key="d0">Older, Rural-Based Generation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group with strong connections to food production. Their passing is noted as a reason for the growing gap in food knowledge among the general populace.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Epidemic of Obesity">
      <data key="d0">National Epidemic of Obesity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A major public health crisis in the United States, fueled by fast, fatty, and sugary foods, with significant annual medical costs. The local food movement is presented as a response to this epidemic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Informed Consumers">
      <data key="d0">Informed Consumers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of people that Mary Seton Corboy aims to create through education at urban farms. These are discerning, concerned, and savvy individuals who make better choices about their food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marra Farm">
      <data key="d0">Marra Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A historic farm in Seattle that serves as a model for establishing a community garden within a public park space."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Marra Farm is a historic farm in South Park, Seattle, owned by the city's Department of Parks and Recreation. It serves as a model for community gardens on public land, hosting multiple projects including a P-Patch garden, a plot for Lettuce Link, a subsistence garden, and a market-gardening project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959e33ece6a9fc720452b33659ca7996</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
