    <node id="Gateway Greening">
      <data key="d0">Gateway Greening</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based in St. Louis, Missouri, Gateway Greening is a non-profit organization that was founded in 1984. Located at 2211 Washington Avenue, the organization's mission is to green the city, stabilize neighborhoods, improve community health, combat homelessness, and build food security. To achieve this, Gateway Greening supports over two hundred community, youth, and school gardens. It is also involved in urban farming and operates the City Seeds Urban Farm, which provides jobs and training to individuals dealing with homelessness and other challenges. Additionally, the organization is associated with the practice of "therapy gardening." Hannah Butz and Travis Hall are listed as representatives of Gateway Greening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aab30ee2b8630558ddd6575d42c69264&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Janus Youth Programs, Inc.">
      <data key="d0">Janus Youth Programs, Inc.</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Janus Youth Programs, Inc. is one of the largest nonprofits in the Pacific Northwest, serving high-risk youth in Oregon and Washington. It uses an urban agriculture program as a tool for community revitalization and youth development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="John Muir">
      <data key="d0">John Muir</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">John Muir was a historical figure, naturalist, and author known for his book 'My First Summer in the Sierra.' A quote from this work highlighting the interconnectedness of nature is used to illustrate that the food system is similarly connected to other aspects of life, such as community, health, and the economy. This idea is paraphrased by Charlie Jackson as, "Pull on one thing, and you'll find it's connected to everything else," to further explain this concept.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fee18f76430d121ab613984069f209e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214f4e9d9f3d3fed74243c9bdf50bf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fa6369dd0584465f07387131c6d28e6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
