    </node>
    <node id="Inspirational">
      <data key="d0">Inspirational</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A subject category for books containing essays and reflections on cultural and agricultural themes, particularly sustainable agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St. Johns Wood Management Company">
      <data key="d0">St. Johns Wood Management Company</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The management company overseeing the St. Johns Woods public housing complex. Initially opposed to the community garden, it later became one of Janus Youth's 'best references' after witnessing the project's positive impact on crime and community cohesion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immigrants">
      <data key="d0">Immigrants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group within the communities mentioned, noted as a potential community strength due to their prior farming experience. They are part of the diverse population served by the Seeds of Harmony garden.&lt;SEP&gt;A key demographic within the St. Johns Woods community, originating from places like East Africa, Laos, and Vietnam. Having been farmers in their homelands, they initiated gardening and were central to the success of the larger community project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a815073ae837fa29833af41e55990ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Elders">
      <data key="d0">Community Elders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The parents and grandparents within the immigrant community at St. Johns Woods. The community garden provided them with an important role to share their agricultural knowledge and experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Runaway Homeless Youth">
      <data key="d0">Runaway Homeless Youth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core client group for Janus Youth, which is described as likely the largest service agency for this population in the Northwest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Teen Parents">
      <data key="d0">Teen Parents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core client group for Janus Youth, which provides services for them primarily in residences and shelters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kids in the Welfare System">
      <data key="d0">Kids in the Welfare System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific group of young people for whom Janus Youth provides dedicated support programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05be2e4c27c7eabe9de8d2ff56b840a5</data>
