    </node>
    <node id="Sauvie Island Organics">
      <data key="d0">Sauvie Island Organics</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Sauvie Island Organics is an established organic farm on Sauvie Island that supported the Food Works program by offering to share equipment and agricultural knowledge with the teens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Housing Authority of Portland">
      <data key="d0">Housing Authority of Portland</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Housing Authority of Portland is a government agency that redeveloped a housing project into the new mixed-income community of New Columbia. They actively sought to include a community garden in the new development and partnered with Janus Youth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sauvie Island">
      <data key="d0">Sauvie Island</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Sauvie Island is an island in the Columbia River where the 1-acre Food Works farm is located. The island has an agricultural heritage, and local families were supportive of the teens' farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Columbia">
      <data key="d0">New Columbia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A mixed-income community where the Seeds of Harmony garden was established. It is the site of a community development project involving Janus Youth and The Housing Authority.&lt;SEP&gt;New Columbia is a new mixed-use and mixed-income community in North Portland, built by the Housing Authority of Portland. A community garden was established here with mentorship from the experienced gardeners of Village Gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d7d0c8754f5ccddfb78068c25f0fa0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Transformation">
      <data key="d0">Community Transformation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Community Transformation is the primary outcome and theme of the Janus Youth gardening programs. It refers to the profound, positive impact on youth, families, and entire neighborhoods, which Dennis Morrow describes as a 'miracle.'</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leadership Program">
      <data key="d0">Leadership Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A program created by Janus Youth where experienced gardeners from one community (St. Johns Woods) mentor a new community (New Columbia), thereby developing leadership skills and expanding the program's benefits.&lt;SEP&gt;The Leadership Program was an initiative created by Janus Youth where experienced gardeners from St. Johns Woods mentored the creation of a new garden in the New Columbia community. It represents an evolution of the organization's model from direct service to mentorship and leadership development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d7d0c8754f5ccddfb78068c25f0fa0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hope VI Grant">
      <data key="d0">Hope VI Grant</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hope VI Grant was a federal grant received by the Housing Authority of Portland. This funding was used to redevelop a poor housing area in North Portland into the new mixed-use, mixed-income community of New Columbia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Columbia River">
      <data key="d0">Columbia River</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Columbia River is a major river where Sauvie Island, the location of the Food Works farm, is situated. The text specifies the farm is 7 miles upstream in the middle of the river.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f8b5f47647da9706f2d5877b878fae0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gretchen Vogel Poisson">
      <data key="d0">Gretchen Vogel Poisson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-author, with Leandre Poisson, of 'Solar Gardening', a book on growing vegetables year-round with mini-greenhouses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
