    <node id="Community Gardeners">
      <data key="d0">Community Gardeners</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A collective group representing the residents who actively manage and set the rules for their community gardens, such as the Seeds of Harmony garden. They are central to the empowerment model described.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a815073ae837fa29833af41e55990ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grandparents">
      <data key="d0">Grandparents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group identified as a community strength. Their knowledge of local children and daytime presence are considered valuable assets for community projects like gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a815073ae837fa29833af41e55990ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dereck Cunningham">
      <data key="d0">Dereck Cunningham</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dereck Cunningham is a co-founder, leader, representative, and the president of Lynchburg Grows. As a stained glass artist born with spina bifida, he has a deep personal connection to the organization's mission of empowering people with special needs. The farm's mission was significantly inspired by Cunningham's personal story of thriving through gardening, a skill he was taught by his grandfather. He advises exercising patience to find appropriate tasks for every individual, citing the success of finding a suitable job for a blind woman with cerebral palsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scott Lowman">
      <data key="d0">Scott Lowman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A co-founder and leader of Lynchburg Grows, who worked with Michael Van Ness and Dereck Cunningham to establish the project."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"A representative for Lynchburg Grows."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Scott Lowman is a co-founder of Lynchburg Grows and serves as its director of stewardship. He believes that working with disabled individuals primarily requires compassion, kindness, and clear communication, rather than specialized training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paul Lam">
      <data key="d0">Paul Lam</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Bedford resident whose story of having his personal garden destroyed after being wrongly institutionalized served as the initial catalyst for the founders to start the project that would become Lynchburg Grows."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Schenkel's">
      <data key="d0">Schenkel's</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The former rose-growing business that operated on the site now occupied by Lynchburg Grows. It was a major supplier of roses in Virginia until it closed in 1998 due to foreign competition."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
