      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A national movement in the 1990s to move people with disabilities out of large institutions and into community-based settings, which is how Paul Lam was transitioned into a group home."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b97e15a0b1c4d19efdaa6af5f597519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Van Ness">
      <data key="d0">Van Ness</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A person associated with the Lynchburg Grows project who is quoted expressing pride in the community-driven growth of the farm's garden.&lt;SEP&gt;An environmental lawyer and one of the four partners who founded Lynchburg Grows. He was instrumental in structuring the land-purchase deal and navigating the complexities of the site's soil contamination, drawing on his expertise in land conservation.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual mentioned at the start of the text who tells an illustrative story about two student interns at a 'creative, learning farm' to demonstrate how it fosters empathy and self-respect.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0ebf31ca8cf0e8a5dbe6d09c3efa272&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69499413d16357f9a2d75749d72ecca8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Schenkel Family">
      <data key="d0">Schenkel Family</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The original owners of the greenhouse property that became Lynchburg Grows. They were thrilled that the land would be repurposed as an urban farm, which they felt would honor their family's history and their greenhouses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lam">
      <data key="d0">Lam</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A person whose situation involving a garden, tiller, and compost is mentioned at the start of the text as an initial point of inspiration for the founders' growing aspirations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virginia Department of Environmental Quality (DEQ)">
      <data key="d0">Virginia Department of Environmental Quality (DEQ)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A state agency that assisted Lynchburg Grows by accepting them into a voluntary brownfields remediation program. The DEQ conducted a full site assessment, which cost over $80,000, and provided guidance on managing the contamination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Milwaukee's Growing Power">
      <data key="d0">Milwaukee's Growing Power</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An influential urban farming organization founded by Will Allen. It served as a source of expert advice for Lynchburg Grows.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brownfields Remediation Program">
      <data key="d0">Brownfields Remediation Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A federally funded program that Lynchburg Grows joined to receive assistance with cleaning up their contaminated property. A key benefit was receiving formal relief from long-term legal liability after completing the program's requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9189adec7b34964ca2342aea2d2b666e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northeast">
      <data key="d0">Northeast</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Northeast is a region of the United States known for several distinct characteristics. It is a hub for innovative baking, particularly in its demand for local grains, similar to the San Francisco Bay Area. The region's organic and low-input farming practices are detailed in the book "The Real Dirt." The Northeast Organic Farming Association provides resources and information related to organic farming in the area. The Northeast, alongside the Midwest, is also mentioned as a potentially unreliable source of meat for restaurants in Washington D.C. Furthermore, the Northwest Atlantic Marine Alliance (NAMA) concentrates its efforts in the Northeast to support fishing communities facing economic hardship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104466acc0eb6f55c05f02aa258ea31a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea1bfb736a960a0ceee22d5eb6f8e955</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Us Department of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Us Department of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A US government department located in Washington, DC, that published 'The Yearbooks of Agriculture', which contain useful information on topics like soil, water, grass, and trees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea1bfb736a960a0ceee22d5eb6f8e955</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
