    </node>
    <node id="At-risk Youth">
      <data key="d0">At-risk Youth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group served by Lynchburg Grows through various programs aimed at job training, community service, and skill development. They work alongside people with disabilities, which helps foster empathy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="People with Disabilities">
      <data key="d0">People with Disabilities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary demographic group that Lynchburg Grows serves, also referred to as 'special-needs populations'. The organization provides them with work, job training, and a sense of community and purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynchburg Farmers' Market">
      <data key="d0">Lynchburg Farmers' Market</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Described as one of the oldest indoor farmers' markets in the country, it is a location in Lynchburg where Lynchburg Grows has become a market anchor and provides job training for disabled individuals, with support from the city's parks and recreation department.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Salsa Micro-Enterprise">
      <data key="d0">Salsa Micro-Enterprise</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A potential small business venture that is the intended final goal of the 'Post-D' program. At-risk youth learn business skills by growing vegetables (tomatoes, peppers, onions, cilantro) with the aim of producing and marketing their own salsa product.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nonprofit Boards">
      <data key="d0">Nonprofit Boards</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A general category of organizational governance structures, including 'working boards' and 'policy and fund-raising boards'. The text contrasts the typical selection process for these boards with Lynchburg Grows' unique requirement that all board applicants must first volunteer for at least six months.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Juvenile Detention Center">
      <data key="d0">Juvenile Detention Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An institution for youth offenders. Lynchburg Grows partners with it through the 'Post-D' program, which enrolls at-risk youth who have recently completed their time at the center to teach them business and life skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-61443c6300dbc7b6e2bd6de269518650</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Daniel Ross">
      <data key="d0">Daniel Ross</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The former executive director of Nuestras Raíces. He emphasized that the organization's work extends beyond food to include environmental justice and cultural validation through arts projects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69499413d16357f9a2d75749d72ecca8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Justice">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Justice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key focus area for Nuestras Raíces, involving educating the community and taking action on environmental issues like mercury contamination in the Connecticut River and testing soil in brownfields for safe gardening.</data>
