    <node id="Teens">
      <data key="d0">Teens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group, which the text links to other topics such as 'farm to college movements', 'The Food Project', and internships.&lt;SEP&gt;Teens are the primary focus group and workforce for The Food Project. The organization provides jobs and leadership opportunities to a diverse mix of teenagers from both wealthy suburbs and low-income inner-city neighborhoods, empowering them through agricultural work.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary demographic group, aged 14 to 17, that The Food Project serves and employs. They are intentionally recruited from both urban (60%) and suburban (40%) areas to foster diversity and personal growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07d81959e2e7dfd429fb34ce74beae5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sustainable Food System">
      <data key="d0">Sustainable Food System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A conceptual food system that aims to be environmentally sustainable (not degrading soil, water, or air), less dependent on nonrenewable energy like petroleum, and economically viable for farmers. It is presented as an alternative to the current industrial model."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A central goal of The Food Project. The organization aims to build a sustainable food system by growing healthy food locally, distributing it to residents, and inspiring community-led change.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07d81959e2e7dfd429fb34ce74beae5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
