      <data key="d0">Rural Property Bulletin</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A periodical that contains listings for rural property sales.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farming Books">
      <data key="d0">Farming Books</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of literature focused on agricultural topics. Several publishers in the list, such as Country Store Books and Storey Publishing, specialize in this area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Income City Farmers' Markets">
      <data key="d0">Low-Income City Farmers' Markets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Venues where The Food Project sells its produce, specifically targeting low-income urban areas to improve food access. The project sold its food at four such markets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07d81959e2e7dfd429fb34ce74beae5e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Food Policy Council">
      <data key="d0">Local Food Policy Council</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A future goal of The Food Project. The organization aims to create a local food policy council as part of its five-year plan to double its impact on the regional food system.&lt;SEP&gt;A proposed council that The Food Project aims to create as part of its five-year goals to influence food policy and increase the availability of healthy food in the city.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07d81959e2e7dfd429fb34ce74beae5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iris Ahronowitz">
      <data key="d0">Iris Ahronowitz</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Harvard undergraduate student who gave permission for a piece of her thesis on The Food Project to be reprinted in the book.&lt;SEP&gt;A researcher who studied the Food Project for her thesis at Harvard University. She characterized the organization's approach to youth empowerment as structured and effective, providing teens with their first paid job and increasing responsibilities based on performance.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual mentioned in the index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Harvard University">
      <data key="d0">Harvard University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Harvard University is an Ivy League university and academic institution that was among the early signatories of the Sustainable Campus Charter, contributing to the establishment of sustainability standards. It is also the university where Iris Zippora Ahronowitz wrote, submitted, and completed her undergraduate research thesis on the Food Project in November 2003.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ba8e532ec9e7db3fd3049537a06db80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d076831b7a91bcd605c1e387112d81e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62dde44a553987a2d4deba0864e69eeb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jen James">
      <data key="d0">Jen James</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for The Food Project."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A person associated with the Food Project who states that the program's most significant impact occurs when youths participate for a minimum of two years.&lt;SEP&gt;The communications director for the Food Project. She emphasizes the importance of the program's diversity, mixing urban and suburban youth to create a transformational experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bounty Bucks">
      <data key="d0">Bounty Bucks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A program created by the Food Project in partnership with the City of Boston. It allows Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) recipients to double the value of their benefits (up to $10) for fresh produce at farmers' markets, supporting both low-income residents and the local farm economy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Food Project Summer Program">
      <data key="d0">The Food Project Summer Program</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The cornerstone of the Food Project, this is an eight-week paid summer job for teens aged 14 to 17. It serves as the first tier of the organization's youth development program, focusing on real work, structured learning, and fostering relationships across diverse backgrounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boston's Farmers' Markets">
      <data key="d0">Boston's Farmers' Markets</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Four low-income city farmers' markets located in Boston where the 'Bounty Bucks' program is implemented. These markets serve as a venue for low-income residents to purchase fresh, healthy produce and support the local farm economy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aviculture">
      <data key="d0">Aviculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The practice of keeping and breeding birds, which is the publishing specialty of Hancock House Publishers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eco-agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Eco-agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of agriculture focused on ecological principles. It is the main topic of the 'Acres USA' periodical.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biodynamic Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Biodynamic Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An organic method of agriculture that is the focus of the 'Biodynamics' periodical and its parent association.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organic Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Organic Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A method of farming that avoids synthetic chemicals. It is the main topic for periodicals like 'The Maine Organic Farmer &amp; Gardener' and 'The Natural Farmer'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rare And Exotic Animals">
      <data key="d0">Rare And Exotic Animals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of unusual animals often kept as pets or for conservation. 'Animal Finders' Guide' provides sale listings for them, and 'Rare Breeds Journal' also focuses on them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Donkeys And Mules">
      <data key="d0">Donkeys And Mules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Equine hybrids and their relatives. They are the focus of 'The Brayer', 'Mules and More', and 'Western Mule' magazines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Draft Horses">
      <data key="d0">Draft Horses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Large horse breeds used for heavy tasks. They are the subject of the 'Draft Horse Journal'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Meat Goats">
      <data key="d0">Meat Goats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Goats raised specifically for meat production. They are the focus of the 'Goat Rancher' magazine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Food System">
      <data key="d0">Regional Food System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept representing the network of food production, distribution, and consumption within a specific geographic area. The Food Project has a stated five-year goal to double its impact on this system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Youth Organizations">
      <data key="d0">Youth Organizations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A general category of organizations to which The Food Project is compared. The Food Project's structured approach is considered 'conservative' compared to more extreme models that cede full control to young people.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Migrant Farm Labor">
      <data key="d0">Migrant Farm Labor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A traditional labor source for large-scale food production. The text contrasts this with The Food Project's innovative model of using teens as a vital, untapped resource for farmwork.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crew Leader">
      <data key="d0">Crew Leader</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A leadership role within The Food Project, typically held by a program alumnus aged 20 to 28. They are responsible for leading and mentoring crews of 10 teens during the summer program.&lt;SEP&gt;A leadership role within the Food Project responsible for motivating, teaching, and managing a crew of teens. Crew leaders are described as high-energy, positive figures who lead by example and are always with their crews, with one being a former marine who initiated a running practice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Assistant Crew Leader">
      <data key="d0">Assistant Crew Leader</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A secondary leadership role within The Food Project's summer program. This position is held by a teen who participated in the program the previous year, providing a pathway for increased responsibility.&lt;SEP&gt;The third tier in the Food Project's leadership track. This role is for experienced teens who have completed the summer and DIRT programs, and involves sharing responsibilities with crew leaders to manage and motivate their crews.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b688540c900515c3ef10df531de83587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Project">
      <data key="d0">Food Project</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-profit organization that runs immersive summer and school-year programs for teenagers. It combines agricultural work on suburban and urban farms with education on topics like diversity and personal finance, and a strong emphasis on community service and leadership development.&lt;SEP&gt;An organization or program model that the Durham Inner-City Gardeners (DIG) program adapted and expanded upon to create its own system of overlapping youth leadership teams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Julien Goulet">
      <data key="d0">Julien Goulet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The director of youth programs at the Food Project. He highlights the program's success in integrating youths from different backgrounds, describing it as a significant transformation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standardbred Poultry">
      <data key="d0">Standardbred Poultry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Purebred poultry breeds conforming to a standard of perfection. 'Poultry Press' promotes this category.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sheep And Goats">
      <data key="d0">Sheep And Goats</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of small livestock. The magazine 'Ranch &amp; Rural Living' focuses on them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5aeb69db517fc797c325acc9dcb88413</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Growing for Market">
      <data key="d0">Growing for Market</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A monthly newsletter that offers news and ideas tailored for market gardeners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sustainable Agriculture Newsletter">
      <data key="d0">Sustainable Agriculture Newsletter</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A bimonthly newsletter published by the Minnesota Institute for Sustainable Agriculture, focusing on sustainable agriculture news.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Livestock Breeds Conservancy News">
      <data key="d0">American Livestock Breeds Conservancy News</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A bimonthly publication from the American Livestock Breeds Conservancy focused on providing information about rare breeds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Livestock Breeds Conservancy">
      <data key="d0">American Livestock Breeds Conservancy</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A nonprofit organization that works to protect and preserve endangered livestock breeds. Its website provides information and resources for raising them.&lt;SEP&gt;An organization dedicated to the conservation of rare livestock breeds, which publishes a newsletter on the topic.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Livestock Breeds Conservancy provides information on rare and endangered breeds of livestock.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57c24510274e9c9fcb046e6b604285fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Center for Rural Affairs">
      <data key="d0">Center for Rural Affairs</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization listed as a source of information on rural and agricultural issues.&lt;SEP&gt;An organization that provides information on national events that affect family farmers. It can be contacted at 402-687-2100 and its website is www.cfra.org.&lt;SEP&gt;The Center for Rural Affairs provides information on national events that affect family farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4725469cd4458c0f5eeeefdfccd30dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57c24510274e9c9fcb046e6b604285fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Cut Flower Quarterly">
      <data key="d0">The Cut Flower Quarterly</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A quarterly publication from the Association of Specialty Cut Flower Growers, containing information on field and greenhouse cut-flower production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dynamic, Intelligent, Responsible Teens (DIRT)">
      <data key="d0">Dynamic, Intelligent, Responsible Teens (DIRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The school-year program (Tier Two) for youths who have completed the Food Project's summer program. The DIRT crew meets after school and on Saturdays to lead volunteer work, conduct special projects, and study topics like hunger and inequality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Durham Inner-City Gardeners (DIG)">
      <data key="d0">Durham Inner-City Gardeners (DIG)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A community-based organization focused on gardening within the inner city of Durham, North Carolina."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A program started in 2000 by SEEDS, Inc. in Durham, North Carolina. It hires teenagers from poor neighborhoods to work in a garden, with the goal of teaching them life skills, job skills, and leadership. The program features a tiered structure with a 'year-round crew' and 'summer crews,' and incorporates education on nutrition, photography, art, and cultural history related to agriculture.&lt;SEP&gt;A program started in 2000 in Durham, North Carolina, by SEEDS, Inc. It aims to help teens from poor neighborhoods learn life skills and form a close-knit team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kavanah Ramsier">
      <data key="d0">Kavanah Ramsier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for SEEDS, INC."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A co-coordinator for the Durham Inner-City Gardeners (DIG) program who describes its goal as helping kids develop a 'close-knit team and family unit.'&lt;SEP&gt;A co-coordinator for the Durham Inner-City Gardeners (DIG) program. She explains the program's philosophy, including its methods for recruitment, internal leadership development, and its focus on the garden as an educational center rather than purely for production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Straight Talk">
      <data key="d0">Straight Talk</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A structured, weekly one-on-one meeting between a youth and a supervisor within the Food Project's curriculum. It is designed to foster open communication and provide structured feedback.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Service">
      <data key="d0">Community Service</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core component of the Food Project's programs, where teens and their leaders volunteer at food pantries and shelters. In 2009, participants contributed 2,800 volunteer hours.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Teen Participants">
      <data key="d0">Teen Participants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The core demographic served by the Food Project and DIG programs. These youths, from diverse urban and suburban backgrounds, engage in agricultural work, workshops, and community service to develop life skills, leadership, and a sense of community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Project Summer Program">
      <data key="d0">Food Project Summer Program</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary, multi-week summer program run by the Food Project. It involves a structured daily schedule of agricultural work, educational workshops (on topics like agriculture, diversity, and finance), community building activities, and community service, culminating in a celebratory feast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Workshops">
      <data key="d0">Workshops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key educational component of the Food Project's curriculum. These hands-on mini-workshops cover a range of topics including agriculture (weeds, soils, insects), diversity (progressing from stereotyping to community building), and personal finance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Association of Specialty Cut Flower Growers">
      <data key="d0">Association of Specialty Cut Flower Growers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An association for producers of specialty cut flowers that publishes 'The Cut Flower Quarterly'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HortIdeas">
      <data key="d0">HortIdeas</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A bimonthly newsletter that reports on the latest research and tools relevant to gardeners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leopold Letter">
      <data key="d0">Leopold Letter</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A quarterly newsletter published by the Leopold Center for Sustainable Agriculture, covering news and research on the topic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leopold Center for Sustainable Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Leopold Center for Sustainable Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A research center located at Iowa State University that focuses on sustainable agriculture and publishes the 'Leopold Letter'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minnesota Institute for Sustainable Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Minnesota Institute for Sustainable Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An institute associated with the University of Minnesota that publishes the 'Sustainable Agriculture' newsletter and provides information on sustainable farming practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Department of Animal Science">
      <data key="d0">Department of Animal Science</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A department within Oklahoma State University that provides information through its website.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oklahoma State University">
      <data key="d0">Oklahoma State University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A university that hosts the Department of Animal Science.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Agriculture Notes">
      <data key="d0">Urban Agriculture Notes</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A web-based resource provided by City Farmer that offers information on urban farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hunger Relief Agencies">
      <data key="d0">Hunger Relief Agencies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Organizations such as food pantries and shelters where Food Project youths perform community service. In 2009, youths contributed 2,800 volunteer hours to these agencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Celebratory Feast">
      <data key="d0">Celebratory Feast</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An event held at the end of the summer program for the Food Project, to which all the teen participants' families are invited to celebrate their work and achievements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6376ac179a2df4bf1471d8fed5f7bc23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anthony">
      <data key="d0">Anthony</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A teenage leader in the Durham Inner-City Gardeners (DIG) program. He provides a personal testimonial about how the program encouraged him to try new things, like chard, and to overcome his fears.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leadership Training">
      <data key="d0">Leadership Training</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core component of the DIG program, particularly for the 'year-round crew.' It is an intensive process where teens learn to manage responsibilities such as ordering seeds, developing applications, interviewing and hiring peers, and training others, thereby building confidence and responsibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food System Injustice">
      <data key="d0">Food System Injustice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An educational theme discussed within the DIG program. It addresses historical and cultural issues in agriculture, such as the stigma of 'farming' in African-American history, to help teens understand how their choices can impact the food system and to redefine farming in a positive context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Year-Round Crew">
      <data key="d0">Year-Round Crew</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core group of five teens hired by the DIG program who work on Friday evenings and Saturdays during the school year. They receive intensive leadership training and are responsible for key operational tasks, including ordering seeds, developing applications, and interviewing and hiring the summer crews.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summer Crews">
      <data key="d0">Summer Crews</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Two separate groups of ten youths each who are hired by the Year-Round Crew to work in the DIG garden for 12-week periods during the summer. They are trained by and work alongside the year-round members, learning gardening and job skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Black Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific group that the teens in the DIG program are often unaware of. The program educates participants about the existence and history of Black farmers who own their own farms, challenging preconceived notions and broadening their understanding of agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="City Farmer">
      <data key="d0">City Farmer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization focused on urban farming which provides the 'Urban Agriculture Notes' web resource.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The CyberFarm">
      <data key="d0">The CyberFarm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A web resource from Ohio State University Extension that provides information for small farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ohio State University Extension">
      <data key="d0">Ohio State University Extension</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The extension service of Ohio State University, providing resources such as 'The CyberFarm'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
