      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The cooperative extension service of North Dakota State University, which publishes educational materials on farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="African-American Owned Farm">
      <data key="d0">African-American Owned Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of farm that SEEDS takes the DIG teens to visit at least once a year. These visits are educational, aiming to broaden the teens' understanding of the modern farming world and provide positive role models by showing them farms owned and operated by African-Americans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-adc2a008e21cba06a8a9ad6e540ff138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ramsier">
      <data key="d0">Ramsier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual cited in the text who provides context on The Food Project's DIG program. Ramsier explains that the program helps redefine the meaning of 'farm' for teens, using its historical stigma to motivate discussions about injustice in agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIG">
      <data key="d0">DIG</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A program within The Food Project aimed at teens, likely an acronym for 'Diversity in the Ground.' It focuses on redefining farming, understanding food system injustices, and learning how to effect change through policy, business, and advocacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEEDS">
      <data key="d0">SEEDS</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An entity that, in partnership with The Food Project's DIG program, takes teenagers on annual trips to farms owned and operated by African-Americans to broaden their understanding of farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shirley-Eustis House">
      <data key="d0">Shirley-Eustis House</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A historic preservation association and national historic landmark located in Roxbury. It partnered with The Food Project in 2006 to create the Urban Learning Farm on its property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Learning Farm">
      <data key="d0">Urban Learning Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A farm established in Roxbury through a partnership between The Food Project and the Shirley-Eustis House. It serves as a community hub for workshops and a training ground for The Food Project's older interns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLAST">
      <data key="d0">BLAST</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An advanced internship program at The Food Project, standing for 'Building Local Agriculture Systems Today.' Started in 2003, it aims to create an international network and prepare young people for careers in sustainable food systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lincoln">
      <data key="d0">Lincoln</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A suburban location where The Food Project manages a major farm, staffed by three full-time farmers. It represents the suburban component of the youth's experience, contrasting with the urban sites.&lt;SEP&gt;The location of one of The Food Project's farms, where a visiting author witnessed an efficient broccoli planting crew of youths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8965f3e3965322fdf5c53aebddde1e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b887765fabe568d174cfa581723af5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740838</data>
    </node>
