    </node>
    <node id="Growing Minds">
      <data key="d0">Growing Minds</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based in Asheville, North Carolina, Growing Minds is the Farm to School program of the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP). The program, directed by its representative Emily Jackson and associated with Anthony Flaccovento, acts as a facilitative partner in the Farm to School movement. Its core mission is to connect children with healthy, locally grown food. To achieve this, Growing Minds helps teachers integrate school gardens, farm field trips, and in-classroom cooking into their curriculum. It also works to increase the amount of locally grown food served in schools by developing resources and establishing local food sources for cafeterias. Furthermore, the program engages school staff through CSA shares and farm tours to build support for local food. On a larger scale, Growing Minds serves as the Southeastern regional lead agency for the National Farm to School Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04be01c6169d0bdd92b10dca615a3936&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c38b37fe24e15bfa43a713c9e38509&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c16ea96bc625614680cc6d8276e6ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abernethy Elementary School">
      <data key="d0">Abernethy Elementary School</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Abernethy Elementary School is an elementary school located in Portland, Oregon, with Sarah Sullivan and Wendy Willis listed as its representatives. The school is known for an innovative garden and kitchen program, which is highlighted as a successful "Farm to School" program and a "Scratch Kitchen Model." This initiative, which began with a single individual and grew to be driven by a motivated parent base, was re-established to serve fresh food and has successfully transformed the school's culture around fresh fruits and vegetables. Abernethy Elementary School was the subject of a baseline assessment for this model and now serves as a pilot kitchen for the entire school district.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04be01c6169d0bdd92b10dca615a3936&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c16ea96bc625614680cc6d8276e6ca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Departments of Agriculture and Health and Human Services">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Departments of Agriculture and Health and Human Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Federal government departments that establish national dietary guidelines. The text notes that only 2% of children currently meet these guidelines, underscoring the need for programs like Farm to School.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04be01c6169d0bdd92b10dca615a3936</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="School Gardens">
      <data key="d0">School Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A key component of the Growing Minds program, used as an educational space for children to learn about growing food. They are not intended for large-scale production for cafeterias but to provide a 'magic' and rich educational experience linking agriculture to subjects like math and science."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A key component of Farm to School programs, serving as a hands-on learning environment where children can engage with healthy food. FoodCorps members actively build and tend these gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04be01c6169d0bdd92b10dca615a3936&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cofounder of FoodCorps">
      <data key="d0">Cofounder of FoodCorps</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An unnamed individual who is identified as a cofounder of FoodCorps and also a staff member of the National Farm to School Network (NFSN). This person advocates for the 'people power' that FoodCorps provides to implement programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04be01c6169d0bdd92b10dca615a3936</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
