    <node id="Appalachian Grown certification process">
      <data key="d0">Appalachian Grown certification process</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A certification program managed by ASAP to identify locally grown food. It was used by the partnership between Growing Minds and Carolina Produce to facilitate sourcing for school cafeterias."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Charlie Jackson">
      <data key="d0">Charlie Jackson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Charlie Jackson is a community organizer and North Carolina native who founded the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project (ASAP) in 1995 and serves as its director. Inspired by Helen and Scott Nearing, he shifted his organization's focus from crop production to the creation and development of markets for small farmers. As a representative of ASAP, Jackson is focused on strategies to attract the younger generation to farming, creating pathways to land ownership through work with land trusts, and providing marketing advice, such as working with local media and developing certified brands like the "Appalachian Grown" certification. He has commented on the success of local food programs, such as those in county school systems, and their integration into distributors' business models. Believing in the interconnectedness of the food system, Charlie Jackson emphasizes a humble, learning-based approach to his community work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c38b37fe24e15bfa43a713c9e38509&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fee18f76430d121ab613984069f209e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5944a88ca415780f3d4838a47d068512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c16ea96bc625614680cc6d8276e6ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2d3545603e988fef215052ef7f681c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Youth Health Crises (Obesity, Diabetes, ADHD)">
      <data key="d0">Youth Health Crises (Obesity, Diabetes, ADHD)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A set of health epidemics, including rising rates of obesity, diabetes, and ADHD among youth, which emerged in the late 1990s and served as the primary catalyst for the creation and growth of the Farm to School movement."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm to School Cooking Program">
      <data key="d0">Farm to School Cooking Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A popular component of the Growing Minds initiative that recruits local chefs to conduct cooking classes in schools. It is tied to the school curriculum, and the program provides funds for local food purchases and cooking kits."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asheville Schools">
      <data key="d0">Asheville Schools</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The initial, smaller school system that the Growing Minds program successfully partnered with to introduce local food, serving as a pilot before expanding to a larger system."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Larger County School System">
      <data key="d0">Larger County School System</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A large school system with 25,000 students and 41 schools that Growing Minds began working with after establishing a sufficient supply of local food through smaller systems."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cc6eed415e2d01ca41ab07d4cac89623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm to School">
      <data key="d0">Farm to School</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Farm to School is a movement, program model, and initiative that connects local farms with educational institutions. The primary goal is to bring healthy, local, and fresh food into school cafeterias and lunchrooms to improve student nutrition. Beyond food procurement, Farm to School also enriches the student experience by integrating educational components such as nutrition classes, farm visits, and gardening. This initiative is promoted by organizations like the USDA and the National Farm to School Network and is a specific focus of JVUF, acting as a thematic category that groups together various programs and organizations with these shared objectives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c0dfef7989eab5ae8b58770f9786cac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d971c6976ab8e71370d85b346b00f467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c16ea96bc625614680cc6d8276e6ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
