    <node id="Growing Gardens of Portland, Oregon">
      <data key="d0">Growing Gardens of Portland, Oregon</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A nonprofit organization based in Portland, Oregon, that supports school garden programs. It provides a three-year commitment to schools to help them build sustainable programs with long-term funding, staffing, and community involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="School Garden Program">
      <data key="d0">School Garden Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An educational initiative to create and maintain gardens in schools. The text outlines key lessons for building a successful program, including securing long-term commitment, forming a committee, starting small, and striving for sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="School Garden Committee">
      <data key="d0">School Garden Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A foundational group, ideally including the principal, teachers, and parents, responsible for providing the vision, motivation, and support for a school's garden program. It handles logistical and curriculum planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nell Tessman">
      <data key="d0">Nell Tessman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Growing Gardens."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An individual named in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The youth educator for Growing Gardens. She suggests piggybacking on existing resources, like after-school clubs, as a low-cost way to integrate gardening into a school.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kelly Elementary School">
      <data key="d0">Kelly Elementary School</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Kelly Elementary School is an educational institution located at 9030 SE Cooper in Portland, Oregon, that serves a lower-income, culturally diverse population. The school is collaborating with the nonprofit organization 'Growing Gardens' to establish a comprehensive school gardening and fresh food program. Representatives for Kelly Elementary School include Bryding Adams, Edwin Marty, and Rachel Reinhart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9297eb734555e7b850045590e4fc5cb6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Students">
      <data key="d0">Students</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Students are a central group within educational institutions, serving as both the primary beneficiaries of and key participants in farm and garden programs. At colleges like Warren Wilson, students actively work in gardens and on farms as part of their education, where they learn practical skills, operate market stalls, and gain real-life experience by dealing with real-world challenges and potential failures. Their investment and labor are crucial for the continuation of these projects, although their high turnover rate can present a significant operational challenge.

Beyond their direct participation, students are powerful change agents on university campuses. Their interest in sustainability and pressure on administration are cited as primary drivers for the "Farm to College" movement and other campus food initiatives, such as pilot programs and commitments to local sourcing. As the population served by school and college cafeterias, students are the target of these programs, which aim to improve their dietary habits. School garden programs, for instance, encourage students to eat more fruits and vegetables through hands-on activities and fun learning environments, addressing the "perverse coupling of weight gain and malnourishment" they can sometimes experience. Ultimately, students gain competency in life skills and educational benefits from their hands-on involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ba8e532ec9e7db3fd3049537a06db80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d971c6976ab8e71370d85b346b00f467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Administration">
      <data key="d0">Administration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The governing body of a university. The text notes that progress in sustainable administration moved in lock-step with progress in campus food, likely due to high visibility and student pressure.&lt;SEP&gt;The leadership body of a school. The text emphasizes that their 'total buy-in,' including long-term funding and staffing commitments, is essential for a school garden program to be effective and avoid failure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a7be9460651dfa3f1395f006df3ef8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ba8e532ec9e7db3fd3049537a06db80</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740848</data>
    </node>
