    </node>
    <node id="Workshops for Educators">
      <data key="d0">Workshops for Educators</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A series of half-day training sessions offered by JVUF for teachers, parents, and community leaders to learn how to use gardens as educational tools and develop effective experiential curricula.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commodity Food">
      <data key="d0">Commodity Food</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of food described as prepared, ready-to-heat, canned, or industrially grown produce bred for a long shelf life. Cafeteria staff are often trained primarily to work with these types of foods, as opposed to fresh, local alternatives.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of industrially produced and prepared foods, such as canned goods and heat-and-serve meals. The text notes that cafeteria staff are often trained to work only with these foods, which is a barrier to introducing fresh food in schools.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d971c6976ab8e71370d85b346b00f467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virginia University">
      <data key="d0">Virginia University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A university in Virginia cited as an example where cafeteria staff were initially unequipped to handle fresh, locally grown heirloom tomatoes. The staff had to be taught how to slice the soft tomatoes by hand, contrasting with the hardier, industrially grown tomatoes they were used to.&lt;SEP&gt;An unnamed university cited in an anecdote to exemplify a widespread problem where cafeteria staff lacked the skills to handle fresh food, highlighting the systemic reliance on commodity food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d971c6976ab8e71370d85b346b00f467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Applied Science">
      <data key="d0">Applied Science</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A term used by JVUF board member Bryding Adams to describe the 'Arts and Science of Agriculture' (ASAP) course, emphasizing its hands-on nature that makes students think differently about food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summer Internships">
      <data key="d0">Summer Internships</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A program designed by JVUF to address farm labor needs during the summer. The organization hires pre-trained graduates from its ASAP course to work as paid interns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Botany">
      <data key="d0">Botany</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A scientific field of study that is part of the interdisciplinary curriculum of the 'Arts and Science of Agriculture' (ASAP) course offered by JVUF.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JVUF">
      <data key="d0">JVUF</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization focused on a 'Farm to School' initiative. It provides a mandatory training program for all county school cafeteria workers to learn about preparing nutritious food and works at policy and practical levels to bring local, fresh food into schools.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d971c6976ab8e71370d85b346b00f467</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm to College Movements">
      <data key="d0">Farm to College Movements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A trend on college campuses driven by an interest in sustainability. These movements involve increasing local food in cafeterias, creating campus gardens, and offering food-related academic programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d971c6976ab8e71370d85b346b00f467</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hardiness Zones">
      <data key="d0">Hardiness Zones</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Hardiness zones are geographical areas defined by climate conditions, relevant for determining which plants and crops can be successfully grown.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health-food Stores">
      <data key="d0">Health-food Stores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health-food stores are a type of retail market where farmers can sell their products, particularly through wholesale arrangements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
