    <node id="Deep Economy">
      <data key="d0">Deep Economy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A book written by Bill McKibben in which he argues that the conventional food system is heavily dependent on oil.&lt;SEP&gt;A concept, detailed in William McKibben's book of the same name, advocating for more local and durable economies as a solution to global challenges like rising energy costs and climate instability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d6381862167ee0d108723409eae36d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Land-grant Institutions">
      <data key="d0">Land-grant Institutions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of universities traditionally responsible for agricultural education and research. The text notes that liberal arts schools are now also becoming involved in food-related studies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liberal Arts Schools">
      <data key="d0">Liberal Arts Schools</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of educational institutions that are increasingly incorporating food-related topics like planning, marketing, and history into their curricula, a field traditionally dominated by land-grant institutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warren Wilson College">
      <data key="d0">Warren Wilson College</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Warren Wilson College is a small liberal arts college located in Asheville, western North Carolina, that was founded in 1894. It is a designated work college operating on a holistic ethic known as "The Triad," which integrates academics, work, and service-learning. A central feature of this model is its prominent agricultural program, which includes a 5-acre garden and a large farm, with sources citing its size as between 275 and 340 acres.

This farm and garden serve as an "outdoor laboratory," providing students with hands-on work experience as part of their education. The college is a primary case study for integrating its campus farm into its food services, with the dining service purchasing produce from the garden and the institution aiming to source a significant portion of its food locally. The farm grosses approximately $200,000 annually and the college's large-scale food-service program is used as an example to illustrate business opportunities for wholesale distributors. The college was attended by Jamie and Amy Ager, for whom the farm provided crucial experience in marketing local food and the community offered a strong social support network for their new business.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e970626c7a63b367949cb5600957bcab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c8f3e81fd6708a9852f46f0d4ac0a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warren Wilson College Farm">
      <data key="d0">Warren Wilson College Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Located in Asheville, NC, the Warren Wilson College Farm is a 340-acre, student-run farm that serves as a key feature and central component of Warren Wilson College's work and educational mission. The farm is an organization managed by a team of two full-time staff members and 25-30 students. Its agricultural output includes rotated crops like corn and barley, grass-fed beef, and pastured pork, poultry, and eggs. Individuals such as Chase Hubbard and Karen Joslyn are associated with or represent the farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southern Blue Ridge Mountains">
      <data key="d0">Southern Blue Ridge Mountains</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The mountain range where Warren Wilson College and its farm are situated."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Triad">
      <data key="d0">The Triad</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The founding holistic educational philosophy of Warren Wilson College. It is an imperative for three paths to educating the whole person: academics for the mind, service-learning for the heart, and work for the hands."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chase Hubbard">
      <data key="d0">Chase Hubbard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Chase Hubbard is the farm manager at Warren Wilson College, where he also serves as a representative for the college farm. As a graduate of the college himself, Hubbard is a firm believer in its holistic educational model. He believes that a focus on production creates intense, real-life learning opportunities for students, making the farm more than just an educational "petting zoo." Hubbard emphasizes that the farm's primary goal is to offer these educational experiences, rather than to turn a profit. In his role, he oversees student work with cattle, pigs, and field crops, and has noted that the students who apply are typically very ambitious and drawn to the strenuous work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ian Robertson">
      <data key="d0">Ian Robertson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ian Robertson is the Dean of Work at Warren Wilson College, where he is also a graduate and serves as a representative for the college's farm. In 1981, he was responsible for creating the college's garden from a one-acre plot, which has since expanded. Robertson believes a college farm should be fully integrated into the institutional mission, functioning as an "outdoor laboratory" where students can learn from real-life consequences. He advocates for hiring knowledgeable managers and treating them as faculty. Furthermore, he suggests that any wholesale service for institutions should not only source food but also take on the role of educating those institutions about what is seasonally and locally available.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad0d4e7d44014acab56e26cbaa12a896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plow Day">
      <data key="d0">Plow Day</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"An annual event held at Warren Wilson College where the farm's Belgian draft horses demonstrate their ability to pull logs from the woodlands."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm-To-College Food Movement">
      <data key="d0">Farm-To-College Food Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A movement focused on changing how people, especially students, eat and think about their food systems. It encourages the use of fresh, locally sourced food in college settings and promotes the academic study of food systems."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warren Wilson College Students">
      <data key="d0">Warren Wilson College Students</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The student body of Warren Wilson College, numbering around 900 in 2010. They are required to work 15 hours a week and complete 100 hours of service-learning. Many work on the farm and in the garden, and are described as graduating with competence and confidence."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warren Wilson College Garden">
      <data key="d0">Warren Wilson College Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A 5-acre, student-run garden at Warren Wilson College. It operates with one full-time manager and students, producing vegetables, fruits, flowers, and herbs which are sold to the dining service, campus markets, and a CSA. It was started in 1981 by Ian Robertson."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warren Wilson College Dining Service">
      <data key="d0">Warren Wilson College Dining Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The department within Warren Wilson College responsible for providing food in the student dining halls. It purchases fresh produce, meats, and eggs directly from the college's own farm and garden."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Appalachian Boys">
      <data key="d0">Appalachian Boys</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The original demographic for which Warren Wilson College was founded in 1894. The school's purpose was to educate and feed boys from Appalachian farms who were expected to return to farm work."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Belgian Draft Horses">
      <data key="d0">Belgian Draft Horses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A pair of large workhorses used on the Warren Wilson College Farm for agricultural tasks such as plowing fields, pulling a manure spreader, and pulling logs from the woodlands."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f8baa196ceabfc9bc0a6cc79c04127e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robertson">
      <data key="d0">Robertson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An expert, likely affiliated with Warren Wilson College, who provides detailed advice on integrating campus-grown food into college food services. His recommendations cover forming stakeholder committees, setting fair market prices, and handling food processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Founder of the Warren Wilson College garden in 1981. He initiated the project to teach students gardening skills and to supply fresh produce to the college's dining service. He implemented Alan Chadwick's biodynamic double-digging method to foster a deep student connection to the soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karen Joslin">
      <data key="d0">Karen Joslin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Karen Joslin is an individual mentioned in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Karen Joslin is the garden manager at Warren Wilson College. She advises colleges to start farm programs small, suggests that programs in agriculture or environmental sciences provide a good student base, and recommends hiring managers with 10-15 years of practical farming experience.&lt;SEP&gt;The current manager of the Warren Wilson Garden. She uses the garden as a teaching tool and deals with operational challenges like crop planning, seasonality, and student turnover.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alan Chadwick">
      <data key="d0">Alan Chadwick</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The originator of the biodynamic method of double-digging, an arduous, hand-executed process that Robertson implemented in the Warren Wilson Garden to help students understand the soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Larson Family">
      <data key="d0">Larson Family</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A family that managed the Warren Wilson Farm for over half a century, from the 1930s to the 1990s, growing and preparing food for the college students.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warren Wilson Garden">
      <data key="d0">Warren Wilson Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A 5-acre garden at Warren Wilson College, started in 1981. It serves as a learning laboratory for students, grows food organically, supplies produce to the college cafeterias, and sells at local farmers' markets. It faces challenges with seasonality and student turnover.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warren Wilson Farm">
      <data key="d0">Warren Wilson Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A 275-acre working farm at Warren Wilson College, largely run by student crews. It raises broilers, beef cows, and hogs, selling its products to the community. The farm emphasizes hands-on learning, self-evaluation, and innovative techniques like mob-stocking. It grosses around $200,000 annually, covering most of its expenses.&lt;SEP&gt;A farm at Warren Wilson College, distinct from the garden, where students work with livestock (cattle, pigs, broilers) and field crops. Managed by Chase Hubbard, it has a history of over a hundred years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="College Garden">
      <data key="d0">College Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept representing a garden on a college campus used for educational purposes. The text highlights common challenges for this type of entity, such as student turnover and aligning production with the academic calendar.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="College Administration">
      <data key="d0">College Administration</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The governing body of Warren Wilson College. Robertson's initial strategy for the garden involved getting students deeply invested so the administration couldn't easily shut down the project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="College Cafeterias">
      <data key="d0">College Cafeterias</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The on-campus dining services at Warren Wilson College. They purchase approximately 60% of the produce, by weight, from the Warren Wilson Garden, though their demand does not always align with what grows well in the garden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biodynamic Method Of Double-Digging">
      <data key="d0">Biodynamic Method Of Double-Digging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific, arduous, and manual gardening technique implemented by Robertson, based on Alan Chadwick's methods. It is used in the Warren Wilson Garden as a teaching tool to help students understand the soil intimately and as a 'great winter project'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="USDA Organic Certification">
      <data key="d0">USDA Organic Certification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A government label for organic food production issued by the USDA. Warren Wilson Garden grows food organically but has deliberately chosen not to pursue this certification, relying instead on the college's reputation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Processing">
      <data key="d0">Food Processing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An activity the Warren Wilson Garden has begun exploring to deal with the challenge of seasonality. Currently, this mainly involves freezing vegetables to make summer produce available in the winter, though they face challenges with quality due to a lack of expensive flash-freezing equipment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16f698d31ba9c05fea44e2d7f53e6d36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Julius Stuart">
      <data key="d0">Julius Stuart</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A rising junior at Warren Wilson College in the summer of 2009. He is a member of the farm crew, works with cattle, and provides a tour of the pasture fields. He discusses experimenting with mob-stocking and, while he loves the work, does not plan to be a full-time farmer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hubbard">
      <data key="d0">Hubbard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A manager or lead staff member of the Warren Wilson Farm. He describes his student crew members as responsible and communicative, outlines the operational finances of the farm, and gives advice on how another college could start a similar program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colleen Vetti">
      <data key="d0">Colleen Vetti</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A rising junior and member of the swine crew at the Warren Wilson Farm. She describes the work as fulfilling and more educational than some classes. She participated in a 'hog cook' and discusses the farm's practice of selling antibiotic-free pork.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual named in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warren Wilson">
      <data key="d0">Warren Wilson</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A college that hosts a comprehensive student-run farm program, which serves as a model for experiential education in agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Crew">
      <data key="d0">Farm Crew</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The student work crew at Warren Wilson Farm. Members must first volunteer to be accepted. The crew is responsible for all aspects of the farm's operation and is described as self-running and self-evaluating.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mob-Stocking">
      <data key="d0">Mob-Stocking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural technique involving grazing livestock at an ultra-high density in a specific area. The Warren Wilson Farm crew experimented with this method as an alternative to cutting hay, based on an article they read.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swine Crew">
      <data key="d0">Swine Crew</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific animal crew within the larger Warren Wilson Farm Crew, responsible for the care of pigs. Tasks include cleaning pens, feeding, and participating in the slaughtering and butchering process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swine">
      <data key="d0">Swine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of livestock raised on the Warren Wilson Farm, managed by the Swine Crew, which includes Colleen Vetti. They are housed in pens and fields, and processed on-site for antibiotic-free pork sold to the community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Broilers">
      <data key="d0">Broilers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of livestock (chickens raised for meat) owned by Polyface and managed by the Model A farmer in 15 broiler shelters. The farmer found managing them to be physically demanding.&lt;SEP&gt;Chickens raised for meat, representing a recent addition to the Warren Wilson Farm's operations. Sales from broilers, along with beef and pork, contribute to the farm's annual revenue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3699752bc52dee1358ad76a26a9c6fb9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hog Cook">
      <data key="d0">Hog Cook</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An intense event at the Warren Wilson Farm involving the killing, slaughtering, and butchering of a hog. Student crew members, such as Colleen Vetti, participate in the process as part of their hands-on learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antibiotic-Free Pork">
      <data key="d0">Antibiotic-Free Pork</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific product standard for pork sold by the Warren Wilson Farm. This rule dictates that any animal treated with antibiotics cannot be sold to the community, influencing herd management and sales decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pasture Fields">
      <data key="d0">Pasture Fields</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific area on the Warren Wilson Farm where the cattle are grazed. It is the setting where Julius Stuart explains the process of moving cattle and the crew's experiments with mob-stocking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alfalfa Field">
      <data key="d0">Alfalfa Field</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific field on the Warren Wilson Farm where the sows (female pigs) are kept. It is the location where Colleen Vetti conducts health inspections on the animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Market">
      <data key="d0">Local Market</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the consumer base within the immediate region. Diablo Burger's role is described as a 'first step' for Diablo Trust ranches to enter this market and build a reputation for their product.&lt;SEP&gt;The sales outlet through which the Warren Wilson Farm sells its products, such as quarter-hogs, to the surrounding community. It serves as the farm's connection to local consumers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-087cef03c5ab256c59a3f536c4533b6d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University Of Missouri">
      <data key="d0">University Of Missouri</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095</data>
      <data key="d2">The Rural Sociology Department is a constituent academic department within the larger University of Missouri institution.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cattle Crew">
      <data key="d0">Cattle Crew</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized team within the larger Warren Wilson Farm Crew, responsible for managing the farm's cattle. Julius Stuart is a member, and their duties include moving the herd and implementing grazing strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da1b16361a783bc112824aaa6fa12fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="College Farming Program">
      <data key="d0">College Farming Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A College Farming Program is an educational model discussed in the text, involving a farm or garden on a college campus. Its purpose is to provide students with real-life work experience, teach responsibility, and complement the institution's academic mission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Institutional Mission">
      <data key="d0">Institutional Mission</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core concept representing the guiding principles and purpose of a college. The text stresses that a farm program must be aligned with and complement the institutional mission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Outdoor Laboratory">
      <data key="d0">Outdoor Laboratory</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept used by Ian Robertson to describe the farm at Warren Wilson College. It represents a hands-on learning environment where students face real-life decisions and consequences, distinct from a traditional classroom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm and Garden Managers">
      <data key="d0">Farm and Garden Managers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A professional role responsible for a college farm's operations and the mentorship of students. The text advises they should have extensive practical experience, be patient mentors, and be treated as faculty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Faculty">
      <data key="d0">Faculty</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The academic staff of a college. The text suggests that farm and garden managers should be classified and paid as faculty due to their significant educational and mentorship role.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liberal Arts Colleges">
      <data key="d0">Liberal Arts Colleges</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of higher education institution mentioned in the text. Ian Robertson distinguishes between those that prepare students for jobs versus those, like Warren Wilson, that prepare students for a life of work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1997 Census Of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">1997 Census Of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f1ce27dcb115879ec30587ba69b4095</data>
      <data key="d2">The 1997 Census of Agriculture is a national survey conducted within the United States, providing official government data on agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Federally Subsidized Work-Study">
      <data key="d0">Federally Subsidized Work-Study</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A student employment program mentioned by Ian Robertson as an opportunity for learning, even on campuses without farms, by encouraging students to reflect on their work experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sociology Professors">
      <data key="d0">Sociology Professors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of faculty used as an example by Ian Robertson to illustrate how professors can help students connect their work-study experiences to academic concepts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Creative Writing Professors">
      <data key="d0">Creative Writing Professors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of faculty used as an example by Ian Robertson to show how educators can prompt students to find deeper meaning and stories in their work experiences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Campus Stakeholder Committee">
      <data key="d0">Campus Stakeholder Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An inclusive group proposed by Robertson, essential for making decisions about sourcing campus-grown food. It includes key players like foodservice managers, vendors, business managers, faculty, and farm managers to set goals and define terms like 'local food'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Economic Crisis of 2008">
      <data key="d0">Economic Crisis of 2008</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A global financial crisis mentioned as one of several major stresses that have tested community resilience, highlighting the need for systems that can withstand such shocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contracted Food Vendor">
      <data key="d0">Contracted Food Vendor</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A key stakeholder in campus food systems, responsible for providing food in cafeterias. The text suggests they must be part of negotiations and can be contractually obligated to purchase local and campus-grown food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Food Committee">
      <data key="d0">Local Food Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A committee, mentioned in the context of Warren Wilson College, responsible for setting the standards and definition for what qualifies as 'local' food for the institution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Third Party Food Processor">
      <data key="d0">Third Party Food Processor</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A contracted, outside entity recommended by Robertson for processing abundant harvests from a campus garden or farm, addressing the time constraints of both garden and food-service staff.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Over 40 Countries">
      <data key="d0">Over 40 Countries</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of nations that have recognized the threat of genetically engineered crops by mandating their labeling or banning them from agriculture altogether.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Global Stressors">
      <data key="d0">Global Stressors</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A collection of crises and threats that test community resilience, including tsunamis, hurricanes, earthquakes, flooding, rising oil prices, food shortages, riots, and terrorism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Campus Garden or Farm">
      <data key="d0">Campus Garden or Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural facility on a college campus that serves as both an educational tool and a source of production for campus food services. It is central to the discussion of local food sourcing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biodiversity">
      <data key="d0">Biodiversity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biodiversity refers to the variety of life, particularly within a specific habitat or ecosystem, and is considered a central theme and key topic. It specifically encompasses agricultural diversity, including crops, animals, and heritage seeds and breeds. Biodiversity is described as the foundation of agriculture and is crucial for human and local resilience, providing essential "options" for food, medicine, and survival through various ecosystem services. Its loss is presented as a catastrophic threat to human well-being and food security, with genetically engineered crops identified as a specific threat. For entities like Three Sisters Farm, increasing biodiversity is a primary goal, which is pursued by expanding habitats and adding ponds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heritage Seeds and Breeds">
      <data key="d0">Heritage Seeds and Breeds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional, time-tested plant and animal varieties that are well-suited to local environments and are not dependent on synthetic chemicals. Supporting them is described as a strategy to enhance community resilience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetically Engineered Crops">
      <data key="d0">Genetically Engineered Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Crops modified through genetic engineering, presented as a threat to resilience by potentially destroying native and heritage seed races. Many countries have regulated or banned them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi">
      <data key="d0">Hopi</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hopi are a Native American tribe and Indigenous people of the Southwest, located in northern Arizona in areas like the village of Moenkopi and near Tuba City. Their culture, life, and ceremonies are described as revolving around corn. Along with the Navajo, the Hopi perfected traditional dryland farming techniques for the high desert, which allowed them to demonstrate extreme resilience and food self-sufficiency, particularly during the Dust Bowl era.

However, the Hopi's traditional way of life is now considered endangered. There is a decline in the number of traditional farmers, such as Carl and Luther Honeyestewa, and a decreased reliance on diverse, traditional crops. This has led to a loss of crop varieties and has had negative health impacts on the community. Their language, beliefs, and culture are under threat of disappearing, a situation some believe was foretold by a prophecy. In response, efforts are underway to preserve their agricultural legacy by inspiring the youth, with community members like Rose Marie Williams contributing through teaching in Hopi schools.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gary Nabhan">
      <data key="d0">Gary Nabhan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Gary Nabhan is an ethnobotanist and author who has been described as the "patron saint of the local food movement." He is the author of the 2009 book "Where Our Food Comes From" and has chronicled the agricultural life of the Hopi and Navajo. His research highlights their historical food self-sufficiency, its subsequent decline, and verifies that their native crops are nutritionally superior to modern imported foods. Furthermore, Nabhan was instrumental in inspiring Derrick Widmark's creation of Diablo Burger by introducing a successful conservation and business model to the Diablo Trust.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c575c019780e689390cd685c690159f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1fa8277818b65f8d3d6665242021ad9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nikolay Vavilov">
      <data key="d0">Nikolay Vavilov</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Russian pioneer in seed and plant banking whose life and work were chronicled by Gary Nabhan. He is a significant figure in the history of preserving agricultural biodiversity.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual named in the text, historically a prominent Russian botanist and geneticist known for his work on crop diversity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Elk">
      <data key="d0">Black Elk</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An Oglala Sioux holy man (1863–1950) whose quote from 'Black Elk Speaks' is used to illustrate the Native American worldview that power, life, and wisdom operate in circles, underscoring the value of learning from the past.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cary Fowler">
      <data key="d0">Cary Fowler</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A self-proclaimed 'biodiversity warrior' who works to raise awareness about the importance of crop diversity. In a TED lecture, he emphasized that this diversity is the biological foundation of agriculture and is facing a mass extinction.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual who learned about the importance of crop diversity from Harlan and champions biodiversity as a source of 'options' that create resilience for the human race in growing food.&lt;SEP&gt;Cary Fowler is an expert who gave a TED Talk in July 2009 titled 'One Seed At A Time: Protecting the Future of Food.'</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c575c019780e689390cd685c690159f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jack Harlan">
      <data key="d0">Jack Harlan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A respected scientist who greatly influenced Cary Fowler. Harlan claimed that crop diversity is all that 'stands between us and catastrophic starvation' and was one of the first to identify the mass extinction of agricultural crops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization">
      <data key="d0">United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An international organization that, in 2006, estimated a significant loss of global biodiversity, including the loss of one animal breed per month and about three-quarters of original agricultural crop varieties since 1900.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="United Nations">
      <data key="d0">United Nations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An international body that initiated the 2005 Millennium Ecosystem Assessment and supported a 2010 study on biodiversity. Both concluded that human actions are causing rapid, and potentially irreversible, losses in global biodiversity.&lt;SEP&gt;An international governing body, inferred as the parent organization of the 'UN World Food Program' and the publisher of the 'UN report'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TED">
      <data key="d0">TED</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization that hosts influential lectures (Technology Entertainment and Design). It provided a platform for Cary Fowler to speak about the critical issue of crop diversity loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tuba City Area">
      <data key="d0">Tuba City Area</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A location within the Painted Desert where the Hopi and Navajo peoples lived and demonstrated remarkable food self-sufficiency through diverse agricultural strategies, even during severe droughts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Painted Desert">
      <data key="d0">Painted Desert</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A desert region with low rainfall that was home to the Hopi and Navajo. It serves as the backdrop for their historical agricultural resilience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Millennium Ecosystem Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Millennium Ecosystem Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A major 2005 UN-initiated scientific report. It concluded that human activities have accelerated biodiversity loss more rapidly in the past 50 years than at any other time in human history.&lt;SEP&gt;A major report conducted by the UN that explains how biodiversity supports human well-being through four categories of services: provisioning, cultural, supporting, and regulating. It is cited to emphasize biodiversity's role in creating resilience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heritage Crops">
      <data key="d0">Heritage Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional, native crop varieties passed down through generations. The text champions their preservation for their cultural significance, superior nutritional value, and their role in ensuring community resilience and food security.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetically Modified Organisms">
      <data key="d0">Genetically Modified Organisms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetically engineered crops presented in the text as a threat to agricultural resilience and biodiversity. They are noted to infect and destroy native and heritage seed races, leading many countries to label or ban them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Nation People">
      <data key="d0">First Nation People</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A term used synonymously with Native Americans in the text. This group is actively rediscovering and revitalizing their native foods and agricultural practices to combat health issues and enhance community resilience, offering a powerful lesson for the rest of America.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oglala Sioux">
      <data key="d0">Oglala Sioux</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific Native American tribe to which the holy man Black Elk belonged. His philosophy is quoted in the text to represent a broader Native American worldview centered on cyclical patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obesity and Diabetes Epidemic">
      <data key="d0">Obesity and Diabetes Epidemic</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A major health crisis, described as diet-induced, affecting Native American communities. The text frames the revitalization of traditional foods as a direct response and cure for this epidemic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grassroots Food Movements">
      <data key="d0">Grassroots Food Movements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Social movements across America focused on local and sustainable food. The efforts of Native Americans are compared to these movements, but noted as unique for their strong emphasis on preserving biodiversity and food heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Earth">
      <data key="d0">Earth</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The planet, mentioned both in a philosophical context by Black Elk as being round and in a scientific context as the place where human actions are causing irreversible losses in the diversity of life.&lt;SEP&gt;The planet, which Marie metaphorically describes as a 'house' that humans are 'renting' from a good spirit, implying a need for stewardship and respect.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a371db96fe54cad23b00f1b8eac0eefc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Harlan">
      <data key="d0">Harlan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An expert who informed Cary Fowler that crop diversity is the 'biological foundation of agriculture' and warned of a 'mass extinction' occurring in agricultural fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UN">
      <data key="d0">UN</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The United Nations, an international organization whose 'Millennium Ecosystem Assessment' is cited to explain the ways in which biodiversity contributes to long-term human well-being. Its agency, the FAO, also provides relevant data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UN FAO">
      <data key="d0">UN FAO</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations. It is cited for a 1993 report stating that a significant percentage of prescriptions in the United States are derived from natural sources like plants and microorganisms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="North Leupp Family Farm">
      <data key="d0">North Leupp Family Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The North Leupp Family Farm is a cooperative, community-based farming project located in Leupp, on the Diné (Navajo) Nation in northwest Arizona. As a community program, it provides land, education, and infrastructure, such as irrigation, to members who wish to grow their own food. The farm's mission is to reestablish traditional Navajo agricultural and culinary practices to promote food security, self-sufficiency, and health. It is often cited as an example of using cultural heritage and traditional foods to address diet-related health issues among Indigenous people. The project aims to rebuild the connection between people and the land, engage the community in growing food, and help rediscover a sense of place. Key representatives include Stacey Jensen, Tyrone Thompson, and Dennis Walker, and the farm maintains an online presence with a website detailing its operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c575c019780e689390cd685c690159f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northwest Arizona">
      <data key="d0">Northwest Arizona</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The geographical region where the North Leupp Family Farm is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo reservation">
      <data key="d0">Navajo reservation</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific territory west of Flagstaff where the North Leupp Family Farm is situated and serves its community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Little Colorado River">
      <data key="d0">Little Colorado River</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A river whose fertile flood plains provide the loamy soil for the North Leupp Family Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stacey Jensen">
      <data key="d0">Stacey Jensen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for the North Leupp Family Farm."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The manager of the North Leupp Family Farm in 2009 and a landscape architect by training. He works to bring elders and youth back to farming and oversees the farm's operations and community outreach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coconino Aquifer">
      <data key="d0">Coconino Aquifer</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">An underground aquifer that serves as the water source for the North Leupp Family Farm's irrigation system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biological Foundation Of Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Biological Foundation Of Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept described by Harlan as the role crop diversity plays in agriculture. It represents the raw material for evolution in agricultural crops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mass Extinction in Fields">
      <data key="d0">Mass Extinction in Fields</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A phenomenon of disappearing crop diversity in agricultural fields, which Harlan noted was occurring largely unnoticed by the public.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Well-being">
      <data key="d0">Human Well-being</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A state of health and prosperity that, according to the UN's Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, is supported by biodiversity in the long term through various services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Provisioning Services">
      <data key="d0">Provisioning Services</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the four categories of services provided by biodiversity, as defined by the UN. It includes the provision of tangible goods like food, fuel, genetic resources, and fresh water.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cultural Services">
      <data key="d0">Cultural Services</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the four categories of services provided by biodiversity, as defined by the UN. It encompasses non-material benefits such as spiritual values, knowledge systems, education, and aesthetic value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supporting Services">
      <data key="d0">Supporting Services</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the four categories of services provided by biodiversity, as defined by the UN. It includes fundamental processes that support other ecosystem services, such as soil formation, nutrient cycling, and primary production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulating Services">
      <data key="d0">Regulating Services</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the four categories of services provided by biodiversity, as defined by the UN. It includes benefits obtained from the regulation of ecosystem processes like climate regulation, pollination, and pest and disease control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resilience">
      <data key="d0">Resilience</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key benefit derived from biodiversity, described as the ability to survive and adapt to challenges like resource stress. It is enabled by the 'options' that biodiversity provides for food and medicine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indigenous and Naturopathic Medicines">
      <data key="d0">Indigenous and Naturopathic Medicines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical systems, including Chinese and other indigenous traditions, that rely heavily on natural herbs and plants for treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmaceutical Medicines">
      <data key="d0">Pharmaceutical Medicines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Manufactured drugs, a significant portion of which, according to the UN FAO, are derived from plants, microorganisms, and animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flagstaff">
      <data key="d0">Flagstaff</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Flagstaff is a city in northern Arizona that serves as a geographical reference point for the location of the Navajo reservation. It is home to several restaurants, such as Diablo Burger and Brix, which face logistical challenges in sourcing food, leading them to form partnerships. The residents of Flagstaff are described as feeling a strong connection to the surrounding landscape, a sentiment that Diablo Burger aims to enhance. The restaurant's mission is to connect the city's community with local ranches by providing an authentic, locally-sourced food experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-087cef03c5ab256c59a3f536c4533b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1fa8277818b65f8d3d6665242021ad9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Indian">
      <data key="d0">American Indian</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A broad demographic group whose farming traditions are being restored by efforts like the North Leupp Family Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Elders">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Elders</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group within the Navajo community that is easily engaged by the North Leupp Family Farm's project to restore traditional agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Youth">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Youth</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group within the Navajo community that Stacey Jensen notes is harder to engage in farming and requires step-by-step teaching. The farm runs programs for youth groups.&lt;SEP&gt;A demographic group within the Navajo community. Jonathan Yazzie identifies them as a key to the future of traditional farming, noting their interest and willingness to volunteer in activities like planting for the elderly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0013e0bf73a3def4eb11a0a48ef9234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jensen">
      <data key="d0">Jensen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jensen is a person involved in the Navajo community who advocates for using and saving heirloom seeds to grow traditional squash. They express a desire to establish a seed bank to ensure seed availability and believe the community can achieve self-sufficiency through its community farm by reviving traditional family-based farming cooperation.&lt;SEP&gt;Jensen is the manager of the community farm. He sets guidelines, such as no pesticides or exotic seeds, and observes the positive health and social impacts the farm has on the community. He aims to establish a seed bank and believes the community can become more self-sufficient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Woman">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Woman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An elderly Navajo woman who grew up farming in New Mexico and now farms a 1-acre plot at the community farm in Leupp with her husband. She lost 30 pounds in one season by working on the farm and eating the food she grew, inspiring her daughter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Community Farm">
      <data key="d0">The Community Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A community farming project in the Navajo Nation, operated by the NLFF and managed by Jensen. It provides land, hosts workshops, and encourages traditional farming with heirloom seeds. The farm serves as a therapeutic space and has led to significant health improvements and renewed cultural interest among community members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NLFF">
      <data key="d0">NLFF</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NLFF (presumably the Navajo-Leupp Family Farm) is the organization whose mission is to run the community farm. Its goal is to re-engage the local community in traditional farming and culinary practices to foster healthy lifestyles and reestablish the spiritual, symbiotic relationship with the land (Mother Earth).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="US Government">
      <data key="d0">US Government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical entity described as having instituted a 'scorched earth' campaign against the Diné people in the mid-1800s to force their submission and assimilation, leading to their internment and the disruption of their traditional food systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diné (The People)">
      <data key="d0">Diné (The People)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The traditional name for the Navajo people. The text describes their history as self-sufficient farmers and gatherers before the US government's intervention disrupted their culture, connection to the land, and traditional diet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diet-Related Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Diet-Related Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of health issues including diabetes, circulatory ailments, stroke, and obesity. The text links the prevalence of these diseases among the Diné to the forced shift from a traditional diet to a 'modern' one consisting of government food handouts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heirloom Seeds">
      <data key="d0">Heirloom Seeds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Heirloom Seeds are traditional seed varieties passed down through generations. Jensen highlights the struggle to obtain them and the desire for a seed bank to preserve traditional squash varieties that are unavailable in supermarkets.&lt;SEP&gt;Traditional, non-hybrid seeds that are passed down through generations. The community farm encourages their use to grow traditional crops, though Jensen notes that sourcing them is a yearly struggle, prompting his desire for a seed bank.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mother Earth">
      <data key="d0">Mother Earth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A spiritual concept central to the NLFF's mission, representing the essential, symbiotic relationship between the Diné people and the natural world. The farm aims to reestablish this connection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leupp">
      <data key="d0">Leupp</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific location within the Navajo Nation where the elderly Navajo woman and her husband farm their 1-acre plot at the community farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fort Sumner">
      <data key="d0">Fort Sumner</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical site in eastern New Mexico where the Diné people were subjected to four years of forced internment following the US government's 'scorched earth' campaign.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Youth Groups">
      <data key="d0">Youth Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Youth Groups are mentioned as visitors to the community farm. Their visits are seen by the manager, Jensen, as a positive sign, with many young people leaving inspired by the idea of farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Workshops and Seasonal Kickoff">
      <data key="d0">Workshops and Seasonal Kickoff</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An annual event hosted by the community farm for the entire community. It includes games, activities, and educational sessions on how to use the farm fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="'Scorched Earth' Campaign">
      <data key="d0">'Scorched Earth' Campaign</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical military campaign instituted by the US government in the mid-1800s. Its objective was to destroy the resources of the Diné people to force them into submission and assimilation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treaty of 1868">
      <data key="d0">Treaty of 1868</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical treaty signed by the Diné people which secured their release from forced internment at Fort Sumner and allowed them to return to their homelands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Government Food Handouts">
      <data key="d0">Government Food Handouts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of sustenance the Diné were forced to rely upon after losing their farmlands and hunting grounds. This reliance led to a drastic and unhealthy deviation from their traditional diet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Crops">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Specific types of produce, such as traditional squash, that are grown at the farm using heirloom seeds. These crops are valued for their cultural significance and are not typically available in local supermarkets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seed Bank">
      <data key="d0">Seed Bank</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A facility that Jensen, the farm manager, wishes to establish. Its purpose would be to store and preserve traditional heirloom seeds, ensuring a stable supply for the community and promoting seed sovereignty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mainstream America">
      <data key="d0">Mainstream America</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A cultural concept representing the dominant, non-indigenous American lifestyle, including interests like 'rock and roll'. The text suggests that the community's children are beginning to turn away from this influence and back toward their traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indigenous Nations">
      <data key="d0">Indigenous Nations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A broad category of peoples, including the Navajo, whose cultures and existence are threatened by the disconnection from their land and traditional food systems. The text frames the Navajo's struggle as part of this larger context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8155414292dfdc925038049e610bde08</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An agricultural outreach program operating c/o Developing Innovations in Navajo Education Inc. (DINÉ Inc.) in Flagstaff, AZ. Representatives include Kyril Calsoyas, the Nez family, Jamescita Peshlakai, and Thomas Walker, Jr."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach (NNTAO) is a program based in Cameron, Arizona, dedicated to increasing the knowledge and practice of traditional dryland agriculture in the Navajo Nation. As a program of DINÉ, Inc., it aims to boost food self-reliance, address food-related health concerns, and helps communities establish gardens, mentors ranchers, and sponsors educational workshops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cameron, Arizona">
      <data key="d0">Cameron, Arizona</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Cameron, Arizona is the geographical location where the Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach program is based.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Developing Innovations In Navajo Education (DINÉ), Inc.">
      <data key="d0">Developing Innovations In Navajo Education (DINÉ), Inc.</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Developing Innovations In Navajo Education (DINÉ), Inc. is a non-profit organization that runs the Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach program. It hired Jamescita Peshlakai in 2009 to lead the program's efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persian Gulf War">
      <data key="d0">Persian Gulf War</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Persian Gulf War is a military conflict mentioned as part of Jamescita Peshlakai's personal history, as she is a U.S. Army combat veteran of this war.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Nation Food Policy Council">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Nation Food Policy Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Navajo Nation Food Policy Council is an independent ad hoc organization being formed to improve the Navajo food system. Its formation is being facilitated by Jamescita Peshlakai, and it held its foundational meeting on August 26, 2010.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Taos Pueblo">
      <data key="d0">Taos Pueblo</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Taos Pueblo is a tribal nation mentioned as an example of a community that has become gated to control access. This is used to illustrate the trend of some Navajo people becoming wary of cultural exploitation by outsiders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Farm">
      <data key="d0">Community Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Community Farm is a collaborative agricultural project on the Navajo reservation. Jensen believes it is a key component for the community to become more self-sufficient and represents a return to traditional cooperative farming practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dryland Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Dryland Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dryland Agriculture is a traditional farming method that relies on natural rainfall without irrigation. The Navajo Nation Traditional Agricultural Outreach program is specifically intended to increase knowledge and practice of this technique.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Self-Reliance">
      <data key="d0">Food Self-Reliance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Food Self-Reliance is a primary objective for the agricultural programs in the Navajo Nation. It signifies the community's ability to produce its own food, thereby reducing dependency on external food sources and improving food security.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food-Related Health Concerns">
      <data key="d0">Food-Related Health Concerns</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Food-Related Health Concerns refer to the epidemics of obesity and diabetes on the reservation. The agricultural initiatives, led by figures like Jamescita Peshlakai, aim to combat these issues by restoring traditional diets and food systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beginning Ranchers">
      <data key="d0">Beginning Ranchers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beginning Ranchers are a group of 50 new Navajo ranchers who are part of a training program initiated by the NNTAO. They are being mentored to learn traditional farming and ranching practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Workshops">
      <data key="d0">Community Workshops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Community Workshops are a primary educational tool used by the NNTAO. These workshops aim to build skills in all aspects of the Navajo food system, from crop selection and butchery to canning and nutrition, focusing on Navajo heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Heritage Food and Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Heritage Food and Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Navajo Heritage Food and Agriculture is the comprehensive traditional food and farming system of the Navajo people. The efforts described in the text, including community gardens and workshops, are all aimed at restoring interest and skills in this heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter Houses">
      <data key="d0">Chapter Houses</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter Houses are the local governance entities within the Navajo Nation. The text mentions their reluctance to grant permission for community gardens due to having become accustomed to a more 'Americanized' lifestyle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Way of Life">
      <data key="d0">American Way of Life</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Way of Life is described as a cultural trend within the Navajo Nation characterized by convenience, fast food, and dependence on stores. Jamescita Peshlakai identifies it as a significant challenge opposing the restoration of traditional, self-sufficient lifestyles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a2fd52898d080f7706c67afe05d27ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peshlakai">
      <data key="d0">Peshlakai</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A community organizer who works to establish community gardens on the Navajo reservation. She focuses on fostering community ownership, organizing events, working with elders, and promoting traditional foods to sustain Navajo cultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jonathan Yazzie">
      <data key="d0">Jonathan Yazzie</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jonathan Yazzie is a Navajo farmer from a family with a long, multigenerational farming heritage. Originally from Sand Springs and now living in Tolani Lake, he is the representative for his family's Yazzie Farm, which unfortunately had to close due to water scarcity. Yazzie is now a central figure working to help his community reclaim its farming tradition. He serves as a mentor farmer for the nonprofit organization DINÉ Inc., collaborating with them to revive tribal heritage and dryland farming techniques. His efforts focus on tackling water scarcity by working to gain water rights and on engaging youth in agriculture to preserve the community's traditions for future generations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="J. Cedric Woods">
      <data key="d0">J. Cedric Woods</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual from the Institute for New England Native American Studies whose quote about the shared responsibility to maintain cultural heritage is recalled by Peshlakai.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual named in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Institute for New England Native American Studies">
      <data key="d0">Institute for New England Native American Studies</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An academic institute at the University of Massachusetts in Boston, associated with J. Cedric Woods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Massachusetts in Boston">
      <data key="d0">University of Massachusetts in Boston</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The university that houses the Institute for New England Native American Studies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southwest">
      <data key="d0">Southwest</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A region of the United States where Gary Nabhan is noted as a particularly influential figure in the local food movement."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A high desert plateau region with an average annual rainfall of less than 10 inches, where Navajo and Hopi people perfected dry farming techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1fa8277818b65f8d3d6665242021ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tolani Lake">
      <data key="d0">Tolani Lake</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A community where Jonathan Yazzie lives with his family. It is located about an hour south of Sand Springs.&lt;SEP&gt;A location on the Navajo reservation where Jonathan Yazzie currently lives with his family.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sand Springs">
      <data key="d0">Sand Springs</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A location that is home to Jonathan Yazzie's family farm. The area suffered from severe water shortages, leading to the abandonment of farming, but Yazzie later succeeded in bringing water back to the farm.&lt;SEP&gt;The location of Jonathan Yazzie's multigenerational family farm, which is now inactive due to lack of water.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dinnebito Wash">
      <data key="d0">Dinnebito Wash</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A dry riverbed near Sand Springs, mentioned to illustrate the severity of the water shortage in the region.&lt;SEP&gt;A wash (streambed) that historically provided water to the Yazzie family farm but has been dry for at least three years, leading to the farm's closure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nevada">
      <data key="d0">Nevada</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The destination state for the coal slurry pipeline that originates in Arizona.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Elders">
      <data key="d0">Elders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group within the Navajo community identified by Peshlakai as a valuable and underappreciated resource of knowledge for traditional practices, food, and gardening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Reservation">
      <data key="d0">The Reservation</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific, unnamed Navajo reservation where the community garden projects and Jonathan Yazzie's farm are located. It is characterized as a high desert plateau in the Southwest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Worm Farmer From Wisconsin">
      <data key="d0">Worm Farmer From Wisconsin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A special guest speaker invited by Peshlakai to a garden-planning event. This person's unique profession was used to attract attendees and spark interest in composting techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Potluck">
      <data key="d0">Potluck</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of community gathering identified by Peshlakai as a particularly effective tool for garden-planning events, as it encourages social sharing and showcases local Navajo dishes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Potting Workshop">
      <data key="d0">Potting Workshop</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An event organized as part of the community garden initiative, specifically designed to engage children by allowing them to pot plants and take them home.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Senior Centers">
      <data key="d0">Senior Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Community facilities that Peshlakai identifies as effective resources and potential starting points for establishing community gardens, particularly for engaging with elders.&lt;SEP&gt;Facilities for the elderly that are beginning to participate in the local food movement in the region, facilitated by ASAP.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cooking Demonstrations">
      <data key="d0">Cooking Demonstrations</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Community social events organized to restore interest in traditional agriculture by featuring elders demonstrating how to prepare heritage Navajo foods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Navajo Foods">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Navajo Foods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of heritage foods, including items like kneel-down bread, earth cake, and steamed-corn stew, which are promoted through cooking demonstrations to preserve cultural practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cultural Heritage">
      <data key="d0">Cultural Heritage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The comprehensive system of traditions, knowledge, foods, and farming practices of the Navajo people. The efforts of both Peshlakai and Yazzie are aimed at sustaining and restoring this heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Water Scarcity">
      <data key="d0">Water Scarcity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical environmental issue in the Southwest, caused by changing weather patterns (less snow) and industrial water withdrawals. It is the primary reason for the failure of traditional farms like Yazzie's.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jonathan Yazzie's Father">
      <data key="d0">Jonathan Yazzie's Father</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A well-known Navajo medicine man. After his death, his son Jonathan followed Navajo tradition by resting his father's fields for two years.&lt;SEP&gt;The previous operator of the Yazzie family farm in Sand Springs. He successfully practiced traditional agriculture to support his large family of 15 children before the farm was closed due to water scarcity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51fe95d161b4f1f67e45381ccbc2d83e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rose Marie Williams">
      <data key="d0">Rose Marie Williams</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Rose Marie Williams is a Navajo farmer, rancher, teacher, and a leading figure in the Navajo grassroots food movement from Tuba City, Arizona. Alongside her husband Dan, she is the co-owner of DanRose Farms, which she passionately operates on her grandfather's land, a farm she is actively working to resurrect. Although she disliked farm work in her youth, she is now an outspoken advocate for traditional agricultural techniques passed down from her father and grandfather.

Williams practices traditional dry farming, utilizing specific methods such as deep planting and a unique seed-saving technique that involves mixing seeds from multiple years and seeking out 'runt' plants. She is also involved in testing crops in Pasture Canyon. A dedicated teacher, she passes on her knowledge of Navajo heritage food and agriculture through workshops at schools, chapter houses, community meetings, and conferences. For Williams, who is the niece of Tsosie Hyden, farming is a sacred, healing, and prayerful act central to Navajo culture and identity. Her farm stand in Tuba City is highly successful.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tuba City, Arizona">
      <data key="d0">Tuba City, Arizona</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Tuba City, Arizona, is a city located in the northwestern part of the state, near the eastern section of the Grand Canyon. It is the hometown of Rose Marie Williams, a Navajo farmer and rancher who lives and practices traditional agriculture in the area. She operates the successful DanRose Farms and a farm stand that routinely sells out. Tuba City also provides the geographical setting for the Hopi village of Moenkopi, which is located on its eastern edge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DanRose Farms">
      <data key="d0">DanRose Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A farm operated by Rose Marie Williams and her family in Tuba City. It serves as an example and inspiration for the revival of traditional Navajo agriculture.&lt;SEP&gt;DanRose Farms is the family farm of Dan and Rose Marie Williams. It received marketing and infrastructure support from DINÉ, Inc.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Daniel">
      <data key="d0">Daniel</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The husband of Rose Marie Williams, who works with her and their sons on DanRose Farms.&lt;SEP&gt;Williams' husband, who is mentioned working in the fields alongside her and their sons, undeterred by harsh weather like sandstorms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grand Canyon">
      <data key="d0">Grand Canyon</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A major geographical landmark mentioned to provide context for the location of Tuba City, Arizona.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Navajo Farming">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Navajo Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A central theme of the text, representing a cultural heritage and way of life for the Navajo people. The practice, which includes growing traditional crops like corn, squash, and melons, is under threat from water scarcity but is being revived by community members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Elderly">
      <data key="d0">The Elderly</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group within the Navajo community who are beneficiaries of volunteer work from the youth, specifically receiving help with planting. This highlights the intergenerational support within the community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Committee">
      <data key="d0">Farm Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A community group that Jonathan Yazzie aims to restart as part of his strategy to revive farming. It is intended to help organize and support local agricultural efforts by managing resources and land use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rose Marie Williams' Grandfather">
      <data key="d0">Rose Marie Williams' Grandfather</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The original owner of the farm that is now DanRose Farms. He instilled a farming lifestyle in his family, and though his farm and beloved peach trees fell into disuse after his death, his granddaughter Rose Marie Williams is now reviving his legacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cattle Herd">
      <data key="d0">Cattle Herd</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A herd of over two hundred Beefmaster and Black Angus cattle managed by Rose Marie Williams and her family as part of their extensive ranching operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lincoln Sheep">
      <data key="d0">Lincoln Sheep</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A flock of 45 Lincoln sheep raised by Rose Marie Williams on her ranchland, representing a key part of her family's diverse agricultural activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rose Marie Williams' Seven Children">
      <data key="d0">Rose Marie Williams' Seven Children</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The children of Rose Marie and Daniel Williams, who work with their parents on DanRose Farms, actively participating in and continuing the family's farming legacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yazzie's Three Sons">
      <data key="d0">Yazzie's Three Sons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The sons of Jonathan Yazzie, who live with him in Tolani Lake. They are reported to enjoy farming and participate in agricultural activities, representing a successful passing of tradition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-250b989e44ece1db26830c2bcb6f8a7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Williams">
      <data key="d0">Williams</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A farmer who practices traditional desert farming methods learned from her father and grandfather. She is meticulous, keeping detailed records to improve her techniques, and is dedicated to restoring her family's land, including a 4-acre orchard. She is knowledgeable about soil health, crop rotation, and the specific art of single-event irrigation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Williams' Grandfather">
      <data key="d0">Williams' Grandfather</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A key ancestral figure who taught farming traditions and once maintained an orchard on the family land, which Williams hopes to restore. He is a symbol of the agricultural heritage she follows.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Williams' Father">
      <data key="d0">Williams' Father</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary source of traditional farming knowledge and wisdom for Williams, who taught her important principles like meticulous observation and crop rotation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi And Navajo Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Hopi And Navajo Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The local indigenous farming community that shares limited water resources from the spring melt via a tightly governed allocation and rotational system. Williams is part of this community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Plateau">
      <data key="d0">The Plateau</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The high, flat, and windy geographical area where Williams' farm is located. It is characterized by sandstorms that create difficult working conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Wash">
      <data key="d0">The Wash</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A dry channel or riverbed that temporarily fills with water during the annual spring melt. It is the sole source of irrigation water for the local farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Desert">
      <data key="d0">The Desert</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The arid climate and geographical region where the farm is located. It receives less than 10 inches of average annual rainfall, making farming extremely challenging and reliant on specialized techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Late-April Irrigation">
      <data key="d0">Late-April Irrigation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The single, crucial annual event where farmers, including Williams, divert water from the wash to their fields. It is governed by a strict rotational system and is the only time the fields are flooded each year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spring Melt">
      <data key="d0">Spring Melt</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The seasonal natural event where snow melts, causing water to flow down the wash, which provides the necessary water for the annual irrigation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Farming Methods">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Farming Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of agricultural practices adapted for desert environments, including dry farming, natural springwater farming, and flood-irrigation farming. These methods are passed down through generations and rely on deep knowledge of the land and climate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Orchard">
      <data key="d0">The Orchard</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A 4-acre section of the family farm where Williams' grandfather once grew a variety of fruits. Williams plans to restore it as part of her commitment to her family's agricultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Fields">
      <data key="d0">The Fields</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific plots of land on Williams' farm where crops are grown. They are laid out in a grid with high berms to contain water and are managed through meticulous irrigation, crop rotation, and soil preparation techniques like plowing and disking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Irrigation Ditches">
      <data key="d0">Irrigation Ditches</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Man-made channels on the farm that must be cleaned and dug out annually. They are critical infrastructure for diverting water from the wash to the fields during the single yearly irrigation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Williams' Sons">
      <data key="d0">Williams' Sons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The two sons of Williams and Daniel who work on the family farm alongside their parents, demonstrating resilience by continuing to work during sandstorms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dry Farming">
      <data key="d0">Dry Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the three distinct traditional desert farming methods that Williams learned from her ancestors, designed for agriculture in arid conditions with minimal rainfall.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific agricultural technique central to traditional Hopi farming, which involves growing crops in an arid environment with no irrigation, relying only on soil moisture and rain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Springwater Farming">
      <data key="d0">Natural Springwater Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the three distinct traditional desert farming methods that Williams learned, which utilizes natural springs as a water source.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flood-Irrigation Farming">
      <data key="d0">Flood-Irrigation Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the three distinct traditional desert farming methods Williams learned. This is the primary method described in the text, involving the flooding of fields from the wash during the spring melt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plowing">
      <data key="d0">Plowing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A soil preparation technique used by Williams. The timing of plowing-either before or after irrigation-is a strategic decision made based on the anticipated harshness of the growing season to maximize water absorption.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b8b2cec18776df7ae9aac8c2a90b8f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carl Honeyestewa">
      <data key="d0">Carl Honeyestewa</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Carl Honeyestewa is a Hopi farmer from the village of Moenkopi, Arizona, and the son of Luther and Marie Honeyestewa. A retired U.S. Marine veteran who served in Iraq, he is also the great-grandson of Yukioma. At Honeyestewa Farm, located between Tuba City and Hotevilla, Carl works alongside his father to practice traditional Hopi farming, cultivating crops like corn, melons, and squash using methods such as dryland farming and natural irrigation. He is a passionate and central figure in the preservation of Hopi agricultural heritage, representing a dwindling number of traditional farmers and serving as an inspiration to a new generation. Deeply concerned about the decline of these cultural practices, he is also noted for his association with the Knox County Community Action Committee.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Luther">
      <data key="d0">Luther</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Luther is the father of Carl Honeyestewa, with whom he tends several plots of land.&lt;SEP&gt;Luther is the father of Carl Honeyestewa. He practices traditional Hopi farming alongside his son in the village of Moenkopi.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shiprock">
      <data key="d0">Shiprock</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Shiprock is a geographical area from which Rose Marie Williams once bought corn seed. The seed was unsuitable for her climate and failed to grow properly, teaching her a lesson about the importance of locally adapted seeds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="San Juan River Valley">
      <data key="d0">San Juan River Valley</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The San Juan River Valley is a location where Rose Marie Williams purchased cheap corn seed that proved to be incompatible with her local climate and growing conditions, ultimately failing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moenkopi">
      <data key="d0">Moenkopi</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A small Hopi village located on the eastern edge of Tuba City, Arizona. It is described as a Hopi outpost on Navajo land and is the home community of the Honeyestewa family.&lt;SEP&gt;Moenkopi is a location just outside Tuba City where Carl Honeyestewa observes a seasonal wash that provides some irrigation for farming.&lt;SEP&gt;Moenkopi is a small Hopi village located on the eastern edge of Tuba City, Arizona. It is the home of traditional farmers Carl and Luther Honeyestewa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi and Navajo K–12 Schools">
      <data key="d0">Hopi and Navajo K–12 Schools</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Hopi and Navajo K–12 Schools are educational institutions where Rose Marie Williams teaches younger generations about traditional farming practices, as part of her effort to preserve cultural knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annual Agricultural Outreach Conference for Native Farmers and Ranchers">
      <data key="d0">Annual Agricultural Outreach Conference for Native Farmers and Ranchers</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This is an annual conference where Rose Marie Williams shares her expertise and spreads the word about traditional farming methods to a broader audience of Native agriculturalists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Grassroots Food Movement">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Grassroots Food Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Navajo grassroots food movement is a cultural and community initiative focused on reviving and promoting traditional Navajo foods and farming practices. Rose Marie Williams is identified as a leader within this movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Farming">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional Farming, as described in the text, is a holistic practice that encompasses specific agricultural techniques (deep planting, seed mixing), a deep spiritual connection to the land and 'holy people,' and is considered the core of Navajo culture, ceremonies, and beliefs. It is contrasted with mainstream agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Williams's Daughter">
      <data key="d0">Williams's Daughter</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Rose Marie Williams's daughter is actively involved in the family's traditional farming practices and has devised her own unique T-shaped instrument for planting corn seeds deep in the ground.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Williams's Grandmother">
      <data key="d0">Williams's Grandmother</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The source of key agricultural wisdom for Rose Marie Williams. Her grandmother taught her the counterintuitive but effective practice of saving seeds from the 'runt' plant, the one that struggled the most, believing it would yield the most resilient crop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Holy People">
      <data key="d0">Holy People</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Spiritual beings central to the Navajo belief system described by Rose Marie Williams. She feels their presence in the field while farming, viewing the act as a form of prayer that brings healing, peace, and guidance. Harmonizing with the Holy People is seen as the key to a good life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Williams's Children">
      <data key="d0">Williams's Children</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The children of Rose Marie Williams who are being taught the traditions of farming. Two of her daughters are described as being eager to take over the farm, representing the next generation that will carry on the family's cultural and agricultural legacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mainstream Farming">
      <data key="d0">Mainstream Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A modern approach to agriculture that is contrasted with the traditional, spiritual, and holistic methods practiced by Rose Marie Williams. It is implicitly framed as lacking the deep cultural connections and harmony found in traditional practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82e726d805d763eecaebf77d4e3218f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Luther Honeyestewa">
      <data key="d0">Luther Honeyestewa</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Honeyestewa Farm and a parent of Carl Honeyestewa."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Carl's father, a Hopi village elder nearing 80. He is a retired labor union member, a US Marine veteran of the Korean War, and continues to actively practice traditional farming, embodying the cultural belief that 'If you don't plant, you're no good.'&lt;SEP&gt;Luther Honeyestewa is an individual mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marie Honeyestewa">
      <data key="d0">Marie Honeyestewa</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Honeyestewa Farm and a parent of Carl Honeyestewa."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Carl's mother and Luther's wife. She is a retired nurse who served for over 30 years with the Indian Health Services and is a keeper of Hopi cultural traditions, such as sacred white corn.&lt;SEP&gt;Marie Honeyestewa is an individual mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indian Health Services">
      <data key="d0">Indian Health Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A federal agency providing healthcare to Native American communities. Marie Honeyestewa was a nurse with this service for over 30 years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marines">
      <data key="d0">Marines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A branch of the United States armed forces. The men in the Honeyestewa family, including Carl (Iraq) and Luther (Korea), are proud veterans, highlighting their dual identity as Americans and Hopi.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi Culture">
      <data key="d0">Hopi Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The distinct cultural identity of the Hopi people, described as being fundamentally intertwined with land, food, and agriculture. The text presents it as a complex 'Volksgeist' (folk spirit) that provides meaning and a sense of place, which is currently threatened.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Hopi Farming">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Hopi Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific agricultural practice central to Hopi culture, utilizing dry farming techniques to grow crops like corn, melon, and squash in the desert without modern irrigation. It is presented as a precarious heritage and a core component of Hopi life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jonathan Herder">
      <data key="d0">Jonathan Herder</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An eighteenth-century figure mentioned in the text who is attributed with coining the term 'Volksgeist' (folk or national spirit) to describe the unique essence of a culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Woodruff Smith">
      <data key="d0">Woodruff Smith</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A historian quoted in the text who provides a definition for 'Volksgeist' as the 'irreducible, irrational spiritual forces' that fundamentally differentiate one nation's people from another.&lt;SEP&gt;Woodruff Smith is the author of an entry on 'Volksgeist' retrieved from Encyclopedia.com.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c575c019780e689390cd685c690159f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Land">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Land</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The larger territory in northwest Arizona within which the Hopi village of Moenkopi is described as being an outpost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iraq">
      <data key="d0">Iraq</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A country whose war costs were used as a point of economic comparison for the costs of obesity in a blog post by 'Food System Factoids'."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Iraq is a country mentioned for a cost comparison, where the annual spending on the war ($108 billion) was less than the annual public health costs of obesity in America."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The country where Carl Honeyestewa served in combat as a US Marine, a fact that contributes to his complex identity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Korea">
      <data key="d0">Korea</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A country mentioned as a destination for specific cuts of meat, like top round, in the global meat distribution system that Molovinsky found inefficient.&lt;SEP&gt;The country where Luther Honeyestewa served in combat as a US Marine, highlighting his family's history of American military service.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Volksgeist">
      <data key="d0">Volksgeist</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A philosophical concept meaning 'folk or national spirit' that is used in the text to describe the indefinable, unique essence of a culture, such as that of the Hopi.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cultural Identity">
      <data key="d0">Cultural Identity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The central theme of the text, defined as the combination of language, beliefs, law, and customs that provides a sense of place and meaning. The Hopi's cultural identity is presented as being inextricably linked to their agricultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Climate Change">
      <data key="d0">Climate Change</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An environmental phenomenon observed by Carl Honeyestewa, evidenced by longer growing seasons, altered seasonal patterns, and less moisture, which poses a threat to traditional farming methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to the environmental shifts described in the text, including lack of rain, drying washes, and unseasonable cold that causes crops to freeze. It is a major threat to Hopi farming and a fulfillment of their prophecy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wildlife Change">
      <data key="d0">Wildlife Change</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An ecological shift noted by Carl Honeyestewa, involving the appearance of new crop-damaging birds and the disappearance of traditional wildlife like snakes and jackrabbits, affecting both farming and diet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modernization">
      <data key="d0">Modernization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A societal trend towards a modern, wage-based lifestyle with amenities like cars and satellite TV, which is identified as a force pulling younger generations away from the commitment to traditional farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f2f20c947452e869eb15dd25d5bcaf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marie">
      <data key="d0">Marie</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Hopi woman from the Honeyestewa family who explains the centrality of corn to Hopi life and details a prophecy about the decline of their culture. She is a practitioner of traditional skills, such as making piki bread.&lt;SEP&gt;A woman, likely Hopi, who recounts prophecies from her uncle about environmental decline. She initially disbelieved them but now sees them coming true and holds out hope that collective community action can bring positive change.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carl">
      <data key="d0">Carl</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Hopi farmer and Marie's husband. He articulates the deep, metaphorical connection between the life cycle of corn and humans in Hopi belief and fears he is the last farmer in his family.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Honeyestewas">
      <data key="d0">Honeyestewas</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The family name of Carl and Marie. As farmers, their way of life is described as being in peril due to changes in water, climate, and wildlife, symbolizing the larger crisis facing the Hopi people.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aztec">
      <data key="d0">Aztec</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical society mentioned as an example of a great civilization that eventually ended, used as a parallel to illustrate the potential fate of the Hopi culture as described in their prophecy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mayans">
      <data key="d0">Mayans</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical society mentioned alongside the Aztecs as an example of a great civilization that declined, serving as a reference point for the Hopi prophecy of cultural dissolution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kachina Society">
      <data key="d0">Kachina Society</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A sacred and intricate society within Hopi culture. According to prophecy, it is the last of its kind, and its end will signify the final stage of the Hopi's cultural decline.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Man's Way">
      <data key="d0">White Man's Way</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A term from a Hopi prophecy representing a modern, non-Indigenous lifestyle. The prophecy predicts that adopting this way will cause the Hopi to forget their traditions and prayers, leading to cultural and ecological collapse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi Prophecy">
      <data key="d0">Hopi Prophecy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A body of traditional Hopi teachings about the future. The text focuses on a prophecy foretelling the end of the Hopi way of life, marked by forgetting traditions, ineffective ceremonies, climate change, and eventual starvation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Snake Dance">
      <data key="d0">Snake Dance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A traditional Hopi ceremony that, in the past, was reliably followed by rain. Its current failure to bring rain is presented as evidence that the connection to the spiritual and natural world is broken, fulfilling the prophecy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Home Dance">
      <data key="d0">Home Dance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A traditional Hopi ceremony mentioned with the Snake Dance. It also no longer brings rain, symbolizing the loss of spiritual efficacy and the decline of traditional ways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wildlife">
      <data key="d0">Wildlife</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the local animal life, which is changing. The text notes the disappearance of snakes and rabbits, and the emergence of a new pest bird, all of which impact the Hopi people and their farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi Farming">
      <data key="d0">Hopi Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The traditional agricultural practice of the Hopi people, centered on growing corn. It is depicted as a spiritual and cultural activity that is now in peril due to environmental changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Piki Bread">
      <data key="d0">Piki Bread</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A traditional Hopi food made from a thin blue corn batter cooked on a hot stone. It is described as a difficult art practiced by Marie, and the bread is a practical food for men in the fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="_ka-tunqwa_">
      <data key="d0">_ka-tunqwa_</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Hopi term meaning 'the shell of what was once there'. It is used to describe both a dead corn stalk and a deceased human body, illustrating the deep metaphorical link between corn and human life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marie's Uncle">
      <data key="d0">Marie's Uncle</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A relative of Marie who told her the Hopi prophecies when she was a child. He served as a traditional elder and transmitter of cultural knowledge.&lt;SEP&gt;The uncle of Marie who, during her childhood, told her prophecies about future starvation, earthquakes, and flooding that would result from abandoning traditional ways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carl's Father">
      <data key="d0">Carl's Father</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An elderly but still strong Hopi farmer. Carl is motivated to continue farming to honor his father and prevent the tradition from dying with his father's generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi Language">
      <data key="d0">Hopi Language</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The native language of the Hopi people. Its decline among the younger generation, who no longer know the words to ceremonial songs, is presented as a key sign of cultural erosion as foretold in the prophecy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yucca">
      <data key="d0">Yucca</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A plant used by the Hopi people for traditional crafts, specifically for weaving into baskets. It is stored alongside the corn, indicating its cultural importance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Piki Sandstone Slab">
      <data key="d0">Piki Sandstone Slab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A finely sanded stone slab that is heated over an open fire to cook piki bread. It is a specialized tool essential to this traditional Hopi culinary art.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi Ceremonies">
      <data key="d0">Hopi Ceremonies</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A general category for Hopi cultural and spiritual gatherings, such as weddings and dances. The text states that these events all revolve around corn, highlighting its centrality to their way of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0875465d0b75e1a7aada73d20c5bbe6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northern Arizona">
      <data key="d0">Northern Arizona</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A high desert plateau region that has been home to the Navajo and Hopi people for hundreds of years, where they have traditionally practiced inventive dryland farming.&lt;SEP&gt;The region where large, traditional ranches are located. Diablo Burger's model is presented as a potential part of the solution to keep these ranches economically viable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-087cef03c5ab256c59a3f536c4533b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DINÉ, Inc.">
      <data key="d0">DINÉ, Inc.</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A nonprofit organization focused on supporting Navajo communities through education and community outreach, with a specific aim to revive tribal heritage and dryland farming.&lt;SEP&gt;DINÉ, Inc. is an organization that supports local farmers by assisting with marketing and infrastructure. It helped DanRose Farms by providing permanent billboards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tsosie Hyden">
      <data key="d0">Tsosie Hyden</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The aunt of Rose Marie Williams, who is visited by her niece in Pasture Canyon.&lt;SEP&gt;Tsosie Hyden is the aunt of Rose Marie Williams and resides in Pasture Canyon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pasture Canyon">
      <data key="d0">Pasture Canyon</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">An oasis-like wash located near Tuba City, featuring year-round springs, fertile soil, and natural irrigation. It is being used as a test site for crops by Rose Marie Williams and others.&lt;SEP&gt;Pasture Canyon is an oasis-like wash located several miles from Tuba City, featuring year-round springs from caves. It is used as a site for testing crops due to its fertile soil and natural irrigation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tuba City">
      <data key="d0">Tuba City</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A city in Arizona mentioned as a geographical reference point for the location of Pasture Canyon.&lt;SEP&gt;Tuba City is a key location in the text, serving as a reference point for Pasture Canyon, Moenkopi, and the farm plots of the Honeyestewa family. Dan sells corn on the main road into the city.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dryland Farming">
      <data key="d0">Dryland Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A traditional and inventive agricultural technique practiced for centuries by the Hopi and Navajo people in the arid environment of northern Arizona. It is a central element of the cultural heritage being preserved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Native American Grassroots Food Movements">
      <data key="d0">Native American Grassroots Food Movements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A broader social and cultural movement focused on shoring up and rebuilding the practices and systems that constitute a strong, local food culture within Native American communities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi Food and Agricultural Heritage">
      <data key="d0">Hopi Food and Agricultural Heritage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The collective body of Hopi traditions, practices, and cultural knowledge related to food and farming. Its preservation is described as being dependent on inspiring Hopi youth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Western Culture">
      <data key="d0">Western Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A cultural force described as having 'moved in' to the reservation, contributing to the drying up of farmlands and the departure of young people from traditional life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tribal Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">Tribal Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The comprehensive cultural knowledge of a tribe, encompassing traditions related to seeds, cultivation, food, prayers, and ceremonies. The text highlights the importance of teaching this to the youth to preserve cultural identity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Marine Corps">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Marine Corps</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The branch of the United States Armed Forces in which Carl Honeyestewa served. He is described as a retired U.S. Marine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spring of 2011">
      <data key="d0">Spring of 2011</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific time when Jonathan Yazzie's farm 'came back to life' after he had rested the fields for two years following his father's death.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Prophecies">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Prophecies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of warnings about future calamities like starvation, earthquakes, and flooding, which were passed down from Marie's uncle and are seen as becoming reality due to the loss of traditional practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa7136637ee2d131a4ae76b9cf2f5717</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dan">
      <data key="d0">Dan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dan is the husband of Rose Marie Williams and a co-owner of DanRose Farms. He sells corn from their truck near Tuba City.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tyrone Thompson">
      <data key="d0">Tyrone Thompson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for the North Leupp Family Farm."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An individual named in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Tyrone Thompson is the farm manager at the North Leupp Family Farm, a community agriculture program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summer Harvest Festival">
      <data key="d0">Summer Harvest Festival</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Summer Harvest Festival is an event that gathers people from Navajo communities and outsiders. It features traditional foods, like kneel-down bread, made from produce grown by local farm members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kneel-down Bread">
      <data key="d0">Kneel-down Bread</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kneel-down bread is a traditional food offering that is a focus of the Summer Harvest Festival, cooked throughout the day over hot coals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yukioma">
      <data key="d0">Yukioma</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Yukioma was the great-grandfather of Carl Honeyestewa and one of the founding elders of Hotevilla.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hotevilla">
      <data key="d0">Hotevilla</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Hotevilla is the site of famous Hopi terraced gardens and the ancestral home of the Honeyestewa family, founded by elders including Yukioma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Hopi Corn">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Hopi Corn</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional Hopi corn is a key crop for Hopi farmers like the Honeyestewas, noted for its diverse varieties and colors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative">
      <data key="d0">Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative (MFSI) is a grassroots movement and organization that was founded in 2005 and is associated with the Muscogee (Creek) Nation. Centered in Okmulgee, Oklahoma, its office is located at 100 East 7th, Suite 101, and its representative is Vicky Karhu. The initiative's primary goals are to preserve Mvskoke food traditions and enhance community health through a focus on tribal food, fitness, related projects, and policy. To serve six local tribes, the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative operates programs such as a seed bank, a farmers' market, and various workshops, and also publishes materials like newsletters and resolutions concerning food and fitness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c575c019780e689390cd685c690159f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Okmulgee, Oklahoma">
      <data key="d0">Okmulgee, Oklahoma</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A city located in Okmulgee County, Oklahoma, cited as a specific location in the text."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Okmulgee, Oklahoma, is the capital of the Mvskoke (Creek) Nation and the headquarters of the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mvskoke (Creek) Nation">
      <data key="d0">Mvskoke (Creek) Nation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mvskoke (Creek) Nation is a Native American nation whose capital is Okmulgee. The Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative is centered within its territory and serves its affiliated tribes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mvskoke-affiliated Tribes">
      <data key="d0">Mvskoke-affiliated Tribes</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of six tribes (Muscogee, Seminole, Chickasaw, Choctaw, Cherokee, and Yuchi) whose cultural and food traditions are the focus of the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative's preservation and health enhancement efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Taos Economic Development Corporation">
      <data key="d0">Taos Economic Development Corporation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Taos Economic Development Corporation is an organization that sponsored an activity for the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative, specifically a fishing expedition and fish fry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vicky Karhu">
      <data key="d0">Vicky Karhu</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Vicky Karhu is the executive director of the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative. She is an experienced organic vegetable gardener and commercial grower who plays a key role in the organization's projects, such as seed saving.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Native Indian Pumpkin">
      <data key="d0">Native Indian Pumpkin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Native Indian Pumpkin is a rediscovered heirloom crop variety that the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative is now growing and preserving in its seed bank.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sofkee Corn">
      <data key="d0">Sofkee Corn</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sofkee corn is a traditional variety of corn, used by the Mvskoke people in a thick soup, that was rescued from near extinction by the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jessie Smith Noyes Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Jessie Smith Noyes Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Jessie Smith Noyes Foundation is an organization that supports social change movements and helped the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative by connecting them with minority farmers for mentorship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscogee National Walk/Run">
      <data key="d0">Muscogee National Walk/Run</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"An annual 420-mile relay run organized by MFSI where youth trace the boundaries of the Muscogee (Creek) Nation along the Opothle Yahola Trail to raise awareness for health and food issues."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The Muscogee National Walk/Run is an annual 420-mile relay organized by the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative. Youth participate to raise awareness about the connection between agriculture, food, and health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Opothle Yahola Trail (Trail of Tears)">
      <data key="d0">Opothle Yahola Trail (Trail of Tears)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Opothle Yahola Trail, part of the Trail of Tears, is the historic route that the Muscogee National Walk/Run follows across the Muscogee (Creek) Nation's boundaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Okmulgee County">
      <data key="d0">Okmulgee County</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The county in Oklahoma where the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative conducted a foundational community food assessment across 17 communities to identify local interests and concerns regarding traditional foods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopi Terraced Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Hopi Terraced Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Famous traditional agricultural structures located in Hotevilla, representing a significant and historical aspect of Hopi farming heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dry-farming Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Dry-farming Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional water-conservation methods used by Hopi farmers like the Honeyestewas to sustain crops with infrequent rainfall, particularly where groundwater is unavailable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heritage Seed Bank">
      <data key="d0">Heritage Seed Bank</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A facility operated by the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative for the purpose of preserving and restoring endangered, culturally significant seeds like the Native Indian Pumpkin and Sofkee corn.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Food Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Community Food Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An initial project undertaken by the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative across 17 communities in Okmulgee County. It identified a major community interest in traditional foods, which subsequently guided the organization's activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sofkee">
      <data key="d0">Sofkee</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A traditional Mvskoke dish described as a thick cracked-corn soup, often served with meat or fish. The specific corn for this dish was rescued from near extinction by the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-032143f2a2a12749c2695a447f73d523</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative (MFSI)">
      <data key="d0">Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative (MFSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An independent, community-based organization in the Muscogee (Creek) Nation that works to raise awareness about the connection between agriculture, food, and health. It organizes youth runs, promotes traditional agricultural practices, and was instrumental in creating the Muscogee Nation Food and Fitness Policy Council, on which it holds two seats."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative (MFSI) is an initiative associated with the Muscogee (Creek) Nation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscogee (Creek) Nation">
      <data key="d0">Muscogee (Creek) Nation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Muscogee (Creek) Nation is a Native American nation with a tribal government structured into legislative, executive, and judicial branches. It is the focus of the MFSI's activities and demonstrates engagement in food sovereignty by being the first in the U.S. to establish a food policy council by tribal law. This was achieved through the passage of a resolution (TR 10-079) that created the Tribal Food and Fitness Policy Council. Additionally, the nation's geographical boundaries are traced during the Muscogee National Walk/Run.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c575c019780e689390cd685c690159f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Opothle Yahola Trail (Trail Of Tears)">
      <data key="d0">Opothle Yahola Trail (Trail Of Tears)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A historic trail that is the route for awareness-raising runs by Muscogee youth. Running the trail connects youth with their heritage and the historical context of their community's health."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Nations United">
      <data key="d0">First Nations United</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A nonprofit organization advocating for the prosperity and unity of First Nations people. It has collaborated with Muscogee youth on a 290-mile relay run along the Opothle Yahola Trail."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cassandra Thompson">
      <data key="d0">Cassandra Thompson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Cassandra Thompson is a staff member for the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative (MFSI). She is the author of an article titled "Youth Running Like the Wind," which was published in the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative Newsletter in June 2010. In her role, she explains that youth runs are meant to encourage the reinvigoration of traditional agricultural practices and promote healthier living.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c575c019780e689390cd685c690159f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karhu">
      <data key="d0">Karhu</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"An individual associated with the Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative (MFSI) who provides commentary on the vision for the tribal food policy council and describes its establishment as an 'unprecedented' collaboration."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscogee Nation Food and Fitness Policy Council">
      <data key="d0">Muscogee Nation Food and Fitness Policy Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A council established by the Muscogee (Creek) Nation under Tribal Resolution TR 10-079 to oversee food and fitness policies for the tribe."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"The first tribal food policy council in the United States established by tribal law in 2010. Its purpose is to create systemic change in the food system, preserve heritage seeds, and address diet-related health problems within the Muscogee Nation."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indian Health Service">
      <data key="d0">Indian Health Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A federal health agency that holds a designated seat on the Muscogee Nation Food and Fitness Policy Council."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscogee Nation Division of Health">
      <data key="d0">Muscogee Nation Division of Health</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The health division of the Muscogee Nation's government. It has a seat on the Muscogee Nation Food and Fitness Policy Council."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rhonda Beaver">
      <data key="d0">Rhonda Beaver</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"The chief performance officer of the Muscogee Nation Division of Health. She emphasizes that reviving heritage food practices improves relationships with food and self, leading to overall health."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A person featured in a video about the Muscogee Nation Food and Fitness Policy Council, posted on YouTube and the MFSI website.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual mentioned in the index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Earth Land Recovery Project (WELRP)">
      <data key="d0">White Earth Land Recovery Project (WELRP)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A grassroots Native American food movement founded by Winona LaDuke in 1989. Located in northern Minnesota, it aims to recover the land base of the White Earth Indian Reservation and preserve the food and agricultural heritage of the Anishinabeg people, including wild rice."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An organization focused on land reclamation and cultural preservation, which the text explicitly links to the topic of 'wild rice'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Winona LaDuke">
      <data key="d0">Winona LaDuke</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Winona LaDuke is an activist, advocate, and writer who is the founder and representative for the White Earth Land Recovery Project (WELRP). She is a strong advocate for the protection of native wild rice (manoomin), particularly in Minnesota. LaDuke has written about the cultural importance of manoomin and the threat it faces from domesticated versions, authoring an article on the topic for 'News From Indian Country' about achieving its legal protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anishinabeg (Ojibwe And Chippewa)">
      <data key="d0">Anishinabeg (Ojibwe And Chippewa)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A Native American people whose cultural and agricultural heritage, particularly the sacred food wild rice (manoomin), is being protected and revived by the White Earth Land Recovery Project."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northern Minnesota">
      <data key="d0">Northern Minnesota</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The geographical region where the White Earth Land Recovery Project is located and where native wild rice is a key part of the ecosystem."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Northern Minnesota is the region where the White Earth Land Recovery Project is located and where native wild rice, central to the Anishinabeg, is found in lakes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Minnesota">
      <data key="d0">University of Minnesota</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An academic institution whose research in the 1980s led to the creation of domesticated, paddy-grown versions of wild rice, which came to dominate the market and threaten the traditionally harvested native wild rice."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The University of Minnesota's project to map the DNA of wild rice was viewed as a major threat by the Ojibwe tribe, who feared it could lead to genetic engineering and contamination of native rice stock.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wild Rice (Manoomin)">
      <data key="d0">Wild Rice (Manoomin)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A grain native to northern North America that is a sacred food central to the culture, traditions, and prophecies of the Anishinabeg people. The text contrasts the native, lake-harvested version with the domesticated, paddy-grown version."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mvskoke Youth">
      <data key="d0">Mvskoke Youth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The young people of the Muscogee (Creek) Nation who are actively involved in raising awareness about health and heritage by participating in long-distance runs and inspiring their communities."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spirit Walk/Runs">
      <data key="d0">Spirit Walk/Runs</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Awareness-raising running events, in addition to the Muscogee National Walk/Run, that Mvskoke youth participate in to promote community health and heritage."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="290-mile relay">
      <data key="d0">290-mile relay</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A relay run along the Opothle Yahola Trail, organized in collaboration with First Nations United and participated in by Muscogee youth to raise awareness."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Health Issues">
      <data key="d0">Community Health Issues</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A set of widespread problems among Native peoples, including diet-related diabetes, obesity, and alcohol and drug addictions, which MFSI and community runs aim to address."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Agricultural Practices">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Agricultural Practices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"The historic farming methods of Native Americans. The Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative encourages communities to reinvigorate these practices to improve health and food sovereignty."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heritage Seeds and Food">
      <data key="d0">Heritage Seeds and Food</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Traditional and heirloom seeds and food varieties of the Muscogee people. The Muscogee Nation Food and Fitness Policy Council aims to create policies to preserve them from threats like genetically modified crops."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetically Modified Crops">
      <data key="d0">Genetically Modified Crops</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Crops altered through genetic engineering, which are viewed as a threat to the tribe's heirloom seed diversity. The Muscogee food policy council aims to ban them."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscogee Nation Tribal Government">
      <data key="d0">Muscogee Nation Tribal Government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The governing body of the Muscogee (Creek) Nation, comprising legislative, executive, and judicial branches. It collaborated with MFSI to establish the Muscogee Nation Food and Fitness Policy Council through tribal law."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farmers and Ranchers">
      <data key="d0">Farmers and Ranchers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A stakeholder group representing agricultural producers within the Muscogee (Creek) Nation. They have designated seats on the Muscogee Nation Food and Fitness Policy Council."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ben Chrisinger">
      <data key="d0">Ben Chrisinger</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A researcher credited with contributing to the section on the White Earth Land Recovery Project."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A University of Virginia undergraduate student who contributed to the book as a researcher and writer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ben Chrisinger is a researcher who contributed to the work while completing his master's in urban and environmental planning at the University of Virginia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Earth Indian Reservation">
      <data key="d0">White Earth Indian Reservation</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"The original land base of the Anishinabeg people in Minnesota. The White Earth Land Recovery Project's mission is to facilitate the recovery of this land."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chippewa National Forest">
      <data key="d0">Chippewa National Forest</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A national forest in northern Minnesota, located east of the area where the White Earth Land Recovery Project operates, providing a geographical reference point."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-362058f4451bd1ddd9fb9f152ff929ba</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LaDuke">
      <data key="d0">LaDuke</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">LaDuke is an author and activist who writes about the cultural and nutritional differences between lake wild rice and paddy rice, the legal battles of the Ojibwe, and the activities of the White Earth Land Recovery Project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ojibwe">
      <data key="d0">Ojibwe</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ojibwe are a Native American tribe that has actively sought legal protection for their traditional wild rice. They filed a lawsuit in 1988 over false advertising and later lobbied the Minnesota legislature to prevent the genetic engineering of wild rice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Busch Agricultural Resources">
      <data key="d0">Busch Agricultural Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Busch Agricultural Resources is an agricultural research and manufacturing company that was sued by the Ojibwe tribe in 1988 for falsely marketing paddy-grown rice as authentic wild rice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wabizii v. Busch Agricultural Resources">
      <data key="d0">Wabizii v. Busch Agricultural Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Wabizii v. Busch Agricultural Resources was a lawsuit filed in 1988 by the Ojibwe tribe against a company for false and misleading advertising of paddy rice. The case was settled out of court.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minnesota State Legislature">
      <data key="d0">Minnesota State Legislature</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Minnesota State Legislature is the governmental body that, in response to lobbying by the Ojibwe, passed a bill in 2007 to protect native wild rice. The bill included a moratorium on genetic engineering and mandated an environmental study.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minnesota Department of Natural Resources">
      <data key="d0">Minnesota Department of Natural Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A state government agency in Minnesota that authored a report titled 'Natural Wild Rice in Minnesota' in 2008.&lt;SEP&gt;The Minnesota Department of Natural Resources is a state agency that was required by the legislature to study environmental threats to wild rice and submit a report with recommendations for its protection and restoration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Earth Land Recovery Project">
      <data key="d0">White Earth Land Recovery Project</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The White Earth Land Recovery Project (WELRP) is an organization dedicated to preserving Anishinabeg food heritage and culture. Its activities include reclaiming tribal land, providing traditional food packages to combat diabetes, and restoring native species like sturgeon.&lt;SEP&gt;The White Earth Land Recovery Project is an organization in northern Minnesota that works to protect native wild rice, a food central to the Anishinabeg culture, through legal and legislative actions.&lt;SEP&gt;The White Earth Land Recovery Project is an organization located in Callaway, MN, with Winona LaDuke as its representative. It operates the website nativeharvest.com.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anishinabeg">
      <data key="d0">Anishinabeg</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Anishinabeg are a Native American tribe for whom native wild rice is a central food for their past and future survival. Their food heritage has been threatened, leading to protective actions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Anishinabeg are a group of Indigenous peoples, including the Ojibwe, whose traditional land and food heritage the White Earth Land Recovery Project is working to reclaim and preserve.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm to School program">
      <data key="d0">Farm to School program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Farm to School program is a national initiative mentioned as an example of a successful, hands-on approach to teaching better nutrition, which is enhanced when framed around cultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renewing America's Food Traditions">
      <data key="d0">Renewing America's Food Traditions</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Renewing America's Food Traditions (RAFT) is a national organization that has created models for documenting heritage agriculture and foods, such as through community workshops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cultural Food Heritage">
      <data key="d0">Cultural Food Heritage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cultural Food Heritage is a central theme of the text, referring to the traditional foods, agricultural practices, and cultural knowledge that Indigenous communities are working to preserve as a tool for positive health and cultural change.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wild Rice">
      <data key="d0">Wild Rice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of aquatic grass and its grain, which the text explicitly links to the White Earth Land Recovery Project (WELRP).&lt;SEP&gt;Also referred to as native or lake-harvested wild rice, it is a traditional food of the Ojibwe people in Minnesota, known for its distinct aroma and quick cooking time. Its native stock is threatened by domesticated varieties and potential genetic engineering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paddy Rice">
      <data key="d0">Paddy Rice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A domesticated form of rice grown primarily in California paddies and harvested by machine. It was controversially marketed as 'wild rice,' leading to a lawsuit by the Ojibwe. It is considered inferior in quality to native lake-harvested wild rice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anishinabeg Food Heritage">
      <data key="d0">Anishinabeg Food Heritage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The traditional foodways and culture of the Anishinabeg people, which the White Earth Land Recovery Project is deeply engaged in preserving. This includes foods like wild rice, buffalo meat, and maple syrup.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Type 2 Diabetes">
      <data key="d0">Type 2 Diabetes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A health epidemic mentioned as being particularly prevalent in Native American populations, with 30% of Minnesotan Native American adults diagnosed. Organizations like WELRP are using traditional food programs to combat it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1867 Treaty">
      <data key="d0">1867 Treaty</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical treaty that granted land to the Anishinabeg people. This land was subsequently lost through a process of allotment and taxation, and the White Earth Land Recovery Project is now working to reclaim it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H2410/S2096">
      <data key="d0">H2410/S2096</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A bill passed by the Minnesota state legislature in May 2007. It protected native lake-harvested wild rice by creating a two-year moratorium on genetic engineering of wild rice and mandating a state study of environmental threats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sturgeon">
      <data key="d0">Sturgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An important fish species in the native ecosystem of the White Earth Reservation. The White Earth Land Recovery Project has collaborated with biologists to release over 50,000 sturgeon into local lakes and streams as part of its cultural and ecological restoration efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navajo Projects">
      <data key="d0">Navajo Projects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of projects from which lessons can be learned about using cultural heritage as a motivator for healthier lifestyles, as mentioned in the 'Lessons Learned' section.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hawaiian Peoples">
      <data key="d0">Hawaiian Peoples</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Indigenous people of Hawaii who are mentioned alongside Native Americans as having lost their communal land through a similar process of division and allotment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef9c9aefa244f3e5e6f50b424cd984c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renewing America's Food Traditions (RAFT)">
      <data key="d0">Renewing America's Food Traditions (RAFT)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An alliance of food, farming, and conservation organizations dedicated to identifying and preserving endangered food traditions across the United States."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A coalition of nonprofits dedicated to rescuing diverse American foods and food traditions at risk of extinction by promoting their cultivation and consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Renewing America's Food Traditions (RAFT) is a national organization that provides established models and methods, such as community workshops, for documenting heritage agriculture and foods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southern Seed Exposure Exchange">
      <data key="d0">Southern Seed Exposure Exchange</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Southern Seed Exposure Exchange is a regional seed-saving organization based in Virginia. It specializes in saving heirloom seeds that are suitable for the Mid-Atlantic and Southeast regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cooperative Extension Service">
      <data key="d0">Cooperative Extension Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cooperative Extension Service is a local entity that can be contacted to inquire about and enlist support for local seed-saving initiatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Policy Alliance">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Policy Alliance</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Genetic Policy Alliance is an organization with broad representation from sectors like agriculture, consumer, health, and social justice. It can be contacted for assistance in exploring legal and legislative options to protect heritage foods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Green Revolution">
      <data key="d0">Green Revolution</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Green Revolution was a significant historical period that began in the mid-twentieth century, after World War II, and focused on increasing global agricultural production and yields. This movement introduced an industrial model to farming through new technologies, research, and development, including the use of pesticides, herbicides, fertilizers, and hybrid seeds. While the Green Revolution succeeded in making food more abundant and cheaper, it also led to significant, unforeseen negative consequences for the environment and public health. It contributed to the creation of the modern food system, which is characterized by a heavy dependence on nonrenewable resources like petroleum and natural gas. The costs of this period were analyzed in an essay by Mike Ellerbrock and Mike Foreman, and it has been described as being ironically accompanied by a "revolution of profligate wastefulness."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f712d3550c8612228f9fb4dc999ba9b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seed-saving Bank">
      <data key="d0">Seed-saving Bank</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A seed-saving bank is a type of initiative focused on collecting and preserving the seeds of heritage plants to maintain agricultural traditions, genetic diversity, and cultural linkage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Sovereignty">
      <data key="d0">Food Sovereignty</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A central concept throughout the text, representing the efforts of communities, particularly Native American nations like the Muscogee (Creek), to control their own food and agricultural systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Food sovereignty is a concept representing the goal of supporting the original stewards of traditional foods in their struggles to control their own food systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heritage Agriculture">
      <data key="d0">Heritage Agriculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Heritage agriculture refers to the farming practices and crop varieties that are passed down through generations, often specific to a particular culture or region, and are at risk of being lost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Wisdom">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Wisdom</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional wisdom is the body of knowledge concerning agricultural practices, seed saving, and environmental understanding, passed down through generations, as exemplified by Rose Marie Williams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rose Marie Williams' Father and Grandfather">
      <data key="d0">Rose Marie Williams' Father and Grandfather</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The father and grandfather of Rose Marie Williams are the source of the traditional agricultural wisdom and seed-saving techniques she practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mid-Atlantic">
      <data key="d0">Mid-Atlantic</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mid-Atlantic is a geographical region in the United States for which the Southern Seed Exposure Exchange specializes in saving suitable heirloom seeds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Legal/Legislative Action">
      <data key="d0">Legal/Legislative Action</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Legal/legislative action represents a formal strategy, including lawsuits and moratoriums, used to protect food heritage when collaboration and negotiation fail.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Native Wild Rice">
      <data key="d0">Native Wild Rice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Native wild rice is a food unique to the northern lakes of North America and is culturally central to the Anishinabeg people. It has been the subject of legal protection efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lawsuit of 1988">
      <data key="d0">Lawsuit of 1988</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The lawsuit of 1988 was a specific legal action filed by the White Earth Land Recovery Project to prevent the misrepresentation of domesticated paddy-grown rice as authentic lake-harvested native wild rice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Moratorium on Genetically Engineered Rice (2007)">
      <data key="d0">State Moratorium on Genetically Engineered Rice (2007)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The state moratorium in 2007 was a legislative action, pushed for by a tribe, that led to a state study recommending the protection and expansion of native wild rice production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetically Engineered Rice">
      <data key="d0">Genetically Engineered Rice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetically engineered rice is a type of crop that was viewed as a threat to native wild rice, leading to a state moratorium to prevent its introduction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Petroleum">
      <data key="d0">Petroleum</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Petroleum is a nonrenewable energy resource that the modern food system, as shaped by the Industrial and Green Revolutions, is heavily dependent on for nearly all its processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Gas">
      <data key="d0">Natural Gas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Natural gas is a nonrenewable energy resource, alongside petroleum, that the current food system is fundamentally dependent upon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Union of Concerned Scientists">
      <data key="d0">Union of Concerned Scientists</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An organization that reported in 2008 on the hidden costs of industrial agriculture. Their report highlighted that the negative environmental and social impacts were ignored in short-term calculations of profit and productivity."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An advocacy organization that publishes information on the negative impacts and hidden costs of industrial agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gulf of Mexico">
      <data key="d0">Gulf of Mexico</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A body of water that suffers from an annual 'dead zone' caused by water pollution from agricultural runoff, which is cited as a major negative environmental consequence of the Green Revolution."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Centers for Disease Control">
      <data key="d0">Centers for Disease Control</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A public health organization that has described American society as 'obesogenic,' characterized by environments that promote unhealthy food intake. The text links this phenomenon to the industrial farming system."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The Centers for Disease Control (CDC) is a national organization for which Debra Eshmeyer manages a media initiative on school gardens and healthy food access.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bryan Walsh">
      <data key="d0">Bryan Walsh</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Bryan Walsh is a writer and journalist for *Time* magazine who wrote a 2009 article titled "Getting Real About the High Price of Cheap Food." He is quoted on the economic reality that unhealthy, subsidized foods are significantly cheaper per calorie than healthy, unsubsidized foods, making it economically difficult to eat well. Highlighting this economic disparity, Walsh stated, "it's no surprise we're so fat: it simply costs too much to be thin."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time">
      <data key="d0">Time</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A magazine or publication where Bryan Walsh published an article discussing the economics of healthy versus unhealthy food choices."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"A major news magazine that published a 2009 article by Bryan Walsh titled 'Getting Real About the High Price of Cheap Food.'"&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Time is a magazine mentioned as the publication for which writer Bryan Walsh works."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Petroleum and Natural Gas Reserves">
      <data key="d0">Petroleum and Natural Gas Reserves</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Nonrenewable energy sources described as waning, costly, and precious. The current industrial food system is heavily dependent on them, and a key goal of a sustainable system is to reduce or eliminate this dependency."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Federal System of Incentives and Supports">
      <data key="d0">Federal System of Incentives and Supports</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A government policy structure that leads farmers to produce massive quantities of specific commodities, such as corn, at artificially low prices. This system is identified as a root cause of issues within the food system."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food-manufacturing Corporations">
      <data key="d0">Food-manufacturing Corporations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A group of businesses that use cheap, taxpayer-subsidized commodities like corn to create large volumes of highly processed, unhealthy foods, which are then sold cheaply to the public."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="America's Newly Obesogenic Lifestyle">
      <data key="d0">America's Newly Obesogenic Lifestyle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A societal condition identified by the CDC, characterized by an environment that promotes unhealthy eating and physical inactivity. The text argues this is a direct consequence of the industrial food system."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59a4b4194753d7082d9b1d100574c96f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgeon General">
      <data key="d0">Surgeon General</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Surgeon General is a public health authority whose 2001 'call to action' reported that the public health costs of obesity in the U.S. were $117 billion per year, a figure used to illustrate the hidden costs of the industrial food system."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sir James Michael Goldsmith">
      <data key="d0">Sir James Michael Goldsmith</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Sir James Michael Goldsmith is a figure who, in a 1994 interview, warned against prioritizing cheap food production over a more holistic approach that considers food quality, environmental health, and social stability in rural areas."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Charlie Rose Show">
      <data key="d0">Charlie Rose Show</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Charlie Rose show is a television program where Sir James Michael Goldsmith gave an interview in 1994, articulating his philosophy on changing priorities in food production."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michael J. Ellerbrock">
      <data key="d0">Michael J. Ellerbrock</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Michael J. Ellerbrock is credited as the co-author of the first section of the text, which critiques industrial agriculture."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Michael J. Ellerbrock is a professor of agricultural and applied economics and director of the Center for Economic Education at Virginia Tech. He holds a PhD from Clemson University.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Permaculture">
      <data key="d0">Permaculture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"Permaculture, or permanent culture, is a system of ecological design aimed at creating sustainable and regenerative communities. Developed in the late 1970s by Bill Mollison and David Holmgren, it is based on ethics like earth care, people care, and fair share, and principles such as diversity, materials cycling, and energy conservation. It is presented as an alternative to industrial agriculture."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A design strategy for sustainable systems, applicable in both rural and urban settings. Its core principles include materials cycling, energy conservation, redundancy, and stacked functions. It is the guiding philosophy for both Radical Roots and Three Sisters Farm.&lt;SEP&gt;Permaculture is a central concept in the text, described as a design system for land stewardship with the goal of increasing biodiversity. It is practiced at both Three Sisters Farm and Innisfree Village to regenerate landscapes and create sustainable agricultural systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Christine Muehlman Gyovai">
      <data key="d0">Christine Muehlman Gyovai</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Christine Muehlman Gyovai is the author of the section 'Permaculture: Regenerating Place,' which defines permaculture and introduces several farms that practice its principles."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Christine Muehlman Gyovai is the principal of Dialogue and Design Associates, an environmental planner, and a permaculture educator. She holds a master's degree from the University of Virginia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Roots Farm">
      <data key="d0">Radical Roots Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A farm at 3083 Flook Lane in Keezletown, VA. Its representatives are Dave and Lee O'Neill."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"Radical Roots Farm is identified as a farm on the leading edge of regenerative farming, demonstrating innovative permaculture design strategies."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Three Sisters Farm">
      <data key="d0">Three Sisters Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Three Sisters Farm, located at 134 Orbitz Road in Sand Lake, Mercer County, Pennsylvania, is a permaculture-based farm founded and managed by its representative, Darrell Frey, in 1989. Established on 5 acres of gardens, the farm serves as an experiment, living laboratory, and teaching center for the regional permaculture movement. It is considered a pioneering farm that uses permaculture design with the primary goals of regenerating soil, increasing biodiversity, and building the health of the land and its community. As part of its operations, Three Sisters Farm sells preordered produce, such as a bioregional salad mix, to restaurants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Innisfree Village Farm">
      <data key="d0">Innisfree Village Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Innisfree Village Farm is a farm that operates within the Innisfree Village community in Crozet, VA, and is co-managed by Peter and Debra Traverse. Recognized as part of the regenerative farming movement, the farm utilizes permaculture design strategies and is undertaking a large-scale project focused on landscape regeneration and carbon sequestration in the soil. To achieve its goals of balancing the production of grass-fed beef with increasing soil fertility and merging land regeneration with research, Innisfree Village Farm employs techniques such as keyline subsoiling, 'cocktail' seeding, and holistic management. It collaborates with consultants to gather data for its research efforts and also supports a CSA program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bill Mollison">
      <data key="d0">Bill Mollison</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"Bill Mollison is one of the co-developers of the permaculture system, originating in Australia."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An influential figure in the permaculture movement who inspired Darrell Frey to establish Three Sisters Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="David Holmgren">
      <data key="d0">David Holmgren</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"David Holmgren is one of the co-developers of the permaculture system, along with Bill Mollison."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Cheap Food Policy">
      <data key="d0">National Cheap Food Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An unwritten commitment in America to prioritize low food prices, which is achieved through subsidizing commodities used in unhealthy, processed foods. This policy creates a cost/price squeeze that has driven many small farmers out of business and is a core driver of industrial agriculture."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regenerative Farming">
      <data key="d0">Regenerative Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"An approach to farming that aims to regenerate the soil and build the health of the land. Permaculture is presented as a system of design strategies to achieve regenerative farming."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peak Oil">
      <data key="d0">Peak Oil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A future challenge mentioned, representing the point at which maximum oil extraction is reached, that will create pressure on current farming systems and necessitate new approaches like permaculture."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peak Water">
      <data key="d0">Peak Water</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A future challenge mentioned, representing growing constraints on water availability, that will impact agriculture and require regenerative strategies."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419e80efe9993622fcb085a34f66a623</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Roots">
      <data key="d0">Radical Roots</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A 5-acre farm in Virginia's Shenandoah Valley founded in 2003 by Dave and Lee O'Neill. The farm is designed using permaculture principles, employing swales and no-till farming. It sells produce through a CSA, farmers' markets, and wholesale outlets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virginia's Shenandoah Valley">
      <data key="d0">Virginia's Shenandoah Valley</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The geographical region in Virginia where the Radical Roots farm is located.&lt;SEP&gt;Virginia's Shenandoah Valley is the geographical location of Polyface Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd285deff310269197e8718b28f6076b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dave O'neill">
      <data key="d0">Dave O'neill</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-founder and farmer at Radical Roots, who established the farm with Lee O'Neill in 2003. He is actively involved in implementing permaculture practices and planning for the farm's future sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lee O'neill">
      <data key="d0">Lee O'neill</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-founder and farmer at Radical Roots. She provides insights into the farm's goals, the importance of community among farmers, and the desire to balance work with family life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mercer County">
      <data key="d0">Mercer County</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A county located in western Pennsylvania which is the location of Three Sisters Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Darrell Frey">
      <data key="d0">Darrell Frey</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Three Sisters Farm."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Author of the book 'Bioshelter Market Garden: A Permaculture Farm,' focusing on sustainable agriculture.&lt;SEP&gt;The owner of Three Sisters Farm, who was inspired by Bill Mollison in 1981 to develop a farm that would serve as a model of permaculture design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bdeb1a250cfa50c7c4dce2c38964661&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Materials Cycling">
      <data key="d0">Materials Cycling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core principle of permaculture where the output from one natural process is used as the input for another, eliminating waste and pollution. An example is composting organic materials to enrich farm soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Energy Conservation">
      <data key="d0">Energy Conservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core principle of permaculture that involves siting and designing system components to minimize the use of human and fuel energy. An example is placing a compost area near the kitchen and gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Redundancy">
      <data key="d0">Redundancy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core principle of permaculture where every essential function of a system is supported by multiple elements. An example is having water supplied by a well, rain barrel, cistern, and pond.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stacked Functions">
      <data key="d0">Stacked Functions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core principle of permaculture where every element in a system serves multiple purposes. An example is using nitrogen-fixing plants for soil fertility, erosion control, and mulch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swales">
      <data key="d0">Swales</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An iconic permaculture technique consisting of drainage ditches dug on contour with the land to capture rainwater and allow it to percolate into the soil. Radical Roots installed a mile of them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="No-till Farming">
      <data key="d0">No-till Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural technique where crops are seeded directly into the residues of the previous crop without tilling the soil. Radical Roots is experimenting with this method on ¾ acre of permanent raised beds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sheet-mulching">
      <data key="d0">Sheet-mulching</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A technique used at Radical Roots where layers of materials like cardboard, compost, newspaper, and mulch are placed over a cover crop to prepare the bed for planting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pennsylvania">
      <data key="d0">Pennsylvania</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A state in the Northeastern United States, mentioned as a location for various food and agriculture-related topics."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A state in the United States where Mercer County is located, which is the home of Three Sisters Farm.&lt;SEP&gt;The state where Robert and Ardath Rodale purchased a farm in 1971 to develop and demonstrate sustainable organic agricultural practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f712d3550c8612228f9fb4dc999ba9b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farmer Community">
      <data key="d0">Farmer Community</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A support network of peer farmers that can be turned to for advice and to share knowledge. Lee O'Neill highlights its importance for learning and efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3a2182767a8a638b5ea1742dd85c31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Frey">
      <data key="d0">Frey</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Frey is a farmer at Three Sisters Farm and the author of 'Bioshelter Market Garden: A Permaculture Farm'. He is a proponent of permaculture, focused on increasing biodiversity, building local food systems, and developing a teaching center on his farm.&lt;SEP&gt;The author of the book 'Bioshelter Market Garden: A Permaculture Farm'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Innisfree Village">
      <data key="d0">Innisfree Village</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An organization located in Crozet, VA, that hosts Innisfree Village Farm."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Innisfree Village is a 550-acre residential, life-sharing community for adults with intellectual disabilities, located near Crozet, Virginia. It includes a farm with a CSA program, various workshops, and is undertaking a large-scale land restoration project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crozet, Virginia">
      <data key="d0">Crozet, Virginia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Crozet, Virginia is the location near which Innisfree Village is situated, at the base of the Blue Ridge Mountains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blue Ridge Mountains">
      <data key="d0">Blue Ridge Mountains</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Blue Ridge Mountains are a mountain range in the eastern United States, with a portion of the range located in Virginia. This range is home to the community of Big Island, which is the birthplace of the Society of St. Andrew; the society's headquarters are also situated in the Blue Ridge Mountains. Additionally, the mountains serve as the geographical backdrop for Innisfree Village, and Hickory Nut Gap Farm is located at their southern gateway.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e930a3eb466e9bb1dfa38b0df50bfd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peter Traverse">
      <data key="d0">Peter Traverse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Peter Traverse is the co-manager of the Innisfree Village Farm and a key figure leading the Innisfree Village Farm Environmental Services Project. Having grown up on a farm in New England, his work now focuses on land regeneration, innovation, and scientific observation. As a representative for the farm, Peter Traverse articulates the project's goals, its challenges, such as the lack of a standard method for measuring soil carbon, and its future plans, which include creating a market for investing in land regeneration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Debra Traverse">
      <data key="d0">Debra Traverse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual named in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Debra Traverse is the co-manager of the Innisfree Village Farm, alongside Peter Traverse. She also grew up on a family farm in New England.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New England">
      <data key="d0">New England</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A region in the United States discussed in a food article as a potential 'Breadbasket of America'."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A region in the northeastern United States where the Heritage Breeds Conservancy, Inc. operates.&lt;SEP&gt;New England is the region where Peter and Debra Traverse grew up on family farms, which informs their agricultural work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214f4e9d9f3d3fed74243c9bdf50bf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e716e5bd5f06009e0468b77709b834d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Innisfree Village Farm Environmental Services Project">
      <data key="d0">Innisfree Village Farm Environmental Services Project</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Initiated in 2010 at Innisfree Village, this is an ambitious land restoration project. It aims to regenerate degraded land, sequester carbon, and explore a business model based on providing environmental services through innovative land husbandry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bioshelter Market Garden: A Permaculture Farm">
      <data key="d0">Bioshelter Market Garden: A Permaculture Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The title of a book authored by Frey, detailing the principles and outcomes of applying permaculture on his farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Three Sisters Farm Teaching Center Initiative">
      <data key="d0">Three Sisters Farm Teaching Center Initiative</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A planned future development at Three Sisters Farm, which aims to create a teaching center and on-farm education program to promote permaculture and local food systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Permaculture Movement">
      <data key="d0">Regional Permaculture Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A broader initiative focused on building community infrastructure and local food systems based on permaculture principles, which Frey and Three Sisters Farm are actively working to support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Innisfree Village CSA Program">
      <data key="d0">Innisfree Village CSA Program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) program operating out of Innisfree Village, which is supplied with vegetables, herbs, flowers, eggs, and fruit from the Innisfree Village Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carbon Sequestration">
      <data key="d0">Carbon Sequestration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key environmental goal of the Innisfree project, which involves capturing and storing atmospheric carbon dioxide in the soil. It is linked to improved soil health and water retention.&lt;SEP&gt;An environmental process that is a key goal of the Innisfree Village Farm Environmental Services Project. It is defined as the process by which carbon is removed from the atmosphere and stored in natural reservoirs like soil.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of capturing and storing atmospheric carbon dioxide, listed as a topic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d5fedbfed862017a8523b8e6129ad35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seeds of Solidarity">
      <data key="d0">Seeds of Solidarity</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"An organization at 165 Chestnut Hill Road in Orange, MA. Its representative is Deborah Habib."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;"An organization based in Orange, Massachusetts, focused on solidarity and likely related to food or agriculture."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Located in western Massachusetts, Seeds of Solidarity is an organization with two arms: a for-profit, 30-acre farm and a nonprofit educational center. It is committed to environmental health, social justice, and demonstrating how to grow food without reliance on fossil fuels by operating completely off the grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Western Massachusetts">
      <data key="d0">Western Massachusetts</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Western Massachusetts is a region in the United States that is home to several local grain initiatives, including the Hungry Ghost Bakery's Wheat Patch Project. Notably, the Wheatberry Bakery established the area's first grain CSA in this region. Additionally, Western Massachusetts is the location of the Seeds of Solidarity farm and educational organization and is the home of photographer Jason Houston.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104466acc0eb6f55c05f02aa258ea31a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d4150698732f393c235b8b3e1cb2d88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2009 Bioneers Annual Conference">
      <data key="d0">2009 Bioneers Annual Conference</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2009 Bioneers annual conference was an event where guest speaker Michael Pollan highlighted the significant amount of fossil fuel energy used in the industrial food system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deborah Habib">
      <data key="d0">Deborah Habib</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Seeds of Solidarity."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Deborah Habib is the co-owner of Seeds of Solidarity. She advocates for off-grid living, believing it is accessible and does not require a sacrifice in quality of life, and she runs the farm and educational center with Ricky Baruc.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ricky Baruc">
      <data key="d0">Ricky Baruc</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A co-founder of Seeds of Solidarity farm. The idea for the North Quabbin Garlic and Arts Festival originated from a conversation he had with a neighbor.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual mentioned in the index.&lt;SEP&gt;Ricky Baruc is the co-owner of the Seeds of Solidarity farm and co-founder of its nonprofit organization, alongside Deborah Habib.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seeds of Solidarity Education Center">
      <data key="d0">Seeds of Solidarity Education Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Seeds of Solidarity Education Center is the nonprofit arm of Seeds of Solidarity. It runs educational programs, such as the Seeds of Leadership (SOL) Garden for low-income teenagers, and hosts workshops to promote health and connection to food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obama">
      <data key="d0">Obama</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mentioned as President-elect Obama, he was the recipient of a 2008 letter from Michael Pollan concerning the high fossil-fuel energy usage in the modern food system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Orange, Massachusetts">
      <data key="d0">Orange, Massachusetts</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">"A town in Massachusetts, identified as the location for the organization Seeds of Solidarity."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Orange is the small town in Massachusetts, near the Quabbin Reservoir, where the 30-acre Seeds of Solidarity farm is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Landscape Regeneration">
      <data key="d0">Landscape Regeneration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Landscape regeneration is a central concept in the text, referring to practices aimed at restoring environmental quality in watersheds. It includes techniques like improving soil health, increasing water infiltration, and sequestering carbon, and is the primary focus of the Innisfree Village Farm project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renewable Energy">
      <data key="d0">Renewable Energy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renewable energy is a key theme, presented as a viable alternative to fossil fuels in agriculture. Seeds of Solidarity farm serves as a primary example, as it operates completely off the grid using solar power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fossil Fuels">
      <data key="d0">Fossil Fuels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-renewable energy source whose use Seeds of Solidarity actively tries to reduce. Their reliance is minimized through solar power, manual farming techniques (no-till), and the use of biodiesel in vehicles.&lt;SEP&gt;Fossil fuels are a central topic, identified by Michael Pollan as a massive and often invisible input in the industrial food system. The work of Seeds of Solidarity is presented as a model for farming that is not reliant on them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Innisfree Project">
      <data key="d0">Innisfree Project</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Innisfree project is a specific initiative undertaken by Innisfree Village Farm focused on landscape regeneration. It involves data gathering and testing various techniques to improve soil health and sequester carbon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consultants">
      <data key="d0">Consultants</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of experts, including a microbiologist and two environmental consulting firms, who are working with Innisfree Village Farm to provide expertise and help gather data for its landscape regeneration project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quabbin Reservoir">
      <data key="d0">Quabbin Reservoir</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A reservoir in Massachusetts located near the town of Orange, where the Seeds of Solidarity farm is situated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seeds of Leadership (SOL) Garden">
      <data key="d0">Seeds of Leadership (SOL) Garden</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A program run by the Seeds of Solidarity Education Center that aims to inspire activism and leadership among low-income teenagers through the cultivation of food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrial Food System">
      <data key="d0">Industrial Food System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept described as being reliant on economies of scale and cheap energy (gas and oil). Widmark views this system as unsustainable in the long term and positions the local food movement as its successor.&lt;SEP&gt;The conventional, large-scale food production system critiqued by Michael Pollan for its heavy and inefficient reliance on fossil fuels and for creating greenhouse gases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-087cef03c5ab256c59a3f536c4533b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e066d43dafd3f92b15fb06eb4836e7c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seeds Of Solidarity">
      <data key="d0">Seeds Of Solidarity</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A farm run on solar power by Habib and Ricky Baruc. It practices intensive, no-till, hand-grown agriculture to reduce reliance on fossil fuels. The farm operates three solar electric systems and a mobile power van called 'SOL Patrol'. It is a co-founder of the North Quabbin Garlic and Arts Festival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Habib">
      <data key="d0">Habib</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A co-founder of Seeds of Solidarity farm and the North Quabbin Garlic and Arts Festival. She is a vocal advocate for solar energy, community collaboration, and sustainable living practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="North Quabbin Garlic And Arts Festival">
      <data key="d0">North Quabbin Garlic And Arts Festival</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An annual festival started in 1999, co-founded by Seeds of Solidarity. It attracts 10,000 visitors, is run by volunteers, and provides a major sales venue for regional farmers and artists, generating significant local economic activity. It is characterized by its focus on sustainability, using solar power, composting, and local materials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Orange">
      <data key="d0">Orange</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The hometown of Habib, where the North Quabbin Garlic and Arts Festival is implied to be located. Its name is used in the environmental slogan 'Orange is the New Green'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Walking Fish">
      <data key="d0">Walking Fish</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Walking Fish is a community-supported fishery (CSF) and cooperative organization that was established in 2008 in Beaufort, North Carolina. Co-founded by Josh Stoll and Duke graduate students, its mission is to support local, small-scale fishermen while reconnecting consumers with their food source. Walking Fish operates on a model of economic, social, and ecological sustainability, often referred to as the triple bottom line.

The CSF connects consumers directly with fishermen by selling shares of a season's diverse, locally harvested seafood in advance. This approach provides a significant benefit to local fishers, offering them a 30% price premium over conventional markets to ensure their economic viability and promote sustainability. The organization partners with Duke University for distribution, and its representatives include Debbie Callaway and Lyn Chestnut, among others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beaufort, North Carolina">
      <data key="d0">Beaufort, North Carolina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A coastal community that is the location of the Walking Fish community-supported fishery and the Fishtowne fish market.&lt;SEP&gt;Beaufort, North Carolina, is the coastal city that is home to the Walking Fish Community-Supported Fishery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community-Supported Fishery (Csf)">
      <data key="d0">Community-Supported Fishery (Csf)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A business model, based on community-supported agriculture (CSA), where consumers buy 'shares' of a seasonal harvest from fishermen upfront. Walking Fish is an example of a CSF.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Josh Stoll">
      <data key="d0">Josh Stoll</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Josh Stoll is a co-founder and representative of the Walking Fish Community Supported Fishery (CSF). In 2008, while he was a graduate student pursuing a master's degree in environmental management at Duke University, he started the project with a small group in collaboration with Bill Rice. Stoll is considered a key figure who articulates the goals of the CSF movement, believing it has the potential to change the entire fishing industry and contrasting its objectives with the more established Community Supported Agriculture (CSA) model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bill Rice">
      <data key="d0">Bill Rice</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Bill Rice is the owner of the Fishtowne fish market and a co-founder of the Walking Fish organization. Motivated by a desire to support local fishermen, he collaborated with Josh Stoll to create the Walking Fish Community Supported Fishery (CSF). In addition to being a representative for the organization, Bill Rice is also the new president of the Walking Fish cooperative's board of directors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Duke">
      <data key="d0">Duke</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The university where Josh Stoll and his colleagues were graduate students in environmental management when they initiated the Walking Fish project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fishtowne">
      <data key="d0">Fishtowne</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A fish market in Beaufort owned by Bill Rice. It serves as the center of operations for Walking Fish and processes the co-op's seafood.&lt;SEP&gt;A fish market located in Beaufort, North Carolina, owned by Bill Rice. It was instrumental in the creation of the Walking Fish CSF.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOL Patrol">
      <data key="d0">SOL Patrol</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A mobile power unit in the form of a van owned by Seeds of Solidarity. It has its own solar-powered battery bank and a Greasecar system, enabling it to run on biodiesel and filtered waste grease to power farm equipment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Solar Energy">
      <data key="d0">Solar Energy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A renewable energy source that is a cornerstone of the Seeds of Solidarity farm's operation. It powers their home, irrigation, and a mobile unit. Habib advocates for its wider adoption, questioning why it is held to a different standard than fossil fuels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="No-Till Methods">
      <data key="d0">No-Till Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural technique practiced by Seeds of Solidarity that involves seeding through the previous year's crop residues. This method reduces the need for fossil-fuel-powered machinery and is suggested to increase carbon sequestration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) Model">
      <data key="d0">Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) Model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An agricultural business model mentioned as the direct inspiration for the Community-Supported Fishery (CSF). The model involves consumers pre-purchasing shares of a season's harvest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Economy">
      <data key="d0">Local Economy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The economic ecosystem of the region where the North Quabbin Garlic and Arts Festival takes place. The festival is a major contributor, with an estimated $500,000 exchanged locally during the event.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf0fadd2de5cdd877a432eba713a59ce</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Duke University">
      <data key="d0">Duke University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An educational institution where the founders of Walking Fish, including Josh Stoll, were graduate students. Henry Coppola is also a current graduate student there.&lt;SEP&gt;Duke University is an academic institution that collaborates with the Walking Fish CSF by serving as a refrigerated delivery and pickup location for the seafood shares. Josh Stoll, a co-founder of Walking Fish, is a graduate student at the university.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beaufort">
      <data key="d0">Beaufort</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A coastal town in North Carolina that was historically a fishing town and is the primary location for Walking Fish's operations. The town's economy has shifted towards tourism.&lt;SEP&gt;A town in North Carolina mentioned in the index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clark Callaway">
      <data key="d0">Clark Callaway</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A local resident of the Beaufort area who formerly ran a highly profitable seafood processing company. He is now an insurance agent and is married to Debra Callaway.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Debra Callaway">
      <data key="d0">Debra Callaway</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The coordinator and jack-of-all-trades for Walking Fish, and its only full-time employee. She is married to Clark Callaway and is involved in the resurrection of the local fishing industry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paul Russell">
      <data key="d0">Paul Russell</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Walking Fish."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A clam farmer in the Beaufort area whose family has lived there for over three hundred years. He supplies clams to Walking Fish shareholders, restaurants, and fish markets.&lt;SEP&gt;Paul Russell is a representative of the Walking Fish organization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lyn Chestnut">
      <data key="d0">Lyn Chestnut</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lyn Chestnut is a fisherman and a representative for the Walking Fish organization. He supplies the organization with flounder, using a sustainable hand-harpooning (gigging) method that avoids by-catch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Henry Coppola">
      <data key="d0">Henry Coppola</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Henry Coppola is a graduate student at Duke University who serves as a representative for the Walking Fish organization. In his specific role as the Durham coordinator, he is responsible for interacting directly with shareholders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community-Supported Fishery (CSF)">
      <data key="d0">Community-Supported Fishery (CSF)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Community-Supported Fishery (CSF) is a business model where consumers purchase 'shares' of seafood directly from local fishers. The text describes CSFs as a growing movement designed to increase income for small-scale fishers, connect consumers to their food source, and drive the fishing industry towards more sustainable practices.&lt;SEP&gt;A business model that connects fishermen directly to consumers.&lt;SEP&gt;An alternative business model, based on Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA), where consumers buy shares from a cooperative of fishers, entitling them to locally harvested seafood. Walking Fish is an example of a CSF.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Durham">
      <data key="d0">Durham</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A city in North Carolina where Walking Fish has shareholders and wholesale customers, such as Watt's Grocery. Henry Coppola is the local coordinator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Watt's Grocery">
      <data key="d0">Watt's Grocery</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A business, likely a grocery store, mentioned in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;A farm-to-table restaurant in Durham, owned by chef Amy Tournquist. It is a regular wholesale customer of Walking Fish.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amy Tournquist">
      <data key="d0">Amy Tournquist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual named in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The owner and chef of Watt's Grocery, a restaurant that is a regular customer of Walking Fish.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Triple Bottom Line">
      <data key="d0">Triple Bottom Line</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">"A business principle aiming for sustainability in three key areas: economic, social, and environmental. This concept is a core motivation for Appalachian Sustainable Development's business model and approach to creating opportunities."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A framework focusing on economic, social, and ecological sustainability. It is a key principle for Walking Fish's endeavor to build resilience and security.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fishing Boom of the 1970s and '80s">
      <data key="d0">Fishing Boom of the 1970s and '80s</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A period of high profitability for the fishing industry in the Beaufort region, which has since declined due to regulations, pollution, and imports.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off The Hook">
      <data key="d0">Off The Hook</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A community-supported fishery located in Canada, mentioned as an example of a CSF that supports low-impact fishing methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alaskans Own">
      <data key="d0">Alaskans Own</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A community-supported fishery operating in Sitka, Alaska. Proceeds from this CSF support the Fisheries Conservation Network.&lt;SEP&gt;Alaskans Own is a Community-Supported Fishery (CSF) that operates in Sitka, Alaska. The proceeds generated from its operations are used to provide financial support to the Fisheries Conservation Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fisheries Conservation Network">
      <data key="d0">Fisheries Conservation Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A group focused on reducing the environmental impact of fishing. It is supported by the proceeds from the Alaskans Own CSF in Sitka, Alaska.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fisheries Conservation Network is an environmental group that receives funding from Alaskans Own. Its primary mission is to reduce the negative impacts of fishing activities on the marine environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maple Ridge Farm and Fishery">
      <data key="d0">Maple Ridge Farm and Fishery</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A business located in Sabattus, Maine, cited as an example of a hybrid model that operates both a Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) for vegetables and honey, and a Community-Supported Fishery (CSF) for lobster and scallops.&lt;SEP&gt;Maple Ridge Farm and Fishery is a business in Sabattus, Maine, that uniquely operates both a Community-Supported Agriculture (CSA) and a Community-Supported Fishery (CSF). It provides consumers with a diverse range of products, including Maine lobster, scallops, fresh vegetables, cut flowers, and raw honey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Seaboard">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Seaboard</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A region along the east coast of the United States. The text mentions that communities up and down this seaboard, like Beaufort, have historically depended on fishing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sitka, Alaska">
      <data key="d0">Sitka, Alaska</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A city in Alaska where the community-supported fishery 'Alaskans Own' operates.&lt;SEP&gt;Sitka, Alaska, is the geographical location where the Community-Supported Fishery 'Alaskans Own' conducts its operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sabattus, Maine">
      <data key="d0">Sabattus, Maine</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Sabattus, Maine, is the town where Maple Ridge Farm and Fishery is located.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific town in Maine where the Maple Ridge Farm and Fishery is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a0cfcfab5c2708055ae5c22dacee5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seafood Watch">
      <data key="d0">Seafood Watch</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Seafood Watch is a program of the Monterey Bay Aquarium that tracks ocean issues, provides seafood recommendations by region, suggests actions, and offers recipes.&lt;SEP&gt;Seafood Watch is a prominent educational program managed by the Monterey Bay Aquarium. It uses a science-based, peer-reviewed system to rate seafood sustainability and publishes these ratings in regional guides. The program also involves restaurant partnerships and awareness events like 'Cooking for Solutions'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monterey Bay Aquarium">
      <data key="d0">Monterey Bay Aquarium</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Monterey Bay Aquarium is a major aquarium in California that created and operates the influential Seafood Watch program to promote sustainable seafood choices among consumers and businesses.&lt;SEP&gt;The Monterey Bay Aquarium is the parent organization that runs the Seafood Watch program, which focuses on sustainable seafood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cooking for Solutions">
      <data key="d0">Cooking for Solutions</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">'Cooking for Solutions' is an annual fundraising and awareness event sponsored by the Seafood Watch program. It features celebrity chefs to highlight the importance of sustainable seafood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northwest Atlantic Marine Alliance (NAMA)">
      <data key="d0">Northwest Atlantic Marine Alliance (NAMA)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Northwest Atlantic Marine Alliance (NAMA) is a regional organization based in Gloucester, Massachusetts. It actively works to establish more CSFs and provides support to economically challenged fishing communities in the Northeast, advocating for holistic, ecosystem-based fisheries management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gloucester, Massachusetts">
      <data key="d0">Gloucester, Massachusetts</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Gloucester, Massachusetts, is the home base for the Northwest Atlantic Marine Alliance (NAMA).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marine Stewardship Council (MSC)">
      <data key="d0">Marine Stewardship Council (MSC)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Marine Stewardship Council (MSC) is a global organization that runs a certification system for sustainable fisheries. It evaluates fisheries worldwide against its standards for sustainability using third-party assessors, providing a blue 'MSC' label to products from certified fisheries to create financial incentives for good management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Forest Stewardship Council">
      <data key="d0">Forest Stewardship Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Forest Stewardship Council is an organization that certifies wood from sustainably managed forests worldwide and served as a model for the Marine Stewardship Council.&lt;SEP&gt;The Forest Stewardship Council is an organization that certifies wood from sustainably managed forests worldwide. Its operational model was used as the basis for creating the Marine Stewardship Council (MSC).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSC Certification">
      <data key="d0">MSC Certification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSC Certification is a status awarded by the Marine Stewardship Council to fisheries that meet its principles for sustainable fishing. The text mentions that 95 fisheries globally had achieved this certification by October 2010.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="October 2010">
      <data key="d0">October 2010</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">October 2010 is a specific date cited in the text as a milestone, by which time 95 fisheries around the world had received MSC certification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef2c308d995bae34c938de2dff3f5cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marine Stewardship Council">
      <data key="d0">Marine Stewardship Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Marine Stewardship Council (MSC) is an organization that certifies fisheries as sustainable, based on third-party evaluations. It is modeled after the Forest Stewardship Council and aims to create financial incentives for sustainable fisheries management.&lt;SEP&gt;The Marine Stewardship Council is a global organization that sets standards for sustainable fishing and seafood traceability, working to reward well-managed fisheries and assist retailers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79fcf59d7b63a10ed28124a2923fc0f1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sunburst Trout Farm">
      <data key="d0">Sunburst Trout Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Sunburst Trout Farm is a third-generation, family-owned fish farm located at 128 Raceway Place in Canton, North Carolina. Represented by Lila and Sally Eason, the farm specializes in raising trout and is presented as a model of aquaculture sustainability. It is known for raising its trout ecologically and humanely without the use of antibiotics or pesticides, as well as for developing innovative food products. The farm's "Our Story" page is cited as a source of information about its operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d6381862167ee0d108723409eae36d3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Canton, North Carolina">
      <data key="d0">Canton, North Carolina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Canton, North Carolina is the location of the Sunburst Trout Farm, situated in the western part of the state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pisgah National Forest">
      <data key="d0">Pisgah National Forest</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Pisgah National Forest is the location in western North Carolina where Sunburst Trout Farm is nestled. Its watershed provides the pristine water crucial for the farm's operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sally Eason">
      <data key="d0">Sally Eason</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Sunburst Trout Farm."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An entrepreneur and trout farmer who educates the public on sustainable land-based fish farming. She discusses the high productivity of her farm as well as the economic challenges facing the aquaculture industry, such as high start-up costs and the lack of federal subsidies.&lt;SEP&gt;Sally Eason is the owner and operator of Sunburst Trout Farm, who inherited it from her father. She is described as having a strong entrepreneurial spirit and is the driving force behind the farm's commitment to sustainability and innovation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16c3fe8c089550fd16987a1c4f301a2e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bon Appetit">
      <data key="d0">Bon Appetit</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Bon Appetit is a publication that has featured Sunburst Trout Farm's golden-orange trout caviar, alongside Gourmet magazine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Network">
      <data key="d0">Food Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Food Network is a national television network that has filmed segments at Sunburst Trout Farm, bringing it national attention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lila Eason">
      <data key="d0">Lila Eason</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Sunburst Trout Farm."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A relative of Sally Eason who comments on the beauty of the fish from her farm, stating they 'look like the sunrise'.&lt;SEP&gt;Lila Eason is the sales manager at Sunburst Trout Farm and a member of the Eason family. She emphasizes the farm's long-standing, principled commitment to sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16c3fe8c089550fd16987a1c4f301a2e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shining Rock Wilderness Area">
      <data key="d0">Shining Rock Wilderness Area</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Shining Rock Wilderness Area is part of the Pisgah National Forest, and its watershed is the source of the filtered, pristine mountain water used by Sunburst Trout Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Charles Hudson">
      <data key="d0">Charles Hudson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Charles Hudson is the chef at Sunburst Trout Farm who creates innovative trout products. He is also involved in community outreach through the Appalachian Sustainable Agriculture Project and was recognized by the White House.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Move Chefs To Schools">
      <data key="d0">Move Chefs To Schools</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">'Move Chefs to Schools' is a national initiative started by Michelle Obama to connect chefs with local schools to promote healthy eating. Chef Charles Hudson was a participant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aquaculture Sustainability">
      <data key="d0">Aquaculture Sustainability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aquaculture Sustainability is the practice of farming fish in an ecological and humane manner to benefit the fish, consumers, the environment, and the local economy. Sunburst Trout Farm is a model for this.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Devastating Arson And Theft In 2006">
      <data key="d0">Devastating Arson And Theft In 2006</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A devastating event in 2006 involving arson and theft that nearly destroyed the Sunburst Trout Farm. The farm's ability to recover from this event is highlighted as a testament to its resilience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60071a35b7d93bd331a3e0d32f9f3fe4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740869</data>
    </node>
