    </node>
    <node id="Connie Hatfield">
      <data key="d0">Connie Hatfield</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A member of the Hatfield ranching family who is depicted performing hands-on ranch maintenance, specifically repairing a post-and-rail fence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yamsi Ranch">
      <data key="d0">Yamsi Ranch</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A member-producer ranch of the Country Natural Beef cooperative, located in Oregon. The ranch holds the headwaters of the Williamson River and is recognized for its significant conservation efforts. It serves as a place of employment for Joe Jayne and a learning ground for his younger cousins.&lt;SEP&gt;A ranch mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dayton Hyde">
      <data key="d0">Dayton Hyde</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical figure associated with the Yamsi Ranch. In the 1950s, he implemented a significant conservation measure by fencing off over 20 miles of the Williamson River on the ranch, which preserved the waterway and created a premier trout fishery.&lt;SEP&gt;Dayton Hyde is an individual mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Joe Jayne">
      <data key="d0">Joe Jayne</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A full-time employee at Yamsi Ranch who grew up working there. His winter duties include feeding cattle, and he mentors his younger cousins, representing the continuation of the ranching legacy supported by Country Natural Beef.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Elizabeth Gerda Hyde">
      <data key="d0">Elizabeth Gerda Hyde</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A younger cousin of Joe Jayne. She is learning about ranching by accompanying Joe Jayne during his work at the Yamsi Ranch, representing the future generation of ranchers.&lt;SEP&gt;Elizabeth Gerda Hyde is an individual mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Henry Walton Hyde">
      <data key="d0">Henry Walton Hyde</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A younger cousin of Joe Jayne. He is learning the ranching business alongside his sister by observing and participating in activities at the Yamsi Ranch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Williamson River">
      <data key="d0">Williamson River</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A river in Oregon whose headwaters are located on the Yamsi Ranch. It is renowned as one of Oregon's best trout fisheries due to conservation efforts initiated on the ranch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm to Table D.C.">
      <data key="d0">Farm to Table D.C.</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Farm to Table D.C. is a restaurant purchasing cooperative founded in 2008 in Washington, D.C. Managed and coordinated by its representative, Oren Molovinsky, the organization acts as an intermediary that connects local and regional farmers with consumers, specifically D.C. chefs and its member restaurants. As part of the local food movement, Farm to Table D.C.'s mission is to provide farmers with a reliable market while offering chefs a steady and transparently sourced supply of quality local food. The cooperative facilitates the direct purchase of local meats, produce, and specifically whole, grass-fed animals. To stabilize costs and ensure efficient, direct sourcing, it has created its own logistics network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Washington, D.C.">
      <data key="d0">Washington, D.C.</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Washington, D.C. is the capital city of the United States and serves as a key location and operational hub for various organizations. It is the city where the Farm to Table D.C. restaurant purchasing cooperative is located and operates, making it a central point for the described farm-to-restaurant network. Additionally, Washington, D.C. is the primary location for the activities of D.C. Central Kitchen, Fresh Start Catering, and the restaurant Mie N Yu, as well as several other mentioned organizations and housing projects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mie N Yu">
      <data key="d0">Mie N Yu</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A restaurant where Molovinsky implemented a successful whole-animal cuisine program. It took 8-12 months to perfect its formula, which involves highlighting the farm source on the menu, creatively using less popular cuts like chuck roast, and making stock from scratch to save costs.&lt;SEP&gt;An ethnically distinct restaurant located in Georgetown, Washington, D.C. It is a key participant in the local food movement, where manager Oren Molovinsky and Executive Chef Tim Miller pioneer the use of whole, locally sourced animals.&lt;SEP&gt;The D.C. restaurant where Molovinsky was the manager and the concept for the local meat cooperative originated. The restaurant's leadership gave Molovinsky full support to develop the idea.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Georgetown">
      <data key="d0">Georgetown</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A neighborhood in Washington, D.C., where the restaurant Mie N Yu is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tim Miller">
      <data key="d0">Tim Miller</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Executive Chef at Mie N Yu. He leverages his background in anthropology to create cuisines based on ancient Silk Road cultures and is a strong proponent of the 'whole hog' or whole animal utilization culinary principle.&lt;SEP&gt;The executive chef at Mie N Yu who worked with Molovinsky to rethink kitchen practices, such as discontinuing the purchase of demi-glace in favor of making stock from scratch using animal bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Tim Miller is an individual who is a key subject in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oren Molovinsky">
      <data key="d0">Oren Molovinsky</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for Farm to Table D.C."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The manager of Mie N Yu and a pioneer in the local food movement. He developed a system to purchase, transport, and serve millions of dollars' worth of whole animals from Virginia farms in D.C. restaurants, solving a major challenge for local farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virginia Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services">
      <data key="d0">Virginia Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A government agency in Virginia whose staff, including Catherine Cash, assisted the Farm to Table D.C. cooperative in developing animal welfare and labeling criteria.&lt;SEP&gt;A state agency that assisted Molovinsky at every stage of establishing the cooperative, helping him find reputable farmers and create program criteria.&lt;SEP&gt;A state government agency that played a crucial role by connecting restaurant manager Oren Molovinsky with farmers in Virginia, which catalyzed his local sourcing initiative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole Animal Utilization">
      <data key="d0">Whole Animal Utilization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A culinary and economic principle focused on using every part of an animal to prevent waste and create a viable business model for farmers. It is championed by Chef Tim Miller and is a central solution to the problem Oren Molovinsky addresses.&lt;SEP&gt;The practice of using all parts of an animal, not just the premium middle cuts. This was a core principle and logistical challenge for the cooperative, necessary to make local sourcing from farmers sustainable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hatfield Ranch">
      <data key="d0">Hatfield Ranch</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A family-run ranch, member of the Country Natural Beef cooperative. The ranch is actively engaged in both humane cattle raising and environmental conservation, such as partnering with the Audubon Society to protect wetlands for waterfowl.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Australian International Meat Distributor">
      <data key="d0">Australian International Meat Distributor</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An unnamed international company that Molovinsky spoke with. Their practice of selling only middle cuts to the U.S. and exporting the rest globally helped solidify Molovinsky's critique of the inefficiency of the global meat market.&lt;SEP&gt;An unnamed international company that sells middle cuts of meat to the United States. A conversation with a representative from this company helped inform Oren Molovinsky's understanding of the meat distribution industry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boxed Beef">
      <data key="d0">Boxed Beef</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The product and marketing system used by the Country Natural Beef cooperative. It involves processing and packaging all the cuts from an animal, allowing the co-op to market the whole animal effectively on behalf of its member ranchers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small-Scale Family-Run Ranch">
      <data key="d0">Small-Scale Family-Run Ranch</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of agricultural operation that is central to the mission of Country Natural Beef. The cooperative's model is designed to ensure the economic viability of these ranches, allowing families to make a living from them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Future Generations">
      <data key="d0">Future Generations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A key concept representing the goal of ensuring the long-term viability of family ranching. Country Natural Beef consciously aims to provide an economic model that allows younger generations, like the Hyde cousins, to take over ranching operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09e000817f207794412f1a62763dda17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molovinsky">
      <data key="d0">Molovinsky</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oren Molovinsky is a chef, restaurateur, and restaurant owner who is a prominent change agent and vocal advocate for whole-animal cuisine. After questioning the global meat supply chain, he founded and organized the Farm to Table D.C. Restaurant Cooperative to create a local system connecting Virginia farmers with restaurants in Washington, D.C. In his role as the unpaid coordinator and manager of the cooperative, Molovinsky is responsible for handling logistics, visiting farms to establish the supply chain, and setting rules for transparency and animal welfare. He works to build relationships between farmers, restaurants, and distributors, believing that chefs have the power to change the industry. At his own restaurant, Mie N Yu, he re-engineered the menu. Furthermore, Molovinsky mentors other chefs, helps other businesses source local food, advises people on creating similar cooperative programs, and emphasizes the need for culinary schools to teach traditional cooking methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Miller">
      <data key="d0">Miller</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The chef at Mie N Yu who collaborated with Molovinsky to creatively reengineer the restaurant's menu to utilize all parts of an animal, a key step in making the local meat sourcing viable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catherine Tatman">
      <data key="d0">Catherine Tatman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Virginia farmer and owner of Hilldale Farm who benefits from the cooperative's model, which guarantees the purchase of her whole animals at a stable price, reducing risk and increasing profitability.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual named in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farm to Table D.C. Restaurant Cooperative">
      <data key="d0">Farm to Table D.C. Restaurant Cooperative</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization founded by Molovinsky that pools orders from member restaurants in D.C. to purchase whole animals directly from Virginia farmers. It manages the logistics of collection and delivery, creating a new, local supply chain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D.C. Central Kitchen (Dcck)">
      <data key="d0">D.C. Central Kitchen (Dcck)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A D.C.-based nonprofit that provides culinary training to people facing barriers to employment. It became the sole distributor for the restaurant cooperative, a synergistic partnership that allowed DCCK to expand its own operations into sourcing local produce.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hilldale Farm">
      <data key="d0">Hilldale Farm</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A farm in central Virginia, run by Catherine Tatman, that raises sheep, cattle, chickens, and eggs. It is a key supplier for the Farm to Table D.C. Restaurant Cooperative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virginia Agriculture Agency">
      <data key="d0">Virginia Agriculture Agency</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A state government body that assisted Molovinsky and the cooperative in finding Virginia farms capable of supplying specific products to the member restaurants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D.C. Restaurant Association">
      <data key="d0">D.C. Restaurant Association</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A professional organization for restaurants in D.C. Molovinsky leveraged his affiliation with the association to connect with other restaurateurs who were eager to join the local meat buying initiative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shenandoah Valley Produce Auction">
      <data key="d0">Shenandoah Valley Produce Auction</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A weekly wholesale market for Mennonite and other valley farmers. D.C. Central Kitchen became its largest produce buyer in 2009 as a direct result of its contract with the restaurant cooperative.&lt;SEP&gt;A weekly wholesale market outlet for Mennonite and other valley farmers in the Shenandoah Valley. DCCK became its largest single buyer of produce in 2009.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shenandoah Valley Produce Auction is a marketplace where D.C. Central Kitchen buys bulk produce and 'seconds' directly from local farmers, enabling them to afford fresh food for their programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D.C.">
      <data key="d0">D.C.</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The city where the cooperative's member restaurants, including Mie N Yu, are located. It serves as the market and destination for the food sourced from Virginia.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary operational area for both the D.C. restaurant cooperative and the D.C. Central Kitchen. DCCK serves a wide-ranging population in this area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cooperative Operation Start">
      <data key="d0">Cooperative Operation Start</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An event marking the beginning of the Farm to Table D.C. Restaurant Cooperative's operations in October 2008. In its first 18 months, it spent over $1 million supporting local Virginia agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Food Supply Chain">
      <data key="d0">Local Food Supply Chain</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category representing the alternative food system model that Molovinsky aimed to create. It emphasizes direct relationships between local producers and consumers, use of the whole animal, and economic benefits for the local community, contrasting with the impersonal and costly global market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Middle East">
      <data key="d0">Middle East</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A region mentioned as a destination for specific cuts of meat, like chuck roast, within the global distribution system criticized by Molovinsky.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Argentina">
      <data key="d0">Argentina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A country mentioned as an example of a distant, unverifiable source for meat, contrasting with the local sourcing model of the cooperative.&lt;SEP&gt;A country, along with Brazil, cited as a source of meat imported into the U.S., highlighting the inefficiencies of the global supply chain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Global Meat Market System">
      <data key="d0">Global Meat Market System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The existing international supply chain for meat, which Molovinsky critiques as illogical, costly, and inefficient. It involves shipping different animal parts to various nations and creates price instability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="'Just-in-Time' Inventory">
      <data key="d0">'Just-in-Time' Inventory</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The traditional restaurant inventory system where chefs place late-night orders for next-morning delivery. Molovinsky's cooperative had to create a new system that provided similar flexibility while accommodating the needs of local farmers and processors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-912655071bfb31832bd5d960fea37c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DCCK">
      <data key="d0">DCCK</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">DCCK is an all-volunteer canning operation that provides locally grown food for the hungry. It formed a synergistic relationship with a restaurant cooperative, enabling it to become the largest single buyer of produce at the Shenandoah Valley Produce Auction in 2009.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shenandoah Valley">
      <data key="d0">Shenandoah Valley</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A region that is a primary source of produce and farm products for the Farm to Table D.C. cooperative and the location of the Shenandoah Valley Produce Auction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catherine Cash">
      <data key="d0">Catherine Cash</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A staff member of the Virginia Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services who worked with Molovinsky to develop specific animal welfare rules and criteria for the cooperative's suppliers.&lt;SEP&gt;An individual mentioned in the index.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Restaurant Cooperative">
      <data key="d0">Restaurant Cooperative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A business model where multiple restaurants collaborate to purchase goods, such as local meat and produce, directly from producers. Molovinsky champions this model as the only sustainable way for restaurants to buy local.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Animal Welfare Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Animal Welfare Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific set of rules developed by Farm to Table D.C. with the help of the Virginia Department of Agriculture. These criteria, which include rules against antibiotic use, define the 'all natural' label for meat and ensure transparency for consumers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Harry's Tap Room">
      <data key="d0">Harry's Tap Room</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A multi-location restaurant in the Washington metropolitan area that Molovinsky is assisting to source organic and naturally raised ingredients from local farms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capital Meat Company">
      <data key="d0">Capital Meat Company</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A meat distributor that now buys whole animals from Maryland and Virginia farms to distribute individual cuts to its customers, as part of an expansion of local meat distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saval Foods">
      <data key="d0">Saval Foods</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A large food distributor for the Baltimore region that has started offering naturally grown beef from Grayson Natural. The company is considering a transition to the whole-animal approach.&lt;SEP&gt;A large food distributor for the Baltimore region that has started offering naturally grown local beef from Grayson Natural, a farm cooperative in Virginia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grayson Natural">
      <data key="d0">Grayson Natural</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A farm cooperative located in southwest Virginia that provides naturally grown beef to distributors like Saval Foods.&lt;SEP&gt;A farm cooperative located in southwest Virginia that supplies naturally grown beef to the distributor Saval Foods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shenandoah Valley Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Shenandoah Valley Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of farmers, including Mennonites, who sell their goods at the Shenandoah Valley Produce Auction. They are a source of local produce for organizations like DCCK.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Existing Distributors">
      <data key="d0">Existing Distributors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional meat or seafood distribution companies. Molovinsky notes that the cooperative model is necessary because it doesn't align with these distributors' models, but suggests engaging them when possible to avoid adversarial relationships.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Restaurant Customers">
      <data key="d0">Restaurant Customers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The patrons of the cooperative's member restaurants. A key goal for Molovinsky is to provide them with complete transparency, allowing them to visit the farms where their food is sourced.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cooperative-supplying Farmers">
      <data key="d0">Cooperative-supplying Farmers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The farmers who supply meat and other products directly to the Farm to Table D.C. cooperative. They are subject to the cooperative's animal welfare criteria and have close, family-like relationships with Molovinsky.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Processors">
      <data key="d0">Local Processors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A beneficiary group of the cooperative model. An expanded cooperative is projected to spend about 20% of its budget with local processors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Trucking">
      <data key="d0">Local Trucking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A beneficiary group of the cooperative model. An expanded cooperative is projected to spend about 5% of its budget with local trucking companies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Washington Metropolitan Area">
      <data key="d0">Washington Metropolitan Area</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The geographic location of Harry's Tap Room, a multi-location restaurant being assisted by Molovinsky.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Baltimore Region">
      <data key="d0">Baltimore Region</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary service area for Saval Foods, a large food distributor that has recently started venturing into local beef distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9f558ccef1d0a97c2f1550636dc3f28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Capital Meat Company">
      <data key="d0">The Capital Meat Company</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A meat distribution company that buys whole animals from farms in Maryland and Virginia. It then distributes individual cuts to its customers, playing a role in the local meat supply chain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Baltimore">
      <data key="d0">Baltimore</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The geographic region served by the large food distributor, Saval Foods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Animal Approach">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Animal Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A culinary and procurement model where restaurants and distributors buy and utilize the entire animal, not just prime cuts. It is promoted as a method to provide genuine support to farmers and requires menu engineering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Restaurant Purchasing Cooperative">
      <data key="d0">Restaurant Purchasing Cooperative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A business model where a group of restaurants collaborates to purchase goods, such as whole animals, directly from farmers. This model increases buying power and creates a stable market for producers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Sourcing">
      <data key="d0">Local Sourcing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The practice of restaurants and distributors purchasing meat directly from local farms. According to Molovinsky, true local sourcing involves supporting the farmer by buying the whole animal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diners">
      <data key="d0">Diners</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The end consumers in restaurants. The text suggests their motivation for choosing dishes with local meat is primarily driven by a perception of higher quality and the uniqueness of a dish associated with a specific farm, rather than just the 'local' label.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Purveyors">
      <data key="d0">Purveyors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Food suppliers and distributors who act as intermediaries in the supply chain. Molovinsky states that they are responsive to the demands of chefs, indicating that restaurant buying power can shape their offerings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Meat Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Local Meat Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sector of the food system focused on getting meat from local farms to local markets. The text notes that this sector is expanding through the efforts of companies like The Capital Meat Company.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Animal Cuisine">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Animal Cuisine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A culinary and business model that involves purchasing and using the entire animal. It requires menu engineering, creative recipes for various cuts, and a shift in marketing to emphasize the farm source over the specific cut of meat.&lt;SEP&gt;A culinary philosophy and practice centered on creating a menu that utilizes every part of an animal. It is presented as a necessary component for restaurants to successfully implement the whole-animal purchasing model, requiring menu engineering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food-Service Industry">
      <data key="d0">Food-Service Industry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The broader commercial sector that includes restaurants and distributors. The text highlights that farmers must learn to navigate its specific business practices, particularly its 30-to-60-day payment terms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Farmers' Market">
      <data key="d0">Local Farmers' Market</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A direct-to-consumer market channel. Molovinsky contrasts buying choice cuts from these markets with the whole-animal approach, which he believes provides more substantial support to farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menu Engineering">
      <data key="d0">Menu Engineering</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The strategic process of designing and pricing a restaurant menu to be profitable and influence customer choices. It is described as essential for making the whole-animal approach viable by ensuring all parts of the animal are sold.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terms of Payment">
      <data key="d0">Terms of Payment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The conditions under which payment is made. This is identified as a critical challenge in establishing relationships between farmers, who are accustomed to immediate payment, and restaurants, which typically operate on 30-to-60-day cycles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ef5e8f2029677f15d6ee8815b5828c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D.C. Restaurant Cooperative">
      <data key="d0">D.C. Restaurant Cooperative</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A cooperative established by Molovinsky to help restaurants purchase whole animals directly from farms. It took six to eight months of significant effort to get off the ground, involving visits to farms in Virginia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Culinary Schools">
      <data key="d0">Culinary Schools</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Educational institutions that Molovinsky believes should teach traditional, slower cooking methods like braising and roasting to better prepare young chefs for working with all parts of an animal, a necessary step for the local food movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virginia Farms">
      <data key="d0">Virginia Farms</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A collective of farms in Virginia that Molovinsky visited and established partnerships with to supply the D.C. Restaurant Cooperative with local, whole animals. They are highlighted by name on the menu at Mie N Yu.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Processing Plants">
      <data key="d0">Processing Plants</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Facilities visited by Molovinsky during the establishment of the D.C. Restaurant Cooperative. They are a key intermediary in the supply chain, handling the animal after it leaves the farm and before it reaches the restaurant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65dc312cbeeaa82060b27a69060e671d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D.C. restaurant cooperative">
      <data key="d0">D.C. restaurant cooperative</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The D.C. restaurant cooperative is a program that connects restaurants in Washington D.C. with farms and processors in Virginia. It was a significant effort to establish, requiring months of planning and coordination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Farmer collaboratives">
      <data key="d0">Farmer collaboratives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Farmer collaboratives are groups of farmers that work together, making it easier for buyers like the D.C. restaurant cooperative to source products by communicating with a single representative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D.C. Central Kitchen (DCCK)">
      <data key="d0">D.C. Central Kitchen (DCCK)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A non-profit organization based in Washington D.C., as implied by its name. It is a food-related entity, likely involved in food recovery, distribution, or providing meals to those in need, as suggested by its link to 'homelessness'."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;D.C. Central Kitchen (DCCK) is a non-profit organization in Washington, D.C. that provides food service training to individuals with challenging backgrounds. It operates programs like Fresh Start Catering and the Campus Kitchens Project, sources food from local auctions, and is presented as a case study in building a nonprofit community food project.&lt;SEP&gt;D.C. Central Kitchen is a large organization in the D.C. area focused on combating poverty, hunger, and homelessness through interlocking programs, including food recovery, culinary job training, and social enterprise catering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Poverty, Hunger, and Homelessness">
      <data key="d0">Poverty, Hunger, and Homelessness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">These are the core social issues that D.C. Central Kitchen aims to combat through its various programs, viewing hunger as a byproduct of a lack of opportunity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Helping">
      <data key="d0">First Helping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A program run by D.C. Central Kitchen where outreach workers distribute breakfast to the homeless and connect them with a network of support services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fresh Start program">
      <data key="d0">Fresh Start program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A social enterprise catering program by D.C. Central Kitchen that employs graduates of its culinary training program. It provides meals to several schools and institutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Washington Jesuit Academy (WJA)">
      <data key="d0">Washington Jesuit Academy (WJA)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A private middle school for at-risk boys that receives catered meals from D.C. Central Kitchen's Fresh Start program.&lt;SEP&gt;An educational institution mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next Step Public Charter School">
      <data key="d0">Next Step Public Charter School</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A school that receives catered meals from D.C. Central Kitchen's Fresh Start program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of the District of Columbia">
      <data key="d0">University of the District of Columbia</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A university that receives catered meals from D.C. Central Kitchen's Fresh Start program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allison Sosna">
      <data key="d0">Allison Sosna</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Allison Sosna is the executive chef for Fresh Start Catering, a program of D.C. Central Kitchen, for which she also serves as a representative. She developed a passion for food while studying in Europe and now combines it with socially responsible work. In her role, Sosna helps manage the catering program, trains others, and has noted that the Fresh Start kitchen staff often serve as mentors to students.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Derek Nelson">
      <data key="d0">Derek Nelson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A caterer's assistant at D.C. Central Kitchen and a graduate of its training program, which he joined after being in a drug rehabilitation program.&lt;SEP&gt;Derek Nelson is a caterer's assistant for Fresh Start Catering. He joined the DCCK program to get back on his feet after being in drug rehabilitation and is motivated by a desire to serve others through food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Culinary Training Program">
      <data key="d0">Culinary Training Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A 16-week program offered by D.C. Central Kitchen tailored to at-risk populations, such as the homeless or recently incarcerated, to provide them with job skills and opportunities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lack of Opportunity">
      <data key="d0">Lack of Opportunity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The core societal issue that D.C. Central Kitchen identifies as the root cause of hunger, poverty, and homelessness. Creating opportunity is the second prong of its mission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D.C. Public Schools">
      <data key="d0">D.C. Public Schools</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of seven public schools in Washington D.C. that receive catered meals from the Fresh Start program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-217f96b464101a2919ccf861b25fe27c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fresh Start Catering">
      <data key="d0">Fresh Start Catering</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A catering business, likely with a focus on local food or providing social enterprise opportunities."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Fresh Start Catering is a program run by D.C. Central Kitchen. Its staff are graduates of DCCK's training program, and it provides fresh, farm-to-table meals to local schools in the Washington, D.C., area, such as the Washington Jesuit Academy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Derwin Gaines">
      <data key="d0">Derwin Gaines</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Derwin Gaines is a cook at Fresh Start Catering. A former electrician, he retrained through D.C. Central Kitchen's program after an injury forced him to retire from his previous profession.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Howard Thomas">
      <data key="d0">Howard Thomas</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual named in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Howard Thomas is the sous chef at Fresh Start Catering. A former drug dealer, he changed his life and found DCCK's training to be invaluable. One of his original recipes was featured by DCCK to promote its work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="James Weeks">
      <data key="d0">James Weeks</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual named in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;James Weeks is a minister and a prep cook for Fresh Start Catering who also delivers meals to schools. He has held several jobs with D.C. Central Kitchen and describes its mission as reaching out to people in need.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Latasha Washington">
      <data key="d0">Latasha Washington</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual named in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Latasha Washington is a graduate of DCCK's seventy-ninth training class and now works for the Fresh Start Catering program. She aspires to open her own catering business in the future.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Washington Jesuit Academy">
      <data key="d0">Washington Jesuit Academy</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Washington Jesuit Academy is a school in Washington, D.C., that receives farm-fresh meals prepared by the crew at Fresh Start Catering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Campus Kitchens Project">
      <data key="d0">Campus Kitchens Project</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Campus Kitchens Project is a program started by DCCK that uses surplus food from over 30 school kitchens across the country to feed at-risk communities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gonzaga College High School">
      <data key="d0">Gonzaga College High School</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Gonzaga College High School is a school in Washington, D.C., whose students participate in the Campus Kitchens Project by delivering meals to residents of the Sibley Plaza housing project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sibley Plaza">
      <data key="d0">Sibley Plaza</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Sibley Plaza is a housing project in downtown Washington, D.C., that receives meals from the Campus Kitchens Project, delivered by students from Gonzaga College High School.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Joelle Johnson">
      <data key="d0">Joelle Johnson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"A representative for D.C. Central Kitchen."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;A representative of D.C. Central Kitchen.&lt;SEP&gt;Joelle Johnson is the buyer for D.C. Central Kitchen. She works directly with the Shenandoah Valley Produce Auction to purchase food from local farmers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dennis Showalter">
      <data key="d0">Dennis Showalter</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dennis Showalter is the auction manager for the Shenandoah Valley Produce Auction. He facilitates the sale of produce to organizations like D.C. Central Kitchen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nonprofit Community Food Project">
      <data key="d0">Nonprofit Community Food Project</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the overarching category of the initiatives described in the text. The document provides 'Lessons Learned' for building such projects, using DCCK, Janus Youth, and Jones Valley Urban Farm as examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peace Corps">
      <data key="d0">Peace Corps</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization whose former volunteers are often attracted to work at Janus Youth, indicating a shared ethos of service.&lt;SEP&gt;The Peace Corps is an organization in which Regine Kennedy served, working in the Ukraine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c46a7f814f9b988da76a9ac71bdab30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-062d9ac74f75199c91576d982ab5a9f4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="W.K. Kellogg Foundation">
      <data key="d0">W.K. Kellogg Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The W.K. Kellogg Foundation is a grant-making organization that provided a nearly $500,000 multi-year grant to Troy Gardens. This funding was described as both a boon and a detriment for the recipient organization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nonprofit">
      <data key="d0">Nonprofit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nonprofit is a central category in the text, which provides advice on how these organizations can achieve stability and success through strategies like paying a living wage, staying focused on a core mission, diversifying income, and translating achievements into meaningful data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Living Wage And Benefits">
      <data key="d0">Living Wage And Benefits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Living Wage and Benefits is a key strategy advocated for nonprofits to ensure staff retention and organizational stability, as exemplified by Jones Valley Urban Farm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Focused Core Mission">
      <data key="d0">Focused Core Mission</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Focused Core Mission is a strategic principle for nonprofits to avoid becoming overworked and inefficient by pursuing too many ideas. Village Gardens' experience is used as an example of adhering to this principle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Translating Achievements Into Dollars">
      <data key="d0">Translating Achievements Into Dollars</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Translating Achievements Into Dollars is a strategy for nonprofits to gain visibility, partnerships, and support from policymakers by quantifying their community impact, as successfully demonstrated by Growing Power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diversified Income Stream">
      <data key="d0">Diversified Income Stream</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Diversified Income Stream is a financial strategy for nonprofits to achieve fiscal stability and avoid 'mission drift' by reducing dependence on grants. The Food Project and Growing Power are cited as successful examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grants">
      <data key="d0">Grants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grants are a form of funding for nonprofits that can create a difficult cycle of dependence and lead to 'mission drift'. The text advises diversifying income away from over-reliance on grants, using Troy Gardens as a cautionary tale.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mission Drift">
      <data key="d0">Mission Drift</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mission Drift is a negative outcome for nonprofits where 'chasing the money,' particularly from grants, can lead them away from their original purpose. Diversifying income is presented as a solution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Green Jobs">
      <data key="d0">Green Jobs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Green Jobs are employment opportunities created by environmentally-focused enterprises. Growing Power is noted for creating green jobs as part of its successful urban farming operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0adcf3a18e5a064a6ed2b9d46dff75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Community Groundworks">
      <data key="d0">Community Groundworks</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A nonprofit organization that initially struggled financially after its grant funding ended. It has since redefined its mission to include revenue-generating activities and is now described as flourishing, with strong programs reaching widely into its community."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An organization located in Madison, WI, with a website at troygardens.org. It has several listed representatives, including Marcia Caton Campbell and Nathan Larson.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1fa8277818b65f8d3d6665242021ad9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kellogg Grant">
      <data key="d0">Kellogg Grant</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A grant that provided initial funding for Community GroundWorks, enabling its early development. The end of this grant period led to financial challenges for the organization."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1fa8277818b65f8d3d6665242021ad9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diablo Burger">
      <data key="d0">Diablo Burger</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Diablo Burger is a restaurant business and organization located at 120 North Leroux Street in Flagstaff, Arizona. Founded by Derrick Widmark in 2009, it operates as a "local foods based burger joint" with a core principle of being "All About Local." The restaurant's mission is to connect community and ecology by exclusively using locally raised, open-range beef from Diablo Trust ranches and sourcing other ingredients from nearby farms. To promote local economic and environmental sustainability, Diablo Burger uses compostable materials to save water and has collaborated with other local restaurants to create an efficient, pooled delivery system for produce. It also serves as a "tasting room" to connect people with the region's food and landscape.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1fa8277818b65f8d3d6665242021ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f188db350c6a6f2fdb22a9e9283e4dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-087cef03c5ab256c59a3f536c4533b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Derrick Widmark">
      <data key="d0">Derrick Widmark</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">"The owner of Diablo Burger and a former communications and program coordinator for the Diablo Trust. He was inspired to open his restaurant to create a local market for beef from the Diablo Canyon area."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;An individual named in the text.</data>
