    </node>
    <node id="The Society of St. Andrew (Sosa)">
      <data key="d0">The Society of St. Andrew (Sosa)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Founded in 1979 and headquartered in Virginia, The Society of St. Andrew (SoSA) runs the largest nationwide gleaning operation, repurposing large quantities of unused food to feed people in need.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marin Organic">
      <data key="d0">Marin Organic</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the descriptions provided, Marin Organic is a nonprofit organization that was founded in 1999 with the mission to preserve organic farming in Marin County. Located in Point Reyes Station, California, which is north of San Francisco, the organization's representative is Scott Davidson.

Marin Organic is particularly known for running an extensive and efficient farm gleaning program. This program involves collecting "seconds," or imperfect produce, from local fields and distributing the gleaned organic food to thousands of students in local schools and community centers. This initiative has been highly influential, serving as a blueprint for similar projects across the nation. To further promote this practice, Marin Organic also founded National Gleaning Day.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81005569d65546868c017fed474ad248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6db795582f52e35a0bb9aea86e66f7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marin County">
      <data key="d0">Marin County</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Marin County is a county north of San Francisco where the organization Marin Organic directs its gleaning efforts to improve youth nutrition in local schools.&lt;SEP&gt;Marin County is a region north of San Francisco where the nonprofit Marin Organic is based. The organization's work focuses on preserving the county's organic farming lifestyle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6db795582f52e35a0bb9aea86e66f7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World War Ii">
      <data key="d0">World War Ii</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">World War II is cited as a historical example of a period of extreme scarcity, during which Parisians would 'glean' and recycle everything, highlighting a contrast with modern wastefulness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michael Waldmann">
      <data key="d0">Michael Waldmann</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Michael Waldmann is the executive director of The Society of St. Andrew (SoSA). He states that if all wasted food in the U.S. were gleaned, it could feed all 40 million hungry Americans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Arizona">
      <data key="d0">University of Arizona</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The University of Arizona is cited for a study which concluded that reducing food waste could result in tens of billions of dollars in savings for U.S. consumers and manufacturers annually.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Protection Agency (Epa)">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Protection Agency (Epa)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is an agency cited for providing data on food waste, stating that it accounts for over 14% of the municipal solid waste stream and totals more than 34 million tons per year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stockholm International Water Institute (Siwi)">
      <data key="d0">Stockholm International Water Institute (Siwi)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An international institute that published a 2008 policy brief titled 'Saving Water: From Field to Fork – Curbing Losses and Wastage in the Food Chain'.&lt;SEP&gt;The Stockholm International Water Institute (SIWI) is an institution that conducted a 2008 study concluding that up to 50% of food is lost between the field and the fork, attributing this to food miles and the number of handlers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Frances Moore Lappé">
      <data key="d0">Frances Moore Lappé</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Author of the 1971 bestseller 'Diet for a Small Planet', which first argued that global hunger is not caused by a lack of food production but by issues of distribution and politics.&lt;SEP&gt;Frances Moore Lappé is an individual, likely an author or expert, mentioned in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Frances Moore Lappé is the author of the 1971 bestseller 'Diet for a Small Planet.' Her work is referenced to support the idea that the world produces enough food, and that hunger is a problem of distribution and waste.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gleaning">
      <data key="d0">Gleaning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Based on the information provided, Gleaning is the practice of collecting leftover crops and surplus food from various sources, such as fields after a commercial harvest, farms, and yards. This act of grassroots or community-supported foraging is a form of food recovery that prevents food from going to waste. Gleaning is presented as a key and practical solution to combat both food waste and hunger, as it increases the efficiency of the food system while providing healthy food for those in need. It is framed as a community-oriented activity, with organizations like The Society of St. Andrew and Marin Organic serving as prime examples of successful gleaning operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0809b82d4ca6415545d8263dbdcfcf73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6db795582f52e35a0bb9aea86e66f7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b79b32f16bacae95e48f2f51ab5dee0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f65dcd9bf73d5a51e1f5f20d7e1772</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Food Waste">
      <data key="d0">Food Waste</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A central topic addressed in the source material, with specific citations from the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency and FoodProductionDaily.com.&lt;SEP&gt;Food Waste is a central theme of the text, described as a 'profligate' problem in the modern food system. The text quantifies its scale with data from the EPA and SIWI and contrasts it with the existence of widespread hunger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffa8b612642fa385d2af41e902aa75d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parisians">
      <data key="d0">Parisians</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Parisians during World War II are used as an example of a group that practiced extreme gleaning and recycling due to resource scarcity, contrasting with modern wastefulness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hunger">
      <data key="d0">Hunger</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hunger is a major societal problem discussed in the text. It is argued that hunger persists not due to a lack of food production, but because of massive food waste and inefficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Hunger is a social issue discussed as a topic in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The central problem addressed in the text. It is described as a paradox where a significant portion of the world's population is undernourished despite sufficient global food production, with the issue being attributed to poor distribution and politics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c9669112f71f37c0273243e8cc59e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db735f250cad30d060b6ea644151e172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UN World Food Program">
      <data key="d0">UN World Food Program</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A United Nations program that provides data on global hunger, confirming that somewhere from 900 million to over 1 billion people are undernourished.&lt;SEP&gt;The food-assistance branch of the United Nations and the world's largest humanitarian organization addressing hunger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38c9174dd4002f6529026fc7fd3c23f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Society of St. Andrew (SoSA)">
      <data key="d0">Society of St. Andrew (SoSA)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">"A non-profit organization that coordinates gleaning efforts to salvage produce for people in need."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;The largest gleaning organization in the United States, founded in Virginia. SoSA coordinates tens of thousands of volunteers to collect and distribute millions of pounds of fresh produce annually that would otherwise be wasted. It has also initiated several other hunger-relief programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60121fd34d6b068a55f53ef625e1f266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ken Horne">
      <data key="d0">Ken Horne</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-founder of the Society of St. Andrew (SoSA). He advocates for gleaning as a 'simple, common sense solution' to hunger in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ray Buchanan">
      <data key="d0">Ray Buchanan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Co-founder of the Society of St. Andrew (SoSA), alongside Ken Horne.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-347d33c4b812366f78a72782c922f5f8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1753740879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="United Methodist Virginia Conference">
      <data key="d0">United Methodist Virginia Conference</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The religious organization that gave its blessing to the founding of the Society of St. Andrew in Virginia.</data>
