      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="North America">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text describes North America as a location that has thousands of bee species but only one species of honey bee.</data>
      <data key="d8">geography,habitat,species distribution</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Southeast Asia">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Southeast Asia is identified as the native region for five of the world's six honey bee species.</data>
      <data key="d8">geography,origin,species distribution</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Western Honey Bee">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Western honey bee is identified as the single species of honey bee present in the Western Hemisphere, making it a specific type of honey bee.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological taxonomy,species classification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Yellow Jacket">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text clarifies that the yellow jacket, a type of wasp, is often mistaken for a honey bee and is frequently responsible for stings blamed on bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">insect distinction,misidentification,public perception</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Bumblebee">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text notes that the bumblebee is a closer relative to the honey bee than a wasp, but is still a different type of bee, which adds to public confusion.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological relationship,misidentification,species comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Animal Kingdom">
      <data key="d6">5.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text places the honey bee within the Animal Kingdom as part of its taxonomic classification to provide context.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological classification,taxonomy</data>
