      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="The Colony">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The life, labor, and development of an individual honey bee are dictated by the collective needs of the colony, which ensures the hive's survival and balance.</data>
      <data key="d8">collective needs,individual purpose,social structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Needs of the Colony">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The needs of the colony are the primary driver determining a honey bee's role, the pace of its development, and the tasks it performs throughout its life.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral driver,social regulation,task allocation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Winter Months">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the winter months, a honey bee's activity level decreases, which reduces wear on its body and allows it to live longer.</data>
      <data key="d8">inactivity,lifespan,seasonal behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Hive">
      <data key="d6">17.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive is the domicile for the honey bee colony, where all life-cycle activities, including the tracheal mite infestation and its treatment, take place.&lt;SEP&gt;The hive is the physical home of the honey bee, where it interacts with other bees and where the wear and tear of a busy environment contributes to the aging process.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony life,environment,habitat,physical space,social environment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Tracheal Mites">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tracheal mites are parasites that live inside the tracheae of honey bees. This infestation shortens the bees' lives and can lead to the death of the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">host-parasite,infestation,mortality</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Winter">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Winter is the most stressful season for honey bees, as their population naturally declines. This stress is severely compounded by a peak in the tracheal mite population, leading to the highest rates of colony death.</data>
      <data key="d8">mortality,population dynamics,seasonal stress</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Northerly Regions">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bee colonies in northerly regions face greater challenges surviving winter, and a tracheal mite infestation further increases the risk of the colony dying during this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">environmental stress,geographic vulnerability,wintering</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Beekeeping Equipment">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeeping equipment is used to house, manage, and transport honey bees for apiculture purposes.</data>
      <data key="d8">apiculture tools,species management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bee" target="Bee Diseases and Pests">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees are the hosts for the various diseases and pests described in the text, such as foulbrood, chalkbrood, and mites.</data>
      <data key="d8">pathology,species vulnerability</data>
