    </edge>
    <edge source="Africanized Bee" target="Apiary Inspection Program">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The control and management of the spreading Africanized bee, a threat to humans, falls under the scope of apiary inspection programs.</data>
      <data key="d8">invasive species,public safety,regulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Africanized Bee" target="Humans">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Africanized bee is described in the text as posing a potentially serious threat to humans.</data>
      <data key="d8">human-animal conflict,invasive species risk,public safety threat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Africanized Bee" target="Beekeeping Regulations">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The spread of the Africanized bee is a primary driver for the creation of new and stricter beekeeping regulations due to public safety concerns.</data>
      <data key="d8">public safety,regulatory driver</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Africanized Bee" target="Honeybees">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Africanized bee is a type of honeybee whose perceived aggression can lead to public backlash and bans on the keeping of all honeybees in an area.</data>
      <data key="d8">public perception,reputational risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Africanized Bee" target="African Bee">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Africanized bee is a hybrid that resulted from crossbreeding involving the African bee race.</data>
      <data key="d8">crossbreeding,genetic origin</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Africanized Bee" target="Swarm">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text warns that a captured swarm, particularly in certain areas, could be a colony of Africanized bees, which poses a significant and unnecessary risk for a novice.</data>
      <data key="d8">safety risk,unknown origin</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Africanized Bee" target="Swarms">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A significant disadvantage of collecting swarms is the risk that they may be Africanized bees, which has led to regulations in some areas requiring their destruction by authorized personnel to prevent their spread.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee type,regulation,risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Africanized Bee" target="State or Provincial Authorities">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The presence of Africanized bees is the direct cause for state or provincial authorities to enact regulations restricting the handling of wild swarms.</data>
      <data key="d8">biosecurity,invasive species,regulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="First Era Of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the First Era of Beekeeping, encouraging swarming was a central management philosophy because it was the primary means of replacing lost colonies and increasing holdings.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony management,historical practice</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Honey Bee Colony">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Swarming is the natural method by which a honey bee colony reproduces, ensuring the perpetuation of the species by creating new colonies.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony reproduction,species survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Beekeepers">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers view swarming as a highly undesirable event because it negatively impacts their goals of honey production and maintaining strong colonies for winter.&lt;SEP&gt;The text highlights that managing and controlling swarming is a critical, and often challenging, aspect of a beekeeper's job.</data>
      <data key="d8">economic impact,hive management,human-animal conflict,management challenge,swarm control</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Queen Bee">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen bee plays a central role in swarming, as she is the individual who leaves the parent colony with the swarm to establish a new one.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony division,leadership</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Parent Colony">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Swarming directly impacts the Parent Colony by causing it to lose approximately half of its bee population and its established queen, severely weakening it.&lt;SEP&gt;The parent colony is the direct result of a swarming event, being the portion of the colony that remains in the original hive to continue its life cycle after the swarm has left.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony division,colony impact,origin,population loss,weakening event</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Swarm Control">
      <data key="d6">29.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Swarm control encompasses the beekeeping techniques designed to prevent or manage the swarming event, ideally by preventing the swarm impulse from developing in the first place.&lt;SEP&gt;Swarm control is a set of beekeeping practices specifically designed to prevent the natural event of swarming in a managed bee colony.&lt;SEP&gt;Swarm control is the management practice specifically aimed at preventing or intervening in the natural event of swarming.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping practice,beekeeping practices,intervention,management,prevention</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Colony">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Swarming is the natural mechanism for a bee colony to reproduce and perpetuate the species. The process involves the division of a single colony into two separate ones.&lt;SEP&gt;Swarming negatively impacts the colony by reducing its population, which can lead to the loss of honey production for the season and jeopardize its ability to survive the winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,colony reproduction,division,population impact,species perpetuation,survival risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Queen">
      <data key="d6">26.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The development and maturation of a new queen is a key part of the swarming cycle, as the old queen leaves with the swarm.&lt;SEP&gt;The queen is a central figure in a swarm. Her age is a contributing factor, and she is prepared by the workers to leave the hive with the swarm to start a new colony.&lt;SEP&gt;The queen leads a swarm when a portion of the colony leaves to establish a new home, which is a key part of colony reproduction.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavior,colony division,colony leadership,colony reproduction,leadership,preparation,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Primary Swarm Season">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Primary Swarm Season is the peak time of year when the vast majority (about 80%) of swarming events take place.</data>
      <data key="d8">peak event,seasonal activity,timing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Secondary Swarm Season">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Secondary Swarm Season is a later, less common period when swarming can occur, though swarms during this time have a lower survival rate.</data>
      <data key="d8">low survival,seasonal activity,timing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarming" target="Honey Production">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Swarming has a direct negative impact on a colony's Honey Production for the season, as the colony's population is halved and egg-laying is disrupted for weeks.</data>
      <data key="d8">cause and effect,economic consequence,productivity loss</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
