      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Honey Bees">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees produce and store large quantities of honey, which is their food source and 'wealth'. This stored surplus is the primary reason they can survive the winter and is what beekeepers may harvest.</data>
      <data key="d8">production,resource,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Predators">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey is the valuable resource that predators seek, and its protection is the primary driver for the bees' defensive stinging behavior.</data>
      <data key="d8">protection,resource,threat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Bees">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees exhibit defensive stinging behavior primarily to protect their large stores of honey, which allows their colony to survive through the winter.&lt;SEP&gt;The text warns that once honey is removed from the hive, bees will be agitated and will attempt to rob it back if it's not securely stored.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal instinct,defensive behavior,resource protection,robbing behavior,survival strategy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Stinging">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The defensive act of stinging is what allows bees to successfully protect and store their honey from would-be predators.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral driver,cause and effect,resource protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Nectar">
      <data key="d6">50.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Based on the descriptions provided, Honey is produced by bees from Nectar, which is the raw material or ingredient that they collect from flowers. Worker bees, sometimes referred to as house bees, then process and convert the collected Nectar to create Honey. This Honey serves as the bee colony's stored food, intended for long-term storage.</data>
      <data key="d8">conversion,food processing,food production,food source,processing,production,resource conversion,transformation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13ca632d7241d4efd7901ef6c477cffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Ordinary Brood Food">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey is a key ingredient, mixed with pollen and glandular secretions, to create ordinary brood food for worker larvae.</data>
      <data key="d8">ingredient,larval nutrition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Honey Bee Colony">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey serves as the essential food store that allows the honey bee colony to survive the winter when fresh nectar is unavailable.</data>
      <data key="d8">food storage,winter survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Beekeepers">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are the producers and managers of honey. The text provides them with detailed instructions on how to properly handle, store, and process their honey to maintain its quality and deal with granulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Honey is the primary product that beekeepers harvest from the colony, representing a symbiotic aspect of their relationship with the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">guidance,harvest,human interaction,management,production</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Nutrition">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey is one of the two principal components of a honey bee's diet, providing essential carbohydrates for energy.</data>
      <data key="d8">diet,energy source,food component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Colony">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A colony stores honey as a vital food reserve for its own survival, not for the beekeeper's benefit.</data>
      <data key="d8">food storage,survival resource</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Pollen">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey naturally contains pollen that is inadvertently mixed in during the collection and production process by bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">composition,natural inclusion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Honey Processing">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The event of Honey Processing results in the production of Honey, the final, stored food product.</data>
      <data key="d8">conversion result,output,product</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Honey Supers">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey supers are the specific hive boxes where bees store honey. The text links super size directly to honey weight, stating a shallow super holds about 30 pounds and a mid-depth holds 35 pounds.</data>
      <data key="d8">capacity measurement,storage function</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Nucleus Hive">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A nucleus hive's inclusion of honey stores is an advantage, as it provides the colony with initial resources and improves its chances of producing a surplus in the first year.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony resources,production potential</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Package Bees">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A significant disadvantage of package bees is that their slow initial growth rate means they are unlikely to produce any surplus honey in the first season.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony growth,production timeline</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Beekeeper">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">For most hobbyist beekeepers, the lack of surplus honey in the first season from a package of bees should not be a major consideration.</data>
      <data key="d8">hobbyist priorities,production expectation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="The Cluster">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The cluster's survival depends on its consumption of honey stores to generate heat. The cluster moves towards and through the honey stored in the frames.</data>
      <data key="d8">consumption,energy source,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Frames">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey is stored by the bees in the wax combs built on the hive's frames. These frames serve as the pantry for the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">food reserve,hive structure,storage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Removing The Crop">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The entire purpose of the 'Removing the Crop' event is to harvest honey from the hive. This operation is the method by which the beekeeper collects the final product.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping goals,harvesting,resource extraction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Burr Comb">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The purpose for which bees build burr comb is to create additional space for storing honey.</data>
      <data key="d8">cause and effect,resource management,storage expansion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Honey Extraction">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey extraction is the specific process of separating the liquid honey from the wax combs after the supers have been harvested.</data>
      <data key="d8">product processing,separation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Supers">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supers are the hive boxes where bees store honey in combs; after being removed from the hive for harvesting, they will drip honey from broken comb.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive component,storage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Honey Robbing">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The harvested honey is the target of the honey robbing event, where bees attempt to reclaim it, potentially leading to the loss of the crop.</data>
      <data key="d8">crop loss,resource conflict</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Dehumidifier">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A dehumidifier is used to protect honey from absorbing too much atmospheric moisture, which could cause it to ferment, by controlling the humidity of the storage area.</data>
      <data key="d8">moisture control,product preservation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Honey Fermentation">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If honey absorbs too much moisture after being harvested (due to being hygroscopic), it can undergo fermentation, which spoils the product.</data>
      <data key="d8">chemical process,product spoilage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Honey Granulation">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Granulation (or crystallization) is described as a natural state that most honeys will enter as a function of time and their specific sugar composition.</data>
      <data key="d8">chemical property,natural process</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Heat">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Heat has a dual relationship with honey: it is used to reverse or prevent granulation, but it also causes deterioration in honey's quality. The text provides specific temperature guidelines to balance these effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Heat is applied to honey for two main reasons: to make it less viscous (flow more easily) during extraction and to reverse granulation during storage.</data>
      <data key="d8">processing technique,property management,quality control,temperature treatment,thermal effects</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="The Extracting Process">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The entire multi-step extracting process is designed to remove honey from the comb, strain it, and prepare it for bottling or storage, while minimizing quality issues like air bubbles.</data>
      <data key="d8">core purpose,product processing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Bubbles And Foaming">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Splashing honey during the extraction, straining, and bottling process can introduce air, causing bubbles and foaming which are considered unsightly in the final product.</data>
      <data key="d8">process defect,quality issue</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Granulation">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Granulation is a natural physical process that most honey undergoes over time. The text is largely focused on explaining why this happens to honey and how to manage it.</data>
      <data key="d8">crystallization,natural process,physical property</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey" target="Extractors">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Extractors are machines specifically designed to remove honey from the hive's combs.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping equipment,honey processing</data>
