    <edge source="Pollination" target="The Second Era of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The understanding of the process of pollination and the crucial role bees play in it was a key scientific advancement that occurred during the Second Era (in the 1700s).</data>
      <data key="d8">ecological understanding,scientific discovery</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollination" target="Honey Bees">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees are the agents of pollination, a vital ecological function whose discovery greatly increased the recognized importance of bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological process,ecological role</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollination" target="Foraging">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The specific, non-random nature of foraging, where a bee visits the same flower species, is the mechanism that accomplishes pollination, making the bee valuable to ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d8">cause and effect,ecological impact,symbiotic process</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feral Colonies" target="Swarms">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text explains that Feral Colonies are sustained as a population because when colonies die, they are replaced by Swarms from other colonies.</data>
