      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Beekeeping Challenges">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees are directly and negatively impacted by Beekeeping Challenges such as disease, drought, and harsh winters, which can cause their colonies to weaken and die.</data>
      <data key="d8">environmental threat,mortality factor,survival pressure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Hive">
      <data key="d6">88.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Hive serves as the home for a colony of Bees, providing a man-made structure and shelter where they live, work, raise their brood, and store food. The beekeeper's work is centered on managing the Hive, which includes installing or transferring Bees from a package or nuc into what will become their permanent home. Once inside, the Bees inhabit the Hive and modify it with propolis and burr comb according to their needs.

As the Hive is the immediate environment for the Bees, they are sensitive to it; any physical disturbance, such as vibrations or sharp bangs, is directly felt by the Bees and affects their temperament. They will sting to defend their home from any perceived aggression or threat, like harassment by predators. The beekeeper's management of the Hive, such as cleaning or reversing it, directly impacts the Bees' environment and ability to thrive. A key external indicator of the colony's health and normalcy is the Bees' foraging activity at the Hive entrance.</data>
      <data key="d8">activity indicator,cohabitation,colonization,colony home,colony housing,colony management,environment management,environmental stimulus,foraging,habitat,habitat protection,housing,instinct,managed environment,population transfer,relocation,seasonal behavior,sensitivity,territorial defense</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Stinging">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text describes stinging as the primary defensive behavior that bees use to protect their colony and honey stores from perceived threats.</data>
      <data key="d8">defensive behavior,instinct,protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Beekeepers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers have a unique and regular interaction with bees, which leads to them being frequently stung and, in turn, developing a tolerance.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal husbandry,exposure,occupational interaction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Predators">
      <data key="d6">15.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees react defensively with stinging against predators who attempt to raid their hive for honey, demonstrating a classic predator-prey defensive interaction.&lt;SEP&gt;The presence or harassment by predators is identified as a potential reason for a bee colony to become unusually defensive or 'mean'.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony defense,conflict,defense,interspecies interaction,threat response</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Hive Location">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The choice of Hive Location should align with the natural preferences of Bees, such as a southerly exposure and protection from excessive wind, to ensure their health and productivity.</data>
      <data key="d8">habitat preference,well-being</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Animals">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees are described as having negative interactions with certain Animals, particularly horses, whose odor can trigger stinging incidents.</data>
      <data key="d8">defensive behavior,interspecies conflict</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Swarms">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Studies of Swarms (bees seeking new homes) provide insight into the natural location preferences of Bees, which informs the advice given in the text.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral study,habitat preference</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Horses">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees and Horses have a problematic relationship; the text states that the odor of horses can trigger stinging incidents from the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">defensive behavior,interspecies conflict</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Pollination Process">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text introduces beekeeping by mentioning that bees enhance the Pollination Process, establishing a primary benefit of the activity.</data>
      <data key="d8">beneficial activity,ecosystem service</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Foundation">
      <data key="d6">35.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Within a hive's frames, Bees use the provided Foundation as a base to build their orderly wax comb. The Bees perform the essential task of "drawing out" the foundation, interacting with it directly to build a full honeycomb that fills the frame. During this process, they fasten the newly constructed comb to the end bars of the frame.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee activity,bee behavior,beekeeping supplies,comb building,comb construction,construction,hive construction,hive development,natural behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Covers">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If a cover sags, it can violate the 'bee space' above the frames, which causes the bees to seal the cover to the frame tops, making hive inspections disruptive.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral response,equipment design</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Smoker">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Smoker is used to puff smoke on the Bees to calm them, a key step in bee management during an inspection.&lt;SEP&gt;The smoker is used to produce smoke that calms and soothes the bees, making it easier and safer for the beekeeper to work with the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal handling,behavior modification,behavioral control,hive management,tool function</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Feeder">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A feeder is used to provide feed to bees, which is especially important during cool weather when they will not leave their cluster to find food.&lt;SEP&gt;The feeder is installed specifically to provide sugar syrup as food for the bees in the new colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal husbandry,colony care,nourishment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Veil">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The veil is an essential piece of protective equipment used to prevent bees from stinging the beekeeper's face and head.</data>
      <data key="d8">personal protection,safety equipment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Gloves">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The use of gloves by a beekeeper increases the risk of accidentally crushing and killing bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee safety,equipment risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Queen Excluder">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees are described as being decidedly reluctant to move past a queen excluder to draw out foundation.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,equipment interaction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Nectar flow">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A weak nectar flow makes bees less likely to draw foundation, affecting hive productivity.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,environmental factors</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Beekeeper's Attire">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text extensively details how a beekeeper's choice of attire directly impacts bee behavior. Bees are said to react adversely to dark, woolly fabrics and shiny objects, making light-colored, smooth, non-reflective clothing the recommended choice to keep them calm.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,protective clothing,safety</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Odors">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The document explains that various odors can either attract or offend bees, altering their behavior. Perfumes can attract bees and cause them to become entangled, while offensive smells like gasoline can agitate them, highlighting the importance of being scent-neutral.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,scent influence,sensory reaction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Beekeeper's Behavior">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A beekeeper's actions and movements are described as a primary factor in influencing bee temperament. Bees are sensitive to motion and vibrations, so calm, slow movements help keep them settled, whereas running or thrashing will provoke an aggressive response.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,methodology,safety</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Vibrations">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees are sensitive to substrate vibrations, meaning a beekeeper's movements on or near the hive stand can be transmitted through the hive and cause the colony to become upset.</data>
      <data key="d8">agitation trigger,cause and effect,sensory perception</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Movement">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees' vision makes them highly perceptive of movement. A beekeeper's slow, calm movements make them less noticeable, whereas sudden, thrashing motions attract negative attention and provoke a defensive reaction.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavior trigger,threat perception,visual stimulus</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Shiny Objects">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Aroused bees are attracted to the flash of shiny objects like rings as a beekeeper's hand moves, potentially mistaking it for a threat and targeting it for attack.</data>
      <data key="d8">mistaken threat,safety hazard,visual attraction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Experienced Beekeeper">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">An experienced beekeeper can be brought in to analyze why a beekeeper is having consistent trouble with their bees, determining if the issue is with the beekeeper's methods or the bees' temperament.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,mentorship,problem-solving</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Beekeeping Equipment">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeeping equipment, such as hives and feeders, is used to house and sustain bees, while tools like smokers and protective gear are used to safely manage them.</data>
      <data key="d8">housing,interaction,management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Sugar Syrup">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees, particularly in a new colony, must be fed sugar syrup to help them get established. The text provides a recipe and instructions on how to feed them.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony support,feeding,nutrition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Queen">
      <data key="d6">27.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen is the central figure whose status (present and laying) determines the calm, routine behavior of the Bees in the colony.&lt;SEP&gt;The bees in the colony must communicate with and accept the queen through her cage before she is released to lead the hive.&lt;SEP&gt;The queen is the central figure of the bee colony, and in a package, the worker bees will cluster around her.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,colony hierarchy,influence,leadership,social acceptance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Weather">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text suggests that bee temperament can be negatively affected by external factors such as oppressive weather.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral influence,environmental factor</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Installation of the Package">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The entire installation event is focused on the successful transfer of the package of bees into their new hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">process subject,relocation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
