    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Pollen Pellets">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The presence of Pollen Pellets on the legs of Bees is a key visual indicator of successful foraging activity.</data>
      <data key="d8">activity indicator,foraging,resource collection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Hive Entrance">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Beekeeper observes the activity of the Bees at the Hive Entrance to make an initial assessment of the colony's health and foraging status.</data>
      <data key="d8">activity indicator,external inspection,observation point</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Colony">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bees are the individual members that collectively form the colony, carrying out all the essential tasks such as raising brood and building comb.</data>
      <data key="d8">collective action,composition,labor</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Wax">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees secrete wax from their bodies, which is the material used to construct the comb in the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological production,construction material</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Fall Feeding">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The bees are the recipients of fall feeding; they must consume and process the sugar syrup into storable food before the weather becomes too cold for this activity."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">colony nutrition,resource processing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Metabolism">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metabolism is the life-sustaining biological process occurring within the bees, which dictates their consumption of resources and the production of waste products like moisture and CO2 within the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological process,resource consumption</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Cluster">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees form a cluster as a primary survival mechanism to stay warm during the cold winter months.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral adaptation,thermoregulation,winter survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Hard Ball Candy">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees consume Hard Ball Candy as a food source provided by the beekeeper during winter. The text warns that if the sugar is burned during preparation, it can kill the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">feeding,nutrition,winter survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Dry Granulated Sugar">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees consume Dry Granulated Sugar as an emergency food for subsistence when their normal food reserves are perilously low.</data>
      <data key="d8">emergency feeding,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Pollen Substitute">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees are fed Pollen Substitute patties to provide necessary nutrients for brood rearing when natural pollen is not available, particularly in late winter and early spring.</data>
      <data key="d8">brood rearing,nutrition,supplementary feeding</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Winter">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees exhibit specific survival behaviors during winter, such as clustering together and moving upwards through the hive to consume honey stores.&lt;SEP&gt;During Winter, the survival of the bees depends on having adequate food stores, which may require the beekeeper to intervene with feeding to prevent starvation.</data>
      <data key="d8">adaptation,seasonal behavior,seasonal impact,survival,survival strategy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Spring">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In Spring, bees become more active, increase their rate of food consumption and brood rearing, and begin to forage outside the hive as the weather improves.&lt;SEP&gt;In spring, the bees' natural instinct is to expand the colony upwards, which drives the need for beekeeper intervention to provide more space.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony growth,expansion instinct,foraging,population growth,seasonal activity,seasonal behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Brood Rearing">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brood rearing is the process by which bees raise their young, ensuring the growth and continuation of the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,colony growth,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Foraging">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Foraging is the natural behavior of bees to seek and collect nectar and pollen from the environment.</data>
      <data key="d8">food collection,natural behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Honey Surpluses">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The creation of honey surpluses is a key productive outcome of the bees' work, which the beekeeper can harvest after the colony is self-sufficient.</data>
      <data key="d8">economic outcome,production goal</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Brood Nest">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bees create and maintain the brood nest, which is fundamental to the colony's reproduction and population growth. The beekeeper observes the brood nest to assess the health and vitality of the bees and their queen.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,life cycle,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Removing The Crop">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The process of 'Removing the Crop' involves separating the bees from the honey supers to allow for harvest. The text mentions specific methods, like using a bee brush, for this purpose.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee management,harvesting technique,human-animal interaction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Propolis">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees create and use propolis to seal their hive in preparation for winter. This is a natural, preparatory behavior.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive preparation,material use,natural behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Burr Comb">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees construct burr comb as a method to expand their honey storage capacity within the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">adaptive behavior,hive construction,resource storage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Fall">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In the fall, bees actively prepare their hive for winter by strategically organizing the location of the cluster and honey stores.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive organization,seasonal preparation,wintering strategy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Honey Stores">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees create and consume honey stores, which are their essential food source, particularly for surviving the winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">food source,resource consumption,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Super">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supers must be cleared of Bees before the honey can be harvested. All the methods described are for this purpose.</data>
      <data key="d8">management,removal</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Honey Harvesting">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A key part of honey harvesting is removing the bees from the honey supers using various methods like a blower or brush.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee removal,harvesting process</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Blower">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A blower is a tool used to forcefully remove bees from the honey supers before the honey is extracted.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee removal,equipment use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Fume Board">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A fume board is a tool used to clear bees from honey supers, utilizing a foul-smelling liquid to drive them out.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee removal,equipment use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Brush">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A brush is used to physically remove the last remaining bees from a super that other methods, like a blower, fail to clear.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee removal,manual tool</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Honey Robbing">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey robbing is an event carried out by bees, including those from neighboring colonies, when they are agitated by the smell of harvested honey and try to steal it.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal instinct,robbing behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bees" target="Comb">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text states that bees build the comb and strengthen it a little more each year, making it less fragile over time.</data>
      <data key="d8">natural creation,structural integrity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarms" target="Police and Fire Departments">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Police and fire departments are a source for finding swarms, as they receive calls from the public about bee problems and are pleased to refer beekeepers to handle them.</data>
      <data key="d8">public service,sourcing,swarm capture</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarms" target="Beekeeper Associations">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeeper associations are a source for learning about available bees, including swarms and existing colonies, through word-of-mouth and publications associated with the beekeeping community.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee acquisition,information source,networking</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarms" target="Authorized Personnel">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In regions with Africanized bees, authorized personnel are the only individuals legally permitted to pick up and are often required to destroy swarms.</data>
      <data key="d8">authorization,regulation,swarm control</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarms" target="Swarm Season">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text explicitly states that swarms are only available during the 'swarm season' in spring to early summer, directly linking the event to the availability of the resource.</data>
      <data key="d8">availability,life cycle,seasonality</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeeping Knowledge" target="Training Program">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Training Program, such as a bee school or workshop, is a primary vehicle for a beekeeper to acquire foundational Beekeeping Knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d8">education,knowledge acquisition,skill development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-60575ab4028f754c314fcbcee392db85</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Worker Bee">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The worker bee's life is defined by the hive; it spends its first phase working inside as a house bee and its second phase foraging for resources to bring back to the hive, which it also maintains and guards.</data>
      <data key="d8">habitat,hive maintenance,labor</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Brood">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The brood, which includes all eggs, larvae, and pupae, develops entirely within the protective waxen cells located in the center of the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">development environment,life cycle,shelter</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Propolis">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Propolis is used by worker bees to strengthen the honeycomb and seal cracks and crevices within the hive, serving as a building and maintenance material.</data>
      <data key="d8">construction material,hive maintenance,sealant</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Brood Nest">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The brood nest is the central nursery area located within the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">internal structure,location,nursery</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Honey Bees">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive is the central dwelling for honey bees, where they conduct essential activities like storing food and raising brood. Their lives and patterned behaviors, such as returning with pollen, are oriented around the hive.&lt;SEP&gt;The hive is the central home for the honey bee colony, where they store food and conduct essential communications.</data>
      <data key="d8">central location,colony life,communication hub,dwelling,habitat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Bee Colony">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive is the central location for the bee colony where the various dances are performed to communicate critical information for the colony's operations.</data>
      <data key="d8">information hub,organizational base</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Hive Location">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text is a detailed guide on selecting a proper Hive Location for a Hive, which is presented as one of the most critical decisions for a beekeeper to make.</data>
      <data key="d8">placement criteria,site selection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Vandalism">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Vandalism is a significant security threat to a Hive, especially if it is placed in a remote or highly visible area. Making the hive less visible is a key preventative measure.</data>
      <data key="d8">security risk,threat mitigation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Animals">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Certain Animals can pose a threat to a Hive; for example, livestock may use it as a scratching post and knock it over, while the proximity of horses can trigger defensive behavior from the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">interspecies conflict,proximity risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Vandals">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Vandals are identified as a direct threat to a Hive, potentially causing significant damage. This risk influences the recommendation to make hives less visible.</data>
      <data key="d8">property damage,security threat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Thieves">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Thieves pose a risk to a Hive, as they may steal the entire colony or its most valuable parts. This is another reason to ensure the hive is not easily visible or accessible.</data>
      <data key="d8">security threat,theft risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Livestock">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Livestock can pose an indirect threat to a Hive by using it as a scratching post and knocking it over.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal interaction,property damage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="New Beekeepers">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text strongly advises New Beekeepers to place their Hive nearby to allow for frequent, casual visits, which are important for learning and proper colony management.</data>
      <data key="d8">accessibility,beginner advice,management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Traffic">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Hive should be located away from heavy Traffic (vehicular, pedestrian) to protect bees from air turbulence and to prevent people from being stung by crossing the bees' flight path.</data>
      <data key="d8">flight path,risk mitigation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Snow">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">While Snow can provide insulation for a Hive, the location should not be one where the hive becomes completely buried, as bees need to be able to exit on warmer winter days.</data>
      <data key="d8">environmental interaction,winter survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Skunks">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Skunks are identified as a special problem and predator for a Hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">predator,security threat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Bears">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bears are identified as a special problem and predator for a Hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">predator,security threat</data>
