    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Honey Supers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey Supers are superstructures added to the main hive bodies to provide extra space for honey storage.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive component,modular design</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="The Continent">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text states that a hive can be successful almost anywhere on the continent, highlighting the wide geographical viability of beekeeping.</data>
      <data key="d8">geographic distribution,location suitability</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Italian Bees">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive serves as the man-made habitat for the colony of Italian bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal husbandry,beekeeping,habitat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Supers">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supers are components of the hive that are removed for the purpose of harvesting the honey stored within them.&lt;SEP&gt;Supers are modular boxes that are stacked as part of the overall hive structure, primarily for honey storage.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,harvesting,hive components,hive structure,modularity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Frames">
      <data key="d6">29.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A hive is the main structure that contains frames, which are essential for the bees to build their comb.&lt;SEP&gt;Frames are the core internal components of a hive, holding the comb. The text discusses how using a single size of frame throughout the hive (for both hive bodies and supers) allows for complete interchangeability of equipment.&lt;SEP&gt;Frames are the fundamental internal components of a hive, providing the structure on which the colony builds its home and stores resources.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,core component,housing,interchangeability,internal structure,structural component,structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Bottom Board">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bottom board is the foundational component of the hive, which must be cleaned by the beekeeper as part of hive maintenance.&lt;SEP&gt;The bottom board serves as the foundational floor of the entire hive structure.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,foundation,hive maintenance,hive structure,structural component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Beekeepers">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are the managers of the hive, responsible for its setup, inspection, and manipulation. The text is written as a guide for them.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal husbandry,human interaction,management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Hive Bodies">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hive bodies are the primary structural components that make up the main living area of the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,configuration,hive structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Hive Stand">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive is placed upon the hive stand, which elevates it from the ground.&lt;SEP&gt;The hive stand is a piece of equipment designed specifically to support and provide a stable base for one or two hives.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment function,equipment setup,foundation,structural support,support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Hive Tool">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive tool is used to pry apart sections of the hive, such as hive bodies and supers. Using the correct tool is critical to avoid damaging the hive structure.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment maintenance,tool use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Screwdriver">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Using a screwdriver instead of a proper hive tool is described as being destructive, causing the hive parts to get chewed up and leading to rapid deterioration.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment misuse,property damage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Installing Bees">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive is the final destination and permanent home for the bees being installed from a package.</data>
      <data key="d8">housing,process destination</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Installation of the Package">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The installation event involves preparing the hive by removing frames and placing the bees inside it.</data>
      <data key="d8">process location,setup</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Package of Bees">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The package of bees is the colony that is being introduced to live in the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">housing,relocation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Hive Frames">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hive frames are essential components of the hive that are manipulated (removed) to make space for the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,preparation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Hive Covers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive covers are the entrance to the hive that must be removed by the beekeeper to begin the installation.</data>
      <data key="d8">access,component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Installing A Package Of Bees">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The entire event of installing a package of bees takes place within and is centered around preparing and populating the permanent hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony housing,process location</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Installing A Nucleus Hive">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The process of installing a nucleus hive involves transferring the nuc's frames and bees into a larger, permanent hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony transfer,housing upgrade</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Queen Cage">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen cage is hung between two frames inside the hive during the introduction period.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment placement,integration process</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Feeder">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The feeder is placed on top of the hive's inner cover to provide nourishment for the newly installed colony within the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony support,equipment integration</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Inner Cover">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The inner cover is a specific component of the hive used for placing supplemental food like candy or dry sugar.&lt;SEP&gt;The inner cover is a standard, functional part of the hive, separating the main colony area from the feeder space and outer cover.</data>
      <data key="d8">feeding location,hive component,structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Outer Cover">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The outer cover is the protective top lid of the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive component,protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="New Colony">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Hive is the physical home provided for the New Colony to live and grow in.</data>
      <data key="d8">domicile,establishment,habitat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Package Installation">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Package Installation is the process of putting the bees into their new Hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">action,establishment,placement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Comb">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Comb is a critical internal structure built by bees inside the Hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">habitat construction,internal structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Colony">
      <data key="d6">17.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The colony lives within the hive, and the text describes how to manage the physical hive structure (e.g., adding a second hive body) to accommodate the growing colony.&lt;SEP&gt;The hive is the physical structure that provides shelter for the bee colony, and its proper winter configuration is crucial for the colony's survival.</data>
      <data key="d8">dependency,expansion,habitat,management,shelter</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Frame">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Frames are the essential internal components of a hive, holding the comb where the bees live, work, and raise brood.</data>
      <data key="d8">internal structure,structural component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Hive Body">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive is built by stacking one or more hive bodies on top of each other. The text describes adding a second hive body to expand the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">expansion,modular design,structural component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Food Stores">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Food stores, specifically honey and pollen, are contained within the frames of the hive. The text mentions that two deep hive bodies can store the required 60-70 pounds of honey."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">resource location,storage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Ventilation and Insulation">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Ventilation and insulation are modifications made directly to the hive structure, such as adding an insulation board, to protect the colony from winter moisture and cold."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">environmental control,hive modification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="The Cluster">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive provides the physical space where the cluster forms and moves; the cluster begins at the bottom of the hive in the fall and migrates upwards towards the top as winter progresses.</data>
      <data key="d8">habitat,seasonal movement,spatial dynamics</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Dead Bees">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Dead bees can accumulate and physically block the hive's entrances, impeding ventilation and flight, which requires clearing by either the beekeeper or the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">blockage,hygiene,maintenance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Super">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Super is a key modular component of a modern beehive, used specifically for honey storage.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping structure,component of</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Beeyard">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The beeyard is the geographical location where one or more hives are kept.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment placement,location</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Menthol Treatment">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Menthol Treatment is administered directly inside the hive, with the placement of the crystals (on top of frames or on the bottom board) depending on temperature.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive environment,medication,treatment application</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive" target="Re-infestation">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Other hives in the same geographic area are identified as a primary source for the re-infestation of a treated hive, making area-wide control extremely difficult.</data>
      <data key="d8">disease transmission,infestation source,proximity risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Colony">
      <data key="d6">30.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A colony is the social unit of honey bees, and its survival depends on the collective behaviors and communication of its members.&lt;SEP&gt;A honey bee colony's successful life is dependent on communication among its members.&lt;SEP&gt;Honey bees are organized into a colony, which is the fundamental social unit that a beekeeper manages and that functions year-round to ensure survival.</data>
      <data key="d8">communication,community,habitat,organization,social structure,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Predators">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Predators are a class of animals that hunt and consume honey bees, posing a threat to the colony.&lt;SEP&gt;The stinging behavior of honey bees is an evolutionary defense mechanism used to protect their colony and honey from being taken by predators.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony threat,defense,ecosystem interaction,predation,survival,threat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="The Second Era of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Second Era, significant scientific progress was made in understanding honey bee biology, and honey bees were first introduced to North America.</data>
      <data key="d8">scientific understanding,species introduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Romans">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Romans represent an early stage in beekeeping history characterized by a lack of scientific understanding of honey bee biology and behavior.</data>
      <data key="d8">early history,scientific misconception</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="East Coast">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees were first introduced to North America on the East Coast around 1628, as they are not native to the Western Hemisphere.</data>
      <data key="d8">geographic origin,species introduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="West Coast">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">By 1850, honey bees had been transported across the continent to the West Coast, marking their expansion in North America.</data>
      <data key="d8">geographic expansion,species migration</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Pilgrims">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text places the arrival of honey bees in North America in the context of early colonial history by stating they arrived 'soon after the Pilgrims'.</data>
      <data key="d8">historical context,timeline correlation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Wax">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees biologically produce wax to build their comb, a fundamental fact that corrected early beekeepers' misconceptions.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological production,material creation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Bee Learning">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees demonstrate the capacity for learning by modifying their behavior through experience, such as associating odors with food or memorizing the location of their hive. The text distinguishes this ability from abstract intelligence.</data>
      <data key="d8">cognitive ability,experience modification,learned behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Pheromones">
      <data key="d6">30.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees produce and interpret pheromones to communicate with each other, which is essential for the successful life and functioning of the colony.&lt;SEP&gt;Pheromones are chemical signals given off by honey bees to stimulate specific behavioral responses in other bees within the colony.&lt;SEP&gt;The lives of honey bees are described as being 'governed by pheromones,' which are odors that dictate their behavior and can influence their reaction to the distinct odors of different humans.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological control,chemical communication,colony behavior,communication,sensory influence,stimulus-response</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Intelligence">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text explicitly contrasts honey bees with the concept of intelligence, stating that while they can learn, they do not have the ability to 'apply knowledge, to reason, and to think abstractly'.</data>
      <data key="d8">cognitive limitation,conceptual distinction,learning vs intelligence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Honey Bee Colony">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Individual honey bees are the constituent members of the colony; their collective actions and life spans create the overall dynamics of the colony as a whole.</data>
      <data key="d8">collective behavior,composition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Brood Rearing">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The adult honey bees in the colony are responsible for carrying out all the tasks of brood rearing, including feeding the young and maintaining the nest temperature.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony duty,labor</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Pollen">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees collect pollen as a primary and essential food source. They add enzymes to it during collection and storage to enhance its dietary value.&lt;SEP&gt;Honey bees collect pollen from flowers to use as their source of protein.</data>
      <data key="d8">collection,food source,foraging,nutrition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Nectar">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees collect nectar from flowers as a source of nutrients, including small amounts of vitamins and minerals.</data>
      <data key="d8">collection,food source</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Sugar">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers feed sugar to honey bees as a food source, particularly when other sources are unavailable. The text specifies that only pure, food-grade sugar should be used.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping,feeding,nutrition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Dance Language">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees use a 'dance language' to communicate specific and vital information, such as the location of food sources or potential new homes for a swarm.</data>
      <data key="d8">communication,foraging</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Flower">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees depend on flowers to collect both nectar and pollen, which are essential components of their diet.</data>
      <data key="d8">food source,foraging,symbiosis</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Water">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees actively collect water as a source of essential minerals.</data>
      <data key="d8">foraging,hydration,nutrition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Salt">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Salt is detrimental to honey bees, as its consumption reduces their longevity.</data>
      <data key="d8">health,negative impact,toxicity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Impure Sugars">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Impure sugars, such as molasses and certain syrups, are specified as being seriously harmful or potentially lethal to honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">danger,feeding warning,toxicity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Pollen Baskets">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees use pollen baskets on their hind legs to transport pollen back to the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">anatomy,function,transport</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Granulated Sugar (Sucrose)">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Granulated sugar (sucrose) is an acceptable and safe form of sugar for beekeepers to feed to honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping,feeding,nutrition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="High-Fructose Corn Syrup">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">High-fructose corn syrup is an acceptable food for honey bees as long as it is food-quality and free of additives.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping,feeding,nutrition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Pollen Substitute">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pollen substitute is a manufactured material fed to honey bees by beekeepers to provide protein, especially during times of natural pollen scarcity.</data>
      <data key="d8">artificial diet,feeding,nutrition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Large Cities">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Large Cities are described as surprisingly successful locations for keeping Honey Bees, indicating their adaptability to urban environments.</data>
      <data key="d8">habitat viability,urban beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Suburbs">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Suburbs are presented as suitable and successful environments for keeping Honey Bees, highlighting their viability outside of purely rural areas.</data>
      <data key="d8">habitat viability,suburban beekeeping</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Flowering Plants">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey Bees collect food from Flowering Plants, which are essential for their survival and honey production. The bees' foraging range is centered around the availability of these plants.</data>
      <data key="d8">food source,pollination</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="American Foulbrood (AFB)">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">American Foulbrood is a fatal disease that infects the brood of Honey Bees, leading to the progressive decline and eventual death of the entire colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">disease,host-pathogen,infection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Ants">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ants are a pest that invade honey bee colonies and can cause their destruction.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony threat,pest</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740503</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Wax Moths">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Wax moths are a significant pest, especially to weak honey bee colonies. Their larvae destroy the hive's comb and can kill bee brood, posing a major threat to the colony's survival.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive destruction,pest</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740503</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Mice">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mice invade honey bee hives to nest during the winter, causing significant damage to the comb and potentially causing the entire colony to abscond.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive invasion,pest</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740503</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Skunks">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Skunks are predators that eat adult honey bees at the hive entrance, which can severely weaken the colony over a period of time.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony threat,predation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740503</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Napthalene">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text explicitly warns that Napthalene, another moth repellent, should not be used as its residue is harmful to honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">safety warning,toxicity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740503</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Propolis">
      <data key="d6">23.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees collect propolis from trees to use as a building and sealing material within the hive.&lt;SEP&gt;Honey bees produce and use propolis as a defensive material, occasionally using it to embalm intruders like mice that they cannot physically remove from the hive.&lt;SEP&gt;Propolis is a substance created and used by honey bees to seal and protect their hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee product,defense mechanism,foraging,hive construction,natural material</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740503</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Comb">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees build, maintain, and police the comb within their hive. A healthy colony actively removes pests and their eggs from the comb.</data>
      <data key="d8">construction,hive maintenance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Mouse Guard">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers use a mouse guard to protect the honey bee colony from invasion and damage by mice during colder months.</data>
      <data key="d8">pest management,protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="The Public">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees are described as 'too important to our way of life' because of their pollination activities, establishing a dependency relationship where public and agricultural welfare relies on healthy bee populations.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural importance,economic value,pollination</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Insecticides">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Insecticides are described as the most significant chemical threat to honey bees, capable of causing mass death either directly to foragers in the field or indirectly through contaminated resources brought back to the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,contamination,lethal threat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Orchards">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Orchards are identified as high-risk environments for honey bees because they are frequently treated with various sprays, leading to unavoidable and harmful exposure for any bee colonies located there.</data>
      <data key="d8">avoidance,environmental hazard,location risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Corn">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees are attracted to corn for its pollen, but this attraction becomes a danger because corn is often sprayed with insecticides during its pollen-yielding period, making it a key source of contamination.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural risk,contamination source,deceptive attraction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Beekeeping Community">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The beekeeping community is fundamentally responsible for the management, care, and protection of honey bee colonies from various threats, including poisoning from insecticides.</data>
      <data key="d8">management,protection,stewardship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Farms">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Farms are listed as one of the primary locations where honey bees are at risk of being exposed to pesticides.</data>
      <data key="d8">agriculture,environmental hazard,exposure risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Home Gardens">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Home gardens are identified as a common area where honey bees can be exposed to various pesticides.</data>
      <data key="d8">environment,exposure risk,suburban hazard</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Corn Pollen">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey bees are described as being very attracted to corn pollen, which draws them into potentially dangerous, insecticide-treated areas.</data>
      <data key="d8">attraction,foraging,risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Varroa Jacobsonii">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Varroa jacobsonii is an external parasite that infests honey bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">infestation,parasitism</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740511</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Dancing">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Dancing is a primary method of communication used by honey bees to share information within the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavior,communication</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740511</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Thorax">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The thorax is the middle body section of a honey bee, to which the legs and wings are attached.</data>
      <data key="d8">anatomy,morphology</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740512</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="U.s.">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text indicates a historical relationship, noting the 'introduction to U.S.' of honey bees, signifying their establishment in this geographical area.</data>
      <data key="d8">geographic distribution,species introduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Mites">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mites are a major category of parasites that infest honey bees and are a significant concern for colony health and survival.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony threat,parasitism,pest</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="European Foulbrood">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">European Foulbrood is a disease that directly impacts the health of honey bee colonies by infecting their larvae.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,disease,pathogen</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="American Foulbrood">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">American Foulbrood is a severe disease that directly impacts the health of honey bee colonies by infecting their larvae.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,disease,pathogen</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Nosema Disease">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Nosema is a disease that infects adult honey bees, impacting their health and the viability of the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,disease,pathogen</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Queen(s)">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen is the essential reproductive individual and leader of the honey bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste system,colony structure,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="Drones">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Drones are the male members of the honey bee colony, responsible for mating.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste system,colony structure,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Bees" target="House Bee">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The House Bee is a life stage of the worker honey bee, performing tasks within the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste system,division of labor,life cycle</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
