      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Up Side">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The 'up side' of beekeeping, such as the enthusiasm and serenity it provides, is the primary motivation for beekeepers to persist with the hobby despite its difficulties.</data>
      <data key="d8">experience,motivation,reward</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Down Side">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers must contend with the 'down side' of the hobby, which includes physical discomfort, working alone, and the certainty of being stung.</data>
      <data key="d8">challenge,experience,hardship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Tolerance to Stings">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text explains that beekeepers are a group of people who typically build a tolerance to stings as a direct result of their regular occupational exposure to bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">adaptation,immunity,occupational exposure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Beekeeper Reaction">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The mildest, first-level reaction to a sting is explicitly referred to as 'the usual beekeeper reaction', highlighting its prevalence in this group due to their developed tolerance.</data>
      <data key="d8">immunity,occupational characteristic,typical response</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="General Public">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text contrasts the perceptions and reactions of the General Public to bee stings with those of Beekeepers, who tend to ignore stings due to acquired tolerance.</data>
      <data key="d8">comparative experience,perception gap,tolerance difference</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5a4a1a431b94a27a92d263b7dde5aa5a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Bee Sting">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are frequently exposed to bee stings through their work and can build up a tolerance, though it may wear off over time.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological tolerance,occupational hazard</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Allergic Reaction">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers and their families are at risk of developing allergic reactions to bee venom, not just from stings but also from inhaling bee-related particles brought into the home on clothing.</data>
      <data key="d8">health concern,occupational risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Apiary Inspection Program">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Apiary inspection programs regulate beekeepers, but budget cuts to these programs have forced beekeepers to take on more personal responsibility for controlling diseases and mites.</data>
      <data key="d8">government support,regulation,responsibility shift</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Tolerance">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers can develop a tolerance to stings through repeated occupational exposure, a state that can fade if they are not stung for a while.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological adaptation,immunity,occupational exposure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Municipalities">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Municipalities impose local codes, bans, and restrictions that directly regulate and limit the activities of beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d8">governance,legal restriction,local regulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Federal Laws">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A federal law banning the importation of live bees directly regulates a potential activity of beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d8">import ban,legal framework,national regulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Honey Bee Brood Diseases">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The responsibility to manage and control honey bee brood diseases is increasingly falling on individual beekeepers due to cuts in inspection programs.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeper responsibility,biosecurity,disease management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fc749ae5b6e2c5fae540d31c52a16691</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Beekeeping Regulations">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers have a direct responsibility to be aware of and faithfully follow the beekeeping regulations of the state in which they keep bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">individual responsibility,legal compliance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="The Public">
      <data key="d6">15.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are encouraged to take a leading role in educating the public about Africanized bees to prevent misinformation and panic.&lt;SEP&gt;The relationship between beekeepers and the public is critical, as negative public perception of bees can lead to regulations that ban beekeepers from operating in specified areas.</data>
      <data key="d8">community outreach,knowledge sharing,public education,public relations,regulatory risk</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Mentor">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A mentor is a source of knowledge and guidance for a beekeeper, helping them navigate the complexities of the craft, including laws and regulations.</data>
      <data key="d8">knowledge transfer,mentorship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Local Equipment Supplier">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Local equipment suppliers are a resource for beekeepers, providing not only necessary equipment but also information about local beekeeping laws.</data>
      <data key="d8">information source,supplier relationship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="State's Department Of Agriculture">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The State's Department of Agriculture is an official source of information that beekeepers can consult to learn about the laws and regulations in their state.</data>
      <data key="d8">government resource,regulatory guidance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Honeybees">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are the stewards of honeybees, responsible for their care, management, and protection from threats like mites and disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal husbandry,stewardship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="The Second Era of Beekeeping">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Second Era, beekeepers' knowledge expanded significantly as they learned key facts about bee biology, including the sexes of the different bees and their role in pollination.</data>
      <data key="d8">historical development,knowledge progression</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Honey Bee Colony">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers manage honey bee colonies, with their practices revolving around the colony's natural behaviors like swarming and honey storage.</data>
      <data key="d8">human interaction,management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Swarm Control">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers apply Swarm Control techniques to prevent the natural swarming impulse in their colonies, thereby avoiding the associated loss of population and honey production.&lt;SEP&gt;Swarm control is a crucial management activity performed by beekeepers to influence a colony's natural reproductive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d8">intervention,management practice,prevention,professional activity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Package Bees">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers use package bees as a common method to establish a new honey bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping method,colony establishment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Nucleus Hive">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers use a nucleus hive as a method to start a new colony, representing an alternative to using package bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping method,colony establishment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Hive Location">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Hive Location that is nearby and accessible is recommended for Beekeepers, especially beginners, to facilitate frequent monitoring and easier management of the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">accessibility,convenience,management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Comfort and Workability">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The beekeeper's personal Comfort and Workability are key factors in selecting a hive site, which needs to be level and have adequate space for maintenance tasks.</data>
      <data key="d8">ergonomics,site planning</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Equipment">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers need to have their Equipment readily available when working on a hive, which is a major reason to locate the hive at home rather than at a remote site.</data>
      <data key="d8">convenience,logistics,work efficiency</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Italian Bees">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers acquire and care for the Italian bees within the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">acquisition,animal husbandry,management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Plastic">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers must choose whether to use plastic components in their hive, with the text noting that many do despite the author's stated preference against it.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment choice,material preference,user decision</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Veil and Helmet">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers wear a veil and helmet as necessary personal protective equipment against bee stings.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment usage,personal safety,protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Gloves">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers may choose to wear gloves as optional protective equipment to prevent stings.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment usage,personal safety,protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Coveralls">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers can wear coveralls as optional full-body protective clothing against bee stings.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment usage,personal safety,protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Covers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text states that commercial beekeepers primarily use migratory covers, a specific type of cover that lacks overhangs, allowing hives to be packed more tightly for transport.</data>
      <data key="d8">commercial beekeeping,equipment preference</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Honey Supers">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The author advises beekeepers to select a single size of honey super (recommending the shallow size) and use it exclusively to avoid the frustration of mismatched frames and boxes.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment recommendation,hive management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Hive Stand">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A hive stand is described as essential equipment for beekeepers to protect the hive's bottom board from ground moisture and rot. The author suggests a specific homemade design as a superior alternative to commercial options.</data>
      <data key="d8">best practice,equipment use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Paint">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers select paint colors for practical reasons; white reflects sunlight to keep the hive cooler, while light green provides camouflage to deter theft or vandalism in outyards.</data>
      <data key="d8">color choice,hive management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Ventilation and Insulation">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text directly instructs beekeepers on how to implement ventilation and insulation systems for their hives, presenting it as a key responsibility of winter hive management.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping practice,hive management,winter preparation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Winter">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text is a guide for beekeepers on how to manage their colonies throughout the winter, covering essential tasks like preparation, monitoring, and understanding the colony's needs during this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal husbandry,beekeeping responsibilities,seasonal management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Colony">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are responsible for managing the colony's health and survival, particularly by preparing the hive for winter and monitoring it until spring.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal husbandry,colony management,stewardship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Autumn">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Autumn is the critical period when beekeepers must perform tasks like installing mouse guards and setting up insulation to prepare the hive for the upcoming winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping tasks,seasonal preparation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Mouse Guard">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The beekeeper is responsible for installing a mouse guard in autumn to protect the hive from being invaded by mice during the winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping task,hive protection,pest control</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Granulation">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers must contend with the natural granulation of their honey. The text provides them with specific techniques, such as using a double boiler to heat honey to 120°F (49°C), to reverse or delay this process.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping technique,problem-solving,process management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Supers">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers add supers to their colonies when they are ready to produce surplus honey. The text instructs them on how to arrange different types of frames within the super.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping equipment,hive management,honey production</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Beehive">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are the stewards of the beehive, responsible for its management and defense against predators and other threats.</data>
      <data key="d8">apiary management,defense,stewardship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Skunks">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers must take defensive measures to protect their hives from skunks, which are a common predator of bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">defense,human-wildlife conflict,pest management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Bears">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers in bear country face a major problem with bears, which are persistent and destructive predators of hives, often forcing beekeepers to resort to expensive defenses like electric fences.</data>
      <data key="d8">human-wildlife conflict,major threat,pest management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="The Africanized Bee">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are directly impacted by the spread of the Africanized bee, which poses a threat to their existing colonies and may be inadvertently spread by beekeepers moving colonies for pollination.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural practice,invasive species impact,risk management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Electric Fence">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are advised to erect an electric fence as a preventative measure to protect their hives from bears.</data>
      <data key="d8">apiary protection,defense implementation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee734f0540c7fd5dab112e837ae5a36b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Africanized Bees">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers are directly impacted by Africanized bees and are crucial for their management. They can inadvertently spread the bees but are also responsible for control measures like requeening and for leading the development of effective management plans.</data>
      <data key="d8">control measures,management,professional impact</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="Public Officials">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text urges beekeepers to proactively educate and work with public officials to ensure that sensible, effective plans for dealing with Africanized bees are developed, rather than having inappropriate regulations imposed on them.</data>
      <data key="d8">advocacy,collaboration,policy influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="State Governments">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text urges beekeepers to proactively help their state governments develop plans for dealing with Africanized bees, lest inappropriate plans be imposed on them.</data>
      <data key="d8">advocacy,collaboration,policy development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeepers" target="European Bees">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeepers traditionally manage European bees and are advised to maintain pure stock by requeening with known European-type queens to counteract the spread of Africanized genetics.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping practice,genetic purity,stock management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Hive Visits">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hive visits, while necessary for beekeeping, are described as a significant disruption to the normal functioning and life within the bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">disruption,intervention,management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Beginner Beekeeper">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text advises a beginner to start with two colonies to have a basis for comparison and a resource to strengthen a weaker hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">beginner advice,colony management,comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Package Bees">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Package bees are described as a method for establishing a new bee colony, which will grow in size over time.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping method,colony establishment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Worker Bee">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Worker bees perform all the labor necessary for the colony's survival and success, with their specific tasks dictated by the immediate needs of the colony as a whole.</data>
      <data key="d8">collective behavior,colony survival,labor division</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Queen Bee">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen is the reproductive heart of the colony; her continuous egg-laying is essential for sustaining the colony's population and ensuring its long-term existence.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony propagation,leadership,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Queen">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen is the central and essential member of the colony, as her egg-laying is required for the colony's growth and survival. The text states that if the queen is working, the colony is growing.&lt;SEP&gt;The queen is the reproductive head of the colony, but her status and physical condition are managed by the colony's workers, especially in preparation for swarming.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,colony hierarchy,hive dynamics,leadership,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee196747c63e46006af016f7f2104f8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Nutrition">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The overall nutrition of the colony, based on its stores of honey and pollen, is essential for its survival, dictating all its activities, including brood rearing and foraging.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,resource management,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Winter">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The colony's survival depends on its ability to adequately prepare for the coming winter by building up food reserves.&lt;SEP&gt;The long-term goal for managing a colony is to ensure it grows strong enough to successfully survive its first winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">long-term goal,resource management,seasonal challenge,seasonal preparation,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Hive Inspection">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hive inspections are the primary method for managing and monitoring the overall health, progress, and needs of the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">assessment,management,monitoring</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Brood">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The presence, quantity, and pattern of brood are the key indicators of the colony's overall health, growth, and future strength.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,growth indicator,viability</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Local Supplier">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A local supplier is a resource for the beekeeper to acquire a replacement queen for the colony if the original one is lost or fails.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony support,replacement,sourcing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Brood Nest">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The brood nest is the central hub of activity in the colony, and its continuous growth is a key sign of the colony's overall health and expansion.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,core area,growth indicator</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Feeder">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The feeder is used to supply a colony with food (syrup), which is critical for the growth and brood rearing of a new or struggling colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony growth,support,sustenance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Brood Rearing">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brood rearing is a vital activity for the colony, as it is the process that increases the adult population, ensuring the colony's strength for the coming spring.&lt;SEP&gt;Brood rearing is the fundamental process by which a colony sustains and grows its population. The text emphasizes that an adequate food supply is paramount for this activity.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony cycle,core activity,population growth,reproduction,sustenance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Food Stores">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The survival and growth of the colony during winter is directly dependent on having sufficient food stores to consume. A lack of stores will lead to starvation.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,resource dependency,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Bears">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bears are listed as a threat to beekeeping, as they are predators that can attack and destroy a honey bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">predation,threat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740512</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Ants">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ants are mentioned as a problem for beekeepers, as they can invade and act as pests within a honey bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">pest,threat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740512</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony" target="Colony Cycle">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The colony cycle describes the annual progression and changes that a honey bee colony undergoes throughout the seasons.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological process,life cycle</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740512</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="New Beekeeper" target="First Year">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The 'first year' is the defining period for a new beekeeper, characterized by high initial costs and the process of learning the hobby without the expectation of profit.</data>
      <data key="d8">cost,initiation,learning curve</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3025455da6b06dabe347fe4878bb963</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="New Beekeeper" target="Experienced Beekeepers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text advises the New Beekeeper to seek knowledge from Experienced Beekeepers to understand local conditions, particularly the timing of the nectar flow.</data>
      <data key="d8">knowledge transfer,local expertise,mentorship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="New Beekeeper" target="Supering">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supering is a primary task for the New Beekeeper, and the text provides specific, simplified instructions on how to approach it during their first year.</data>
