    <edge source="Beekeeping Regulations" target="State's Department Of Agriculture">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The State's Department of Agriculture is an official source of information for beekeepers regarding beekeeping regulations.</data>
      <data key="d8">government source,regulatory information</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeeping Regulations" target="Local Beekeeping Organization">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Local beekeeping organizations provide guidance to beekeepers to help them find and comply with state and local beekeeping regulations.</data>
      <data key="d8">compliance assistance,member support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beekeeping Regulations" target="Certificate Of Inspection">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Certificate of Inspection is a specific requirement within some state-level beekeeping regulations, acting as a tool to enforce rules on bee importation.</data>
      <data key="d8">disease control,regulatory enforcement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honeybees" target="The Public">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text states that the public generally does not understand the critical importance of honeybees to the food supply and environment, highlighting a knowledge gap.</data>
      <data key="d8">ecological importance,public awareness</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honeybees" target="Earthworms">
      <data key="d6">5.0</data>
      <data key="d7">These two entities represent alternative, non-traditional agricultural enterprises. They are related as they fall outside the typical livestock or poultry categories and focus on niche products (bait/castings and honey). They both show relatively low startup costs and scale.</data>
      <data key="d8">Alternative Agriculture,Niche Markets</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honeybees" target="Chickens, Purebred">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">This relationship highlights the extreme range of profitability among the listed enterprises. Purebred Chickens show the highest net profit ($7,440.00), while Honeybees show the lowest ($90.00), providing a clear comparison of the financial potential at the opposite ends of the spectrum in this document.</data>
      <data key="d8">Economic Performance,Financial Viability,Profitability Comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-59117004fc63073a0e772f7ea1ce4399</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honeybees" target="Roger A. Morse">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Roger A. Morse wrote a complete guide to beekeeping, making him an expert author within the 'Honeybees' category.</data>
      <data key="d8">author expertise,subject category</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f19adc067f85e17ae856f88441c8e7d8</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Era Of Beekeeping" target="Skep">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The skep was a key piece of equipment used during the First Era of Beekeeping and represents a technological step before modern hives.</data>
      <data key="d8">craft evolution,historical technology</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Era Of Beekeeping" target="Romans">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Romans are mentioned as a key group within the First Era of Beekeeping who contributed to the early, albeit limited, knowledge of bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">historical society,knowledge development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skep" target="Hollow Log Hives">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hollow log hives represent an earlier, more primitive technology in beekeeping history, which was later advanced by the development of hives like the skep.</data>
      <data key="d8">historical equipment,technological progression</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96f78f364d74eca8b91a5a10f5f9de4b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Public" target="Africanized Bees">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The public is directly affected by Africanized bees through stinging incidents and the need to coexist with them. Their potential panic, fueled by lack of information, is a major concern that can lead to poor policy decisions.</data>
      <data key="d8">coexistence,public perception,public safety</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Public" target="Public Officials">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Public panic about Africanized bees can pressure public officials into creating overly restrictive and inappropriate regulations on beekeeping.</data>
      <data key="d8">policy making,political pressure,public opinion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff2a1270133813dbf92927fcbf14a3c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mentor" target="Growing Gardens">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Growing Gardens' support structure for new gardeners includes providing them with a Mentor.</data>
      <data key="d8">mentorship program,support structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mentor" target="New Home Gardeners">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">New Home Gardeners are paired with a Mentor who provides them with guidance and support throughout the growing season.</data>
      <data key="d8">mentorship,support relationship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9b822de4a60aeb5f83933b9cd0a946c9</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Romans" target="The Queen">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Romans, representing early beekeepers, held the incorrect belief that the hive's monarch was a male king, demonstrating the limited understanding of the era.</data>
      <data key="d8">historical misconception,scientific error</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Second Era of Beekeeping" target="The Queen">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Second Era (specifically the 1500s), the queen was correctly identified as an egg-laying female, which was a major correction to the earlier belief that the hive's monarch was a male 'king'.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological correction,scientific discovery</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Second Era of Beekeeping" target="Wax">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The true origin of beeswax, as a substance produced by bees rather than collected from plants, was discovered during the 1700s in the Second Era.</data>
      <data key="d8">material origin,scientific discovery</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Third Era of Beekeeping" target="Lorenzo L. Langstroth">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lorenzo L. Langstroth's discovery of bee space in 1850 is the event that initiated and defined the Third Era of Beekeeping, marking a revolution in the field.</data>
      <data key="d8">era initiation,foundational figure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Third Era of Beekeeping" target="Movable Frames">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Third Era is characterized by the development and use of key equipment, such as movable frames, the honey extractor, and the queen excluder, all made possible by the discovery of bee space.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping methods,technological advancement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Third Era of Beekeeping" target="Honey Extractor">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The honey extractor is an important invention from the Third Era that, along with movable frames, allowed for larger honey crops because the comb did not have to be destroyed.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural efficiency,technological development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Third Era of Beekeeping" target="Queen Excluder">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen excluder was developed as part of the technological revolution in the Third Era of beekeeping to improve hive management and honey purity.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive management,technological development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lorenzo L. Langstroth" target="Bee Space">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lorenzo L. Langstroth is credited with discovering the concept of bee space, a breakthrough that became the cornerstone of modern beekeeping.</data>
      <data key="d8">conceptual breakthrough,scientific discovery</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lorenzo L. Langstroth" target="Langstroth Hive">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Langstroth Hive, a specific type of beehive, is associated with Lorenzo L. Langstroth, who is recognized for its development.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping history,invention,namesake</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Space" target="Langstroth Hive">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Langstroth hive is designed entirely around the principle of bee space, which ensures that the frames within it remain movable and not attached to the hive walls.</data>
      <data key="d8">design principle,functional basis</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Space" target="Movable Frames">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The discovery of bee space was the essential understanding required to create truly functional movable frames, as it explained why bees would leave certain gaps open, preventing frames from being glued in place.</data>
      <data key="d8">enabling concept,technological innovation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Space" target="Propolis">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bee space is defined as a gap that bees will not fill with propolis, a crucial observation that allows hive components to remain separate and manageable.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral principle,material application</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Space" target="Queen Cage">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The placement of the Queen Cage violates the natural Bee Space, which causes the bees to build Wild Comb in the resulting gap.</data>
      <data key="d8">cause and effect,problem creation,spatial violation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Space" target="Wild Comb">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Wild Comb is built by bees in areas where the proper Bee Space is not maintained, representing a direct consequence of violating this principle.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,cause and effect,hive construction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Space" target="Beekeeping, History Of">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The discovery of 'bee space' was a pivotal event in the history of beekeeping, enabling the invention of modern hives with removable frames during its 'second era'.</data>
      <data key="d8">historical development,innovation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740512</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Movable Frames" target="Honey Extractor">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The honey extractor was designed to work in conjunction with movable frames, enabling honey removal while preserving the wax comb for reuse by the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment dependency,technological synergy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Movable Frames" target="Beeswax Foundation">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beeswax foundation is a component used inside movable frames to provide a template for bees, ensuring they build straight, manageable comb.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment synergy,hive construction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Queen" target="Queen Excluder">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen excluder is a tool designed specifically to physically block the queen from moving into certain parts of the hive, thereby controlling where she can lay eggs.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral control,equipment function</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93fd599c3721904b0c0c72f8f1f0969f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Queen" target="Brood Pattern">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The brood pattern is the direct result of the queen's egg-laying activity and is the primary indicator used to assess her health and laying performance.</data>
      <data key="d8">performance indicator,queen health,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Queen" target="Eggs">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen's essential function within the colony is to lay eggs, which is the foundation of the colony's growth.</data>
      <data key="d8">causality,colony growth,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Queen" target="Colony Population">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen's egg-laying rate directly determines the size and age demographic of the Colony Population. Requeening is a strategy used to bolster a declining population before winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony management,population dynamics,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Queen" target="Brood">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen is the sole producer of eggs, which develop into the colony's Brood. Her health and laying patterns are fundamental to all brood rearing.</data>
      <data key="d8">life cycle,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Queen" target="Fall Requeening">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fall Requeening is the event during which beekeepers intentionally replace the old Queen with a new one to improve the colony's health and productivity.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony management,intervention,replacement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Queen" target="Nectar Flow">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The end of the Nectar Flow signals The Queen to reduce her egg-laying, which in turn affects the rate of population growth in the late season.</data>
      <data key="d8">environmental trigger,reproduction,seasonal cycle</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wax" target="Sugar">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The secretion of wax by honey bees is directly dependent on their consumption of sugar, not pollen.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological process,production,resource dependency</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-824704426592ed72e9ae44a04bd1ddbd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wax" target="Syrup">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Feeding the colony with syrup stimulates the bees to secrete wax and draw out new comb, which is crucial for a new colony's development.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony growth,production,stimulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollen" target="Foraging">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pollen is one of the primary food resources collected from flowers during foraging activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Pollen is one of the primary resources collected by honey bees during foraging to provide protein for the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">food source,nutrition,resource collection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollen" target="Ordinary Brood Food">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pollen is a key ingredient, mixed with honey and glandular secretions, to create ordinary brood food for worker larvae.</data>
      <data key="d8">ingredient,larval nutrition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollen" target="Bee Behavior">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The act of collecting and storing pollen is presented as a classic example of patterned bee behavior, where a bee will complete the entire sequence of actions even if it's futile (i.e., the pollen is lost). A bee does not realize its pollen baskets are empty.</data>
      <data key="d8">futile action,instinct,patterned behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollen" target="Insecticide">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Insecticide can be inadvertently brought back to the hive when it contaminates pollen collected by foraging bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">contamination,environmental hazard,resource poisoning</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollen" target="Field Bee">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Field bees collect pollen during their foraging trips, which serves as a food source for the colony and facilitates pollination.</data>
      <data key="d8">foraging,pollination,resource collection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollen" target="Nurse Bees">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Young, newly emerged bees, described as potential nurse bees, can be killed when they feed on stored pollen that has been contaminated with insecticide.</data>
      <data key="d8">contamination,food chain,mortality</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollen" target="Brood Rearing">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brood rearing is critically dependent on pollen; the process stops entirely if there are no pollen stores or incoming pollen.&lt;SEP&gt;Pollen is a critical resource consumed by the colony to support brood rearing, providing the necessary protein for new bees to develop.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony activity,development,life cycle,resource consumption,resource dependency</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollen" target="Nutrition">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pollen is one of the two principal components of a honey bee's diet, providing essential protein and fats for development and health.</data>
      <data key="d8">diet,food component,protein source</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pollen" target="Brood">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Pollen is an essential food for bees, and the text states that brood rearing will not happen if there is no pollen present in the hive, directly linking pollen availability to the colony's ability to raise young."&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Pollen is a critical food source for brood; the text states that without a supply of pollen, all brood rearing in the colony stops.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony reproduction,developmental dependency,diet,larval nutrition,life cycle</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59970fe46f1c3b11508247730ae6145d</data>
