    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Excluder" target="Supers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen excluder is an optional piece of equipment specifically used in conjunction with supers to prevent the queen bee from entering them to lay eggs, thereby keeping the honey pure.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment synergy,functional relationship,hive management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Excluder" target="Beginner Beekeeper">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text strongly advises that a beginner beekeeper does not need and should not purchase a queen excluder in their first year.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment advice,purchasing recommendation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Excluder" target="Supers with foundation">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text explains that bees are reluctant to draw foundation in supers that are placed above a queen excluder.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment interaction,hive development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Excluder" target="Nectar flow">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The negative impact of a queen excluder on hive development is exacerbated by a weak nectar flow.</data>
      <data key="d8">compounding factors,hive management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Excluder" target="Queen">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A queen excluder is a piece of beekeeping equipment specifically used to restrict the movement of the queen within the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment use,hive management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740511</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Excluder" target="Hive(s)">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A queen excluder is a piece of equipment used within a hive to control the queen's movement and prevent her from laying eggs in honey supers.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping equipment,brood control,hive management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Langstroth" target="Commercial Operator">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Langstroth's revolutionary discoveries in hive management directly enabled the development of true commercial beekeeping operations, allowing for honey production by the ton.</data>
      <data key="d8">industry creation,technological impact</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Langstroth" target="Movable, Renewable Frames">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Langstroth is credited with the discovery of movable, renewable frames, which was a revolutionary development in beekeeping.</data>
      <data key="d8">invention,technological advance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Industrial Revolution" target="Hobbyist Beekeeper">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The societal shifts during the Industrial Revolution hastened the evolution of modern beekeeper classifications, including the hobbyist or backyard beekeeper.</data>
      <data key="d8">classification evolution,societal change</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Industrial Revolution" target="Commercial Operator">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Industrial Revolution's impact on attitudes and lifestyles accelerated the development of beekeeper classifications, including the professional commercial operator.</data>
      <data key="d8">classification evolution,societal change</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Industrial Revolution" target="Green Revolution">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text describes the Green Revolution as the 'younger sister' of the Industrial Revolution, indicating a direct succession and shared impact on the food system.</data>
      <data key="d8">conceptual link,historical sequence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fac6f2d596c9d9fe2bc3b9afda4f10df</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hobbyist Beekeeper" target="Package of Bees">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text specifically recommends that a novice, who is most likely a hobbyist beekeeper, should start with a package of bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">beginner guidance,starting method</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hobbyist Beekeeper" target="Nucleus Hive">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A nucleus hive is presented as a recommended starting option for beginners, who are typically classified as hobbyist beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d8">beginner guidance,starting method</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hobbyist Beekeeper" target="Italian Bees">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text recommends that beginners, who are usually hobbyists, should start with Italian bees because they are gentle and easily handled.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee selection,beginner guidance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sideline Operator" target="Commercial Operator">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text explicitly states that sideline operators, who run beekeeping as a small business, sometimes evolve to become full-time commercial operators.</data>
      <data key="d8">business growth,career progression</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Commercial Operator" target="Beekeeping Industry">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Commercial operators are a critical part of the beekeeping industry, as they own the vast majority of hives and are responsible for large-scale honey production and crop pollination.</data>
      <data key="d8">economic backbone,industry segment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Commercial Operator" target="Honey Production">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey production is listed as one of the primary ways a commercial operator makes their entire income from bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">commercial activity,income source</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Commercial Operator" target="Queen or Package Production">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The production of queens and packages is a specialized business that commercial operators engage in for their entire income.</data>
      <data key="d8">commercial activity,specialized business</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1b5f53667458d570c2b31bf11f9a153b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Italian Bees" target="Smoker">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The smoker is a necessary tool used to calm the Italian bees, making it safer for the beekeeper to work on the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal handling,beekeeping practice,safety</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Italian Bees" target="Veil and Helmet">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The veil and helmet are necessary protective equipment worn by a beekeeper to prevent stings from the bees, particularly to the face and head.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping practice,personal protection,safety equipment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Italian Bees" target="Queen">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen is the central figure of the Italian bees colony, and a package of bees is typically purchased with its own queen.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony structure,hierarchy,leadership</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
