    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Drone Bee">
      <data key="d6">39.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Drone Bee and the Queen Bee are the two reproductive castes within a honeybee hive, and their relationship is centered on reproduction. The drone bee's sole and critical purpose is to mate with a queen bee, an act essential for the colony's survival and ability to reproduce. This primary interaction occurs during the queen's mating flights, where she will mate with a dozen or so drones. This process provides the queen with a lifetime supply of sperm, and upon successfully mating, the drone bee dies.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony roles,life purpose,mating,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Worker Bee">
      <data key="d6">36.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen Bee and Worker Bees share a deeply interconnected and supportive relationship within the colony. The Queen Bee is the mother of all Worker Bees, and her primary role is to lay the fertilized eggs that develop into these workers. The development of a bee into a queen versus a worker is determined by diet, a process controlled by the Worker Bees. Worker bees feed queen larvae an exclusive diet of royal jelly, while worker larvae receive it for only the first three days. In turn, the Worker Bees perform all the tasks necessary to support the Queen Bee and the colony. Their duties include a direct service relationship, with young worker bees "attending the queen" and specialized "nurse" bees feeding the queen larva and all other brood. The Queen Bee's presence is also crucial for maintaining the morale of the Worker Bees, who constitute the vast majority of the colony's population.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste differentiation,caste system,colony labor,colony structure,dependence,hierarchy,hive hierarchy,nurturing,reproduction,social structure,support,task allocation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Royal Jelly">
      <data key="d6">29.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A bee larva must be fed exclusively on royal jelly for the duration of its larval stage to develop into a queen.&lt;SEP&gt;An exclusive diet of royal jelly during the larval stage is the specific trigger that causes a bee to develop into a queen, making it the critical factor in her development.&lt;SEP&gt;The diet of a queen larva consists exclusively of royal jelly for its entire larval stage. This specific diet is the determining factor that causes a fertilized egg to develop into a queen rather than a worker bee.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste determination,development,diet</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Fertile Eggs">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fertile eggs can develop into new queens, ensuring the continuation of the colony's reproductive leadership.&lt;SEP&gt;The queen is the only member of the colony capable of laying the fertile eggs that produce all female bees (workers and other queens).</data>
      <data key="d8">colony foundation,creation,development,life cycle,reproduction,succession</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Honey Bee Colony">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen Bee is the leader and mother of the entire colony. She is the sole egg-layer and her presence is essential for maintaining the colony's cohesion and morale.</data>
      <data key="d8">leadership,reproduction,social structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Parthenogenesis">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen Bee initiates parthenogenesis by choosing to lay an unfertilized egg. This control over fertilization allows her to determine the sex of her offspring and produce drones for the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste system,genetic selection,reproductive control</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Egg">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen Bee is solely responsible for laying all the eggs in the colony, determining if each egg is fertilized or not based on the cell size.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste system,colony origin,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Mating Flights">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen must undertake mating flights to become fertile, which is a critical step before she can begin her primary function of laying eggs for the colony.&lt;SEP&gt;The queen undertakes a series of mating flights early in her adult life to gather a lifetime supply of sperm.</data>
      <data key="d8">life event,maturation,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Spermatheca">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen uses her spermatheca to store sperm received during her mating flights, which she then uses to fertilize eggs for the rest of her life.</data>
      <data key="d8">anatomy,fertilization,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Orientation Flights">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The queen takes orientation flights to learn the hive's surroundings as a necessary preparation for her vital mating flights.</data>
      <data key="d8">learning,navigation,preparation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Brood Nest">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">After emerging, the queen wanders about the brood nest to mature before her mating flights.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavior,habitat,maturation location</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Egg Laying">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Egg laying is described as the routine and primary function of the queen bee after she has successfully mated.</data>
      <data key="d8">primary function,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Brood Rearing">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen Bee initiates Brood Rearing by laying eggs, a process that continues even in late winter.&lt;SEP&gt;The queen bee initiates and drives the process of brood rearing by laying eggs; the rate of her laying directly determines the rate of population growth.</data>
      <data key="d8">causation,colony reproduction,leadership role,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Pheromones">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen Bee produces and releases a specific pheromone that signals her presence throughout the hive, which is critical for maintaining the Bee Colony's cohesion and social order.</data>
      <data key="d8">chemical signal,colony cohesion,leadership</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Bee Colony">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen Bee is central to the Bee Colony's social structure, signaling her presence and maintaining order through the pheromones she releases.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony cohesion,leadership,social structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="New Colony">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The survival and growth of the New Colony are entirely dependent on the successful introduction and egg-laying of the Queen Bee.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony leadership,reproduction,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="The First Inspection">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The specific and primary purpose of The First Inspection is to confirm that the Queen Bee has been successfully released from her cage into the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">primary objective,queen status,verification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Package Installation">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The stress of Package Installation on the bees requires a delayed release method for the Queen Bee to prevent them from potentially killing her.</data>
      <data key="d8">installation procedure,queen safety,stress management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Brood">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Queen Bee is the source of all Brood, as her primary function is to lay the eggs that will develop into new bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony growth,origin,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Nucleus Colony (Nuc)">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Nucleus Colony is contrasted with a package because its Queen Bee is typically already free and established, unlike the caged queen in a package.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony type,comparison,queen status</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Winter">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Winter, the Queen Bee temporarily ceases egg-laying, which is a significant change in the colony's routine.</data>
      <data key="d8">reproductive cycle,seasonal behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Tracheal Mites">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A productive Queen Bee can help the colony's population grow faster than the mite population, serving as a natural method of controlling the severity of the infestation.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony resilience,natural control,population dynamics</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Bee" target="Spring Requeening">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Spring requeening is the beekeeping practice of replacing an old or failing queen bee with a new one to invigorate the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping practice,colony management,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Drone Bee">
      <data key="d6">24.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Drones are entirely dependent on worker bees for food as they are not equipped to work or forage. At the end of the season, workers will evict unsuccessful drones from the colony.&lt;SEP&gt;Worker bees care for drone brood but expel adult drones from the hive at the end of the active season to conserve resources, demonstrating a conflict between raising them and ensuring colony efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Workers and drones are two of the three essential castes within a honey bee colony, each with distinct sexes (female and male, respectively) and non-overlapping roles.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste system,colony maintenance,colony structure,dependency,life cycle conflict,resource management,roles,social interaction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Fertile Eggs">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fertile eggs laid by the queen develop into worker bees, which constitute the 'life force of the colony'.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste system,development,life cycle</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-218f65ec9b659cddff295e2ed6826cdc</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Honey Bee Colony">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The worker bee is described as the 'primary life force of the colony,' responsible for carrying out most of the tasks that sustain it, such as brood rearing and foraging.&lt;SEP&gt;Worker bees are the labor force of the colony, performing all necessary tasks such as foraging, nursing the young, and hive maintenance. The colony's survival and productivity depend entirely on the work of the worker bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony function,colony maintenance,labor,life force,social hierarchy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Royal Jelly">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The limited diet of royal jelly for only 3 days is what ensures a female larva develops into a worker bee instead of a queen.&lt;SEP&gt;Worker bees, in their 'nurse bee' phase, are the producers of royal jelly, which they secrete from glands in their heads to feed the developing larvae in the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste determination,caste development,development,diet,nurturing,production</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Brood Food">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Worker bees in their 'nurse bee' phase are responsible for producing and feeding brood food to developing worker and drone larvae.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony labor,diet,nurturing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="House Bee">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A worker bee's life begins in the House Bee stage, where it performs duties within the hive, such as being a nurse bee.&lt;SEP&gt;The 'House Bee' is the first role and life stage of an adult worker bee, where it performs tasks inside the hive for 2-3 weeks.</data>
      <data key="d8">development,division of labor,labor division,life cycle,life stage,maturation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Egg">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Worker bees, as nurse bees, clean the cells in preparation for the queen to lay eggs, and later feed the larvae that hatch from them.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony labor,hive maintenance,nurturing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Brood">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Worker bees are entirely responsible for the care of the brood, which includes cleaning their cells and feeding the larvae with the appropriate food to ensure their development.</data>
      <data key="d8">caretaking,larval development,nurturing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Field Bee">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The 'Field Bee' is the second and final life stage of an adult worker bee, where it spends about 2 weeks foraging outside the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">foraging,labor division,life stage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Ordinary Brood Food">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Worker bee larvae are switched to a diet of ordinary brood food after their first three days on royal jelly.</data>
      <data key="d8">caste differentiation,development,larval diet</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Worker Bee" target="Emergence">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Emergence from the pupal cell marks the beginning of a worker bee's adult life.</data>
      <data key="d8">life cycle transition,maturation</data>
