    </edge>
    <edge source="Drone Bee" target="Parthenogenesis">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Drones are the direct result of the process of parthenogenesis, as they develop from unfertilized eggs laid by the queen.</data>
      <data key="d8">asexual reproduction,development,genetic origin</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Drone Bee" target="Mating Flights">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Participation in mating flights is the primary activity and purpose of an adult drone's life; it is his only opportunity to reproduce.&lt;SEP&gt;The drone's only reason to leave the hive is to participate in mating flights, where he attempts to mate with a queen in mid-air.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavior,life purpose,mating,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Drone Bee" target="Active Season">
      <data key="d6">17.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Drones are only born and live during the colony's active season, as their function (mating) is only relevant during this period of growth and expansion.&lt;SEP&gt;Drones are only tolerated in the hive during the active season; they are expelled to die at the end of it, tying their survival to this specific time period.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony roles,life cycle,life cycle dependency,seasonal impact,seasonality,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Drone Bee" target="Mating">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text states that the drone's critical role within the 'total scheme of things' is to mate with the queen.</data>
      <data key="d8">primary function,reproduction</data>
