    </edge>
    <edge source="Winter" target="Hive Location">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The suitability of a Hive Location must be assessed based on its conditions during Winter, including wind exposure and potential snow depth.</data>
      <data key="d8">seasonal planning,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-048ad5fead1e52a66cfd2e40b5467d7b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Winter" target="Bee Colony">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Bee Colony must endure the Winter by changing its behavior, such as clustering for warmth and ceasing foraging, making it a critical period for colony survival.&lt;SEP&gt;The bee colony's primary goal during its first season is to build up its population, comb, and food stores in order to survive the upcoming winter. The likelihood of survival is directly tied to its progress before winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">environmental challenge,preparation,seasonal adaptation,seasonal challenge,survival,survival behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Winter" target="Fall Management">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The entire purpose of Fall Management is to prepare the bee colony to survive the harsh conditions and confinement of the upcoming Winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">cause and effect,seasonal preparation,survival strategy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Winter" target="Ventilation and Insulation">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ventilation and insulation are essential management techniques that beekeepers employ to help a bee colony survive the winter. These practices address the challenges of moisture, carbon dioxide buildup, and cold temperatures inside the hive during this season.</data>
      <data key="d8">environmental control,hive survival,seasonal management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Winter" target="Wrapping Hives">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Wrapping hives is described as a specific, albeit less common, method of insulation used to protect bee colonies during extreme winters.</data>
      <data key="d8">insulation technique,seasonal preparation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Winter" target="Spring">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The conditions and colony activities during winter, such as food consumption and brood rearing, directly determine the health and population size of the colony as it emerges into spring.</data>
      <data key="d8">cause and effect,colony development,seasonal transition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Winter" target="Livestock">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text details how livestock care must change during the winter, as they require more feed to compensate for declining pasture grasses and need protection from wind and access to unfrozen water.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal husbandry,environmental adaptation,seasonal care</data>
