    <edge source="Water" target="Resource Evaluation">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The index lists 'water' as a specific category under the main heading 'Resource evaluation', indicating that assessing water availability and quality is a key part of this process.</data>
      <data key="d8">planning,resource assessment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Water" target="Sheep">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The index has a specific sub-entry 'water requirements for' under 'Sheep', indicating a direct and specific need for water for this type of livestock.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal care,resource requirement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarm" target="Diseases">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A significant disadvantage of starting with a swarm is that its origin is likely unknown, meaning it may carry diseases or mites, which are unnecessary problems for a novice.</data>
      <data key="d8">disease risk,unknown origin</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Swarm" target="Mites">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A major risk of using a swarm to start a colony is that it may carry mites due to its unknown origin.</data>
      <data key="d8">pest infestation,unknown origin</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Deciduous Forest Area">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bee colony that was the subject of the research study was located in a deciduous forest area, where it foraged over a 36-square mile territory.</data>
      <data key="d8">foraging range,habitat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="The First Summer">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The first summer is the critical establishment period for the new bee colony, where its population must grow and it must prepare for the future. The beekeeper must conduct weekly visits during this time to manage it.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony management,seasonal development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Hive Body">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive body is the physical home for the bee colony. The colony's expansion and comb-building activities are managed by adding a second hive body as the population grows.</data>
      <data key="d8">expansion,habitat,hive management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Boardman Feeder">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Boardman feeder is a tool used to provide supplementary food (syrup) to the bee colony to support its growth, though it comes with risks.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony support,equipment use,feeding</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Robber Bees">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Robber bees from other colonies pose a significant threat to a new, relatively weak bee colony by attempting to steal its resources, which can be incited by a Boardman feeder.</data>
      <data key="d8">defense,inter-colony conflict,threat</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="First Season Population Growth">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The first season population growth is the primary indicator of the bee colony's health and its likelihood of surviving the first winter. The beekeeper must monitor this growth curve closely.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,population dynamics,viability indicator</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="You (The Beekeeper)">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The beekeeper is responsible for all aspects of managing the bee colony, including feeding, housing, and monitoring its health to ensure it survives the first year.</data>
      <data key="d8">husbandry,management,stewardship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Comb">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bee colony must build comb on all available frames to create space for raising brood and storing food for the winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">construction,resource management,survival preparation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Syrup">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bee colony consumes syrup provided by the beekeeper as a supplementary food source to support its growth and establishment.</data>
      <data key="d8">consumption,feeding,sustenance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Foragers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Foragers are a vital part of the bee colony's workforce, responsible for gathering all external food resources.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony component,sustenance,workforce</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Hive Weight">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The beekeeper monitors the relative hive weight over time as an indicator of the bee colony's progress in collecting and storing resources like nectar.</data>
      <data key="d8">health indicator,progress monitoring,resource storage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Fall Management">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fall Management comprises the set of interventions and preparations a beekeeper performs on the Bee Colony to ensure its health and resources are adequate for winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping task,colony care,hive intervention</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Winter Cluster">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Bee Colony forms a Winter Cluster as a collective survival mechanism against the cold.</data>
      <data key="d8">collective action,survival behavior,thermoregulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Foundation">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Bee Colony's response to Foundation is conditional; it will draw it out during a nectar flow but may ignore or chew it up if no nectar is coming in.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral response,resource dependency</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Weather Patterns">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bee colony's activity, food consumption, and overall chances of survival are directly dictated by weather patterns. Extreme or unseasonable weather poses a significant threat to the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral influence,environmental dependency,survival factor</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Food Stores">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bee colony's survival through winter depends entirely on consuming its food stores to generate heat and energy. The colony systematically moves upwards through the hive as it consumes the stores.</data>
      <data key="d8">resource consumption,survival,sustenance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Cleansing Flights">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bee colony performs cleansing flights on warm days as a natural and necessary behavior for hive hygiene and health, which the beekeeper observes as a positive sign.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,hygiene,natural behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Dead Bees">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During cleansing flights, the bee colony actively works to remove the bodies of dead bees from the bottom of the hive, performing a crucial hygienic task.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive hygiene,maintenance,natural behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Colony" target="Wax Moths">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The health of the bee colony directly determines its vulnerability to wax moths; a weak colony is susceptible to infestation, while a strong one can defend itself.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,vulnerability</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Scout Bee" target="The Dance Language">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Scout Bees utilize The Dance Language to communicate the precise location, quality, and existence of a newly discovered resource source to other potential foragers in the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">communication,information transfer,recruitment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Scout Bee" target="Potential Homesites">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Scout Bees are responsible for finding and evaluating Potential Homesites for the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">exploration,site selection,swarm behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Dance Language" target="Round Dance">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Round Dance is a specific form of The Dance Language used by bees to communicate about nearby resources.</data>
      <data key="d8">communication method,proximity,signal type</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Dance Language" target="Wag-Tail Dance">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Wag-Tail Dance is a specific, complex form of The Dance Language used by bees to communicate detailed information about distant resources.</data>
      <data key="d8">communication method,navigation,signal type</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Dance Language" target="Potential Homesites">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Scout bees use The Dance Language to communicate the location and viability of Potential Homesites to a swarm.</data>
      <data key="d8">information transfer,navigation,swarm behavior</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Dance Language" target="Forager Bee">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Forager bees perform dances, which are part of the Dance Language, upon returning to the hive to communicate about the resources they have found.</data>
      <data key="d8">communication,information sharing,resource reporting</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Round Dance" target="Sickle or Crescent Dance">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The sickle or crescent dance is identified as a transitional dance, acting as an intermediate form between the round dance and the wag-tail dance.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral transition,communication spectrum</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wag-tail Dance" target="Sickle or Crescent Dance">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The sickle or crescent dance is identified as a transitional dance, acting as an intermediate form between the round dance and the wag-tail dance.</data>
      <data key="d8">behavioral transition,communication spectrum</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wag-tail Dance" target="Sun">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The orientation of the wag-tail dance is directly related to the position of the sun. The dancer translates the angle between the hive, the food, and the sun into the angle of the dance's straight run.</data>
      <data key="d8">celestial navigation,directional communication</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Alarm Dance" target="Hazards">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The alarm dance is a direct response to hazards like contaminated food, serving as a warning that halts all flight activity to protect the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">danger response,risk management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Alarm Dance" target="House and Field Bees">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text specifies that both house and field bees participate in performing the alarm dance, indicating a widespread, integrated response to danger within the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">collective behavior,emergency response</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ca6767b0793c7530226a6b224d0f21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Alarm Dance" target="Food">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The alarm dance is specifically triggered by the arrival of contaminated or poisonous food, serving as a warning system to prevent the colony from consuming harmful substances.</data>
      <data key="d8">danger signal,food safety</data>
