    </edge>
    <edge source="Livestock" target="Free Energy Sources">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Livestock are identified as a free energy source on the farm, capable of plowing pastures and producing manure to enrich the soil.</data>
      <data key="d8">farm management,resource integration,sustainable agriculture</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740861</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Livestock" target="Crops">
      <data key="d6">42.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Crops and Livestock are two major farm enterprises that are often integrated into a symbiotic and synergistic relationship. In this system, crops, particularly forage, pasture grasses, crop stubble, and cover crops, serve as the primary food source for livestock. The nutritional needs of animals, such as ewes, are met by grazing on specific types of crops; for example, legumes like alfalfa provide high protein, while a large quantity of grass can compensate for its lower nutritional quality. Good pasture can also reduce an animal's need for drinking water. In return, livestock provide fertility for vegetable and other crops. This reciprocal relationship is illustrated by the example of hogs eating corn, subsequently turning the pasture, and providing fertilizer for future corn crops.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal nutrition,cost reduction,enterprise integration,farm ecosystem,farm enterprise,food source,forage management,integrated farming,interdependence,resource management,soil fertility,symbiotic relationship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-454a001b38f4bf2cba51915cb1f4804e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-033bfeab9bfaed05ef236ddef40c557b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfed52d46f5bf9d5489c4d6b6dddd2cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04b17869172fff33b1c7bc2284173657</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740861</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Livestock" target="Equipment">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text states that one must consider the equipment needed for various livestock enterprises.</data>
      <data key="d8">farming tools,operational requirement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740861</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Livestock" target="Energy From Livestock">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Livestock are the source of 'Energy from Livestock', providing plowing action and manure for soil enrichment.</data>
      <data key="d8">animal labor,farm integration,source</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740861</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Livestock" target="Soil Enhancers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Manure from livestock is described as a key soil enhancer that can be used in raw or composted form to enrich the soil.</data>
      <data key="d8">farm integration,nutrient cycling,soil health</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740861</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Livestock" target="University Extension Office">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The University Extension Office provides expert guidance to farmers on what types of livestock are suitable for their region.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural guidance,expert advice</data>
