      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Bodies" target="Frames">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Frames are placed inside the Hive Bodies to provide the structure for the bees' comb.</data>
      <data key="d8">component relationship,internal structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Bodies" target="Supers">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supers are boxes placed on top of hive bodies, typically for honey storage. The text discusses using interchangeable mid-size boxes for both, simplifying equipment management.&lt;SEP&gt;Supers are placed on top of the Hive Bodies. A key management consideration is ensuring bees fully utilize the Hive Bodies before moving up into the Supers.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony expansion,hive configuration,hive structure,interchangeability,modularity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Bodies" target="Hive Stand">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive stand provides a stable, elevated base for the entire hive structure, which consists of stacked hive bodies. A wider stand is recommended to support the hive as it grows taller and less stable.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive stability,structural support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Bodies" target="Covers">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Telescoping outer covers are designed to fit over and extend beyond the edges of the hive body, creating a weathertight seal. The inner cover rests between the outer cover and the top hive body.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive construction,weather protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Bodies" target="Hive Construction Materials">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text specifies that hive bodies are typically made from pine or cypress, which are satisfactory materials. It also explains why hardwoods are not used for these components (due to weight and cost).</data>
      <data key="d8">material selection,product manufacturing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Bodies" target="Spring Chores">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Reversing the hive bodies is mentioned as one of the four essential spring chores for a beekeeper.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive manipulation,seasonal task</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Foundation">
      <data key="d6">55.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Foundation is a material that is placed, fitted, or installed within Frames to serve as a base and a guide for bees to build their comb. The purpose of the Foundation is to encourage the bees to build straight and uniform honeycomb. Frames are specifically built to hold the Foundation, which can be fastened in place using methods like pinning or wiring. For a successful installation, it is crucial that the type of Foundation (e.g., hook-wired) is compatible with the style of the Frame (e.g., wedge-style top) to prevent the Foundation from buckling.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping setup,compatibility,component dependency,component housing,component integration,component relationship,construction guide,equipment setup,frame component,guidance,hive construction,internal structure,structural component,structural support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Hive Tool">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive tool is used to manipulate and pry apart frames during hive inspections. The text explicitly mentions that a hive tool can easily break plastic frames, showing a direct physical interaction and a consideration for material choice.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping practice,material weakness,tool usage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Support Pins">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Support pins are used to fasten foundation securely into frames, preventing it from bending before the bees build comb on it.</data>
      <data key="d8">component assembly,fastening,stability</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Frame Wire">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Frame wire is the traditional method for reinforcing frames and securing the foundation within them.</data>
      <data key="d8">component assembly,reinforcement,traditional method</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Wood">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Wood is the traditional and author-preferred material for constructing frames.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment choice,material composition,preference</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
