      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Brood Area">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Brood Area is the specific location within the hive that is established across several central Frames.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive geography,location,structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Hive Body">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Frames are the components housed within the hive body, providing the structure for all colony activities like brood rearing and food storage.</data>
      <data key="d8">containment,hive architecture,structural component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Feeding Syrup">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text describes a specific method of feeding where the beekeeper removes empty frames, fills them with sugar syrup, and places them back into the hive near the brood area.</data>
      <data key="d8">application method,feeding technique,hive management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="The Cluster">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The cluster's ability to access food is dependent on the proximity of the honey-filled frames. A frame of honey that is not adjacent to the cluster is useless to the bees in cold weather.</data>
      <data key="d8">proximity,resource access,survival factor</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="The Extracting Process">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The extracting process is centered around handling frames: loading them into an extractor, spinning them, reversing them (in a tangential extractor), and balancing them by weight.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment use,process flow</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Extractor">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Extractors are sized and designed specifically to accommodate a certain number of frames at one time for spinning. The load of frames must be balanced to prevent the extractor from vibrating and moving.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment capacity,operational constraint</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Tangential Extractor">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A tangential extractor positions frames at an angle and only spins honey from one side at a time, requiring the frames to be manually removed, reversed, and replaced during the process.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment use,operational method</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Radial Extractor">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A radial extractor positions frames along a radius, which allows it to spin honey from both sides of the frames at once, eliminating the need to reverse them.</data>
      <data key="d8">efficiency,operational method</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frames" target="Hive(s)">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Frames are an integral, removable component inside a modern beehive, on which the bees build their comb.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping equipment,hive components</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foundation" target="Beeswax">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beeswax is the implied and traditional material for foundation, which bees readily work with and can rebuild if damaged.</data>
      <data key="d8">material composition,natural material,preference</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foundation" target="Plastic">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Plastic is used as a base for some types of foundation, which the author notes is not as readily rebuilt by bees if the wax coating is stripped.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,material composition,material weakness</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foundation" target="Pins">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pins are used to fasten the edges of the foundation into the end bars of the frame, ensuring it stays straight. The text notes this is a simpler and adequate alternative to wiring for most beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment assembly,fastening method</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foundation" target="Wiring">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Wiring is a method used to attach and reinforce the foundation within a frame, adding strength to the resulting comb. It is presented as an alternative to using pins.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment assembly,structural reinforcement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2a7084302d57591ff02e89969b4c3abd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foundation" target="Frame">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Foundation is placed within the frames to provide a guide for the bees to build straight and uniform comb.</data>
      <data key="d8">construction guide,structural component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foundation" target="Drawn Comb">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Foundation is the base upon which bees build Drawn Comb. The process of converting foundation to drawn comb is a key activity in a new hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive development,material transformation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foundation" target="Supers">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supers for a new hive are often filled with Foundation, which the bees must draw out to create storage space for honey.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive components,honey production</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foundation" target="Crushing and Straining">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The crushing and straining method destroys the wax comb, which means the bees will need to be given new foundation to rebuild on for the next season.</data>
      <data key="d8">process consequence,resource destruction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inner Cover" target="Feeder">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The feeder is designed to be inverted and placed over the hole in the inner cover, creating a functional interface for feeding the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment placement,functional interface</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inner Cover" target="Insulation Board">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text describes a specific setup where the insulation board is placed directly on top of the reversed inner cover to improve warmth and reduce condensation.</data>
      <data key="d8">component assembly,hive configuration,moisture control</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inner Cover" target="The Cluster">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The position of the cluster relative to the inner cover is a critical diagnostic sign for the beekeeper; a cluster visible at the inner cover signifies that food stores are dangerously low.</data>
      <data key="d8">diagnostic indicator,resource depletion,visual assessment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inner Cover" target="Porter Bee Escape">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Porter Bee Escape is a device that is specifically designed to be inserted into the hole of an Inner Cover to function.</data>
      <data key="d8">functional requirement,installation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Supers" target="Feeder Jar">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text suggests that empty supers can be used as an alternative to a hive body for the purpose of holding a feeder jar.</data>
      <data key="d8">alternative use,equipment setup,feeding arrangement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Supers" target="Removing The Crop">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The process of 'Removing the Crop' is fundamentally about taking the honey-filled supers away from the hive for extraction.</data>
      <data key="d8">harvesting process,hive component,primary objective</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Supers" target="Honey Harvesting">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The primary goal of honey harvesting is to remove the honey-filled supers from the beehive for processing.</data>
      <data key="d8">harvesting process,hive components</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Supers" target="Burr and Bridge Comb">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Burr and bridge comb is wax structure built by bees within the supers, which can make clearing bees more difficult.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive structure,process obstruction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Tool" target="Plastic">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text warns that a hive tool can easily break plastic frames, indicating a negative interaction between the tool and the material.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment handling,material weakness,tool usage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-91fddbed5da654e2156cc50a2222b4ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Tool" target="Frame Lifter">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The frame lifter is explicitly identified as 'a second style of hive tool' and is highly recommended for its specific function of removing frames without damage.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment specialization,tool type</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Tool" target="Installing Bees">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A hive tool is a necessary tool used during the installation of a package of bees, specifically for prying open the package.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping process,tool usage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Tool" target="Frame">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hive tool is a critical piece of equipment used to separate frames from each other during an inspection, as they are often stuck together by the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment use,inspection,manipulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Smoker" target="Veil and Helmet">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text establishes a functional relationship between the two essential tools, stating that a beekeeper who does not use a smoker to calm bees must rely heavily on protective gear like a veil.</data>
      <data key="d8">functional relationship,safety equipment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Smoker" target="Installing Bees">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A smoker is a tool that should be prepared and available during the installation of bees, in case the bees become upset.</data>
      <data key="d8">safety measure,tool usage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Smoker" target="Hive(s)">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The smoker is a tool used by beekeepers to calm the bees within the hive during inspections.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee handling,beekeeping equipment,colony management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740514</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feeder" target="Boardman Feeder">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Boardman feeder is presented as a specific, but inadequate, style of feeder. The text contrasts it with more effective feeding methods.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment comparison,functional critique</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feeder" target="Gallon Glass Jars">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Gallon glass jars are presented as a highly effective and recommended implementation of a feeder, used for feeding bees in large quantities.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment example,recommended practice</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feeder" target="Plastic Pails">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Plastic pails designed for feeding are mentioned as a commercially available type of feeder, an alternative to repurposed jars.</data>
      <data key="d8">commercial product,equipment alternative</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feeder" target="Installing Bees">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A feeder filled with sugar syrup must be prepared and ready as part of the process of installing a new bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">feeding,procedural requirement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feeder" target="Syrup">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Syrup is the food substance contained within and dispensed by the feeder.&lt;SEP&gt;The feeder is the specific piece of equipment used to hold and provide syrup to the bee colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">content,equipment function,food delivery,nourishment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feeder" target="First Inspection">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A procedural step of the First Inspection is to check the Feeder, refill it if necessary, and replace it.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony support,hive maintenance,procedure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Veil and Helmet" target="Alexander Veil">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Alexander veil is described as an 'alternate style' of veil that is used without a helmet, making it a direct alternative to the standard veil and helmet combination.</data>
      <data key="d8">design variation,equipment alternative</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Veil and Helmet" target="Gloves">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both the veil/helmet and gloves are identified as components of a beekeeper's protective gear. The veil is described as essential, while gloves are recommended as a good idea.</data>
      <data key="d8">protective gear,safety equipment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Stand" target="Commercially-available Stands">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The homemade hive stand is presented as a superior alternative to commercially-available stands, which are described as narrower and less stable.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment recommendation,product comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Stand" target="Treated Lumber">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treated lumber is the recommended material for constructing a homemade hive stand due to its durability and resistance to decay.</data>
      <data key="d8">construction,material specification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f11f928b6b6595520556e61b690ad234</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Coveralls" target="Bee Suits">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text defines a bee suit as being identical to coveralls with the addition of a zippered, attached veil.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment definition,product comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beeswax" target="Hive Products">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beeswax is explicitly listed and described as one of the primary 'other hive products' that a beekeeper can collect.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,product classification,source</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beeswax" target="Apitherapy">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beeswax is a product of the hive and is therefore one of the substances used in Apitherapy, the health treatment field involving all hive products.</data>
      <data key="d8">application,component,therapeutic use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beeswax" target="Cappings">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cappings from the honey extraction process are a key source material for producing high-quality, light-colored beeswax.</data>
      <data key="d8">quality,raw product,source material</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beeswax" target="Burr And Bridge Comb">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Scrapings from burr and bridge comb are identified as a source of high-quality beeswax for the beekeeper.</data>
      <data key="d8">by-product,collection,source material</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beeswax" target="Brood Comb">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Melted-down brood comb is a source of dark, impure beeswax, which is considered lower in quality and should be kept separate from lighter waxes.</data>
      <data key="d8">impurity,quality distinction,source material</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beeswax" target="Wired Extracting Comb">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bees strengthen weaker comb by adding beeswax to it over time, with the goal of it becoming as durable as a wired extracting comb.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee activity,material reinforcement,structural integrity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beeswax" target="Extractors/Extracting Method">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
