    </edge>
    <edge source="Drawn Comb" target="Wax Production">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The wax produced by bees during Wax Production is used to build Drawn Comb on the foundation.</data>
      <data key="d8">construction process,hive building</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96d3190b056360cfac9d6ae0c079078e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Package Installation" target="Delayed Queen Release">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Delayed Queen Release method is a direct response to the traumatic and stressful nature of Package Installation for the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">procedural response,stress mitigation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Package Installation" target="First Inspection">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The First Inspection is a procedural follow-up that is scheduled to occur on the third day after the Package Installation.</data>
      <data key="d8">follow-up,procedure,timing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Inspection" target="Wild Comb">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the First Inspection, a key task is to check for and remove any Wild Comb that bees have built in the excess space where the queen cage was located.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive maintenance,problem removal,procedure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wild Comb" target="Bridge Comb">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If Wild Comb is not removed promptly, the bees will continue to build on it, potentially creating Bridge Comb that fuses frames together.</data>
      <data key="d8">consequence,problem progression,structural issue</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Inspection" target="Frame">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The removal and examination of one or more frames is a central part of the hive inspection process described in the text.</data>
      <data key="d8">examination,inspection procedure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Inspection" target="Brood Pattern">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During a detailed hive inspection, the beekeeper pulls frames to examine the brood pattern, which serves as a direct indicator of the queen bee's health and laying performance.</data>
      <data key="d8">detailed examination,performance assessment,queen health</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Inspection" target="Hive Weight">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Checking the hive weight by lifting it is a key external inspection method to gauge the amount of honey stores without opening the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping practice,external assessment,monitoring</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Inspection" target="Larvae">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text provides a specific caution for hive inspections: do not expose frames containing larvae to excessive wind or direct sunlight for prolonged periods.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony care,inspection protocol,protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Inspection" target="Food Stores">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A key purpose of a hive inspection is for the beekeeper to determine if the colony has enough food stores to survive, which dictates whether emergency feeding is necessary.</data>
      <data key="d8">inspection purpose,management decision,resource assessment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Eggs" target="Larvae">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Eggs hatch into larvae, representing two consecutive stages of the brood's life cycle that are both sought during inspections to confirm colony health.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae represent the next stage of bee development after hatching from eggs, as part of the bee life cycle.</data>
      <data key="d8">brood component,development,life cycle,progression</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Larvae" target="Pupae">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Larvae develop into pupae, which is the subsequent stage in the bee life cycle before emerging as an adult.</data>
      <data key="d8">development,life cycle,progression</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frame" target="Hive Body">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A hive body is the box-like component of the hive that is designed to hold a set number of frames.</data>
      <data key="d8">containment,structural relationship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-50100ec9bbd638fb693fc24eeb2b3110</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frame" target="Super">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Frames are the removable structures contained within a Super, on which bees build honeycomb for honey storage.</data>
      <data key="d8">component of,structural relationship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Frame" target="Bee Brush">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Bee Brush is used to physically sweep remaining bees off of a Frame after it has been removed from the super.</data>
      <data key="d8">physical interaction,tool application</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Body" target="Feeder Jar">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A feeder jar is often placed within a second, empty hive body to provide syrup to the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">containment,equipment placement,feeding setup</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Body" target="Super">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Super is placed on top of the Hive Body. Bee escape devices are positioned between them to allow bees to move down into the Hive Body.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive structure,positional relationship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The First Summer" target="Summer Management">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Summer management is the set of ongoing beekeeping activities that must be performed during the first summer to ensure the colony thrives.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping practice,management phase,seasonal activity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Robber Bees" target="Entrance Reducer">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The entrance reducer is a tool used specifically to defend against robber bees by making the hive entrance smaller and easier for the colony to guard.</data>
      <data key="d8">defense,prevention,security</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="You (The Beekeeper)" target="Weekly Visits">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The beekeeper is instructed to perform weekly visits to the hive to monitor the colony's progress and health.</data>
      <data key="d8">inspection,monitoring,task</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2b5f69931d3a60795392f1cd07c4c3a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Super" target="Colony Population">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A honey super should only be added when the colony population is strong enough to occupy most of the existing hive bodies, typically seven or eight frames in the second body.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive strength,management decision,prerequisite</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Super" target="Feeding">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text gives a strict rule that feeding should be stopped when honey supers are placed on the hive, to prevent contamination of the honey harvest.</data>
      <data key="d8">conflict,honey purity,management rule</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony Population" target="Winter Cluster">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A substantial Colony Population is required to form a viable Winter Cluster. If the population falls below a minimum level, the cluster cannot generate enough heat to survive the cold, as its surface area becomes too large relative to its volume.</data>
      <data key="d8">critical mass,survival,thermoregulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony Population" target="Fall Requeening">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The primary goal of Fall Requeening is to cause a 'population burst' of new, young bees late in the season, which strengthens the Colony Population for the upcoming winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony management,population boost,winter preparation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony Population" target="Varroa Mites">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Varroa Mites are a major threat to colony health and can negatively impact the Colony Population. Their presence is a key concern that beekeepers must address to ensure a strong population for winter.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,parasitism,risk management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony Population" target="Spring Growth">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A substantial Colony Population that survives winter is a prerequisite for strong Spring Growth, as more bees are needed to expand the cluster and rear new brood.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony recovery,population dynamics,seasonal cycle</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony Population" target="Food Stores">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A larger Colony Population forms a larger, warmer cluster that is better able to move within the hive to access Food Stores, preventing starvation when nearby honey is depleted.</data>
      <data key="d8">resource access,survival,thermoregulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pupae" target="Capped Brood">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Capped brood are the sealed cells that contain and protect the developing pupae.</data>
