    <edge source="Windbreaks" target="Corn">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A University of Nebraska study showed that windbreaks increased corn yields by 20 percent. Corn itself can also be used as a windbreak.</data>
      <data key="d8">crop yield,dual-use crop</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740871</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Windbreaks" target="Wheat">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A University of Nebraska study showed that windbreaks increased wheat yields by 22 percent.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural benefit,crop yield</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740871</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Windbreaks" target="Wildlife Habitat">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text lists providing valuable wildlife habitat as one of the key benefits of establishing windbreaks.</data>
      <data key="d8">ecosystem support,environmental benefit</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740875</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Windbreaks" target="Medicinal Herbs">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Windbreaks create suitable, protected microclimates for growing high-dollar medicinal herbs.</data>
      <data key="d8">microclimate,specialty crops</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740875</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Windbreaks" target="Evergreens">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Evergreens are recommended as a good choice for windbreaks because their density and needle retention provide effective year-round protection.</data>
      <data key="d8">plant selection,windbreak design</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740875</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Windbreaks" target="Deciduous Trees">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Deciduous trees are mentioned as a less effective option for winter windbreaks compared to evergreens because they lose their leaves.</data>
      <data key="d8">plant comparison,seasonal effectiveness</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740875</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Windbreaks" target="Small Grains">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Strips of small grains can be planted to serve as annual windbreaks to slow wind erosion and protect other crops.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural technique,alternative material</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740875</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Windbreaks" target="Vegetables">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Windbreaks, particularly those made of small grains, are used to create a suitable microclimate for vegetables that need wind protection.</data>
      <data key="d8">crop protection,microclimate</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740875</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Windbreaks" target="University Extension Office">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The University Extension Office is recommended as a resource to consult for advice on selecting the best types of trees for local windbreaks.</data>
      <data key="d8">advisory source,agricultural support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-df7ec17c31731168941c83b97fd64a89</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740875</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ventilation and Insulation" target="Metabolism">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The practices of ventilation and insulation are specifically designed to counteract the negative effects of the byproducts of bee metabolism, namely by removing excess moisture and carbon dioxide.</data>
      <data key="d8">cause and effect,environmental control,problem-solution</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ventilation and Insulation" target="Wrapping Hives">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Wrapping hives is presented as an alternative, more labor-intensive method of insulation compared to the primary method described using an insulation board.</data>
      <data key="d8">alternative method,comparative practice,insulation technique</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wrapping Hives" target="Tar Paper">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tar paper is identified as the material most often used for the practice of wrapping hives.</data>
      <data key="d8">insulation method,material usage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Spring" target="Spring Chores">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Spring Chores are a set of hive management tasks that are performed by the beekeeper during the spring season to prepare the colony for the main nectar flow.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive maintenance,seasonal management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Spring" target="Reversing Hive Bodies">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">'Reversing hive bodies' is a specific management action performed by the beekeeper during the spring to facilitate the colony's natural upward expansion.</data>
      <data key="d8">management technique,seasonal task,timing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Insulation Board" target="Upper Entrance">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The insulation board is constructed with a channel on its underside that creates the upper entrance, facilitating ventilation and allowing bees to exit.</data>
      <data key="d8">component function,hive construction,ventilation system</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Upper Entrance" target="Cleansing Trips">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The upper entrance provides an essential exit for bees to take cleansing trips on warm winter days, contributing to hive hygiene.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee behavior,enabling activity,hive access</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-27f6271ed08f494278ee4aa9601556c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mouse Guard" target="Spring Chores">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Removing the mouse guard is one of the preparatory tasks a beekeeper must perform as part of the spring chores.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment removal,seasonal task</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mouse Guard" target="Mice">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A mouse guard is the recommended and best defense to physically prevent mice from entering a beehive.</data>
      <data key="d8">pest prevention,physical barrier</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740503</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cleansing Flights" target="Winter Thaw">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A winter thaw provides the necessary warm conditions that enable the bee colony to perform essential cleansing flights.</data>
      <data key="d8">enabling condition,hygiene,seasonal activity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Emergency Feeding" target="Feeding Syrup">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Feeding syrup is a specific technique for carrying out emergency feeding, recommended for its effectiveness when placed directly inside the hive near the cluster.</data>
      <data key="d8">feeding technique,implementation,methodology</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Emergency Feeding" target="Feeding Candy">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Feeding candy is presented as an alternative method for emergency feeding, requiring a time-consuming preparation process but offering another way to provide sugar to the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">alternative solution,feeding technique,methodology</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Meaningful Nectar Flow" target="Spring Chores">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The beginning of the First Meaningful Nectar Flow is the primary signal for the beekeeper to stop feeding and to perform the essential Spring Chores.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping signal,task trigger</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Meaningful Nectar Flow" target="Dandelions">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In many regions, the blooming of Dandelions serves as the key indicator that the First Meaningful Nectar Flow has begun.</data>
      <data key="d8">foraging signal,natural indicator</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Meaningful Nectar Flow" target="Local Area / Geography">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The specific date and timing of the First Meaningful Nectar Flow is entirely dependent on the local geography and climate of the beekeeper's area.</data>
      <data key="d8">climate influence,geographical dependency</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The New Season" target="Disease And Mite Control">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Dealing with diseases and mites is presented as a crucial aspect of beekeeping that gains importance and requires more attention during 'The New Season'.</data>
      <data key="d8">colony health,preventative care,risk management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The New Season" target="Queen Excluders">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The use of queen excluders is presented as a new technique or aspect of beekeeping that the beekeeper must learn to handle during 'The New Season'.</data>
      <data key="d8">advanced technique,equipment use,skill progression</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Brush" target="Honey Harvest">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Bee Brush is a tool used primarily during the Honey Harvest to clear bees from frames before extracting honey.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping process,equipment use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Brush" target="Fume Board">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Bee Brush (a manual method) and the Fume Board (a chemical method) are presented as alternative techniques for accomplishing the same goal of removing bees from supers.</data>
      <data key="d8">alternative methods,bee removal</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Porter Bee Escape" target="Bee Escape">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Porter Bee Escape is identified in the text as a specific implementation of the general Bee Escape concept.</data>
      <data key="d8">device classification,type of</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Porter Bee Escape" target="Escape Board">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Escape Board and the Porter Bee Escape operate on the same one-way exit principle. The text notes that an inner cover fitted with a Porter Bee Escape effectively becomes an escape board.</data>
      <data key="d8">functional equivalent,shared principle</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Escape Board" target="Honey Harvest">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Escape Board is a device used to passively empty bees from a super over a 24-hour period in preparation for the Honey Harvest.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping process,equipment use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fume Board" target="Honey Harvest">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Fume Board is a tool used to quickly drive bees out of a super with a chemical repellent, which is a key step during the Honey Harvest.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping process,equipment use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fume Board" target="Foul-Smelling Liquid">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Foul-Smelling Liquid is applied to the Fume Board to create the repellent fumes that drive bees out of a super.</data>
      <data key="d8">chemical repellent,used with</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blower" target="Honey Harvest">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Blower is a tool used to effectively remove bees from supers, a task central to the Honey Harvest.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping process,equipment use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blower" target="Super">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Blower is an effective tool used to force air into a Super to remove bees from the frames within it.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee removal,tool application</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blower" target="Burr and Bridge Comb">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The presence of burr and bridge comb makes it more difficult to use a blower to clear bees from a super, as the bees can hide behind the obstructions.</data>
      <data key="d8">harvesting difficulty,process obstruction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Harvest" target="Super">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Super is the primary component from which honey is collected during a Honey Harvest. The process involves removing the supers from the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping,process object</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foul-Smelling Liquid" target="Carbolic Acid">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Carbolic Acid is mentioned as the older, traditional type of Foul-Smelling Liquid used for this purpose.</data>
      <data key="d8">chemical alternative,historical type</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Foul-Smelling Liquid" target="Bee Equipment Suppliers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Modern, less-tainting Foul-Smelling Liquids are available for purchase from Bee Equipment Suppliers.</data>
      <data key="d8">commercial product,source of</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92090c6a7f40e420c9b8e4d961a9a21b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Equipment Suppliers" target="Square Plastic Boxes">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bee equipment suppliers are the commercial source from which beekeepers can obtain square plastic boxes for packing comb honey.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping equipment,commerce,supply chain</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Equipment Suppliers" target="Pollen-Trapping Apparatus">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bee equipment suppliers are the commercial source for the specialized pollen-trapping apparatus used to collect pollen.</data>
      <data key="d8">commerce,specialized equipment,supply chain</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Extractor">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">An extractor is the recommended piece of equipment for performing honey extraction, as it efficiently removes honey from the frames using centrifugal force.&lt;SEP&gt;The extractor is the primary machine used for efficient honey extraction, representing a significant investment or rental decision for the beekeeper.</data>
      <data key="d8">core technology,equipment use,extraction method,process equipment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Crushing and Straining">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Crushing and straining is presented as a messy, less desirable alternative method for honey extraction compared to using a centrifugal extractor.</data>
      <data key="d8">alternative method,process comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Uncapping Knife">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The uncapping knife is a critical tool used in the first step of honey extraction to remove wax cappings from the comb, allowing the honey to be released.</data>
      <data key="d8">process step,tool requirement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Strainer">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Straining is a key step in the honey extraction process to purify the honey by removing wax, propolis, and other debris after it has been removed from the comb.</data>
      <data key="d8">purification,quality control</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Kitchen">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The kitchen is identified as a common, albeit messy, location for conducting honey extraction, particularly for those new to the process.</data>
      <data key="d8">process location,workspace</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Crushing Method">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The crushing method is presented as a messy, less practical, and destructive alternative to using an extractor for honey extraction.</data>
      <data key="d8">alternative method,process comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Hive Debris">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hive debris is an unwanted byproduct generated during the honey extraction process that contaminates the honey and must be removed.</data>
      <data key="d8">byproduct,contamination</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Cappings Container">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A cappings container is a required piece of equipment for the honey extraction process, used during the uncapping stage to collect removed wax.</data>
      <data key="d8">collection,process equipment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Bottling Tank">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bottling tank is used in the final stage of the honey extraction workflow, holding the purified honey before it is put into jars.</data>
      <data key="d8">final stage,process equipment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Extraction" target="Uncapping">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Uncapping the frames is a mandatory preliminary step in the honey extraction process before the frames can be placed in an extractor.</data>
      <data key="d8">prerequisite,process step</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extractor" target="Crushing and Straining">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The extractor is described as the best method for honey extraction, while crushing and straining is offered as a less effective and messier alternative.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment efficiency,method comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-539e9876329b00dde77700328dfd38d1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extractor" target="Honey Gate">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Most extractors are equipped with a honey gate at the bottom of their reservoir to allow for draining the collected honey.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,equipment design</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extractor" target="The Extracting Process">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The extractor is the central piece of equipment used to perform the extracting process, which involves spinning frames to remove honey.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment use,process</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extractor" target="Tangential Extractor">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The tangential extractor is described as one of the two main styles of extractor, and is the most common type for beginners and small operations.</data>
      <data key="d8">classification,sub-type</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extractor" target="Radial Extractor">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The radial extractor is described as the other main style of extractor, typically used in larger operations, and is contrasted with the tangential style.</data>
      <data key="d8">classification,sub-type</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extractor" target="Beginners">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text states that beginners most often use extractors with a small capacity, specifically mentioning two-, three-, or four-frame models.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment scale,user demographic</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uncapping Knife" target="Capping Scratcher">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The capping scratcher is a complementary tool to the uncapping knife, used to open honey cells in recessed areas of the comb that the knife misses.</data>
      <data key="d8">complementary tools,process refinement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uncapping Knife" target="Uncapping">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The uncapping knife is the primary tool used to perform the uncapping process, by slicing the wax seals off the honeycomb.</data>
      <data key="d8">primary function,process tool</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uncapping Knife" target="Electric Plane">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The electric plane is presented as a more sophisticated, albeit heavier and more awkward, alternative to a standard or electric uncapping knife.</data>
      <data key="d8">alternative tools,equipment comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Capping Scratcher" target="Inspecting Brood">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The capping scratcher is a tool used by beekeepers specifically for the task of inspecting brood, as it allows them to uncap cells and remove pupae.</data>
      <data key="d8">beekeeping,diagnostic procedure,tool use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740498</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cappings Container" target="Wax Cappings">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The cappings container is the piece of equipment used to catch and hold the wax cappings as they are sliced off the comb during the uncapping process.</data>
      <data key="d8">collection,equipment purpose</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Strainer" target="Hive Debris">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The primary function of a strainer in the honey extraction process is to filter out and remove hive debris from the liquid honey.</data>
      <data key="d8">filtering,purification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Strainer" target="Skimming">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text presents coarse straining and skimming as two alternative initial methods a beekeeper can choose from to purify honey after extraction.</data>
      <data key="d8">alternative methods,purification choice</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Strainer" target="Filter Cloth">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A filter cloth is a specific type of fine-mesh strainer used for a more thorough, secondary straining of honey to remove smaller particles.</data>
      <data key="d8">refinement,specific type</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Gates" target="Bottling Tank">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A honey gate is an essential component used to construct a honey bottling tank, typically by fitting it to a large plastic pail.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,equipment assembly</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bottling Tank" target="Honey Gate">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A honey gate is an essential component of a bottling tank, used to control the flow of honey into jars cleanly and efficiently.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,equipment design</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bottling Tank" target="The Extracting Process">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The bottling tank is used in the stage immediately following the extracting and straining process to hold the honey before it is bottled.</data>
      <data key="d8">process flow,workflow</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bottling Tank" target="Plastic Pail">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text provides instructions on how to make a bottling tank using a 5- or 6-gallon plastic pail as the main container.</data>
      <data key="d8">DIY equipment,construction</data>
