      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uncapping" target="Wax Cappings">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Uncapping is the specific action that removes the wax cappings from the comb, which are then collected in a separate container.</data>
      <data key="d8">material removal,process output</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9fe3ef9f53a3c23c8d05dafdfe1c88f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Extracting Process" target="Heat">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text suggests warming the honey supers for a day before starting the extracting process, as the applied heat makes the honey flow from the frames and through strainers much more easily.</data>
      <data key="d8">process optimization,temperature</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tangential Extractor" target="Radial Extractor">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text directly compares and contrasts the tangential and radial extractor styles based on how they position frames and extract honey, noting that tangential extracts from one side at a time while radial extracts from both.</data>
      <data key="d8">comparison,equipment types</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tangential Extractor" target="Beginners">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text explicitly states that beginners in beekeeping most often use tangential extractors, particularly those with a small capacity of two to four frames.</data>
      <data key="d8">equipment choice,user demographic</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Honey Granulation" target="Storing Your Honey">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Honey granulation is a primary consideration when storing honey, as it is a natural process that will likely occur over time and may need to be managed.</data>
      <data key="d8">natural phenomenon,process management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bc4c6eeeb090dac26dee2e9e9154d95f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Beginners" target="Beekeeping Books">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Beekeeping books are an essential resource for beginners, who are advised to select recent and nationally relevant publications.</data>
      <data key="d8">education,guidance,resource</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d711f27560f7987b017b85fa9c8a2e56</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740508</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Heat" target="Granulation">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Heat is the primary defense against granulation. Judiciously applied heat works by dissolving the microscopic seed crystals that initiate the granulation process in honey.</data>
      <data key="d8">crystallization,prevention,process control</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Heat" target="Small Operators">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Small operators are specifically advised to limit the use of heat to a maximum of 120°F (49°C) and to use it only when absolutely necessary, to minimize damage to their honey.</data>
      <data key="d8">best practices,operational guidance,risk management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Granulation" target="Large Commercial Honey Producers And Packers">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Large commercial honey producers use an industrial heating process (up to 160°F) specifically to prevent the natural process of granulation in the honey they sell.</data>
      <data key="d8">industrial processing,preservation,quality control</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Granulation" target="Microscopic Seed Crystals">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The presence of microscopic seed crystals in honey is the direct cause of the granulation process; they act as nuclei for larger sugar crystals to form around.</data>
      <data key="d8">causality,crystallization mechanism,physical process</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Large Commercial Honey Producers And Packers" target="Small Operators">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text contrasts the honey processing methods of small operators and large commercial producers, particularly regarding the application of heat to prevent granulation. Large producers use high heat (160°F) with rapid cooling, a process unavailable to most small operators, who are advised to use lower heat (120°F).</data>
      <data key="d8">heat treatment,operational scale,processing methods</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extracted Honey" target="Wired Foundation">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Wired foundation is the specific equipment used within frames to produce extracted honey, as the wires add strength to the comb for the extraction process.</data>
      <data key="d8">production equipment,specific use,structural support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-da9216023ced68a9aa545d43ca1d419f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Products" target="Apitherapy">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Apitherapy is defined as the practice of using all products from the hive for health-related treatments. Hive Products are the core components of this practice.</data>
      <data key="d8">core components,health treatment,methodology</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Products" target="Venom">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Venom is explicitly identified as one of the five main 'other hive products' that are frequently discussed, alongside beeswax, pollen, propolis, and royal jelly.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,example,product classification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hive Products" target="Health Food Supplement">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Many hive products are described as being used or sold as natural foods or health supplements for humans.</data>
      <data key="d8">commercial use,health market,product application</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Apitherapy" target="American Apitherapy Society">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The American Apitherapy Society is an organization directly related to the practice of Apitherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Apitherapy Society is an organization that exists to promote and study the field of Apitherapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">advocacy,organization,practice,promotion,research</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Apitherapy" target="Medical Community">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text establishes a relationship of disagreement, stating that the medical community does not concur with the health treatment claims made by proponents of apitherapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">opposing views,scientific conflict,skepticism</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Apitherapy Society" target="North Hartland, VT">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The American Apitherapy Society is located in North Hartland, VT, as indicated by its mailing address.</data>
      <data key="d8">address,headquarters,location</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Medical Community" target="Bee Venom">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The medical community has a skeptical view of bee venom's health benefits, with the notable exception of its accepted use in desensitization treatments for sting allergies.</data>
      <data key="d8">professional acceptance,scientific validation,skepticism</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bee Venom" target="Drug Companies">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Drug companies actively seek and use bee venom as a raw material to create medical treatments for desensitizing individuals who are allergic to bee stings.</data>
      <data key="d8">allergy treatment,medical sourcing,product development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="State Apiary Inspection Programs" target="American Foulbrood (AFB)">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">State apiary inspection programs were created as a direct response to the severe threat posed by American Foulbrood (AFB), which is a highly contagious and deadly bee disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">disease control,public safety,regulatory response</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="State Apiary Inspection Programs" target="State Apiary Inspector">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The State Apiary Inspector is the individual expert who carries out the work of the State Apiary Inspection Programs, providing diagnostic support to beekeepers.</data>
      <data key="d8">expertise,professional role,program execution</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Foulbrood (AFB)" target="Brood Diseases">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">American Foulbrood is presented as the most serious example within the category of brood diseases, highlighting its severe impact on bee colonies.</data>
      <data key="d8">categorization,disease severity,example</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Foulbrood (AFB)" target="Terramycin">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Terramycin is the primary antibiotic discussed for preventing and treating American Foulbrood, although it does not kill the spores but helps the brood resist infection.</data>
      <data key="d8">antibiotic treatment,disease control,medication</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Foulbrood (AFB)" target="Spores">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Spores are the infectious agents that cause American Foulbrood disease. They are transmitted through brood food and contaminate the hive.</data>
      <data key="d8">cause of disease,infectious agent,pathogen</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-850b95b5ab1cf1d4aef287383ef1ecfe</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740472</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Foulbrood (AFB)" target="State Regulations">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">State Regulations dictate how a beekeeper must respond to an American Foulbrood infection, with some states prohibiting treatment and requiring destruction of the colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">disease control,legal mandate,regulatory oversight</data>
