    <edge source="Brood Cell" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The brood cell is essential for the Varroa mite's reproduction. The adult female mite enters a brood cell, hides in the brood food, and lays its eggs after the cell is capped by the bees, allowing a new generation of mites to develop.</data>
      <data key="d8">life cycle,parasitism,reproduction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740492</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Drone Brood" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Varroa mite displays a strong preference for drone brood, entering drone cells in greater numbers than worker cells to reproduce.&lt;SEP&gt;Varroa mites are more easily found on drone brood, making the inspection of drone pupae a key diagnostic technique for detecting and assessing mite infestations in a colony.</data>
      <data key="d8">diagnostics,host preference,infestation detection,parasite preference,parasitism,reproductive strategy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c20cb47e818c53af03d9f00b71d1b9f</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740492</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Drone Brood" target="Inspecting Brood">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Inspecting brood is most effective when focused on drone brood, as mites are more easily detected on the drone pupae.</data>
      <data key="d8">diagnostic target,inspection focus,pest detection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740498</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Apis Mellifera Scutellata" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Varroa mite's reproductive success is limited when infesting the African bee (Apis mellifera scutellata) because the bee's shorter development time is often insufficient for the mite to mature.</data>
      <data key="d8">development cycle,host resistance,parasitism</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740498</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Apis Mellifera Capensis" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Cape bee (Apis mellifera capensis) is a less suitable host for the Varroa mite due to its shorter development cycle, which disrupts the mite's ability to mature and reproduce.</data>
      <data key="d8">development cycle,host resistance,parasitism</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740498</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ether Roll" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Ether Roll is a specific procedure developed to detect the presence of Varroa mites on adult bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">detection method,diagnostics,pest monitoring</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740498</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Shaking Method" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Shaking Method is a technique used to find Varroa mites by forcibly separating them from adult bees using a liquid.</data>
      <data key="d8">detection method,diagnostics,pest monitoring</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740499</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony Damage and Mortality" target="Puncture Wound">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The puncture wounds created by mites are a significant factor contributing to colony decline because they allow secondary infections and diseases to enter the bees.</data>
      <data key="d8">bee health,disease vector,secondary infection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740498</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colony Damage and Mortality" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The presence and proliferation of Varroa mites within a honey bee colony is the direct cause of serious harm and eventual colony mortality if left unmanaged.</data>
      <data key="d8">causation,colony collapse,pest impact</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740502</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Loss of Hemolymph" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Varroa mites feed on the hemolymph of bees, causing a direct loss of this vital fluid, which weakens the bee.</data>
      <data key="d8">feeding,parasitism,physiological impact</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740502</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Puncture Wound" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The act of a Varroa mite feeding on a bee creates a puncture wound, which is a key mechanism of harm.</data>
      <data key="d8">disease vector,feeding mechanism,injury</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740502</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Physical Deformities" target="Varroa Mite">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Infestation by Varroa mites during the pupal stage causes bees to develop physical deformities such as asymmetrical wings and misshapen legs.</data>
      <data key="d8">birth defects,developmental impact,parasitism</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c856302a8c65c144c91bc5d8547cf8ed</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740502</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inspecting Brood" target="Drone Foundation">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Drone foundation is used to concentrate drone brood in a specific area, making the process of inspecting brood for mites more efficient.</data>
      <data key="d8">hive management,process efficiency,tool use</data>
