      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gardening" target="Voltaire">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text uses Voltaire's character Candide's resolution to 'cultivate his own garden' as a philosophical and metaphorical endorsement for gardening as a response to worldly despair.</data>
      <data key="d8">coping mechanism,metaphor,philosophy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gardening" target="The Elitism Argument">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The elitism argument is a specific critique directed at the practice of home gardening, claiming it is an activity inaccessible and unproductive for lower-income people.</data>
      <data key="d8">accessibility,critique,social debate</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gardening" target="Community">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Gardening is presented as a primary means of renewing and building a sense of community, both in individual neighborhoods and through organized community garden groups.</data>
      <data key="d8">community building,grassroots activity,social connection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gardening" target="Spirituality">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The act of gardening is described as a source of internal, spiritual joy and a way of connecting with the soil, plants, and the world.</data>
      <data key="d8">nature connection,personal fulfillment,spiritual connection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740880</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gardening" target="Candide">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The character Candide's decision to 'cultivate his own garden' is used as a powerful metaphor for gardening being a practical and spiritual solution to despair about the state of the world.</data>
      <data key="d8">metaphor,philosophical solution,symbolism</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-727af20c5834776e4c44ee5e64fd02aa</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
