    <edge source="Legumes" target="Nitrogen">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Legumes are defined by their ability to 'fix' nitrogen from the atmosphere into the soil via a symbiotic relationship with bacteria, making the nutrient available for subsequent crops.&lt;SEP&gt;The text explicitly states that legumes are important for their role in nitrogen fixation in the soil, a key aspect of their agricultural value.</data>
      <data key="d8">nitrogen fixation,nutrient cycling,plant function,soil enrichment,soil science</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Legumes" target="Rhizobia">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Legumes form a symbiotic relationship with Rhizobia bacteria, which live in the plant's root nodules and carry out the process of nitrogen fixation.&lt;SEP&gt;Legumes have a symbiotic connection with Rhizobia bacteria, which enables the nitrogen fixation process by forming nodules on the plant roots to store nitrogen from the air.</data>
      <data key="d8">microbiology,nitrogen fixation,root nodules,symbiosis</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Legumes" target="Green Manure">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Legumes are a primary type of crop used as green manure because of the high amount of nitrogen they release into the soil upon decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;The text identifies legumes as a primary type of plant used for green manure to improve soil quality.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural practice,soil fertility</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a464eab8eee71e80f3498a79e8163ee7</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Legumes" target="Non-legumes">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Legumes and non-legumes are often planted together as a mix. The non-legume (e.g., rye) helps capture soil nitrogen while the legume (e.g., vetch) fixes new nitrogen from the air, creating a balanced system for soil improvement.</data>
      <data key="d8">companion planting,nutrient management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Legumes" target="Cover Crops">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Legumes are a major category of cover crops, specifically valued for their ability to add nitrogen to the soil.</data>
      <data key="d8">crop classification,soil management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Legumes" target="Dr. C. J. Willard">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Dr. C. J. Willard is an expert on legumes whose research demonstrated that the timing of plowing under a legume crop (sweet clover) directly impacts the amount of nitrogen it adds to the soil.</data>
      <data key="d8">expert research,nitrogen yield</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Legumes" target="Inoculant">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The inoculant is applied directly to the seeds of the legume you are growing to ensure the presence of the correct Rhizobia bacteria for nitrogen fixation.</data>
      <data key="d8">application,crop preparation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Legumes" target="Time Of Cutting">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The timing of cutting or plowing under a legume crop directly determines the total amount of nitrogen it will contribute to the soil, as shown by Dr. Willard's research.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural timing,yield determination</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b66ffae2696b4f9024085ef90af5320a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Environmental Soundness" target="Soil">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Protecting soil from erosion is given as a primary example of what it means to be environmentally sound in farming.</data>
      <data key="d8">ecological practice,resource conservation</data>
