    </edge>
    <edge source="Organic Matter" target="Azotobacter">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The addition of organic matter to the soil encourages the multiplication of Azotobacter populations.</data>
      <data key="d8">nutrient source,population growth</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740863</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Organic Matter" target="Fungi">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fungi derive their nutrients from decomposing organic material, playing a key role as decomposers in the soil food web.</data>
      <data key="d8">decomposition,nutrient source</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740865</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Organic Matter" target="Soil Microorganisms">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Soil microorganisms, particularly bacteria, consume organic matter (such as dead root tissue) as their raw food source, breaking it down into nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d8">decomposition,food source,nutrient cycle</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Organic Matter" target="Continuous Cropping">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The practice of continuous cropping is explicitly stated to cause a rapid loss of soil organic matter.</data>
      <data key="d8">cause and effect,soil degradation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Organic Matter" target="Green Manure">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A green-manure crop is turned under specifically for the purpose of adding organic matter and/or nitrogen to the soil.</data>
      <data key="d8">organic farming,soil amendment,soil composition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8b6332789cdbd68f546cf7328c6217fd</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Organic Matter" target="Microbial Action">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Increased microbial action is described as 'burning up' organic matter in the soil, which reduces soil fertility.</data>
      <data key="d8">biological process,soil degradation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8c12c90d0c9b0f56b008795013b9e85a</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740870</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Living Soil" target="Earthworms">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Earthworms are described as the 'largest of the soil's creatures' and a key component of the living soil ecosystem, performing multiple vital tasks like aeration and fertilization.</data>
      <data key="d8">ecosystem component,key inhabitant</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740863</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Living Soil" target="Bacteria">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bacteria are a fundamental component of living soil, with billions of organisms existing in healthy topsoil, playing a critical role in nutrient cycling.</data>
      <data key="d8">ecosystem component,microbial population</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740863</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Living Soil" target="Fungi">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Fungi are identified as one of the key organism groups within living soil, acting as decomposers and, in some cases, forming symbiotic relationships with plants.</data>
      <data key="d8">decomposition,ecosystem component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740863</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Living Soil" target="Topsoil">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text states that most of the organisms that constitute 'Living Soil' inhabit the topsoil, defining it as the primary zone of biological activity.</data>
      <data key="d8">habitat,location</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740863</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Living Soil" target="Plant Roots">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Plant roots are an active part of the soil ecosystem, helping to supply the needs (food, water, air) of the community of living soil organisms.</data>
      <data key="d8">ecosystem support,symbiotic environment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740863</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Living Soil" target="Plants">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Living soil provides the physical structure to hold plants upright and a biological system that provides essential nutrients for their survival and growth.</data>
      <data key="d8">foundational support,nutrient provision</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
