    </edge>
    <edge source="Mycorrhiza" target="Plant Roots">
      <data key="d6">19.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mycorrhiza forms a mutually beneficial, or symbiotic, relationship with plant roots. The fungus helps the plant absorb nutrients and water, while the plant provides carbohydrates to the fungus.&lt;SEP&gt;Mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association that directly involves plant roots. The fungus partners with the roots to enhance the plant's ability to absorb nutrients and water.</data>
      <data key="d8">nutrient exchange,nutrient uptake,root function,symbiosis,symbiotic relationship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740863</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mycorrhiza" target="Plants">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A mycorrhizal association is very common, occurring with about 80 percent of agricultural plants, highlighting a widespread symbiotic dependency.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural significance,symbiosis</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-445fbff3137d26bce84d879934fb156e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740863</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mycorrhiza" target="Nutrients">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The mycorrhizal association is beneficial because it enhances the ability of a plant to absorb nutrients and water from the soil.</data>
      <data key="d8">nutrient uptake,plant benefit,symbiosis</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f407f39fbfb8d8caa0eba09a242cee00</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740867</data>
    </edge>
