    <edge source="Feminism" target="Title IX">
      <data key="d6">17.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Title IX is cited by Mary Seton Corboy as a concrete example of how the abstract principles of a movement (feminism) can be successfully ingrained into law and society.&lt;SEP&gt;Title IX is presented as a concrete, successful outcome of the feminist movement, which made its protections against discrimination a guaranteed part of the law.</data>
      <data key="d8">historical example,legal achievement,legal embodiment,movement outcome,social change</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-6576c76d8821b0180981d641c63968e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd762b4d6c9647db05924d3e745a6991</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feminism" target="Women's Rights">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The feminist movement was dedicated to establishing and protecting women's rights, to the point where they are now often taken for granted.</data>
