    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Sustainable Development" target="&quot;Jobs Or The Environment&quot; Debate">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The entire mission of Appalachian Sustainable Development is to reject the 'jobs or the environment' debate by creating economic opportunities through environmentally sustainable practices like organic farming.</data>
      <data key="d8">mission statement,organizational purpose</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Sustainable Development" target="Post-Coal and Post-Tobacco Era">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Appalachian Sustainable Development's mission is a direct response to the economic challenges facing Appalachia in the post-coal and post-tobacco era, aiming to create a new, sustainable economic foundation.</data>
      <data key="d8">economic response,regional revitalization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Sustainable Development" target="Sustainable Farming and Forestry">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Appalachian Sustainable Development strategically chose to focus its efforts on sustainable farming and forestry because they were identified as existing strengths of the Appalachian region.</data>
      <data key="d8">regional assets,strategic focus</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Sustainable Development" target="Tobacco Industry">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A core part of Appalachian Sustainable Development's work is to help farmers transition away from the declining tobacco industry by providing training and market access for alternative crops like organic vegetables.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural transition,industry shift</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Sustainable Development" target="Ukrops">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Appalachian Sustainable Development approached the grocery chain Ukrops as part of its bold strategy to secure larger, more reliable markets for the produce grown by its network of farmers.</data>
      <data key="d8">business partnership,market development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Sustainable Development" target="Whole Foods">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Appalachian Sustainable Development approached the grocery chain Whole Foods in a strategic move to build a substantial market for local produce and secure contracts that would incentivize farmers to transition to new crops.</data>
      <data key="d8">business partnership,market development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Sustainable Development" target="Abingdon, Va">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Appalachian Sustainable Development is physically located in Abingdon, VA.</data>
      <data key="d8">geography,organization location</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d3d51c80e398fde0d7a70a89888fa0e</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740887</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bioregional Food Clusters" target="Appalachian Harvest">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The 'Appalachian Harvest' brand is presented as a concrete example of the regional branding component that is essential to Joel Salatin's 'Bioregional Food Clusters' concept.</data>
      <data key="d8">case study,concept validation,regional branding</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-74e3df5ce7be88ffd8bdf29e5b896da3</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bioregional Food Clusters" target="Appalachian Grown">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The 'Appalachian Grown' brand serves as a real-world illustration that the regional branding aspect of Joel Salatin's 'Bioregional Food Clusters' model is already being successfully implemented.</data>
      <data key="d8">case study,concept validation,regional branding</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-74e3df5ce7be88ffd8bdf29e5b896da3</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polyface Apprenticeship Program" target="Polyface Internship Program">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The internship and apprenticeship programs represent two distinct tiers of farmer training offered by Polyface, with the internship being a seasonal, introductory experience and the apprenticeship being a year-round, immersive commitment.</data>
      <data key="d8">farmer training,labor strategy,program comparison</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-74e3df5ce7be88ffd8bdf29e5b896da3</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Harvest" target="Certified Brand Name">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">'Appalachian Harvest' is cited as another successful example of a Certified Brand Name, directly paralleling the 'Appalachian Grown' brand.</data>
      <data key="d8">brand example,comparative success</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2d3545603e988fef215052ef7f681c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Harvest" target="Appalachian Grown">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Appalachian Harvest brand is explicitly compared to the Appalachian Grown brand, as both serve as umbrella labels for small-scale agricultural producers within the Appalachian region.</data>
      <data key="d8">brand comparison,regional strategy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b26f04bc8df6a747ead835cc8d210eec</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Grown" target="ASAP">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">ASAP created and manages the 'Appalachian Grown' brand and certification program to define 'local' food, ensure quality, and build consumer trust.&lt;SEP&gt;ASAP is the creator and manager of the 'Appalachian Grown' certified brand, which is a key part of its strategy to build buyer confidence and market local food.</data>
      <data key="d8">brand creation,brand development,certification,local marketing,ownership</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2d3545603e988fef215052ef7f681c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Grown" target="Southern Appalachian Mountains">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The 'Appalachian Grown' brand is explicitly tied to the identity of the Southern Appalachian Mountains, defining its 'local' area based on the region's geography and culture.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural heritage,place-based branding,regional identity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Grown" target="Retailers">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Retailers sell 'Appalachian Grown' products, which are supported by ASAP-developed displays and signage to tell the story of the farms.</data>
      <data key="d8">brand integrity,product sales,retail marketing</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ba841e41630d5b5e46b271cb1a11d579</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Grown" target="Certified Brand Name">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">'Appalachian Grown' is the primary example of a Certified Brand Name that was successfully developed and implemented by ASAP.</data>
      <data key="d8">brand example,implementation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2d3545603e988fef215052ef7f681c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appalachian Grown" target="Southern Appalachia">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The 'Appalachian Grown' brand certifies food from the Southern Appalachia region, reflecting the distinct qualities and provenance of its area of origin.</data>
      <data key="d8">geographic identity,regional brand</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a2d3545603e988fef215052ef7f681c4</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Model A" target="Industrial-size Chicken Contract Growers">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Model A is presented as a direct, risk-averse alternative to the conventional debt-heavy model under which industrial-size chicken contract growers operate.</data>
      <data key="d8">alternative business model,contract farming,risk mitigation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3699752bc52dee1358ad76a26a9c6fb9</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Model A" target="Model A Farmer">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Model A Farmer operates entirely within the framework of Model A, which defines her role, compensation, and lack of financial risk.</data>
      <data key="d8">business framework,contract farming,economic role</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3699752bc52dee1358ad76a26a9c6fb9</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Model B" target="Private 10-cow Dairy Herd">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The framework of Model B explicitly allows and supports the establishment of the farmer's private dairy herd on the same land used for Polyface operations.</data>
      <data key="d8">business opportunity,entrepreneurship,synergistic model</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3699752bc52dee1358ad76a26a9c6fb9</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Model B Farmer" target="Polyface Cow Herd">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Model B farmer's primary contractual obligation to Polyface is to move the Polyface Cow Herd every day, for which he receives a lump-sum payment.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural service,herd management,labor for payment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3699752bc52dee1358ad76a26a9c6fb9</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Model B Farmer" target="Private 10-cow Dairy Herd">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Model B Farmer establishes and owns his Private 10-cow Dairy Herd as a separate business, which is enabled by his partnership with Polyface.</data>
      <data key="d8">business ownership,diversification,entrepreneurship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3699752bc52dee1358ad76a26a9c6fb9</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Model B Farmer" target="Farm Owner (Model B)">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Model B Farmer lives on the farm owner's property and pays the owner a royalty on milk sales from his private herd.</data>
      <data key="d8">business relationship,royalty agreement,tenancy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3699752bc52dee1358ad76a26a9c6fb9</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Model C: Own Brand" target="Polyface Subcontract Farmer">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Model C is the specific business framework that defines the role and operations of a Polyface Subcontract Farmer, enabling them to start their own business with Polyface's support.</data>
      <data key="d8">business framework,entrepreneurship program,role definition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-77562613f0c5d12383f7ffab65ce2e45</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Model D: Lease to Own" target="New Farmer (Model D)">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Model D is the 'Lease to Own' program specifically designed for the New Farmer, who is a former apprentice, outlining the terms of their work, financing, and path to ownership.</data>
      <data key="d8">business framework,equity pathway,farmer development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-77562613f0c5d12383f7ffab65ce2e45</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Steve Ells" target="Chipotle-Polyface relationship">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">As CEO and founder of Chipotle, Steve Ells initiated the Chipotle-Polyface relationship, believing it was a perfect fit for his company's ethos.</data>
      <data key="d8">corporate vision,partnership initiation,strategic alignment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-77562613f0c5d12383f7ffab65ce2e45</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Steve Ells" target="Petrilli">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Petrilli, a Chipotle employee, directly attributes the success of the Polyface partnership to the empowerment and relentless commitment provided by the company's leader, Steve Ells, highlighting the importance of top-down support.</data>
      <data key="d8">corporate culture,empowerment,internal leadership</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63208f55f5e282db2dc1514f989da622</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Steve Ells" target="Joel Holland">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Joel Holland, as Chipotle's head chef, was tasked with adapting the original recipe created by the company's leader, Steve Ells, to suit the specific qualities of Polyface's pork.</data>
      <data key="d8">internal collaboration,recipe adaptation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63208f55f5e282db2dc1514f989da622</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Joel Holland" target="Charlottesville">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Joel Holland traveled to Charlottesville multiple times for the purpose of working with Polyface to adjust Chipotle's pork recipe.</data>
      <data key="d8">collaboration site,work travel</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-77562613f0c5d12383f7ffab65ce2e45</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Joel Holland" target="RECIPE ADJUSTMENTS">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">As Chipotle's head chef, Joel Holland was directly responsible for carrying out the recipe adjustments, flying to Charlottesville multiple times to perfect the use of Polyface's pork.</data>
      <data key="d8">culinary execution,problem-solving,product development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-77562613f0c5d12383f7ffab65ce2e45</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polyface Subcontract Farmer" target="Polyface Protocols and Standards">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Polyface Subcontract Farmer is required to raise their animals according to the Polyface Protocols and Standards, ensuring quality and brand consistency.</data>
      <data key="d8">brand standards,operational compliance,quality assurance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-77562613f0c5d12383f7ffab65ce2e45</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chipotle-Polyface relationship" target="RECIPE ADJUSTMENTS">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Recipe adjustments were a critical and necessary component of the Chipotle-Polyface relationship, required to make the partnership logistically and economically viable for Polyface.</data>
      <data key="d8">operational solution,partnership challenge,product adaptation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-77562613f0c5d12383f7ffab65ce2e45</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chipotle-Polyface relationship" target="COOKING METHODS">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Adapting cooking methods was a key challenge that had to be overcome to make the Chipotle-Polyface relationship successful, due to the unique properties of Polyface's pork.</data>
      <data key="d8">operational adaptation,partnership challenge,quality control</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-77562613f0c5d12383f7ffab65ce2e45</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Salatin" target="Temperature Log Microchip Thermometers">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The problem of Salatin not being able to afford a refrigerated van was solved by the implementation of 'temperature log' microchip thermometers, which allowed the deal with Chipotle to proceed.</data>
      <data key="d8">enabling technology,logistical solution,problem-solving</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63208f55f5e282db2dc1514f989da622</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Salatin" target="Hourly Wages">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Salatin expresses a strong dislike for hourly wages, viewing them as a subjective and ineffective way to measure performance compared to an incentivized system.</data>
      <data key="d8">compensation philosophy,labor critique,performance metrics</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c8b5f0f35b6bfccad911410ecdcdb7c1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Salatin" target="Local Copper Fittings Manufacturing Plant">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Salatin uses a worker from the local copper fittings manufacturing plant as an anecdote to illustrate the power and success of an 'incentivized' payment system.</data>
      <data key="d8">business example,compensation model,illustrative anecdote</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c8b5f0f35b6bfccad911410ecdcdb7c1</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Salatin" target="Petrilli">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Salatin and Petrilli collaborated directly, sitting down together to develop a timetable and strategy for introducing Polyface meat into the Chipotle supply chain. Their joint experience led to shared lessons about scaling and communication.</data>
      <data key="d8">business collaboration,partnership,supply chain planning</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3b16258e5de8192443e482a18e2beeb8</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Salatin" target="Whole Animal Sales">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Salatin is the primary advocate for the 'Whole Animal Sales' strategy, explaining that a diversified portfolio of buyers is essential for a livestock producer's survival.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural economics,business philosophy,sales strategy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3b16258e5de8192443e482a18e2beeb8</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Salatin" target="Buffalo Ridge">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Salatin uses the story of Buffalo Ridge's bankruptcy as a cautionary tale to illustrate the critical importance of his 'Whole Animal Sales' business philosophy.</data>
      <data key="d8">business failure,cautionary tale,industry analysis</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3b16258e5de8192443e482a18e2beeb8</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Heidi Wederquist" target="The Cold Chain">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Heidi Wederquist's innovative suggestion to use microchip thermometers directly solved the problem of how to audit and ensure the integrity of the 'cold chain' for meat transported from Polyface.</data>
      <data key="d8">food safety,logistics,problem-solving</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63208f55f5e282db2dc1514f989da622</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cascadian Farm" target="Gene Kahn">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Gene Kahn was the founder of Cascadian Farm, starting it as a small organic operation and overseeing its growth into a major corporate brand.</data>
      <data key="d8">business evolution,founder,organic farming</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63208f55f5e282db2dc1514f989da622</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cascadian Farm" target="Welch's">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cascadian Farm was sold by its founder, Gene Kahn, to Welch's as a key step in its transition from an independent farm to a corporate entity.</data>
      <data key="d8">business sale,corporate acquisition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63208f55f5e282db2dc1514f989da622</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cascadian Farm" target="Industrial Organic">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The story of Cascadian Farm's growth from a small farm to a corporate entity is used as a primary example of the rise of 'industrial organic' and the debate surrounding it.</data>
      <data key="d8">case study,corporatization,industry trend</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63208f55f5e282db2dc1514f989da622</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cascadian Farm" target="Washington's Cascade Mountains">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Washington's Cascade Mountains are the original location where Gene Kahn founded Cascadian Farm in 1972.</data>
      <data key="d8">founding location,geographical origin</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-63208f55f5e282db2dc1514f989da622</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cascadian Farm" target="General Mills">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">General Mills is the current corporate owner of the Cascadian Farm brand, an example of the conglomeration occurring within the organic industry.</data>
      <data key="d8">brand ownership,corporate acquisition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0d31a2f89a77b64e0ae04120df1fdb3b</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1753740886</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cascadian Farm" target="Kahn">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Kahn was the founder of Cascadian Farm and made the decision to sell it to mainstream corporations, beginning with Welch's. This act is central to his role in the text as a symbol of the organic movement's commercialization.</data>
      <data key="d8">business sale,commercialization,founder</data>
